window.TEAMS = [{"team":"McGill","id":"6040","section":"Undergrad","country":"Canada","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/mcgill/","projectTitle":"LanTurn","tagline":"Lanmodulin displayed on yeast, harvested by light-triggered flocculation, deployed on-site as a tolling service for mine tailings","summary":"McGill engineered Saccharomyces cerevisiae EBY100 to display lanmodulin variants on its surface via the Aga1-Aga2 system for selective rare-earth binding, and to flocculate on command through a blue-light VP16-EL222 / C120 optogenetic circuit driving a truncated 678FLO1 flocculin, so that REE-loaded cells can be separated by gravity settling instead of centrifugation. Light-induced flocculation reached 66-75 percent against 24-25 percent in dark controls, and LanM surface display was confirmed by flow cytometry and confocal microscopy. The commercial case is a full industrial techno-economic analysis rather than a canvas: gate-to-gate CAPEX and OPEX, a unit cost of $11.83-20.58 USD per kg REO, a full-scale NPV case study on the Ashram tailings in Nunavik, Quebec, and seven separate sensitivity analyses. They were nominated for Best Entrepreneurship (Undergrad) and Best Biomanufacturing.","awards":["Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Biomanufacturing Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Technology Readiness Levels (NASA nine-level scale, chosen specifically because Canadian cleantech and mining funding programmes gate on TRL) as the backbone of a four-phase staged development plan, each phase carrying goals, named partners, a funding figure and source, and a risk-and-mitigation table. Plus techno-economic analysis in three parts (gate-to-gate, full-scale case study, sensitivity), competitor tiering into direct, substitute and future, a four-option exit plan, a skill-gap analysis mapped against the advisory board, an IP and freedom-to-operate review, and a one-year incorporation plan with a one-pager.","dataviz":"A tabbed entrepreneurship page with a Sankey flowchart of critical-materials flows, a rare-earth demand-and-project-gap chart, a TEA workflow flowsheet, CAPEX/OPEX tables for both gate-to-gate and full-scale cases, a financial-analysis panel, and seven sensitivity charts (adsorption capacity, desorption efficiency, product purity discount, feedstock grade, yeast dosing concentration, PLS pH, discount rate), plus a tolling-model process diagram and an advisory-board gallery.","datascience":"Genuinely industrial-grade analysis. Mass balances built in Excel at a PLS flowthrough of 1,200,000 cubic metres per year, 1 g/L REE and 80 percent target recovery; unit-cost benchmarking per kg TREO against solvent extraction, column-immobilised lanmodulin and a Caulobacter-based bioadsorbent; NPV of USD 51M over 15 years in the high-uptake case versus USD -0.2M in the low-uptake case, with payback of 2.6-5.9 years. The sensitivity work generates actual engineering targets: breakeven at about 23 mg/g adsorption capacity, rising to about 35 mg/g once a 20-30 percent impurity discount on mixed REO is applied; every 5 percent loss of desorption efficiency costs roughly USD 10M of project value; profitability is governed almost entirely by initial PLS pH rather than the bioadsorbent's operating pH; and the low-uptake case goes negative above a 15 percent discount rate. Separately, the TORCH dry-lab project runs GROMACS molecular dynamics with QM/MM to engineer the LanM binding site, including a real debugging story about periodic boundary conditions inflating RMSD tenfold.","software":"TORCH, a QM/MM and molecular-dynamics pipeline for optimising the lanmodulin binding site, with custom scripts for RMSD, clustering, radial distribution functions and explicit oxygen-distance coordination counting.","stack":["GROMACS","VMD","Python","Excel mass-balance and TEA models","S. cerevisiae EBY100 / pYD1 Aga1-Aga2 display","VP16-EL222 / C120 optogenetics","flow cytometry","confocal microscopy","ICP-MS","Xylenol Orange colorimetry"],"techniques":["yeast surface display with FLAG-tag detection","blue-light-inducible flocculation and Helm's flocculation assay","REE adsorption and desorption from flocs","restriction-enzyme cloning with LiOAc/SDS yeast colony PCR","sulfuric acid leaching of real tailings samples","sensitivity and NPV analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"They host their own annual synthetic-biology conference, On the Verge, themed in 2026 as 'On the Verge of Running Out: Building the Bioeconomy', deliberately assembling corporate industry leaders, startup founders, policy experts, researchers and students around resource extraction. They also ran school and summer-camp workshops (microbial painting, acid-base chemistry) and are responding to a teacher's request for more advanced genetic-engineering workshops by exploring chromoproteins for new colours.","ihp":"Over 100 stakeholders engaged, with a consultation map that traces who informed what and who they were referred to next, explicitly arguing that no consultation stands alone. The consequential ones are documented as reversals. They abandoned ultra-purification of REEs entirely after Dr. Michael Free and others showed the separation factor cannot support the claim and that the economics would hinge on only the last 0.9 percent of purity, repositioning instead on selective pre-solvent-extraction concentration. Feedstock choice came from Dr. Jayme Fehyl-Buska and Dr. Kevin Moran of CVW Sustainable Royalties; Ms. Julia Vilela Carvalho of UNEP steered them to fresh rather than legacy tailings for liability and environmental-regulation reasons; Peter van Alphen, Bastien Fresia and Gilles Roy of Nuvau Minerals Corp. confirmed mines have space and are used to disposing of new materials, which is why the process was designed around equipment mines already run, and recommended the tolling model. Protocol-level consultations repeatedly rescued failing experiments: Dr. Denzel Ignacia raised the flocculation induction OD to 3-5, Mira Loock supplied a surface-display-specific protocol at OD 0.8-1.2 that finally produced positive metal-binding results, Michael Bellucci spotted restriction sites less than 30 bp apart as the reason digests failed. Jan Morrill of Earthworks pushed back on their sustainability framing by insisting re-mining can inflict the same harm as direct mining and taught them a Free, Prior and Informed Consent framework. Dr. Yvette Baninla widened the analysis to the geopolitics of offshore REE processing and Indigenous consultation.","realWorld":"Among the strongest commercialisation cases in iGEM 2026, and the only one in this group with real third-party commitments on paper. The business model is a B2B on-site tolling service: the mine keeps ownership and legal liability for its tailings, LanTurn operates a small modular plant on site and returns a concentrated mixed rare earth oxide, which avoids transport cost and keeps tailings-management liability off LanTurn's books. That model was recommended by Nuvau Minerals Corp. and then re-validated at the REM3 Executive Summit with potential offtakers and feedstock providers. Unit economics are properly built rather than asserted: $11.83-20.58 USD per kg REO gate-to-gate, with about 92 percent of CAPEX in desorption columns and resins and 54-68 percent of OPEX in fermentation, compared against solvent extraction (at worst 12 percent more expensive, at best 370-720 percent cheaper on impure low-grade feedstock), column-immobilised lanmodulin (16-100 percent cheaper) and a lanthanide-binding-tag bioadsorbent. The design choices are individually cost-justified, which is rare: surface display versus purified LanM in agarose resin, flocculation versus continuous centrifugation (though they honestly note this saves only $0.21 per kg TREO and is 'not hugely significant'), and light induction versus copper, ethanol or galactose. The Ashram case study is a real deposit with 51 Mt of flotation tailings at about 0.4 percent TREO, giving a USD 51M NPV over 15 years with a 2.6-year payback in the optimistic case and a marginally negative NPV with a 5.9-year payback in the pessimistic case, and they say so rather than quoting only the good number. The headline $560 billion of locked value in tailings is derived, not borrowed: tailings tonnage times REE concentration times market prices, with a 70 percent discount applied for mixed rather than pure REEs, and they immediately contextualise it against a global REE market of only about USD 4.5 billion. Institutional traction is concrete: entry into the Dobson Lean Startup Accelerator at McGill's Dobson Centre for Entrepreneurship in September 2026, ongoing mentorship from Felicity Meyer, a cleantech investment manager at BoxOne Ventures, guidance from Chris Corkery on McGill's research-commercialisation and tech-transfer route, an eleven-person advisory board spanning hydrometallurgy, mining, VC, biomanufacturing and regulatory affairs, published letters of support from Bastien Fresia of Nuvau Minerals Corp. and Dr. Boyd Davis of Kingston Process Metallurgy, real tailings samples already secured from a large Canadian mining company that plans to continue research with them, and named scale-up facilities (Concordia Bioprocessing Facility at 1000 L, KPM's 10,000 sq ft Kingston pilot plant and its biomining division launching 2027, a Sudbury bioleaching facility). The funding plan is staged and specific: about $28,800 across four quarters for the laboratory phase from university funds and non-dilutive grants (NSERC, NRC IRAP, NFRF), then angel and cleantech VC after incorporation, with later phases pointing at NRCan's CMRDD programme (typically $4M+ for TRL 6-8) and MICA Canada (up to $2.5M for TRL 7-9), benchmarked against actual biomining raises (Allonnia USD 20M Series A, pH7 USD 32M Series B). The IP work is the real thing rather than a slogan: they checked that the foundational yeast surface-display patents US6699658B1 and US7465787B2 have expired, identified live LanM patents (CA3170828A1, CA3223988A1, US20240183787A1) and a pending Princeton application WO2025240129A2 that explicitly claims EL222/C120 optogenetics in S. cerevisiae, and concluded they must differentiate technically, disclose to McGill's Tech Transfer Office before publishing anything patentable, and run a formal freedom-to-operate analysis. Blunt caveats: there is no incorporated company yet, only a one-year incorporation plan; no revenue, no signed tolling contract and no term sheet; and they state outright that many TEA parameters are literature estimates rather than their own measurements, which is exactly why the sensitivity analysis exists as a de-risking to-do list for the wet and dry labs. Their ethical self-assessment is unusually unflattering, admitting LanTurn neither remediates tailings nor meaningfully reduces their volume, that re-mining can reintroduce mining harms to communities that have not seen a project for decades, and that the rare earths will mostly end up in consumer goods and defence rather than the clean-energy story, with a stated commitment not to market it otherwise.","partners":["Nuvau Minerals Corp. (Peter van Alphen, Bastien Fresia, Gilles Roy; tolling model advice and a letter of support)","Kingston Process Metallurgy Inc. (Dr. Boyd Davis, Dr. Fariborz Faraji; letter of support, scale-up and demonstration, biomining division launching 2027)","BoxOne Ventures (Felicity Meyer, cleantech investment manager; ongoing mentorship)","McGill Dobson Centre for Entrepreneurship / Dobson Lean Startup Accelerator (entered September 2026; Dr. Neils Billou)","Chris Corkery, McGill research commercialisation and tech transfer","Concordia Bioprocessing Facility (Dr. Lalit Kumar; 1000 L fermentation scale-up)","National Research Council Canada (mine tailings samples)","An unnamed large Canadian mining company (real tailings samples, intends to continue research together)","UNEP Minerals Unit (Julia Vilela Carvalho), Earthworks (Jan Morrill), C2M2A (Marilyn Spink)","Dr. Andrew Zimmerman, University of Florida (biochar conversion of spent yeast)","The Lumb Group, McGill; Mira Loock; Dr. Denzel Ignacia; Dr. Kevin Gardner; Barstow lab and REEgen (in contact)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6040/wiki/entrep-page/tab-2/pt-financial-analysis.avif","caption":"Financial analysis of the Ashram tailings re-mining case study, giving USD 51M NPV over 15 years in the high-uptake scenario against a marginally negative NPV in the low-uptake case","credit":"Team McGill, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6040/wiki/entrep-page/tab-2/pt1-opex-capex.avif","caption":"Gate-to-gate CAPEX and OPEX breakdown for LanTurn, showing desorption columns and resins at about 92 percent of capital cost and fermentation at 54-68 percent of operating cost","credit":"Team McGill, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6040/wiki/entrep-page/tab-3/tolling-model-2.avif","caption":"The B2B tolling business model: the mine keeps ownership and liability for its tailings, LanTurn processes on site and returns a concentrated mixed rare earth oxide","credit":"Team McGill, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6040/wiki/entrep-page/tab-2/ashram-map.avif","caption":"The proposed Ashram Rare Earths Project in Nunavik, Quebec, chosen as the demonstration case because its tailings are too dilute for existing methods but rich enough for LanTurn","credit":"Team McGill, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Run the techno-economic analysis early and let its sensitivity results set the lab's experimental targets: McGill's TEA produced a hard number (about 35 mg/g adsorption capacity once the mixed-oxide impurity discount is applied) that became the wet lab's de-risking goal, which is how commercialisation work earns its place rather than sitting beside the science. Pair that with their TRL-staged plan, where each phase names its funding figure, funding source and named partner facility, so an institution can see exactly what the next stage costs and who must say yes.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/mcgill/","https://2026.igem.wiki/mcgill/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/mcgill/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/mcgill/assets/index-DZ45NXgC.js"],"rank":14},{"team":"UOregon","id":"6041","section":"Overgrad","country":"United States","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/","projectTitle":"AquaFlock","tagline":"E. coli engineered to sequester groundwater nitrate as cyanophycin, then flocculate so the nitrogen can be harvested back as fertilizer","summary":"UOregon engineered E. coli to pull excess environmental nitrate into the nitrogen storage polymer cyanophycin and, once a cyanophycin threshold is reached, to express flocculation proteins so cells sink and can be harvested as a fertilizer substitute, closing the nitrogen loop. The target users are rural well owners and farmers in Oregon whose water is rarely tested. The education practice is the standout: three original curricula built for three audiences with increasing prior knowledge, developed through an explicit Design-Build-Test-Learn loop and grounded in Universal Design for Learning plus STEAM. The team also built an offline desktop search engine indexing the full text of 1,354 iGEM team wikis and 20,761 pages from 2022-2025.","awards":["Best Education — Nominee (Overgrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Education itself was run as a DBTL engineering cycle, with a documented Reflective Theory of Change diagram and a standardised success rubric (engagement, science understanding, creativity), both adapted from an Oregon State University STEM Research Center presentation at the Polar STEAM Summit 2026. Learning objectives were written against the measurable verbs of Bloom's Taxonomy (Remember, Understand, Apply, Analyze, Evaluate, Create).","dataviz":"Hand-illustrated scientific diagrams throughout: all educational materials were digitally illustrated by hand by the team, including a central-dogma comic, illustrated workbooks, and illustrated synthetic-biology-tool cards used as a tactile matching activity.","datascience":"Post-workshop feedback surveys with section-level ranking; for Workshop 3, 8 of 15 respondents had heard of synthetic biology before attending, and bioethics and science communication ranked as the favourite sections, with the illustrated workbook, real-world examples and small-group discussions named the most effective learning tools.","software":"iGEM Wiki Search: a desktop application that searches the full text of 1,354 team wikis and 20,761 pages from the 2022-2025 competitions from a single search box, running offline with no server. It automates wiki collection and processing, builds a full-text index, ranks whole projects rather than individual pages, crawls responsibly, handles JavaScript-only wikis, and was tested cross-platform. The team benchmarked it explicitly against iGEM's Phoenix Project (metadata only) and iGEM Munich 2024's Data Explorer (2019 wikis only, no source links), and links every result back to its original page.","stack":["desktop application (offline)","full-text indexing","web crawler with JavaScript rendering","Universal Design for Learning (UDL) framework","STEAM curriculum design","Bloom's Taxonomy"],"techniques":["cyanophycin nitrogen-storage pathway engineering","flocculation-protein inducible circuit","split biosensor","nitrate sequestration assays","AquaFlock hardware settling system"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"UOregon's central design principle is quotable and transferable: 'Accessibility should lower the barrier of entry into complex science, not the complexity of the science itself.' Instead of one generic outreach talk, the team wrote three original curricula for three audiences with increasing prior knowledge and ran them as three DBTL iterations across June, July and October 2026, carrying the lessons of each into the next. Workshop 1 ('Intro to Synthetic Biology') was a one-hour session plus an illustrated take-home worksheet delivered to 30 high school students at the Oregon Humane Society summer camp; students reviewed the central dogma and plasmid parts and then designed their own engineered bacterium to solve a problem of their choosing. Post-event debriefs with students and camp staff produced four concrete fixes (establish prior knowledge first, add more real-world relevance, shorten transitions to reduce cognitive load, create more peer-interaction points), which were fed directly into Workshop 2. Workshop 2 ('Engineering SynBio Solutions') was a two-hour session for 20 high school students at SAIL (Student Academy to Inspire Learning), an existing University of Oregon STEM summer camp for students from diverse learning backgrounds; materials were a fully illustrated workbook, a hand-drawn comic that told the central dogma as a story, slides, and hands-on activities, with each of five sections framed by its own guiding question and sequenced to scaffold from foundational biology to CRISPR and plasmids to genetic circuits to experimental design. Workshop 3 ('SynBio Solutions to Real-World Problems') was a two-hour session for 20 first- and second-year undergraduate science students at the University of Oregon, funded by a University of Oregon Knight Campus Graduate Student Association outreach mini-grant; because this audience already had foundational biology, the team swapped the comic for a matching-game refresher, added directed evolution and de novo protein design alongside CRISPR and plasmids, and then extended into two sections the earlier workshops could not reach: small-group bioethics analysis of three real-world synthetic biology applications, and a science-communication activity in which students decorated tote bags to explain what they had learned to an audience of their own choosing. Evidence of impact is tied to the standardised rubric rather than headcount alone: engagement, understanding and creativity were tracked across iterations through direct post-workshop feedback forms and indirect observation, Workshop 2 students asked unprompted questions beyond the taught material (one asked whether CRISPR and plasmids could be used at once), and the Workshop 3 survey ranked bioethics and science communication highest. Beyond workshops, the team published a comic illustrating the AquaFlock project's journey and released the iGEM Wiki Search desktop tool specifically to make it easier for future teams to find and build on predecessors' work - an education contribution aimed at the iGEM community itself.","ihp":"Human Practices was framed around rural equity: people living outside major cities receive less financial backing, less testing of wells and waterways, and less proactive healthcare, so the project is designed as a circular chain of agricultural, health and economic support for communities marginalised by municipal systems. The team mapped a four-step implementation path (in the home, then at the agricultural source, then harvest, then reduce/reuse/recycle) and stated the striking design goal that 'our product is made to eventually become obsolete' once nitrate levels fall out of the unsafe range. A field trip to the Metropolitan Wastewater Management Commission regional wastewater treatment facility serving Eugene and Springfield let the team interview plant operators, who confirmed nitrate control (alongside PFAS and silicon) is an unsolved challenge, validating a gap their smaller-scale system could fill. Four UN SDGs were adopted as focal areas: 3 (good health), 4 (quality education), 6 (clean water and sanitation) and 12 (responsible consumption and production).","realWorld":"The intended users are rural Oregon well owners and farmers; harvested flocs become a local fertilizer substitute, localising fertilizer access and cutting nitrate at source. Context established on the description page: roughly half of applied nitrogen fertilizer is taken up by plants and the rest leaches as nitrate, driving eutrophication, harmful algal blooms and hypoxia as well as direct human health risk from contaminated drinking water.","partners":["Oregon Humane Society (summer camp, Workshop 1 venue and audience)","SAIL - Student Academy to Inspire Learning, University of Oregon STEM summer camp (Workshop 2)","University of Oregon Knight Campus Graduate Student Association (outreach mini-grant funding Workshop 3)","Metropolitan Wastewater Management Commission (MWMC) Regional Wastewater Treatment Facility, Eugene-Springfield","Oregon State University STEM Research Center (Reflective Theory of Change and success-measurement frameworks)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/edu-wksp1-main-photo.avif","caption":"Workshop 1: one-hour intro to synthetic biology for 30 high school students at the Oregon Humane Society camp","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/edu-wksp2-main-photo.avif","caption":"Workshop 2 at the SAIL STEM summer camp: 20 high school students working through the illustrated workbook and comic","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/edu-wksp1-wkst.avif","caption":"The hand-illustrated take-home worksheet from Workshop 1, covering the central dogma and plasmid parts","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/wksp3-1.avif","caption":"Workshop 3 for 20 undergraduate science students, adding bioethics discussion and a tote-bag science-communication activity","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/measuring-success.avif","caption":"The team's standardised measure of success across workshop iterations: engagement, science understanding and creativity","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Run outreach as a DBTL cycle with a fixed success rubric: build three differentiated versions of the same curriculum for audiences at different prior-knowledge levels, debrief with the host staff after each delivery, and score every iteration on the same three criteria (engagement, understanding, creativity) so improvement is demonstrable rather than asserted.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/field-trip","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/sustainability"],"rank":7},{"team":"Vilnius-Lithuania","id":"6043","section":"Undergrad","country":"Lithuania","village":"Foundational Advance","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/","projectTitle":"BMClick: One Shell to Click Them All","tagline":"Bacterial microcompartment shells rebuilt outside the cell and made programmable by click chemistry, plus Ladder Detector, which annotates a gel in about a minute.","summary":"BMClick rebuilds bacterial microcompartments (BMCs), the proteinaceous shells bacteria use to sequester metabolic reactions, in vitro and makes their surface programmable through click chemistry at an amber-codon-incorporated non-canonical amino acid, turning a natural containment system into a modular platform for working with purified biomolecules. The aim is to recover the organisation a cell provides, protecting therapeutic nucleic acids from nucleases, separating the steps of a reconstructed pathway, and extending the temperature and pH range over which enzymes work, while keeping the precision of in vitro conditions. The project also produced functional macroscopic materials from BMC-T shell proteins, and in vitro encapsulation was demonstrated with a HiBiT-SpyCatcher cargo and confirmed by cryo-EM. The dry-lab deliverable is Ladder Detector, a React + FastAPI web app that segments bands in a gel photograph using GelGenie's released U-Net models and then identifies which commercial ladder was run in the marker lane, a step no existing tool automated.","awards":["Best New Basic Part — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Software — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"React web client plus a Python FastAPI server exposing a REST API documented as OpenAPI 3.1 (Ladder Detector API 0.1.0, Apache 2.0), with five endpoints split into a Segmentation group (GET /segmentation-models, POST /segment-image, POST /extract-band-details) and a Ladder group (GET /markers, POST /detect-ladder). There is deliberately no database layer: the application collects and stores no user data and keeps all configuration in files on the server. Deployment is Docker Compose with three services (frontend on 5173, backend on 8000, interactive API docs at /docs), and the segmentation models are pulled at build time from the GelGenie model registry on Hugging Face, so a HF access token is a prerequisite alongside Git and Docker.","dataviz":"The core output is the annotated gel image itself: upload a photograph, optionally crop or rotate, mark the marker lanes, and the app detects bands and writes the size labels on. Accepted input is PNG, JPEG, WebP and TIFF; the annotated result comes back as PNG. Where automation is imperfect the user gets real editing tools rather than a take-it-or-leave-it answer: a brush, an eraser and a magic wand for refining band detection, plus the ability to override the identified ladder or edit assigned values directly. A project can be saved and reloaded as a .gelproj file carrying the band segmentation and lane markup, and frontend and backend exchange JSON.","datascience":"The ladder-identification evaluation is the strongest part of the work. The team contacted Thermo Fisher Scientific for the band patterns of its DNA, RNA and protein ladders, scoped to six ladders their own lab uses, and then built a dataset of 172 marker-lane samples by combining their own gels, figures from published articles, and donated photographs solicited from the Vilnius University Life Sciences Center community and the organisers of the International Biology Olympiad. Because the algorithm is not machine learning but still has tunable parameters, they split 30% for parameter tuning and 70% for evaluation, used K-fold cross-validation within the tuning set, manually grouped samples that came from the same source or the same image so related samples could not leak across the split, and stratified by ladder. Four combinations were then tested on the held-out set: Dynamic Time Warping reached 52.42% (absolute run distance) and 62.90% (relative), while adjusted Needleman-Wunsch reached 89.52% and 91.13%. They published per-class tables, stated the two relevant baselines explicitly (about 17% for guessing one of six, about 26% for always guessing the most common class), and diagnosed their own failure case: Protein_6 was consistently confused with DNA_14 because both ladders have the same number of bands, so the gap penalties that normally separate ladders of different band counts provide no signal and only band positions remain. Relative run distance was chosen over absolute despite similar overall accuracy, because it handled Protein_6 better (61.9% vs 47.62%) and is the simpler of the two. Measured end-to-end annotation time is about one minute or less.","software":"Ladder Detector solves a real and unglamorous problem: gel annotation is done by hand, is repetitive and inconsistent, and the team found that although several gel-analysis tools exist, none automated the annotation step, even though almost every published gel figure is annotated. The pipeline has two stages. Band segmentation reuses rather than reinvents: GelGenie (Aquilina et al., Nature Communications 2025) showed that a lightweight U-Net and nnU-Net clearly beat classical Watershed and Multi-Otsu approaches, so Ladder Detector runs GelGenie's released models, defaulting to the Universal lightweight U-Net (seconds on an ordinary CPU) with nnU-Net and a sharp-band fine-tune selectable. Ladder identification is the team's own contribution, and the design decision behind it is instructive: their first idea was an image classifier over marker-lane crops, which they dropped after a consultation on two grounds, that they could not gather enough samples to train it reliably and that an image classifier would likely latch onto resolution, lighting or gel provenance instead of the band positions that actually distinguish ladders. They moved to numerical data instead, extracting band centroids from the segmentation mask and deriving absolute run distance (distance from the top of the lane) and relative run distance (normalised by lane height). The absolute approach exploits the empirical near-linearity of migration distance against log10(size): for each ladder in the registry, both sequences are min-max normalised, the log sizes inverted, aligned with an adjusted Needleman-Wunsch, a least-squares line fitted to the matched pairs, and the ladder scored by R-squared minus a penalty per unmatched band. The relative approach compares against per-ladder reference profiles taken from vendor charts and scores 1 - RMSE with the same unmatched-band penalty. The process flow deliberately keeps two human checkpoints, segmentation review and marker-lane selection, and the wiki explains why: segmentation is not always accurate, and when a gel has several marker lanes the app has no reliable way to tell which holds the ladder. The API exists specifically so the tool can become one step in an automated pipeline. It was deployed as a public web client at https://ladder-detector.onrender.com, which is the right instinct, but as of checking the service returns 'This service has been suspended by its owner', so the only working route today is self-hosting: 'git clone https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/vilnius-lithuania/ladder-detector.git', add a Hugging Face token to backend/.env, 'docker compose up --build'. Honest limitations are listed: only six ladders supported because of data availability, segmentation imperfect enough to need manual correction, and the classical identification approach possibly improvable by a trained model given a larger dataset. Licence Apache 2.0; it is described as a prototype.","stack":["React","Python","FastAPI","REST / OpenAPI 3.1","Docker + Docker Compose","Hugging Face model registry","GelGenie U-Net and nnU-Net models","PyTorch (via GelGenie models)","JSON","Render (deployment, now suspended)","Apache 2.0","iGEM GitLab"],"techniques":["U-Net / nnU-Net semantic segmentation of gel bands","band centroid extraction per lane","adjusted Needleman-Wunsch sequence alignment","Dynamic Time Warping (evaluated and rejected)","log10 size vs migration distance least-squares fitting with R-squared scoring","min-max normalisation for scale-invariant matching","RMSE scoring against vendor reference profiles","gap-penalty scoring for unmatched bands","grouped and stratified train/test splitting to prevent leakage","K-fold cross-validation for parameter tuning","amber-codon non-canonical amino acid incorporation and click chemistry (wet lab)","cryo-EM confirmation of in vitro shell assembly"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was rebuilt from scratch after a stakeholder told them their plan was wrong. The team had scouted a venue, contacted student organisations and planned to book a music band for one large science festival; Ieva Lingytė, a past team lead, warned them that a bigger event does not mean a bigger audience and that they should go to people rather than expect people to come to them. They scrapped it. The replacement framework borrows Ray Oldenburg's 'third place' sociology: home is the first place, university and formal events the second, and informal public spaces where people gather by choice (cafés, museums, clubs, shelters) the third, where status matters little and conversation is the main activity. Every event was then placed inside a community that already existed, with the format kept flat and walk-away-able for someone with no biology background who had not planned to meet science that evening: genetics discussed through the animals in the room at a cat shelter, fungi taught as a painting workshop ('Science Paints') at the MO Museum, and slots joined at the 'BŪTENT!' discussion festival and the VU Alumni Festival where the audience already existed. They iterated on format too, shortening and sharpening the questions for the second 'Science Talks' after the first showed people wanted more direct ones, and the discussion festival surfaced recognising fake science marketing as the topic people were most misinformed about. Separate consultations with Uršulė Barkauskaitė and Austėja Klevaitė specifically covered adapting activities for older adults, a group the team had no resources to involve meaningfully before.","ihp":"More than 30 stakeholder meetings over 9 months, structured as open dialogue aimed at constructive criticism rather than presentation, with each insight systematically evaluated afterwards and failing approaches dropped. They named the loop the CLICK cycle (Consider, Listen, Implement, Check, Know your limitations) and published five values including Critical Reflection, which they define as examining their own baseline assumptions and being honest about limitations given that parts of the platform could have therapeutic value. Advice is mapped on an interactive timeline where every meeting records Description, Insights, Integration and Next steps, with three explicit revision points. Concrete redirections: alumni from the 2023 'Exullose' and 2025 'iXPOSE' teams set a realistic nine-month scope; the Kerfeld Laboratory advised on BMC assembly and protein modification; Prof. Rolandas Meškys shaped turning in vitro assembling BMC proteins into a modular platform; Dr. Yali Wang confirmed BMC-T functional material formation was possible after the team's own tests had repeatedly failed; critique from Ieva Lingytė (Biomatter Designs) and Dr. Juozas Nainys (Atrandi Biosciences) expanded a narrow thermostability aim into pH resistance testing and pushed them to find more creative applications, which produced the three-branch toolkit; and a consultation with Augustas Rinkevičius is recorded as 'Rethinking our ladder detection', the meeting that moved the software off an image classifier onto numerical band-distance matching.","realWorld":"Commercial viability was reviewed with industry rather than assumed: Ieva Lingytė at Biomatter Designs and Dr. Juozas Nainys at Atrandi Biosciences reviewed and reprioritised the three project paths, Dr. Ignas Galminas took a pitch on the platform's commercial potential, Eglė Radzevičienė advised on intellectual property and industry realities, and the Vilnius University Innovation department ran a market analysis in October. Therapeutic plausibility of BMC shells as drug-delivery nanocarriers was checked with Prof. Aurelija Žvirblienė rather than claimed. The team also presented at the Nordic iGEM Conference.","partners":["Kerfeld Laboratory","Thermo Fisher Scientific (ladder band-pattern data)","Vilnius University Life Sciences Center community (gel image donations)","International Biology Olympiad organisers (gel image donations)","Biomatter Designs","Atrandi Biosciences","Promega","Vilnius University Innovation department","MO Museum","BŪTENT! discussion festival","iGEM Vilnius-Lithuania alumni (Exullose 2023, iXPOSE 2025)","Caszyme, Seqvision, Thorlabs, Noatech, SnapGene (sponsors)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6043/wiki/software-images/original-photo-vs-result-2.avif","caption":"The original gel electrophoresis photograph next to Ladder Detector's automatically annotated result.","credit":"Team Vilnius-Lithuania, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6043/wiki/software-images/tech-stack.avif","caption":"Ladder Detector's technology stack: a React web client over a Python FastAPI server, with no database layer because no user data is stored.","credit":"Team Vilnius-Lithuania, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6043/wiki/software-images/ladder-detector-final-process-flow.avif","caption":"The process flow, showing which steps the application performs and which two verification checkpoints are deliberately left to the user.","credit":"Team Vilnius-Lithuania, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6043/wiki/software-images/metric-explanation-dark.avif","caption":"The two metrics derived from band centroids: absolute run distance from the top of the lane, and the same value normalised by marker-lane height.","credit":"Team Vilnius-Lithuania, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Build on the published model rather than training your own, and say why: Ladder Detector takes GelGenie's peer-reviewed U-Net weights for segmentation and spends its own effort on the one step nobody had automated, then rejects an image classifier on the explicit grounds that it would learn lighting and resolution instead of band positions. The transferable practice is their evaluation hygiene, a grouped and stratified split that stops samples from the same gel image leaking between tuning and test, stated random and majority-class baselines, per-class accuracy tables, and a published diagnosis of the one class the method fails on.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/","https://2026.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/vilnius-lithuania/education","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/vilnius-lithuania/ladder-detector","https://ladder-detector.onrender.com/"],"rank":21},{"team":"HKSSC","id":"6053","section":"High School","country":"Hong Kong","village":"Food & Nutrition","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/","projectTitle":"Engineering E. coli to express prbA, a paraben-degrading esterase, as a pre-treatment for food waste recycling","tagline":"If preservatives are designed to stop microbes, can we design microbes to stop preservatives?","summary":"HKSSC (St. Stephen's College, Hong Kong) noticed a bottleneck nobody was talking about: Hong Kong's O-PARK organic resources recovery centre runs anaerobic digestion on food waste, but the chemical preservatives already in that waste inhibit the very microbes doing the digesting. They cloned prbA, a paraben esterase from Enterobacter cloacae that hydrolyses parabens to 4-hydroxybenzoic acid and needs no complex cofactors, into pET22b+ under a T7 promoter with a pelB signal peptide directing it to the periplasm, then characterised it with CFU-based MIC assays, a pNPA esterase assay and HPLC. They won Best New Basic Part at high school level largely on the strength of the prbA part family - including the previously unregistered native promoter and RBS, which they located in silico with BPROM and then built into a promoterless GFP reporter construct. The dry lab and statistics are the standout: structure-guided mutant design in HotSpot Wizard on an AlphaFold model, and a Michaelis-Menten kinetic analysis defended with two-way ANOVA, a Brown-Forsythe Levene variance check and Welch-Satterthwaite contrasts with Holm correction.","awards":["Top 10 (High School)","Best New Basic Part — Winner (High School)","Best Education — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Nominee (Agriculture / Food & Nutrition / Fashion & Cosmetics / Art & Design)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three distinct computational strands. (1) Structure and protein engineering: the prbA structure was taken from AlphaFold and visualised in UCSF Chimera, which revealed a long flexible N-terminal tail consistent with the 31-residue signal peptide and justified the Cycle 2 switch to pelB periplasmic targeting. HotSpot Wizard 3.1 (Loschmidt Laboratories) was then used for a full structure-guided mutagenesis workflow: stability hotspots were ranked by B-factor and relative accessible surface area (ASA), buried residues filtered out, and three candidates triaged - A198 (50% ASA, highest flexibility) rejected because the Correlated Positions tab showed it needs a compensating mutation elsewhere; P329 rejected because only one amino acid occurs at that homologous position; N240 (34% ASA) selected, with Amino Acid Frequencies showing histidine as the commonest homolog residue and Mutational Landscape giving Asp/His/Ser an 88% probability of preserving function. They used the Design Library function with a minimal frequency threshold of 10% to generate degenerate codons for saturation mutagenesis at N240 and back-translated an E. coli K12-optimised mutant sequence. A second, separate hotspot run targeted catalytic efficiency rather than stability, prioritising A221 and A140 (mutability score 6, both in the catalytic pocket and substrate access tunnel) and designing an RCY degenerate codon library giving 50% Ala / 50% Thr with ~94% chance of seeing both variants in 5 colonies. (2) Molecular docking: methyl-paraben (PubChem CID 117065203) was docked across the whole prbA structure using HotSpot Wizard's docking module and visualised in Chimera to confirm N240 is not in the ligand-binding region - and they honestly flagged that histidine ionises near pH 6, so the mutation could backfire. Earlier docking with CB-Dock2 in PyMOL identified the catalytic serines. (3) Promoter prediction: BPROM was run on the 247 bp upstream region of prbA, predicting a sigma-70 promoter with an LDF score of 5.30 (threshold 0.20), a consensus -10 box TATAAT at position 163 (score 68), a near-consensus -35 box TTGAAA at 142 (score 23), 21 bp spacing and TSS at 178. (4) Enzyme kinetics: a Michaelis-Menten model fitted to pNPA assay data, plus a 35-page mathematical modelling report, with Monod kinetics recommended by Professor Patrick Lee for future digester-scale work.","dataviz":"The wiki carries a 35-page mathematical modelling PDF embedded inline for the kinetics section. More interesting is how they made the data defensible rather than merely pretty: initial velocity versus substrate concentration plotted for all four enzyme conditions (1 and 10 ug/mL porcine liver esterase control, 1 and 8 ug/mL recombinant prbA), Michaelis-Menten fits overlaid, and the statistical validation laid out as readable tables with the F statistics, degrees of freedom and p-values spelled out and each test explained in plain language for a non-statistician reader. Every chart in results is captioned with a complete alt text describing the plate layout or chromatogram, and both HPLC calibration chromatograms and time-course chromatograms (T0, T1, T2, T4) are shown rather than just a summary number.","datascience":"This is the most statistically disciplined high school dataset of the five teams reviewed. Because they could only measure enzyme input by total powder weight rather than molar concentration, they refused to report kcat/Km and instead defined a mass-normalised catalytic capacity metric Q = Vmax / (Cmass x Km), valid only in the low-substrate regime (S << Km), with units cancelling to L g-1 min-1. Recombinant prbA at 1 ug/mL scored Q = 9.518 L g-1 min-1 versus Q = 4.545 for commercial porcine liver esterase at the same mass concentration - a 2.09-fold advantage. They then proved the difference was not pipetting noise: a two-way ANOVA across 4 enzyme conditions x 7 non-zero substrate concentrations gave enzyme condition F(3,64) = 52.530, p = 3.01e-17; substrate F(6,64) = 92.111, p = 1.49e-29; interaction F(18,64) = 21.012, p = 3.15e-20. A Brown-Forsythe Levene test (median-based, chosen over mean-based Levene precisely because outliers drag the mean) returned p = 0.01898, rejecting equal variances - so rather than ignore it they switched to Welch-Satterthwaite contrasts with Holm correction for multiple comparisons, confirming significance at 0.25 mM (p = 0.03452), 0.20 mM (p = 0.04225) and 0.10 mM (p = 0.00408). Separately, they chose CFU plate counts over OD600 for MIC determination because OD600 is confounded by precipitation and dead cells, and built an OD600-to-CFU/mL standard curve for proper per-cell normalisation. MIC was bracketed empirically: 400-500 mg/L methyl-paraben for wild-type BL21(DE3), 1000-1500 mg/L for the Cycle 1 prbA-idt construct, and 500-600 mg/L for the Cycle 2 pelB construct - and they published the awkward finding that Cycle 2 was worse than Cycle 1, diagnosed it from faint SDS-PAGE bands as a protein-isolation methodology problem, and opened Cycle 3 with 6xHis-tag IMAC purification to fix it.","software":"Two released pieces of software, both genuinely built rather than mocked up. FoodSafe is a native iOS app in Swift (source at https://github.com/judeliucodex/Foodsave, initial scaffold AI-assisted then refined by the software team) with two features: a 60-second swipe-left/swipe-right food-sorting game (100 points per correct call plus combo bonuses, backed by a food library for post-game review) and an AI-assisted food safety checker that sends a photo to DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash's vision model and returns a danger-meter rating with the observations behind the judgement. They are explicit about the limits - a photograph cannot establish safety, pathogens can be invisible, and the meter is an AI estimate, not a measurement - and about distribution, noting it is on TestFlight for invited testers rather than the App Store. Microbe Wars is a browser-based 2D arena game written entirely in Python by the team and hosted at https://igem-game.vercel.app, where the player fights paraben enemies as prbA using weapons like an enzyme pipette, with campaign/endless/practice modes, four difficulty tiers and a levelling system. On the parts side they contributed BBa_26SZRXOY (prbA CDS), BBa_264E443U and BBa_26XQ4J7K (stop/start-codon-free variants), BBa_262HI0IB (native promoter + RBS, the Best New Basic Part), composite parts BBa_26SU8KOK (pelB + prbA) and BBa_26MSEOFB (native promoter/RBS + GFPuv), colony PCR and Gibson primers, and a curated 'PrbA's Family' part collection (UUID 9f037d92-78bb-470b-b139-c4ce28d4cc86).","stack":["AlphaFold","UCSF Chimera","HotSpot Wizard 3.1","CB-Dock2","PyMOL","BPROM","PubChem","Michaelis-Menten kinetics","two-way ANOVA","Brown-Forsythe Levene test","Welch-Satterthwaite contrasts with Holm correction","Swift / iOS / TestFlight","DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash vision API","Python","Vercel","GitHub","HPLC","pNPA spectrophotometric esterase assay"],"techniques":["structure-guided saturation mutagenesis site triage (ASA, B-factor, correlated positions, mutational landscape)","degenerate codon library design","whole-surface molecular docking to rule out interference with the active site","in silico sigma-70 promoter prediction then promoter-trap GFP validation","CFU-based MIC rather than OD600, with an OD600-to-CFU standard curve","mass-normalised catalytic capacity metric (Q) when molar data is unavailable","variance-homogeneity diagnostics driving the choice of post-hoc test","multiple-comparison correction","publishing a negative/contradictory cycle result and diagnosing it"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education ran under a framework they called LITTLE GEM (Gauge, Engage, Modify), reaching ages 3 to 101 across one kindergarten (Pods Kindergarten, Wan Chai), two primary schools (SSCPS and Shap Pat Heung Rural Committee Kung Yik She Primary School), a senior centre run by Suicide Prevention Services, their own school, and partner high school teams. Formats were built per audience rather than recycled: a DNA rainbow coin-toss game booth at a public carnival that engaged over 170 participants in three hours; a 'Find a Match' food-waste card game and DNA clay-modelling for 3-6 year olds; strawberry and banana DNA extraction for primary students; a Kahoot quiz; a live beta test of the FoodSafe app; and an original comic book, 'The Tragedy of Margaret in Food Waste', which they also staged as a drama skit with Margaret the frog as the lead. For the elderly they ran a physical food-waste card-matching game and clay craft. Internally, the HKSSC iGEM Club ran 8 after-school workshops over 7 months for ages 11-16 (4 lab, 3 IHP, 1 software) covering DNA, pipetting and serial dilution with P200 and P10 micropipettes across 28 participants, gel electrophoresis, and DNA keychain making, with Wayground quizzes for review - a deliberate pipeline for future team members. They measured learning rather than attendance: at SSCPS, 0% of 20 students knew what iGEM was and 10% knew synthetic biology before the workshop, versus 100% able to state iGEM's core goal and define synthetic biology afterwards, with 100% successfully extracting visible DNA; at KYS the post-presentation Kahoot returned 90% (18/20) accuracy. They also moved from one-off baseline surveys to paired pre/post questionnaires after realising the carnival survey only measured perception, not learning gain.","ihp":"IHP ran on a six-step cycle of their own design, the iCycle (Identify, Initiate, Interact, Insight, Improve, and a sixth step, Inspire), with the deliberate twist that Improve feeds back into Interact so broad stakeholders can be revisited. Stakeholders were sorted into four functions - Information, Implementation, Safety & Technical, and iGEMer feedback - and they covered the whole disposal-to-treatment pipeline: public and restaurants (disposal), EPD (collection), O-PARK (treatment). The design changes are specific. CityU Professor Patrick Lee told them flatly that engineered microbes cannot legally be placed in collection bins in Hong Kong and must be used in a controlled environment with a kill switch, which reshaped their entire safety and implementation framing; he also said demonstrating paraben loss is stronger evidence than showing translation and told them to use both HPLC and the pNPA assay alongside Monod kinetics - which is exactly what the results page then does. HKU advised them they were over-engineering enzyme optimisation, that expressing the protein was enough to demonstrate the concept, that they should focus on the bacteria rather than the enzyme, and that instead of immediately adding a kill switch they should compare growth rates of wild-type versus transgenic E. coli and determine MIC by growing bacteria across paraben concentrations - which became the project's central experimental design. The Environmental Protection Department replied by institutional email with real operating data: food waste pH is 4-6 and varies with contents, impurities are removed and the waste slurried with process water, and the slurry stabilises in a buffer tank for 2-3 days before digestion. HKSSC acted on both constraints, testing their microbes under different pH conditions (which is also why pH stability became the target of the HotSpot Wizard work) and adding bin-contamination messaging to their education workshops. The HKUST Black Soldier Fly Bin team confirmed preservatives are a real but under-documented problem, endorsed pre-treatment in the bin before food reaches O-PARK, and pointed out HKSSC had not considered how to feed their E. coli - advising a layer of food replaced weekly. The KYS student survey revealed a misconception that catering, not households, produces most food waste, and that students knew about the problem but not the solutions, so the team added Smart Food Waste Bin content to the comic and to later activities. The elderly survey showed forgetting purchased food was a key driver of waste, which generated a planned 'food record' fridge-inventory reminder feature for the FoodSafe app.","realWorld":"The intended deployment is a pre-treatment step dropped into infrastructure that already exists - the EPD's smart food-waste bins in public rental housing estates and the front end of O-PARK's anaerobic digestion line - rather than a new facility, and the team repeatedly checks the design against real operating constraints (pH 4-6, impurities, 2-3 day buffer-tank residence, and the legal bar on releasing GMOs into public bins). They frame the contribution as a reusable framework rather than a single product: parabens are one preservative class, and the enzymatic pre-treatment approach is pitched as adaptable to other persistent inhibitors and micropollutants. Impact is mapped to SDG 4 (target 4.7), SDG 7 (target 7.2, higher biomethane yield from more complete digestion), SDG 11, SDG 12 (targets 12.4 and 12.5) and SDG 13. There is no formal business model, revenue forecast or regulatory filing plan on the wiki, and FoodSafe deliberately stops short of public App Store release on cost and effort grounds - both stated plainly rather than overclaimed.","partners":["Hong Kong Environmental Protection Department (EPD), Organic Waste Infrastructure group","O-PARK (Organic Resources Recovery Centre)","Professor Patrick Lee, City University of Hong Kong","The University of Hong Kong (enzyme characterisation and biosafety consultation)","HKUST Black Soldier Fly Bin team","Yataki Japanese Restaurant","Suicide Prevention Services (carnival booth and Elderly Community Centre)","Pods Kindergarten","St. Stephen's College Preparatory School (SSCPS)","Shap Pat Heung Rural Committee Kung Yik She Primary School (KYS)","HongKong-PuiChing iGEM team","Asia-Pacific iGEMer Community (APiC) and Greater Bay Area SynBio Alliance (iGBA)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6053/wiki/modelling/image1.avif","caption":"The prbA paraben esterase structure retrieved from AlphaFold and visualised in UCSF Chimera; the long flexible N-terminal tail matches the 31-residue signal peptide and justified switching to pelB periplasmic targeting.","credit":"Team HKSSC, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6053/wiki/results/image20.avif","caption":"Initial velocity versus pNPA substrate concentration for all four enzyme conditions, fitted to Michaelis-Menten kinetics - the data behind the mass-normalised catalytic capacity metric Q showing recombinant prbA outperforming commercial liver esterase 2.09-fold at equal mass.","credit":"Team HKSSC, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6053/wiki/software/image14.avif","caption":"FoodSafe's AI-assisted food safety checker: a photographed apple rated 'Totally Fine' by DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash's vision model, with the supporting observations shown so users can inspect the reasoning rather than just the verdict.","credit":"Team HKSSC, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6053/wiki/software/image10.avif","caption":"Microbe Wars campaign gameplay - a browser-based 2D arena game written in Python by the team, in which players fight paraben enemies as prbA.","credit":"Team HKSSC, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"The copyable thing here is statistical honesty as a competitive advantage: HKSSC could not measure molar enzyme concentration, so instead of quietly reporting an invalid kcat/Km they invented and justified a mass-normalised metric (Q) valid only below Km, then ran a two-way ANOVA, discovered unequal variances with a median-based Brown-Forsythe test, and switched to Welch-Satterthwaite contrasts with Holm correction rather than ignoring the violation. Pair that with their habit of publishing the cycle that went backwards (Cycle 2's MIC was worse than Cycle 1's) and diagnosing why - a high-school-plus centre will gain more credibility from that than from any extra experiment.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/hkssc/contribution","https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6053/wiki/modelling/wiki-modelling-final.pdf","https://github.com/judeliucodex/Foodsave","https://igem-game.vercel.app","https://api.igem.org/v1/teams/6053"],"rank":110},{"team":"SJTU-BioX-Shanghai","id":"6054","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/sjtu-biox-shanghai/","projectTitle":"Dual-Armor","tagline":"An engineered living dressing that breaks open wound biofilm, clears pathogens and supports tissue repair in chronic wounds","summary":"SJTU-BioX-Shanghai built Dual-Armor, an engineered-microbe dressing for chronic wounds, targeting the biofilm that a meta-analysis of 2,666 chronic wounds found in 68% of cases; about 18.6 million people develop diabetic foot ulcers each year and roughly 65% recur within five years. The team's inclusivity work is the most systematically theorised of the 2026 cohort: they built a two-layer framework in which four dimensions (VOICE, OPPORTUNITY, ACCESSIBILITY, PARTICIPATION) locate where participation is blocked and a five-step pathway, HEALS (Hear, Examine, Act, Learn, Share), governs how each barrier is answered. Twelve activities are each written up against both layers, and patient input visibly changed the product: Dual-Armor was repositioned from a stand-alone treatment to an auxiliary post-debridement dressing, and patients' fear of live bacteria touching the wound drove the physical-containment hardware design.","awards":["Inclusivity Award — Nominee (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Self-built two-layer inclusivity framework: four dimensions named by the question each asks - VOICE (Can I be heard?), OPPORTUNITY (Can I get in?), ACCESSIBILITY (Can I engage in a way that works for me?), PARTICIPATION (Can I stay and contribute?) - plus HEALS (Hear, Examine, Act, Learn, Share) as the common action pathway every activity follows. Education was mapped onto three learner pathways (students: Understand to Learn to Create; public: Encounter to Understand; peer educators: Share to Adapt to Continue) and grounded in Constructionism and UDL 3.0, applied retrospectively as a mirror rather than imposed in advance. Photovoice was used with a proper citation to Wang and Burris (1997).","dataviz":"Co-designed care journey maps with colour-coded burden stickers marking pain, inconvenience, cost and psychological pressure; a handbook development flow figure; the four-dimension and HEALS framework figures; stakeholder overview of included groups.","datascience":"Paired pre/post questionnaires across activities: six patient questionnaires and six in-depth inpatient interviews with published anonymised results (66.7% had three or more episodes of infection, worsening or recurrence in the previous year; 83.3% described the wound's impact on daily life as large or very large); the Beneath the Wound role-play playtest collected five anonymous questionnaires at a 100% response rate, with mean self-rated understanding of five core concepts rising from 1.8 to 3.52/5, understanding of MRSA from 1.2 to 3.4/5 (the largest gain), inclusivity-experience mean 4.03/5, and 'every role carried real weight' and 'the host favored no position' both 4.4/5; pre/post testing at the Life Science x AI summer camp. The team repeatedly states sample limits rather than overclaiming.","software":"Two open-source tools, both built from a named barrier. LabSafeMom gives pregnant, pregnancy-planning or breastfeeding researchers per-protocol risk guidance: submit a protocol and get a structured risk overview, safety controls and a PPE checklist against a knowledge base of 119 hazard entries built from public occupational-health references including NIOSH. Critically it has two separated modes - Basic runs entirely in the browser against a local deterministic rule set with no generative model, so the same input always yields the same reasoning and every score is traceable to a stated rule and reference; Enhanced adds natural-language protocol analysis only when the user supplies their own API key, and risk scores still come from auditable rules. Every report states it is an aid for discussion with an institutional EHS office and a qualified clinician, never a substitute. LabMate reduces bench friction for new researchers: it ingests protocols as PDF, TXT, Markdown, CSV or pasted text in Chinese or English, generates a preparation checklist, guides steps with smart timers, keeps an exportable lab record (Markdown, text or print), offers a Chinese-English toggle, four themes (Light, Lab, Dark, Sunset) and large-font options, and runs locally and offline with privacy-first handling.","stack":["browser-based deterministic rule engine (LabSafeMom Basic mode)","optional user-supplied-key LLM analysis (Enhanced mode)","offline-first local protocol parser (LabMate)","accessible web front end with dyslexia-font and animation toggles","Photovoice participatory method","paired pre/post survey instruments"],"techniques":["biofilm disruption","pathogen clearance by engineered microbes","naringenin-related mechanisms","physical containment hardware for live-bacteria dressings","post-debridement auxiliary dressing design"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"The framing sentence is the one to steal: 'Open is not enough. A door can stand open while some people never have the chance to reach it.' Under VOICE, the team visited the vascular disease department of Shanghai Hospital of Integrated Traditional Chinese and Western Medicine, collecting six patient questionnaires and six in-depth inpatient interviews plus conversations with attending clinicians and ward care workers, and published the bilingual instrument and the anonymised results as PDFs. Patients' top fears were recurrent infection, healing time, pain and amputation risk; clinicians focused on debridement, exudate management and dressing fit; care workers identified post-discharge dressing changes and disinfection as the weakest link in the care chain. This directly repositioned Dual-Armor as an auxiliary dressing for continuous post-debridement care and drove the physical-containment hardware. A Photovoice study then handed the camera to patients, who chose which objects and scenes represented their treatment - wheelchairs, bedside equipment, nursing supplies - and explained why; for inpatients who could not shoot themselves, the team operated the camera but the subject, the explanation and the consent to display always remained the participant's. The authorised, de-identified photographs with the participants' own accounts became an exhibition at Shanghai Jiao Tong University that drew more than one hundred visits, with faces, wound close-ups, bed numbers and records deliberately excluded. The team's reflection is unusually principled: 'creating an opportunity for expression does not give the researchers ownership of the images', and the quality of an exhibition can never take priority over informed consent. Co-design went further: patients, caregivers and care workers drew the whole chronic-wound care journey with colour-coded stickers for pain, inconvenience, cost and psychological pressure, and caregivers added the hidden labour of accompanying visits and watching for abnormalities. The questions they raised repeatedly ('what do I do when the dressing sticks to the wound?', 'which smell means something is wrong?') became the actual table of contents of a 23-page Home-Care Handbook, which translates professional guidance into red-amber-green warning signals, a six-step dressing-change method and hand-fillable care record tables, deliberately offers an executable alternative after every 'do not do this', and gives separate dressing-change options for urban and primary-care settings. The draft was reviewed by clinicians from burn and reconstructive surgery and from endocrinology, who corrected the iodine and hydrogen peroxide guidance, the timing of diabetic foot care and primary-care dressing substitutes. The handbook is published free in Chinese and English, with printable appendix tables so it works for people without digital devices. The team also wrote Beneath the Wound, a 46-page host-facilitated ethics role-play (plus 10 pages of character cards in five- and six-player sets) in which players take the parts of a 72-year-old diabetic-foot patient, family members, the attending physician and ethics roles, and reach a decision with no standard answer over about two and a half hours; information is deliberately distributed - the physician holds risk data, the ethics member remembers past failures, the family waits for authority, the patient carries fear - so players must persuade each other with incomplete information. The full script and cards are released for other teams. Under OPPORTUNITY, the team ran no-prerequisite synthetic biology lessons at a middle school in Bazhong, Sichuan and a small rural class in Hou'an Village, Taizhou, Zhejiang, chosen precisely because students there rarely meet frontier science, using physical role-play of the central dogma and a 'Building a Cell Factory' design task, requiring no specialised equipment, delivered partly by video lesson in Hou'an, and evaluated with pre- and post-questionnaires; students produced their own posters, plasmid maps and models. These were followed by guided SJTU wet-lab visits to supply the missing middle step between a school lesson and university research, and by sustained mentoring of VGA-Pinion, a first-time US high-school team, from emails and online meetings through to an in-person Shanghai lab visit, with advice on project narrative, model-parameter grounding and Human Practices structure, always framed as ideas rather than decisions made for them. Under ACCESSIBILITY, 'Touch Life, See Hope' was a braille chronic-wound postcard co-creation at SJTU in which participants from several majors learned the braille alphabet, writing board and stylus, then discovered that accessibility is redesign rather than transcription - a tactile reader cannot skim, so messages had to be shortened, care advice separated from decorative text, and card layout built around how a finger moves; the cards carry Dual-Armor characters so sighted and non-sighted readers recognise them, and an 11-page English Braille Postcard Co-Creation Toolkit was released so others can run the session. 'Light Deep in the Lilong' is a sixteen-page bilingual illustrated story about Grandma Li, a 76-year-old retired Chinese-language teacher with an unhealed diabetic foot wound, written for the readers conventional material ignores - above all children in the patient's family - with wound microbes as characters mapped one-to-one onto Dual-Armor's functional modules; a bilingual audio edition is in preparation so reading need not depend on sight, and the story was read live to students in Bazhong. The wiki itself is an accessibility artefact, prompted by a senior student with ADHD who reported reading difficulty on many websites: it uses the Okabe-Ito colourblind-safe palette (#E69F00, #56B4E9, #F0E442, #D55E00, #0072B2), a one-click dyslexia-friendly font toggle, a one-click animation kill switch and a reading progress bar. Under PARTICIPATION the team turned inward to people already in science, building LabSafeMom for pregnant researchers and LabMate for new bench members. Education also produced a Base-Pairing Challenge card game, a diabetes scripted game, 'Dual-Armor Werewolf' (a social-deduction game where E. coli, S. epidermidis, immune cells and S. aureus are the roles and naringenin mechanisms are built into role relationships), a picture book and audiobook, clay cell-model making, a bioethics debate with no preset answer, and a six-step student design task (Problem, Chassis, Function, Safety, Teamwork, Presentation). Finally, the team co-authored a cross-team Education Activity Handbook with other university iGEM teams, taking responsibility for Part 7 on spatialised education and exhibitions with their Urban Orienteering chapter, explicitly so that future iGEMers do not start from scratch.","ihp":"The project began with a team member's own fall injury and the experience of caring for the wound. Patient and caregiver evidence was treated as design input rather than validation: ward findings are documented on the Integrated Human Practices page and traced through to three specific changes (product repositioning to post-debridement auxiliary dressing, physical containment of live bacteria, and the decision to co-write a care handbook with clinical staff). The team draws an explicit principle: patients may not decide gene circuits or material parameters, but they decide what the technology must respond to, and lack of specialist knowledge is never a reason to exclude them from design. Limits are stated rather than hidden - six patients cannot represent everyone, and acceptance expressed in a hypothetical interview must still be verified in real use.","realWorld":"Open, reusable outputs: the 23-page Home-Care Handbook in Chinese and English with printable records; the Beneath the Wound full script, host guide and character cards; the Braille Postcard Co-Creation Toolkit; the bilingual illustrated story and forthcoming audio edition; LabSafeMom and LabMate as live apps with open source; teaching decks for the central dogma, cell factory, bioethics debate and engineered-microorganism design; game cards; and the co-authored cross-team Education Activity Handbook. A beginner's guide for new iGEM teams covering schedule planning, stage tasks and common questions is planned for open release on the wiki.","partners":["Shanghai Hospital of Integrated Traditional Chinese and Western Medicine - vascular disease department (patients, clinicians, ward care workers)","clinicians in burn and reconstructive surgery and endocrinology (handbook review)","Bazhong Middle School, Sichuan","Hou'an Village rural class, Taizhou, Zhejiang","Shanghai Jiao Tong University (exhibition venue, wet lab visits, braille co-creation)","VGA-Pinion (first-time US high-school iGEM team, project AstraEV)","ECNU iGEM","iGEM East China Regional Exchange teams","co-authors of the cross-team Education Activity Handbook","East China Five Universities Science & Art Micro-painting Competition"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6054/wiki/images/inclusivity/braille-1.avif","caption":"'Touch Life, See Hope': writing chronic-wound knowledge onto braille postcards, with an open English toolkit released so others can run it","credit":"Team SJTU-BioX-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6054/wiki/images/inclusivity/act2-exhibition-1.avif","caption":"The de-identified Photovoice exhibition at Shanghai Jiao Tong University, visited by more than one hundred people","credit":"Team SJTU-BioX-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6054/wiki/images/inclusivity/act3-journey-map.avif","caption":"Co-drawn chronic-wound care journey maps with colour-coded burden stickers, which became the handbook's table of contents","credit":"Team SJTU-BioX-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6054/wiki/images/inclusivity/act4-playtest.avif","caption":"First playtest of 'Beneath the Wound', a two-and-a-half-hour ethics role-play about an experimental living therapy","credit":"Team SJTU-BioX-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6054/wiki/images/inclusivity/labsafemom-ui-1.avif","caption":"LabSafeMom: auditable per-protocol hazard guidance for pregnant researchers, with a deterministic browser-only default mode","credit":"Team SJTU-BioX-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6054/wiki/images/inclusivity/bazhong-1.avif","caption":"No-prerequisite synthetic biology lessons at a middle school in Bazhong, Sichuan, using role-play instead of equipment","credit":"Team SJTU-BioX-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Adopt SJTU's two-layer discipline: classify every engagement by which barrier it removes (can I be heard, get in, engage in my format, stay and contribute) and run each one through Hear-Examine-Act-Learn-Share, ending every activity with a downloadable artefact someone else can run - a toolkit, a script, a handbook - and a written statement of what the evidence does not prove.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/sjtu-biox-shanghai/","https://2026.igem.wiki/sjtu-biox-shanghai/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/sjtu-biox-shanghai/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/sjtu-biox-shanghai/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/sjtu-biox-shanghai/description"],"rank":7},{"team":"DTU-Denmark","id":"6060","section":"Overgrad","country":"Denmark","village":"Bioremediation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/","projectTitle":"Enzymatic degradation of wind turbine blade epoxy","tagline":"A chemo-enzymatic route to the resin fraction of decommissioned wind turbine blades, assessed honestly against the alternatives","summary":"DTU engineered laccases (ItL-03 from a spruce bark beetle gut metagenome, CueO variants and homologues) and unspecific peroxygenases (PaDa-I, WamPa, PabUPO-II, MroUPO, Fett) to attack the amine crosslinks of cured epoxy from wind turbine blades, layered on top of a published acid-plus-peroxide solvolysis pretreatment. They used NNBT, a small water-soluble molecule carrying the tertiary amine of the crosslink, as a defined model substrate, and showed by MALDI-TOF that PaDa-I performs N-dealkylation on it with Purpald confirmation of lactaldehyde release. The decisive work is a prospective comparative life-cycle assessment in SimaPro (ReCiPe 2016 Midpoint H) of five end-of-life routes, which found their own enzymatic route is NOT the best option on almost any indicator - and they published that. Seven interviews across DTU Wind, DTU Sustain, DTU Chemistry, SDU and industry, plus documented non-responses from Siemens Gamesa, Orsted and MAKEEN Energy, drove changes to the chemical target, the model substrate, the host organism, the enzyme-ranking criterion and the project's stated goal.","awards":["Best New Basic Part — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Sustainable Development Impact — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Bioremediation Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three parallel enzyme-discovery pipelines agreed after advice from the 2025 team's dry-lab head: an 'easy' track of literature enzymes known to oxidise the relevant bonds, a 'medium' track of homologue mining, and a 'hard' track of rational engineering and generative design. Plus a prospective comparative LCA (SimaPro, ReCiPe 2016 Midpoint H, functional unit of blade mass with cutting/transport/metal separation excluded as identical across routes) and a Kremser-equation model of the countercurrent solvent extraction stage.","dataviz":"An interactive clickable process-flow diagram that zooms from the five-route overview into the enzymatic routes with every parameter on the box (acid molarity, temperature, residence time, enzyme loading in mg/L, number of reuses, recovery fractions); one SDG per plot with burdens above zero and credits below; an interactive stakeholder map where each pin opens the interview, colour-coded by engagement status so non-responses stay visible.","datascience":"Sequence mining of UniProt, Pfam and BLAST rather than metagenomic libraries; multiple sequence alignment to transfer organic-solvent-tolerance mutations from PaDa-I onto GroGu and PabUPO-II; a novel substrate-access-channel discovery pipeline used in place of docking affinity ranking; molecular dynamics; generative enzyme design on the DTU HPC. Every LCA parameter is published with its source, the mismatch between source and use, and a 'weakest link' label (e.g. partition coefficients taken from a 1980 phenol-extraction paper using MIBK rather than ethyl acetate, flagged explicitly).","software":"LCA input data, assumptions and mapping code released open source so other teams can rebuild the model in SimaPro or another tool; lectures, workshops and guides collected on the contribution page.","stack":["SimaPro","Ecoinvent","ReCiPe 2016 Midpoint (H)","Boltz-2","molecular dynamics","BAGEL","Raygun","BLAST / UniProt / Pfam","DTU HPC","Opentrons liquid handler"],"techniques":["molecular docking (deliberately demoted)","substrate channel geometry analysis","multiple sequence alignment mutation transfer","generative protein design","Gibson assembly (laccases)","Golden Gate (UPOs)","96-well colorimetric screening (ABTS, NNBT, NBD, Purpald)","CO-difference spectroscopy for correctly folded heme","MALDI-TOF product identification","prospective LCA with contribution analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was pointed at the specific blind spot the project exposed rather than at synthetic biology in general: visits to international high schools taught the wind sector's end-of-life emissions and the idea of thinking in full sustainability circles early, and a Lifelong Learning workshop took the same low-impact recycling discussion to an audience of working professionals. For the iGEM community they ran a BioBrick workshop, an intellectual-property introduction ('Protecting Your Ideas'), a World Cafe team-to-team roundtable and a podcast, and presented at the Nordic iGEM Conference. State of Green gave them a platform to put the blade-waste problem to people from ministries, academia and the energy sector.","ihp":"DTU's IHP is built as a decision trail, not a visitor log: every interview card carries an explicit 'Implementation' list, and there is a separate section on approaches they chose NOT to pursue. The single biggest change came from Dr Javier (Postdoc, DTU Sustain, one of the few in his group working on resin chemistry rather than glass-fibre recovery) across two interviews on 3 March and 24 June 2026. He posed what the team calls the sharpest experimental question raised in the project - can an enzyme replace the cleaving solvent, acting on a swollen but still crosslinked network? - and told them to start from a well-documented literature pretreatment rather than invent one and layer enzymes on top. The result was a hard reframing: the enzymatic step stopped being a standalone degradation claim and became a complement to solvolysis, with a new explicit objective of testing whether enzymes could substitute for part of the solvent-cleavage step, and a second research line on engineering solvent tolerance into the enzymes. Justine Beauson (Senior Researcher, Composites Manufacturing and Testing, DTU Wind) was the first contact; she confirmed thermoset epoxy is what is coming down now, referred the team to a turbine OEM, and in a 6 October email follow-up explained that the Blade Material Passport is compiled independently of any thinking about disposal and will tell you a blade is epoxy but never the formulation - which is why the team abandoned any attempt to match real blade resin and why 'collaboration between the production and the waste-management industries' became the organising claim on the sustainability page. Jesper Kjaer Jorgensen (Postdoc, Composites, DTU) then supplied fully cured SWANCOR 2519-1A/BS, a commercial non-NDA amine-cured bisphenol-A epoxy, and agreed with the team's hypothesis that the tertiary amine crosslink is the vulnerable point: the team writes plainly that 'without those cubes we would have had either no workable material or a different one', and the target shifted from the epoxy backbone to the amine crosslinks, changing which bonds they went after, how they measured degradation and which products they hoped to recover. Robert (PhD, Eukaryotic Molecular Cell Biology) delivered the hardest reality check - thermoset degradation is heavily researched, the field contains many low-quality papers with missing controls and uncharacterised substrates, and a six-month student project cannot run a metagenomic discovery campaign - and the team's response is laid out item by item: they screened roughly 33 enzymes rather than thousands, worked from public databases rather than metagenomic libraries, used a colorimetric assay rather than a fluorescent reporter, added MALDI-TOF, and had a postdoc rather than students perform the hazardous epoxy pretreatment. Carlos G. Acevedo-Rocha (Senior Researcher, Computational Protein Engineering, BRIGHT) told them not to base findings on docking affinity values because there is no significant correlation with experimental activity, and endorsed their early-stage channel-discovery pipeline: they abandoned the plan to filter homologue candidates on Boltz-2 affinity scores and rebuilt candidate selection around channel geometry plus molecular dynamics. Cecilia Castellvi Domingo (PhD, heterologous expression of UPOs) changed the chassis and the protocol - Komagataella phaffii on an episomal AP17 backbone rather than S. cerevisiae or E. coli, harvest at day 5 instead of day 6 to reduce proteolysis, lower induction temperature, and the CO-difference spectrum assay that gave them a direct measurement of correctly folded heme-containing enzyme (0.019 uM in a six-fold concentrated sample) that activity assays could not. Lykke Margot Ricard (Associate Professor in Sustainable Innovation, SDU, who had mapped Danish blade treatment options for the Danish EPA) asked who the retaker is, and in response every workstream was required to name a recoverable product, the quality it would need and a potential buyer - which is also why the team says the ideal process recovers a product rather than incinerating residues. The European Environment Agency answered that it holds no data on retired blade management, that the WEEE Directive does not cover wind turbines and that no dedicated EU legislation exists, so there is no reporting obligation; the team then marked the landfill share as an unverifiable input in the LCA rather than quoting a number. Most unusually, they publish their failures of access: Siemens Gamesa never replied to email and had no relevant expert at the DSE careers fair, nobody at Orsted could speak to blade end-of-life, MAKEEN Energy did not reply, and the team states outright that the groups with the greatest influence over adoption 'were therefore among the least represented in our discussions, limiting our analysis' - and makes those gaps visible on the stakeholder map, which rates each group by influence over adoption and interest separately, including 'unknown' for potential buyers of recovered compounds because no market has been shown to exist.","realWorld":"The need is a quantified bottleneck rather than a slogan: Danish blade waste currently goes to mixed-waste landfill or is shipped abroad by lorry and ferry, the Danish wind industry's voluntary landfill ban took effect on 1 January 2026, a Danish EPA screening detected bisphenol A in every tank leaching test on blade material, and the EEA confirmed there is no EU legislation or reporting obligation for wind-turbine waste at all pending the WEEE revision in the Circular Economy Act. SDGs are justified target by target with numbers from their own LCA, and the headline finding is one most teams would hide: in the base industrial scale-up scenario (projected to about 2035) the enzymatic route emits 2.1 kg CO2 eq per kg of blade against 1.4 for solvolysis and 0.9 for pyrolysis, adds 0.38 person-equivalents of human carcinogenic toxicity against 0.022 for solvolysis, uses about 36% more water, and cement co-processing is best on almost every indicator - so they state that enzymatic and solvolysis are the only routes with clear net burdens, trace the hotspot to the H2O2 supply chain and UPO production, and convert that into a research direction (make UPO production more efficient, recover a product instead of incinerating residues, reuse immobilised enzyme and compute the break-even number of reuses). SDG targets are matched one-to-one with their own data on SDG 3 (illness from hazardous chemicals), 6 (water-use efficiency), 7 (renewable share and clean-energy research cooperation), 8 (the Energy Transition Alliance estimate that a UK blade-recycling segment could add 20,000 jobs to offshore wind's 60,000-by-2030 target), 9, 12, 13, 14, 15 and 17. Limitations are declared inside the SDG analysis rather than buried: unknown reaction product, no demonstration of enzyme performance on real blade material, one blade type only, transport excluded by the system boundary, microplastic and BPA leaching not characterised by ReCiPe, and no penalty in the cut-off model for losing glass fibre as a resource. What happens next: the LCA inputs, assumptions and mapping code are open source, the OEM's requirements (handle multi-material blades at reasonable cost and pace) are recorded as requirements for future development rather than achievements, and relationships with State of Green, the Center for Biosolutions and DTU are described as collaborations that can continue beyond iGEM.","partners":["DTU Wind and Energy Systems","DTU Sustain","DTU Chemistry","DTU Biotechnology and Biomedicine","DTU Biosustain","University of Southern Denmark (SDU Innovation and Design Engineering)","Biotechnology Research Institute for the Green Transition (BRIGHT)","European Environment Agency","State of Green","Center for Biosolutions","an anonymous wind turbine OEM","Tailwind Bio","Danish Working Environment Authority (Arbejdstilsynet) epoxy certification"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6060/wiki/human-practices/stakeholder-photos/javier.webp","caption":"Javier, postdoc at DTU Sustain working on resin chemistry, whose question about whether an enzyme could replace the cleaving solvent reframed the whole project as a complement to solvolysis","credit":"Team DTU-Denmark, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6060/wiki/human-practices/stakeholder-photos/lykke.avif","caption":"Lykke Margot Ricard (SDU), who mapped Danish blade treatment options for the Danish EPA and made every workstream name a recoverable product and a buyer","credit":"Team DTU-Denmark, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6060/wiki/header-illustrations/engagement-human-practices.avif","caption":"Header illustration for the integrated human practices section, which is organised as an interactive stakeholder map rated by influence over adoption and interest","credit":"Team DTU-Denmark, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6060/wiki/education/photos/sog-1.avif","caption":"At State of Green, where the team put the blade-waste problem to people from ministries, academia and the energy sector","credit":"Team DTU-Denmark, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Run a comparative LCA against the incumbent technologies early enough that it can still change the research plan, and publish it even when it says your own route loses - then convert each losing indicator into a named engineering target, and record the stakeholders who never replied so the gaps in your evidence base are visible rather than invisible.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/","https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/dtu-denmark/software"],"rank":47},{"team":"GEMS-Taiwan","id":"6072","section":"High School","country":"Taiwan","village":"Space, Foundational Advance, Software & AI, Biomanufacturing","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/","projectTitle":"ReLEAF","tagline":"A stress-responsive optogenetic bioreactor that predicts crop stress from field sensors and forecasts, then uses green light to make plant-protective proteins on the farm.","summary":"ReLEAF closes a loop: field sensors and weather forecasts feed a stress-index model, the model sets a green-light dose, green light drives the cyanobacterial CcaSR two-component system in Bacillus subtilis WB800, and the cells secrete ACC deaminase into a hollow-fiber perfusion bioreactor that lets protein out and keeps cells in. The unit is about 40 x 24 x 62 cm, designed to stand beside a Taiwanese smallholder paddy where the average agricultural manager is 63.8 years old and precision agriculture is otherwise out of reach. It swept the high-school technical awards (Measurement, Hardware, Integrated Human Practices) largely on provenance discipline: four self-built devices with release build manuals, a digital twin that caught a failing photometer 26.7 hours before it died, a pre-registered growth forecast scored against a later run, and an input ledger that marks every number as measured, cited or modelled. Its distinguishing quality is honesty — the wiki repeatedly withdraws its own claims, and that is also where its dry-lab weaknesses are visible.","awards":["Top 10 (High School)","Best Measurement — Winner (High School)","Best Hardware — Winner (High School)","Best Presentation — Nominee (High School)","Best Wiki — Nominee (High School)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Winner (High School)","Best Village Project — Nominee (Space / Foundational Advance / Software & AI / Biomanufacturing)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Four strands. (1) A fitted crop-stress index: rather than weighting temperature, water and light by expert judgement, they fitted weights to Taiwan's own harvest record — 25 years of county rice yields from the Agriculture and Food Agency against ERA5 daily reanalysis weather (2001-2025), treating a season below the county trend as a stressed season, and exhaustively scoring all 2^15 - 1 = 32,767 combinations of fifteen candidate stress terms on held-out years. First and second rice crops were fitted separately; the second crop pays for wind, light and badly timed water, and heat adds nothing once those three are in — which the wiki notes undercuts the wet lab's heat programme. County error over 2016-2025 was 365 kg/ha against 419 for a plain trend line (32% error reduction on the second crop). A genuinely prospective test was frozen on 26 September 2026 before any county published; four of sixteen counties reported at 7.9% error against the trend's 8.8%, which the team correctly calls too few to establish performance. (2) Protein design (the GRAFT methodology: Generate, Restraints, Assembly, Flex, Triage) selected and shaped three protectants before any cloning — BoPEP4 (a 23-aa Brassica oleracea plant elicitor peptide docked against the PEPR1 receptor and run as nine all-atom MD trajectories), LEA14 (151-aa late embryogenesis abundant protein, five N-His constructs including a borrowed K-segment membrane-binding motif joined by a GGGGS linker), and ACC deaminase. The standout findings are negative and caught in time: a C-terminal 6xHis purification tag that every earlier check had approved was shown in simulation to cap the chemical group forming the peptide's strongest receptor contact, so the tag moved to the N-terminus; an Asn23 clamp was found to be five contacts rather than one salt bridge; a truncation ladder identified 9-23 as the production length (33% less mass) with the grip holding 98.5% of frames for 7-23 against 77.1% for the next rung down; and an AlphaFold confidence gate set before the result came back was failed for LEA14 with its partner, so by prior agreement no structure was handed on. For ACC deaminase no mutant was ordered at all, because the modelling said the enzyme meets far less substrate than it could handle and the largest available gain is a change to the medium (apoplast pH 5.5, where the Glu12-His227 contact holds in 99.9% of frames). (3) A reactor digital twin: an autoencoder trained on the healthy first 120 hours of a 434-hour run, scoring a sensor axis (the reference beam, which never passes through the culture) separately from a process axis (specific growth rate), so it can distinguish a culture event from a failing instrument; plus a Kalman-filter batch forecaster and a logistic growth prior fitted from the light plate. (4) Dose and fleet models: an arithmetic ledger from millilitres on a petri dish to US dollars per hectare, and a payback curve plotted across the whole efficacy axis.","dataviz":"The wiki's signature device is provenance colour-coding. The M4 dose ledger is drawn as a single top-to-bottom chain from 1.67 mL on a 10 cm plate to US$3800 capital per hectare, with a rail down the left that is green while every row still rests on a measurement and turns amber at the rice canopy correction, so a reader can see exactly where measurement stops and modelling begins; rows below inherit the model and two add assumptions of their own. Every model input is tabulated with a provenance column (seven from their own bench, four public records, two published constants, one from Geospatial Analysis, one neither measured nor cited), and the five assumptions get a table of 'what rests on it' and 'what would overturn it'. A timing diagram overlays the three-day Central Weather Administration forecast horizon, the three arrival arms the plant team ran, and the 5.4-day reactor lead time on a days-from-stress axis to show visually that they do not fit. The three disagreeing timing runs are shown side by side with the third panel re-reading the middle panel's own tubes as total chlorophyll instead of per gram, so the ranking visibly reverses — the figure argues against the team's own earlier claim. Decay figures plot both candidate interpretations through the same 28-hour reading and label the 22-fold divergence ('What one endpoint cannot say'). The payback plot marks their own 68-years-at-efficacy-1 point and Geospatial Analysis's 4.9-year estimate on the same axis and names the disagreement rather than hiding it. The digital twin UI itself puts optical density, pH, four pressure traces and the pump and valve controls on one screen; the description page opens with an interactive seven-block system map and a GIS choropleth of county nighttime temperature with circles scaled by rice yield trend.","datascience":"Three practices lift this above typical high-school dry lab. First, pre-registration: on 1 October, before any WB800 bioreactor run, they registered a specific growth rate of 0.2986 h^-1 with a 90% interval of 0.2705-0.3300, a logistic curve shape with an NRMSE pass threshold of 15%, and inoculum-conditional times to OD 0.5, together with stated pass criteria. Run 6 gave 0.286 +/- 0.010 h^-1 (R2 0.978), inside the interval; the curve-shape endpoint was recorded as not scored because the prior misses the first seven hours by 30% and the run passes the prior's ceiling before hour 7. Run 7 was explicitly excluded from scoring because its three-hour lag has no term in the registered logistic and its fitted rate swings 0.16-0.46 h^-1 with the window choice. Second, anomaly detection with a diagnostic split: the autoencoder flagged the photometer at 216.3 h, 26.7 h before the OD channel failed at 243.0 h, and the window table shows the sensor axis at 100% alarms with the process axis at 0% during the drift — the model names which subsystem is broken, not just that something is. Third, forecast benchmarking against persistence rather than against nothing: the Kalman filter beat a no-change baseline by 41% at 2 h on the September run and by 24% at 8 and 12 h on run 7, with global-linear, exponential and local-linear alternatives reported as losers and the honest caveat that most of the run is above the photometer's linear range so the model forecasts the reading, not biomass. Other measurement work: the in-line photometer tracks a benchtop BioDrop with slope 1.018 (R2 0.995) to OD 1.2 across 45 paired TiO2 points and an iGEM silica protocol run in 2.5 mg steps, with a dilute-and-restore test returning within 0.2%; wall shear computed from pressure sensors and from the flow meter agree to 2.8%, so each sensor checks the other; and leaf-sensor bench characterisation found a +0.47 to +1.71 degC site-dependent offset against the air reference across 540 paired readings, with the conclusion stated that a 1.2-1.9 degC bias can hide or invent the very water-stress signal the twin would act on. Honest dry-lab weaknesses, stated by the team: a secreted-activity figure of 7.25 mU/mL at n = 1 and below quantitation; two induction runs that disagree on the sign of activity change over hours; the published CcaSR Hill curve (Castillo-Hair et al. 2019) assumed valid at 20 L when every induction was run at a single light intensity, so nothing tests it; a rice canopy correction of x2.44 that is modelled, not measured, and spans 1.68-3.52; a minimum effective dose that is unknown because the spray experiment varied salt and timing but never the applied amount; a withdrawn efficacy coefficient of 0.58; and only 17 of 25 modelled seasons ever crossing the trigger, so a ten-unit fleet runs on 4.3% of the calendar.","software":"The digital twin is released MIT-licensed at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/gems-taiwan/digital-twin. It reads live OD600, pH, four pressure channels and turbine flow from the reactor loop, drives a pinch valve and four Luer valves, runs the autoencoder sensor alarm and the Kalman batch forecast, computes lumen pressure drop, transmembrane pressure and a fouling ratio through a hydraulic model, and applies the BLASTAM leaf-blast infection rule (a wet period counts when the leaf stays wet at least 10 hours at a mean of 22-25 degC, no infection below 15 or above 25 degC, past hours from the sensor and future hours estimated as wet at 90% relative humidity) to tell a grower to look for lesions five to ten days later. The team is clear about what is not wired up: green-light induction is tested but not integrated, dissolved-oxygen calibration did not hold, the protectant amount cannot be read in-line and comes from hand-drawn ELISA and DNPH samples, and every model so far has run on recorded data after a run with a person making every decision — so it is honestly a digital shadow moving toward a twin. Four open hardware devices ship with build manuals and print files: the hollow-fiber perfusion bioreactor (10-page assembly guide, 17-page brochure, 16-page user manual), a Pro and a Slim in-line photometer (12- and 13-page guides), and a 24-tube dual-wavelength light plate apparatus (10-page guide), plus a floating hydroponics plate with 13 seed positions, a 6.5 MB hardware notebook covering 23 March to 17 October 2026, and an LLM-assisted QGIS geospatial workflow over 346 farming townships with the data-preparation prompt published. Also contributed: 35 Registry parts in five SubtiToolKit Golden Gate modules, a 15-page plant protocol, a twentyfold scale-down of the ACC deaminase DNPH activity assay onto a 96-well plate, and waste-media protocols (hay, okara and sugarcane extracts benchmarked against LB).","stack":["Python","autoencoder (anomaly detection)","Kalman filter","logistic growth fitting","exhaustive subset selection over 2^15 feature combinations","ERA5 reanalysis","QGIS","LLM-assisted GIS data preparation","AlphaFold (with pre-set confidence gate)","molecular docking","all-atom molecular dynamics","Arduino / ESP32","SHT31 air sensor","SN-3001-YM-N01 leaf sensor","Modbus RTU over RS485","I2C","BLASTAM leaf blast model","Hill function inversion (CcaSR transfer function)","MAD-based outlier filtering","GitLab (iGEM), MIT licence"],"techniques":["pre-registered prediction with stated pass criteria, scored after the fact","provenance ledger marking every input measured / cited / modelled","assumption register with falsification conditions","held-out and prospective (frozen-forecast) validation","forecast benchmarking against a persistence baseline","two-axis autoencoder separating sensor fault from process event","in-line photometer cross-calibration against a benchtop spectrophotometer (TiO2 and SiO2 standards)","dilute-and-restore proportionality test","redundant sensing (wall shear from pressure vs from flow)","anti-His ELISA and DNPH colorimetric activity assay","sensitivity analysis showing the activity term cancels out of the capital result","explicit withdrawal of an unsupported efficacy coefficient","payback plotted across the full efficacy axis instead of a point estimate"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"140 students across 5 schools in tiered lessons for elementary, junior high and high school audiences, with pre- and post-Kahoot surveys per cohort and the results published as per-question accuracy charts — including the failures, such as DNA base pairing where only 30% of students answered correctly. Everything is released on a dedicated education website: three lesson plans, three slide decks, two card games, two online games and a plant stress simulator (CropShield), free to download, with the stated aim of making the lessons work without the team in the room. Hands-on activities included plant stress experiments, planter making, an ATCG card game and vocabulary work. The team also edited its own claims down for honesty — a visible wiki comment records removing a '49-point' improvement claim and a 'standard teaching methods' comparison, and correcting a student count from 144 to 140. Separately, a Taiwan-specific engagement channel: a LINE official account called 'Nong You Zhu Shou' (Farmer's Assistant) with six functions — latest news, product introduction, crop stress alerts, a crop assistant AI chatbot keyed to terms like salinity, heat stress and pests, a Farmer's Diary where users post weather, crop photos, soil moisture and pest conditions and earn tokens redeemable for LINE stickers, and a Google Form feedback loop — built because interviews at the Farmer Expo and with Ms. Chen Hui-Wen showed smallholders could not reach the information that already exists online. A public event at Taipei Water Garden Organic Farmers' Market on 5 September 2026 (Taiwan Coffee x Cocoa Gelato Festival) drew over 400 visitors with over 200 registered participants, running a forum, booth activities including a data-physicalisation installation and a ring toss, and a plant stress event.","ihp":"31 stakeholders over seven months, structured as a project evolution map in four stages with named dates, plus four thematic expert pipelines (deployment, protectant design, plant and stress biology, bioreactor and hardware) and continuous engagement with five core experts. The decisive change came at the very start: the original design released engineered Bacillus subtilis into the soil with a DesK-DesR system reading temperature directly. Prof. Chen Wen-Liang (10 April) and bioprocess experts said two things were wrong — the bacteria could not be contained, and biological sensing was neither precise nor controllable enough — so sensing moved out of the cell into electronic sensors and an AIoT model, and a hollow-fiber perfusion bioreactor was built to hold the bacteria in while letting the protectants out. That one conversation produced the entire architecture. Other changes with a clear before/after: Dr. Paul Verslues (Academia Sinica, 3 April) argued that supplying pure proline is inefficient and risks suppressing the plant's own P5CS1 biosynthesis and proline-redox balance, so they abandoned proline delivery for a plant-regulation framework using bacterial signals and precursors, shifted from stress recovery to early detection, and re-scored outcomes on root growth and biomass. Dr. Elizabeth Sattely (Stanford, 27 June) told them their proof of concept was too broad and to do one stress very well instead of many superficially, and to look at peptides — which is why BoPEP4 entered the panel and why plant testing narrowed to heat and salinity. CH Biotech (9 July) pushed them toward computational screening to optimise protectants, comparison of root irrigation vs foliar vs drone spray, production cost per area, and Taiwanese regulatory research before acting. Prof. Huang Chieh-Chen (NCHU, 28 July and 6 August) moved the reactor from continuous to periodic operation, raised seedling-stage application, warned that excessive protectant concentrations harm plants so thresholds must be validated, and supplied the B. subtilis WB800 strain the project now runs on. Prof. Cheng Mei-Chun (28 August) found that inconsistent plant ages and treatment conditions meant earlier results may have reflected unintended stress rather than treatment, and the protocols were standardised in response. Yes Health iFarm (11 August) prompted reconsidering the 8/16 light cycle for 12/12, adding DO and OD to parameter planning, switching from pure oxygen to gentle aeration, and minimising transplants because transplantation itself is a stressor. Most interesting is the natural-farming thread: at a public forum co-hosted with Green Media on 5 September, Dr. Li Hsu-Ying and Ms. Chen argued that a plant protected from every stress never builds tolerance — 'raising a crop is like raising a child'. Rather than dismiss it, the team made that the specification for M1: the reactor should fire only when a threshold that genuinely reflects crop condition is crossed, which is why the 32% error reduction matters as evidence for a release rule rather than as a number. A farmer at the same forum asked whether the system breaks organic certification; the team researched the regulations and concluded it cannot be used on organic farms, then interviewed Mr. Chen Ke-Wei of the Lanyang River Friendly Farming Production Cooperative to work out what that means commercially. The project's origin question came from Ghanaian farmers via Gilbert Nkpeniyeng of Ubuntu Social Enterprise Ghana (1 February).","realWorld":"Positioned as a social enterprise with the vision 'every farmer is a biomanufacturer', deployed through farmer collectives and pilot cooperatives rather than direct sales, because the fleet analysis shows a reactor owned by a single farm is idle most of the season. The MVP is costed component by component: current bioreactor US$2280, leaf humidity sensor US$125, a drop-in sealed bacteria cartridge US$90 that opens automatically once seated so no one handles an open culture, and a Field Console mobile app; capital works out at about US$3800 per hectare before refills, service or labour. The regulatory target is contained-use answers in Taiwan by October 2027, and the team worked the pathway with Director Wu Tzu-Hsien (12 October), who pointed out their cost model was missing factory, labour, bacterial cultivation and waste-management costs, and with Prof. Chang Hung-Hao (31 August), who told them to narrow the initial application to a specific stress and crop. A conversation with Bioeconomy Corporation at BIO Asia-Taiwan redirected expansion research toward Southeast Asia, where agricultural sectors are large and GMO rules looser than the US or EU. Smart Agriweek Taiwan identified young farmers as the realistic early adopters and flagged that older farmers need a separate persuasion route. SWOT and PESTLE analyses name the real threats plainly: unclear regulation for cell-free agricultural biologics that may fall between biological pesticide and synthetic-biology-derived compound; protectant washing away; biofouling reducing yield; and the fact that only 3.47% of Taiwan's farmland was organic or eco-friendly by 2024, which both bounds and shapes the market. The payback analysis is the most commercially honest thing on the wiki: on their own loss figure of US$138 per hectare per crop, no point on the efficacy axis returns the capital on a useful horizon — 68 years even at perfect efficacy — while Geospatial Analysis reaches 4.9 years from a net benefit of US$1931 per crop, and the team states that the two do not yet price the same hectare rather than quietly adopting the favourable number. Deliverables include an 85-page business plan, a farmer-friendly user manual, and the three bioreactor documents.","partners":["Prof. Chen Wen-Liang (containment and business plan)","Dr. Paul Verslues (Academia Sinica)","Dr. Elizabeth Sattely (Stanford University)","Prof. Drew Endy (Stanford University)","Dr. Jennifer Brophy","Prof. Cheng Mei-Chun","Prof. Huang Chieh-Chen (National Chung Hsing University; supplied B. subtilis WB800)","Prof. Huang Tzu-Pi (National Chung Hsing University)","Prof. Chang Jo-Shu","Prof. Chang Hung-Hao","Dr. Lin Wei-Yi","CH Biotech (Taiwan)","Yes Health iFarm","World Vegetable Center","Green Media (public forum co-host)","Gilbert Nkpeniyeng, Ubuntu Social Enterprise Ghana","Dr. Li Hsu-Ying and Ms. Chen (natural farming)","Mr. Chen Ke-Wei, Lanyang River Friendly Farming Production Cooperative","Ms. Chen Hui-Wen","Director Wu Tzu-Hsien","Mr. Riddler Lu","Taipei Expo Farmers Market","BIO Asia-Taiwan Exhibition","Taiwan SMART Agriweek","Bureau Francais de Taipei","Owl Lab","Geospatial Analysis (partner team)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6072/wiki/software/twin-screen-20261018-software.webp","caption":"The ReLEAF digital twin control screen on 18 October 2026, showing optical density, pH, four pressure traces and the pump and valve controls for a live bioreactor run on one page.","credit":"Team GEMS-Taiwan, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6072/wiki/software/fig-r3-run434-software.svg","caption":"The 434-hour run: optical density above and the photometer's reference channel below, with the autoencoder sensor alarm at 216.3 hours marked 26.7 hours before the OD channel failed.","credit":"Team GEMS-Taiwan, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6072/wiki/model/stress-validation-island-model.webp","caption":"Island first-crop rice yield against predictions made from earlier years only; county error over 2016-2025 is 419 kg/ha for the plain trend and 365 kg/ha for the fitted stress-index model.","credit":"Team GEMS-Taiwan, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6072/wiki/description/bioreactor-front-description.webp","caption":"The ReLEAF hollow-fiber perfusion bioreactor, about 40 x 24 x 62 cm, built to stand beside the field it supplies and to let protectant protein out while keeping the engineered bacteria in.","credit":"Team GEMS-Taiwan, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"The transferable asset is the provenance discipline, and it is cheaper than it looks: tag every number in a model as measured-by-us, cited, or modelled; publish an assumption register that states what would overturn each step; and when two of your own runs disagree, show both and name the disagreement instead of picking the flattering one. Pair that with one pre-registered, date-stamped prediction scored against a later experiment — GEMS-Taiwan's registered growth rate is the single highest-credibility item on its wiki, and it cost nothing but committing in writing before the data arrived.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/measurement/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/hardware/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/protein-design/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/contribution/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gems-taiwan/parts/","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/gems-taiwan/digital-twin"],"rank":169},{"team":"UOttawa","id":"6077","section":"Undergrad","country":"Canada","village":"Bioremediation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/","projectTitle":"Nulfur","tagline":"Break bonds, not the fuel: engineered biodesulfurization as a polishing step for refractory sulfur in diesel.","summary":"Nulfur rebuilds the four-enzyme 4S biodesulfurization pathway (DszA, DszB, DszC plus a flavin reductase) from Rhodococcus erythropolis inside Pseudomonas putida KT2440, targeting dibenzothiophene (DBT) — the refractory sulfur compound that conventional hydrodesulfurization struggles to remove without running at 300-400 C and 3-6 MPa. Rather than replacing hydrodesulfurization, the team reframed the project after industry consultation as a modular side-stream polishing step on diesel. The project won on the strength of its alternative chassis work: because P. putida is a non-model organism, the team built the missing toolkit from scratch (a Golden Gate-compatible broad-host-range vector, a measured four-promoter set, and a working heat-shock transformation protocol) and published it as a handbook for other teams. That platform contribution, paired with an unusually deep dry lab (COBRApy genome-scale modelling, a FoldX/ThermoMPNN protein engineering pipeline, and an agentic AI research platform called Cofactor), won Best Alternative Platform and Best Wiki and Best Bioremediation Project nominations.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Alternative Platform — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Bioremediation Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three distinct computational layers. (1) Genome-scale metabolic modelling: the published P. putida KT2440 model iJN1463 was extended in COBRApy with the heterologous 4S pathway reactions (DszA/B/C/D), and flux balance analysis solved through GLPK over a 1000-point sweep of the DBT exchange flux bound showed metabolism never bottlenecks uptake, while forcing DszB flux revealed an NADH-limited ceiling of 9.80 mmol gDCW-1 h-1 that formate co-feeding raises 25.5% to 12.30. (2) A first-principles mass-transfer model of the biphasic reactor combining Hinze-Kolmogorov droplet-breakup scaling, film theory at the stagnant-sphere limit (Sh = 2), micellar solubilization above the CMC and Michaelis-Menten uptake, solved by bisection for the steady-state free DBT concentration; it predicts rhamnolipid raises effective DBT availability 2.8- to 34-fold. (3) A protein engineering pipeline that screened over 1,100 candidate substitutions in DszB and DszC using ConSurf conservation grades, MD-derived backbone RMSF, and an ensemble of FoldX (empirical force field) plus ThermoMPNN (deep learning on ProteinMPNN embeddings) for ddG, then validated catalytic integrity with explicit-solvent GROMACS MD (amber99sb-ildn, TIP3P) and AutoDock Vina ligand docking before shortlisting five mutants per enzyme.","dataviz":"The wiki is a server-rendered-feel React site with a genuinely engineered visual layer: a WebGL/Three.js distillation-tower scene and animated pathway loop on the landing page, every equation rendered as its own labelled SVG so the derivation reads as a chain, and predictor-agreement scatter plots (Figure 9) that shade the quadrant where FoldX and ThermoMPNN both call a substitution stabilising and enlarge it to label the five mutations actually sent to the bench. The Human Practices page is built as a clickable stacked column where each band is one of seven kinds of conversation across 21 entries, so a judge can jump straight to the stakeholder group they care about. Every figure carries a long descriptive alt attribute that states the figure's actual numbers, not just its subject.","datascience":"The headline data-science contribution is turning the protein engineering loop into a measured, auditable artefact. The team built a six-arm evaluation suite for Cofactor with four ablation regimes (bare, bare_prompt, tools, full) that add exactly one harness layer each, then scored outcomes: 37 deterministic probes in Arms A and B all pass, Arm C recorded zero workflow violations across 27 scorable runs under the phase machine versus two in the bare condition, and Arm D found the ungrounded condition hallucinated amino-acid positions while both grounded conditions did not. Arm E asked the agent to reconstruct the initial experimental designs of six published papers (three postdating the model's knowledge cutoff) and audited tool transcripts for contamination. On the wet-lab side, the promoter library was quantified as log-normalized mScarlet-i fluorescence per OD600 corrected for media background and P. putida autofluorescence, which revealed only three distinct expression tiers rather than the four reported in the literature.","software":"Cofactor, an agentic protein engineering platform released under MIT licence at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uottawa/cofactor. It wraps a general-purpose LLM in an explicit phase state machine whose preconditions are checked against a 16-table SQLite store (.cofactor/research.sqlite3, WAL journaling, foreign keys) rather than against the model's claims, so runs resume across sessions and the research record stays readable without the tool. A deterministic tool layer covers sequence operations, RFC10 compliance checking, codon optimisation, restriction analysis, solubility scanning, and retrieval from UniProt, NCBI, RCSB PDB, AlphaFold, PubMed and Europe PMC; an ExecutionManager submits jobs to local, SSH or Slurm backends. Three frontends ship: a FastAPI web UI, a Textual TUI and a CLI, all bring-your-own-key and model-agnostic against any OpenAI-compatible endpoint. The team also released the P. putida handbook with protocols and the sanitized Golden Gate-ready pBBR1MCS-2 vector to the iGEM Registry.","stack":["COBRApy","GLPK","iJN1463 genome-scale model","FoldX","ThermoMPNN","ProteinMPNN embeddings","ConSurf","GROMACS","amber99sb-ildn / TIP3P","AutoDock Vina","Python 3.11","SQLite (WAL)","FastAPI","Textual TUI","Slurm / SSH job submission","React","Three.js / WebGL","Open Babel"],"techniques":["Flux balance analysis","Genome-scale metabolic model extension","Hinze-Kolmogorov droplet-breakup scaling","Film-theory interfacial mass transfer","Micellar solubilization modelling","Michaelis-Menten uptake kinetics","Bisection root-finding","Sensitivity analysis over droplet size","ddG ensemble prediction","Molecular dynamics (RMSF, native-contact persistence)","Ligand docking","LLM agent harness with phase-gated state machine","Ablation-based evaluation (4 regimes, 6 arms)","Rubric scoring by two independent judges","Techno-economic analysis","Life cycle assessment","HAZOP / P&ID process safety analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education is organised around four stated principles - Access, Adapt, Engage, Extend - and each initiative is written up against one of them. The team ran bilingual (English/French) sessions for 370+ children aged 4-13 in the Ottawa-Carleton District School Board's International and Indigenous Languages Program summer school, reached 70 more through the Ottawa Catholic School Board, and visited AcadeCap International School for ages 4-7. Original hands-on activities include Lava Lamps (oil properties), The Art of Consortia (a collaborative mural standing in for microbial division of labour), Mix and Match Genes, The Power of Bacterial DNA, and a Heavy vs Light Oil worksheet. For durability they published a free interactive children's magazine, Bio-Builder, distributed through local Ottawa public libraries explicitly to reach low-income families who cannot buy such resources, plus a demo list, infographics, a science and research podcast, and a full downloadable resource library of presentations and worksheets so the activities run without the team present. They also hosted the Great Canadian iGEM Incubator poster competition (with QGEM) and the Great Canadian Meetup pitch competition, both with published scoring rubrics.","ihp":"21 conversations across seven categories, each logged in the same three-part structure: why we went, what we heard, what we changed. The changes are concrete. Dr. Jinwen Chen (Natural Resources Canada, CanmetENERGY-Devon) and Dr. Stephen Larter (Calgary, petroleum geochemist) both told the team to abandon heavy diluted bitumen for diesel because of viscosity, mass-transfer and emulsion-separation barriers, and because diesel carries a hard <10 ppm sulfur limit and concentrated refractory DBTs - the team moved its entire target stream to diesel polishing. Dr. Rasha Maal-Bared warned that viscous crude mixes in localised laminar flow, which put static mixing elements and bioreactor sizing into the continuous-flow design. Patrick Lumley (Safe Sulphur Systems, 33 years in sulfur recovery) said the aqueous sulfite byproduct needs pH control to prevent SO2 release and that Claus-plant load reduction is economically worthless - so a pH control loop went into the P&ID and the business case was rebuilt around hydrogen savings and reduced hydrotreating severity instead of sulfur throughput. Larter's warning about microbially influenced corrosion added corrosion and material-compatibility criteria to the reactor design. Faculty of Engineering Design Day feedback forced the pivot from a full HDS-replacement narrative to the complementary polishing model. Dr. Corrie DaCosta, a protein chemist, was consulted specifically about what Cofactor's output report must contain to be usable by the person doing the engineering, and the report format changed as a result.","realWorld":"A fully worked commercialization case. Market sizing: TAM ~$3.1B (global HDS catalyst market), SAM ~$66M (Canada's 2.2% share, 16 refineries, ~2M bbl/day), SOM ~$1.4M from a single-refinery side-stream demo at a $0.40/bbl royalty on a 10,000 bbl/day stream. Revenue model is per-barrel licensing royalties, validated in conversation with an operations and project engineer. A techno-economic analysis compares BDS against HDS CAPEX of $16-42M USD for a 20,000-25,000 bbl/day refinery and projects 50-60% CAPEX reduction from eliminating high-pressure hydrogen hardware; a life cycle assessment with a declared system boundary and BDS/HDS inventories backs the environmental claim rather than asserting it. A staged timeline runs from proof of concept (2026) through publication and NSERC Alliance / Mitacs Accelerate / CRIN non-dilutive funding (2027), pilot demonstration on real feedstock plus formal pre-engagement with Environment and Climate Change Canada under New Substances Notification regulations for contained LMO deployment (2028-29), to licensing (2030+) and expansion into biodenitrogenation. The team deliberately targets smaller, older refineries facing Euro V limits with constrained hydrogen capacity rather than major oil sands operators, on Lumley's advice, and flags biogas producers as a faster early-revenue path. Risk analysis, SWOT, Business Model Canvas and a competitive positioning matrix are all published, and the team is explicit that Nulfur is harm reduction during the energy transition - \"a bridge, not a destination\".","partners":["Natural Resources Canada (CanmetENERGY-Devon)","Environment and Climate Change Canada, Low Carbon Fuels Division","Safe Sulphur Systems Ltd.","CDM Smith","Spanish National Research Council (CSIC), Systems Biology Program","Bedford Institute of Oceanography, Fisheries and Oceans Canada","Institute for Oil Sands Innovation / University of Alberta","University of Calgary","Queen's University","Arabian Gulf University","Hawk & Squirrel Innovation Inc.","Ottawa-Carleton District School Board (IILP Summer Program)","Ottawa Catholic School Board","AcadeCap International School","Ottawa Public Libraries","uOttawa eHub RevUp Circuit","MindFuel Tech Futures Challenge","QGEM (Great Canadian iGEM Incubator)","Clean Resource Innovation Network (CRIN)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/dry-lab/protein-engineering/fig-3.svg","caption":"Predictor-agreement scatter for 437 scored DszB mutations: the shaded quadrant is where FoldX and ThermoMPNN both call a substitution stabilising, enlarged to label the five variants advanced to the wet lab.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/dry-lab/cofactor/figures/fig-4b.avif","caption":"The Cofactor GUI - a local FastAPI web interface over the agentic protein engineering platform, showing chat, hypothesis and evidence views, job tracking and artifact browsing.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/dry-lab/consortia/fig-3.avif","caption":"Rate gain in DBT uptake from interfacial-area enhancement and micellar solubilization in isolation and combined; the shaded region is the synergy in excess of the product of the two isolated effects.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/education/adapt/art-consortia/pxl-20260716-173536329-mp.avif","caption":"The Art of Consortia activity in the Ottawa-Carleton District School Board summer language programme, where children build a shared mural to learn how a microbial consortium divides labour.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/process/igem-p-id-final.avif","caption":"Process and instrumentation diagram for the full-scale biodesulfurization plant, including the pH control loop added after Patrick Lumley warned that uncontrolled sulfite pH would release SO2.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy uOttawa's human-practices template literally: a single page where every one of 21 stakeholder conversations is logged in the same three fields - why we went, what we heard, what we changed - with the change written as a specific design edit (a pH loop in the P&ID, a switch of feedstock, static mixers). It costs nothing to adopt, makes a student team's reasoning auditable, and is the clearest way a school research centre can show that outside input actually moved the project rather than decorating it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/alternative-platform","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/process-design-and-analysis","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/gcm","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uottawa/cofactor"],"rank":110},{"team":"UPF-Barcelona","id":"6078","section":"Undergrad","country":"Spain","village":"Food & Agriculture","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/","projectTitle":"Vinesys","tagline":"Where the rain runs out, the roots hold on: engineering root-associated Bacillus subtilis to hold water around grapevine roots.","summary":"Vinesys engineers Bacillus subtilis to colonise the grapevine rhizosphere and overexpress gamma-PGA, a water-retaining biopolymer, plus ACC deaminase to trim the drought-stress hormone precursor ACC, forming a thin hydration shell where the bacterium attaches to the root. Drought is detected by a two-layer switch: an osmolarity-responsive promoter plus a salt-dependent intein (HutMCM2) that the team re-engineered in silico to splice at the roughly 600-725 mM intracellular K+ that B. subtilis actually reaches, rather than its native 1.5 M. The dry lab produced three distinct deliverables: RK4 solver, a standalone no-code genetic-circuit designer and simulator; a documented molecular-dynamics pipeline for tuning salt-dependent proteins; and iGEMforge, a Claude Code plugin of four research skills that they evaluated honestly and then declined to use for project decisions. Awards came for Software, Model and Education.","awards":["Best Software — Winner (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"RK4 solver is a desktop Python application with a GUI built in Dear PyGui, structured as three partially independent modules (GUI, compiler, solver) so the solver can be driven from Python without drawing anything on screen. It ships for Windows, macOS and Linux. Interchange is handled by two formats: a readable personal JSON format and SBML Level 3, which gives round-trip compatibility with COPASI and Tellurium. The molecular-dynamics pipeline runs ColabFold/AlphaFold for structures, FoldX for stability screening, MAFFT and BLAST for candidate selection, UCSF Chimera for inspection, and OpenMM with Amber force fields (tleap setup) for the simulations. iGEMforge is a Claude Code plugin: four Markdown SKILL files plus a couple of Python scripts in a folder, deliberately with no subagents, hooks or custom MCP servers.","dataviz":"RK4 solver's results screen plots species concentrations while every parameter has a live slider that takes effect during the running simulation, which is the core interaction idea. The editor uses blocks whose icons follow SBOL Visual glyphs, with input pins always on the left and output pins on the right so links read left to right, collapsible blocks, and a diagnostics/warnings pane with connection rules that refuse to let you build an invalid circuit. The wiki itself is built as explanatory interactive JavaScript rather than static figures: an in-browser Euler-vs-RK4 comparison on x'' = -x, a step-through animation of the solver's operator stack, a replayable eval harness showing the same prompt answered with and without an iGEMforge skill, and a hoverable stakeholder map on the entrepreneurship page.","datascience":"The solver was validated numerically against COPASI: three example circuits were exported to SBML and re-simulated in COPASI under LSODA with identical parameters, initial concentrations and simulation time, and every quantity that means the same thing in both tools agreed within 0.21%, most within 0.01%. The apparent 13.4% and 98.5% discrepancies on free mazF and mazE were traced to a labelling difference in rapid-equilibrium binding, not integration error, and resolved by comparing totals (mazF + 2*FEF, mazE + FEF), which then matched within 0.01% and 0.09%. iGEMforge was evaluated in two rounds: 11 hand-graded assertion tests in round one, then 28 cases run three times per arm (with-skill vs. without-skill) using Claude Code's `claude plugin eval` with an LLM-as-a-judge against team-written rubrics, including real cases, synthetic cases with deliberately planted flaws, and 'silence' cases where a skill should stay out of the way. The MD work used replicas, a wild-type positive control at its known 1.5 M threshold, an uncharged no-effect control, and re-ran its analysis under several different attack-distance cutoffs to show the conclusion did not depend on one arbitrary value.","software":"RK4 solver is the main tool: a free, open-source, cross-platform application that turns a hand-drawn genetic circuit into a running ODE simulation with no code at all, aimed squarely at iGEM teams, high-school and undergraduate students, and explicitly not at replacing COPASI or SimBiology. Eight block types are implemented (production with Hill activation/repression, degradation, complex formation in rapid-equilibrium or kinetic mode, extracellular species with six dosing regimes, cell entry, export, catalysis with Michaelis-Menten or mass action, and a link probe), and the compiler translates the drawing into a flat list of stack operators which the solver evaluates with Euler or 4th-order Runge-Kutta. The architecture is worth copying: the translator sorts each block's contribution by the species it points to rather than by the block's own species, a redesign forced by discovering that complex formation was writing its negative terms into the wrong derivative. It was validated twice, against COPASI numerically and against the team's own biocontainment toxin-antitoxin model biologically, and it was user-tested, with non-expert testers building and tuning a toggle switch in 5 to 20 minutes after a short introduction to the notation. Limitations are stated plainly on the wiki (deterministic only, partial SBML import, custom block unfinished, GUI not yet as intuitive as they want) with a three-horizon roadmap including SBOL-based integration with the iGEM Registry that would pull part parameters in and mark any user override as traceably overwritten. Source: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/upf-barcelona/upf-software (team repo https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/upf-barcelona). It is distributed as a downloadable cross-platform application, not a hosted web service, so there is no click-and-use URL. The second tool, iGEMforge, is the most interesting negative result of the season: a Claude Code plugin of four skills (scientific-evidence, experimental-design, mathematical-modeling, bio-wiki-animations) built over two DBTL cycles, in which round-one hand-grading suggested the skills helped (bio-wiki-animations 12/13 assertions with the skill against 5/12 without) but round two with three runs per arm and an LLM judge showed no clear average gain (experimental-design -0.11, scientific-evidence +0.04, mathematical-modeling no clear difference), at substantially higher token cost (309k vs 224k; 468k vs 201k). Testing revealed that three of four skills intermittently stalled waiting on user confirmation and returned nothing; cycle two fixed it with a single rule written into the skills ('state the assumption or caveat and continue'), which restored output in 3 of 3 runs but still did not beat the base model. The team's conclusion, published on the wiki, is that the evidence was too thin to use iGEMforge for Vinesys decisions, and they list cost, privacy, over-trust and installation risks explicitly.","stack":["Python","Dear PyGui","SBML Level 3","SBOL Visual","JSON","COPASI (validation target)","Tellurium","OpenMM","Amber force field / tleap","ColabFold","AlphaFold","FoldX","MAFFT","BLAST","UCSF Chimera","Claude Code plugin (Markdown skills + Python)","claude plugin eval","iGEM GitLab","JavaScript (interactive wiki figures)"],"techniques":["ODE modelling of genetic circuits","4th-order Runge-Kutta and Euler integration","stack-machine operator compilation of differential equations","Hill activation/repression kinetics","rapid-equilibrium and kinetic complex formation","Michaelis-Menten catalysis","molecular dynamics with near-attack-conformation (NAC) readout","ddG stability screening","pLDDT/PAE structure confidence assessment","LLM-as-a-judge evaluation with pre-written rubrics","A/B testing with planted-flaw synthetic cases","SBML round-trip cross-validation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education is organised around an explicit six-step method the team named WONDER (Who, Objectives, Notice, Dialogue, Evidence, Record), whose last step feeds back into the first like a DBTL cycle, and which is rooted in Catalan educacio del lleure (leisure education) practice that several team members came from through scouting. Audiences were plotted on two axes, age and prior synbio interest, and the session language (Catalan, Spanish or English) was chosen from the barrier they wanted to lower. Each session required a before-and-after measure, at least one artefact participants made themselves, a rubric completed within 24 hours, and a written summary within a day covering what actually happened and what they would change, published on the wiki so other teams can run the session without them. Activities spanned a MiniGEM where pre-university students designed their own synthetic biology projects, an escape room at CosmoCaixa, La Nit de la Recerca, the UPF Summer School, a role-play at the PRBB, SynBYSS, Centre Civic Guinardo, scout groups (Cau La Floresta) and several schools, plus a round table with iGEM UB.","ihp":"Human practices is written as three acts with a stated principle of intentionality: every design choice should have a reason statable to an outsider, and where there is none they say so. 32 people outside the team across 8 countries, 52 documented exchanges and 103 survey respondents, with 9 of 10 project phases opened to outside input. The first grower they met corrected their founding premise, that drought was killing Catalan vineyards, by explaining that summer water stress is necessary for proper ripening and the real damage is to next season's crop; a second grower said vines did die in the first drought year, and the team kept both rather than reconciling them. A gamma-PGA geneticist in Pavia shrank the central claim from bulk soil water retention to a local hydration shell at the root. A Bordeaux researcher set a condition the design cannot meet (no deficit at flowering, moderate deficit later) and the team publishes it as a limit, not a feature. Two further conventions are worth copying: advice they chose not to follow stays on the page with the reason, and disagreements they could not resolve are printed unresolved. They also disclose that one advisor, Dr. Carolina Elizondo, holds an iGEM role, so readers can weigh her input.","realWorld":"The entrepreneurship page is explicit that the current value is knowledge, education and visibility rather than product sales, with the project at contained laboratory proof of concept and no environmental release. More than 50 organisations were contacted since June 2026; wineries Clos Montblanc (DO Conca de Barberà), Sumarroca and Vallformosa (DO Penedès) were interviewed about how a new input would fit existing practice, with Vallformosa noting that acting at root level could mean lower adoption friction. IRTA, the Catalan government's agrifood research institute, reviewed the approach against its own Penedès soil-and-water initiative and provided winery contacts. The regulatory finding was that certification, not GMO status, is the binding constraint, and the business work reached a cost per hectare and a route to a field trial. Scale context: 52,000 ha of Catalan vineyard, 8,500 viticulturists, EUR 3.27 bn annual turnover, reservoirs at 14.3% in 2024.","partners":["IRTA (Institut de Recerca i Tecnologia Agroalimentàries)","Celler Clos Montblanc","Sumarroca","Vallformosa","Ajuntament de Barcelona (Departament de Ciència i Innovació)","CosmoCaixa","PRBB","iGEM UB","Anthropic (sponsor)","University of Pavia (gamma-PGA genetics)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6078/wiki/dry-lab/software/figure6.avif","caption":"Screenshot of the RK4 solver editor with the biocontainment toxin-antitoxin circuit built from linked blocks.","credit":"Team UPF-Barcelona, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6078/wiki/dry-lab/software/figure7.avif","caption":"RK4 solver output: internal resveratrol, mazF and FEF levels over time under sinusoidal resveratrol production.","credit":"Team UPF-Barcelona, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6078/wiki/dry-lab/software/figure4.avif","caption":"The first hand-drawn sketches of the RK4 solver blocks, designed in the style of electronic circuit diagrams.","credit":"Team UPF-Barcelona, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Validate a simulator against the established tool in the field and publish the discrepancy table, including the one that looked like a 98% error and turned out to be a labelling difference, because that table is what makes a student-built solver trustworthy. Their AI work is the sharper lesson for a research centre: build the agent plugin, then actually A/B evaluate it with planted-flaw cases and an LLM judge, and be willing to publish that it did not beat the base model and that you therefore did not use it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/igem-forge","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/dry-tips","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/description","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/upf-barcelona/upf-software"],"rank":26},{"team":"BAID-China","id":"6089","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/baid-china/","projectTitle":"Neurofilux","tagline":"An AI-designed antibody sandwich assay for blood neurofilament light chain, for cheap non-invasive early screening of neurodegenerative disease","summary":"BAID-China built Neurofilux, a blood test for neurofilament light chain (NfL), a biomarker of neuronal damage that rises years before Alzheimer's or ALS symptoms appear but is currently detectable only by expensive, invasive or insufficiently sensitive methods. They expressed the NfL rod domain (residues 93-396) as antigen, used the generative model RFantibody to design candidate antibodies, screened them with a multi-objective scoring pipeline, validated eight candidates experimentally and found two pairs outperforming commercial antibodies in sandwich ELISA, then converted the validated antibodies into nanobodies for stability and cost. Their inclusivity work is organised around a self-defined 3A framework - Adaptation, Awareness, Accessibility - and centres on a tactile magnetic genetic-circuit kit for blind children, developed in two phases and donated to a school for the visually impaired.","awards":["Inclusivity Award — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Self-defined 3A inclusivity framework (Adaptation: convert visually-presented science into tactile, auditory and interactive forms; Awareness: dispel stereotypes and spread barrier-free science; Accessibility: remove visual, language and technical thresholds), with every activity required to map onto at least one dimension. Grounded in a literature review citing Hayes and Proulx (2023) on barriers for visually impaired science students and CAST (2024) Universal Design for Learning, plus Design Thinking, Empathy Mapping and Universal Design concepts taught by an invited expert. Accessibility work was benchmarked against WCAG.","dataviz":"All wiki charts redrawn in the eight-colour Okabe-Ito colour-blind-safe palette, chosen because each colour keeps enough luminance difference to stay readable in greyscale; the team published a colour-blindness simulation figure verifying that elements remain distinguishable under red-green, blue-yellow and total colour-blindness simulation.","datascience":"Multi-objective computational scoring to rank AI-generated antibody candidates by predicted sensitivity; comparative legal and building-code research across China, Egypt, Pakistan and the United States compiled into an open-access international accessibility database; pre-activity knowledge survey of junior and senior high school students on neurodegenerative disease awareness.","software":"RFantibody generative AI model used for de novo antibody design, followed by computational screening and virtual validation of nanobody conversions; a laboratory accessibility audit tool and evaluation form built on universal design principles; a wiki accessibility plugin offering a five-step text magnifier, a language switcher and one-click return-to-top, with semantic HTML restructured so screen readers can parse the navigation hierarchy.","stack":["RFantibody (generative antibody design)","multi-objective computational screening","sandwich ELISA","Okabe-Ito colour palette","semantic HTML / screen-reader-compatible markup","WCAG","trilingual wiki (Chinese, English, French)"],"techniques":["NfL rod domain (93-396 aa) expression as antigen","antibody pair selection for sandwich ELISA","nanobody conversion","CDR loop design on dominant epitopes","planned digital ELISA at fg/mL sensitivity"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"BAID-China started from evidence rather than instinct: a literature review on STEM barriers for disabled students (high lab benches, reading instrument data, observing phenomena, the need for written or sign-language support) plus a field visit to Bethel China, a charity for preschool children with visual impairments, where they identified two concrete problems - classes run with several children to one teacher, which blocks understanding, and tactile picture books that deliver touch sensation but almost no actual knowledge content. Both findings set the agenda. First strand: a team member, Audrey, joined the iGEM community project 'Accessibility in Synbio Labs' at the iGEM Annual Community Day in early 2026 and led BAID-China's inclusivity group through Phase 1 (research and database development) and Phase 2 (lab audits) alongside iGEM members from China, Egypt, Pakistan and the United States. With team Concordia-Shanghai they researched China's Accessibility Environment Construction Law, the Law on the Protection of Persons with Disabilities and GB 55019-2021 General Specifications for Accessibility in Architecture and Municipal Engineering, focusing on doorway clearance and wheelchair turning diameter, and the national research sets were compiled into an open-access comparative international database. The audit tool was then reviewed by Chloe Franklin, a scientist who uses a wheelchair, whose feedback changed it substantially: replace the Yes/No/N/A format with a scale that reflects degrees of accessibility because wheelchair users' needs differ widely; add specific measurable criteria with dimensional requirements; and weight audit items by importance, since building entrances, elevators, emergency evacuation routes and restrooms are foundational while equipment height and storage can be adjusted more easily. The team logged her suggestions, took them to weekly meetings, produced a new version, and credits co-creators by name (Sami from Team Toronto 2026, Mohamed, Sophie and Linda). They are honest that pilot testing was not completed within the season and commit to continuing next year. Second strand, and the signature contribution: Chloe Franklin's remark that a beautiful accessible lab on a high floor of a building with no accessible elevator is meaningless pushed the team from physical access to information access. They built a tactile magnetic genetic-circuit kit in which each component is a distinct shape rather than a colour or diagram: two circles of different sizes for input signals (large = normal cell, small = cancer cell), a star for an activator, an X for a repressor, an arrow for a promoter, a rectangle for a gene, a semicircle for a terminator and a pentagon for the output (anticancer drug release). This was embedded in a 45-minute workshop of four activities, run five times for more than 110 participants in total. Activity One sent sighted participants into the team's own laboratory in a wheelchair, where they discovered the doorway was barely wider than the chair, a slope and uneven bricks blocked independent movement, and bench spacing left no turning room. Activity Two brought in David Doyle, Project Head of the Accessibility in Synbio Labs community project, to teach Design Thinking, Empathy Mapping and Universal Design - the point being that environments should be designed around users rather than users adapting to environments. Activity Three recreated a standard Chinese middle-school microscope exercise, but half the participants were denied visual access: one student described cell shapes, features and spatial arrangement in words while the others had to reconstruct the specimen on paper, which reliably failed, demonstrating that people can sit in the same classroom and receive very different amounts of information. Activity Four had blindfolded participants assemble, by touch alone, a circuit that releases a drug when cancer cells are detected but not when normal cells are present. Phase 1 feedback exposed real design flaws - unclear verbal instructions about logical relationships between blocks, and block shapes and sizes that were hard to grasp or distinguish - which taught the team that simply converting a visual circuit diagram into differently shaped magnets is not sufficient. For Phase 2 they contacted a Beijing school for the visually impaired and visited twice. The first visit was deliberately not a lesson: team members read biology picture books to the children, using a caterpillar's growth to introduce change in living things, while observing how the children used language to describe scientific phenomena and how they responded to different question types. Because the seven participating children were aged 3-8 and had no basis for promoters, terminators or expression regulation, the team stripped the terminology out and kept only the Input-Regulation-Output logic, recast as a 'sound machine': children explored block shapes by touch, assembled the machine, and a volunteer clapped three times as the audio output when it worked. The star block was introduced as the machine's 'good assistant' that makes it run and the X block as the 'bad assistant' that stops it, so activation and repression were learned through immediate auditory feedback. The kit was then donated to the school so teachers can keep using and adapting it for different ages. Third strand: wiki accessibility treated as a deliverable with real user testing. All charts were redrawn in the Okabe-Ito palette and verified against red-green, blue-yellow and total colour-blindness simulations; the page code was rebuilt with semantic tags so screen readers can identify headings, paragraphs and buttons; an accessibility plugin in the lower right corner offers five text sizes with line and character spacing adjusted in step so layout does not break when enlarged, aimed explicitly at elderly readers; and the entire project is translated into Chinese, English and French with two switching paths. The team then invited a person with red-green colour blindness, elderly users and non-native English speakers to test it and iterated on their feedback. Fourth strand: public science communication on neurodegenerative disease, prompted by a survey finding that most junior high school students did not know what neurodegenerative diseases are, associated them only with memory decline in the elderly, or said 'this has nothing to do with me', while high school students had the capacity but no systematic channel; an interview with the local health bureau director in the Guangying area established that medical staff, government and charities are all delivering information but public acceptance is low and prevention habits are not forming, partly because the material is monotonous and dependent on visual text.","ihp":"The project's human practices thread runs through disease awareness and screening access: interviews with the local health bureau director in the Guangying area on why existing neurodegenerative-disease education fails to land, plus in-school surveys establishing an age-stratified knowledge gap, shaped the public communication strategy to differentiate content depth by audience. The inclusivity work is itself integrated practice - the Chloe Franklin and David Doyle consultations each produced documented, traceable changes to an artefact rather than general encouragement.","realWorld":"Open outputs: the comparative international laboratory-accessibility legal database, the laboratory accessibility audit tool PDF, the audit evaluation form PDF, the Chinese legal research document, the trilingual accessible wiki itself, and the physical tactile magnetic genetic-circuit kit, which was donated to a Beijing school for the visually impaired for continued teaching use. Commitments are explicit: pilot testing of the audit tool continues into the next season in collaboration with the wider iGEM community.","partners":["Bethel China (charity for preschool children with visual impairments; seven children tested the kit)","a school for the visually impaired in Beijing (kit donated)","iGEM community project 'Accessibility in Synbio Labs' (David Doyle, Project Head)","Chloe Franklin, scientist and wheelchair user (audit tool review)","Concordia-Shanghai iGEM (joint Chinese legal research)","Team Toronto 2026 (Sami) and Mohamed, co-creators of the audit tool","iGEM members from Egypt, Pakistan and the United States","Guangying area local health bureau"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6089/wiki/5.avif","caption":"Workshop Activity One: participants navigate the team's own laboratory in a wheelchair and record the barriers they meet","credit":"Team BAID-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6089/wiki/12.avif","caption":"Workshop Activity Three: one participant describes a microscope specimen in words while others try to reconstruct it on paper","credit":"Team BAID-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6089/wiki/16.avif","caption":"Workshop Activity Four: blindfolded participants build a genetic circuit from shape-coded magnetic pieces by touch alone","credit":"Team BAID-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6089/wiki/24.avif","caption":"Phase 2 at a Beijing school for the visually impaired: seven children aged 3-8 build a 'sound machine' using the tactile kit","credit":"Team BAID-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6089/wiki/in1.avif","caption":"The Okabe-Ito colour-blind-safe palette adopted for every chart on the wiki, verified with colour-blindness simulation","credit":"Team BAID-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6089/wiki/28.avif","caption":"Across five workshop sessions the team reached more than 110 participants","credit":"Team BAID-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Build the accessible teaching aid, then pilot it twice: first with sighted participants under blindfold so you catch your own design flaws in a controlled setting, then with the actual users after stripping out the terminology they do not yet have - and donate the finished kit to the partner school so it keeps teaching after you leave.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/baid-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/baid-china/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/baid-china/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/baid-china/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/baid-china/entrepreneurship"],"rank":7},{"team":"BOKU-Vienna","id":"6090","section":"Overgrad","country":"Austria","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/","projectTitle":"Algaeneering","tagline":"Engineering the microalga Chlamydomonas reinhardtii into a vitamin C and folate factory to fight hidden hunger","summary":"BOKU-Vienna overexpressed GGP (vitamin C) and GTPCHI plus ADCS (folate) in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii using Golden Gate Level 0/1/2 assembly, reaching a 4.5x increase in vitamin C in the GGP strain. The project was framed around hidden hunger, which affects an estimated two billion people, and was linked to UN SDGs 2, 3 and 12. Its education practice was built on an explicit didactic framework (the Didactic Triangle) and spanned four goals: sparking curiosity, classroom science, public dialogue and educational media. A genome-scale metabolic model was coupled to a calibrated bioreactor model and a nutritional impact model to translate lab titres into how many people a production plant could feed.","awards":["Best Education — Nominee (Overgrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Genome-scale metabolic modelling of C. reinhardtii (iCre1355, mixotrophic version) with flux balance analysis (FBA), flux variability analysis (FVA), in-silico knockouts and enzyme protein-cost ranking; dynamic FBA bioreactor model calibrated and validated against the team's own flask cultures; a nutritional impact model converting measured vitamin content into daily requirements and people supplied.","dataviz":"Interactive 'Try the trade-off' widget on the wiki: sliders let a visitor pick a vitamin and see (left) maximum vitamin flux versus growth rate from the genome-scale model and (right) the calibrated flask culture's biomass, ammonium, acetate and vitamin titre over up to 120 h, with the team's own measured points overlaid and a harvest-point marker. Each graph carries an explicit 'How to read this graph' panel and a parameter-provenance table.","datascience":"Lab-to-model feedback loop: flask measurements (biomass, ammonium, acetate, vitamin titre) were used to calibrate model parameters, and the model's pre-registered 1-10x overexpression band was compared against the measured 4.5x vitamin C result. Known model gaps were stated openly (measured ammonium ~2 mM below model; no cell-death term).","software":"Custom interactive children's learning platform (web app, guided by an algae mascot, virtual-microscope zoom from cell to molecule, 3D DNA double helix with VR headset support, base-pairing puzzle game) and a separate 13-module self-paced interactive learning platform with mini-games (lac operon switch, PCR exponential-growth slider, mRNA translation, Cas9 cut-site finder), instant feedback, no sign-up, and a teacher preview mode that unlocks all modules for classroom prep.","stack":["genome-scale metabolic model iCre1355","flux balance analysis","flux variability analysis","dynamic FBA","web-based interactive learning platform","WebVR / 3D DNA model","Kahoot","Mentimeter / QR-code live surveys"],"techniques":["Golden Gate Assembly (Level 0 to Level 2)","C. reinhardtii transformation (UVM4)","vitamin C and folate assays","microscopy","protein-cost analysis of enzyme targets","bioreactor scale-up scenario modelling"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"BOKU-Vienna ran a four-pillar education strategy explicitly grounded in the Didactic Triangle (learner-educator-content), and designed every child-facing print and digital resource in the dyslexia-friendly ABeeZee font. Pillar 1 (sparking curiosity) included a three-day Children's University (KinderUNI) booth built as a six-station progressive learning path: project poster, live Chlorella tubes and Chlamydomonas microscope images, a handcrafted 3D algae organelle model, a custom web learning platform plus a VR headset for exploring a 3D DNA double helix, an interactive genetic-engineering poster, and a gamified 'plasmid toss' into a large algae model to represent transformation; gummy-bear DNA building ran alongside. The same station set was then carried to Karwan Haus, a Caritas Wien refugee shelter in Vienna, on 17 August 2026 for children aged 4-12, with the flyer translated into four languages, Arabic- and Russian-speaking team members explaining stations, illustration-heavy materials, child-sized lab coats and goggles lent by KinderUNI BOKU, Lego-brick Golden Gate Cloning, stamps and science badges; in the exit survey most children chose 'I would love to work in science in the future'. Pillar 2 put a three-week, four-visit curriculum into two primary schools (Ruckergasse in Vienna and Neuarzl in Tyrol) with strawberry DNA extraction, lab-safety and pipetting sessions, a construct-assembly exercise for the GGP enzyme, vitamin C detection, Kahoot recaps and a field trip to the ZOOM Children's Museum where pupils wrote, drew and voiced two stop-motion films about algae and genetic modification; a middle-school workshop ('Code of Life - How We Program Cells') added a red-cabbage anthocyanin biosensor experiment, and a high-school visit to BG/BRG Tulln had students identify C. reinhardtii against Spirulina and Chlorella under the microscope. Measured impact: in the primary-school pre/post evaluation, zero children named science as a desired profession at the start versus five at the end, and 75.0% said they had become curious about programming cells (16.7% wanted to learn more first, 8.3% said they definitely want to do it in future). Pillar 3 (public dialogue) comprised a booth at Austria's Long Night of Research (24 April 2026) with a live visitor survey board on GMO acceptance and awareness that over 5 billion people face micronutrient deficiency, the Muthgassen Summer Festival, a 'Future of Food' panel discussion at TU Wien's Boecklsaal on 3 October 2026 with about 60 attendees and four panelists spanning algae cosmetics (Rita Mota), UNICEF Austria malnutrition work (Michael Blauensteiner), algae production engineering (Lukas Neuwirth, ex-Ecoduna) and microalgae research (Johannes Stadlmann), run in three blocks with anonymous QR-code audience polling and spirulina cotton candy, plus a pub quiz for about thirty STEM and non-STEM students. Pillar 4 produced durable media: an illustrated German children's book 'Welche Sprache spricht das Leben?' co-developed with KinderBOKU (characters such as 'Grandma Polymerase' who knits DNA together), launched at Vienna's Pappenheimgasse public library for a 4th-grade and a 5th-grade class with a copy donated to the library's lending collection and a copy given to every child; a comic advised by Martina Froehlich of KinderBOKU; the German-language podcast 'AlgaeHearing' on Spotify, deliberately in German because accessible German science content is scarce; the 13-module interactive learning platform with a teacher preview mode; and a feature article in the German-language life-science magazine BioSpektrum (issue 32, 4 June 2026). Critically, all tested worksheets, workshop guides and experiment protocols were published as downloadable Open Educational Resources on eduvidual.at, the official learning platform of the Austrian Federal Ministry of Education, Science and Research, so Austrian teachers nationwide can reuse them. Named-attribution testimonials were collected from the teacher at Ruckergasse, the head of the Pappenheimgasse library, the head of KinderBOKU and a social worker at Karwan Haus.","ihp":"Human Practices was structured into three sequential phases, each with its own stakeholder set: (1) lab experts for technical feasibility, which changed key design decisions; (2) outside critics on GMOs, sustainability and ethics, where the team explicitly sought disconfirming rather than validating views; (3) nutrition and product-design conversations about who should be prioritised as a target group and how the product would be delivered. The team framed iHP against the reflective/responsible/responsive triad, mapped the project to SDGs 2, 3 and 12, and used the 'Future of Food' panel plus audience polling as a live iHP instrument. The panel's main takeaway was recorded honestly as 'our work is meaningful, but GMO-based projects are not without risk and open questions, especially when implemented at a larger scale'.","realWorld":"The impact model translated measured vitamin content into concrete figures: 25 g dry algae per day covers one daily folate requirement and 116 g covers vitamin C; a hypothetical 100 m3 production plant at wild-type folate content could cover the yearly folate need of roughly 900 adults or 490 pregnant women. The design reactor scenario produced 0.46 g/L/day, nitrogen-capped, at about 1.5x the flask vitamin titre. The team also published a separate 'iGEM guide' page for teams starting out.","partners":["KinderBOKU / KinderUNI BOKU (BOKU University children's science programme)","Caritas Wien - Karwan Haus refugee shelter","ZOOM Children's Museum, Vienna","Pappenheimgasse public library, Vienna","Ruckergasse Primary School, Vienna","Neuarzl Primary School, Tyrol","BG/BRG 21 bilingual middle school","BG/BRG Tulln high school","TU Wien (Boecklsaal venue)","eduvidual.at - Austrian Federal Ministry of Education, Science and Research learning platform","BioSpektrum magazine","UNICEF Austria (panelist)","Stroeck (sponsor, panel catering)","Polymun"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/kinderuni/kinderuni-02.avif","caption":"Children at the Children's University (KinderUNI) six-station synthetic biology booth in Vienna","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/karwan-haus/karwan-haus-golden-gate-assembly-via-lego-bricks.avif","caption":"Teaching Golden Gate assembly with Lego bricks at Karwan Haus, a Caritas Wien refugee shelter","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/childrensbook/childrensbook-front.avif","caption":"The team's illustrated German children's book, co-developed with KinderBOKU and donated to a Vienna public library","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/lernplattform/1-overview-of-our-interactive-learning-platform-with-13-modules.avif","caption":"The free 13-module interactive learning platform, with no sign-up and a teacher preview mode","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/panel-discussion/panel-discussion-people.avif","caption":"The 'Future of Food' public panel discussion at TU Wien, about 60 attendees with live QR-code polling","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/primary-school/primary-school-02.avif","caption":"Pupils extracting DNA during the three-week primary-school curriculum","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Publish every tested worksheet, workshop guide and experiment protocol as open educational resources on the national ministry education platform (as BOKU did on eduvidual.at), so school use continues after the project ends, and measure the same cohort before and after (career aspirations plus curiosity items) so learning gain is a number, not an anecdote.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/model"],"rank":7},{"team":"XJTLU-China","id":"6096","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/","projectTitle":"dogmaOS — programming translation rhythm for soluble protein expression","tagline":"Design where and for how long the ribosome should pause, then re-engineer the host tRNA pool to make that pause possible.","summary":"XJTLU-China asked whether soluble yield of hard-to-express recombinant proteins in E. coli can be improved by deliberately programming *where* translation slows down, rather than by maximising expression. Their dry-lab system catmaOS (Codon-Aware Translation Modeling and Analysis Operating System) chains four models: structural co-translational folding priority (M1), evolutionary conserved slow-codon evidence (M2), a five-layer translation-kinetics and ribosome-traffic engine with constrained Pareto selection (M3-A to M3-E), and host tRNA-pool intervention design (M4). The framework picked a single synonymous G-CSF design (W1-S) out of six candidates and an engineered BL21-AlaPro chassis, both of which went to the bench; Four-Leaf Clover RT-qPCR then measured the actual tRNA knockdown and that number was fed back into M3-A as an updated host context. It won Best Model (Undergraduate) and Best Education (Undergraduate).","awards":["Best Measurement — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Model — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Education — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"catmaOS is a four-model, five-layer multiscale pipeline and is unusually explicit about what each layer is and is not. M1 is a structural-bioinformatics scoring model: it builds a residue-level evidence table from PDB coordinates (G-CSF, PDB 1RHG chain B, UniProt P09919, RefSeq NM_000759.4), runs DSSP for secondary structure and relative solvent accessibility, solves a self-consistent native-contact occupancy model as chain length grows (Jacobs-Shakhnovich) to detect folding events at a 1 kBT free-energy-drop threshold with a 4 Angstrom contact cutoff, adds Kyte-Doolittle hydrophobic exposure, cysteine-neighbourhood scores, DSSP transition and beta-initiation tracks and an AGGRESCAN-style aggregation propensity, then fuses them with availability-aware weights into a 0-100 priority score and shifts it downstream by a 35-codon ribosome exit-tunnel offset (scanned 30-40 codons for sensitivity). M2 is a comparative-genomics model: MAFFT L-INS-i protein alignment projected back to codons with PAL2NAL, GtRNAdb/tRNAscan-SE high-confidence tRNA gene copy numbers per species, a frozen codon-anticodon wobble-penalty table, a tRNA-adaptation-style effective supply W_s(c) converted to within-species relative 'resistance', a top-25%-resistance slow-codon label, a strict same-amino-acid filter, and Henikoff position-based sequence weighting to produce a weighted slow-codon conservation score with an explicit effective-coverage track. M3 is the kinetic core: M3-A decomposes intrinsic codon dwell into decoding (inverse effective cognate/wobble supply with a near-cognate competition term, K_dec = 0.80, gamma_near = 0.50), peptidyl-transfer (proline/EF-P arrest context), basal translocation and ViennaRNA RNAplfold local-unpaired-probability RNA resistance, with four ablation configurations A0-A3 to attribute mechanism; M3-B feeds that rate vector into TWO independent ribosome-traffic backends — a deterministic Ribosome Flow Model with Extended Objects (RFMEO) steady state and a stochastic Gillespie kinetic Monte Carlo extended-object TASEP (5 replicates, SEM bands, warm-up discarded) — and cross-validates them with Pearson, Spearman, concordance and top-k hotspot overlap plus an alpha-beta boundary-sensitivity grid; M3-C maps traffic-safe dwell envelopes, exit-tunnel availability gates, relative contact-order folding demand, a finite-state productive-pathway propagator p_{L+1} = p_L exp(s Q_L dt_L) run over a 0.5/1/2 scale grid (direction called 'robust' only when the sign holds across scales), and a time-weighted unsatisfied-native-contact exposure proxy E_U = sum_L U(L) tau(L); M3-D closes a first-order steady-state mRNA balance M_ss = k_tx / (k_deg,eff + mu) with a scikit-learn mRNA-decay predictor trained on 5,486 curated E. coli half-life records (Chen 2015, Selinger 2003) split gene-grouped 3,883/765/838; M3-E generates synonymous candidates under reading-frame, GC, motif, restriction-site and homopolymer constraints and selects by constrained Pareto dominance over four frozen objectives (maximise terminal flux J, minimise ribosome load R, maximise productive-pathway C4, minimise exposure C5), with exact enumeration for small spaces and NSGA-II for large ones, scored for precision/recall against exact fronts. M4 extends design to the host: it maps target codons to BL21 tRNA genes, clusters and promoters with a weighted K-12-to-BL21 transfer score (S_map = 0.45 S_signature + 0.20 S_ordered + 0.15 S_count + 0.15 I_80 + 0.05 I_200), proposes copy-number reduction or 1-3 substitutions in a 29-bp promoter core against 15/30/50% weakening tiers, and ranks them with a three-model ML ensemble trained on 112,955 measured core promoters from the published MBRS L4 library (Zhou 2025), held out by -35/-10 motif pairs (67,978/7,680/37,297) achieving r = 0.677, MAE = 0.506 on log2(1+strength).","dataviz":"Eighteen numbered model figures that read as a single argument. Aligned multi-track profiles put six normalised structural feature tracks (F1-F6) above the availability-aware composite score so a reader can see which feature drove a peak; priority profiles are paired with an evidence-coverage track underneath so a high score with thin evidence cannot be misread as a finding. Figure 8 is a tunnel-length sensitivity heatmap (rows = assumed exit-tunnel length 30-40 codons, columns = codon position, fixed 0-100 teal scale, light-grey cells for genuinely unavailable data rather than zero) so the reader can watch high-priority bands migrate as an assumption changes. Figure 11 is a codon-level audit matrix (18 homolog rows x codons 110-121, green = slow in that species' tRNA context, white = comparable, grey = non-comparable). Figure 14 is a log2(candidate/WT) dwell heatmap restricted to edited positions only, with the x-axis explicitly flagged as non-continuous and the legend explaining that +1 means 2x. Figure 16 is a three-panel dot plot of WT-normalised flux, ribosome load and exposure on matched candidate rows, deliberately NOT collapsed into one score. KMC traces carry +/- SEM bands and separate unblocked residence from blocked waiting. Captions state the direction of preference and whether a quantity is measured or a relative model proxy.","datascience":"Two supervised models were trained from public measurement data and one from their own: an mRNA degradation proxy (D2) on 5,486 curated E. coli half-life records with gene/protein/CDS-grouped splits, a 29-bp promoter-strength ensemble on 112,955 measured core promoters held out by -35/-10 motif pairs (a genuinely out-of-distribution split, r = 0.677), and a codon-occupancy regression on 107,627 codon-level Ribo-seq observations across 125 genes with a documented 14-nt P-site offset, first/last 10 codons excluded, and gene-held-out evaluation (22,025 test observations). The team audits its own provenance in public: several dataset source fields are annotated 'unconfirmed', 'remains to be confirmed' or 'not an attributed published dataset size' rather than quietly cited. Model 4's proL promoter screen predicted 0.727 (native) vs 0.386/0.366/0.358 (P1-P3); the built strain BL21-AlaPro was then measured by Four-Leaf Clover RT-qPCR, showing rpoD-normalised mature tRNA falling from 63.98 to 23.42 (alaXW) and 6.828 to 2.254 (proL), and those measured numbers were returned to M3-A as a new effective-supply table for a full A-E rerun. The headline wet-lab readout: TolB-G-CSF model-designed sequence in the engineered host reached a mean soluble/insoluble ratio of 0.140 vs 0.075 for the commercial sequence in WT host (DnaK-normalised, n = 3 biological replicates, ~1.87-fold). Crucially the team also reports the negative result honestly — for PfCHT1 and leptin, insoluble bands weakened but no clear soluble enrichment appeared, and the results figure is literally titled 'Solubility Gains Remain Unclear'.","software":"catmaOS 4.02.06 ships as a downloadable Windows x64 source release with Setup.cmd/Start.cmd, a pinned requirements-lock.txt, a private .venv, a local browser workspace on 127.0.0.1:8767 (sequences never leave the user's machine), a 28-page PDF user manual, and four precomputed read-only interactive demos embedded in the wiki with JSON/CSV export. Every run emits a reproducibility bundle: request.json (typed input, label, source, parameters), result.json on a versioned schema 1.0.0 with input hash, module status and reproducibility record, curves.csv, metrics.csv, exact_candidates.csv, native/evidence-result.json and run.log, all zipped per run_id. The design rule stated on the page — 'missing values stay missing' and 'a completed run confirms the requested task finished; it does not imply every model was executed' — is the discipline most teams lack.","stack":["Python 3.14","NumPy","SciPy","pandas","Biopython","scikit-learn","Matplotlib","DSSP","MAFFT L-INS-i","PAL2NAL","ViennaRNA / RNAplfold","AGGRESCAN","GtRNAdb / tRNAscan-SE","NSGA-II","custom RFMEO solver","custom Gillespie KMC"],"techniques":["co-translational folding free-energy model","structural bioinformatics / DSSP feature fusion","exit-tunnel coordinate mapping with sensitivity scan","comparative genomics codon alignment","tRNA adaptation index-style supply model","Henikoff sequence weighting","ODE/steady-state ribosome flow model (RFMEO)","stochastic Gillespie kinetic Monte Carlo TASEP with extended objects","finite-state Markov folding-pathway propagation","steady-state mRNA mass balance","multi-objective constrained Pareto optimisation","NSGA-II genetic algorithm","machine-learning regression (promoter strength, mRNA decay, codon occupancy)","out-of-distribution held-out validation","ablation studies","backend cross-validation","parameter-scenario robustness analysis","model-to-measurement feedback loop"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Best Education winner (Undergraduate). The team built an explicit education framework rather than a list of events: a four-tier 'Lifelong Learning Pathway' (interest inspiration, knowledge building, interdisciplinary co-creation, professional impact) that replaces age-banding with depth of engagement, crossed with a four-stage activity structure (Reach, Construct, Dialogue, Extension) and Gardner's theory of multiple intelligences as the design lens for choosing an activity format. They reached over 2,000 people through 21 activities — workshops, exhibitions and the Learning Mall Premium online platform — and produced 18 reusable education toolkits, with the Extension stage deliberately designed so each activity leaves behind a game, handbook or toolkit rather than ending at the event.","ihp":"A CARE framework distilled from stakeholder conversations: Create Accessibility, Accountable Storytelling, Real-world Alignment, Ethical Design. It originated in a question from Dr Lanlan Han about who actually benefits and how strong the evidence is. The interview timeline is structured as Problem / Design / Scope, and the team documents how it narrowed its own claim: starting from 'boost heterologous protein expression in E. coli', literature and interviews with Dr Antony Kam and industry expert Dr Xijun Piao (CATUG Biotechnology, Suzhou) pushed them to redefine success as soluble usable protein rather than total expression, and to state explicitly that dogmaOS is 'not positioned as a universal protein-production platform'. Mid-stage consultations with Dr Yongtao Zhu, Dr Chun Chan, Prof Sheng Yang (Shanghai Institutes for Biological Sciences, CAS), Prof Zhiliang Lu, Dr Avinash Pandreka (Oxford Suzhou Centre for Advanced Research), Dr Kozo Hamada and Prof Zhongmei Liu (Jiangnan University) convinced them that translation speed alone could not explain folding outcomes, which is what created Model 3 and Model 4.","realWorld":"Target selection was driven by real demand: G-CSF, leptin, PfCHT1 and FGF2 were chosen with researchers and protein-production companies as proteins that are hard to express in E. coli and have clear research or clinical uses spanning public health, rare disease and basic research. The team maps the project to SDGs 3, 9, 12 and 17. Accessibility is the commercial thesis — rather than selling a service, catmaOS is released as free local software so academic labs and iGEM teams with only basic E. coli facilities can do upstream optimisation instead of paying for repeated trial-and-error. Safety is built in: non-pathogenic strains, BSL-1 practice, autoclaved waste, and FLP-FRT removal of the kanamycin cassette after lambda-Red genome editing.","partners":["Xi'an Jiaotong-Liverpool University","CATUG Biotechnology (Suzhou) Co., Ltd.","Shanghai Institutes for Biological Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Oxford Suzhou Centre for Advanced Research","Jiangnan University","GenScript","SnapGene"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/drylab/model/figure-08.svg","caption":"Figure 8: exit-tunnel sensitivity heatmap. Rows are assumed ribosome exit-tunnel lengths of 30-40 codons, columns are G-CSF codon positions, and the fixed 0-100 teal scale shows structural priority. Light-grey cells mark genuinely unavailable structural data rather than a score of zero, so the reader can watch high-priority bands migrate as a modelling assumption changes.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/drylab/model/figure-11.svg","caption":"Figure 11: codon-level audit matrix for reference positions 110-121. Each row is a retained homolog; green marks codons called slow in that species' own tRNA context, white marks other comparable synonymous codons, grey retains non-comparable cells. Positions 115 and 121 were slow in all 18 homologs while 118 was slow in none.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/merge-20261020/catmaos-figure-12.svg","caption":"Figure 12: the full 'how long to pause' panel, linking synonymous candidate generation to intrinsic dwell (M3-A), kinetic Monte Carlo ribosome traffic with SEM bands (M3-B), folding trajectories and contact exposure (M3-C), the mRNA decay proxy (M3-D) and the constrained objective screen (M3-E) in which four of six candidates pass the flux and ribosome-burden gates.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/drylab/model/figure-16.svg","caption":"Figure 16: throughput, ribosome load and exposure trade-offs for wild type plus six synonymous candidates, shown as three WT-normalised panels on matched rows and deliberately not collapsed into a single score. W1-F had flux 0.781 and load 1.079, failing the flux >= 0.95 constraint; W1-S passed on all four objectives and went to the bench.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Pair every model score with an explicit evidence-coverage track and run a sensitivity scan over your least-defensible assumption (here, exit-tunnel length), so a figure shows not just the answer but how much of it is real data; and keep competing objectives as separate aligned panels rather than summing them into one invented score. Both are achievable with matplotlib by a motivated 16-year-old and immediately make a model honest.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/measurement/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/education/"],"rank":66},{"team":"ICJFLS","id":"6098","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/icjfls/","projectTitle":"ORA-GUARD","tagline":"An engineered oral probiotic that degrades the sulfur compounds causing halitosis instead of sterilising the mouth","summary":"ICJFLS engineered Streptococcus salivarius K12, a GRAS oral probiotic, to express a Sox enzyme cluster plus an MTO enzyme that catalytically degrade the volatile sulfur compounds (H2S, methanethiol, dimethyl sulfide) responsible for bad breath, positioning it as a Live Biotherapeutic Product rather than a mouthwash. Plasmid expression was abandoned after stability tests showed near-total plasmid loss within five days without selection, so the cassettes were moved into the chromosome by CRISPR-Cas9, and a four-layer biosafety system (D-alanine auxotrophy via alr knockout, a PcspA RNA-thermometer suicide switch, no antibiotic resistance markers) was added in direct response to public fear of live GMO bacteria. The entrepreneurship case is numerically specific, with per-dose cost, retail price, gross margin and a named contract manufacturer. They were nominated for Best Entrepreneurship in the High School section.","awards":["Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"TAM-SAM-SOM with figures carried through to a revenue target, Business Model Canvas, three-tier business model (B2C subscription, B2B2C through dental clinics, technology licensing), cost structure and pricing with gross margin, a capability gap analysis, a 2026-2035 development roadmap, a risk register, and a comparative regulatory table for LBPs across the US, EU and China. Seven named appendices are listed, including a five-year financial projection model and a patent landscape and IP strategy.","dataviz":"Twelve entrepreneurship figures plus six data tables: TAM/SAM/SOM breakdown, business model canvas, revenue projection chart, product-format panel, roadmap, capability-gap chart and the three-jurisdiction LBP regulatory comparison. Survey results are charted as public attitudes, concerns and information needs.","datascience":"A 745-response questionnaire analysed by age, gender and region, with roughly one third of respondents having experienced halitosis, and results segmented into pain points, safety concerns and information needs. In-vitro degradation efficiency in artificial saliva was benchmarked against purified enzymes. Plasmid-stability passaging produced the five-day loss figure that drove the architecture change.","software":"","stack":["S. salivarius K12 chassis","CRISPR-Cas9 genomic integration","Sox enzyme gene cluster","MTO enzyme","PcspA RNA thermometer","artificial saliva in-vitro system"],"techniques":["chromosomal integration instead of plasmid expression","D-alanine auxotrophy by alr knockout","temperature-inducible toxin kill switch","VSC quantification in artificial saliva","plasmid retention passaging assay"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A 745-response online questionnaire spanning ages, genders and regions, surfacing the three questions the public actually asks about live engineered bacteria: will it get out of control in the body, will there be side effects, could it be transmitted to others. They then reported a finding most teams would bury, that patients care about what is in it for me and how it works rather than technical sophistication, and rewrote their communication from 'the technology is advanced' to 'it is safe and effective for you'. They also ran public education activities and attended CCIC, the Conference of China iGEMer Community.","ihp":"An explicit dialogue-reflection-iteration-redialogue loop with six documented stakeholder groups, and in each case the design consequence is named. Public fear turned a single safety mechanism into the four-layer defence system. Practising dentists and an oral microbiology researcher killed the original oral-capsule concept on the grounds that capsules have limited local effect in the mouth, which is what produced the mouthwash, lozenge, chewing gum and toothpaste format set and raised the priority on mucosal adhesion and colonisation. At CCIC, senior teams warned that gene editing in non-model organisms would be a bottleneck, so L. reuteri was adopted as a backup chassis, and they shared kill-switch leakage data that caused the team to drop a high-leakage design for the temperature-inducible one. A bioethics professor and a biosafety policy advisor are the reason plasmid expression was abandoned for CRISPR-Cas9 chromosomal integration and why genome-stability and animal toxicology studies were pre-planned.","realWorld":"This is a genuine business case with real numbers, and it is the unit economics that separate it from the typical canvas exercise. They state a production cost of roughly RMB 0.5 per dose for freeze-dried bacterial powder plus RMB 0.3 for packaging and filling, totalling about RMB 0.8 per dose falling to RMB 0.3-0.5 at volume; retail pricing of RMB 199 for a 30-dose mouthwash bottle and RMB 159 for a 30-tablet box; and a resulting gross margin of 70-80 percent benchmarked against high-end probiotics. The SOM is built bottom-up rather than taken as a share of the TAM: 100,000 halitosis sufferers at an average RMB 200 per month giving roughly RMB 20 million (about USD 2.8 million) in year one or two, through Tmall, JD.com, Douyin and dental-clinic channels. Market research is partly real and partly desk work: the 745-response questionnaire and dentist interviews are theirs, while the USD 13.44 billion global halitosis market and the RMB 77.9 billion China oral-care figure come from Insight Partners, Frost and Sullivan and similar reports. The competitive analysis is specific and fair, naming BLIS Technologies' BLIS K12 and correctly observing that its deodorising mechanism is indirect because it works through salivaricin bacteriocins, whereas ORA-GUARD catalytically destroys the VSC molecules. The regulatory analysis is the strongest part and is genuinely credible: they identify that NMPA's 2020 biological-products registration classification treats LBPs as therapeutic biological products containing microorganisms, note honestly that neither NMPA nor the FDA has yet issued complete LBP-specific technical guidance, and draw the correct strategic conclusion that this is both policy risk and a chance to help set the standard, with early regulator engagement as the stated tactic. Partners are named rather than implied: Shandong Bona Technology Group for probiotic fermentation and freeze-drying, with industrialisation discussions already begun, and Shijia Doctor Dental Hospital as a clinical and channel partner. Be blunt about the gaps, though. There is no registered company, no incubator place, no grant and no investor commitment; investment partners are described only as a category ('biotechnology VCs and oral care industry funds'). The IP position is weaker than it first reads: the strain construction strategy and genetic circuits are said to form technical barriers that are 'planned to be protected through patent applications', so nothing is filed. The revenue projection figure is labelled as internal estimates, and the appendices (five-year financial model, patent landscape) are listed as titles rather than linked documents, so the deepest financial work is asserted rather than shown. Continuation is a licensing-and-partnering intent rather than a funded plan, and an LBP approval route of that length will outlast any student team unless the Bona relationship becomes a formal transfer.","partners":["Shandong Bona Technology Group Co., Ltd. (probiotic fermentation, freeze-drying, technology transfer and industrialisation discussions)","Shijia Doctor Dental Hospital and dental clinics (clinical trials and channel)","Practising dentists and an oral microbiology researcher (dosage form and colonisation advice)","A bioethics professor and a biosafety policy advisor","CCIC (Conference of China iGEMer Community) experts and past award-winning teams","University synthetic biology laboratories"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6098/wiki/entrepre/fig-3-entrepre.avif","caption":"TAM, SAM and SOM analysis for ORA-GUARD, with the SOM built bottom-up from 100,000 target users at RMB 200 per month","credit":"Team ICJFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6098/wiki/entrepre/fig-11-entrepre.avif","caption":"Comparative overview of Live Biotherapeutic Product regulatory pathways in the US, EU and China","credit":"Team ICJFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6098/wiki/entrepre/fig-10-entrepre10.avif","caption":"ORA-GUARD development roadmap from 2026 proof of concept to 2035","credit":"Team ICJFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6098/wiki/entrepre/fig-12-entrepre.avif","caption":"Gap analysis between current team capabilities and the capabilities needed to commercialise ORA-GUARD","credit":"Team ICJFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Insist on three numbers before any market slide: cost per dose, price per unit and resulting gross margin, built from the actual process (freeze-drying plus fill-and-finish) rather than from a market-report percentage. And copy their SOM discipline, where the obtainable market is users times spend per user per month, not a fraction of a global TAM.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/icjfls/","https://2026.igem.wiki/icjfls/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/icjfls/human-practices/"],"rank":7},{"team":"ZJU-China","id":"6102","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/zju-china/","projectTitle":"NEST","tagline":"A sequence-defined SELP hydrogel with a SnoopTag2 plug socket, so one cartilage scaffold can be reconfigured by swapping functional peptides","summary":"ZJU-China built NEST, a silk-elastin-like protein (SELP) hydrogel carrying SnoopTag2 as a standardised ligation interface, so that repair peptides (Link-N, a chondroitin-sulfate-retaining CPC module, a magnetically responsive anisotropy module, a mitochondrial delivery module) can be attached without redesigning the material. They engineered a SnoopLigase2-ZJU variant for the ligation chemistry, purified SELP by inverse transition cycling optimised to 65 degrees C in PBS with 2 M NaCl, and validated GAG binding by AviTag-BirA site-specific biotinylation onto streptavidin beads with DMMB quantification. The commercial case is a full process-economics build rather than a canvas, with equipment, land, working capital, medium, IPTG, kanamycin, labour by shift and waste volumes all costed, plus a 2026-2042 stage-gated roadmap and a ten-year discounted cash-flow model. They were nominated for Best Entrepreneurship (Undergrad) and Best Biomanufacturing.","awards":["Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Biomanufacturing Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"TAM-SAM-SOM with a documented estimation basis and a stated conversion coefficient from osteoarthritis prevalence to the cartilage-repair addressable population, three-tier competitor analysis, NREL-style techno-economic cost structure (fixed capital investment, land, working capital, variable and fixed operating costs, annual capex), a ten-year cash-flow and NPV model, a Sustainable Business Model Canvas, a tiered critical/high risk register tied to stage-gate decisions, a staged financing plan from angel through Series B, a skill-gap analysis across eight capability areas, and a TCFD-shaped ESG framework (governance, strategy, risk and opportunity, metrics and targets).","dataviz":"Nine entrepreneurship figures: SAM and SOM projections, a three-panel budget classification and estimation-basis summary, the 2026-2042 roadmap across R&D, manufacturing, regulatory, fundraising and commercialisation lanes, a ten-year cash-flow chart distinguishing inflows, outflows, net and discounted cash flows, the business model canvas, a skills radar, and three competition-award photographs. A downloadable NEST cost sheet PDF carries the underlying calculations, and five per-module notebook PDFs back the wet-lab work.","datascience":"The costing is done from first principles rather than from benchmarks. Required SELP output for the initial market is derived first, then equipment is sized against it, giving major equipment of CNY 7.45 million (about USD 1.11 million) estimated with input from Beijing Weixinkang Medical. Consumables are itemised: roughly CNY 290,700 per year for the three main LB components, 7.47 kg of IPTG and 1.57 kg of kanamycin annually, plus PBS and sodium chloride for purification, and about 31,360 L of spent culture medium as annual waste. Labour is modelled as a sum over operations of fully loaded hourly cost, with day and night shifts priced separately against actual Zhejiang biomanufacturing job adverts (CNY 6,000-8,000 per month, conservatively the low end) and Hangzhou cleaner salaries, totalling about CNY 2.6603 million per year. The cash-flow model uses a 10 percent real cost of capital, a 13.27 percent revenue CAGR from a cartilage-repair market report and 2 percent annual cost inflation at a fixed USD 1 = CNY 6.72.","software":"","stack":["E. coli BL21(DE3)","SELP (silk-elastin-like protein) inverse transition cycling","SnoopTag2 / SnoopLigase2-ZJU","AviTag-BirA biotinylation","streptavidin magnetic beads","DMMB GAG assay","Fe3O4 magnetic nanoparticles with DogTag-TVN","DOPE/DOTAP/DSPE-PEG-Mal liposomes","confocal microscopy and TEM"],"techniques":["modular peptide-peptide ligation as a product interface","temperature and salt-optimised ITC purification (65 C, 2 M NaCl)","site-specific biotinylation for immobilised binding assays","TEV and ULP1 protease processing","liposome-mitochondria complex assembly and uptake","lyophilisation and hydrogel re-formation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A committed rather than rhetorical plan: during R&D they intend to spend at least CNY 50,000 annually on patient education, public lectures and open science materials, support SynEduGlobal for synthetic-biology education, establish a charitable fund for rural medical outreach and cartilage-health awareness, and continue working with Non-place Studio to use art to build understanding of synthetic biology and empathy for people with cartilage injuries. They also name limited public understanding of hydrogel therapy as a genuine commercial risk to patient confidence and access, not just a communications gap.","ihp":"Their iHP is organised as design-build-test-learn cycles, with an Implementation stage split into hardware, industrial production and entrepreneurship cycles. The entrepreneurship cycle consulted stakeholders across manufacturing, sales, government regulation, finance and entrepreneurship support, and the documented consequence is specific: they gave cost greater weight in the material design, built the detailed business model, went looking for a CDMO partner, and secured a first expression of investment interest. Clinicians fed back that the material's complexity could complicate market approval, which pushed attention toward regulatory simplicity. Professor Erik Forsberg guided the cash-flow and NPV analysis, and Beijing Weixinkang Medical supplied the equipment cost estimates, so industry input is embedded in the numbers rather than quoted alongside them.","realWorld":"A genuinely serious venture attempt with one glaring soft spot. The strong parts first. The business model is a phased B2B: research partnerships and technology-development licensing early to build relationships and validate demand, then supply of implant material and delivery solutions to clinical institutions, with orthopaedic companies and hospitals as customers and patients explicitly identified as beneficiaries rather than initial sales targets. Research grants and equity are accounted separately from operating income, which is a distinction most teams blur. The MVP is honestly narrow, the SELP-SnoopTag2 hydrogel alone, with production and storage stability already demonstrated in the lab and the remaining path stated plainly: classification confirmation, product testing, animal studies, clinical trials, NMPA registration, and a medical-device manufacturing licence for the manufacturer, expected to take ten years. The first indication is focal knee-cartilage defects in China, chosen for surgical volume and clinical-evidence base. The cost model is the standout: equipment, land, working capital, itemised media and reagents, shift-differentiated labour, and waste volumes, published as a downloadable cost sheet. Financing is staged and concrete, with real named programmes and amounts: Hangzhou's 5050 Plan (CNY 1 million start-up, up to CNY 10 million in R&D grants), up to CNY 6 million for a first Class III registration manufactured in Hangzhou via Hangzhou Medical Port, up to USD 500,000 from the GenScript Life Science Research Fund, and crucially a named investor, Ms. Zhang Jie of Huadan Angel, who in a stakeholder interview expressed willingness to discuss roughly CNY 1.5 million after first-round samples meet expectations. Industrial relationships are specific and functional rather than decorative: Weixinkang and Lanyiwei on Class III manufacturing and market access, Yuanyi Bio on fermentation and purification, Yiru Bio on scale-up, Shanghai Lingjiu Medical as a possible CDMO, WuXi AppTec's device-testing centre for registration testing, and Evonik, Arthrex and Smith+Nephew as international targets. They won Gold at Zhejiang University's Dandelion Student Innovation Competition (with prize money) and two Golds at SynBio Challenges 2026, in both the main and the industry track, the latter for the SnoopLigase2-ZJU enzyme system, which is independent validation outside iGEM. IP is planned with real specificity: a Chinese invention patent to be filed in November 2026 covering the SELP-SnoopTag2 structure, manufacturing and purification methods, hydrogel formulation, crosslinking system and peptide attachment, with a prior-art search on SnoopTag2, SELP and Ru/SPS crosslinking before any public data release, and the correct observation that treatment methods are not patentable in China so claims must sit on products and manufacturing. The roadmap is stage-gated across 2026-2042 with a criterion and a required-resources list for each window, and they say outright that they will not assign a break-even year or market-share forecast without a defensible financial model. Now the blunt part. That restraint is contradicted by the headline number: a ten-year NPV of about USD 427 million on an initial investment of about USD 8.69 million, produced by assuming NEST captures 10, 25, 50, 75 and then 100 percent of its SOM in years one to five and holds 100 percent of SOM for years six to ten. Capturing the entire serviceable obtainable market and keeping it, for a Class III implant that by their own roadmap will not be registered until roughly 2037, is not a scenario; it is an upper bound, and they label it only as 'favorable assumptions'. There is also no incorporated entity (the name NexCode Biotechnologies appears but no registration is claimed), no filed patent yet, no signed investment, and no animal data at all, which they state clearly as a limitation on any safety or efficacy claim. The honest reading is that the cost side of this model is excellent and the revenue side is aspirational.","partners":["Beijing Weixinkang Medical Co., Ltd. (Class III manufacturing, sales qualification, market access; equipment cost estimates)","Lanyiwei (Class III medical-device manufacturing and market access)","Yuanyi Bio (synthetic-biology production, fermentation, purification)","Yiru Bio (entrepreneurship and scale-up experience)","Shanghai Lingjiu Medical Technology (biomedical materials, prospective CDMO)","Huadan Angel (Ms. Zhang Jie; expressed willingness to discuss ~CNY 1.5 million early investment)","Professor Erik Forsberg (cash-flow and NPV methodology)","WuXi AppTec medical-device testing centre (prospective registration testing)","Zhejiang University School of Innovation and Entrepreneurship (Dandelion competition)","Non-place Studio (art and public understanding), SynEduGlobal (education)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6102/wiki/images/hp/entrepreneurship/003.avif","caption":"NEST development roadmap and milestones for R&D, manufacturing, regulatory affairs, fundraising and commercialisation, 2026-2042","credit":"Team ZJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6102/wiki/images/hp/entrepreneurship/004.avif","caption":"Ten-year cash-flow analysis separating inflows, outflows, net and discounted cash flows at a 10 percent real cost of capital","credit":"Team ZJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6102/wiki/images/hp/entrepreneurship/005.avif","caption":"NEST sustainable business model canvas for the phased B2B licensing-then-supply model","credit":"Team ZJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6102/wiki/images/hp/entrepreneurship/009.avif","caption":"Gold Award for the SnoopLigase2-ZJU enzyme system in the industry track of SynBio Challenges 2026","credit":"Team ZJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy their bottom-up cost sheet: size the required output first, then let equipment, media, IPTG and antibiotic quantities, shift-differentiated labour rates taken from real local job adverts, and annual waste volumes fall out of it, and publish the whole thing as a downloadable file. Then apply the discipline they stated but did not follow on their own revenue line: never model capturing 100 percent of a serviceable obtainable market, and label any such figure as an upper bound rather than a forecast.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/zju-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/zju-china/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/zju-china/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/zju-china/assets/wiki-Bwf3O9W2.js","https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6102/wiki/hp-file/nest-cost-sheet.pdf"],"rank":14},{"team":"GU Frankfurt","id":"6108","section":"Overgrad","country":"Germany","village":"Therapeutics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/","projectTitle":"HIDE","tagline":"Hiding a transplanted organ from the immune system instead of suppressing the immune system","summary":"Around 183,000 transplants a year are followed by lifelong immunosuppression, which protects the graft by weakening the whole patient. HIDE attacks the cause instead: a synNotch receptor carrying a computationally designed binder recognises the donor organ's mismatched HLA class I alleles, triggers production and export of shRNA in extracellular vesicles, and silences only those 'non-self' HLAs by RNA interference, leaving the rest of the immune system intact. Three research fields are bridged at once (synNotch, RNAi, exosome delivery), with the binder and the shRNA both designed in silico by the dry lab and validated by FACS, qPCR, western blot and nanoparticle tracking analysis. It won Best Part Collection and Best Education because the by-products are as usable as the therapy: HUSH, an open-source RNAi design framework for polymorphic gene families, and TRUST, a framework for communicating about death and grief that iGEM did not previously have.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Part Collection — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Software — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Education — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Therapeutics Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two pipelines solving an allele-discrimination problem that has no off-the-shelf method. For the binder, the team deliberately picked the harder target: HLA-A*01 (allele frequency around 15%) rather than the more common HLA-A*02, because A*01 carries fewer polymorphisms relative to other common alleles and therefore makes a specific binder harder to design; an IPD-IMGT multiple sequence alignment of HLA-A*01:01:01:01 against A*02:01:01:01 and A*03:01:01:01 located the usable mismatches, concentrated in and around the peptide-binding groove of the polymorphic alpha1 and alpha2 domains. They then generated roughly 2,000 de novo binders with BindCraft and RFdiffusion3, scored affinity and specificity against target and off-target HLAs with Boltz-2 and AlphaFold, and carried the eight most promising designs into molecular dynamics simulations for stability, across six design batches with combined MD results. For the shRNA, sequence alignment identified nucleotide-level discriminating positions, candidates were designed and evaluated with siDirect, and every candidate was screened against the human genome for off-targets in other human mRNAs. HUSH then added 150 further shRNAs with alignment-aware targeting.","dataviz":"The wiki is built as an explorable system rather than a document. The dry-lab overview is a hover-and-click hitbox diagram where each component (Binder, shRNA, HUSH) reveals its rationale and links through to its own results page. The HLA class I architecture is a draggable 3D structure coloured by domain (alpha1, alpha2, alpha3, beta2m) so the reader can see for themselves that the variation sits in the groove. The MSA is shown in classic dash notation against the A*01 reference so each mismatch is countable. MD runs are published as per-binder, per-allele animations (for example binder FR57 against HLA-A*01, A*02 and A*03 side by side), which makes specificity visible rather than tabulated. The HUSH page embeds a working mockup of the five-stage wizard with sample data that the reader can step through, and the mascot Heidi narrates each step. Human practices is an interactive stakeholder map filterable by affiliation and by part of the construct, so a reader can ask 'who shaped the binder?' and get an answer. Education statistics are presented as before-and-after pairs, not totals.","datascience":"HUSH exists because the team audited the tool landscape and found it stagnant: commercial RNAi designers are proprietary black boxes whose algorithms and option sets are undocumented, and even non-commercial siDirect is neither open-source nor pipeline-integrable, while recent published advances for discriminating highly similar sequences had been implemented nowhere. HUSH therefore folds multiple sequence alignment into the design step itself: a target sequence plus an optional panel of related sequences (the alleles to spare) switches on allele-aware targeting and an MSA stage, and the tool inspects the input up front (molecule type, length, GC content, panel size) and warns, for example, that a GC-rich target may cause the seed filter to reject every candidate, then offers to relax that filter in the criteria step. Protein input is flagged because it must be resolved to a transcript first. Safety is a scoring stage, not a disclaimer: candidates are aligned with Bowtie against NCBI human RefSeq, and the team proposes extending the standard off-target check to known single-nucleotide polymorphisms that could create unwanted off-targets. Designed siRNAs convert to shRNAs with hairpin-loop guidance and thermodynamic evaluation of structure integrity.","software":"HUSH (Human genome-Universal Silencing Hairpin designer), open-source on GitLab, ships in four interfaces around one core HushSession object: a guided five-step web wizard plus a compact expert mode exposing thermodynamic constants and selection rules, a CLI for one-shot pipeline use, a persistent in-terminal shell with saved input and panels, and an MCP server so an AI agent can drive it. A REST API provides compatibility for external pipelines. The three stated design tenets are research-first (every option documented with the research behind it), accessibility (upload a sequence and be guided, with proposed optimal choices derived from the sequence), and safety. The team also released START (SynBio Travellers, an educational browser game) and TRUST (a sensitive-topics framework), and the wiki repository is gitlab.igem.org/2026/gu-frankfurt.","stack":["BindCraft","RFdiffusion3","Boltz-2","AlphaFold","molecular dynamics simulation","siDirect","Bowtie","NCBI RefSeq","IPD-IMGT/HLA alignment tool","GitLab (open source)","REST API","MCP server","CLI + terminal shell + web wizard"],"techniques":["de novo protein binder generation at ~2000-candidate scale","affinity and specificity co-scoring against off-target alleles","MD stability triage of top candidates","multiple sequence alignment driven allele discrimination","genome-wide off-target screening","SNP-aware off-target proposal","shRNA hairpin thermodynamic evaluation","synNotch receptor engineering","extracellular-vesicle RNA delivery","qPCR, FACS, western blot, nanoparticle tracking analysis readouts"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A five-media strategy (game, workshops, social dialogue, print, audio) built on training the team first: 19 members took the 'Basics of Science Communication' workshop from NaWik, the German National Institute for Science Communication, and used its core-message, cloverleaf and arrow tools as the basis of everything after. Outputs were then measured, not just counted: nine school workshops at two schools reached over 140 students, with the share who knew that the family decides when no donation wish is documented rising from 66.1% to 91.9%, and 103 of 147 students saying they would talk to others (counted conservatively at four people each as a take-home estimate). HIDE & SEEK, a printed educational magazine distributed in pharmacies, doctors' practices and gyms, raised average correct answers across four knowledge questions from 88% to 100%. They ran a Night of Science survey booth, presented HIDE on hr3 radio (about one million daily listeners), were featured in BIOspektrum, UniReport and Wissen wird, appeared on iGEM Lund's podcast 'The Sequence', built the START browser game, and distributed hundreds of organ donation cards.","ihp":"50 stakeholders in 44 interviews across six countries, sorted into five functions (defining the need, designing the therapy, route to the clinic, the donation debate, sensitive communication and education) and mapped against the part of the construct each one shaped. Documented changes: DSO and Eurotransplant quantified the logistics and donor shortage that justify the project; Prof. Urs Christen's account of lifelong immunosuppression side effects pushed the team toward a personalised approach; Dr. Venugopal Gudipati made binder accuracy the governing constraint on the receptor arm, and Dr. Gregory Allen shaped the synNotch design; CAR-T experts set the manufacturing, logistics and cost frame for an autologous therapy. In the donation debate the team interviewed politicians on both sides (Dr. Armin Grau and Helge Lindh), found both agreed the real deficit is education, and wrote a political proposal to make their workshops a mandatory part of school curricula, presented on 22 September 2026 to the cross-party Bundestag group 'Freiwilligkeit in der Organspende', which agreed to consider it. The largest change was procedural: interviewing people with lived transplant experience showed the team that iGEM has biosafety guidance but nothing on sensitive topics, which iGEM's own Human Practices committee confirmed when asked, so they built TRUST (Talking Responsibly and Understanding Sensitive Topics) with Dr. Katharina Karcher of the MASTERY project at Birmingham, transplant psychologist Dr. Angela Buchholz (who showed that unfamiliar language creates fear rather than understanding), supervisor Ariane Mantey-Strauss, ethicist Dr. Timo Sauer, nurse Sanjin Krupic, DSO's PD Dr. Ana Paula Barreiros, and former iGEM Safety and Security chief Alonso Flores, who pushed back that no framework can move responsibility off the teams themselves.","realWorld":"The commercialisation strategy is an exclusive licensing agreement: HIDE retains the intellectual property while a large pharmaceutical partner takes manufacturing, distribution and payer negotiation, chosen because an autologous cell therapy's facility, consumable and specialist-salary costs cannot be matched by a start-up. Miltenyi Biotec has issued a letter of support. The analysis names the customer chain (pharmaceutical companies, statutory health insurers, transplant centres) rather than patients, because an advanced cell therapy cannot be sold direct, and works through German reimbursement specifically: near-universal statutory coverage funded at 14.6% of income, with price set by negotiation with the manufacturer. The page carries cost of goods per dose, a seven-year forecast, a regulatory roadmap with milestones and risks, a Business Model Canvas, SWOT and skill-gap analyses the team describes as an honest look at their own gaps, a PEST analysis, and a mapping to the UN Sustainable Development Goals. Input came from the iGEM Entrepreneurship Bootcamp, Innovectis and the Charite Berlin Regulatory Support Unit.","partners":["Miltenyi Biotec (letter of support)","NaWik, National Institute for Science Communication","Deutsche Stiftung Organtransplantation (DSO)","Eurotransplant","Cross-party Bundestag group 'Freiwilligkeit in der Organspende'","Dr. Katharina Karcher, MASTERY, University of Birmingham","Dr. Angela Buchholz, University Hospital Hamburg-Eppendorf","Alonso Flores, former iGEM Safety and Security Programme Chief","Innovectis (Goethe University technology transfer)","Regulatory Support Unit, Charite Berlin","Prof. Rainer Blasczyk, Hannover Medical School","Prof. Klemens Budde, Charite","MD PhD Gregory Allen","Prof. Jens Kurreck, TU Berlin","iGEM Lund ('The Sequence' podcast)","hr3 (Hessischer Rundfunk)","BIOspektrum"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6108/wiki/images/drylab/hush/hush-1-input-page.avif","caption":"HUSH step one: the target sequence to silence plus an optional panel of related alleles to spare, with live input inspection reporting molecule type, length, GC content and readiness, and warning when a GC-rich target may defeat the seed filter.","credit":"Team GU Frankfurt, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6108/wiki/images/drylab/hush/hush-architecture.svg","caption":"HUSH architecture: one central HushSession interface serving the web wizard, the CLI, the terminal shell and the MCP server, so the same design logic is reachable by a newcomer, a pipeline or an AI agent.","credit":"Team GU Frankfurt, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6108/wiki/images/wetlab/wetlab-figures-fixed/synnotch/fig-19-fr57-facs.svg","caption":"FACS characterisation of the designed binder FR57 in the synNotch receptor, testing whether a de novo binder discriminates HLA-A*01 from the near-identical A*02 and A*03 alleles.","credit":"Team GU Frankfurt, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6108/wiki/images/education-page/images-cropped/workshop-figure-1.avif","caption":"One of nine school workshops on organ donation and synthetic biology, measured with before-and-after knowledge questions; the proposal to make these workshops curriculum-mandatory reached the German Bundestag.","credit":"Team GU Frankfurt, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Two things are directly copyable. First, HUSH's premise: when the standard tool for your problem is a proprietary web form, the gap itself is a publishable contribution, so build the open version with the alignment step folded in and the safety check scored rather than disclaimed. Second, treat outreach as an experiment with a pre-and-post instrument (66.1% to 91.9% on a single factual question is worth more than 'we reached 500 people'), and build a TRUST-style written protocol before students run any activity touching death, illness or grief.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/","https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/drylab","https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/binder-design","https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/hush","https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/trust","https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/gu-frankfurt/start"],"rank":143},{"team":"TU Delft","id":"6110","section":"Overgrad","country":"Netherlands","village":"Biomanufacturing","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/","projectTitle":"MOSAIC","tagline":"A modular elastin-like polypeptide coating platform that lets one implant surface be tuned to the tissue, and the patient, it sits in.","summary":"MOSAIC is a modular implant coating built from elastin-like polypeptides (ELPs) that can be swapped part by part so a single platform serves many implants and tissues. The team's insight is that an implant coating has to match the stiffness and biochemistry of its target tissue: tissue stiffness spans 0.1 kPa in brain to over 1 MPa in bone, while titanium is around 100 GPa, so a one-size coating gives cells the wrong signal. They built five linked models to steer design before the lab work - an XGBoost predictor of ELP transition temperature, coarse-grained molecular dynamics of the surface-tethered hydrogel, an omics pipeline turning transcriptomic and proteomic atlases into tissue-specific coating recipes, a SuperPro Designer scale-up of ELP manufacturing, and a life-cycle assessment of that process - and they ran Value Sensitive Design across 13 interviews so stakeholders shaped the construct while it could still change. It took the Overgrad Grand Prize along with Best Wiki, Best Integrated Human Practices and Best Biomanufacturing Project.","awards":["Grand Prize Winner (Overgrad)","Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Model — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Wiki — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Biomanufacturing Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Five models, each tied to a design question. (1) LCST prediction: an XGBoost regressor trained on 230 literature-curated entries predicting ELP transition temperature from sequence composition, length, charge, hydrophobicity and solution conditions, with monotonic constraints forcing predicted LCST to fall as NaCl, ELP concentration or hydrophobicity rise, 216-combination exhaustive grid search over six hyperparameters under composition-grouped 5-fold CV, and a held-out set of 43 entries covering only 10 unique sequences; MAE 3.17 C, beating a linear fit of the Meyer-Chilkoti empirical relation, a 5-nearest-neighbour model and a mean baseline. (2) Molecular dynamics: coarse-grained CALVADOS 0.8.0 with CALVADOS 2 residue parameters in OpenMM 8.2 (one bead per residue, implicit solvent, Debye-Huckel screening, 293.15 K, 10 fs timestep, Langevin thermostat) simulating 16-chain surface-tethered ELP brushes on DelftBlue, the TU Delft supercomputer - chosen explicitly because ELPs are intrinsically disordered and AlphaFold is of limited use. Three MD modules resolve hydrogel stiffness (inter-chain lysine contacts as a proxy), crosslink placement (bridges versus wasted intra-chain loops) and RGD motif solvent accessibility, analysed by response surface modelling and per-lysine regression. (3) Omics-guided motif selection: an enhancement-based test across Human Protein Atlas bulk RNA-seq, the Proteome Atlas DIA-MS intensities, Tabula Sapiens single-cell via CZ CELLxGENE, and GEO datasets (GSE104782, GSE169396, GSE147287, GSE114007), annotated by matrisome, integrin and protease category, with per-dataset thresholds (HPA: >4x the mean of other tissues above 1 nTPM, plus stricter floors of 10 nTPM for ECM ligands and 3 nTPM for integrins) producing an ECM adhesion domain, an integrin target and a targetable protease per implant tissue. (4) Large-scale production: a pilot bioprocess in SuperPro Designer with three consecutive reactors reaching 5.11 g/L ELP, sized to coat 117 million and 22 million continuous glucose monitors, with full catabolic, anabolic and glycerol metabolic balances, downstream ITC/salting-out, homogenisation, flocculation, ion exchange and lyophilisation. (5) Environmental impact: an ISO 14040-style LCA of that flowsheet expressed in eco-costs across 14 categories (acidification, water stress, biodiversity and land-use change, carbon footprint, freshwater ecotoxicity, eutrophication, human toxicity cancer and non-cancer, landfill, metals scarcity, particulate matter, photochemical ozone, plastic soup, resource use), framed against the ESPR and the fact that wearables emit 1.1-6.1 kg CO2e per device.","dataviz":"Best Wiki winner, and the mechanics are worth copying: the wiki is authored in Markdown with structured components (Image with named figure references, FigNum auto-numbering, Collapsable deep-dive blocks, Reference by DOI) and it publishes a machine-readable mirror - every page is served as plain .md at /tu-delft/<page>.md with an llms.txt index, so the whole wiki is readable without JavaScript. The MOSAIC Designer ships as a live four-step in-browser app (annotate, codon-shuffle, swap, assemble-and-export). The clearest single visual argument is a pair of dot plots of the same six-VPGVG protein: one codon per amino acid leaves 190 repeated 8 nt stretches as diagonal stripes, the scrambled spelling leaves 3. The scale-up model is presented as an interactive flowsheet where clicking any equipment unit opens its specifications and stream details, and the entrepreneurship page carries a BOM Sankey, a company-analysis matrix and a CGM signal/lag model.","datascience":"Their data engineering is as careful as the modelling. For LCST they searched 86 candidate sources (71 papers or reviews, 15 patents) and hand-extracted sequence, transition temperature, ELP-to-buffer concentration, buffer type, pH and added NaCl; where values existed only as published figures they built their own browser-based LCST Extractor that calibrates axes against tick marks, lets the user trace points along a curve, smooths with LOESS and takes the transition temperature from the steepest change in absorbance - a reproducible digitisation step instead of visual estimation. They treat two rows as the same ELP when amino acid composition matches and keep all rows of one ELP in the same fold, which is an explicit leakage guard, and they document the limitation that composition-only features cannot distinguish block from alternating arrangements of the same residues. The omics pipeline is the other half: it converts public transcriptomic and proteomic atlases into concrete, orderable sequence recipes per tissue, with documented fallback motifs when no tissue signature resolves.","software":"Four tools on iGEM GitLab. MOSAIC ELP Designer (https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/tu-delft/mosaic-elp-designer) solves the problem that repetitive ELP genes are near-impossible to synthesise: it annotates a pasted protein into parts and linkers, codon-scrambles the parts using a De Bruijn-window repeat check (default k = 3 codons, repeats counted from 6 to 18 nt, homopolymer rule on, BsaI/NdeI/BseRI/BamHI/XbaI excluded on both strands), designs compatible Golden Gate cut sites in the locked linkers so every new part combination assembles with the same overhangs, checks synthesisability, and exports order-ready library sequences; it runs as a web app at https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/designer. LCST Prediction (https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/tu-delft/lcst-prediction) is a Python pipeline with clean_data.ipynb, features.py, train_model.py, test_model.py, run_pipeline.py and predict.py, and bundles the LCST Extractor digitiser under tools/curve_extract. Molecular Dynamics Simulator (https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/tu-delft/molecular-dynamics-simulator) wraps the CALVADOS/OpenMM brush setup and per-module analysis. Tissue-Specific ELP Design (https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/tu-delft/tissue-specific-elp-design) is the omics pipeline. Data deposited at doi:10.5281/zenodo.23532676.","stack":["XGBoost","scikit-learn","Python / Jupyter","CALVADOS 0.8.0 (CALVADOS 2 parameters)","OpenMM 8.2","DelftBlue HPC","SuperPro Designer","Idemat / eco-costs LCA","Human Protein Atlas","Proteome Atlas (DIA-MS)","Tabula Sapiens via CZ CELLxGENE Census","NCBI GEO","De Bruijn-graph codon scrambling","Golden Gate / MoClo assembly design","LOESS smoothing","Markdown + component-based wiki with llms.txt","QCM-D (MVP validation)","iGEM GitLab","Zenodo"],"techniques":["monotonic feature constraints encoding known physics into a tree model","composition-grouped cross-validation as a leakage guard","exhaustive grid search with 5-fold CV on the training split only","benchmarking against a published empirical equation, kNN and a mean baseline","coarse-grained MD of intrinsically disordered proteins","response surface modelling and per-lysine regression on MD trajectories","bridge-versus-loop crosslink partition analysis","solvent-accessible-surface-area motif accessibility","enhancement-based tissue-specificity testing across four omics resolutions","techno-economic process simulation with full metabolic balances","ISO 14040 life-cycle assessment in single-indicator eco-costs","figure digitisation with axis calibration and LOESS","repeat-aware codon optimisation for synthesisable repetitive genes"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Outreach was built as two-way exchange rather than broadcast, and the recurring artefact is the DNA bracelet: visitors translate a DNA sequence to RNA to protein, each protein becoming a differently coloured bead, so they leave with both the concept and the object. They ran it at the Weekend of Science on the TU Delft campus in The Hague (part of a national programme of 2,000 activities in one weekend), at Neighbours' Day in the district where team members live using beads donated by a local artist, and alongside a codon wheel and a 'building blocks' horse-race game. They put a pond scoop next to a microscope and taught children to focus it, letting curiosity do the teaching for the younger visitors who found the implant problem itself uninteresting. Delft Dialogue brought the Human Practices co-lead, team lead and outreach manager on to discuss the technology's implications for medicine. They also hosted a PhD student from the Daan Brinks Lab for a cloning knowledge exchange and met other iGEM teams, noting specifically what they learned from Brussels' bees and Wageningen's PFAS project.","ihp":"Best IHP winner, and the structure is the reason. They used Value Sensitive Design in three explicit passes - conceptual (who MOSAIC affects), empirical (13 semi-structured interviews plus literature) and technical (values translated into design requirements) - adapted the Rathenau Institute's iGEM Guide to the Future into five actor groups (directly affected, those who govern access, industry, those who inform, nature), placed every actor on a power-interest grid using two questions (can this actor formally block market access, and does it have a direct stake), borrowed a 1-3 MCDA scoring scale to extend GRADE, and got formal TU Delft Human Research Ethics Committee approval before any interviews. Requirements they could not meet this year became explicitly labelled Future Perspectives rather than quiet omissions. The concrete changes: a philosopher of technology pointed out that modularity is simultaneously the strength and the regulatory risk, which pushed them to involve patients, clinicians and regulators while the construct was still open and to be wary of words like 'biomimicry' that make people assume safety. Antibiotic-resistance concerns made them drop antibiotics from the antimicrobial module entirely and search the literature for alternatives, landing on antimicrobial peptides. Kirby Lattwein (Erasmus MC, cardiovascular device infections) said their assays would not predict real-world performance, so they switched to clinically relevant strains and media and ran the AMP work in her lab on three clinical isolates from LVAD driveline infections plus two reference strains, and added LVAD drivelines as a use case. Surgeon Robin Atallah told them every minute in the operating room costs 50-70 euros and that surgeons resist deviating from routine, which pushed them toward a manufacturer-pre-coated implant rather than an in-theatre paste. Ambiguity about protein coatings in permanent blood contact made them set blood-contacting implants aside and focus on tissue-embedded and skin-crossing implants. Amir Zadpoor's point that loosening is a major failure mode drove the omics-guided motif model. A CGM industry interviewee's distinction between sensor and adhesive focused the MVP on the inserted sensor and his warning that manufacturers have little incentive to reduce sensor replacement sales sent the entrepreneurship team back to the business model.","realWorld":"The MVP is deliberately the hardest possible test rather than the best-performing coating: a single construct, (VPGIG)23-VPGKG-LDASTVYAVTGRGDSPASSAASA-(VPGIG)24, with one lysine placed directly beside the RGD domain mid-chain, bonded through that lysine to a polydopamine-primed CGM polyurethane membrane, validated by QCM-D for attachment and a cell-adhesion assay against scrambled-RDG and lysine-free controls on the same purified batch. Commercially they chose continuous glucose monitors after a regulatory-driven application screen: they studied EU MDR and FDA pathways in detail (notified body versus 510(k)/PMA, Rule 14 class III escalation when a coating carries an active substance, Annex IX 4.10 review for changes to an approved device) and let that analysis eliminate candidates. The business model is ELP supply paid as a royalty per coated sensor, set as a share of the partner's net price per sensor, which they justify as how medical-technology licences are normally priced, with contracts structured to limit risk. They ran TAM/SAM/SOM, Porter's Five Forces, SWOT with explicit strategies, CGM market consolidation and GLP-1 agonist trend analysis, and three competitor case studies (LipoCoat, Surmodics, Bactiguard). Scale-up is costed for 117 million and 22 million CGMs per year with full technical and financial KPIs, backed by the LCA. IP is mapped as US and European ELP patent landscapes against TU Delft's ownership policy, with an explicit discussion of IP versus open-source science. A 3D-printed-organ bioink route is flagged as future work, since ELPs phase-transition reversibly above a set temperature.","partners":["Kirby Lattwein, Erasmus Medical Center (cardiovascular device infections; hosted the AMP assays)","Dr. Robin Atallah (bone-anchored prostheses surgeon)","Prof. Amir Zadpoor, TU Delft (implant loosening)","Prof. Gijsje Koenderink, TU Delft","Dirk Eijkel","Luca Hodes (ELP functionalisation / bioink idea)","Nikolas Gaio and Tim Bruines (industry interviews)","Daan Brinks Lab, TU Delft","TU Delft Human Research Ethics Committee","Rathenau Institute (iGEM Guide to the Future framework)","DHPC DelftBlue supercomputer"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6110/wiki/software/workflow.avif","caption":"What the MOSAIC Designer takes over: from a researcher's amino acid sequence through part identification, codon scrambling, Golden Gate cut-site design and synthesis feasibility checks to order-ready library sequences.","credit":"Team TU Delft, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6110/wiki/software/annotate.avif","caption":"The MOSAIC Designer annotation step in the browser: a pasted sequence split into parts and linkers, each listed with its range, length and sequence, auto-filled from the detected repeat motif.","credit":"Team TU Delft, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6110/wiki/model/bioinformatics-pipeline-blockscheme.avif","caption":"The omics-guided motif selection pipeline: public transcriptomic and proteomic data filtered to implant-relevant tissues, annotated by matrisome, integrin and protease category, and reduced by an enhancement test to a per-tissue coating recipe.","credit":"Team TU Delft, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6110/wiki/outreach/bracelets/workshop-preview.avif","caption":"The DNA bracelet workshop, run at the Weekend of Science and Neighbours' Day: visitors translate DNA to RNA to protein, with each protein becoming a coloured bead they take home.","credit":"Team TU Delft, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Adopt their two cheap, high-leverage habits. First, build known physics into a small model instead of hoping it learns it: monotonic constraints on NaCl, concentration and hydrophobicity let 230 hand-curated data points beat a published empirical equation, which is exactly the regime a school lab works in. Second, make the wiki machine-readable - authoring pages as Markdown and publishing them at <page>.md with an llms.txt index cost them almost nothing and made the Best Wiki winner fully legible to any reader or tool, including judges skimming on a bad connection.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/llms.txt","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/description.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/model.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/software.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/results.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/engineering.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/human-practices.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/entrepreneurship.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/outreach.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/contribution.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/parts.md","https://2026.igem.wiki/tu-delft/experiments.md"],"rank":289},{"team":"BNDS-China","id":"6115","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/","projectTitle":"OrbitColi","tagline":"An AND-logic-gate engineered probiotic that senses oxidative stress in space and produces vitamin B2 and trehalose to protect astronauts","summary":"BNDS-China built OrbitColi, an engineered E. coli probiotic designed to sense oxidative conditions arising from cosmic radiation and microgravity and respond by producing vitamin B2 and trehalose, replacing fixed-dose supplements that degrade over multi-year missions. The scientific framing is quantitative: ISS crews receive 100-200 mSv per year against a 20 mSv occupational limit, and a three-year Mars round trip may accumulate 300-450 mSv. The team's distinguishing contribution is methodological: they invented and applied a five-step education framework, R.E.A.C.H. (Recognize, Experience, Apply, Communicate, Harvest), to every single outreach activity, with a pre-event diagnostic survey and a matched post-event survey, and they report their own failures against it. Integrated Human Practices used a parallel STAR framework (Scan, Test, Assemble, Reflect) across five cycles.","awards":["Best Education — Nominee (High School)","Best Wiki — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two self-designed frameworks, both documented and reusable: R.E.A.C.H. for education (Recognize the audience's pre-existing attitudes before teaching; Experience through interactive formats; Apply by moving the audience from listeners to designers; Communicate to close the loop by harvesting informed project feedback into Integrated Human Practices; Harvest by quantifying the event's success and planning the next iteration) and STAR for iHP (Scan, Test, Assemble, Reflect) across five cycles: problem validation, end-user and market needs, technical design, biosafety and ethics, and production and final-product development. The REACH structure is published in detailed appendices so other teams can reuse it.","dataviz":"Before-and-after bar charts of self-reported technique familiarity and interest; questionnaire-result figures published for each activity (pre and post); a timeline figure of all educational activities; pre-interview and final stakeholder influence/relevance maps for iHP, with the honest caveat printed on the figure that inclusion in the pre-interview map does not mean an interview took place.","datascience":"Systematic paired pre/post surveying with reported N and percentages, e.g. kindergarten cohort N = 14 matched responses, 21 student athletes aged 10-13 surveyed before and after, 16 valid responses per question from the elderly session, eight older adults aged 68-82 interviewed at the scoping stage, and two school-wide perception surveys on synthetic biology and on attitudes to engineered-bacteria consumption and space microbial applications, all published as downloadable files.","software":"The project includes hardware and software components presented to audiences for feedback; the team learned an AI protein-design workflow from team NBJF (generate structure, fill amino-acid sequence, in-silico screening, wet-lab validation) and explicitly reserved wet-lab capacity because high in-silico scores can lose function in reality.","stack":["R.E.A.C.H. education framework","STAR iHP framework","paired pre/post questionnaires","downloadable lesson slide decks and protocols","AND logic-gate genetic circuit design"],"techniques":["oxidative-stress-responsive promoter sensing","AND logic gate circuit","vitamin B2 and trehalose biosynthesis","protein thermal and radiation resistance engineering","strawberry DNA extraction","fluorescent E. coli painting"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"BNDS-China's practice is distinctive because every activity is written up against the same five-step template, R.E.A.C.H., which forces a pre-event diagnostic, an interactive delivery, a design task, a project-feedback loop and a quantified post-event evaluation - and the team reports honestly where a step was skipped. The highest-value example is a kindergarten programme for pre-literate 5-6 year olds. The Recognize step established through a pre-event survey that children already saw microbes as invisible enemies (hand-washing framing) and that 71.43% preferred interactive games to lectures. The team then rendered BioBrick standard assembly as a colour-coded tactile block system: a blue 'switch' promoter block that must come first, a red 'function' coding-sequence block compared to a light bulb, a yellow 'stop' terminator block compared to a vehicle brake, and a green 'little house' vector and chassis block, with assembly order taught as a chanted mantra ('Flip open the blue switch, let the red function bloom, halt with the yellow brake, and live inside the green room!'). Children worked in pairs and trios called 'syndicates' with visual challenge cards posing local problems (make plants glow to light a path to a playground at night; make a flower that masks the smell of garbage). The cleverest design move: the team deliberately rationed parts, giving one table switches but no coding blocks and the next table the reverse, so children had to discover what they lacked, approach other groups and barter - a lived introduction to the open-source iGEM Registry. A five-act puppet show, 'Microscopic Defense Line', dramatised a grazed arm at cell scale, and deliberately avoided weapons or war imagery: the White Blood Cell puppet hugged the bacterium to represent phagocytosis, two red blood cell actors held hands to bridge a vessel, and platelet actors pressed yellow paper against stretchy cloth to show clotting. Measured results from matched surveys (N = 14): before, 78.57% did not know what it meant to pass a function from one organism to another and 64.29% did not know or only vaguely knew what a gene is; after, 92.86% described synthetic biology as 'building blocks', 92.86% knew the promoter starts expression and 100% knew the coding sequence determines function; 42.86% had thought the body fights invaders like 'little soldiers' and none knew clots form, versus 100% afterwards understanding both white blood cell clearance and platelet repair; 100% enjoyed the activities and 92.86% wanted more science stories. The feedback even changed the project: children asked why immune clearance has to kill ('Can't they just change them? Make them friends?') and worried what happens if the yellow stop block falls off, which the team mapped onto transcriptional runaway, biocontainment and biocontamination and used to question their own antagonistic-clearance therapeutic model. Other formats, each with REACH write-ups: a BioCamp lecture on the whole molecular toolkit (PCR, gel electrophoresis, Southern/Northern/Western blotting, qPCR, rtPCR, FISH, CRISPR-Cas9, gene circuits) where each technique was framed by the question it answers, with the team candidly recording that the Apply and Communicate steps did not happen and that students still found the techniques complicated without hands-on work, and publishing the fix (fewer techniques per session, add pipetting and gel loading, one workflow diagram); a formal debate on 9 March 2026 with the BNDS British Debate Club on whether synthetic biology should modify human traits unrelated to serious disease, supported by a purpose-written Synthetic Bio Debate Information Packet covering definitions, design principles, systems thinking, intervention targets, risks, ethics and governance, with pre- and post-debate questionnaires; a campus art exhibition on environmental protection with a pop-art digital painting and an explanatory signboard, made with a visual-arts student, where the recorded criticism was that the symbols were too abstract; a bilingual comic published in the school newspaper BNDS Review and released as downloadable Chinese and English digital versions; and merchandise-as-conversation-starter at the Hongchuanghui Fair and school carnival, with original E. coli, yeast and budding-yeast cartoon designs on postcards, stickers and acrylic hair clips, where the team explicitly explained which features were biological and which were artistic licence. The inclusivity work is specific about barriers. At Tianyun Rehabilitation Center in Liulihe, Fangshan District, Beijing, working with hearing-impaired children and children with genetic conditions, the team discovered the children had no independent electronic devices and that a conventional questionnaire did not suit their communication needs, so after consulting the centre's teachers they switched to an illustrated storybook, 'Little Chef Mimi and Her Magical Yeast Friends', plus a colouring activity, and relied on the teachers to interpret the children's sign language - concluding that inclusive science education is about making sure every child's voice can be heard, not just making knowledge easier. For children with osteogenesis imperfecta ('porcelain doll' patients), the team first interviewed directors of the Sun and Rain Center rare-disease organisation, who explicitly advised against treating participants as fragile or showing pity; sessions were kept short, and when the team noticed holding pens was physically hard for some children, they abandoned the planned drawing activity mid-session for an open discussion and a 'wishing' segment, then shifted their long-term approach to accessible online workshops and distributing game materials directly to hospitals. For 21 student athletes aged 10-13 with limited access to systematic biology teaching or lab equipment, three experiments (strawberry DNA extraction, saliva pH around exercise, ruler-drop reaction tests) tied biology to their own bodies; afterwards all 21 reported confidence in understanding and completing experiments and all answered the DNA and synthetic-biology questions correctly. A lecture for older adults, built on scoping interviews with eight people aged 68-82 whose concerns were medical safety, side effects and the reliability of health information, used genetically modified food, vaccines and microbial insulin as familiar anchors, taught how to spot misleading health advertising and how AI can help check claims, and included making DNA-themed bracelets and illustrated tote bags carrying health-scam reminders; of 16 post-event responses, 15 (93.75%) rejected a misleading miracle-product claim, all 16 answered the GM food, vaccine, insulin and AI-checking items as intended, 81.25% found explanations easy to understand and 87.5% called the activity very helpful.","ihp":"Five iHP cycles (problem validation, end-user and market needs, technical design, biosafety and ethics, production and final product) were each run through the team's STAR loop. A pre-interview stakeholder map linked each cycle to the expert groups whose input it needed, and a final map recorded each stakeholder's influence on the project and the project's relevance to them. The team documented a hard limit transparently: regulatory restrictions prevented them from interviewing astronauts or national regulatory authorities. Aerospace food and nutrition experts defined the nutritional context; space medicine and clinical experts shaped the health-need and biosafety assessment. Education fed directly back into design, most notably the kindergarten children's biocontainment and mutualism questions. A cross-team exchange with team NBJF on AI protein design taught the team to compartmentalise the workflow and to avoid over-reliance on in-silico prediction, and they adopted NBJF's 'phenomenon-first, jargon-later' principle for explaining their AND logic-gate probiotic to lay audiences.","realWorld":"The project targets a documented gap in astronaut health protection: pre-packaged vitamins and antioxidants provide fixed dosing that cannot track real-time biochemical change, and shelf-life degradation makes them unreliable over multi-year missions. Education outputs were deliberately packaged for reuse: an openly available children's book, downloadable comic in Chinese and English, the debate information packet, lecture slide decks, bilingual activity PowerPoints, the strawberry DNA extraction protocol, and detailed REACH appendices so other teams and educators can replicate the structure rather than just the activities.","partners":["BNDS British Debate Club","Tianyun Rehabilitation Center, Liulihe, Fangshan District, Beijing (hearing-impaired and genetic-disease children)","Sun and Rain Center (rare-disease organisation, osteogenesis imperfecta families)","BNDS Review (International Department student newspaper)","iGEM team NBJF (AI protein design exchange)","Hongchuanghui Fair","local kindergarten","youth sports school / student athlete programme","senior citizens' club ('iGEM and seniors together club')"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image20.webp","caption":"Kindergarten children with the BioBrick 'building block' constructs they assembled in small 'syndicates'","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image22.webp","caption":"The five-act puppet show 'Microscopic Defense Line', which showed phagocytosis as a hug rather than combat","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image24.webp","caption":"Guiding children at Tianyun Rehabilitation Center through the storybook 'Little Chef Mimi and Her Magical Yeast Friends'","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image29.webp","caption":"Student athletes aged 10-13 extracting strawberry DNA during the under-resourced-children workshop","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image36.webp","caption":"Older adults showing the DNA-themed bracelets they made during the health-literacy lecture","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Adopt a single named template for every outreach event - BNDS-China's R.E.A.C.H. (Recognize with a pre-event diagnostic, Experience, Apply, Communicate project feedback back into design, Harvest with a matched post-event survey) - and publish the results including the steps you failed to complete; the paired pre/post numbers and the honest gaps are what turn a scrapbook of events into evidence.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/contribution"],"rank":14},{"team":"UMA Malaga","id":"6119","section":"Undergrad","country":"Spain","village":"Diagnostics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uma-malaga/","projectTitle":"Frida","tagline":"A SHERLOCK-type LwCas13a screen for rare-disease variants, with Diana, a tool that takes a disease name all the way to a plate map.","summary":"Frida uses CRISPR/LwCas13a (SHERLOCK-type collateral cleavage read as fluorescence) to screen for the mutations behind rare genetic diseases, with cystic fibrosis as the model disease and a tandem gRNA assembly system (Neith) for multiplexing. Because a single disease can have hundreds of pathogenic variants and each needs its own guide, the team built Diana (diana-grna-designer), a free MIT-licensed web app, command-line tool and F# library that goes from a disease name, OMIM number or MONDO id, through ClinVar's variant list, to ranked and scored guide RNAs, to an order-ready oligo list, to a combinatorial pooling plan and a decoder for the positive wells. The engineering is unusually disciplined for iGEM: 1,164 unit and component tests plus 75 Playwright browser tests, automated axe-core WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility scans of every page state, every dependency pinned and checksum-verified, and exports in GenBank, SBOL 3 and SBOL 2 that are read back by Biopython and pySBOL3 as part of the test suite. Most striking, the wet lab contradicted the software's central heuristic and the team published that: the synthetic-mismatch substitution rule did not discriminate alleles in the placements tried, so the rule became an option to compare rather than a promise.","awards":["Best Alternative Platform — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Software — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Diagnostics Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"A single Blazor Server application (ASP.NET Core 9, C#, Razor components over SignalR, Bootstrap 5) calling an F# library ('gRNA') directly in the same process, with the grna CLI as a second front end over the identical calls so the web page and the terminal always agree. Native science runs as child processes: Bowtie 1.3.1 against a GRCh38 no-ALT index for off-targets, and ViennaRNA via Python 3 for folding. Everything ships in one Docker image (Compose v2) that bakes in the genome index deliberately, because Bowtie memory-maps the index and a volume or bind mount makes alignment more than ten times slower. External calls are limited to NCBI E-utilities, dbSNP Variation Services, ClinVar and the Monarch Initiative. The F# library also builds as a NuGet package (unpublished) referenceable from any .NET project, and the repository carries an agent skill so an AI assistant can install, start, health-check and drive the tool.","dataviz":"Ranked guide tables with nine sortable columns (spacer, GC score, GC %, homopolymers, off-targets, folding energy, unpaired share of spacer, mismatches against the other allele, score), filters on score, off-targets, GC range, allele mismatches and sequence fragment, the seed region underlined and the mutation marked in red, and status conveyed by shape and text rather than colour alone. Off-target chips read none/unique/caution/6+ saturated; expanding a row lists every GRCh38 alignment with chromosome, position, strand and mismatches. RNA structure is shown as dot-bracket plus a FORNA force-directed diagram rendered inside the page, so the sequence never leaves the machine unless the user clicks 'Open in FORNA'. Pooling produces a clickable, keyboard-navigable 96- or 384-well plate map where hovering a well lists its guides, and decoded positive wells are marked with a plus sign. Every term carries a plain-language tooltip, held in one file (HelpText.cs). Light and dark themes, a skip link, keyboard-operable tabs, and automated axe-core scans across WCAG 2.0-2.2 A and AA for every page state.","datascience":"Validation is reported as a matrix of what each part of the tool rests on, with three honest categories: automated tests, real sequences and services, and wet lab, where 'Tested' means lab work has started with no results yet and 'Not shown' is recorded as a result rather than a gap. Numbers: 1,164 NUnit/bUnit cases (1,151 pass, 13 self-skip when live services or ViennaRNA are absent), 75 Playwright/Chromium tests against a deterministic pipeline stand-in, pooling invariants proved over many (V, K) pairs (each guide in exactly R wells, no pool over K, every guide with a unique well set, consistent addressing on both plate formats), and decoding tested for single hits, collisions and dropouts. Hand checks rebuilt the rs334 and F508del designs from the NCBI sequences and confirmed the top deletion guides occur only in the deleted sequence, 6-8 letters from any wild-type window. The whole web flow was re-run against the real container on 2026-10-18 (cystic fibrosis disease search returning 1,517 ClinVar records, five designs, adapters, structure, shortlist, pooling and decoding). Performance was timed inside the container: 8-10 s for a transcript-position design, 28-38 s for a chromosome-position design (dominated by the NCBI download of the whole accession), 17 s for the disease search, about 1 s for a 100-guide pooling plan and 97 s for 100,000 guides. Export correctness was checked structurally: the annotated parts tile the sequence with no gap or overlap and equal the oligo DNA across all 64 combinations of repeat, adapters, flanks and T7 promoter, and the files are read back by Biopython and pySBOL3 with no error or warning.","software":"Diana has three chained but independently usable parts. Disease variants looks a disease up in the Monarch Initiative knowledge graph and lists the ClinVar records NCBI links to its OMIM number, showing ClinVar's own classification and review stars without re-interpreting them, capped at 3,000 records with the list flagged incomplete when it truncates. The Builder takes up to 50 inputs per run as rsIDs (resolved through NCBI Variation Services, keeping only the disease-relevant allele by default so rs334 gives one notation rather than twelve) or strict HGVS including c. positions on NM_/XM_ transcripts, which it converts using the GenBank coding range and says so in a notice; a c. position on a genomic record is refused with advice, because introns would put the guides in the wrong place. It then slides a window (23 nt default, 18-60 allowed) over both the original and mutated sequence on both strands, labels which strand is complementary to the mRNA and puts it first because LwCas13a binds RNA, and scores every candidate on four transparent criteria: GC content against a 40-60% target, homopolymer runs of 4 or more, Bowtie off-target hits in GRCh38 with up to two mismatches, and ViennaRNA minimum free energy of the complete guide under Turner 2004. Candidates sort on the ascending key (off-targets, -energy, -GC score, homopolymers), and the derived 0-1 score is explicitly a relative rank, not a prediction of activity. Two structure checks (is the direct-repeat hairpin intact in the fold, how much of the spacer stays unpaired) and an allele-discrimination count (fewest letters by which the target differs from the other allele, with a 'not discriminating' flag at 0 or 1) inform the choice without changing the ranking. The complete gRNA is built from the 36-nt LwCas13a direct repeat plus the spacer, optionally wrapped in the team's own Frida adapter (BsmBI Type IIS sites plus AAAC and GCTT fusion sites, so the repeat is acquired during Golden Gate assembly instead of being synthesised for every guide) and the BioBricks RFC[10] prefix/suffix adapter, which nest correctly so the junctions complete the standard sequences exactly. Cloning checks scan the DNA that would actually be ordered, on both strands, for ten enzymes (BsaI, BsmBI, SapI, BbsI, EcoRI, XbaI, SpeI, PstI, NotI, EcoRV), excluding sites an adapter carries on purpose, and show RFC[10] and RFC[1000] compatibility badges mirroring the Registry's own Assembly Compatibility rules. Guide Pooling then offers three combinatorial layouts (2D Fragmented, 2D Matrix, 3D) with stated well-count formulas, picks the cheapest automatically, places the result on 96- or 384-well plates and decodes a screen afterwards, distinguishing definite from ambiguous implications rather than hiding the collision case; the classic binary log2 model is deliberately excluded because it requires K = V/2 and breaks the five-guides-per-tandem-array limit. For 100 guides at K = 5 that is 40 wells instead of 100 reactions, a 60% saving. Exports: oligo CSV with optional T7 promoter and top/bottom strands plus a Restriction sites column so the warning travels with the order, FASTA, annotated GenBank, SBOL 3 (RDF/XML, Sequence Ontology roles) and SBOL 2 (added only because a libSBOLj-based tool rejected the SBOL 3 file). CSV fields starting with =, +, - or @ are neutralised so a spreadsheet does not execute them. Deployment is local by design: 'git clone https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uma-malaga/diana-grna-designer.git && ./start.sh', which checks Docker, disk and Compose, then reports success only once http://localhost:5000/healthz answers; about 10 GB disk and 3 GB RAM. There is no hosted design API, and the wiki says why (the 4 GB index and native tools make a public server costly, and local running keeps patient-adjacent data private) and then demonstrates that a HTTP front end would be thin by writing and running a minimal ASP.NET Core /export endpoint over the same library, while labelling that sketch a test of the idea rather than a shipped service. What is live and clickable is the example gallery at https://diana-grna-designer-7723f5.igem.wiki/, which publishes the unedited grna output for rs334 (48 candidate spacers), F508del (36 candidates) and a 100-guide pooling plan, each recording the date (2026-10-19), the commit (19a8e08), the Docker image sha256, the tool versions (Bowtie 1.3.1, ViennaRNA 2.7.2), the exact commands run, and the note that a second run the same day gave identical designs. Documentation is layered: README.md for usage, architecture, configuration and troubleshooting; docs/ for the runtime contract, library usage, a manual test plan and a validation record; and BIOLOGICAL_REPORT.md, which maps each biological step to the code for a non-programming audience and lists the open scientific questions. The 'Extending Diana' section walks a specific future feature (a wobble-aware allele check) through all six places in the codebase it would touch, with a sketch of the F# function. Licence MIT, with third-party licences listed (Bowtie Artistic 2.0; ViennaRNA free for academic use, commercial use requiring a separate licence), and an explicit statement that parts of the code were written with an AI coding assistant and reviewed by the team, which remains responsible.","stack":[".NET 9 / ASP.NET Core","Blazor Server","C#","F#","SignalR","Bootstrap 5","Python 3","Bowtie 1.3.1","ViennaRNA 2.7.2","Docker + Compose v2","NUnit","bUnit","Playwright (Chromium)","axe-core","Biopython (export verification)","pySBOL3 / pySBOL2","SBOL 3 and SBOL 2","GenBank flat file","HGVS","NCBI E-utilities / dbSNP Variation Services","ClinVar","Monarch Initiative API","FORNA","GRCh38","MIT licence","iGEM GitLab"],"techniques":["LwCas13a / SHERLOCK guide RNA design","sliding-window candidate generation on both strands","multi-criteria scoring and lexicographic ranking","short-read alignment for off-target counting","RNA secondary structure minimum free energy (Turner 2004)","synthetic-mismatch (substitution rule) allele discrimination","combinatorial group testing / pooled screening (2D fragmented, 2D matrix, 3D layouts)","ambiguity-aware decoding of pooled positives","Type IIS Golden Gate adapter design","restriction-site screening against RFC[10] and RFC[1000]","SPDI to HGVS conversion","property-based invariant testing over (V, K) pairs","automated accessibility auditing","dependency pinning with checksum verification","resumable batch processing with distinct exit codes"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A survey of 227 people probed public awareness of rare diseases, and the team reported it with the limits visible: 90.7% recognised the importance of early diagnosis and 87.7% knew newborn screening differs between Spain's autonomous communities, but only 11% identified the cost as a share of family income and only 9.3% the true global prevalence. They also surfaced an awareness-engagement gap, with 63% reporting no rare-disease activity in the past year (144 of 227 chose 'none of the above'). Demographics are published in full (gender, education, age, occupation, contact with a patient) and the wiki states explicitly that the sample is exploratory, that the percentages describe the sample and are not estimates for the general population, and that the reference answers themselves need validating before an overall knowledge score can be claimed.","ihp":"The human practices team (named Athena) ran an iterative cycle they call BARI: Bibliographic research, Application, Reflection, Integration, explicitly not a linear pipeline, with each cycle able to send them back to reconsider an earlier assumption. The substantive shift was away from treating Frida as a technology gap: conversations with patients, healthcare professionals, researchers and patient associations revealed territorial inequalities in newborn screening across Spanish autonomous communities, differences in care pathways, and the consequences of diagnostic delay, which moved the question from 'can we build a detector' to 'where in the diagnostic pathway could such a tool actually respond to a need'. The team also records that screening programmes are not definitive diagnostic tools and require confirmatory testing, and that the associated wait can cause irreversible damage in infants. The wet-lab feedback into the software is the clearest integration of all: the lab's failure to discriminate alleles, traced partly to G-U wobble pairs that LwCas13a tolerates, was published, demoted the substitution rule to one option among the ranked candidates, turned the rule's positions and spacer length into user settings so revised placements can be tested without changing code, and set a wobble-aware allele check as the next feature.","realWorld":"Frida targets the rare-disease diagnostic odyssey, where heterogeneous presentation and many candidate mutations can mean years of uncertainty and repeated consultations. The project grew out of the University of Málaga's existing screening and early-detection research, and the PIs' professional network provided the first stakeholder contacts. Diana is deliberately shaped for a clinical-adjacent setting: no account needed, nothing persisted, loopback-only binding, non-root container, and local-only deployment so sequence data never leaves the researcher's machine.","partners":["University of Málaga (UMA) screening and early-detection groups","rare disease patient associations","Spanish newborn screening professionals","Anthropic (sponsor)","GenScript (sponsor)","Integrated DNA Technologies (sponsor)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6119/wiki/images/software/v6-07-builder-ranked-table.avif","caption":"Diana's ranked spacer table: GC score, homopolymers, off-targets, assembly compatibility badges, folding energy, unpaired spacer share, mismatches against the other allele and the relative score, with the seed underlined and the mutation in red.","credit":"Team UMA Malaga, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6119/wiki/images/software/v6-11-disease-results.avif","caption":"The ClinVar variants of cystic fibrosis pulled through the Monarch Initiative knowledge graph, with classification, review stars and conditions shown as ClinVar gives them.","credit":"Team UMA Malaga, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6119/wiki/images/software/v6-22-pooling-plate-map.avif","caption":"The combinatorial pooling plate map: hovering a well shows which guides go into that tube, and arrow keys move between wells.","credit":"Team UMA Malaga, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6119/wiki/images/software/v6-14-builder-complete-grna.avif","caption":"The complete gRNA card: the LwCas13a direct repeat in green and the chosen spacer in magenta, with choices for the repeat, the Frida and BioBricks adapters and custom flanks.","credit":"Team UMA Malaga, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Publish a validation matrix that separates what automated tests prove, what a real run against live services proves, and what the bench has actually shown, and record 'not shown' as a result: Diana's wiki states outright that its ranking order, its substitution rule and its pooling layout are untested in the lab, and that the lab contradicted the substitution rule. A research centre should pair that honesty with Diana's two reusable mechanics, a pure scientific core (F#) that the GUI, the CLI and an AI agent skill all sit on as thin front ends, and a published example gallery pinned to a commit, image digest and exact commands so strangers can see real output without installing anything.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uma-malaga/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uma-malaga/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uma-malaga/","https://diana-grna-designer-7723f5.igem.wiki/","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uma-malaga/diana-grna-designer","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/uma-malaga"],"rank":21},{"team":"Cornell","id":"6122","section":"Undergrad","country":"United States","village":"Fashion & Cosmetics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/","projectTitle":"Klothos","tagline":"A smart bandage: engineered probiotics inside electrospun alginate nanofibers that release an anti-fibrotic peptide only where a wound is scarring badly.","summary":"Every scar treatment on the market today starts after the scar has formed — silicone sheeting, intralesional steroid, laser and grafts are all passive or reactive, and none addresses fibrosis biology during the critical healing window. Klothos intervenes during healing instead: engineered bacteria secrete 810A, a 12-amino-acid peptide that binds the exposed C-terminal domain of CTGF (CCN2) and blocks fibroblast-to-myofibroblast activation, and expression is held behind a two-input AND gate built from the Pseudomonas syringae hrpR/hrpS/hrpL system so the peptide is only made when reactive oxygen species (via TrxC) and hypoxia (via Pfnr) are both present — the signature of unchecked remodelling. The bacteria are encapsulated in an alginate/PEO nanofiber matrix spun on a coaxial electrospinner the team designed and built themselves, because commercial instruments cost far more than an undergraduate team can reach. It won Best Hardware and Best Presentation on the strength of that open, flat-packable, interlock-protected electrospinner plus a modular 3D-printed coaxial spinneret adapter, and Best Fashion & Cosmetics on a project that is unusually complete end to end: 20-plus risk-rated commercialisation analysis, a six-phase FDA path, and a human practices programme built around people who actually live with scars.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Hardware — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Education — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Presentation — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Fashion & Cosmetics Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Cornell's modelling splits cleanly into wet lab and product development. On the biology side: a production-degradation ODE (dF/dt = alpha*X - delta_F*F) for SUMO-His-810A accumulation against biomass; a Hill function for Tet and future inducible promoter activity; Michaelis-Menten kinetics for SUMO protease cleavage to recover active peptide; an ELISA-based Hill-Langmuir binding isotherm for 810A against CTGF/C, fixed at n=1 because there is a single site, with an explicit note that a fitted n far from 1 would indicate error rather than cooperativity; and a quadratic ligand-depletion model for the regime where accessible CTGF and peptide concentrations are comparable and the ordinary Langmuir assumption breaks. Downstream of binding they built a reduced-order dynamic systems model of fibroblast state transition, parameterised from a protein interaction network assembled in STRING v12.5 — filtered on the Scarring Human Phenotype Ontology term HP:0100699, recovering 48 weighted edges, merged with CCN2's 20 nearest first-degree and 10 nearest second-degree neighbours, surfacing SMAD3, FN1 and TGFB2 as major nodes. Those nodes and weights were then run in Netflux as a logic-based ODE network (normalised node activity with a time constant, modified Hill activation and inhibition functions) using COL1A1 accumulation as the proxy for extracellular matrix hardening, and the simulation tracks the downstream TGF-beta inhibition reported in Xiao et al. 2020. Wound environment is modelled explicitly: ROS as a transient peak function calibrated against Carney et al. 2019 time-series qRT-PCR of SOD1, MGST1 and PRDX6 in hypertrophic scars, oxygen as an exponential recovery function, each feeding a Hill-function promoter. AlphaFold was used to render 810A and the CTGF/C-810A-6xHis complex. On the hardware side: a semi-empirical predictor of shell-solution viscosity (polymer overlap and entanglement statistics via Mark-Houwink), conductivity (mobile ion contribution) and surface tension (surfactant adsorption with a CMC/CAC plateau), the Fridrikh scaling law for terminal jet radius from flow rate, jet current and surface tension, plus continuity-based flow rate and stepper-motor plunger speed models.","dataviz":"The standout is the Unified Readiness Classifier, which collapses three separate property predictions into a single green/yellow/red call on whether a formulation should go to the electrospinner at all — green means proceed to voltage-window testing, yellow allows one short trial or minor reformulation, red means reformulate first. It is rendered as a spinning-viability contour map over sodium alginate and PEO weight percentages produced by Monte Carlo sampling against literature results, so a reader can see the spinnable region rather than read a table of numbers. The alginate molecular-weight analysis is similarly well-framed: one plot puts viscosity on the left axis spanning more than four orders of magnitude while conductivity and surface tension on the right axis stay flat, which makes the whole argument visible at a glance — conductivity caps how much alginate you can dissolve, molecular weight decides whether that limit is reachable, and the optimum is interior rather than monotonic. The STRING network is shown as a force-directed layout and the Netflux run as normalised activity curves for CTGF, TGFB1, FN1, COL1A1, SMAD2 and SMAD3 together. The wiki itself is a scroll-driven 3D island journey ('scroll to travel, click an island to explore') with an interactive AND-gate widget letting the reader switch each input signal on and off, and a WebGL model of the electrospinner.","datascience":"The classifier is the clearest case of turning data into a predictive tool: rather than measuring solutions after purchase, Cornell predicts viscosity, conductivity and surface tension from formulation inputs (alginate concentration, PEO concentration and molecular weight, surfactant type and concentration, salt or buffer content, temperature) and uses the prediction to avoid spinning runs that cannot work. They name candidate implementations — logistic regression, random forest, or Gaussian process classification, favouring the Gaussian process on a small dataset because it returns uncertainty — and define a two-level validation plan: property-level (mean absolute error for viscosity, percent error for conductivity, absolute error in mN/m for surface tension against held-out measured formulations) and outcome-level (whether predicted-green formulations actually produce stable shell-only fibres, judged on Taylor cone formation, current stability, bead formation, dry deposition and fibre diameter distribution). They say outright that the classifier matters more than exact numerical accuracy, and that literature Mark-Houwink, CMC and adsorption constants are starting estimates to be recalibrated against the team's own measurements. Fibre morphology was quantified by image analysis: tortuosity as the ratio of fibre contour length to end-to-end distance, with a >1.10 threshold defining 'curly', giving 8% curly and 92% straight. Failure diagnosis was also model-driven — droplet breakup was traced to insufficient PEO chain entanglement at 100 kDa, leading them to raise PEO from 1.5% to 2% and cut back PS80 surfactant.","software":"No standalone software release; the released artefact is the hardware. The electrospinner is published openly as a bill of materials, CAD files, circuit diagrams, assembly documentation, an operation manual and a dedicated safety manual, designed to be disassembled and flat-packed for transport by teams in shared or temporary workspaces. Control is an ESP32 DevKitC microcontroller driving an AHV24V30KVR5MAW high-voltage module (24 V in, up to 30 kV out) for digitised control in place of a potentiometer, with an access-door interlock and emergency stop that cut power and ground the high-voltage circuit. A separate contribution is the modular 3D-printed coaxial needle adapter, which keeps metal needle tips for conductivity while 3D-printing the structure, adaptable across needle gauges and core-shell flow configurations. They also publish a disulfide-bond-optimised SHuffle expression protocol (6xHis-SUMO fusion, SUMO as chaperone and clean cleavage handle) framed for reuse on other hard-to-express proteins such as insulin-like growth factors, antibody fragments and venom peptides, plus protocols for alginate preparation, crosslinking viability testing, escape testing, light microscopy ImageJ analysis and spectrographic bacterial settling.","stack":["STRING v12.5","Netflux (logic-based ODE network simulation)","AlphaFold","ImageJ","Monte Carlo sampling","Gaussian process / random forest / logistic regression classification","ESP32 DevKitC microcontroller","AHV24V30KVR5MAW 30 kV high-voltage module","CAD and 3D printing (coaxial spinneret, enclosure, extruder)","SDS-PAGE, Ni-NTA affinity chromatography, LC-MS, ELISA","16S rRNA sequencing against the NIH database"],"techniques":["Production-degradation ODE modelling","Hill function promoter induction modelling","Michaelis-Menten enzyme kinetics","Hill-Langmuir / one-site Langmuir binding isotherm fitting to ELISA dose-response","Quadratic ligand-depletion equilibrium modelling","Reduced-order signalling network modelling","Protein-protein interaction network assembly filtered by Human Phenotype Ontology term","Logic-based network simulation with modified Hill activation and inhibition","Transient-peak and exponential-recovery environmental signal models","Mark-Houwink viscosity scaling and polymer entanglement statistics","Fridrikh scaling law for terminal jet radius","Semi-empirical property prediction with a green/yellow/red readiness classifier","Fibre tortuosity quantification by image analysis","AND-gate transcriptional logic (hrpR/hrpS/hrpL)"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Cornell ran an unusually wide and genuinely iterative education programme. Plasmid Pals, their own educational game, was playtested with Big Brothers Big Sisters participants who then suggested how to make it more interactive. Plasmid bracelets plus an open-ended idea board asking visitors how they would use synthetic biology to change the world evolved into age-adapted versions for daycare, the Sciencenter and the State Fair — and when reading and spelling turned out to be barriers for roughly 20 preschoolers, the team switched to a cooking analogy (DNA as cookbook, RNA as copied recipe, protein as finished dish), writing up the lesson that activities must match developmental ability. A summer-camp session had about 80 students debate which scientific problems deserve funding using live polls, and a 30-minute lesson drew another 20-25 minutes of student-led questions about research careers and contributing without lab access. STEM Her Way was a four-session series in which high school girls learned synthetic biology and presented their own research proposals, rated 4.7/5 by 15 respondents across seven countries with every respondent recommending it. A shadowing programme drew more than 260 applications from the US and 17 countries for six high school scholars, later extended to five international students whose own project proposals the team helped assess for feasibility — a participant from Uzbekistan brought soil salinity as a community problem and the students developed Salt Guardians around it. They also ran a worldwide synthetic biology governance competition (regulatory pathways, biosafety and containment, access and equity, plus an open governance challenge), produced a narrative synthetic biology podcast, taught Lambert iGEM how to prepare stakeholder questions by sharing their own Human Practices deck and brainstorming guide, and swapped children's books with NUGEM — NUGEM translating Cornell's into Kazakh and Russian while Cornell translated NUGEM's into Mandarin, Spanish and French.","ihp":"Several interviews changed the project rather than decorating it. The clearest is hardware: advised not to build their own high-voltage power supply (especially from microwave parts) because the electrical risk is serious, the team dropped the homemade supply entirely, consulted Cornell's Afridi Lab, and bought a commercial regulated unit while sourcing other parts through Digi-Key and Mouser to hold cost down — the advice was that boosting voltage is easy but a stable regulated DC output is the hard part. Dr Insoo Hyun reframed safety itself: he introduced weighing risks and benefits against existing alternatives rather than assuming absolute safety, and warned that familiarity with a microorganism from food use does not demonstrate safety in an open wound, also pressing them to choose experimental models that resemble real wound conditions rather than the most convenient ones. Dr Joseph Fins pushed on preclinical evidence and asked whether delivering the peptide directly, without living bacteria, would be safer and simpler — which the team now carries explicitly as a documented de-risking architecture, with the trade-off (a pre-loaded matrix gives a fixed dose and loses the AND-gated release) stated rather than hidden. Dr Andrew Leask's input on CCN2/CTGF signalling shaped the target rationale, and reviewer pressure to confirm H2O2 is still detectable at the wound stage they target led them to work on sensor sensitivity and add the hypoxia AND gate for specificity. Burn survivor and former burn nurse Amy Acton and Andrea at the Phoenix Society for Burn Survivors changed how the team talks: survivors need support long after wounds heal — financial help for compression garments and procedures, emotional support, connection with others — treatments are costly, uncomfortable and variable, so do not promise how much a treatment will reduce scarring. Roughly 20 older adults and visitors at open events taught them that a scar's size or appearance does not predict its personal impact, and that open conversation surfaced concerns their structured survey questions missed; they concluded existing scales describe the scar but not what the person would want improved. Alison Bateman-House at NYU identified composition and method-of-production as their strongest patent claims and walked them through the one-year US grace period and PCT economics, which fed directly into the commercialisation plan. A kelp-farming conversation moved the sustainability case from biodegradability to sourcing, noting that North American kelp farmers' binding constraint is finding buyers rather than growing kelp since wild-harvested seaweed is substantially cheaper — a point that complicates rather than flatters their own narrative.","realWorld":"This is the most developed commercial package of the five. Target market is quantified: burn centres and academic hospitals in the American Northeast, where 2,255 medical institutions — 26 verified burn centres, 604 hospitals (31 with ACGME dermatology residencies) and 1,625 medical spas — serve an estimated 1.8 million addressable patients per year, or 2.2 patients per institution per day, with academic hospitals and verified burn centres targeted first as likeliest to trial new technology. The regulatory analysis identifies the real hazard: Klothos is a combination product (alginate nanofiber device plus engineered live bacteria), so FDA assigns a primary mode of action deciding whether CBER leads via IND/BLA or CDRH via 510(k)/PMA, and misclassification could force a restart costing an estimated 18-24 months and $500K-plus — hence a formal Pre-RFD consultation with the Office of Combination Products before committing development resources. They cite Azitra's engineered S. epidermidis with an open IND and FDA Fast Track as precedent that CBER will accept an IND for topical engineered bacteria. A six-phase plan runs from prototype and fibroblast assays, through an IND/IDE-enabling package with dermal safety, immunogenicity, biodistribution and microbial containment, to Phase I (with viable-organism monitoring because it is a live biotherapeutic) and a Phase II randomised controlled trial in surgical, burn, hypertrophic-scar-risk and revision-scar settings scored on POSAS, VSS and SCAR-Q. A freedom-to-operate analysis covers ten patents across four technology areas, with specific flags (the University of Manitoba colour-changing antibacterial nanofiber US20220047523A1 and NYCU's pending pH-sensitive electrospun bandage would need review before adding a pH indicator), plus PCT costs of roughly $17K to hold in 150 signatories and $20K per country thereafter. They map regulatory strategy across seven regions, including the MHRA consultation on modernising the GTMP definition (closed 22 June 2026) and Korea's MFDS review reforms cutting target timelines from about 420 to 295 days. Over 20 risks across technical, market, financial and regulatory categories are rated on severity, likelihood and detectability with mitigations and rendered as a heat map; a skill gap radar names what the 45-plus member team cannot yet do (pricing, physician adoption, launch strategy, clinical trial design) and how they would fill it. Reimbursement is treated honestly: novel combination products often lack an established billing code, so they model unit economics under both self-pay and reimbursed scenarios. The team won an ANSA grant and flagged Cornell's technology transfer office licensing terms as a Year 1 gating factor for any spinout.","partners":["Dr Andrew Leask (CCN2/CTGF signalling and pathological scarring)","Dr Insoo Hyun (bioethics)","Dr Joseph Fins (medical ethics)","Alison Bateman-House, NYU Grossman School of Medicine (medical ethics and IP)","Amy Acton, Phoenix Society for Burn Survivors","Lisa Placanica (prescription/outpatient model)","Afridi Lab, Cornell (high-power electronics)","Cornell technology transfer office and biosafety office / IBC","Lambert iGEM (human practices mentoring)","NUGEM (children's book translation exchange)","STEM Her Way","Big Brothers Big Sisters","Ithaca Sciencenter","Cornell Division B Science Olympiad","ANSA grant programme"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6122/wiki/modeling/productdev-14-spinning-viability-alginate-peo-classifier-map.avif","caption":"Predicted spinning viability across sodium alginate and PEO weight percentages, generated by Monte Carlo sampling over the viscosity, conductivity and surface tension models — the map behind the green/yellow/red readiness classifier that decides whether a formulation reaches the electrospinner.","credit":"Team Cornell, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6122/wiki/modeling/wetlab-17-fig4-string-ctgf-scarring-network.avif","caption":"Force-directed CTGF (CCN2)-centred protein interaction and scarring regulatory network built in STRING v12.5 by filtering on Human Phenotype Ontology term HP:0100699, with SMAD3, FN1 and TGFB2 as major nodes. Its 48 weighted edges became the parameters of the Netflux fibroblast simulation.","credit":"Team Cornell, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6122/wiki/engineering-success/hardware-extruder-reservoir-coaxial-needle-collection-plate/eng-suc-hardware-web-2-0-fig-1.avif","caption":"The custom coaxial electrospinner: extruder, reservoir, coaxial needle and collection plate, built from inexpensive stock parts and 3D-printed components and designed to flat-pack for teams working in shared or temporary spaces.","credit":"Team Cornell, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6122/wiki/hardware/microscope/gfp-coaxial.avif","caption":"Micrograph of coaxially electrospun fibres containing GFP-expressing engineered E. coli, showing that cells remain fluorescent and therefore viable after encapsulation.","credit":"Team Cornell, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Build the cheap version of the instrument and publish it properly. Cornell's winning move was deciding that an electrospinner — normally a specialised commercial purchase — could be made from stock parts and 3D prints, then releasing the bill of materials, CAD, circuit diagrams, assembly guide, operation manual and a separate high-voltage safety manual with interlocks and an emergency stop designed in. For a school-based centre the paired lesson is the readiness classifier: predict a formulation's viscosity, conductivity and surface tension from its recipe, label it green/yellow/red, and spend scarce lab time only on green — a cheap model that saves expensive experiments is worth more than an elaborate one that does not change what you do next.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/circuitry","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/extrusion-mechanism","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/encasement","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/fibers","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/wet-lab","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/notebook","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/timeline"],"rank":155},{"team":"MIT-MAHE","id":"6145","section":"Undergrad","country":"India","village":"Food & Agriculture / Manufacturing","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/","projectTitle":"MAHEK","tagline":"Sustainable cuminaldehyde from Pseudomonas putida, and NetFlux, a web tool that turns a genome-scale metabolic model into a drawable pathway and a ranked list of knockouts.","summary":"MAHEK addresses the fragrance industry's dependence on petrochemical synthesis and wasteful seed extraction by producing cuminaldehyde biologically. The chassis is Pseudomonas putida F1, a solvent-tolerant soil bacterium that already makes cuminaldehyde as an intermediate in p-cymene degradation; CRISPR-Cas9-mediated homologous recombination knocks out cymC, the p-cumic aldehyde dehydrogenase, arresting the pathway exactly at the target compound. The dry lab's deliverable is NetFlux, a React + FastAPI web application that compresses a genome-scale metabolic model into a readable 'production corridor' using a novel extraction algorithm (SCOPE) and then pools OptKnock and OptForce into one co-ranked list of knockout and regulation strategies, each traced to the gene and linked to BiGG. The team also won the Inclusivity Award for work with visually impaired learners that ended in Vision Empower adopting their tactile models for use in schools across India.","awards":["Best Software — Nominee (Undergrad)","Inclusivity Award — Winner (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"React + TypeScript single-page frontend split into three modules (parameter panel, pathway map viewer, interventions table) sharing one run state; FastAPI backend with Pydantic request/response schemas, auto-generated OpenAPI 3.1 documentation and a Swagger UI at /docs; a JobManager running strain design on a bounded background worker pool so heavy requests do not time out; SQLite (mfghub.db) as the model/reaction/metabolite/gene/medium catalogue, rebuilt from the GEMs on every server image build; and Docker Compose bringing up database, API and web client together. Core science runs on COBRApy.","dataviz":"Pathways are drawn as interactive metabolic maps flowing strictly left to right, with line width proportional to mass flow, metabolites as nodes carrying compartment suffixes, reactions as arrows colour-coded by their FVA annotation, and the substrate-to-target corridor explicitly highlighted. Hovering a reaction opens an inspection card with reaction name and ID, knockout-candidate status, flux and FVA range, coupling and bounds, subsystem, gene associations and gene rules, and its producers and consumers. Each click of Generate opens its parameters in a new tab so settings can be compared side by side. Results export as GraphML, JSON and CSV straight from the browser. The interventions table shows a baseline header (wild-type growth rate, yield floor and ceiling) against which every ranked design is judged, and separates committed 'Ranked Designs' from weaker single-reaction 'Hypotheses (leads)', with the wiki telling users not to compare yield numbers across the two tables.","datascience":"Benchmarked without a ground truth by defining what a useful pathway must do and measuring that instead. SCOPE was run on 9 GEMs with up to 5 target products each, 40 organism-product pairs in total: 100% of extracted pathways were fully connected from substrate to target, and the mean corridor was 55.1 reactions against a mean model size of 934, i.e. 7.8% of the network (models up to 1,762 reactions reduced to about 55). Essential reactions were identified independently with FVA and then checked for recall: 20.3 essential reactions per product on average, 17.5 retained, overall recall 0.863, above 0.90 in five of nine organisms, with the weakest cases (iJN1463 at 0.616, iJN1026 at 0.719) reported rather than hidden. Eleven curated GEMs ship with the tool, covering bacteria, a cyanobacterium, an archaeon, yeast and a diatom. The metabolic model behind the wet lab extended iJN1463 with the full p-cymene degradation route (cymA, cymB, cymC, cmtA-cmtE), linking to native metabolism through OP4ENH.","software":"NetFlux solves two linked problems in metabolic engineering: genome-scale models are too large to read, and strain-design algorithms waste most of their compute on reactions that can never carry flux. Its five-stage pipeline is the substance. Stage 1 solves FBA then pFBA and converts the flux distribution into a Mass Flow Graph (nodes = reactions, edges = metabolite mass flow). Stage 2 applies FASTCC against a fixed consistency threshold to strip blocked reactions, which the team identifies as the single largest source of computational expense. Stage 3 is SCOPE, their GUDIE-derived extractor: log-normalise the heavy-tailed edge weights, compute a flux-coupling score per reaction against the target, seed an interest signal at the target and propagate it with Personalised PageRank, combine coupling and interest into a per-edge score, expand outward over a bounded number of hops admitting reactions above a threshold anchored to the target's immediate neighbourhood, then an optional repair step that closes a broken corridor with a flux-weighted shortest path. Stage 4 annotates the corridor with FVA so each reaction is typed as essential-and-fixed, flexible, or a natural up/downregulation candidate. Stage 5 pools OptForce and OptKnock and co-ranks their proposals into one ordered list, resolving each to a gene linked to BiGG. Two design decisions stand out for reuse: the REST API is versioned, OpenAPI 3.1-specified and language-agnostic with async job polling (submit, get a job_id, poll), and all compute is deliberately RAM-only, with uploaded models referenced by short-lived tokens, a 30-minute SessionCache, and nothing a client submits ever written to disk or database. Users can upload their own GEM as .json or .sbml. It is self-hosted rather than publicly deployed: 'git clone https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/mit-mahe/netflux && docker compose up --build', then http://localhost:5173/, with stated system requirements of 2-4 GB RAM, any modern multi-core CPU and no GPU. A video guide and a step-by-step interpretation guide are on the wiki, and the limitation is stated plainly: constraint-based predictions describe what the network allows at steady state, not kinetics or regulation, so every strategy is a wet-lab hypothesis. Repo: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/mit-mahe/netflux (team repo https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/mit-mahe).","stack":["React","TypeScript","FastAPI","Pydantic","Python","COBRApy","SQLite","Docker","Docker Compose","OpenAPI 3.1 / Swagger UI","REST / JSON-over-HTTP","GraphML","SBML","BiGG database","KaTeX (wiki maths)","iGEM GitLab"],"techniques":["Flux Balance Analysis (FBA)","parsimonious FBA (pFBA)","Flux Variability Analysis (FVA)","Mass Flow Graph representation of metabolism","FASTCC flux-consistency pruning","flux coupling analysis","Personalised PageRank network propagation","GUDIE-style user-defined subgraph extraction","flux-weighted shortest-path repair","OptKnock bilevel knockout search","OptForce intervention search","co-ranking of competing optimisation outputs","recall benchmarking against independently derived essential reactions","CRISPR-Cas9 homologous recombination (wet lab)"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Sessions were run across India in English, Hindi, Telugu, Kannada and Marathi so language would not be a barrier, and deliberately extended beyond students: the team visited old age homes to learn how residents' associations with traditional scents had changed over their lifetimes, then introduced them to synthetic biology. Workshops included household-materials DNA extraction from bananas at Purple Space Library Manipal (50 students, grades 1-7, split by age group), recombinant DNA via insulin at GenWise Camp, and deeper project discussion with grade 11-12 biology students at Vidya Mandir Chennai. Teachers were walked through each protocol step so they could keep running the demonstration after the team left. 'Beyond Sight', an audio-only podcast, carried five conversations with visually impaired professionals (a radio jockey, a geneticist, the founder of assistive-technology company TorchIT, an ophthalmologist, and one of only three blind women to have submitted before the Supreme Court of India). A Colourblind Awareness Day Instagram series ran on 6 September.","ihp":"Stakeholder analysis walked the whole cumin supply chain, from traders who buy directly from farmers to internationally working perfumers, finding that each group holds a different version of the same problem. Hard numbers anchor the premise: India supplies about 70-80% of world cumin, the 2026 crop was projected at 9.0-9.2 million bags against roughly 11 million in 2025 (a 15-20% decline), hydrodistillation yields about 2.56% essential oil, and cuminaldehyde is a fraction of that. The decisive conversation was with Givaudan, which revealed that fragrance houses procure whole cumin essential oil rather than isolated cuminaldehyde; this redirected the team's target customer from fragrance houses to aroma compound manufacturers, where demand for isolated, sustainably produced cuminaldehyde actually exists. The inclusivity work was itself integrated rather than decorative: conversations with Vision Empower (IIIT Bangalore) identified that Indian educational structures restrict visually impaired students from science, and the team then built tactile models, trialled them with Mrs Nagarathna, a visually impaired educator, revised on her feedback, and tested with a ninth-grade student. Fixes were deliberately minimal and buildable from cheap materials: the existing DNA model distinguished bases only by colour, so each base got a texture instead (adenine smooth tape, thymine jute, guanine bubble wrap, cytosine velvet) wrapped around the existing model so it did not need rebuilding; the mitosis model's components were only drawn on, so they were rebuilt in clay with pipe-cleaner chromosomes. Vision Empower approved both the content and the models and will use them across their associated schools. A separate conversation with an Indian Sign Language expert established that, unlike ASL, ISL has no centralised place to learn it, which the team records as a gap they did not close.","realWorld":"A consumer survey found the barrier is information rather than opposition, with respondents already aware of land use, extraction and climate pressures and nearly half willing to pay slightly more for lower environmental impact, though more were worried about long-term effects of ingredients made by engineered microorganisms. Producer-side work found that Indian companies import aroma compounds from China, the UAE and France despite domestic cumin dominance, and that price is the deciding factor for buyers because raw material cost passes straight to consumers. The team wrote 'An iGEMer's Guide to Upscaling', a structured upscaling and biomanufacturing guide, after finding no iGEM-oriented resource bridging flask scale to industrial scale, and 'The Aroma Atlas', a catalogue of everyday scents, their active ingredients and current production methods, as a starting point for teams looking for compounds worth making sustainably. Six cym operon genes were submitted to the Registry as basic parts (BBa_26CFFF0H, BBa_26R7VUFV, BBa_26MJ8EI9, BBa_26SS8791, BBa_26AS8UG2, BBa_26AW7YAF).","partners":["Vision Empower / IIIT Bangalore","Givaudan","Score Foundation / Project Eyeway","TorchIT","Navjeevan Blind School, Pune","Purple Space Library Manipal","GenWise Camp","Vidya Mandir Senior Secondary School, Chennai","Manipal Academy of Higher Education","Anthropic (sponsor)","BioRender, GenScript, IDT, NEB, SnapGene (sponsors)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6145/wiki/dry-lab/software/fig-2-architecture.avif","caption":"NetFlux architecture: React/TypeScript frontend, FastAPI backend with Pydantic schemas and a bounded JobManager worker pool, over a SQLite model catalogue and precomputed Mass Flow Graphs.","credit":"Team MIT-MAHE, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6145/wiki/dry-lab/software/fig-1-pipeline.avif","caption":"The five-stage NetFlux pipeline: Mass Flow Graph construction, FASTCC pruning, SCOPE/GUDIE corridor extraction, FVA annotation, and the pooled OptKnock/OptForce design engine.","credit":"Team MIT-MAHE, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6145/wiki/dry-lab/software/fig-4-user-guide.avif","caption":"An extracted production corridor drawn automatically from a genome-scale model, flowing left to right with line width proportional to mass flow and reactions coloured by FVA annotation.","credit":"Team MIT-MAHE, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6145/wiki/dry-lab/software/fig-6-user-guide.avif","caption":"The intervention strategy suite: ranked designs with yield floor, retained growth, coupling strength and the specific gene to edit, separated from weaker exploratory leads.","credit":"Team MIT-MAHE, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"When there is no ground truth to benchmark against, define what a correct output must satisfy and measure that: NetFlux's credibility rests on three published numbers (100% corridor connectivity, 7.8% of the network retained, 0.863 recall of independently FVA-derived essential reactions), with the weakest organisms named. A research centre should also copy the ephemeral-compute design, user-uploaded models held in RAM behind short-lived tokens and never written to disk, which makes a shared analysis service usable with data people will not hand over.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/implementation","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/contribution","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/mit-mahe/netflux"],"rank":33},{"team":"Uni-Padua-IT","id":"6151","section":"Undergrad","country":"Italy","village":"Space","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/","projectTitle":"C.O.S.M.I.C. (Cyanobacterial Oxygen Supply for Martian Inhabitant Complexes)","tagline":"A Nostoc-based life-support chassis for Mars, plus ReTIsigner, an open codon optimiser for the 35,799 hosts other tools ignore.","summary":"Uni-Padua-IT engineered the filamentous cyanobacterium Nostoc sp. PCC 7120 as a Martian life-support organism: it fixes CO2 into breathable oxygen, degrades the perchlorates that poison Martian water (via native NarB and a heterocyst-compartmentalised pcrABD plus chlorite dismutase), and carries pprA from Deinococcus radiodurans for radiation-damage repair, behind a copper-dependent MazF kill switch. Because no free codon optimiser listed their chassis, they forked TIsigner into ReTIsigner, a frequency-weighted codon optimiser that reads any of the 35,799 Kazusa codon usage tables, enforces GC bounds, actively excises RFC restriction sites and reports CAI, GC and translation-initiation opening energy. ReTIsigner was validated in silico over 100 sequences and four hosts, then in vivo: a ReTIsigner-optimised eYFP gave a 2.16-fold common-effect increase in specific fluorescence across DH5-alpha and BL21 (p = 0.002, permutation test stratified by strain). They also built an open-source acoustic (piezo + Arduino) bubble sensor so oxygen production can be followed without opening a sealed culture.","awards":["Best Software — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Space Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"SvelteKit single-page frontend built from scratch, talking to a Flask backend over a single documented HTTP endpoint (POST /optimise); a worker pool behind Flask shells out to the compiled scientific binaries. The whole stack is shipped as a two-stage Docker image (Node.js 22 build stage; Miniconda runtime pinning Python 3.6.9, ViennaRNA 2.5.1 and Infernal 1.1.5) exposing port 5050.","dataviz":"Results view compares input against each optimised sequence in a results card showing CAI, GC content and opening energy, so sequences no longer have to be analysed one at a time in external tools. A built-in guided tour walks a first-time user through every field of the run configuration. Exports in three reproducible formats (CSV, PDF, FASTA), each recording the full run configuration plus the computed analyses. Validation figures (specific-fluorescence bar charts and ratio plots with 95% CIs) are published as SVG on the wiki, and the system is explained with two architecture diagrams: the POST /optimise request path and one iteration of the annealing loop.","datascience":"In silico benchmark over 100 coding sequences optimised for four hosts spanning roughly 31-75% GC, recording GC, CAI, opening energy and failure rate with and without a codon usage table: supplying a table cut the failure rate from 52% to 2% while leaving opening energy essentially unchanged (mean difference +/-0.06 kcal/mol). Wet-lab validation used independent cultures as the unit of replication, three cultures per sample each in technical triplicate, fluorescence compared at matched optical density rather than fixed time, two detection channels, and a permutation test stratified by strain; the team explicitly states the confidence interval (about 1.6-2.8x) and what the data does not license them to claim.","software":"ReTIsigner is a free, Apache 2.0-licensed codon optimiser for non-model hosts, and it is the clearest example this year of forking an existing scientific tool rather than rebuilding one. The team started from TIsigner (Bhandari, Lim & Gardner), contacted its authors for feedback on their proposal, and then added four things TIsigner lacked: frequency-weighted synonymous codon sampling driven by any Kazusa codon usage table (35,799 organisms) instead of one-amino-acid-one-codon, an optional GC bound enforced as a feasibility filter, active position-targeted removal of restriction sites with a re-check for newly introduced sites, and in-app CAI/GC analysis per output sequence. The restriction-site work is the headline engineering result: optimising perchlorate reductase subunit A for Nostoc under RFC 10 and RFC 1000 constraints was still running after 15 hours under the original random-substitution behaviour, and completed in seconds afterwards, yielding registry part BBa_268WDVEB. Architecturally the optimiser sits behind POST /optimise so it is usable headlessly without the Svelte interface, and the Docker image removes the platform problem that ViennaRNA and Infernal binaries create on Windows ('docker build -t retisigner . && docker run -p 5050:5050 retisigner'). It ships a user guide, a development guide documenting the system and its API calls, a video tutorial (https://video.igem.org/w/rN6WnodTKYxiNitzfSUVZ3) that optimises BBa_E0040 GFP for Clostridium acetobutylicum ATCC 824, and a public repository at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uni-padua-it/retisigner-svelte (team repo: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/uni-padua-it). It is self-hosted via Docker rather than offered as a hosted public web service, which is the one gap: there is no URL a stranger can click. They also positioned ReTIsigner honestly against the field, reproducing Table 1 of Demissie et al. (2025) and adding their own row with a tilde rather than a tick in the CAI column, and comparing feature-by-feature against the two previous iGEM codon optimisers (HebrewU 2018, MIT 2023). They published an open call for collaboration on 9 August 2026 offering to optimise any team's sequence for their chassis and received no replies, and they say so on the wiki.","stack":["SvelteKit","Svelte","Node.js 22","Python 3.6.9","Flask","Docker (multi-stage)","Miniconda/Conda","ViennaRNA 2.5.1 (RNAplfold)","Infernal 1.1.5","REST/HTTP API","Apache 2.0","iGEM GitLab","Arduino (hardware)"],"techniques":["Simulated annealing search","frequency-weighted synonymous codon sampling","mRNA translation-initiation opening-energy minimisation","Codon Adaptation Index (CAIcal/Sharp-Li definition)","GC-bound feasibility filtering","active restriction-site excision","Kazusa codon-usage-table parsing","permutation testing stratified by strain","in silico benchmarking across GC-spanning hosts","acoustic (piezoelectric) frequency-sweep sensing"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A public survey on space research and biotechnology directly reshaped the team's communication: 76.5% of respondents rated using living organisms for oxygen and water highly while only 28.2% rated colonisation highly, so the team separated the usefulness of the technology from support for settling Mars; 42.2% knew little or nothing about GMOs, so explanations of what they modify and why were rewritten more accessibly; and 94.8% called a genetic safety system fundamental or very important, so the kill switch and confined bioreactor were given more space. Education activities grew out of the same consultations.","ihp":"Human practices is written as a sequence of questions with an explicit 'what we changed' and, unusually, a 'what we couldn't fix' for each. Consultations with photosynthesis and cyanobacteria professors (Nicoletta La Rocca, Daniela Billi) cut the cold-tolerance module. iGEM Darmstadt 2025, who had built the same perchlorate constructs in B. subtilis, warned them off pcrABCD's oxygen sensitivity, which led to testing native NarB first and then compartmentalising pcrABD in heterocysts. A presentation to NASA's Open Science Data Repository Analysis Working Group moved the radiation module from survival-at-high-dose to genome integrity over generations, and pushed them toward a terrestrial use case (perchlorate in groundwater near propellant plants) when the Moon turned out to have no perchlorate. ESA flight-operations engineers (Davide Vertuani, Paolo Ferri) made them reason about the journey, not just arrival. Philosopher Telmo Pievani and space-habitat designer Serena Crotti changed how the bioreactor is framed within a crew's daily life. Most relevant to a dry-lab centre: they took their hardware manual and code to an instrumentation professor and an electronics PhD student (Simone Del Favero, Samuele Faccin) before publishing, and as a result weakened their own claim (the sensor detects gas bubbles, it does not measure oxygen), redrew the circuit with no crossing wires, documented timing resolution, and added a table separating proven from untested.","realWorld":"The team identified a terrestrial application independent of any Mars mission: perchlorate contaminates groundwater near propellant and pyrotechnics manufacturing and is regulated, and biological removal is already an active research area. They were unable to secure an ionising radiation source (INFN Legnaro's Van de Graaff accelerator unavailable; IOV Padova's linear accelerator caps at roughly 6 Gy/min against a 6 kGy LD50), and report that the pprA radiation claim is therefore untested rather than glossing over it.","partners":["iGEM Darmstadt 2025","NASA Open Science Data Repository Analysis Working Group","European Space Agency (flight operations)","University of Padua (Biology, Chemistry, Information Engineering)","University of Rome Tor Vergata","TISIGNER authors (Bhandari, Lim, Gardner)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/new-frontend.avif","caption":"The ReTIsigner interface the team built from scratch in SvelteKit, replacing the original TIsigner form.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/guided-tour.avif","caption":"The in-app guided tour that walks a first-time user through configuring a whole optimisation run.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/results-opt.avif","caption":"The results card: input sequence against an optimised output, with CAI and GC content computed in-app.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/request-schema.avif","caption":"System architecture: the SvelteKit frontend posts a run to Flask, which hands it to a worker pool that shells out to RNAplfold and Infernal.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/optimisation-schema.avif","caption":"One iteration of the simulated annealing search, with the team's two additions: restriction-site removal before scoring and a GC-bound feasibility check.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Fork and extend an existing, cited scientific tool instead of building a new one, and validate the extension at the bench: ReTIsigner's credibility comes from a 2.16-fold wet-lab expression result, a 52%-to-2% failure-rate benchmark, and a published 'what we are entitled to claim' section that names its own limits. A research centre should also copy their packaging discipline, one Docker command plus a documented HTTP endpoint so the engine is reusable headlessly, and their habit of sending documentation to an outside instrumentation expert before publishing it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/judging","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uni-padua-it/retisigner-svelte","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/uni-padua-it"],"rank":59},{"team":"UCAS-China","id":"6157","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"Therapeutics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/","projectTitle":"PARCEL","tagline":"An inhaled, protein-based mRNA carrier that makes airway cells produce their own asthma medicine.","summary":"PARCEL is an inhaled mRNA delivery platform built from elastin-like polypeptides (ELPs) rather than lipid nanoparticles. The fusion protein ELP-Pum2-EEP13 uses Pum2 to grab mRNA carrying a PRS tag in its 3' UTR, ELP phase separation to encapsulate and protect it, an endosomal escape peptide to get out of the endosome, and a Sortase A-conjugated anti-UPK1B scFv to target airway club cells; the cargo is mRNA for SLPI (inhibiting neutrophil elastase and the mucus hypersecretion it triggers) plus a lower dose of IFN-lambda-1 (antiviral defence and Th2 suppression). It won 1st Runner-up and Best Integrated Human Practices because the stakeholder work genuinely steered the science: clinicians moved the project from rescue therapy to maintenance therapy, and an asthma mechanism researcher killed one of the two original cargoes outright. The dry lab is unusually complete, modelling the whole chain from E. coli production through nebulizer aerosol deposition to a PK-PD readout in FEV1 and FeNO, with every module feeding the next.","awards":["1st Runner-up (Undergrad)","Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best New Composite Part — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Therapeutics Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Six connected modules sharing parameters and declared input-output relationships. (1) Production: a SimBiology ODE model of a quorum-sensing kill switch (LuxI/LuxRm A1 variant, pLuxRm-driven lysis) combining growth-dependent dilution with AHL kinetics, swept across Anderson promoters to find the one that lyses the culture just before ELP phase transition - this chose the promoter the wet lab used. (2) Assembly: AlphaFold 3 predicted the three fusion-protein monomers (1052-1322 residues), coarse-grained into Martini 3 and run in GROMACS for 2-3 microseconds at 298 K, 1 bar, 150 mM NaCl, 50 mg/mL protein, with pLDDT used to justify stabilising the Pum2 fold while leaving ELP flexible; alongside this they built CAPE (Coverage and Assembly Prediction for Protein Ensembles), a reduced free-energy model calibrated against wet-lab data, and used Bayesian optimization over CAPE as the objective to redistribute ELP repeats around Pum2 instead of brute-forcing every combination. (3) Pulmonary delivery: MPPD v3.04 on the adult Yeh-Schum five-lobe geometry screened log-normal aerosol size distributions, flow rates and inhaled volumes against deposition in the last three conducting generations per lobe, then ANSYS Fluent CFD simulated 3D airway transport, feeding a distal airway and airway-surface-liquid transport model and a reference cell-composition mapping that converts deposition into surface contacts per club cell. (4) Intracellular release: a hybrid ODE-stochastic model of one particle per endosome (median 560 nm, ~2,400 fusion chains and therefore 2,400 EEP copies per particle), tracking acidification, ELP disassembly, EEP-mediated membrane opening and RNA degradation to output intact cytosolic RNA over time, with intact RNA tracked separately from fluorescent label. (5) PK-PD: a four-compartment model (bone marrow, blood, airway wall, epithelial lining fluid) with 25 species, 53 assignment rules and 51 reactions, adapted from Gadkar et al.'s severe asthma immune network, giving four clinical readouts - FeNO, FEV1, relative goblet-cell count and 15-minute mucin release - used to compare SLPI:IFN-lambda-1 payload ratios and dosing frequency. (6) Response prediction: an affine mapping baseline plus residual CellFlow (flow matching) trained on public cytokine-perturbation scRNA-seq.","dataviz":"The model landing page opens with a scripted animation that walks the whole mechanism in sequence - the E. coli shell fills with protein, lyses, chains assemble into PARCEL, the particle travels into the bronchi, binds a club-cell receptor, is endocytosed, disassembles, releases mRNA, and mucus drops one step per round - with replay and skip controls and a text fallback when JavaScript is off. AlphaFold monomer structures are embedded as interactive viewers with pLDDT and protein-region colouring and cartoon/C-alpha toggles, so a reader can see directly why Pum2 was treated as rigid and ELP as flexible. The wiki also carries font-size controls (A-/Default/A+) for accessibility. On the software side, the AeroEase Android app turns raw device timestamps into an expandable-card Overview with a seven-day activity chart and a date-filtered History, and the FeroEase app plots live temperature, pH and OD600 with a falling-trend detector that flags the harvest window.","datascience":"Two concrete pieces. The target screen: using the Human Lung Cell Atlas they compared healthy adult club cells against other lung epithelial cells on fold change, adjusted p-value and detection fraction, and from 68 genes passing the criteria selected UPK1B - 2.28-fold enriched, adjusted p = 0.00559, RNA detected in 45.92% of club cells versus 3.43% of other epithelial cells. They then checked antibody accessibility (Human Surfaceome; UniProt O75841, 260 aa, four transmembrane regions, extracellular stretches at 37-60 and 108-229) and cross-tissue selectivity (archived GTEx: 0.11 TPM lung vs 21.90 TPM bladder; Human Protein Atlas showing membranous expression in urothelium), and stated plainly that UPK1B is not club-cell exclusive so the analysis prioritises an experimental candidate rather than demonstrating selective targeting. The response-prediction benchmark is equally honest: affine mapping cut mean-expression error 7.1% against predicting no change and beat the mean-response baseline, while the nonlinear residual CellFlow model improved some mean-expression predictions but worsened distributional agreement and the selected RNA panels - so they kept affine as the reference model and named cross-donor validation and RNA reconstruction as the open problems. Tasks were set up as donor-held-out rotations on GSE249307 and GSE246368 with unpaired source and target cells.","software":"Four software deliverables. AeroEase, an Android companion app for the nebulizer, syncs ESP32-C3 button timestamps over BLE through a validated handshake with checksum, session and duplicate checks, clock calibration from the phone, and acknowledgement only after durable storage, then presents them as a locally stored medication-use diary with daily and seven-day counts, date-filtered history, CSV export, and a rule-based offline assistant that answers questions without any cloud call; the team repeatedly states the records document device actions, not delivered dose or confirmed inhalation. FeroEase, written in Flutter, monitors the two-stage fermentation over Wi-Fi with temperature, pH, stirring control and a falling-OD600 trend detector that identifies the lysis harvest window a bench fermenter does not report. The bioinformatics pipeline for club-cell target selection is the third. A browser-based quiz game supports the education activities. Hardware: AeroEase is a portable vibrating-mesh nebulizer on an ESP32-C3 with rechargeable power, session logging, BLE, a gravity-fed reservoir and physically separated liquid and electronics compartments in a modular 3D-printed housing; FeroEase is an ESP32-S3 two-stage culture platform with pH and temperature sensing, controlled heating, motor stirring and transmitted-light OD600 tracking. Build resources and a user manual with source code access are published on the wiki.","stack":["AlphaFold 3","GROMACS","Martini 3 coarse-grained force field","MATLAB SimBiology","MPPD v3.04 (Multiple-Path Particle Dosimetry)","ANSYS Fluent","CellFlow (flow matching)","Human Lung Cell Atlas (HLCA)","Human Surfaceome","UniProt","GTEx","Human Protein Atlas","GEO (GSE249307, GSE246368)","Flutter","Android / Kotlin","ESP32-C3","ESP32-S3","Bluetooth Low Energy","SQLite (on-device)"],"techniques":["Coarse-grained molecular dynamics","Reduced free-energy modelling (custom CAPE model)","Bayesian optimization for sequence design","Quorum-sensing ODE modelling with growth dilution","Computational fluid dynamics aerosol deposition","Regional lung dosimetry screening","Hybrid ODE-stochastic single-endosome simulation","Compartmental PK-PD with Hill-function assignment rules","Differential expression with detection-fraction filtering","Affine distribution mapping","Flow matching for single-cell perturbation response","Donor-held-out cross-validation","Membrane topology and surfaceome annotation screening","SWOT analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was designed from two findings in their own clinician interviews and 461-person questionnaire: patients stop medication once they feel better, and parents refuse to start it for children out of fear of side effects, so compliance - not just awareness - became the target. Activities are segmented by audience. For children: a booth with biology-themed games at the Huairou District National Science Popularization Month fair (September 2026), doubling as a questionnaire collection point, plus a Life Science Classroom proposal for fifth and sixth graders in Huairou primary schools teaching respiratory tract structure through illustrations and interactive demos, published as a downloadable PDF so other teams can run it. For university students: an iGEM Winter Camp training new members with offline wet-lab tutorials (plasmid extraction, PCR, homologous recombination, protein purification) and online dry-lab and modelling tracks, and an annual Microbial Painting competition open to all majors as a recruitment funnel - which failed to produce the expected colony patterns, and the team published the three reasons why (cell sedimentation from static suspension, insufficient streaking density, IPTG induction of EGFP suppressing growth) and used it to teach participants that negative results are normal and troubleshooting is the skill. For the general public: a Public Science Day stall and a browser quiz game on synthetic biology and iGEM.","ihp":"The team formalised its own cycle, RCSF (Reflect-Connect-Share-Feedback), as a deliberate parallel to the DBTL engineering cycle, and ran the project through nine documented iterations with 20 individuals, teams and institutions across six defined communities (academia, medical professionals, industry, supervisors, patients and families, the public), 44 asthma patient representatives and 419 public representatives from under-10s to over-60s. Each community is introduced with who they are, what they know, and why their position matters. The changes are specific and traceable. Doctor Fei Wang's input moved the project from rapid-acting therapy to asthma maintenance treatment, which reset the whole target population. Professor Huaiyong Chen, who studies respiratory disease mechanisms, explained that in asthma many club cells differentiate into mucus-producing goblet cells - supporting the shift away from immune cells to airway epithelium - and told them that NKX2-1, one of their two original cargoes, matters in development but has little effect in adulthood, so it was dropped and SLPI took its place. Professor Pingsheng Liu's survey of liposome, LNP and polymeric carrier limitations fed directly into the ELP carrier rationale and the protein sequence design. Professor Yufei Xia flagged subtle flaws in the experimental design and walked them through the standard in vivo/in vitro validation workflow. Yaozhong Cao, a former iGEMer, argued with them about target specificity, which is upstream of the UPK1B antibody screen. Professor Xiaoru Huang, an ethics of science and technology researcher, reviewed the biosafety and bioethics discipline. They contacted the Huairou District Bureau of Landscaping and Forestry about local pollen and catkin management, grounding the project in Beijing's own regulation (the Measures implementing the Forest Law, effective 1 January 2026) and in observed thunderstorm asthma cases. Patient work was handled with explicit informed consent and privacy statements, and the questionnaire reports its own demographics (21 recovered, 21 currently medicated, 419 unaffected). The team states its guiding principle as 'local people solving local problems', and the HP group's standing job was to track every subteam's bottlenecks and feed external input back into each one.","realWorld":"The entrepreneurship work is a self-critical commercial assessment rather than a pitch. The team states outright that PARCEL's case must rest on defined unmet needs rather than technological novelty, and tabulates the strengths as well as the limitations of inhaled corticosteroids, bronchodilators and biologics before positioning PARCEL as a non-hormonal, lower-frequency, modular maintenance option. The SWOT names real weaknesses: delivery efficiency and duration of expression were only shown in HEK293T in vitro, so mucus, mucociliary clearance, local enzymes and immune uptake remain unaddressed; a combined protein-plus-mRNA product multiplies CMC burden across ELP molecular weight, purity, impurity profile and phase-transition behaviour and mRNA sequence integrity and stability, then payload ratio, condensate characteristics, batch consistency and storage; and the team lists the expertise it does not have (toxicology, regulatory affairs, clinical development, IP, market access). Opportunities are equally concrete - the modular Pum2/PRS-tag design means a new payload needs no new carrier, CAS institutes supply the analytical capability they lack, and China's pay-for-performance medical insurance pilot offers a reimbursement pathway for a therapy that can demonstrate efficacy. They commit to re-reviewing the SWOT at each development milestone.","partners":["Institute of Biophysics, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Institute of Process Engineering, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Institute of Chemistry, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Tsinghua University","Haihe Hospital, Tianjin University","Inner Mongolia International Hospital of Mongolian Medicine","Qingdao Municipal Hospital","Central Hospital of Kaifeng, Henan Province","Meishan Cardio Cerebrovascular Disease Hospital","242 Hospital, Shenyang Medical College","Weikang Pingsheng (Beijing) Biotechnology Co., Ltd.","Huairou District Bureau of Landscaping and Forestry, Beijing","iGEM ENTER team (Harvard Medical School)","Conference of China iGEMer Community (CCiC)","Huairou District primary schools","GenScript","MathWorks","SnapGene"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/humanpractices/hp-overview0.avif","caption":"The Human Practices overview: 20 individuals and institutions across six defined communities, 44 asthma patient representatives and 419 public representatives, mapped onto nine project iterations of the team's own RCSF cycle.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/software/upk1b-volcano-2.avif","caption":"The club-cell antibody target screen: UPK1B highlighted in the differential-expression volcano against other lung epithelial cells, 2.28-fold enriched with adjusted p = 0.00559 and detected in 45.92% of club cells.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/hardware/aeroease/pictures/fig6-hardware-2-yhz.avif","caption":"The AeroEase portable vibrating-mesh nebulizer prototype, built on an ESP32-C3 with BLE session logging and a modular 3D-printed housing separating the liquid reservoir from the electronics.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/education/education-huairou0.avif","caption":"The team's booth at the Huairou District National Science Popularization Month fair, running biology games with children while collecting the public asthma-perception questionnaire.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/software/app-charts-3up.avif","caption":"The AeroEase Android app turning raw BLE device timestamps into a medication-use diary with daily and seven-day activity charts, date-filtered history and CSV export, all stored on the phone.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Steal the RCSF cycle: UCAS-China gave human practices the same named, repeatable four-step loop (Reflect, Connect, Share, Feedback) that engineering gets with DBTL, mapped every stakeholder into one of six communities with a stated reason for including them, and then showed nine iterations where the loop closed - including dropping a cargo gene because one researcher said it does nothing in adults. A school research centre can run that loop on any project, and the second lesson is just as cheap to copy: publish what failed, as they did with the microbial painting that never grew, and the reasons why.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part1","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part2","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part3","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part4","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part5","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part6","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/measurement"],"rank":212},{"team":"HongKong-JSS","id":"6158","section":"High School","country":"Hong Kong","village":"Agriculture","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/","projectTitle":"MerMAID - MerR-based Mercury Assay for In-field Detection","tagline":"One mercury ion among a billion molecules of water - a living sensor that glows red, read by a portable detector fed by a modular sample-extraction platform.","summary":"HongKong-JSS is a four-school joint team (Y.W.C.A. Hioe Tjo Yoeng College, Po Leung Kuk Celine Ho Yam Tong College, Pentecostal School, Yan Oi Tong Tin Ka Ping Secondary School) that built a complete mercury-screening system for agricultural water and soil: a MerR-regulated E. coli biosensor expressing mRFP1e, a portable modular fluorescence reader, and a motorised sample-extraction platform that turns soil and water into a standard assay input. They designed, built and tested 13 biosensor prototypes - 2 aptamer-based and 11 protein-based - of which two protein designs responded to Hg2+, with the best showing low background, a response down to about 1 ppb (0.005 uM, roughly sixfold below the WHO drinking-water guideline of 6 ug/L), no cross-response to Cd2+, Pb2+ or Zn2+ at the concentrations tested, and retained function after alginate encapsulation. It took 1st Runner-up Grand Prize in the High School section and won Best Village Project, and the reason is visible on every page: the wiki's standing rule is that every number carries its conditions and a source, and the team separates demonstrated results from deployment claims more rigorously than most university teams.","awards":["1st Runner-up (High School)","Top 10 (High School)","Best New Composite Part — Nominee (High School)","Best Model — Nominee (High School)","Best Hardware — Nominee (High School)","Best Presentation — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Winner (Agriculture / Food & Nutrition / Fashion & Cosmetics / Art & Design)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"The headline model is a calibration-and-inverse-prediction framework rather than a mechanistic simulation, and it is unusually well executed. Image signals were defined explicitly before any fitting: colour response C = 1 - (G_pellet / G_white) from green-channel intensity measured in ImageJ on pellets photographed against a white tile, and fluorescence response F = R_pellet / R_background from red-channel intensity inside a light box, with the matched untreated control from the same run and timepoint subtracted (delta-C, delta-F) and no OD normalisation. They then fitted a log-linear model delta-S = A + b log10(c / 0.005 uM) with 0.005 uM chosen as the reference so A is directly interpretable, giving delta-C = 0.04363 + 0.07591 log10(c/0.005) and delta-F = 0.42290 + 1.08721 log10(c/0.005). Crucially, calibration and validation data were separated up front - 20 calibration measurements from four runs (2, 6, 8 and 13 October) and 10 held-out validation measurements from two further runs (14 and 15 October) for each readout, all at the four-hour timepoint across 0.005, 0.01, 0.03, 0.05 and 0.1 uM. The model was inverted to estimate unknown concentration, c-hat = 0.005 uM x 10^((delta-S - A)/b), and scored by RMSE on the held-out runs: approximately 0.0483 uM for colour and 0.0319 uM for fluorescence, with all numerical predictions included so that failures were not hidden (a 0.1 uM sample on 15 October was estimated at 0.246 uM by colour). On the hardware side they ran CAD-based dry-lab studies for a UV advanced-oxidation-process reactor, modelling fluid flow dynamics, UV photo-oxidation kinetics and lamp exposure times for both a batch quartz-tube reactor and a thin-layer millifluidic cassette, computing a residence time of 192.6 s and a modelled minimum-to-maximum light ratio of 92.2% across the 1 mm layer; the filter assembly was developed parametrically in OpenSCAD. Promoter selection also used in silico promoter-strength analysis, which predicted J23106 to be comparable to P429 and so added it as a comparison design.","dataviz":"Presentation is built around separating what was measured from what is assumed. Charts show individual run points alongside coloured means and error bars rather than only pooled summaries, the model page plots known versus estimated concentration against a dashed perfect-prediction line with out-of-calibration-range estimates marked separately, and tables consistently split 'experimental finding' from 'design goal' and 'prototype performance' from 'deployment claim'. Every figure caption states its conditions. The hardware readout itself is a data-visualisation artefact: Detection Unit V3.0 pairs a Hamamatsu C12880 miniature spectrometer (288-pixel detector, 340-850 nm, 12 nm typical FWHM) with two ESP32-S3-N16R8 microcontrollers driving a local TFT display and a Wi-Fi-connected web interface that shows the recorded spectrum and the raw signal at a user-selected target wavelength, with online data logging, registered devices, selected-wavelength trends and recorded runs reviewable on a phone or computer. A red/amber/green status display was added later, and the team deliberately separated device status, fluorescence reading, concentration estimate and safety judgement on screen so that a green operational light cannot be misread as 'this sample is safe'.","datascience":"The analysis is notable for publishing its own limitations as a work plan. They state that the pooled model does not separately estimate run-to-run variation, that the reported concentrations are point estimates with no uncertainty intervals, that no detection or quantification limit has been established, and that rounded signal values were used as fitting inputs. They then specify the fixes concretely: a random-intercept mixed model delta-S_ri = A + b log10(c_ri/0.005) + u_r + e_ri with run-specific slopes only if the data support them and with convergence and singularity checks; inverse-calibration intervals that distinguish prediction for a new run from prediction for a run containing a measured reference standard; a per-run intercept correction A_r = delta-S_standard,r - b log10(c_standard/0.005) estimated from a known standard run alongside every experiment; leave-one-run-out cross-validation over six folds with the warning that selecting and evaluating on the same cross-validation results produces optimistic estimates; and comparison of the local log-linear fit against a four-parameter logistic model if data across 0.001-5 uM becomes available. They also ran and reported an exploratory sensitivity check swapping calibration runs, and honestly noted that because the validation runs were examined during that comparison they no longer constitute a completely untouched final assessment. Results-page statistics use two-sided paired t-tests across five independent paired runs with explicit notice that p-values are unadjusted for the three readouts. Hardware performance was quantified the same way: automated water delivery gave a coefficient of variation of 1.95% against 7.41% across 10 manual syringe runs, drainage retained volumes of 0.28, 0.31 and 0.30 mL (mean ~0.30 mL, about 95% liquid recovery), and reducing tubing bore from 7 mm to 1 mm over a 10 cm segment was computed to cut geometric dead volume accordingly.","software":"Open hardware and firmware rather than a bioinformatics tool. The Detection Unit CAD is published in Onshape (https://cad.onshape.com/documents/2228cb45d05bdbc67479791d) and the firmware and software at https://github.com/hkjssplkchc2026/mercurysense, containing ESP32 firmware, the C12880 spectrometer driver, acquisition instructions and Python/web monitoring software, with a live monitoring web app for reviewing spectra and runs. The Extraction Unit code is at https://github.com/davidlueng986-alt/MerMAID-Extraction-Unit. An itemised bill of materials is published with a ~USD 357 core-component subtotal (LED 2x 560 nm at USD 2.60, etc.), and the team states plainly that this overshot the USD 40 affordability target stakeholders had asked for. The detection unit went through four versions (AS7343 14-channel spectral sensor in a laser-cut acrylic box, to an opaque 3D-printed enclosure with recessed lid and adjustable optical mounts, to the C12880 spectrometer with interchangeable filters, fluid handling and web interface) and the extraction platform through three, with state-machine control sequences published for the water, soil and UV-AOP modules together with error-handling tests covering pump timeouts, watchdog recovery, soil-mass checks, stirring feedback, reagent availability and weighing faults. An educational browser game, 'Small Signal: a MerMAID field school', is published as a 3-5 minute pixel-art laboratory game in which players build a biological signal path, compare an unknown sample with its controls, and decide what the evidence supports - and is labelled a beta prototype whose educational effectiveness has not yet been evaluated. Repository: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/hongkong-jss.","stack":["ImageJ (green/red channel intensity extraction)","log-linear calibration with inverse prediction","RMSE on held-out runs","planned random-intercept mixed model and leave-one-run-out cross-validation","four-parameter logistic model (proposed)","paired two-sided t-tests","Onshape CAD","OpenSCAD (parametric filter assembly)","3D printing and laser cutting","ESP32-S3-N16R8 microcontrollers","Hamamatsu C12880MA miniature spectrometer","AS7343 spectral sensor","Python / web monitoring interface","GitHub","CFD and UV photo-oxidation kinetics dry-lab simulation","alginate encapsulation"],"techniques":["pre-registered split of calibration and validation runs before fitting","control subtraction within the same run and timepoint","inverse calibration to estimate unknown concentrations","flagging estimates outside the calibration range instead of reporting them silently","including failed predictions in the error metric","exploratory calibration-set sensitivity check, with disclosure that it contaminated the held-out set","publishing a specific statistical work plan for unmodelled run-to-run variance","coefficient-of-variation comparison of automated versus manual fluid handling","spectral matching of LED, excitation filter and reporter emission to suppress background","interface design that separates device status from safety judgement"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Nine targeted outreach events reaching over 2,400 participants, organised as an explicit four-tier framework. Tier 1 (primary students and parents, over 1,600 people) ran interactive Open Day and Information Day booths at Y.W.C.A. Hioe Tjo Yoeng College and Po Leung Kuk Celine Ho Yam Tong College with strawberry DNA extraction and live gel electrophoresis demonstrations. Tier 2 (over 730 secondary peers) covered Microbiology 101 for Grade 10 students on microbe handling, laboratory safety, cleanliness and contamination prevention, and a morning assembly for 700 Grade 7-12 students that framed synthetic biology through agricultural mercury contamination and explained how cellular components can be repurposed as logic gates and biosensors. Tier 3 is the most durable: a two-day Teacher Biotech Training Workshop for 30 local school teachers covering PCR and agarose gel electrophoresis, combined with a standing loan programme letting those teachers borrow DNA samples and laboratory equipment from the team - a deliberate attempt to leave teaching capacity behind after the competition ends. Tier 4 took the work into public and civic space: an exhibitor stand at InnoEX 2026, the international innovation and technology exhibition in Hong Kong, and a presentation alongside the Drainage Services Department at its Annual Media Briefing, which put the project on an official government webpage and into local news. They also ran a street interview with 30 members of the public specifically about a 'mercury-free' produce certification label, finding unanimous support for the idea, 14 people rating it very important for public health, 12 supporting it even at higher vegetable prices, 22 willing to buy certified produce at normal prices and 4 willing to pay extra - while also recording that 19 respondents remained somewhat or very worried about engineered bacteria leaking from the sealed device against only 8 who were not, a finding they published rather than buried. The browser game 'Small Signal' extends this by teaching the reasoning - compare the unknown against its controls, then decide what the evidence can support.","ihp":"Human Practices ran on a stated operating principle - respond promptly to stakeholder feedback, and let it drive the next hardware revision - structured across four stages and a five-question cycle (Inspiration, Intercommunication, Investigation, Implementation, Impact) with the rule that each engagement must end in either a documented change or a documented reason for no change. There is also a five-tier Target User Prioritisation Pyramid which they explicitly label a working framework rather than a validated exposure ranking, and which puts underserved communities near suspected contamination sources such as informal e-waste processing sites above farmers, researchers, regulators and the general public. The traceable changes: the Drainage Services Department's guidance moved them off the chromoproteins used in earlier projects onto mRFP1e as a dual readout - visible colour for non-specialists plus a more sensitive fluorescence channel for quantification - and DSD, the Water Supplies Department and university researchers together pushed them to test lower concentrations, benchmark against international reference values, and assess cross-response to interfering heavy metals (hence the Cd2+/Pb2+/Zn2+ selectivity panel). Koo's Farm and local wet market vendors demanded numerical readings instead of qualitative colour indicators, which is the direct reason the AS7343 14-channel sensor was replaced by the wavelength-resolved Hamamatsu C12880 and why a numerical display and web readout exist. University professors said soil particulates must be removed before optical measurement and recommended a modular design, which produced the separate water, soil and planned organic-mercury extraction modules. Users reported excessive syringe force on the V1 filter stack (2 um pre-filter plus 0.45 um membrane), so manual syringe delivery was replaced by pump-driven dosing - measured as an improvement from 7.41% to 1.95% coefficient of variation. On-site trials at Blue Girl Organic Farm and Yeung Marine Products Limited with agricultural soil extracts, irrigation water and fish pond water (run without GM bacteria, since they could not legally be taken out of the lab) produced requests for clearer indicators and better protection against water and mud, answered with a red/amber/green status display, a reinforced housing, and maintenance access and modularity designed into the proposed V4.0. A second public survey combining street interviews with hands-on demonstrations for 195 people found 87.7% more confident that ordinary people could participate in checking soil and water safety and 84.1% finding the digital readout easy to understand - and the team immediately noted that perceived confidence and readability do not establish analytical accuracy, and separated device status from safety judgement in the interface as a direct result. More Happy Farm said plainly that not every farmer wants to own a device, which pushed them towards a service model. Throughout, they keep stating what their evidence does not cover: a response in a controlled biological assay does not establish accurate measurement in soil extracts, reliable operation of the complete device, or suitability for regulatory decisions.","realWorld":"The commercial concept is Certification-as-a-Service rather than instrument sales: farmers pay an annual subscription, the team visits to test soil on site with MerMAID, passing farms receive a 'Heavy Metal-Free' certificate and logo for supermarket packaging that lets them command a premium, and random surprise re-tests throughout the year (affordable precisely because the test is cheap and fast) guard the certificate's integrity. The plan is backed by a SWOT matrix, a competitor comparison against off-site laboratory testing (HKD 500+ per test, weeks of turnaround) and quick chemical test strips, and a five-step business plan naming AFCD, the Hong Kong Organic Resource Centre and premium supermarkets as the partners whose endorsement would make the certificate a standard. What lifts it above a typical student business plan is the case-study section: they analyse three real commercial analogues - Hygiena's reader-plus-consumables-plus-records model, SGS's accredited food-safety testing service, and Hong Kong's AFCD/VMO Accredited Farm Scheme - and draw out the uncomfortable implications, including that MerR-responsive bioavailable Hg(II) and a reference method's total-mercury result may be different measurands that cannot be assumed to agree just because both are labelled mercury, that a logo on packaging cannot replace a sample-to-report record tied to farm, location, date, sample ID and method scope, and that the full cost per valid result must include consumables, controls, staff time, storage, failed runs and waste handling. They state outright that the USD 350 build cost missed the USD 40 target, that the certification journey is a proposed direction, that the current prototype does not establish that any farm or product is heavy-metal-free, and that they claim no certification authority. Real-world constraints are acknowledged rather than wished away: engineered microbes cannot legally be placed in bins or taken into the field in Hong Kong, the on-site demonstrations therefore ran without GM bacteria, and the sub-five-minute loading and readout sequence is distinguished from the five hours of reporter expression the actual assay needs.","partners":["Drainage Services Department (DSD), Hong Kong - external adviser, including Dr Van","Environmental Protection Department (EPD)","Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD)","Water Supplies Department (WSD)","WEEE-PARK","Dr. Li Wai Chin, The Education University of Hong Kong","Prof. Liang and CUHK researchers","Blue Girl Organic Farm","Yeung Marine Products Limited","HKFYG Jockey Club Organic Farm","Koo's Farm","More Happy Farm","local wet market vendors","InnoEX 2026","IDT, New England Biolabs, Twist Bioscience, miniPCR/miniOne, BTEA (sponsors)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6158/wiki/model/figure-1.avif","caption":"Control-subtracted colour and pellet-fluorescence signals against log mercury concentration, with each calibration run coloured separately and the pooled log-linear fit overlaid.","credit":"Team HongKong-JSS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6158/wiki/model/figure-2.avif","caption":"Known versus estimated mercury concentration for the held-out validation runs, against a dashed perfect-prediction line, with estimates falling outside the calibration range marked separately rather than dropped.","credit":"Team HongKong-JSS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6158/wiki/hardware/hw13.avif","caption":"Detection Unit V3.0: the Hamamatsu C12880 miniature spectrometer with its driver board, an ESP32-S3-N16R8 for Wi-Fi, and the mobile web interface showing the recorded spectrum and raw signal at a selected wavelength - the upgrade stakeholders forced by demanding numbers, not colours.","credit":"Team HongKong-JSS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6158/wiki/education/edu05-new-png.avif","caption":"The two-day Teacher Biotech Training Workshop for 30 local school teachers, covering PCR and agarose gel electrophoresis and backed by an ongoing DNA sample and equipment loan programme so the teaching capacity outlasts the team.","credit":"Team HongKong-JSS, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Adopt their calibration discipline wholesale: split runs into calibration and validation before fitting anything, subtract a control from the same run and timepoint, invert the fit to predict unknowns, report RMSE on the held-out runs with the failures left in, flag out-of-range estimates, and then publish the statistical work you have not done yet (run-aware mixed model, inverse-calibration uncertainty intervals, leave-one-run-out cross-validation, a per-run reference standard) as a named plan. The second lesson is their teacher-capacity play - training 30 local teachers in PCR and gel electrophoresis and lending them the reagents and kit - which multiplies a school team's reach far more cheaply than any one-off workshop.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-jss/contribution","https://github.com/hkjssplkchc2026/mercurysense","https://github.com/davidlueng986-alt/MerMAID-Extraction-Unit","https://cad.onshape.com/documents/2228cb45d05bdbc67479791d","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/hongkong-jss","https://api.igem.org/v1/teams/6158"],"rank":219},{"team":"GHCIS","id":"6166","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"Climate Crisis, Conservation & Bioremediation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/","projectTitle":"ARGUS","tagline":"A paper-based toehold-switch assay plus a browser-based image-analysis and surveillance platform for environmental antimicrobial resistance gene monitoring.","summary":"ARGUS pairs a cell-free, paper-based toehold switch assay for three antimicrobial resistance genes (mcr-1, blaNDM-1, blaKPC-2) with portable temperature-control hardware and a full-stack web platform that reads the colorimetric plate from a phone photo, grades each gene, and stores location-linked records for regional review. Recombinase polymerase amplification feeds trigger RNA to switches that drive colour-producing enzymes — blue for MCR1 (GusA), yellow for NDM1 (XylE), purple-shifted for KPC2 (LacZ). The project is framed inside the One Health approach, targeting the gap between institution-led metagenomic surveys and the routine, fine-grained environmental monitoring that networks like CARSS do not cover. It won Best Software and Best Part Collection on the strength of a genuinely deployable platform (Docker or native Node, SQLite, role-based access, documented deployment) and a NUPACK design pipeline that reproduced a published sequence grammar and then found an error in it — all presented with an unusually disciplined limitations section that refuses to claim sensitivity, specificity or a detection limit it has not measured.","awards":["Top 10 (High School)","Best Part Collection — Winner (High School)","Best Software — Winner (High School)","Best Wiki — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Nominee (Climate Crisis / Conservation / Bioremediation)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"The modelling is a toehold switch design and screening pipeline in locally installed NUPACK 4.1.0.1 (rna95-nupack3 Serra-Turner 1995 material model, 37 degC, 1.0 M Na+, 0 M Mg2+, 100 nM switch and 100 nM trigger, maximum complex size 2). They reproduced the 36-nt mRNA sensor sequence grammar from Table S4 of Green et al. 2014 exactly, and verified the construction formula against four published sequences (mCherry B, mCherry C, cat, aadA) with exact matches on all four before designing anything new. Each switch is 126 nt: GGG leader, 30-nt exposed toehold, 6-bp target-driven invasion region, stem-loop containing the RBS and AUG, 9-nt programmed refolding module, 21-nt linker and the first 9 nt of GFP. For every 36-nt window across each complete CDS they computed the NUPACK base-pairing matrix of the full mRNA and scored target local single-strandedness, switch toehold single-strandedness, the normalised ensemble defect of the intended OFF conformation, and mean occupancy of the 15 expected stem base pairs — excluding windows with GC content below 30% or above 70%, four consecutive identical bases, a GGGG stretch created by the GGG leader, or a stop codon in the switch reading frame, and requiring candidate start positions at least 15 nt apart. Notably, they found that Equation 5 of the original supplementary information is internally inconsistent (it describes higher local single-strandedness as preferable while ranking the total score low-to-high), kept the published weights of 5, 4 and 3, and converted both single-strandedness terms into deficits so that lower penalty ranks better. Conservation screening used family CDSs from NCBI AMRFinderPlus release 2026-08-07.1: the primary designs cover 98/98 blaNDM, 295/295 blaKPC and 37/37 mcr-1 panel sequences exactly, with the alternatives' exceptions named (NDM1-S3 misses blaNDM-56, KPC2-S3 misses blaKPC-248). Cross-reactivity was computed as a tube-equilibrium matrix: cognate activation proxies of 0.906 (NDM1-S1), 0.940 (KPC2-S1) and 0.873 (MCR1-S1) against off-diagonal values from 8.10e-07 to 2.92e-04. Input integrity was checked and hashed (SHA256 prefixes recorded per file), including catching that the blaNDM-1 SnapGene record is stored in reverse orientation so the reverse complement had to be used — exactly the kind of silent error that would have produced a useless trigger.","dataviz":"Figures are oriented so higher is always better on the candidate-metrics panel, which is a small but real accessibility decision. The NUPACK cross-gene activation proxy matrix is drawn as a heatmap of the three primary designs so the three-order-of-magnitude gap between diagonal and off-diagonal is immediately legible. The sensor sequence architecture is rendered as an annotated 126-nt schematic. In the software, the data visualisation is the product: the administration console combines maps, regional rankings, per-gene trend charts and record-level inspection, with configurable alert rules and CSV/JSON export, while the client presents each plate as a four-grade result per gene (Negative, Weak Positive, Positive, Strong Positive) over the reviewed photograph. An annotated one-sample example plate figure documents the expected output for a published example image so anyone can reproduce the workflow end to end. The team is explicit that this example is a reproducible workflow demonstration, not a validation dataset, and that regional maps describe submitted records under the selected filters rather than population prevalence.","datascience":"The colour-grading algorithm is the headline and it is specified completely enough to reimplement. Analysis runs locally in JavaScript in the browser with no external AI or image-conversion service at runtime. The pipeline prepares the image (max dimension 2400 px; grid detection on a working image of max 1400 px), locates the active well grid (9, 12 or 15 wells for one, two or three loaded samples, always including the positive and negative control in every gene row), averages all sRGB pixels inside a disk of half the well radius, converts through linear RGB and XYZ D65 to CIE Lab, and then deliberately discards lightness L*, projecting only on a* and b* so the score tracks chromatic change. For a sample s with same-row negative control n and positive control p it computes the control direction v = p - n, a normalised score t = ((s - n) . v) / (v . v), and a residual r = ||(s - n) - tv||, so NC sits at t = 0 and PC at t = 1 and scores are not clamped to that interval. Quality gates are quantitative: control separation must satisfy ||v|| >= 2, the residual must satisfy r <= max(4, 0.6 x ||v||), expected directions are delta-b < 0 for MCR1, delta-b > 0 for NDM1 and delta-a - delta-b > 0 for KPC2, and missing, black, identical, reversed or insufficiently separated controls or |t| > 100 produce no estimate at all. Crucially, an absent classification is defined as unresolved and the documentation states it must not be treated as Negative — a failure mode most teams would silently default. Per-gene thresholds are published (MCR1 t < 0.20 Negative to t >= 1.60 Strong Positive; NDM1 and KPC2 on their own scales), along with the deliberately awkward note that a sample at MCR1 t = 1.00 is Weak Positive under the sample thresholds even though the PC label displays Strong Positive, because the PC and NC labels are control-role defaults rather than independent validation. The limitations section then declines to claim what was not measured: the 0, 3 and 5 micromolar trigger RNA conditions do not establish sensitivity, specificity, a detection limit or a concentration calibration, and the team states that large-scale real-world samples will be needed to set optimal colour thresholds and evaluate sensitivity, specificity, NPV and PPV. Wet-lab feedback iteratively adjusted the colour interpretation and capture workflow, and the team notes this aligned software with their process but does not substitute for testing across devices and lighting conditions.","software":"ARGUS is a complete, deployable web platform released at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/ghcis/argus with a hosted client, a user guide PDF, a README quick start and a lossless example plate image (assets/example-plate.webp). Architecture: a browser client for field users and an administration console for institutions, with image processing in the browser and reviewed results validated server-side and persisted to SQLite, with documented backup procedures. Deployment is genuinely turnkey — Docker Engine or Desktop with Compose v2 via scripts/deploy.sh, or native Node.js (24.21.0 pinned in .nvmrc, documented minimum 22.16) via scripts/deploy-native.mjs, both of which build, check readiness and print local and LAN access links; public HTTPS deployment takes a domain and email, and automatic street-level location uses a Geoapify key. Three roles are implemented — field user, administrator and root — where field users work only with their own captures, administrators review regional data, export records and manage alerts, and root manages accounts and audit or demonstration-data operations; passwords are 12-200 characters, an initial root password is generated privately, and existing passwords survive restart and redeployment. Records are searchable by gene, result and date, downloadable as JSON summaries, and printable to PDF. The team also honestly flags that local analysis still depends on the server for authentication and storage, that a retained upload draft does not make it offline, that SQLite allows only one application instance per database volume, and that institution verification, jurisdiction-specific permissions, a documented versioned integration API and connections to CARSS or WHO networks all remain future work with no endorsement claimed. Hardware is released as STEP and STL files (top cover, backing card, electronic enclosure, sleeve, sample stage, three RPA unit variants) covering the ARGUS Temperature Controller — a three-layer design giving constant temperature for RPA and the colorimetric reaction plus stable uniform illumination for software-assisted capture — and the ARGUS Test Strip Card, whose substrate was chosen with material recovery and biodegradability in mind.","stack":["NUPACK 4.1.0.1 (local install)","rna95-nupack3 / Serra-Turner 1995 material model","NCBI AMRFinderPlus (release 2026-08-07.1)","GenBank / SnapGene sequence records","JavaScript (in-browser image analysis)","Node.js 24.21.0","Docker + Compose v2","SQLite","REST API with role-based access control","Geoapify geocoding","CIE Lab colour space (sRGB -> linear RGB -> XYZ D65 -> Lab)","recombinase polymerase amplification (RPA)","cell-free paper-based expression","GusA / XylE / LacZ colorimetric reporters","STEP and STL CAD","GitLab (iGEM)"],"techniques":["exhaustive sliding-window candidate scanning over full CDSs","ensemble defect and base-pairing probability analysis","tube equilibrium cross-reactivity matrix","family-level conservation coverage screening","sequence integrity checking with SHA256 hashing and orientation verification","reproduction and error-correction of a published scoring equation","chromaticity-only colour classification (L* discarded)","same-row internal control projection with normalised score and residual","explicit quality gates and an unresolved (not Negative) failure state","staged two-round experimental validation plan with predefined replacement designs","role-based access control and audit separation","reproducible one-command deployment"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A tiered education programme with its own documented iteration framework, INSPIRE (Identify the audience, Notice prior knowledge and needs, Set learning goals, Plan content and format, Implement the activity, Review responses and feedback, Evolve the design), applied uniformly to every activity so each record shows design, delivery, feedback and the resulting change. Four audience groups are defined with explicit prior-knowledge characteristics, stage goals and formats: primary school (stories, analogies, stickers, games, scenario Q&A), junior high (pre-class questions, mechanism explanations, group discussion and retelling), high school (posters, questionnaires, lectures, cards, experiments, peer exchange) and the general public (visual-text content, social media). The feedback-to-change table is the strongest artefact: teachers preferred familiar health examples so hygiene activities and organ stickers were added; children struggled with abstract concepts so stories and analogies replaced them; poster votes lacked explanations so posters plus a questionnaire were introduced; the questionnaire revealed interests and gaps which drove targeted lectures; long lectures reduced engagement so a 'Biology Werewolf' social-deduction game was created; Werewolf rounds ran too long so a 5-8 minute online version was built; online participation lacked learning evidence so Club Fair question cards were added; students preferred hands-on learning so medicinal-herb experiments were introduced; students struggled with protein function so examples and group discussion were added. Partner feedback from Ulink prompted platform-specific adaptations for Xiaohongshu and WeChat, and questions raised at a Morning Meetup caused a revision of the afternoon pitch. The team also traced a knowledge gap found in human practices — that the public associates AMR with hospitals and personal antibiotic use but has weak understanding of the One Health links between animals, environment and humans — directly into the education content, extending it from rational antibiotic use to livestock and environmental transmission pathways.","ihp":"A deliberately sequenced, evidence-first process rather than a stakeholder list. They began by asking not 'who should we interview' but 'who would be affected, who would participate in use or deployment, and who could change the project's direction', grouping stakeholders into five exploratory categories and then deliberately starting with the public — not because the public decides anything, but to build a social-needs baseline whose gaps would determine who to approach next. A bilingual online questionnaire returned 281 valid responses: safety, result accuracy and price were selected by 72.24%, 61.57% and 46.98% of respondents; 67.26% worried about inaccurate results and 55.87% about product safety; 44.48% worried they would not know what to do after detecting a risk and 33.45% that they could not understand the results. The methodological discipline here is notable for a high-school team: because questions Q12-Q19 branched on respondents' stated first priority, the price and speed branches had small samples, so they explicitly refused to convert 'within RMB 30' or 'results within 30 minutes' into product requirements and instead carried them forward as questions to validate with frontline users. They then re-mapped stakeholders on power and interest axes, noting that the map represented types rather than named interviewees and that specific participants were identified only as each cycle raised new questions. Concrete design changes are tabulated: frontline practitioners wanted information useful for antibiotic selection, so the team retained preliminary monitoring and data support while explicitly acknowledging the unmet diagnostic need rather than overclaiming; industry advice on hardware and consumable costs replaced a proposed microfluidic format with cellulose filter paper and revised strip assembly and substrate use; and industry advice to swap the expression system for crude lysate was considered and rejected, keeping the current cell-free system because of background-interference concerns — a documented decision to not follow advice, with a reason. Expert discussions about controlled access to environmental and public-health data are what produced the three-tier role model in the software.","realWorld":"ARGUS is positioned for institution-led procurement rather than consumer sale: government monitoring programmes or authorised implementing institutions buy and organise the system, trained frontline inspectors, veterinary-service personnel or environmental monitoring staff run the screening, and farms and herders support sample access, with results recorded in a defined programme and escalated for confirmatory testing. The near-term target is preliminary screening of water samples from livestock breeding sites, with medical wastewater named as a separate adjacent application and any clinical use explicitly deferred to independent validation and regulatory assessment. The long-term scale target price is about US$3.73 per routine screen, subject to cost and production validation, with a target detection time of about 40 minutes and isothermal amplification at roughly 37-42 degC. The proposed governance model assigns national coordination, provincial review and local sample collection to different institutional levels, and the team is careful to state that implementing it requires verified organisational identities, jurisdiction-restricted permissions and defined reporting responsibilities, that possible alignment with CARSS would require agreement on remit, data definitions and reporting interfaces, and that no official endorsement, institutional adoption or network connection is claimed at this stage. Product boundaries are written into the marketing position: environmental AMR preliminary screening and trend monitoring only, never the sole basis for clinical diagnosis, administrative penalties or individual medication decisions, and an alert should prompt investigation rather than be read as a population prevalence estimate. The named development path is broader validated applications (more phones, lighting conditions, assay batches and independent samples, with a shared evaluation dataset for measuring classification consistency), then a versioned integration API with defined schemas, authentication, permissions, error responses and reproducible examples distinguishing capture identifiers from sample positions and describing location precision, sampling time, assay version, controls and quality status, then institution-led deployment.","partners":["Ulink (education platform feedback)","Frontline livestock practitioners and grassroots veterinarians","Industry advisors on hardware and consumable cost","AMR, molecular detection, environment and public-health technical experts","Agricultural and livestock management authorities (engagement target)","CARSS / China Antimicrobial Resistance Surveillance System (named alignment goal, not a partnership)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6166/wiki/software/figure-02.avif","caption":"ARGUS feature map: client functions (location, plate photographs, local four-grade analysis, personal records), console functions (regional review, alerts, exports), account management and operations.","credit":"Team GHCIS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6166/wiki/software/figure-05.avif","caption":"The local colour recognition and storage pipeline: image preparation, active well-grid detection, central well pixel sampling, conversion to CIE Lab, control validation, gene-specific grading and SQLite storage.","credit":"Team GHCIS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6166/wiki/model/candidate-nupack-metrics.avif","caption":"NUPACK metrics for the nine toehold switch candidates across mcr-1, blaNDM-1 and blaKPC-2, with every column oriented so higher values are preferable.","credit":"Team GHCIS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6166/wiki/hardware/assembled-controller.avif","caption":"The assembled ARGUS Temperature Controller with viewing window, power connection and temperature control unit, providing both the reaction environment and uniform illumination for phone-based image capture.","credit":"Team GHCIS, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Two things to copy. In software, write the algorithm down to the equation and the threshold — same-row internal controls, a chromaticity-only projection, numeric quality gates, and an explicit 'unresolved, and not to be read as Negative' state — then ship it with one-command Docker deployment and a reproducible example input, because a tool a judge can actually run beats a screenshot of one. In human practices, copy their refusal to convert a small branched survey sample into a product requirement: carrying '30 minutes' forward as a question to validate rather than a specification is the single cheapest credibility gain available to a student team.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/hardware/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/inclusivity/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ghcis/contribution/","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/ghcis/argus"],"rank":136},{"team":"HFI","id":"6178","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"Therapeutics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/","projectTitle":"TWO EcN, ONE METABOLIC MISSION - A Probiotic Synergistic Therapy for the Rare Disease Hyperlysinemia","tagline":"A dual-strain E. coli Nissle 1917 consortium, physically stapled together with SpyTag/SpyCatcher, that degrades dietary lysine in the gut so hyperlysinemia patients can escape lifelong diet restriction.","summary":"HFI (International Department, The Affiliated High School of South China Normal University, Guangzhou) built an orally delivered live biotherapeutic for hyperlysinemia, a rare AASS-mutation disorder with no approved therapy and an incidence near 1 in 411,000. They split the human saccharopine pathway across two EcN populations - an upstream strain expressing LKR and SDH, a downstream strain expressing ALDH and AASDHPPT - knocked out the endogenous ldcC lysine decarboxylase by CRISPR-Cas9 to remove background lysine consumption, and covalently coupled the two strains using eCPX-displayed SpyTag/SpyCatcher so intermediates pass between cells over a short diffusion distance. Biosafety is handled by a dual-input kill switch where an arabinose-driven Bxb1 serine integrase inverts the lysis-E cassette and an oxygen-sensing PcyoA promoter only fires outside the anaerobic gut. The project won because the dry lab genuinely steered the wet lab (NetLogo screening picked the RBS before cloning), the part collection was fully characterised with HPLC-MS, and the education and IHP programmes were unusually systematic for a high school team.","awards":["Top 10 (High School)","Best Part Collection — Nominee (High School)","Best Education — Winner (High School)","Best Presentation — Nominee (High School)","Best Wiki — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Winner (Infectious Diseases / Therapeutics / Diagnostics / Oncology)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two complementary models, both used to make decisions rather than decorate the wiki. First, an agent-based stochastic contact model in NetLogo 3D 7.0.4: 200 cells (100 SpyTag type A, 100 SpyCatcher type B) random-walk in a periodic box spanning -16 to 16 units, diffusion speed 0.17 per tick, interaction radius 1.4, 2,000 ticks; each successful A-B contact creates an undirected graph edge, decrements both cells' remaining binding capacity, and merges cluster IDs, so a bacterial aggregate is literally a connected component of a graph. They swept seven RBS parts (B0032, B0033, B0035, B0029, B0031, B0030, B0034) mapped to 5-100 surface display sites and tracked mean cluster size, max cluster size and the full size distribution, expanding the noisy 30-site condition to 60 replicate runs to characterise its stochastic variance (max 109.6 +/- 34.5). Second, a mass-conserving 13-state-variable compartmental ODE simulator of the lysine cascade: metabolites L (lysine), S (saccharopine), A (AASA) and P (AAA) across three compartments (extracellular medium, upstream SpyCatcher cytoplasm, downstream SpyTag cytoplasm), Michaelis-Menten rate laws with half-saturation 0.2, a saturating contact signal H = B/(0.05+B) converting occupied binding capacity into extra inter-strain exchange conductance g_CT, integrated with fourth-order Runge-Kutta at base step 0.001 (reported backbone mass-balance error ~3.77e-15), with toxic AASA exposure summarised by trapezoidal AUC.","dataviz":"Both models were ported to interactive browser pages embedded in the wiki, so a judge or reader can move the sliders and re-run the simulation live with no local install: the ODE metabolic simulator at https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/static/model/metabolism.html and the NetLogo cluster model at https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/static/model/netlogo.html. Outputs are dynamic cluster visualisations, metabolite time-course curves and quantitative readouts. Alongside this they reported the full cluster-size distribution (not just means), particle-weighted versus number-weighted distributions, and a table pairing each RBS part number with its translational strength tier and simulated outcome, which makes the in-silico screen auditable.","datascience":"The dry lab was used specifically to fill a measurement gap: they could not quantify the short-lived intermediates saccharopine and AASA in the wet lab, so the ODE model predicted them and exposed an explicit design trade-off - at SC:ST 2:1 lysine clearance is fastest (residual lysine 0.0519) but AASA peaks highest (0.3767, AUC 10.43), while at 1:2 AASA stays low (peak 0.1123, AUC 3.14) but 0.2980 lysine is left unconsumed. That framing (two goals that cannot be maximised at once) drove the choice of co-culture ratios actually tested. The cluster model's prediction that moderate display (B0035, 15 sites, mean max cluster 48.67 cells, about 24% of the system) sits in the usable middle regime while 50+ sites collapses 91-98% of cells into one giant component was then checked at the bench by fluorescence microscopy with ImageJ quantification of red-green overlapping cluster count and core area (3 clusters/field at 2:1 rising to 16 at 1:1 and 14 at 1:2, mean core area 15.04 to 19.41 um2), and the simulated ratio ranking matched the HPLC lysine-removal ranking. Measurement side: a cheap ninhydrin colorimetric screen was calibrated against FDAA-derivatised HPLC-MS (lysine derivative at 17.04 min, m/z 398.1, calibration R2 = 0.9985).","software":"No standalone software tool was released, but the two models are published as free-standing interactive web pages inside the wiki (metabolism.html and netlogo.html) and the complete NetLogo source (setup, go, update-cluster-distribution, avg-cluster-size, max-cluster-size, sweep-rbs, scan-catcher-series procedures) is printed in full on the Model page so another team can rerun the screen. Repository: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/hfi. They also contributed a documented part collection to the Registry including BBa_26S7HLMM (ldcC target sequence) and BBa_269KMPAL (pEcgRNA-ldcC), plus the two models listed as community contributions.","stack":["NetLogo 3D 7.0.4","agent-based / graph connected-component modelling","ODE system (13 state variables, 3 compartments)","fourth-order Runge-Kutta integration","Michaelis-Menten kinetics","trapezoidal AUC integration","interactive HTML/JS model front-ends","ImageJ","HPLC-MS (FDAA derivatisation)","CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing","GitLab"],"techniques":["in-silico RBS strength screening before cloning","replicate-expanded stochastic variance characterisation (n=60 at the unstable condition)","cluster-size distribution analysis","strain-ratio flux trade-off analysis (AASA peak vs AUC vs residual lysine)","model-to-bench validation by fluorescence microscopy + ImageJ","colorimetric assay calibrated against HPLC-MS","mass-balance error reporting as a numerical sanity check"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was designed from survey data rather than guesswork, and ran on an explicit five-step cycle they named CLEAR (Connect, Layer, Engage, Assess, Reinforce) in which every activity had to report a measured outcome and a change for the next iteration. Highlights: a self-written microbiology comic book issued in Cantonese and Mandarin dialect versions, with English, Japanese and German recorded audiobooks and text versions in Arabic, German, Russian, French, Korean, Portuguese, Japanese, Thai, Spanish and Indonesian - explicitly supplied as text so local educators can generate their own AI text-to-speech audio in languages the team does not speak. They ran a 100+ student conference at HFI International, a HFI Academic Summit with hands-on fruit DNA extraction, recorded lab-technique video lessons (pipetting, agarose gel electrophoresis, clean bench, plus an SMA rare-disease case study) on iGEM Video and Bilibili, an online Q&A session on Tencent Meeting, a university biology major exhibition poster, a Gobi Desert biology trip, a synthetic biology student society, outreach at the Guangzhou Social Welfare Institute, and a professional session with HSBC on financial literacy. Impact was measured with paired pre/post surveys: at the Academic Summit (n=24) correct identification of synthetic biology rose 79% to 96% with the 'synthetic biology is another name for genetic testing' misconception falling 8% to 0%, and correct understanding that DNA is in every living cell including fruit cells rose 42% to 88%. They also tracked platform metrics honestly, noting that 15 WeChat posts drew 248 likes, 955 views, 63 shares and zero comments, concluding one-way delivery was failing and pivoting to interactive formats, after which video lessons drew comments for the first time.","ihp":"IHP ran through a named six-step cycle (LYSERA: Listen, Yield, Shape, Execute, Reflect, Apply) across six sequenced phases - stakeholder mapping on an interest/power matrix, a 434-response public survey, technical validation, clinical and patient engagement, commercial and regulatory consultation, and peer review - then looped back through phases 3 to 6 a second time. The changes are concrete. Dr. Mingjun Sun (Sichuan University, synthetic biology and AI protein design) argued the lag between lysine detection and enzyme expression would make the probiotics miss the postprandial treatment window, so HFI abandoned the lysine-responsive promoter entirely and switched to constitutive expression of all degradation enzymes; he also warned that SpyTag/SpyCatcher aggregation risk scales with expression level and suggested building a computational model instead of trial and error, which is the direct origin of the NetLogo cluster model and the choice of medium-to-low strength RBS. Professor Liujie Huo questioned whether intestinal lysine concentration tracks blood lysine at all, forcing them to re-examine the physiological basis and repositioning of the product. The public survey changed the language of every outreach material: 'genetically modified' was replaced with 'engineered' across all materials because the survey showed the former triggered unease, and because 76.04% of respondents had heard of rare diseases but could not name one while only 8.29% could name three, education was rebuilt to start from absolute basics. Survey findings that 56.22% supported national insurance coverage and 59.45% preferred prescription-drug regulation validated the hospital-first go-to-market route and tiered pricing. Mr. Guodong Huo of the Illness Challenge Foundation insisted patients are not defined by their condition, which set a non-stigmatising language policy and a no-false-hope rule for all materials. Safety feedback (protease degradation risk) added simulated intestinal fluid stability tests to the experimental plan.","realWorld":"A full commercial dossier for EcN-BioShield covering unmet need, TAM/SAM/SOM, PESTEL, Porter's Five Forces, SWOT, competitor analysis against lysine-free medical foods such as Hyper LYS Anamix Infant (UK NHS ~GBP 54 per 400 g can, roughly GBP 1,500-2,000/year), a Business Model Canvas, and a 20-year financial projection. Pricing was set at 2,000 CNY per 30-capsule bottle (24,000 CNY per patient-year) after stakeholder consultation on affordability, with a modelled initial funding requirement of 1,260,000 CNY, break-even in year 16.79 and cumulative payoff of 59,020,546 CNY by year 20 - unusually long-horizon honesty for a student plan. They mapped three regulatory routes in parallel because hyperlysinemia is not in China's Rare Disease Catalog and there is no settled Live Biotherapeutic Product registration pathway, including applying to NMPA's Center for Drug Evaluation for Orphan Drug Designation. Risk management is broken out by regulatory, clinical, commercial, technical, public-acceptance, competitive and financial risk, with an early/mid/long-term (1-5, 5-10, 11+ year) milestone timeline. Ethical commitments include tiered pricing plus advocacy for national insurance coverage, no overstatement of readiness, age-appropriate formulations, and patient-data confidentiality.","partners":["Dr. Mingjun Sun, Sichuan University","Professor Liujie Huo","Professor Shengbo Wu","Professor Ran An","Professor Xiaofeng Yang","Dr. Guangquan Chen (clinician)","Mr. Guodong Huo, Illness Challenge Foundation (rare disease advocacy)","Mr. Junhao Li, Mr. K and Mr. Mao Ye (commercial / regulatory advisers)","HSBC (financial literacy and investment workshop)","2nd iGBA Exchange Event","South China Regional iGEM Exchange","HKUST (GZ) iGEM team","Guangzhou Social Welfare Institute","Cataverse Lab"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6178/wiki/model/4.avif","caption":"NetLogo agent-based output: simulation trajectories (A) and cluster-size distributions (B) under different SpyCatcher binding capacities, showing the transition from fragmented association at low display to near-global condensation at 50+ sites.","credit":"Team HFI, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6178/wiki/model/1.avif","caption":"The team's NetLogo 3D model interface used to screen candidate RBS parts for SpyCatcher surface display before any cloning was done.","credit":"Team HFI, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6178/wiki/model/10.avif","caption":"HPLC quantification of residual lysine in culture supernatant across three strain ratios, with and without the SpyTag/SpyCatcher binding module - the wet-lab test of the model's prediction (the 2:1 ratio performed best, as simulated; the crosslinking benefit itself was not significant).","credit":"Team HFI, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6178/wiki/education/figure-9.avif","caption":"The HFI Academic Summit, where a hands-on fruit DNA extraction activity was added in response to feedback that the previous session had been too academically dense; pre/post surveys measured the learning gain.","credit":"Team HFI, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy HFI's habit of making the dry lab a gatekeeper for the wet lab: a free NetLogo agent-based model screened seven candidate RBS parts and picked B0035 before a single plasmid was built, and an ODE model predicted the intermediates the team physically could not measure - both then republished as live browser sliders so a reader can rerun them. The second transferable move is their CLEAR/LYSERA discipline of refusing to log any outreach or stakeholder meeting unless it produced a measured outcome (paired pre/post surveys, engagement metrics including the embarrassing zero-comment result) and a named change in the next iteration.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/awards","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/static/model/metabolism.html","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/hfi","https://api.igem.org/v1/teams/6178"],"rank":143},{"team":"Lambert-GA","id":"6185","section":"High School","country":"United States","village":"Conservation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/","projectTitle":"SWARM - de novo minibinders against Deformed Wing Virus paired with multiplexed RNAi against Varroa destructor","tagline":"Protecting honeybees by hitting both the virus and the mite that carries it, with the first reported minibinder confirmed to bind DWV's VP3.","summary":"Lambert iGEM (Suwanee, Georgia) built SWARM to attack the two linked causes of honeybee colony collapse: Deformed Wing Virus and the Varroa destructor mite that vectors it. US beekeepers reported losing 55.6% of managed colonies between April 2024 and April 2025, and Georgia's large operations lost roughly 88,000 colonies in nine months. SWARM pairs a computationally designed 79-amino-acid minibinder against the exposed P-domain of DWV's VP3 capsid protein - experimentally confirmed by biolayer interferometry at KD = 603.6 nM (95% CI 497.1-733.3 nM), to the team's knowledge the first reported minibinder with confirmed binding to VP3 - with a multiplexed dsRNA construct silencing three Varroa salivary-gland genes (Vd-CHIsal, Vd-CHIBIN, VdAV422) at once to blunt resistance. Both components are delivered through feeding practices beekeepers already use. They took the High School Grand Prize plus Best Model, Best Wiki, Best Sustainable Development Impact and Best Village Project, and the modelling is the headline: four linked models spanning protein design benchmarking on 5,721 experimentally labelled designs, oral pharmacokinetics with Sobol sensitivity analysis, a 13-ODE RNAi network, and an individual-based six-year colony epidemiology simulation across two climates.","awards":["Grand Prize Winner (High School)","Top 10 (High School)","Best Model — Winner (High School)","Best Hardware — Nominee (High School)","Best Wiki — Winner (High School)","Best Sustainable Development Impact — Winner (High School)","Best Village Project — Winner (Climate Crisis / Conservation / Bioremediation)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Four models, each doing distinct work. (1) Minibinder design. No de novo minibinder or validated minibinder epitope existed for DWV, so they compared isolated P-domain structures against the assembled capsid to assess which surfaces are actually accessible, and investigated five candidate epitopes (an initial C-terminal region at I405/F408, EP-1 at I302/Y359/R395, EP-2 at V322/Q325/Y346, the QP/A-ridge at Q325/P326, and a putative catalytic triad at H277/S278/D294). They benchmarked computational confidence metrics against 5,721 labelled designs across 41 protein targets (849 confirmed binders, 4,872 non-binders) using ROC-AUC: minimum ipSAE scored 0.806 and ipSAE 0.801 pooled, Boltz-2 ipTM only 0.628, and a combined 38-metric ensemble just 0.541 - barely above chance - so adding metrics did not help. They also found minimum ipSAE degrades when comparing designs within a single target, which is exactly their use case, so they built a filtering framework combining predicted interface quality, amino-acid composition, predicted aggregation propensity, independent monomer prediction and cross-tool agreement; the motivating evidence was a candidate whose repeated minimum ipSAE predictions ranged from 0.000 to 0.676. Designs were generated with three independent tools - Mosaic, BindCraft and BoltzProt - with Boltz and Protenix used for additional structural evaluation; across 1,176 pairwise comparisons the median Jaccard similarity between BindCraft and Mosaic target-contact residue sets was 0.000 and their predicted interface centres were a median 42.9 angstroms apart, proving the tools explore genuinely different surfaces. Round 1 submitted 15 designs and produced zero confirmed binders (including 5G51_V4_seed01 at ipSAE 0.927 and ipTM 0.958, which failed); Round 2 generated 103 candidate entries, submitted 20, and produced one confirmed binder, l79_s469532_mpnn9, a BindCraft design associated with the QP/A-ridge that notably did not have the highest minimum ipSAE in the batch. Total compute spend was under USD 400 across 35 tested candidates, which they contrast against the USD 10,000 GPU budget per single-target campaign in Anthropic's 2026 autonomous binder-design technical report. (2) Minibinder dosing. A two-compartment oral pharmacokinetic model following functional protein from feeder to crop to gut to hemolymph: dG/dt = R_crop(t) - (ka + kt + kg)G and dH/dt = kaG - khH, with crop emptying fitted from Fournier et al. as V_crop = max(0, 17.966 - 0.159 t_min) uL reaching zero at about 113 minutes, digestive half-lives sampled log-normally around 31 and 54 minute values measured for native Manse-AS peptide in Myzus persicae and Acyrthosiphon pisum aphid gut extracts, and circulating stability from a juvenile hormone esterase analogue. 1,000 parameter sets were simulated for a 40,000-bee colony over 56 days; at a selected 100 nM average circulating concentration the median requirement was 29.60 g prepared protein (0.529 g/day prepared, 0.447 g/day active swallowed). Sobol total-effect sensitivity indices on log10(course mass), from 1,024 base samples and 7,168 model evaluations, ranked circulating functional loss 0.606, digestive loss 0.263, gut absorption 0.116, hemolymph volume 0.018 and intestinal transit 0.005 - and they reported that the gram-scale analysis failed its numerical checks so only the log-scale ranking is given. (3) RNAi model. A 13-species, 23-reaction ODE network in MATLAB SimBiology covering Dicer-2 processing of multiplexed dsRNA into three siRNAs, Ago2 loading into RISC, RISC-mediated Michaelis-Menten cleavage of a targeted Renilla transcript, untargeted Firefly as the control, plus transcription, translation, plasmid dilution, protein turnover and free-RNA degradation, with every rate parameter tabulated against its source and explicitly labelled as a measurement, a team estimate or an assumption. Predicted signal decrease was 37.50% with one siRNA, 67.51% with two, 83.41% with three directly delivered siRNAs, and 83.04% with the multiplexed dsRNA construct - the key result, since it predicts that bundling three guides into one molecule preserves knockdown. Local normalised sensitivity put RISC-mediated cleavage (Vm_1) at -1.72, the dominant assumption, while free Ago2 was near zero. (4) Epidemiological model. An individual-based six-year daily simulation of a two-deep-box Langstroth hive with about 42,000 brood cells, tracking adult bees individually by age, task, infection status and relative syrup intake, and mites on adults individually with sealed-cell brood and mites grouped by developmental day. It composes published frameworks - Sumpter and Martin for mite-virus-colony dynamics, Khoury/Myerscough/Barron for the saturating colony-size term N/(w+N) and forager recruitment R = alpha - sigma(F/N), Betti/Wahl/Zamir for infection-driven mortality, BEEPOP and BEEHAVE for seasonal development and weather-dependent foraging, and Martin and Calis for mite development and transfer. Daily weather was generated from NOAA 1991-2020 climate normals and 2011-2020 daily records preserving warm/cold runs, with foraging requiring a dry day above 12.8 C; 20 matched weather sequences per scenario across Atlanta and Ithaca gave 800 six-year simulations. Minibinder protection enters per bee as p_i(t) = 1 - exp(-K x_i u(t)) calibrated to 52.6% mean reduction in infection probability at full uptake with a lognormal intake distribution (mean-to-median ratio 1.68, sigma 1.0214); RNAi enters as an exposure state dF_i/dt = k_u x_i u(t)(1 - F_i) - k_loss F_i feeding f_treated(t_c) = f_0[1 - r_max F(t_c)] at brood capping, with r_max = 93.6% from an external study reporting 0.13 offspring per treated mother mite versus 2.04 in control.","dataviz":"The Best Wiki award is earned by making the models inspectable rather than illustrated. The VP3 epitope map is an interactive 3D viewer with five selectable, labelled regions and a toggle to overlay the confirmed minibinder's predicted pose, backed by a table giving each region's residues, structural rationale and experimental outcome. All 35 submitted minibinder designs are published in a sortable, filterable, searchable table - by design round, by experimental result - with each row expandable to show ipTM, ipSAE (labelled as to whether it is directional minimum ipSAE), design region, BLI sensor loading, the assay note and the source record, plus a full CSV download; failures and 'no usable result' entries are given equal prominence to the one success. A separate full-screen minibinder viewer shows the VP3 and GFP designs. The epidemiological model has a clickable four-stage 'one day in the model' diagram, a weekly-resolution Colony Viewer, and location-switchable figure sets for Atlanta and Ithaca covering colony survival, annual pollination, per-crop pollination and crop bloom windows, with results expressed relative to a paired Varroa-free colony under matched weather. Sensitivity results are shown as Matplotlib SVGs with bootstrap error bars. PRINCE ships a local web application with an interactive Structural context panel showing geometry findings, assessment status and crystal-contact summaries with per-residue measurements, JSON and CSV export, and two screencast demo videos (web app and CLI). The Modeling Instructional Series is seven videos teaching other teams a four-stage workflow for turning wet-lab protocols into MATLAB SimBiology models.","datascience":"The defining habit is turning other people's experimental data into a decision rule, then keeping the honest ledger. PRINCE's antibody epitope ranker was trained on 6,937 experimentally observed antibody-antigen interfaces across 1,549 antigen clusters, split by antigen cluster with sequence-homology checks between train, validation and test; it scored a cluster-averaged top-three majority-hit rate of 50.8% across 251 test clusters against 23.3% random, and on the harder retrospective subset of 93 clusters with no training homolog at 30% sequence identity or above it scored 45.8% against 23.8% random. An earlier residue-level benchmark on 110 common interfaces gave PRINCE a macro AUROC of 0.6722 against BepiPred-3.0 at 0.6524 and PeSTo at 0.6521, and the lightweight web-app scorer was benchmarked separately at 0.648 against 0.645 for exposure alone - a near-null result they published. Separately they retrospectively analysed 1,307 experimentally tested minibinder designs across 15 targets and found that across the 13 targets evaluable for site-level comparison the best-performing site averaged a 64% success rate against a 32% target-wide average, meaning site choice roughly doubles the hit rate; ranking by surface exposure alone put the best site in the top three for 10 of 13 targets (77%) against about 49% random, reported p = 0.032. Reporter work was properly calibrated with linear fits RLU_R = 206,101[Rluc] + 70,894 and RLU_F = 624[Fluc] + 57.8. Model validation against external data is laid out as a side-by-side table of published observation versus model output with an interpretation column that repeatedly says the comparison does not constitute validation: simulated managed mite loads of 2.42 (Atlanta) and 1.26 (Ithaca) per 100 bees against the USDA APHIS National Honey Bee Survey mean of 4.14 in positive samples, 27.8% and 8.8% of colony-months above three mites per 100 bees against 41.7% of survey samples, median untreated survival of 2.1 and 2.0 years against a Finnish study's 4-5 years from low initial infestation, and a simulated mean autumn population of 285 mites (range 165-514) against an observed 340. Headline outcome: all untreated colonies were lost by the end of year three, while SWARM alone retained 96.7% of reference crop pollination in Atlanta and 94.7% in Ithaca at year six against 45.1% and 79.8% for amitraz under an assumed five-year decline in effectiveness, with SWARM ahead in all 20 paired simulations at each location. Under the chosen assumptions RNAi supplied most of the benefit and the minibinder a smaller increment - a conclusion that undercuts their own flagship protein work, and they say so.","software":"PRINCE (released open source) is the team's software tool: it ranks candidate binding sites on a user-supplied protein structure before any compute is spent on design, combining a trained site ranker over geometric, atomic and protein-language-model features with a lightweight structural surface scorer, a crystal-contact and geometry checker that compares a chain alone against its crystal surroundings to flag where neighbouring copies cover a proposed site and where sections are missing or atomic positions uncertain, and a literature-evidence layer. It ships as a locally run web application for interactive use and a CLI for automated workflows, with JSON/CSV export, documentation, a command-line reference and a reproduction guide. Model: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/lambert-ga/prince-model ; web app: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/lambert-ga/prince-webapp ; team repo: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/lambert-ga. Hardware (Best Hardware nominee): Vapor-X, an automated vacuum dryer built on the Clausius-Clapeyron relation, using a 12 V vacuum pump to lower pressure in a sealed Mason jar while a flexible resistive heating pad gently warms the sample, controlled by a Raspberry Pi Pico W on a custom PCB with an OLED display and rotary encoder for target temperature, vacuum strength and drying time. Prototype components cost USD 80.07 (USD 95.52 including PCB assembly) against USD 5,000-25,000 for a benchtop lyophiliser. It was validated externally: testing at the University of Alabama at Birmingham recovered 38 of 43 measured compounds (88.4%) within 5% of undried controls, and testing at Georgia Institute of Technology showed GFP fluorescence comparable to controls after drying GFP DNA and cell lysates; internal Bradford-assay work and a classroom deployment assessed recovery and usability. Also released: the seven-video Modeling Instructional Series for teams with no modelling experience, and the full model result records and 1,000 paired parameter records for the dosing model as downloadable JSON and CSV.","stack":["BindCraft","Mosaic","BoltzProt","Boltz-2","Protenix","ipSAE / minimum ipSAE / ipTM / pLDDT confidence metrics","ROC-AUC benchmarking","Jaccard similarity of interface contact sets","self-consistency RMSD","protein language model features","scikit-learn","MATLAB SimBiology","ODE systems (13 species, 23 reactions)","individual-based / agent-based colony simulation","Sobol variance-based sensitivity analysis","local normalised sensitivity coefficients","Matplotlib","biolayer interferometry (BLI, via Adaptyv Bio)","dual-luciferase reporter assay","Sf9 insect cell culture","NOAA climate normals and daily records","Raspberry Pi Pico W","custom PCB","3D printing","Nebius GPU compute","Opentrons","GitLab"],"techniques":["benchmarking design-confidence metrics against thousands of labelled experimental outcomes instead of trusting them","detecting metric instability by repeating predictions on the same candidate","measuring tool diversity quantitatively (Jaccard 0.000, 42.9 angstrom interface separation) to justify using three design tools","site-level rather than design-level success analysis (best site 64% vs target-wide 32%)","cluster-split benchmarking with sequence-homology checks and a no-homolog retrospective subset","variance-based global sensitivity analysis with bootstrap intervals and disclosed failed numerical checks","paired-weather-sequence experimental design so treatments are compared under matched conditions","composing several published models into one individual-based simulation with per-framework attribution","separating measured inputs from assumed inputs in every parameter table","publishing all 35 tested designs including failures and unusable assays, with a CSV download","external third-party hardware validation at two universities"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Over 30 initiatives reaching more than 530 participants worldwide, organised around three stated principles (quality information, diversity and outreach, hands-on lessons) and mapped individually to UN SDGs. For elementary audiences they wrote and illustrated an original children's book, 'The Mite-ty Adventure of Bentley the Bee', for ages 5-9, iterated against feedback from elementary teachers, designed to be legible to readers with colour vision deficiencies, translated into 13 languages, read aloud to first-graders at Sharon Elementary, and now held in the Atlanta Botanical Garden's library as part of its pollinator education. They also ran a Sharon Elementary Critter Club workshop in which students learned pollination while physically preparing the plots of their own pollinator garden. For middle school they ran Riverwatch workshops and a week-long Biotech Bootcamp for 62 campers structured one day per iGEM committee, with candy DNA models, micropipetting, strawberry DNA extraction, a C. elegans RNAi feeding experiment observed under the microscope across the week, and a 'germiest place in school' swabbing experiment; pre/post survey comparisons were collected. For older audiences there were pollinator presentations, a high school debate, a Connectivity Night, webinars, a keynote at the NWGA RESA STEM for Tomorrow Forum, a Concrete Jungle workshop, a Gardener's Handbook with practical pollinator-friendly planting guidance, a Bee PSA, mentorship, and a GBA Fall Conference and mini jamboree. Two inclusivity programmes carry the Best Sustainable Development Impact award. Flowers for the Future partners with a non-profit supporting Afghan girls barred from school past sixth grade to obtain a GED; Lambert teaches a multi-week 'Intro to Synthetic Biology' course there (central dogma, units and dimensional analysis, DNA and mutations, gene expression, genetic engineering techniques including transformation, transfection, viral vectors and CRISPR, PCR and gel electrophoresis, experimental design, scientific writing, bioethics), now in its third semester and reaching about 120 students, with a linked FFF-BioBuilderClub. Exceptional Science! brings kinesthetic science lessons to the neurodivergent class at their own school - acid-base reactions, genetics, density, candy DNA models, a rainbow pinwheel density experiment - plus a honeybee/DWV movement game where students first forage freely as healthy bees and then may only hop, citing research that physical activity improves inhibition and rule-switching response times in children with ADHD; they also published a Neurodivergent Handbook.","ihp":"IHP ran across four sequenced phases (food insecurity/agriculture/city planners, beekeepers, experts and professors, then policymakers and governmental organisations), with every interaction processed through a four-step cycle they call H.I.V.E. - Hear, Inquire, Validate, Evolve - and a hard rule that a cycle is incomplete unless it ends in a concrete change or a documented reason why no change was warranted. Each stakeholder is also tagged with the SDGs they bear on, which is how the SDG scoring on the Sustainability page is built. The pivots are named, dated and attributed on a published timeline. February 2026: Dr. Aashish Manglik (UCSF) told them computational nanobody discovery still needs libraries of hundreds to thousands of candidates - 'For our computational design projects, we still make libraries of hundreds or thousands of candidates' - so testing 10 to 50 would likely fail even with a correct pipeline, and pointed them at BindCraft and Protenix; the team abandoned nanobodies for minibinders, which changed the entire wet lab. July 2026: Dr. Morgane Golan prompted the move to Sf9 cells as the RNAi model system. August 2026: Tudor Cotet and Corey Howe of Adaptyv Bio told them to stop targeting the P-domain broadly and focus designs on specific binding hotspots, and Cotet reviewed the failed first campaign before round two. September 2026: Dr. Mark Styczynski (Georgia Tech) pushed them off over-specific model parameters onto literature-supported ones, which is why every parameter table now carries a source attribution and an honest label; and Samantha Houck's input added SDG 11 to their framework. Professors Benjamin Lamp and Tatsuhiko Kadowaki, both DWV specialists, narrowed the target to VP3's exposed P-domain and warned how hard epitope selection is without experimental binding data, which made epitope evaluation a first-class modelling step and directly motivated PRINCE - as did Dr. Michael Hecht (Princeton) emphasising the uncertainty of epitope selection. Professor Raymond Owens (Oxford / Rosalind Franklin Institute) reframed binder choice around production cost and failure rate. Entomologist Dr. Jeff Pettis answered the ecological question head-on, saying eliminating Varroa would be 'completely fine' because they are obligate parasites invasive to the US, which underpins the safety case; he also named the political context, that pollinators are undervalued and under-resourced. On the practice side, Peter Helfrich (President, Metro Atlanta Beekeepers Association) identified DWV as the most dangerous threat and stressed that treatments must not harm brood, which made brood safety an explicit project priority; board member Claressa Lucas supplied the line that frames the whole project, 'It's not a matter of if there are Varroa mites. Every colony has them.' Professor Lewis Bartlett (University of Georgia) endorsed sucrose solution as a practical delivery vehicle, and contacts associated with Norroa, the registered RNA-based Varroa treatment, informed the syrup-pouch format. Sharanya Madhavan of Concrete Jungle, after six months of conversations and a joint workshop, connected bee health to rising food insecurity and urban pollinator corridors, which reshaped the outreach programme.","realWorld":"A business-to-business licensing strategy rather than a direct-sales fantasy: established agricultural biotechnology manufacturers would produce and distribute SWARM through existing commercial networks, priced at USD 39.99 per hive treatment against an estimated mature manufacturing cost of USD 25.00, targeting commercial beekeepers who supply pollination services to high-value agriculture. The plan models 300,000 annual treatments by 2034 (base case 150,000, long-term goal one million), giving roughly USD 12 million in retail sales and USD 525,000 in licensing royalties under a proposed 5% royalty, and is supported by TAM/SAM/SOM, market competition and SWOT analysis, an AIDA distribution model, beekeeper return-on-investment calculations, revenue and sales forecasts, manufacturing and operating expense breakdowns, break-even analysis and a risk-management section. The four-year plan is sequenced around the real regulatory gate: 2027 R&D, 2028 product optimisation and safety, 2029 field validation and partnerships, 2030 commercialisation preparation, with a manufacturing partner and pesticide registration secured before launch - sensible given Norroa/Vadescana was registered as a biopesticide in 2025 and is the obvious precedent. Vapor-X is handled as a separate commercialisation track, offered either through manufacturer licensing or open-source release to educational and research laboratories, with prototype and commercial manufacturing cost breakdowns and an explicit open-source contribution to future iGEM teams. Implementation is built around equipment beekeepers already own: dsRNA in a sealed flexible syrup pouch with an adhesive cover over proboscis-sized feeding openings placed in the brood box (modelled on the Norroa format, where worker bees redistribute syrup into brood stores and nurse bees incorporate it into brood food), and the minibinder as a dried packet mixed into sucrose solution and served through a standard hive-top feeder. Throughout, the team draws the line clearly: BLI established binding to a VP3 construct, not neutralisation of assembled virus; neither component has been tested in a honeybee colony; the 100 nM circulating concentration is a selected simulation value, not an established protective threshold; and the RNAi potency figure is an external conditional effect, not a measurement of SWARM's own construct. Impact is mapped through a 'Roots-to-Fruits' model linking seven SDGs - 2, 4, 5, 8, 11, 15 and 17.","partners":["Dr. Aashish Manglik, University of California, San Francisco","Professor Benjamin Lamp","Professor Tatsuhiko Kadowaki","Dr. Michael Hecht, Princeton University","Professor Raymond Owens, University of Oxford / Rosalind Franklin Institute","Dr. Mark Styczynski, Georgia Institute of Technology (Styczynski Lab)","Dr. Jeff Pettis, entomologist","Professor Lewis Bartlett, University of Georgia","Professor Jamie Ellis","Professor Jennifer Leavey, Georgia Tech","Dr. Morgane Golan","Tudor Cotet and Corey Howe, Adaptyv Bio (minibinder design advice and BLI testing)","an engineer at Boltz (target structure and hotspot redirection)","Metro Atlanta Beekeepers Association (Peter Helfrich, Claressa Lucas)","Concrete Jungle (Sharanya Madhavan)","GreenLight Biosciences","representatives associated with Norroa / Vadescana","Flowers for the Future (Afghan girls' GED programme)","University of Alabama at Birmingham (John Moore - Vapor-X validation)","Georgia Institute of Technology (Vapor-X GFP validation)","Nebius, Boltz, Twist Bioscience, GenScript, Promega, New England Biolabs, Opentrons, Addgene (sponsors)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6185/wiki/modeling/img-6251.avif","caption":"The epidemiological model pipeline: how minibinder protection (assigned per bee from its syrup intake) and RNAi exposure at brood capping enter the individual-based colony simulation.","credit":"Team Lambert-GA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6185/wiki/modeling/no-treatment-against-the-swarm-therapeutic-atlanta.avif","caption":"Simulated adult bee population in Atlanta over six years: seasonal cycles persist under SWARM while the untreated colony trajectory declines to zero - all untreated colonies were lost by the end of year three.","credit":"Team Lambert-GA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6185/wiki/modeling/crop-pollination-delivered-per-year-atlanta-georgia-blueberry-watermelon-squash-cucumber-apple.avif","caption":"Predicted annual crop pollination in Atlanta (blueberry, watermelon, squash, cucumber, apple), each treatment normalised against its own matched Varroa-free colony before averaging across 20 weather sequences.","credit":"Team Lambert-GA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6185/wiki/hardware/vapor-x-prototype.avif","caption":"Vapor-X, the team's USD 80 automated vacuum dryer, with its Mason jar vacuum chamber, heated sample stand, OLED display and rotary encoder - externally validated at UAB (38 of 43 compounds recovered within 5% of undried controls) and Georgia Tech.","credit":"Team Lambert-GA, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"The transferable insight is that Lambert beat the metric rather than trusting it: they benchmarked ipSAE, ipTM and a 38-metric ensemble against 5,721 labelled experimental designs, found the ensemble barely beat chance (ROC-AUC 0.541) and that their eventual hit did not even have the best score, then showed with 1,307 prior designs that choosing the right epitope roughly doubles the hit rate (64% at the best site vs 32% target-wide) and released that insight as open-source software, PRINCE - landing the first confirmed DWV VP3 binder on under USD 400 of compute. A high-school-plus centre should copy that whole posture: cheap compute plus a public dataset plus honest benchmarking, and publish every tested candidate including the failures in a sortable, downloadable table.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/model-design","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/model-minibinder","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/model-rnai","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/model-epidemiological","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/implementation","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/safety","https://2026.igem.wiki/lambert-ga/contribution","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/lambert-ga/prince-model","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/lambert-ga/prince-webapp","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/lambert-ga","https://api.igem.org/v1/teams/6185"],"rank":301},{"team":"Toronto","id":"6187","section":"Overgrad","country":"Canada","village":"Bioremediation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/","projectTitle":"PetaBite","tagline":"Planetary-scale bioprospecting for plastic-degrading enzymes: mine 300M candidates, then match an enzyme to each real-world condition.","summary":"PetaBite attacks PET and microplastic pollution by abandoning the field's usual strategy of engineering a handful of known enzymes and instead searching the natural sequence landscape at planetary scale. The dry lab rebuilt PETadex from scratch into PETadex V2: 1.74 billion plastic-degrading enzyme homologs recovered from NCBI nr and the Logan assembly of the entire Sequence Read Archive, filtered by 53 hand-curated catalytic HMMs to 291,922,243 complete catalytic ORFs, each annotated with the pH, temperature, salinity and environment of the sample it came from. They paired this with PETai activity labels scraped from literature by an LLM pipeline and by 20+ human curators, pretrained PlasticESM (LoRA adapters on ESM-C) on the corpus, and built a hardware bioreactor array to feed condition-specific measurements back into the model. They won Best Model largely for scientific honesty: a homology-component-grouped split showed that sequence length beats their own embeddings, and they reported that rather than the inflated random-split numbers.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Model — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Hardware — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Bioremediation Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"The PETadex V2 pipeline chains DIAMOND homology search against NCBI nr (42,053,081 hits down to 7,825,107 non-redundant sequences at E < 1e-5) with 53 catalytic profile HMMs built by a jackhmmer-analogous four-iteration loop that only advanced homologs retaining every canonical catalytic residue, then searched 2,740,458 catalytic-domain centroids against the Logan assembly of the SRA (87.0 Pb of raw reads compressed to 1.8 Pb of contigs) to recover 5.57 billion hits and 291,922,243 complete catalytic ORFs. Structures came from AlphaFold DB (575,770 hits, via UniProt idmapping) plus their own ESMFold2 run: 1,793,781 sequences folded in 16.6 hours on up to 80 GPUs (851.6 GPU-hours, median pLDDT 92.3), benchmarked against 244 experimental PDB structures to choose the full model under 150 aa and ESMFold2-Fast above it at 32 diffusion steps and 3 refinement loops. On the learning side they continued masked-language-model pretraining of ESM-C 300M on PETadex using LoRA adapters on all four projections of every transformer block (240 adapter tensors, 14.7M trainable parameters, backbone frozen) to produce PlasticESM, and probed its embeddings with ridge regression under homology-component-grouped cross-validation. Supporting layers include CATH and Pfam domain assignment, SignalP 6.0 secretion prediction over all ORFs, Biopython ProtParam biochemical properties, InterProScan plus HMMER accessory-domain analysis, and a reconstruction of the TED (The Encyclopedia of Domains) novelty-discovery pipeline adapted to PETadex HMMs.","dataviz":"The wiki is the clearest dataviz work in the cohort: a 300-million-protein 'PETadex Model Landscape' rendered as three explorable layers (what we found, what we know, what we predict), a draggable component map of the 46 PETadex-PAZy families, a 3D structure viewer that highlights the catalytic triad, oxyanion hole and docked PET dimer for each of 16 CATH fold groups, and a BioSample viewer that drills from a real sample (e.g. SAMN03075713, Arctic tundra soil) down through its sequencing run to individual ORFs and their 90% identity clusters. The Sharded DIAMOND search and the per-enzyme record card both ship as live in-page demos that replay ten real searches, sped up from 45 seconds to two. There is also a 'PETadex Project Calendar' that walks week by week from March to October with an explicit 'What went wrong, and what we changed' panel per workstream, and a PETamon mini-game that teaches why no single enzyme cleans up every plastic.","datascience":"Their central data-science contribution is turning a literature corpus into training labels and then proving honestly that the labels, not the corpus, are the bottleneck. An automated LLM extraction pipeline gathered 11,000 identifiers via the PAZy API, screened them to 900 seed papers, expanded by forward and backward citation to 50,000 candidates, filtered to ~4,600, converted full texts to structured JSON with Docling, Grobid and PyMuPDF, and extracted 51,455 cleaned measurement entries across 31,441 enzymes; Claude Sonnet benchmarked best (95.0% precision, 87.1% recall) with zero hallucinated enzymes across 32 double-scraped papers. In parallel, 20+ members spent 450+ hours hand-extracting 61 papers through a standardized interactive HTML schema with autogenerated IDs and built-in error checks, giving a ground truth where 78% of independent scraper pairs agreed within 10%. The benchmark result is the headline: under random five-fold splits stock ESM-C reached rho = 0.72 on kcat and 0.74 on melting temperature, but splitting folds by connected homology component dropped the same embeddings by 0.49 to 1.01 while a single sequence-length column lost at most 0.18 — because 90-92% of labelled sequences have a homologous relative elsewhere in the set. Length itself reached rho = +0.343 on kcat and beat both embeddings on all twelve target sets, so they reported the negative result, published the model card alongside the weights so nobody reuses the adapters expecting activity prediction, and designed round 2 to be chosen by model uncertainty rather than predicted activity.","software":"Two tools released under MIT at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/toronto/petabite. LOBSTER (Library Optimization for Bioprospecting Sequences Through Economical Recombination) splits PETase cores into fragment positions and designs degenerate-codon oligos that combinatorially assemble by Golden Gate to cover as many natural cores as possible — it synthesized a 1,744-sequence library at 45% of individual-synthesis cost (55% nucleotide reduction) for the IsPETase and LCC 90% identity clusters. Sharded DIAMOND Search splits the 307M-sequence corpus into 32 shards searched in parallel by serverless cloud workers, returning ranked neighbours for a pasted protein in under a minute with nothing to install, live at petadex.net. Datasets are on Zenodo (PETadex catalytic ORF corpus v1.1 + sharded search DB, 23.8 GB, doi:10.5281/zenodo.23457986) and Hugging Face (petadex/plasticesm-dualseed, petadex/catalytic-orfs-90pid, petadex/petai-activity-labels, each label source published separately); analysis notebooks live at github.com/petadex/igem-toronto and github.com/petadex/petabite. Hardware: a parallel bioreactor array, up to 96 units, at about $204 CAD per reactor, with PID temperature control holding 37 C within +/-0.3 C, dual-photodiode OD600 with three-point quadratic calibration (R^2 = 0.993 on yeast), peristaltic pumps under Arduino PWM control, 150 RPM stirring, and a single-file Python dashboard over USB serial; it grew E. coli over 4 hours in the final test.","stack":["DIAMOND","HMMER / jackhmmer-style iterative HMM building","MMseqs2 (cross-check)","USEARCH","Logan SRA assembly","NCBI nr","PAZy","PlasticDB","BacDive","NCBI BioSample","ESM-C 300M / ESMC-600M","LoRA (PlasticESM)","ESMFold2 / ESMFold2-Fast","AlphaFold DB","ColabFold v1.5.5","SignalP 6.0","InterProScan","CATH","Pfam","Foldseek","umap-learn","scikit-learn ridge regression","Biopython ProtParam","Weights & Biases","Hugging Face Datasets","Docling","Grobid","PyMuPDF","Claude Sonnet (LLM extraction)","Parquet","AWS S3 + serverless workers","Arduino Nano","Python (dashboard)"],"techniques":["planetary-scale metagenomic homology mining","catalytic-residue-constrained profile HMMs","hierarchical clustering at 90/60/30% identity","homology-component-grouped cross-validation","permutation tests and fold-centred scoring","model-free controls (sequence length, amino-acid composition)","continued masked-language-model pretraining with LoRA adapters","UMAP embedding atlas with shuffled/random/fragment negative controls","silhouette scoring per CATH domain","LLM literature extraction benchmarked against double-human curation","uncertainty-driven active learning for round 2 library selection","degenerate-codon Golden Gate library design","sharded parallel sequence search","PID temperature control and dual-photodiode OD600 calibration"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Toronto hosted three conferences, including a 'Rethink plastic' microplastics conference built around two questions (what happens to plastic once it enters the environment, and what can we realistically do about it), deliberately framing PetaBite as one approach among many alongside prevention, policy and community action. They mentored two high-school synthetic biology teams, ran sustained exchanges with four universities abroad, and carried out eight campus collaborations across the University of Toronto reaching over 800 students and community members. Outreach ran on humour rather than lecture: a promotion video combining live action with original animation and PETase-inspired axolotl mascots, a 'Road to Jamboree' Instagram Reels series using trends and familiar formats, and a newsletter, The Waterfront, with 300+ subscribers. Instagram content drew over 105,000 views, 250+ new followers, 2,500+ profile visits and 374 link clicks through to the project and the conference.","ihp":"Roughly 70 stakeholders across four continents, and the changes are specific. A public event where biosafety dominated the questions told them containment has to come before technical detail, so every subsequent public-facing event opened with containment. Regulatory and ethics conversations led them to prioritize enzyme candidates with close homology to naturally occurring proteins and to lead with a contained bioreactor rather than dosing enzymes into open process streams. A researcher's clarification that PETases are only a subset of plastic-degrading enzymes made them explicitly define scope throughout the documentation. Hardware feedback on optical channels led them to separate the optical-density path from the colorimetric degradation readout so dye cannot corrupt cell-density estimates, and a GFP recommendation gave them a route to reporting activity per enzyme rather than per cell; the same conversations reframed the 40 mL reactor as the directed-evolution and Michaelis-Menten instrument and split off the 1,000-to-50 intermediate screen as a separate smaller-format problem. A CGM-adjacent interviewee's distinction between sensor and adhesive reshaped MVP scope. Indigenous knowledge holders reframed plastic pollution as a matter of relationships among people, land and water rather than a technical problem, which fed country-specific regulatory profiles for Pakistan, India, Nigeria, Indonesia and Canadian Indigenous communities addressing waste colonialism and ethics dumping. All of this was codified into a reusable MERGE framework (Multidisciplinary outreach, Education, Regulatory oversight, Global database, Environmental consideration) and a Regulatory Oversight Framework covering Canada's Roadmap for Open Science, FAIR, the Nagoya Protocol, dual-use research of concern, PIPEDA, the Directive on Automated Decision-Making and the EU AI Act.","realWorld":"PetaScale is the commercialization arm, explicitly framed as taking PetaBite from 0-to-1 to 1-to-n. The beachhead is wastewater treatment plants, validated through 20 feasibility conversations with operators, engineers and managers across Ontario, Alberta and Newfoundland, with PETases immobilized in packed-bed reactors so the enzyme stays contained. Three adjacent verticals are mapped: microfibre filtration, landfill leachate and textile effluent. Their differentiation is explicit against Carbios, Samsara Eco and Protein Evolution, all of which build around one engineered enzyme and one operating window (Carbios amorphizes PET and runs near 70 C) — PetaScale instead matches the enzyme to the site's existing temperature, pH and salinity so no heating or pretreatment is needed. Regulatory mapping covers CEPA, EPA TSCA Section 5 and provincial discharge permits, with tailwinds from EU Regulation 2023/2055, Directive 2024/3019 and Canada's Federal Plastics Registry. Validation signal: their associate RNAlab screened 161 randomly selected natural enzymes and two outperformed FAST-PETase. They tested the model across five Canadian national and four global competitions plus two international conferences, placed top 3 in six, attended Y Combinator Startup School, Web Summit Vancouver, SynBioBeta Global and the Canadian Food Forward Summit, completed the Wilbe incubator sprint, opened early discussions with three Canadian VC firms, and raised support from 17 sponsors.","partners":["RNAlab (associate lab, Logan project)","Environment and Climate Change Canada","U.S. EPA","U of T Trash Team","Wastewater treatment plant operators across Ontario, Alberta and Newfoundland","Wilbe startup incubator","Y Combinator Startup School","SynBioBeta Global","Web Summit Vancouver","Indigenous knowledge holders (Canada)","Four partner universities abroad","Two mentored high-school iGEM teams"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6187/wiki/drylab-page/figures/petadex-workflow-overview.svg","caption":"Overview of the PETadex V2 discovery pipeline: curated PAZy enzymes seed catalytic HMMs, which mine NCBI nr and the Logan SRA assembly down to 291,922,243 complete catalytic ORFs.","credit":"Team Toronto, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6187/wiki/drylab-page/figures/homology-leakage.svg","caption":"The team's headline negative result: the same ridge probe scored under homology-component-grouped splits (hollow) versus random five-fold splits (filled). ESM-C embeddings gain 0.49-1.01 from the random split; a single sequence-length column gains at most 0.18.","credit":"Team Toronto, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6187/wiki/drylab-page/figures/landscape-spin-poster.avif","caption":"The interactive PETadex Model Landscape, which lets a visitor explore 300 million proteins in three layers: what was found, what is known about each sequence, and what the model predicts.","credit":"Team Toronto, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6187/wiki/hardware-notebook/overview/finalresult/1.avif","caption":"The final parallel bioreactor build: about $204 CAD per unit, with PID temperature control, dual-photodiode OD600, peristaltic pumps and stirring, driven from a single-file Python dashboard over USB serial.","credit":"Team Toronto, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy their evaluation discipline, not their compute budget: grouping train/test splits by homology component and forcing every model to beat zero-parameter controls (sequence length, amino-acid composition) is free, and it is what turned a plausible-looking rho = 0.72 into an honest negative result that reviewers rewarded. Pair that with their hybrid labelling pattern — an LLM extraction pipeline benchmarked against a small, double-scored, schema-driven human corpus — which is exactly the right shape for a school research centre that has many students and little GPU time.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/project/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/dry-lab/overview/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/dry-lab/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/hardware/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/beyond-the-bench/outreach/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/contribution/","https://2026.igem.wiki/toronto/safety-and-security/"],"rank":124},{"team":"TAU-Israel","id":"6196","section":"Overgrad","country":"Israel","village":"Therapeutics / Foundational Advance","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/tau-israel/","projectTitle":"CERNAL","tagline":"A biological compiler: supply RNA expression data, get ranked RNA logic-gate circuit designs that fire only in the cell state you specify.","summary":"CERNAL treats the transcriptome as a fingerprint of cellular state and compiles that fingerprint into an RNA logic circuit: it picks the transcripts that distinguish a target cell from its neighbour, scans each transcript for an accessible, unique, constructible trigger window, designs a toehold-style switch whose hairpin hides the ribosome binding site until that trigger unzips it, combines switches into AND and NOT gates, ranks every candidate on shared thermodynamic metrics, and assembles the winner into an annotated plasmid. Because the sensing element is the cell's own RNA rather than a characterised transcription factor, the approach is intended to be host-agnostic, and the team demonstrated it in three proofs of concept: endometriosis logic in HEK293T, a PFOA/PFAS biosensor in E. coli, and CRISPRa gates in yeast. The software is a Django Ninja + React 19 application with a strictly isolated scientific engine, a versioned HTTP API with Python, R and MATLAB clients, SBOL 3 export, and an IBBIS Common Mechanism biosecurity gate that withholds sequence files when screening cannot be completed. The team filed a provisional patent through Tel Aviv University's TTO and built a full market case around cell and gene therapy developers.","awards":["Best Software — Nominee (Overgrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"A React 19 static single-page application (TanStack Router, React Query for server state, polling and mutations) built into Django's static assets, over Django Ninja typed HTTP routes with a shared error envelope, Django ORM models for persistent state, and a thin django-q2 task that dispatches work through the engine interface. The v1 runtime deliberately uses SQLite with write-ahead logging, foreign keys and a busy timeout, plus a single ORM-backed queue worker, so the queue is a database table and no separate broker (Redis, RabbitMQ) is needed; rate-limit state also lives in a database cache rather than per-process counters. Worker timeout is one hour with max attempts set to one, explicitly to avoid silently repeating an expensive calculation. The scientific engine is a separate package: it does not import Django, routers, application modules, the database or Django settings, configuration arrives as frozen request records, and an abstract-syntax-tree test catches ordinary, conditional and function-local imports that would break that boundary. Platform code may import only engine.contract and engine.client, never the scientific internals, which means the engine runs and is tested with no web server, database or network, and could be moved to a remote process behind the same request/result interface. Requirements: Python 3.13, uv, Node 22+ for the frontend build, ViennaRNA and Biopython; run './do install && ./do migrate && ./do superuser && ./do build-frontend' then the dev server and a worker in a second terminal, because without the worker submissions just sit queued.","dataviz":"The project landing page is an extended scroll-driven explainer that animates the actual mechanism: zooming into a cytoplasm of coloured transcripts, lining them up by abundance to show the state fingerprint, highlighting the transcripts strong in cell A only versus cell B only, picking one, finding the trigger window, then showing the hairpin lock with its buried RBS being unzipped by the key, and finally the same locks becoming AND and NOT gates. The software page is organised as a 'field guide' with chapters, every claim linked to its implementation record, and a five-step auto-playing screen recording of a real run (recorded from the local app on 11 October 2026) with per-step 'you should see' statements and a static-image fallback for users without JavaScript. The results explorer shows ranked candidates with full metric decomposition, construct views, warnings, and the rejected candidates kept visible; user decision tags (none, pinned, shortlisted, rejected, synthesize) are annotations that explicitly do not change any computational or release result. Run states are drawn as a six-state machine with eight permitted transitions.","datascience":"Validation is answered against the Best Software ballot questions one by one, each linked to an implementation or measurement record, and separated into four kinds of evidence that the wiki says do not substitute for one another: software checks, recorded execution, structure comparisons and host experiments. Two external comparisons anchor the predictions: the RNA-accessibility score was compared against published in-cell SHAPE measurements across 450 E. coli RNA records, and the switch-ranking metrics were tested against Green et al.'s measured toehold ON/OFF ratios. Gene selection used published expression data for the PFOA analysis and, separately, an endometriosis analysis with healthy-tissue context, stratifying AREG and SELE across superficial peritoneal and deep ovarian lesions. The AND-gate study enumerated hundreds of thousands of candidate designs, and examining the best and worst of each architecture surfaced failure modes before anything was built, notably switches that score well only because they stay closed and targets that look good only on paper. At the bench, dozens of toehold candidates were designed and a subset tested in bacteria, with bacterial NOT-gate results, yeast guide-RNA experiments and human-cell input-expression studies providing different pieces of evidence; the wiki says testing is ongoing and names the remaining gate-validation work. Capabilities are labelled Implemented, Conditional (execution or release depends on infrastructure, data or screening) or Incomplete, with the stated rule that missing evidence is never scored as a numerical zero and a high score cannot fill it. The removed off-target scanner is explicitly not replaced by a zero-valued measurement. The recorded demonstration run is reported with its failure included: a 711-nt mCherry input produced 21 ranked designs, but FASTA, GenBank and SBOL release was held because the screening service was unavailable.","software":"CERNAL is the most engineering-mature iGEM software this year, and its distinguishing feature is that almost every claim is qualified by what it does not establish. The pipeline runs: differential-expression table or direct transcript in; gene ranking; sliding-window trigger enumeration scored by ViennaRNA-predicted accessibility (RNAplfold partition functions, base-pair probabilities, minimum free energy) with positions and sequence context retained; gate design across six families (single-input toehold, prokaryotic and eukaryotic AND, prokaryotic and eukaryotic NOT, CRISPR-based); cross-family ranking on shared metric definitions with normalisation and weights so candidates from compatible gate families sit in one list; circuit composition from a Boolean expression; and annotated plasmid assembly with RFC10 and RFC1000 restriction-site checks against the selected assembly standard. Three interfaces sit over one engine: a guided four-step web wizard (inputs, logic, payload, vector), a versioned HTTP API with OpenAPI docs at /api/docs and five core endpoints (GET /api/version for capabilities, POST /api/design returning a job handle, GET /api/design/{id} for cheap polling, GET /api/design/{id}/results with ?format=csv, GET /api/artifacts/{id}/download with ownership checks), and an optional LLM chatbot for picking a reference gene. Client packages ship for Python, R and MATLAB plus curl examples, and the team publishes which clients are actually verified: Python has recorded live-server CI conformance, R has later checks, MATLAB has archived wrapper defects and no fresh conformance claimed. API keys carry a cern_live_ prefix with only a SHA-256 digest stored, two scopes (read, design), expiry, revocation, 60 requests per minute and two concurrent runs by default, and an object owned by another account returns 404 rather than confirming its existence. Strict submission rejects misspelled constraint keys with a structured 422 and suggestions rather than silently applying a default, and the stated compatibility promise within an API schema version is additive-only. Formats: RNA sequences and differential-expression tables in, custom GenBank backbones in, FASTA, annotated GenBank, SBOL 3 (preserving sequence features, part roles and provenance) and CSV out, with Biopython handling GenBank and ViennaRNA the thermodynamics. Two things are unusual. First, biosecurity is wired into the pipeline, not bolted on: an IBBIS Common Mechanism integration (HMM sequence-profile models plus similarity search) screens the final switch and the assembled construct and holds sequence export when screening evidence is unavailable or unresolved, and the wiki states that a hold is not a completed screen. Second, the team separates scientific computation from AI-assisted development and says so in a dedicated section: the reviewed runtime contains no generative AI, LLM, neural network, trained classifier or remote inference service, the Python and JavaScript manifests include no AI inference SDK, and design runs send no biological data to an AI provider, while AI assistance used in interface design, coding and documentation is recorded in attribution. Reproducibility is handled by publishing a fixed 7.1 MiB source snapshot (revision 9a5ca657) with a checksum manifest, noting candidly that this revision was not on the public repository when checked and that the public repo may contain a different revision, so the snapshot is not substituted for it. Documentation is layered: a public documentation index, architecture diagrams, a scientific-engine module map, an API reference generated into a committed file with a freshness test that detects drift, decision records (including one on choosing SBOL 3), typed contracts and docstrings on cross-component data, and a repository map for new contributors. Four named reuse points are offered for teams who want only a piece: the engine contract, gate-family hooks, the scoring layer and the window utility. Repo: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/tau-israel/cernal. A hosted deployment exists at https://cernal.app (also cernal.duckdns.org, same host) but it is gated, the wiki says to sign in with a lab account or request access, and the host did not respond when checked, so the dependable route is the published source snapshot and a local install. Upstream credit is kept explicit: a separate eukaryotic AND research notebook reads ranked CSVs exported from the third-party NucSyn Platform, and the wiki states that NucSyn is neither invoked nor bundled by the production engine and that the two sets of scores remain distinct.","stack":["Python 3.13","Django","Django Ninja","django-q2","SQLite (WAL)","uv","React 19","TanStack Router","React Query","Vite","Node 22+","ViennaRNA (RNAplfold, RNAfold, RNAcofold)","Biopython","SBOL 3","GenBank","OpenAPI","IBBIS Common Mechanism","Python / R / MATLAB client packages","DESeq2-style differential expression input","iGEM GitLab"],"techniques":["RNA secondary-structure prediction via partition functions and base-pair probabilities","accessibility profiling of sliding trigger windows","toehold switch design (hairpin-sequestered RBS)","prokaryotic and eukaryotic AND and NOT gate architectures","CRISPRa gate design","cross-family metric normalisation and weighted ranking","Boolean circuit composition","differential-expression gene ranking with Benjamini-Hochberg adjustment and log2 fold thresholds","benchmarking predictions against in-cell SHAPE data","benchmarking against Green et al. measured ON/OFF ratios","HMM sequence-profile biosecurity screening","architecture-boundary enforcement via AST import tests","state-machine run lifecycle with explicit terminal outcomes","transactional result import with checksum verification"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"The team ran a Mini-Jamboree where CERNAL was demonstrated live to other iGEM teams and then surveyed the participants (7 responses): all agreed synthetic biology lacks a generalizable approach for designing intracellular circuits responding to user-defined stimuli, 71.4% said they would use such a tool during iGEM and 85.7% afterwards, with interest spread across biosensing (57.1%), cell engineering (57.1%), and diagnostics, therapeutics and environmental sensing (28.6% each). The team states plainly that this is a directional signal from a small, self-selected group of peers and not market evidence, and that its value is that the demand they claim was checked with outside users rather than asserted. AIS-China was given the interface to use and returned three concrete usability fixes that the team adopted: clearer placement of downloads, a more prominent New Circuit entry point, and meaningful run names. A video walkthrough, in-app FAQ and public FAQ source support new users.","ihp":"Human practices operated under a stated 'Responsibility-by-Design' philosophy whose point was to turn the phrase into constraints. Patient accounts set the design goal: Shir Parchef waited 18 years for an endometriosis diagnosis and underwent an 11-hour procedure with six surgeons; Netta Ben Moshe was told incapacitating pain was ordinary cramps; Helen Altschuler described patients' willingness to support research because so little else exists. From these the team set the target as eliminating pathology rather than masking symptoms, and without systemic hormonal ablation. Dr. Berkowitz told them a therapy that shrinks lesions without relieving pain or preserving fertility has no clinical utility, which made them stratify AREG and SELE across both superficial peritoneal and deep ovarian lesions so the logic covers the 'invisible' lesions driving the worst pain rather than only what imaging shows. Several loops closed by narrowing the claim rather than widening it: after input from Dr. Berman the PFAS biosensor is positioned as a low-cost triage tool that prioritises which of Israel's hundreds of wells need urgent lab testing, explicitly not a replacement for LC-MS, and the team states it has not solved RNA delivery. Conversations with Neta Agmon about practical inputs and outputs and with AIS-China about user workflow shaped which interfaces were documented for reuse.","realWorld":"A provisional patent application was filed through Tel Aviv University's technology transfer office. The market case is built from Alliance for Regenerative Medicine sector data: 1,089 commercial cell and gene therapy developers in a US$1.7B total addressable market, of which 553-731 use genetic modification, and applying a stated 30% in-house design assumption gives a beachhead of 166-219 accounts worth US$0.25-0.33B, with four organisations shortlisted for first contact and HEK293/HEK293T chosen as the entry cell line because it is widely used in industry. The competitive positioning is specific rather than dismissive: Cello 2.0 maps circuit specifications to characterised parts and Asimov Kernel provides construct design, simulation and workflow tools, but both depend on predefined specifications, characterised parts or host-specific workflows, whereas CERNAL starts from transcriptomic state; the wiki notes these citations support design context and are not an absolute claim that no competing route exists. The MVP is a 5-10 week design-partner cycle from discovery to structured feedback, and the team reports CERNAL has already produced 730 E. coli and 110 yeast circuit candidates as a routine pipeline rather than a demo.","partners":["Tel Aviv University technology transfer office","endometriosis patients and advocates (Shir Parchef, Netta Ben Moshe, Helen Altschuler)","clinicians (Dr. Berkowitz)","water-research and PFAS analysis experts (Dr. Berman)","AIS-China (interface user testing)","Neta Agmon","IBBIS (Common Mechanism screening)","NucSyn Platform (upstream, offline data interface)","Alliance for Regenerative Medicine (market data)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6196/wiki/software/01-inputs.avif?v=4","caption":"CERNAL's Inputs panel: E. coli selected as host and a 711-nucleotide mCherry transcript entered as a Direct Trigger mRNA, from the recorded five-step walkthrough.","credit":"Team TAU-Israel, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6196/wiki/software/cernal-s03-generated-v1.webp","caption":"The three decisions behind one RNA design: recognition-site selection, compatible switch design and comparison, with host experiments and not computation establishing function.","credit":"Team TAU-Israel, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6196/wiki/software/cernal-s19-generated-v1.webp","caption":"The engine/platform boundary: the engine takes a versioned request and returns candidates plus an artifact manifest, with a source-level import test enforcing the separation.","credit":"Team TAU-Israel, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6196/wiki/software/cernal-s21-generated-v1.webp","caption":"The automation surface: query the version endpoint for capabilities, submit a design once, keep the job handle, poll status, inspect results and request only permitted files.","credit":"Team TAU-Israel, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Build the scientific engine as a package that cannot import the web framework, the database or the settings, and enforce it with an automated import test, so the science can be run, tested and reused headlessly and the UI is only one of three front ends. Then adopt their reporting discipline: label every capability Implemented, Conditional or Incomplete, never score a missing measurement as zero, publish a recorded run that includes the step where it failed, and keep AI-assisted development in the attribution record and out of the scientific runtime.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/tau-israel/","https://2026.igem.wiki/tau-israel/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/tau-israel/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/tau-israel/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/tau-israel/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/tau-israel/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/tau-israel/contribution","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/tau-israel/cernal","https://cernal.app"],"rank":7},{"team":"HangzhouFourteenAP","id":"6197","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"Space, Foundational Advance, Software & AI, Biomanufacturing","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/","projectTitle":"DCO (Dental Calculus Obstructor)","tagline":"A four-enzyme cocktail plus a hydroxyapatite-binding antimicrobial peptide coating that dismantles dental plaque biofilm before it mineralises into calculus.","summary":"DCO is a three-module biological oral care system: an enzyme cocktail that degrades the organic matrix holding plaque biofilm together, a tooth-binding antimicrobial peptide that coats enamel to block re-colonisation, and a controlled-release system to coordinate them. The enzymes are two recombinantly expressed glycoside hydrolases — PmGH87 (alpha-1,3-glucanase) and CoGH66 (alpha-1,6-glucanase), which act cooperatively because CoGH66 removes alpha-1,6 branches and exposes new PmGH87 cleavage sites — plus commercially sourced DNase I and papain for extracellular DNA and protein. It addresses a real and specific burden: 96.7% of Chinese adults aged 35-44 have dental calculus, over 900 million have periodontal disease, only 20.1% of elderly people saw a dentist in the past year, and the dentist-to-population ratio is about 1:7,768. It won Best Village Project on a tight problem-to-product chain, a FoldX mutation-prioritisation pipeline whose prediction was wrong and whose failure is reported in full, and an unusually commercially literate entrepreneurship section that refuses to make anti-calculus claims it cannot support.","awards":["Top 10 (High School)","Best Measurement — Nominee (High School)","Best Presentation — Nominee (High School)","Best Wiki — Nominee (High School)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Winner (Space / Foundational Advance / Software & AI / Biomanufacturing)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two computational pipelines, both modest in tooling but well-reasoned and, importantly, both reported against their experimental outcome. (1) A CoGH66 mutation prioritisation pipeline built to avoid screening hundreds of mutants in the wet lab. Starting from the substrate-bound structure PDB 8IUB (CoGH66 with isomaltotriose bound, so no re-docking was needed), they used PyMOL to extract all protein residues within 4.0 A of the substrate, giving an 18-residue binding pocket (Y166, D167, Q169, Q172, I190, Y217, L219, H250, L252, F256, I260, D293, L295, N395, K429, E430, Y431, N434) consistent with the published catalytic cleft and +2 to -4 subsites. Domain annotation came from CD-Search (Glyco_hydro_66 at residues 22-560, E-value 0) and InterPro (glycosidases at 104-440), with sequence translation via Novopro. They then layered evolutionary information: BLASTp against the NCBI non-redundant protein database, deliberately selecting homologs from different bacterial species at 30-70% identity to avoid over-representing near-identical sequences and to capture naturally tolerated substitutions, aligned with MAFFT and visualised as an ESPript conservation map. Of the 18 contact residues, seven (Y166, Q169, Q172, I190, H250, N395, K429) showed species-specific natural substitutions and were taken as plastic; the remaining eleven were conserved and deliberately left alone. Mutants were built in PyMOL, repaired and scored in FoldX for both stability change and binding change, with an operational priority threshold of binding delta-delta-G < -1 kcal/mol chosen explicitly because it exceeds typical FoldX error. (2) Antimicrobial peptide selection: after a literature review identified four hydroxyapatite-binding AMP candidates, they used AlphaFold Server to predict peptide-calcium ion coordination as a first computational filter, reasoning that hydroxyapatite presents Ca2+ as the primary interaction site, and ranked candidates by pTM and interface pTM (ipTM) scores. SHABP (21 aa, cyclic and disulfide-constrained) and G12R (18 aa, linear alpha-helical) both reached pTM around 0.4, above DNase-fused and HBAMP alternatives, and both were advanced into parallel experimental validation on the grounds of their distinct structural strategies (conformational stability versus membrane disruption).","dataviz":"The model page leads with a schematic workflow diagram of the whole mutation-prioritisation pipeline, so the logic from structure to alignment to energy calculation is readable before any numbers appear, and ends with a three-dimensional localisation figure placing the two prioritised positions in the pocket (protein green, mutated positions red with arrows, substrate yellow, other 4.0 A contacts cyan). The genuinely notable choice is an interactive 3D model of the CoGH66-isomaltotriose complex embedded in the wiki as a glTF file exported from PyMOL, so a reader can rotate the enzyme and inspect the contact residues rather than squint at a static render — a cheap but effective way to make a structural argument inspectable. Tables carry the natural substitutions observed per non-conserved position with counts, and the full FoldX energy results for every tested mutant rather than only the winners. Measurement figures pair the physical artefact with its quantification throughout: a 96-well plate photograph alongside the bar chart derived from it for the DNS reducing-sugar assay, the crystal violet extracts, and the acridine orange fluorescence images with the ImageJ intensity quantification in the identical group order so the reader can check the numbers against the wells. Standard curves are shown with their regression equations and R2 values (BCA at Y = 0.8964X - 0.2574, R2 = 0.9951).","datascience":"The most valuable dry-lab content is a documented computational failure. The pipeline predicted Y166Q as the top candidate (the only mutant meeting the -1 kcal/mol binding threshold, at -1.63 kcal/mol) with Q172P as a secondary candidate retained despite only -0.38 kcal/mol because of its predicted stabilising effect and natural occurrence in homologs. Site-directed mutagenesis (reverse PCR with back-to-back mutation-carrying primers, DpnI digestion of methylated parental template, self-ligation), colony PCR at the expected ~1726 bp, and Sanger sequencing confirmed both constructs (Y166Q TAC to CAG; Q172P CAG to CCG). The DNS activity assay then inverted the ranking: Q172P gave the highest apparent activity at net OD 1.881, wild type 0.996, and Y166Q only 0.629 — the computational winner was worse than wild type. The team's response is the right one: they revised the ranking in the text, named Q172P the leading experimental candidate, reclassified Y166Q as a mechanistic probe rather than quietly dropping it, and had already flagged in advance that Q172P introduces a proline and may alter main-chain conformation so its FoldX estimate should be read with caution. They also controlled the comparison properly rather than letting protein load explain the result: BCA quantification gave WT 12.1, Y166Q 10.9 and Q172P 9.1 microgram/mL, crude lysates were normalised to equal total protein before the DNS assay, and the wiki explicitly notes that Q172P's lower total protein means its activity signal cannot be attributed to a higher load. The measurement work on the artificial dental calculus model is similarly self-critical. Gravimetric analysis of six treatment conditions (negative control, four single-component omissions, complete cocktail) found no significant weight-loss differences, and the team attributed this to balance sensitivity and to non-uniform mineralisation producing variability that masked the effect, then moved to more sensitive assays rather than reporting a null as a result. Crystal violet quantification at OD gave the complete cocktail a significant reduction against the negative control (p < 0.05), with the papain-omission comparison reported honestly as a trend toward significance at p = 0.08 and interpreted as papain playing a supporting rather than dominant role under single-treatment conditions. Acridine orange fluorescence with ImageJ quantification showed significance against the negative control and against the DNase-I-omission group, supporting the mechanistic model that eDNA cleavage lets the other enzymes penetrate, while the overall small between-group differences led them to conclude that the primary mode of action is structural matrix dismantling rather than direct bactericidal potency. Limitations are stated plainly, including that the B. subtilis surrogate model does not replicate the Streptococcus mutans-specific EPS composition the glucanases actually target. The AlphaFold peptide screen also carries its own caveat: the team notes that peptide-Ca2+ coordination cannot fully replicate crystal-surface binding, that pTM around 0.4 indicates only moderate confidence, and that the scores were used for relative ranking rather than as affinity estimates.","software":"No standalone released software tool; the dry lab is a documented analysis pipeline rather than a product, and the wiki itself is a React/Vite single-page application with GSAP animation. Released reusable resources instead: the full mutation-prioritisation methodology as a reproducible protocol, the interactive glTF structural model, PDF slide decks for all four education audiences (two elementary sessions dated 5-28 and 6-11, a high school deck, and an elderly-residents deck), a consolidated enzyme stabilisation and controlled-release guide offered as a generalised framework for multi-enzyme formulations across biocatalysis, food processing and environmental remediation, and a 'lessons learned for future iGEM teams' section. Constructs were expressed from codon-optimised genes in pET-32a via seamless cloning, carrying an N-terminal thioredoxin (TrxA) tag for soluble expression, a 6xHis tag for IMAC purification and an enterokinase site for optional tag removal, under T7 promoter control in E. coli Rosetta(DE3), with CoGH66 confirmed at approximately 76.54 kDa. The team also documented a safety-driven substitution they consulted the iGEM committee about: because Streptococcus mutans is not on the iGEM White List they used B. subtilis as a surrogate in the artificial calculus model, and because Aspergillus niger is not listed they used commercially available edible fungi as a material source.","stack":["PyMOL","FoldX","MAFFT","ESPript","BLASTp / NCBI non-redundant database","NCBI CD-Search","InterPro","AlphaFold Server (pTM / ipTM)","glTF (interactive 3D export)","ImageJ","PDB 8IUB","Novopro translation tool","DNS (3,5-dinitrosalicylic acid) reducing-sugar assay","BCA protein quantification","crystal violet biofilm quantification","acridine orange fluorescence microscopy","pET-32a / E. coli Rosetta(DE3)","React + Vite + GSAP (wiki)"],"techniques":["structure-guided binding-pocket contact extraction at 4.0 A","evolutionary tolerance filtering via homolog MSA conservation mapping","FoldX delta-delta-G stability and binding energy calculation with a pre-set error-aware threshold","reporting a failed computational prediction and reclassifying the candidate as a mechanistic probe","total-protein normalisation before activity comparison (BCA then equal-load DNS)","site-directed mutagenesis by reverse PCR with DpnI selection and self-ligation","Sanger sequencing verification of point mutations","single-component omission (leave-one-out) assay design to test synergy","escalation from an insensitive assay (gravimetry) to more sensitive orthogonal assays","Student's t-test with reported non-significant and borderline p values","AlphaFold interface confidence scores for relative peptide ranking","White List-driven surrogate organism substitution with committee consultation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Four-tier education (primary school, high school, elderly residents, general public) with the explicit design principle that they did not just teach knowledge but recorded what changed after participants went home — brushing, flossing, denture care, diet, dental visits and attitudes toward synthetic biology. The best single tool is a tangible genetic circuit built from electronic building blocks: a touch sensor stands for the promoter, a toggle switch module for the lac operator, a button for lactose as inducer, an LED for the RBS, a small fan for the target gene and a manual power-off switch for the terminator, so children physically discover that touching the sensor alone does nothing because the operator blocks by default, and that the circuit only runs when the inducer is held. For younger children they wrote child-friendly science comics and ran plaque disclosing tablets as a reveal — children chewed the tablets, rinsed, saw red and blue patches on their own teeth, were asked to guess and ask questions first, and were then shown microscope images with the framing 'this is not dirt, this is a city'. The team records the emotional shift honestly: children moved from fearing bacteria to being curious about them and from passive listening to asking and designing. High school sessions used a full script and deck covering project background, enzyme mechanism, experimental process and application value, and specifically targeted the soft-plaque-versus-hard-calculus confusion. The nursing home session simplified everything into plain language and images and delivered concretely actionable care: a soft-bristled small-headed brush at 45 degrees to the gumline, gentle brushing with no horizontal scrubbing, bedtime brushing more important than morning, floss or interdental brushes rather than toothpicks, and denture care rules (rinse after meals, remove and soak overnight, never boiling water, never toothpaste, never sleep in them), with the explicit disclaimer that this is science communication and does not replace professional dental advice. Public outreach at Wushan Square used display boards, roll-up banners, hand fans and leaflets on plaque versus calculus and scaling misconceptions.","ihp":"Twelve named advisors spanning clinical dentistry, periodontics, microbial control, pharmaceutics, gene circuit regulation, management and marketing, and oral care product regulatory and quality management, engaged in a staged problem-framing process. Problem framing combined a 350-respondent user questionnaire, an in-depth clinical interview with Dr. Lei Lihong (Second Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine) and an industry interview with Mr. Fan Wenjun (healthcare investment), triangulated against the Fourth National Oral Health Epidemiological Survey specifically to avoid bias from a single interview source. The survey quantified both misconception and the knowing-doing gap: 61% worry that scaling damages enamel, 23% believe brushing removes calculus, only 6% claim to fully understand it; and separately, 52% know calculus forms at the gingival margin and in deep interdental spaces but only 25% floss or use an irrigator daily, 48% know brushing cannot remove calculus but only 38% get scaling once or twice a year, and 84% know calculus causes periodontitis but only 25% have a six-monthly examination. The project-defining intervention came from Dr. Wang Yongwu (Hangzhou First People's Hospital), who said that using enzymes to dissolve already-mineralised calculus is 'like an egg hitting a stone' — and the team did not just accept the efficacy point but derived a safety consequence from it: because calculus and enamel and dentin are both calcium phosphate mineralised tissues, any non-selective demineralising agent would risk enamel or dentin demineralisation, sensitivity or erosion. That reframed the entire target from mineralised calculus to the organic matrix of the early pre-mineralisation biofilm, which is the intervention window the whole project now occupies. Dr. Lei and Dr. Yuan Yifang (Affiliated Stomatological Hospital of Zhejiang Chinese Medical University) corroborated it and specified what to attack: the polysaccharide-protein cross-linking and the stabilising role of eDNA that together hold the biofilm's three-dimensional structure against salivary washing. Four design constraints emerged from the interview set — efficacy validation, penetration ability, cytotoxicity safety and stability assurance — which became the selection criteria for the enzyme cocktail and delivery system. A second concrete pivot: experts pointed out that enzymatic removal does not prevent recolonisation because salivary proteins form a pellicle within minutes, which is why the hydroxyapatite-binding AMP module exists at all; the computational peptide screen was conducted on their explicit recommendation. A third: on discovering that bovine pancreatic DNase I is already commercially available at roughly 80-160 yuan per 100 mg and widely used, they strategically stopped trying to recombinantly express it and concentrated the synthetic biology effort on the glycoside hydrolases where the EPS matrix offered a real opportunity. Dr. Lei also set a boundary the team kept: any active ingredient in a daily product must be safe and must not disrupt the normal oral microbiome or damage soft tissues.","realWorld":"The most commercially literate section of the five teams, and notable for restraint. The problem is scoped deliberately narrowly: the project does not aim to replace professional scaling, but to serve adults who cannot or will not undergo frequent scaling with a daily, safe, evidence-based product that interferes with plaque maturation and reduces mineralisation risk before it happens — daily prevention and risk reduction, not treatment of existing calculus or periodontal disease. Target segment is urban young people aged 20-30, students and people one to five years into their first jobs, chosen because they have purchasing power, care about social image and have not yet entrenched habits. Channels are specified rather than gestured at: online seeding on Douyin, Xiaohongshu and Bilibili, e-commerce subscriptions, and offline placement in convenience stores, beauty collection stores and supermarkets, with trial experience areas in beauty collection stores. A B-end clinic seed-trial path runs in parallel with the C-end consumer path, and contingencies are written down (if clinic repeat-purchase is below expectation, optimise usage instructions and physician script training; if C-end subscription retention declines, control ad spend pace and iterate the product experience). Six quarterly milestones run from improving the dual recombinant enzyme lab data and stability, patent drafting and seed trials in three to five clinics, through pilot scale-up and third-party safety testing, to daily chemical regulatory filing, first commercial production, retail rollout and a monthly subscription. A three-year P&L is projected (revenue 45 with net -30 and about 35% gross margin, then revenue 280 with net +120 and about 58% gross margin, then revenue 950 with net +530 and about 65% gross margin). IP strategy notes that existing Chinese patents in this space are mostly single-enzyme or simple multi-enzyme combinations and rarely apply microcapsule encapsulation systematically to oral enzyme care, and acquisition by listed biotech oral care entrants such as Bloomage Biotech or Weimeizi (Saky) is named as the exit after commercial validation. Most creditable: all marketing materials and product claims will state explicitly that the product does not replace professional scaling or periodontal treatment, and the portable chewing gum format is to be marketed only for post-meal freshness with no preventive or anti-calculus claim at all — a team voluntarily giving up its best marketing line because it has not earned it yet. Sustainability is argued concretely rather than with SDG slogans: all four actives are proteins expected to degrade to amino acids and peptides after use, and no live organisms are released.","partners":["Dr. Lei Lihong, Associate Chief Physician, Second Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine","Dr. Wang Yongwu, Director and Chief Physician of Stomatology, Hangzhou First People's Hospital","Dr. Yuan Yifang, Affiliated Stomatological Hospital of Zhejiang Chinese Medical University","Dr. Huang Jie, Attending Physician of Stomatology, Hangzhou First People's Hospital","Prof. Jia Shiliang, Zhejiang University of Technology (microbial control and protocol validation)","Prof. Yu Haining, Zhejiang University of Technology (natural products and gut microecology)","Dr. Wang Zhiguo, Institute of Aging Research, Hangzhou Normal University (tRNA engineering, gene circuit regulation)","Dr. Shi Jing, Zhejiang University / Texas A&M / Westlake University","Mr. Fan Wenjun, Hangzhou Zhuanjiabang Medical Investment Management","Dr. Wang Haoran, Founder and CEO, Niulan Biotech","Prof. Wu Shiqi, Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics (business plan, market analysis)","Mr. Yu Feng, Quality and Research Center Director, Hangzhou Namei Zhikang Technology (oral care regulatory filing and QC)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6197/wiki/content/model/modeling-figure1.avif","caption":"The CoGH66 mutation prioritisation workflow: PyMOL contact identification in PDB 8IUB, mapping onto the MAFFT/ESPript conservation map to select substitutable sites, point mutation in PyMOL, and FoldX delta-delta-G calculation to rank candidates.","credit":"Team HangzhouFourteenAP, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6197/wiki/content/model/modeling-figure5.avif","caption":"Three-dimensional localisation of the two prioritised candidate positions (Y166 and Q172, red) in the CoGH66 substrate-binding pocket, with isomaltotriose in yellow and the other 4.0 A contact residues in cyan.","credit":"Team HangzhouFourteenAP, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6197/wiki/content/engineer/engineering-figure12.avif","caption":"Crystal violet quantification of residual artificial biofilm after treatment with the complete four-enzyme cocktail against each single-component omission and the no-enzyme control.","credit":"Team HangzhouFourteenAP, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6197/wiki/content/education/education-figure10.avif","caption":"Education session in which children build a lac-operon genetic circuit from electronic building blocks, with a touch sensor as promoter, toggle switch as operator, button as inducer, LED as RBS and fan as target gene.","credit":"Team HangzhouFourteenAP, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Their best transferable move is reporting the computational prediction that lost: FoldX picked Y166Q, the bench picked Q172P, and the wiki revises its own ranking and demotes the failed candidate to a mechanistic probe while showing that BCA normalisation rules out protein load as the explanation. A high-school dry lab earns more credibility from one honestly scored wrong prediction with the confound controlled than from three unvalidated ones. Also copy the cheap inspectability trick — export the docked structure as a glTF so a reader can rotate it — and the commercial restraint of marketing a format with no efficacy claim until the evidence exists.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/measurement/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/sustainable/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/safety/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/assets/index-CoIamzKT.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/assets/Model-CLIAyY10.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/hangzhoufourteenap/assets/HumanPractice-B3gOYwBY.js"],"rank":124},{"team":"SUIS-SFLS","id":"6199","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/suis-sfls/","projectTitle":"TyroFix","tagline":"Recyclable magnetic-bead tyrosinase that cheaply DOPA-modifies recombinant mussel adhesive protein, sold as a B2B raw material","summary":"SUIS-SFLS expressed three microbial tyrosinases, immobilised them on magnetic beads so the enzyme can be magnetically recovered and reused, and used the recycled catalyst to hydroxylate Mfp3-linker-Mfp6 fusion mussel foot proteins into DOPA-bearing wet adhesives for skin and wound repair. The commercial thesis is unusually self-aware: after expert interviews they abandoned the idea of selling a finished dressing and repositioned as an upstream B2B raw-material supplier, splitting the same protein stock into TyroFix Mini (cosmetic grade) and TyroFix ProMax (Class II medical grade) and running both regulatory tracks in parallel from day one so that test data never has to be repeated. They are the only high-school team here with a genuine financial model, including capex, cost per litre, pricing, gross margin and breakeven year for each line, published as downloadable PDFs. They were nominated for Best Entrepreneurship in the High School section.","awards":["Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Golden-circle style Why/How/What mission, PESTLE run separately for each product line, SWOT per line, 4P marketing model, Business Model Canvas, a stakeholder power-interest map, TAM-SAM-SOM per line with stated assumptions, a five-phase 36-month milestone plan, and a two-line financial model with breakeven analysis.","dataviz":"Thirteen entrepreneurship figures including two separate PESTLE diagrams, competitive positioning, purity and performance indicator panels, a technology roadmap, a business model canvas, a milestone chart and two financial-planning charts. Two downloadable PDFs (financial-planning-mini.pdf, financial-planning-promax.pdf) carry the underlying budgets. Survey results are reported with response percentages and mean Likert scores.","datascience":"A 290-response consumer survey quantified to a usable level: over 90 percent reporting skin issues, acne and marks at 62.76 percent, dryness at 48.62 percent, sensitivity at 45.17 percent, satisfaction with existing repair products at 3.34 out of 5, weak efficacy as the main complaint at 72.61 percent, safety at 82.07 percent and repair efficacy at 75.86 percent as the top purchase factors, willingness to try at 3.57 out of 5, and over 60 percent accepting CNY 100-150 with CNY 110-120 as the sweet spot. Cost modelling is benchmarked against recombinant human insulin industrial fermentation.","software":"","stack":["magnetic-bead immobilised tyrosinase","E. coli expression","Mfp3-linker-Mfp6 fusion constructs","chromatography and TFF ultrafiltration","HPLC purity assay","NIH/3T3 cytotoxicity assay"],"techniques":["enzyme immobilisation and magnetic recovery","controlled DOPA hydroxylation","three DBTL cycles on fermentation and purification","wet adhesion measurement in kPa","endotoxin quantification","lyophilisation and reconstitution feasibility"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A 290-response online questionnaire that went beyond sentiment into purchase behaviour, capturing preferred formats, trusted information sources (dermatologists, real user reviews, official brand channels), trial triggers (free samples at 30.69 percent, professional recommendation at 30 percent) and an explicit price band. They also read the result honestly, noting that trust comes from experimental and clinical data (76.21 percent) and transparent mechanism (63.79 percent), and then used those channels to plan promotion rather than inventing a campaign.","ihp":"Five named expert interviews, each with the background stated, the question asked, the feedback, the team's reflection and the concrete project adjustment. Dr. Dongfeng Wang (Deputy Chief Physician, Wuhan Petrochemical Hospital) converted vague 'good adhesion' into care-process requirements and gave quantitative design targets: film formation in about 5 seconds, breathability with water resistance, partial degradation in 3-5 days, and validation moving from superficial to deeper wounds. Mr. Yanxuan Song (medical device engineer and founder of a Guangzhou tech-beauty chain) widened their notion of cost to include purification, storage, transport and handling losses, and suggested lyophilised powder with a reconstitution solution to cut cold-chain burden. Mr. Jiujiang Zhang of CIRS Group, a Class II and III device regulatory specialist, established the rule that shared raw-material origin does not permit shared submission pathways. Mr. Xiaofei Ji, an ISO 13485 internal auditor and NPDP holder who has taken more than ten Class III products to approval, delivered the decisive advice: a student team should start at raw-material supply, treat reproducibility and batch records as early concerns, and settle IP before approaching any manufacturer. That advice visibly rewrote the deliverable, from a finished dressing to a characterised protein plus quality documentation.","realWorld":"The most financially literate high-school entrepreneurship case in this set, and the honesty is what makes it credible. Each line has a built-up cost model rather than a percentage: Mini needs about CNY 6.8 million of equipment (seed shakers, a 200 L fermenter, high-pressure homogeniser, chromatography system, industrial centrifuge, TFF ultrafiltration) depreciated straight-line over six years, about CNY 2.3 million of R&D, and about CNY 71 per litre to produce against a $12-18 per litre price, giving roughly 34 percent gross margin and cumulative profitability in year 3 to 4. ProMax adds an aseptic filling line, sterile filtration and a lyophiliser for about CNY 11.65 million, carries about CNY 8.15 million of R&D split CNY 5.1 million expensed and CNY 3.05 million capitalised, costs about CNY 163.5 per litre against a $22-32 price, and yields only about 15.9 percent gross margin with breakeven in year 5 to 6. Volume ramps are stated (Mini 1,000 then 5,000 then 15,000 then 30,000 litres; ProMax 200 then 1,000 then 3,000 then 8,000), and the whole budget is downloadable as two PDFs. The regulatory path is the most detailed of any team in this group and is clearly researched rather than guessed: ordinary non-special-use cosmetic filing with NMPA plus a CMA-certified safety assessment for a new cosmetic ingredient and a three-year post-market monitoring period, versus provincial Class II contact-wound-dressing registration requiring ISO 13485, 100,000-class cleanrooms, GB/T 16886 biocompatibility and a clinical evaluation route, with the deliberate choice of the equivalent-product comparison pathway to avoid human trials. They identify the specific trap that cosmetic test reports cannot be reused for device registration and design around it by running both safety programmes in parallel, and cite Giant Biogene and Fu Erjia as real companies that use the shared-campus, separated-workshop model they intend to copy. Market sizing is more careful than usual, with Frost and Sullivan figures for both markets, stated percentage assumptions for SAM, and a SOM expressed as a penetration range tied to winning 10-15 recurring B2B accounts or 2-3 licensed device partners. Now the blunt part. There is no registered entity, no funding, no incubator place, no signed letter of intent and no patent filed, only a stated plan to file three categories of patents before any external collaboration. More seriously, the competitive-efficacy section blurs sources: head-to-head performance claims against Komfymed's recombinant collagen dressing, and a 65-patient trial where MAP hydrogel healed wounds in 5.7 days versus 7.3 for rhEGF, read as literature values for mussel adhesive protein generally rather than data the team generated, yet are written as 'our advantage'. A reader should treat those numbers as borrowed benchmarks. Their own SWOT is candid about this, listing an absent intended use, no confirmed device classification, no design controls, no risk-management file, no biocompatibility package and no clinical evidence strategy. Continuation is a licensing-and-supply intent built on a correct strategic insight (cash from the fast cosmetic line funds the slow medical line), but the entity that would execute it does not yet exist.","partners":["Dr. Dongfeng Wang, Deputy Chief Physician, Wuhan Petrochemical Hospital (clinical wound care)","Mr. Yanxuan Song, medical device engineer and founder of Guangzhou Baiyoubai Tech-Beauty Chain (clinic operations and procurement)","Dr. Shengbo Wu (synthetic biology)","Mr. Jiujiang Zhang, CIRS Group Medical Devices Division (Class II and III regulatory affairs)","Mr. Xiaofei Ji, medical device development and quality systems expert (value-chain positioning)","SnapGene and Tsingke (listed sponsors)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6199/wiki/entrepreneurship/figure-11.avif","caption":"Financial planning for TyroFix Mini: capex, R&D spend, CNY 71 per litre production cost and the volume ramp to 30,000 litres by year five","credit":"Team SUIS-SFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6199/wiki/entrepreneurship/figure-12.avif","caption":"Financial planning for TyroFix ProMax, the Class II medical line, showing the lower 15.9 percent gross margin and later breakeven","credit":"Team SUIS-SFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6199/wiki/entrepreneurship/figure-9.avif","caption":"Business model canvas for the two-line B2B raw-material supply model","credit":"Team SUIS-SFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6199/wiki/entrepreneurship/figure-10.avif","caption":"Thirty-six-month milestone plan across five phases, from DBTL cycles to first B2B supply agreements","credit":"Team SUIS-SFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Adopt their repositioning discipline: ask industry experts where a student-scale team actually belongs in the value chain, and be willing to drop the finished product for an upstream raw material plus characterisation and quality documentation, which is a deliverable a small lab can genuinely own. Then publish the budget as a downloadable model with capex, cost per unit, price, gross margin and breakeven year, so the plan can be criticised on its arithmetic rather than its adjectives.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/suis-sfls/","https://2026.igem.wiki/suis-sfls/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/suis-sfls/human-practices/"],"rank":7},{"team":"HongKong-HS","id":"6214","section":"High School","country":"Hong Kong","village":"Diagnostics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-hs/","projectTitle":"AmpliRen - a urine-based home screening kit concept for chronic kidney disease using urinary miRNA and hybridisation chain reaction","tagline":"Early signals. Made visible. A small sample telling a bigger story.","summary":"HongKong-HS is a five-school joint team (Wah Yan College Kowloon, Pope Paul VI College, C&MA Sun Kei Secondary School, Tsung Tsin Christian Academy, St. Paul's School Lam Tin) tackling chronic kidney disease, which affects roughly 788 million adults worldwide, has risen past 405,000 recorded adult cases in Hong Kong, and is usually caught only at stage 3-5 because creatinine-based eGFR does not move until about half of kidney function is gone. AmpliRen detects two urinary microRNAs - miR-21-5p for fibrosis severity and miR-192-5p as an early warning, together reported at 89% sensitivity and 82% specificity - and amplifies the signal enzyme-free at room temperature with hybridisation chain reaction, whose G-rich hairpin loops fold into G-quadruplexes that bind hemin to form a DNAzyme and oxidise TMB into a colour change visible in natural light. The project is a genuine four-cycle engineering story: the team started with toehold switches designed in miRADAR and screened in NUPACK, found EGFP output too weak and invisible, pivoted to HCR, then to G-quadruplex-containing probes, then to hemin/TMB colorimetry. It was nominated for Best Wiki on one of the most ambitious scroll-driven, 3D-visualised wikis in the competition.","awards":["Top 10 (High School)","Best Wiki — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Nominee (Infectious Diseases / Therapeutics / Diagnostics / Oncology)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"The dry lab is nucleic-acid thermodynamics rather than protein design, and it is used as a hard screen at every cycle. Candidate toehold switches were generated with miRADAR, the design tool released by the 2024 Wageningen iGEM team - a deliberate reuse of another team's software rather than reinventing it - producing 9 candidates for miR-21-5p and 7 for miR-192-5p. Every candidate was then simulated in NUPACK (RNA mode, 37 C, default model options and algorithm settings, 1 uM toehold and 3 uM miRNA to keep the trigger in excess) and scored against five explicit criteria: bound-toehold equilibrium concentration, absence of unintended secondary structure downstream of the ribosome binding site (which would block the ribosome and cause false negatives), hybrid minimum free energy (HMFE) versus toehold minimum free energy (TMFE) so that the bound state is thermodynamically favoured and the switch does not prefer to dimerise with another switch, C-G content for hairpin stability, and RBS location. They tabulated the HMFE-TMFE difference for every candidate in kcal/mol and read NUPACK's base-pair equilibrium-probability plots to check that loop-maintaining base pairs drop to low probability on target binding so the switch can fully linearise - selecting toeholds 6 and 7 for miR-192-5p and 7 and 9 for miR-21-5p. The same NUPACK workflow was then reapplied to HCR: H1/H2 hairpin pairs (the miR-21-5p pair adopted from Miao et al. 2016, the miR-192-5p pair built in house) were simulated at 37 C with 1 uM hairpin and 3 uM miRNA, confirming long H1-H2 polymer formation with target but also predicting unintended H1-H2 binding in the absence of target - a background-leakage problem the model found before the gel did. Minimum-free-energy structures were computed for H1 alone, H2 alone, H1+miRNA and the full miRNA-H1-H2 complex for both targets. In Cycle 4, double-loop G4H1/G4H2 hairpins based on Peng et al. (2022) had InG4 G-quadruplex motifs inserted into the loops, and NUPACK was used to confirm both complex formation and that the InG4 sequences sit in the loops where they can fold and leave the polymer after HCR. The two probe pairs were deliberately given different geometries - longer stems and smaller loops for miR-192-5p, shorter stems and larger loops for miR-21-5p.","dataviz":"The wiki itself is the data-visualisation achievement and is why it was nominated for Best Wiki. The landing page is a scroll-driven narrative that zooms from two kidneys, through tissue and the filtration barrier, down to the molecular signal, with each stage labelled as an illustrative view rather than clinical eGFR categories. The HCR mechanism is animated across four scroll states - two locked hairpins, target binding and toehold opening, chain growth into a double-stranded nanowire, then potassium-dependent G-quadruplex folding with hemin - so a reader physically watches the amplification cascade instead of reading a paragraph about it. Epidemiology is presented as individually sourced stat cards, each carrying its own population, year and citation (788 million adults globally, GBD 2023/The Lancet 2025; 87% unaware, CDC 2026 NHANES 2021-2023; 405,833 Hong Kong adult cases 2021, Yue et al.; 2,571 on the transplant waiting list; 1,226 registered deaths in 2024) with an explicit note that one figure is a recorded-case count, not a current population estimate. The kit concept is presented as an interactive unboxing with three fitted compartments and a pannable close-up, and the team labels it openly as AI-assisted imagery and animation made with Higgsfield and as a research concept, not a validated diagnostic product. The engineering page is built as a clickable 22-note timeline across four Design-Build-Test-Learn cycles. Every figure carries a full descriptive caption and alt text.","datascience":"Analysis is modest in volume but correctly specified. Cell-free EGFP expression was compared with and without target miRNA across five repeats against both positive controls (pET-14b-EGFP with no toehold) and no-plasmid negative controls, read on a fluorometer at 488 nm excitation and 507-513 nm emission, with significance tested by one-sample t-test on fold change; the honest outcome was that miR-21 candidates showed no significant difference, miR-192-5p-6 was significant but minimal at 1.03-fold, and miR-192-5p-7 showed a more pronounced 1.51-fold increase that did not reach significance - and they reported that rather than spin it. ThT fluorescence and hemin/TMB colorimetric assays were analysed by one-way ANOVA with Dunnett post-hoc against the 0 pM control: miR-21 gave significant signal at 100 pM, 10 nM and 100 nM (n=3, p<0.001), while miR-192 was significant at 100 nM (p=0.01 and p=0.002) but also, unexpectedly, significantly lower than control at 1 nM - an anomaly they diagnosed as free miR-192-5p G4H1 self-folding into a G-quadruplex and producing colour without target, and then turned into a concrete future-work list (splitting the G4, stabilising the hairpin stem, tuning C-G/A-T content, optimising toehold length). Plasmid work was confirmed by Sanger sequencing and miniprep A260/280 purity, and gel evidence was escalated from agarose to PAGE when agarose smearing proved inconclusive.","software":"No standalone software or hardware was released. The dry lab is the application of existing tools - NUPACK for thermodynamic simulation and another iGEM team's miRADAR for candidate generation - plus a bespoke wiki front end. Two toehold switch candidates per target were cloned into pET-14b-EGFP using XbaI and NdeI sites between the T7 promoter and the EGFP reporter and verified by Sanger sequencing, and an mCherry colorimetric variant was in progress when the season ended.","stack":["NUPACK","miRADAR (Wageningen iGEM 2024)","one-way ANOVA with Dunnett post-hoc","one-sample t-test","Sanger sequencing","cell-free expression + fluorometry","microplate reader","PAGE and agarose gel electrophoresis","ThT fluorescence assay","hemin / TMB G-quadruplex DNAzyme colorimetry","Higgsfield (AI-assisted concept imagery)","scroll-driven 3D web visualisation"],"techniques":["HMFE vs TMFE thermodynamic ranking of toehold candidates","equilibrium base-pair probability inspection to verify hairpin linearisation on target binding","in-silico prediction of background leakage (unintended H1-H2 binding without target) before bench testing","MFE structure comparison across free hairpin, hairpin+target and full ternary complex","loop-position verification for inserted G-quadruplex motifs","fold-change analysis against both positive and no-plasmid negative controls","escalating gel resolution (agarose to PAGE) when a result is inconclusive","diagnosing a statistically anomalous result as probe self-folding and converting it into a redesign list"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education reached over 800 participants and was built on three stated principles - a Cascading Peer-Educator Model, Gamified Educational Experiences, and Reflective Learning. The peer-educator model is the distinctive one: rather than only teaching end-learners, they trained non-iGEM junior-form Science Team members as Student Facilitators with both content and facilitation skills, then had those facilitators independently run workshops for their schoolmates, and published a training guide for holding a secondary school workshop plus a CKD brochure for older adults so the materials outlive the team. Activities were designed per audience and deliberately low-cost and replicable: at Salesian Yip Hon Millennium Primary School's STEAM Day (about 600 students and parents) they ran a micropipette drip-art station, a magnetic miRNA detection game in which only the target miRNA card hides a magnet so it alone sticks to a paper toehold-switch model, and a health-knowledge ping-pong ball toss into an illustrated face; at Shek Lei Catholic Primary School, strawberry DNA extraction delivered entirely in Cantonese with terms simplified ('cell walls' became 'the surface of the strawberry') and a 'Blood Vessel Clearing Game' using paper, drawing supplies and a hand-made wooden vessel model, with students drawing their own food items and testing whether they fit through. Secondary outreach covered St. Paul's School (Lam Tin) with 169 participants and Hoi Ping Chamber of Commerce Secondary School with 19, using a micropipetting precision-dyeing challenge and a 'Detective on Call' online DNA fingerprinting game. They also ran a card game for community-centre children including ethnic-minority children, an elderly workshop, a Hong Kong Joint Secondary Schools Science Fair booth, and regional middle-school outreach in the Greater Bay Area. Reflection was built in with structured worksheets, exit cards and open Q&A, and every activity records what they changed next - after noticing their terminology was too advanced for young children they switched to plainer language, more gestures and more Cantonese, and they adapted in real time when a student asked 'Why strawberries? Why not apples?' by adding an impromptu discussion.","ihp":"IHP was organised as four parallel two-way feedback loops (Public, Healthcare, Science, Industry) feeding both the wet lab and the education team, structured over three stages (Understanding the Problem, Identifying the Needs, Develop & Co-design) and processed through a four-step cycle they call LIVP (Locate, Iterate, Validate, Propose). The design changes are traceable to named people. Professor Chan Ting Fung (RNA biology) assessed the toehold-switch limitations and identified HCR as the alternative worth trying for signal amplification - the single biggest pivot in the project - and later recommended expanding the H1/H2 sequence design space to mask the ribosome binding site to eliminate background leakage, noted that lithium interference in urine is negligible, and told them to test sodium's effect on the DNAzyme experimentally. Professor Szeto Cheuk Chun (Head of Medicine and Therapeutics, CUHK, whose research is urinary biomarkers) confirmed miRNA as near-ideal for urine because of its stability, told them to run miR-21-5p and miR-192-5p in parallel as a complementary test (which is why the kit has two detection pathways and a six-well chamber), said biomarkers are detectable in urine that has not been high-speed centrifuged so they could drop centrifugation from the workflow, warned that urinary chromogens may interfere with a visible colour readout, set a user-experience target of roughly half an hour to result, and endorsed a colour change readable under natural light - which is precisely why the team ended up on G4/hemin colorimetry rather than ThT fluorescence, since ThT needs UV excitation and a fluorescence reader. Professor Ma Ming Yao established that the kit must be positioned as a screening flag prompting medical follow-up, never a diagnosis, and that false positives and negatives must be managed responsibly, and that high-risk patients are the target - which reshaped how the whole wiki speaks about the product and prompted a community education workshop for the elderly. A medical laboratory technologist insisted on a clear, reasonable cut-off concentration for the target miRNA, rigorous quality control, timely sample processing, and a larger sample size for credibility, which pushed the team towards a multi-target design. E-SENSE Innovation & Technology Limited said clinicians will only recommend a home test backed by real-world clinical data with low false-positive and false-negative rates, which pushed the wet lab to run the cell-free EGFP validation and the HCR sensitivity series. Public input was quantified: a street interview of 137 people and an online survey of 283 found 52% had never heard of CKD or diabetic kidney disease and only 28% had heard of both, 60% did not know or were unsure that early kidney disease can be symptomless, and 63.2% of street respondents cited absence of symptoms as their reason for not getting screened.","realWorld":"The intended product is an at-home urine screening kit, costed against a system where each Hong Kong dialysis patient runs about HKD 50,000 per year and where confirming CKD currently needs repeat clinic visits over a three-month window because uACR varies by 30% or more day to day and cystatin C is several times the price of creatinine and rarely reimbursed. Every design decision is pulled towards deployability: urine rather than blood, no centrifuge, enzyme-free isothermal amplification at room temperature, and a colour change readable in natural light without any instrument. The team is consistently careful about regulatory and clinical positioning - the kit is repeatedly described as a screening flag that prompts follow-up and complements, rather than replaces, eGFR and uACR, and the kit visualisation is explicitly captioned as a research concept, not a validated diagnostic product. What is not present on the wiki is a business model, pricing, manufacturing plan or named regulatory pathway; industry engagement with E-SENSE is framed around clinical credibility and usability requirements rather than commercialisation, and the honest gap they name is the need for real-world paired clinical data before a home device is justified.","partners":["Professor Szeto Cheuk Chun, Chinese University of Hong Kong (urinary biomarkers)","Professor Chan Ting Fung (RNA biology)","Professor Ma Ming Yao (nephrologist)","Professor Tang Chi Wai, Sydney (nephrologist)","Dr. Au Wing Han (nephrologist)","E-SENSE Innovation & Technology Limited","a clinical medical laboratory technologist","Salesian Yip Hon Millennium Primary School","Shek Lei Catholic Primary School","St. Paul's School (Lam Tin)","Hoi Ping Chamber of Commerce Secondary School","Wageningen iGEM 2024 (miRADAR software reused)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6214/wiki/project/engineering/fig-10-complex-formation-of-toehold-7-for-mir-192-5p-nupack.avif","caption":"NUPACK equilibrium complex-formation analysis for toehold 7 against miR-192-5p; the red bars show the active toehold-miRNA complex concentration used to rank candidates alongside the HMFE-versus-TMFE comparison.","credit":"Team HongKong-HS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6214/wiki/project/engineering/fig-46-mirna-h1-h2-complex.avif","caption":"Predicted minimum-free-energy structure of the miR-192-5p-H1-H2 hybridisation chain reaction complex, used to verify polymer formation and that the inserted G-quadruplex motifs sit in the hairpin loops.","credit":"Team HongKong-HS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6214/wiki/project/results/hcr/results-fig18-coloured-result-of-h1-h2-g-quadruplex-with-hemin-and-tmbs.avif","caption":"The naked-eye readout the whole project was redesigned to reach: HCR products with G-quadruplex/hemin DNAzyme oxidising TMB across a range of miR-21 and miR-192 concentrations, stopped with sulphuric acid and read at 450 nm.","credit":"Team HongKong-HS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6214/wiki/education/figure-5.avif","caption":"A primary student using the team's magnetic toehold-switch model to simulate miRNA detection - only the target miRNA card contains a hidden magnet, so molecular specificity becomes something a child can feel.","credit":"Team HongKong-HS, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Two things to steal. First, NUPACK plus a reused iGEM software tool (Wageningen's miRADAR) is a complete, free, high-school-feasible dry lab: HongKong-HS ranked 16 candidate switches on explicit thermodynamic criteria (HMFE vs TMFE, downstream secondary structure blocking the RBS, equilibrium base-pair probability) and predicted the background-leakage failure mode before the gel showed it. Second, their wiki proves that the best data visualisation is mechanism animation plus per-figure provenance - every statistic carries its own population, year and source, and the AI-generated product imagery is labelled as such, which is exactly the standard a research centre's outputs should hold itself to.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-hs/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-hs/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-hs/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-hs/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-hs/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-hs/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-hs/safety","https://2026.igem.wiki/hongkong-hs/contribution","https://api.igem.org/v1/teams/6214"],"rank":84},{"team":"WageningenUR","id":"6222","section":"Overgrad","country":"Netherlands","village":"Bioremediation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/","projectTitle":"Triton","tagline":"Capture, degrade and sense trifluoroacetate: a three-module biological system for the smallest forever chemical, aimed at reverse-osmosis concentrate.","summary":"Triton targets trifluoroacetate (TFA), the ultra-short-chain PFAS that makes up more than 98% of all PFAS measured in EU river and groundwater and was found in the blood of all 110 Europeans tested in a 2026 study. Rather than one trick, they built three modules shaped entirely by stakeholder input: a de novo protein binder on bacterial cellulose beads to concentrate TFA out of reverse-osmosis concentrate, a growth-coupled directed hyper-evolution platform to push fluoroacetate dehalogenases from monofluoroacetate toward difluoroacetate and then TFA, and a riboswitch biosensor for continuous on-site monitoring. The dry lab contributed two reusable tools - PIGEON, an active-learning in silico evolution loop that lifted a Boltz-2 binding probability by over 33%, and DISARM, which retargets existing aptamers to new small-molecule ligands without SELEX. They took 1st Runner-up Grand Prize plus Best Measurement, Best Presentation and Best Bioremediation Project.","awards":["1st Runner-up (Overgrad)","Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Measurement — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Presentation — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Bioremediation Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"The capture module is a full generative-plus-evaluative protein design stack. They first searched the literature and the PDB for anything reported to bind TFA and found no strong binder, then generated de novo candidates with Proteina-Complexa and BoltzGen (both denoising generative models, seeded with the TFA conformation taken from PDB 4FVX), reasoning that evolution never had selective pressure to bind a molecule that did not exist before 1922. Every candidate was scored with Boltz-2 rather than docking, with the choice justified explicitly: AutoDock Vina treats the protein as rigid and correlated poorly with experiment (R^2 = 0.604 on a 2016 benchmark), while Boltz-2 is trained on millions of experimental binding measurements and approaches free-energy-perturbation accuracy over 1000x faster. No natural or de novo scaffold passed 0.540, held by the L-2-haloacid dehalogenase RSc1362 (PDB 3UMB). Rational pocket design came next: they mined the literature for residues that prefer the CF3 group over CH3 (arginine, methionine, glycine, histidine, glutamine, phenylalanine) with their distance preferences, used PLIP to find TFA-contacting residues, and applied a three-criterion automated mutation script - 12 of 14 mutable scaffolds improved, with a caltractin A4H substitution jumping 0.118 to 0.518, and RSc1362_v2 reaching 0.566. Then PIGEON (Protein In silico Guided Evolution and Optimization Network) closed the loop: ESM3 embeddings of the RSc1362 family paired with Boltz-2 scores train a ridge regression under repeated leave-one-out CV; ESM3 guided decoding then proposes mutations against a custom objective that rewards binding gains while preserving pTM and charges binding losses tenfold (so a 0.01 binding loss would need +0.67 pTM to break even); binding-pocket residues are protected, four designs per parent across ten parents per round, best-per-parent carried forward, newly folded scores appended to the training set. Over 50 rounds RSc1362_v3 reached 0.723, a 33% gain over wild type, with the regression's Spearman rho rising from 0.65 to 0.92 and mean pTM flat - evidence the gain was learned, not lucky. Degradation design was grounded in published QM/MM work mapping the full FAcD catalytic cycle (Asp-His-Asp triad, fluoride-binding pocket His150/Trp151/Tyr213) onto MFA, DFA and TFA, which showed the rate-determining step shifts from nucleophilic attack to C-F activation after the first fluorine and that the barrier rises ~11.8 kcal/mol from MFA to DFA but only ~1.8 from DFA to TFA; that single insight set their whole strategy of evolving for DFA first.","dataviz":"The wiki is a scroll-driven explainer that teaches the problem before the solution: TFA's ECHA Repr. 1B classification, the 110-of-110 blood result, the >98%-of-all-PFAS share and the 34-of-36 tap water samples each land as their own beat, with a stacked TFA-versus-all-other-PFAS bar and a four-property card (accumulating, unregulated, unbreakable, tiny) before the three-step Capture/Degrade/Sense walkthrough. Stakeholder quotes are first-class design elements, each with a named face, rather than buried on a human practices page - Peter Fischer's 'it's not a question of if TFA will be added to the Drinking Water Directive, it's a question of when' and Bert van der Wal's 'a TFA binder would be fantastic, then you have gold in your hands' carry the narrative. The measurement page is built as a decision aid rather than a result log: a comparison scorecard across five media lets another team pick conditions for their own question. The front page also includes a scoring widget that opens the visitor's own mail client to send feedback to the team.","datascience":"The Best Measurement work is the data-science highlight because they treated an assay as a product. Starting from the Belcher alizarin-lanthanum fluoride chemistry as used qualitatively by Bygd et al., they rebuilt it as a quantitative method: a ratiometric readout found by searching for the isosbestic point and selecting the optimal numerator wavelength in each medium, with stated acceptance thresholds (R^2 >= 0.95, >= 40 nm separation, >= 20% signal change, positive slope) and selection by lowest LOD, then LOD = 3.3 sigma/b and LOQ = 10 sigma/b with an explicit rule for which sigma to use. Fixed conditions are all stated (100 uM 1:1 alizarin:lanthanum, 84 mM acetate buffer pH 5.2, 25 v/v% acetone added after a 1-hour dark incubation, per-well volumes in 96-well format) and the data analysis is written as a procedure rather than a result, so another team reproduces the method even if their numbers differ. They characterised it as a tool across four fluorochemicals (MFA, DFA, TFA, PFOA), two hosts (E. coli MG1655, P. putida KT2440) and five media (demi water, M9, HEPES-buffered M9, 20 mM HEPES, tap water), and documented the limits as carefully as the working range: phosphate interferes, response saturates above 40 uM, 5 mM fluorochemicals suppress signal up to 60%. Controls are enumerated by class - measurement (10 blank wells for sigma, 40 uM spike, literature condition reproduced before any change), matrix (M9 as a phosphate-interference control, 93-hour incubated supernatant, fluorochemical spikes) and biological (wild-type and empty-vector negatives, MFA as a should-work substrate, PFOA as a should-not).","software":"PIGEON and DISARM, both with code on the iGEM GitLab at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/wageningenur. PIGEON is the generalisable one: an active-learning loop over ESM3 embeddings, ridge regression and ESM3 guided decoding against a user-defined objective, which the team explicitly documents as retargetable to solubility, thermostability or ipTM rather than only Boltz-2 binding probability, and which they distinguish from a conventional ML model because it proposes mutations rather than only scoring given inputs. DISARM (Detecting Interaction Sites for Aptamer Retargeting by Mutation) recommends mutations that retarget an existing aptamer or riboswitch to a new ligand of similar size: it identifies favourable nucleotide positions around the ligand, compares them with the existing aptamer's nucleotide positions and backbone connection points, and returns 50 candidates while preserving secondary structure - positioned as a partial replacement for SELEX, or at least a rational starting library. They validated 10 DISARM riboswitches in the wet lab (4 PFOA, 4 TFA, 2 scrambled controls) and reported honestly that one PFOA riboswitch met the success criterion and no TFA riboswitch did. The contribution page also packages a reusable defluorination framework - the fluoride assay with calibration procedure, controls, sample prep and analysis workflow plus a step-by-step experimental video - and a hypermutation platform and expression architecture described as adaptable to any enzyme.","stack":["Boltz-2","BoltzGen","Proteina-Complexa","ESM3 (embeddings + guided decoding)","Ridge regression (scikit-learn)","PLIP (Protein-Ligand Interaction Profiler)","AutoDock Vina (benchmarked against, not used)","AMBER / molecular dynamics","Protein Data Bank","AlphaFold (model AF-P00506-F1)","Python","iGEM GitLab","96-well plate UV-Vis absorbance (400-700 nm)","BLASTp / BLASTn against patent sequence databases"],"techniques":["de novo binder generation by denoising generative models","learned affinity prediction in place of rigid-body docking","literature-derived CF3 residue-preference rules applied as automated pocket mutations","active-learning in silico directed evolution with a custom multi-objective reward","asymmetric penalty weighting to prevent objective gaming (10x on binding loss)","binding-pocket residue protection during mutagenesis","repeated leave-one-out cross-validation on small design sets","tracking surrogate-model Spearman rho across rounds as evidence of learning","ratiometric absorbance assay with isosbestic-point calibration","LOD/LOQ determination from blank replicates","per-medium recalibration as a documented local step","growth-coupled directed hyper-evolution","in silico aptamer retargeting as a SELEX alternative"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Triton has no separate education or outreach page; its public-facing work is the wiki itself, built as a scroll-driven public explainer that teaches why TFA matters before it mentions synthetic biology - the ECHA reproductive-toxicity classification, the 110-of-110 blood study, the >98% PFAS share, the 34-of-36 tap water result - with named stakeholder quotes and faces carrying the argument, a front-page feedback widget that opens the visitor's own mail client, and a step-by-step experimental video for the fluoride assay published for other labs to follow. Their documented engagement effort went into the professional and policy community rather than schools: over 30 stakeholders through interviews, company visits and events, with every stakeholder given an explanation of why the team reached out, what was learned and what was or was not incorporated, plus a published analysis of future regulatory and industrial developments offered as a starting point for anyone working on TFA contamination.","ihp":"The most traceable chain of design changes in the cohort, and the shifts are structural rather than cosmetic. They started intending only to degrade TFA; stakeholders told them they were missing an essential step. Peter Fischer (Aqualab Zuid, one of six companies legally tasked with assessing Dutch drinking water quality) showed that TFA can only be measured by LC-MS on samples shipped to a central lab, so it is expensive, discontinuous and ex situ - which meant the bioreactor could not be operated at all, and directly created the biosensor module. Frank Schoonenberg (process engineer, Vitens) explained that reverse osmosis filters TFA but does not destroy it, that the resulting concentrate is currently discharged, and that discharging it will be prohibited - so they designed Triton to treat RO concentrate rather than drinking water, filling a need nobody currently serves. Bert van der Waal and Peyman Salehian then showed that even 5-10x RO concentration leaves TFA far too dilute (an ideal 10x on the Dutch 2.2 ug/L indicative value reaches only 22 ug/L against a milligram-per-litre target), which created the capture module. Amanda Larasati ruled out ion exchange resins because resin leaching bars them from Dutch water infrastructure. Tue Kjaergaard Nielsen warned that measuring TFA reduction produces false positives because TFA adsorbs to surfaces rather than being degraded, pushing them to measure released fluoride instead. Clemens Mayer (molecular evolution, Groningen) led them to directed evolution for an enzyme that does not yet exist. Amin Espah Borujeni helped redesign the riboswitch proof-of-concept experiments after the published plasmid failed to reproduce. Regulatory interviews established that a product containing no viable GMOs faces a considerably simpler approval path, which they explored alongside deliberate release and which shaped biosafety from an early stage. They also document conflicting stakeholder views and explain why some were not implemented.","realWorld":"Implementation is specified as a real retrofit rather than a concept: Triton sits downstream of existing split-stream reverse osmosis at a drinking water plant, with the capture module as a granular filter of bacterial cellulose beads carrying TFA-binding proteins attached through a cellulose-binding domain, eluted with a wash buffer so beads are reused, feeding a three-day bioreactor where evolved dehalogenase turns TFA into oxalate and fluoride while the riboswitch biosensor monitors continuously. Biosafety is specified per module: the beads contain no living cells and need no extra measures, the biosensor's small water stream is collected and autoclaved, and the bioreactor effluent gets UV sterilisation before discharge. Timeline is roughly 10 years to drinking-water implementation, with permitting, certification and grid congestion named as the bottlenecks rather than construction, and the biosensor identified as the faster path. The market analysis is sharply scoped - not water treatment, not PFAS at large, but the destruction of PFAS that filtration cannot retain, noting destruction was 24.9% of the PFAS treatment market in 2025 and is the fastest-growing segment at a projected 10.1% CAGR against 7.1% overall. The business plan runs a six-phase TRL 3 to 9 roadmap with a gate per phase, a cost analysis across physical and human capital, 2.6M euro to raise and break-even in 2033, six commercial and adoption risks each with a mitigation, five technical failure modes each with a fallback, an operational plan with a Dreyfus-model skill gap analysis resolved build/borrow/outsource, and a SWOT identifying a co-development partner as the predominant stakeholder. IP is worked out concretely: the software is not patentable because its source is deposited on the iGEM GitLab (but is copyright-protected), while BLASTp against the patented protein database returned only 43.30% maximum identity for DAR3835 and H1_ML against claims requiring 95%, so the evolved enzymes remain patentable.","partners":["Vitens (Frank Schoonenberg, process engineer)","Aqualab Zuid (Peter Fischer, scientific advisor)","Prof. Bert van der Waal, Wageningen University (environmental sciences)","Peyman Salehian (enzyme development)","Clemens Mayer, University of Groningen (molecular evolution)","Amin Espah Borujeni (riboswitch design)","Tue Kjaergaard Nielsen","Amanda Larasati","WaterIQ (Freddy Dekkers)","Harrie Timmer","RIVM","Arcadis","Witteveen+Bos","Nijhuis Saur","Bas Eickhout (policy)","Christos Batianis and Enrique Asin-Garcia, Wageningen University"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6222/wiki/text-images/general/triton-wholesystem-v5.avif","caption":"The full Triton system: reverse-osmosis concentrate passes a bacterial-cellulose capture filter to concentrate TFA, then a bioreactor where evolved dehalogenase cleaves the C-F bond while a riboswitch biosensor monitors concentration continuously.","credit":"Team WageningenUR, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6222/wiki/text-images/dry-lab/capturing-drylab/kevin-engineering-figure-7b.webp","caption":"Fifty rounds of PIGEON in silico evolution across five runs, lifting RSc1362's Boltz-2 binding probability from 0.540 to 0.723 while the surrogate model's Spearman rho rises from 0.65 to 0.92 and mean pTM stays flat.","credit":"Team WageningenUR, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6222/wiki/text-images/measurement/triton-measurement-scorecard.avif","caption":"The medium comparison scorecard from the Best Measurement work, which lets another team choose assay conditions for their own research question rather than copying a single validated setup.","credit":"Team WageningenUR, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6222/wiki/text-images/measurement/triton-measurement-cuvettes.webp","caption":"The alizarin-lanthanum fluoride assay in practice: the spectral shift on fluoride release is visible to the naked eye above roughly 20 uM, so no fluoride electrode, ion chromatography or 19F-NMR is needed.","credit":"Team WageningenUR, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Build the measurement first and publish it as a product, not a result: their fluoride assay won Best Measurement because the protocol, the LOD/LOQ derivation, the acceptance thresholds, the comparison across five media and the documented failure modes (phosphate interference, saturation above 40 uM) all let another lab decide in advance whether it fits - and it needs only a 96-well plate reader and two cheap reagents, which is squarely within a high-school research centre's reach. The companion habit worth copying is PIGEON's reward design: charging a loss in the primary objective tenfold against the secondary one stops a model from quietly trading away the thing you actually care about.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/design/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/dry-lab-overview/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/capturing/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/capturing-dry-lab/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/degradation/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/sensing/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/riboswitch/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/measurement/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/implementation/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wageningenur/contribution/"],"rank":264},{"team":"William-and-Mary","id":"6243","section":"Undergrad","country":"United States","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/","projectTitle":"BROKEN: Building Resilient Optimized Engineered Networks","tagline":"Bringing failure analysis from mature engineering disciplines into synthetic biology: detect, mitigate and predict how genetic circuits break.","summary":"William & Mary argued that synthetic biology measures whether a circuit works and almost never measures how long it keeps working. BROKEN therefore ran longitudinal experiments on four previously engineered circuits in laboratory cultures and in seawater microcosms, following them through serial passaging to deterioration rather than stopping at initial success. The team paired functional readouts (OD600, beta-carotene production, GFP, biofilm morphology, starch hydrolysis, colony-forming units) with molecular verification (PCR, restriction digests, gels, NanoDrop) and with multi-omics (RNA-seq, genomic DNA sequencing, long-read plasmid sequencing), then built mechanistic ODE models and machine-learning approaches to predict time to failure. The reliability framing came directly from Integrated Human Practices and turned safety into the project's central motivation, which is reflected in its Safety and Security Award.","awards":["Best Measurement — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Undergrad)","Safety and Security Award — Winner (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"A stated measurement philosophy of multitiered (population, circuit output, viability, DNA integrity, transcription), multimodal (optical, biochemical, microbiological, sequencing) and redundant measurement, with the explicit rationale that a falling fluorescence signal could equally mean circuit dysfunction, reduced growth, altered reporter maturation or changed physiology. An extension of the standard Design-Build-Test-Learn cycle so that engineering does not end at a functioning construct but continues through systematic longevity and failure testing in both laboratory and realistic environmental conditions, with results feeding back into design.","dataviz":"Longitudinal fluorescence and beta-carotene time-course figures, kill-switch and thymineless-death assay panels, DNA mutation tables for the corrosion-prevention construct, leakiness panels and circuit-expression comparisons across experiments, all numbered as a continuous figure series across the results and measurement pages.","datascience":"The headline measurement contribution is absolute-style fluorescence calibration: the team prepared concentration series of fluorescein and sulforhodamine 101 in PBS, measured four replicates at each concentration with PBS blanks to assess solvent background, fitted linear regressions of fluorophore concentration against RFU with reported R2 greater than 0.99, and then used published extinction coefficients and quantum yields to estimate conversion factors between the calibration dyes and S65T GFP, mKate2, mCherry and cpGFP, so that plate-reader readings could enter the mathematical models as concentration-like variables instead of instrument-dependent arbitrary units. They state the limitation plainly, that because dye and protein optical properties differ these conversions are estimates contingent on the correction factors. Normalisation is handled explicitly: beta-carotene is reported as A454/OD600 so that per-cell production is separated from cell number, uncontaminated LB is measured as a blank, and OD600 is used both to standardise serial transfers and to interpret every fluorescence comparison. Where optical turbidity cannot answer the biological question, the technique changes: CFU counts are used for kill-switch viability and for thymineless death and rescue, on the advice of Dr Andrei Kuzminov, with plating on thymidine-supplemented medium so that the assay does not itself impose further starvation. Reporter maturation delay was built into measurement timing after Dr Sean Sleight pointed out that a delay in fluorescence is not a delay in transcription. The RNA-seq pipeline is specified end to end: fastp for adapter trimming and quality filtering, SortMeRNA for residual rRNA removal, HISAT2 alignment against combined host-plus-plasmid references built as merged FASTA and GFF files, featureCounts for gene-level assignment and DESeq2 for differential expression; genome assemblies from Plasmidsaurus were annotated with Bakta in Geneious Prime and variant tables exported. Modelling carries uncertainty properly: SciPy least-squares fits of the lambda-repressor model give R2 = 0.82 and chi2/dof = 0.88 at 30 C, and the team reports that the 42 C prediction degrades to R2 = 0.55 with chi2/dof = 7.57, six to eight times worse than the in-sample value and therefore larger than measurement noise explains, rather than presenting the prediction as validated. A light-responsive fit is reported as poor at the assumed error (chi2/dof = 4.7, p = 0.0009) with the specific failure described (the dark curve fits, the light curve is flat then bursts). Because the labelled dataset is tiny (N = 16), model selection uses leave-one-out and leave-one-series-out rather than a train/test split, with benchmarking across OLS, Ridge, Lasso, Elastic Net, random forest and Gaussian process; Ridge on circuit fraction plus stress genes gives leave-one-out R2 = 0.61, and a classifier reaches AUC 0.880 and accuracy 0.830, both reported with the caveat that 16 samples cannot establish a general predictor.","software":"All modelling and analysis code is published with documentation on the team's iGEM GitLab, with instructions for both terminal use and a web interface. A Guidebook for Circuit Failure Measurement is released as a PDF, offering a tiered approach scaled to a laboratory's budget, from accessible functional assays through molecular diagnostics to sequencing and multi-omics. Longitudinal RNA-seq and DNA-seq datasets are released for the community, paired with functional, growth and viability measurements so that molecular change can be linked to observed performance. The hardware, software, alternative-platform and entrepreneurship wiki pages remain unfilled iGEM templates.","stack":["fastp","SortMeRNA","HISAT2","featureCounts","DESeq2","Bakta","Geneious Prime","Plasmidsaurus (Oxford Nanopore long-read)","SciPy (least_squares)","scikit-learn-style regression benchmarking","Plate reader fluorescence and OD600","Flow-cytometry-free CFU viability"],"techniques":["Fluorescein and sulforhodamine 101 calibration curves (R2 > 0.99)","Dye-to-protein conversion factors via extinction coefficient and quantum yield","OD600 normalisation and blank subtraction","A454/OD600 product normalisation","CFU viability in place of turbidity","Reporter maturation-aware timing","Longitudinal serial passaging to a failure endpoint","Seawater microcosm environmental testing","chi2/dof goodness of fit","Leave-one-out and leave-one-series-out validation","Model benchmarking on a fixed small dataset"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"","ihp":"Integrated Human Practices reframed the project three times over. Contributors from aerospace, civil and electrical engineering backgrounds pushed the team to adopt not only the methods of failure engineering but its mindset, that failure should be anticipated and designed for rather than observed after the fact. The most consequential shift was the recognition that circuit failure is a biosafety problem, not a performance problem, which moved safety from a secondary consideration to the central motivation and underpins the team's Safety and Security Award. Technical guidance entered the DBTL cycle directly: Dr Alex Porter pressed the team on the gap between laboratory performance and behaviour under realistic environmental conditions, which produced the seawater microcosm experiments; Dr Sean Sleight raised fluorescent-protein maturation, which changed measurement timing; and Dr Andrei Kuzminov argued that OD600 and fluorescence could not measure survival during thymineless death, which replaced the planned readout with CFU-based viability.","realWorld":"The project's deliverables are aimed at the field rather than at a product: an open-source set of standard operating protocols written so that someone who was not present can follow them, a tiered Guidebook for Circuit Failure Measurement scaled to different budgets, publicly released longitudinal multi-omics datasets for comparative failure analysis and model validation, and a proposed extension of DBTL that adds systematic longevity and failure testing. The team also models building on previously published circuits rather than treating initial characterisation as an endpoint, and frames kill switches and other safety-critical circuits as the components that most need the failure analysis mature engineering disciplines already apply.","partners":["Dr Alex J. Porter","Dr Sean Sleight","Dr Andrei Kuzminov","Plasmidsaurus"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6243/wiki/figures/figure1-betacarotene-assay.avif","caption":"Beta-carotene assay following circuit output over serial passaging. Absorbance at 454 nm is normalised to OD600 so that a fall in total pigment can be separated from a fall in cell number.","credit":"Team William-and-Mary, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6243/wiki/figures/figure3-killswitch-assay.avif","caption":"Kill-switch assay. Colony-forming unit counts before and after induction are used instead of OD600 or fluorescence, because the intended output of a kill switch is loss of viability rather than loss of reporter signal.","credit":"Team William-and-Mary, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6243/wiki/figures/figure10-thymineless-rescue.avif","caption":"Thymineless rescue measured by CFU on thymidine-supplemented medium, an assay design adopted after Dr Andrei Kuzminov advised that optical density and fluorescence would not measure survival directly.","credit":"Team William-and-Mary, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6243/wiki/figures/fig11-hd260828-gfp-fluorescence-timecourse.avif","caption":"GFP fluorescence time course for an engineered construct. Raw plate-reader RFU values are converted toward concentration-like units using fluorescein and sulforhodamine 101 calibration curves (R2 > 0.99) before entering the mathematical models.","credit":"Team William-and-Mary, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Calibrate fluorescence against a dye concentration series before any number leaves the plate reader, and report model fits with chi2/dof against the measurement error rather than R2 alone, so that an out-of-sample prediction that is six times worse than the in-sample fit is visible as a failure instead of being dressed up as a moderate correlation.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/william-and-mary/engineering"],"rank":40},{"team":"Heidelberg","id":"6249","section":"Undergrad","country":"Germany","village":"Foundational Advance","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/","projectTitle":"VOYAGE","tagline":"An acoustic platform for observation and organization of living systems: gas vesicles engineered into a programmable interface between ultrasound and biology.","summary":"Fluorescence microscopy sees molecular detail but light scattering stops it at tissue depth; X-ray reaches deep but uses ionising radiation; ultrasound penetrates deep without radiation and can even push cells around, but has no molecular specificity. VOYAGE closes that gap with gas vesicles — gas-filled protein nanostructures from aquatic microorganisms, built from GvpA with GvpC reinforcing the outer shell — which scatter ultrasound as genetically encodable contrast agents and buckle at a collapse pressure that can be tuned by engineering GvpC. From that one part the team built four capabilities: GvpC variants with distinct pressure responses as a basis for multiplexed acoustic imaging, a kinase-responsive GvpC that turns phosphorylation into an acoustic signal, latch-based biosensors that report soluble targets, and DARPin- or SpyCatcher-directed vesicles that act as acoustic handles, enriching labelled HEK293T cells roughly tenfold at the side outlet of an acoustophoretic chip and lining them up at pressure antinodes while unlabelled cells sit at the nodes. It won Best Wiki and Best Part Collection because the delivery matched the science: a modular part collection with expression systems for Dolichospermum flos-aquae gas vesicles in mammalian cells and Serratia gas vesicles in E. coli, an accessibility guide for working with gas vesicles on a budget, and ATLAS — a full computational companion spanning all-atom molecular dynamics, three protein design pipelines, a fine-tuned segmentation model and an ultrasound signal model.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Part Collection — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Foundational Advance Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"ATLAS is the most physics-heavy dry lab of the five, in four parts. (1) All-atom molecular dynamics: two neighbouring ribs of 20-25 GvpA from PDB 8GBS in a periodic box, TIP3P water with 150 mM NaCl, CHARMM36 force field, GvpC 33-residue repeats docked on the outer face with full-length termini predicted by AlphaFold2, energy minimisation then restrained equilibration then 6-10 ns production at 310 K in GROMACS 2024, at least three independent runs per variant. Bending stiffness EI is extracted by decomposing each rib's height profile into waves and fitting the equipartition relation where mean squared amplitude falls as the fourth power of wave number, analysed in equal 1.5-2 ns blocks with Welch's t-test across runs, then converted to a critical buckling pressure by classical Timoshenko shell theory at R = 42.5 nm and rib spacing s = 4.9 nm. GvpC binding free energy comes from steered MD (1 nm/ns, force constant 1000 kJ/nm^2) seeding 25 umbrella sampling windows along the centre-of-mass reaction coordinate, recombined by WHAM into a potential of mean force. Wettability was tested by measuring water contact angle on the gas-facing wall face. (2) An in silico evolution pipeline for GvpC: AlphaFold2 predictions of a Serratia GvpA tetramer and a GvpC repeat aligned onto the experimental Dolichospermum GvpA-GvpC complex and repacked, then LigandMPNN redesigning interface residues across four tiers from fully native to all ten positions, with candidates screened by Boltz-2 co-folding, AlphaFold2 in initial-guess mode and PyRosetta relaxation. (3) A de novo binder pipeline: RFdiffusion3 generating backbones against four adjacent GvpA chains, ProteinMPNN designing eight sequences per backbone, AlphaFold2 re-prediction, then filtering on interface PAE, binder pLDDT and RMSD. (4) A biosensor pipeline where RFdiffusion3 designs a blocking latch fused to the binder at freely chosen hotspots, ProteinMPNN redesigns it, and AlphaFold2 scores three states — construct plus GvpA alone (should not bind), construct plus target alone (latch should displace by more than 3.0 A RMSD), and construct plus both (both bound, latch fully dissociated) — with PyRosetta interface delta-G estimates on each bound state.","dataviz":"This is the Best Wiki winner and the visualisation is doing real work rather than decoration. Every technical term carries an inline definition on hover (acoustic contrast, acoustic pressure, xAM, acoustophoresis, DARPin, pLDDT, i_pAE), and every computational tool gets an 'Explore this software' panel explaining what it is, how it works and what its published benchmarks are — so a reader can follow the pipeline without already knowing LigandMPNN or CellSAM. Structures are interactive: a 'View in 3D' control turns the progression from one GvpA, to a rib inside its cryo-EM density (EMD-14238), to the 13,450-copy vesicle, to the GvpC cage; MD trajectories play as interactive animations of the wall rippling; and biosensor constructs can be rotated with and without bound GFP. The GVSAM results are presented as a ten-image gallery where the reader switches each test image between raw, ground truth, GVSAM prediction with true positives, false positives and false negatives marked, and the base CellSAM prediction — which lets a sceptic check the model rather than take the F1 score on trust. Design pipelines appear as schematic flow diagrams, and the binder filtering is shown as KDE plots overlaying all 1,429 designs against the 13 accepted ones on each metric, making visible that many designs pass individual gates while almost none pass all three.","datascience":"The honesty is the distinguishing feature. The MD model predicts a 1.63-fold stiffness increase from truncated GvpC against 1.48-fold measured experimentally — close — but its absolute collapse pressure estimate of 124-137 kPa without GvpC sits well below the experimental 195 kPa, and the team states plainly that ATLAS is therefore suited to ranking variants rather than predicting absolute pressures, because a whole vesicle is not an idealised wall patch. The umbrella sampling gives 43.5 kJ/mol to remove one GvpC repeat (about 17 k_B T at 310 K) and notes that the free energy plateaus once the repeat has moved 0.55 nm, when only 24% of original atom contacts remain, so most of the cost is paid before the last contacts break. The wetting hypothesis was tested and rejected on its merits: 122.8 degrees without GvpC against 122.2 with, a 0.6-degree difference against the roughly 6 degrees a condensation mechanism would require. Screening thresholds were calibrated against controls rather than chosen by feel — the redesigned sequences were run alongside native Serratia GvpC repeat 3 and nine negative controls, and of all metrics tested only AlphaFold2 interface PAE separated them cleanly (135 of 200 designs passed the 12.0 gate, all nine negative controls failed, the native repeat passed at 11.42), with the team stating explicitly that only that one threshold is calibrated and the other three are a priori safeguards whose predictive value is unproven. PyRosetta interface energies are reported but excluded from selection because they did not discriminate the controls. GVSAM's evaluation was adapted to the problem: intersection over union is unsuitable for objects this small because a one-pixel shift wrecks it, so a hit requires the annotated box centre to lie inside the prediction within 2 px and the areas to differ by at most fourfold, each annotation matchable once, highest-scoring first — with recall, precision and F1 pooled across images and 95% confidence intervals bootstrapped over images. They report F1 rising from 0.2% (base CellSAM) to 42% (GVSAM) with false positives down 97%, and openly diagnose overfitting: validation loss bottomed at epoch 6 while training loss kept falling, so that checkpoint was taken. Two team members annotated independently to reduce single-annotator bias, images of deliberately varying quality and images with no vesicles at all were kept for robustness, and the 108 annotated images were split 70/20/10 at image level with vesicle and non-vesicle images split separately. Ultrasound analysis in GAS uses median intensity and median absolute deviation rather than mean and standard deviation because ultrasound speckle makes single-pixel brightness fluctuate with intensity, with background subtracted as the median recorded after all vesicles were destroyed, and collapse pressure taken as P50 from a four-parameter regression fit (R-squared above 0.99 on all curves).","software":"ATLAS ships as a documented end-to-end pipeline with its own comprehensive user guide, covering the MD characterisation route, the GvpC in silico evolution pipeline and the de novo binder and biosensor design pipelines. GVSAM is a fine-tuned derivative of CellSAM (itself built on Meta's Segment Anything Model with an Anchor DETR based CellFinder detector and a ViT-B encoder) retrained to detect gas vesicle clusters rather than whole cells, trained with Dice plus binary cross-entropy with logits, batch size 3, learning rate 1e-4 (backbone 1e-5), weight decay 1e-4, annotated in napari and converted to bounding boxes by a Python script, with 90 and 270 degree rotation augmentation to cope with only 108 images. GAS is the physics-informed fitting model that converts raw cross-amplitude-modulation ultrasound data into pressure-dependent signal curves. On the wet side the team released the VOYAGE part collection (expression systems for D. flos-aquae gas vesicles in mammalian cells and Serratia gas vesicles in E. coli, plus characterisation and purification parts), an extensive collection of optimised gas vesicle protocols including AFM and darkfield imaging, and an accessibility guide for working with gas vesicles on a budget — which includes using the open-source 3D-printed OpenFlexure microscope for darkfield imaging of purified vesicles and live-cell imaging of producers, explicitly so that teams with limited resources can replicate the work. The Fail Forward Guide is the fourth released artefact.","stack":["GROMACS 2024 with CHARMM36 and TIP3P","Umbrella sampling with WHAM","Steered molecular dynamics","Martini coarse-grained MD (tested and rejected)","AlphaFold2 (including initial-guess mode)","Boltz-2","LigandMPNN","ProteinMPNN","RFdiffusion3 (Rosetta Commons Foundry)","BindCraft (hallucination, with custom loss penalty)","PyRosetta","CellSAM / Segment Anything Model (fine-tuned as GVSAM)","napari","k-Wave acoustic wave simulation","Verasonics Vantage NXT (v2.3.3), 5.2 MHz linear probe, xAM sequence","OpenFlexure open-source 3D-printed microscope","Atomic force microscopy"],"techniques":["Thermal-fluctuation spectrum analysis to extract bending stiffness","Timoshenko shell theory conversion of stiffness to buckling pressure","Potential of mean force / binding free energy calculation","Contact-angle measurement to test a wetting collapse mechanism","Cross-species structural templating justified by sequence identity and repeat periodicity","Tiered interface redesign (native through full redesign)","Negative-control-calibrated metric gating","Multi-state conditional scoring of a conformational switch","Transfer learning / fine-tuning a segmentation foundation model on 108 images","Position-and-size matching criterion in place of IoU for small objects","Bootstrapped confidence intervals over images","Robust statistics (median, MAD) against ultrasound speckle","Cross-amplitude modulation nonlinear ultrasound imaging with hydrophone calibration","Four-parameter regression for P50 collapse pressure","Acoustophoretic cell sorting and standing-wave spatial patterning"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Heidelberg's education programme, Fail Forward, takes error culture in science as its subject rather than synthetic biology content, which makes it unusually distinctive. The centrepiece is the Error Bar, billed as 'a night of scientific f*ck ups': around 80 students and researchers in the university's student bar in July, with successful professionals telling their own stories of things going wrong and what they learned. It was deliberately not a lecture — a short presentation introduced structural problems including publication bias against negative results and mental health stressors in academia, then interactive activities invited participants to share their own experiences, and recreation therapist Brady Hughes ran a workshop on distinguishing a fiasco from a crisis, reappraising situations and using grounding exercises, ending with the group shredding their recent failures together. A panel discussion with around 120 participants extended the theme from personal to institutional failure in the healthcare system, bringing researchers, healthcare professionals, patient advocates and the public into one room, with one panellist making the publication-bias argument in concrete terms: unpublished negative studies mean the same approach gets tried repeatedly, wasting resources and re-exposing participants to risk. All of it was distilled into the Fail Forward Guide, which includes background on failure in academia, expert interviews, and a tutorial for hosting your own Error Bar anywhere in the world — and the team has already committed to making the Error Bar recurring, with an open-mic session planned and PIs who missed it asking to join the next one. Alongside this they ran a Science Cinema with the local student cinema Unikino Heidelberg (over 70 participants for GATTACA, followed by a presentation and open discussion on how realistic the scenario is and which elements of that society we can already find in our own), a documentary screening with gynaecologist Dr Kathrin Haßdenteufel providing medical context and a Q&A (40 participants, every seat taken), a Science Pub Quiz, a Science Slam, school workshops, a laboratory summer school, a lecture series, and a full-day alumni event at the Marsilius-Kolleg bringing around 45 current and former iGEM Heidelberg members from every year the university has competed.","ihp":"The clearest example of stakeholder input changing method rather than framing came from Prof Dr Frauke Gräter, interviewed on modelling strategy. The team first built exactly what she proposed — two Martini coarse-grained sheets with gas between them, tested at different pressures — and it taught them something by failing: the flat patch collapsed even without any applied pressure, because it lacks the closed curved geometry that holds a real vesicle open. That killed the approach and, building on her point that relative differences are what matter for ranking and that a small wall section is representative, they switched to measuring bending stiffness on an all-atom repeating patch and converting it through shell theory. Her warning about sampling is why they chose bending rather than stretching as the primary measurement, ran every simulation at least three times and compared several GvpC variants rather than only wild type; her advice on pulling speed went into the steered MD, where they pulled at several speeds and used only runs in which GvpC genuinely came off. They also tried her suggested coarse-grained model as a fast pre-screen, found it could not distinguish the variants, and state that every reported result therefore comes from all-atom simulation. Elsewhere: Prof Pfeifer warned that GvpA is highly conserved and vulnerable to mutation, redirecting the whole engineering effort onto GvpC as the modular target, and explained which accessory genes the expression constructs needed. Dr Huber's feedback on how long even one sub-idea takes changed how many aspects they attempted and forced explicit prioritisation. A stakeholder conversation led them to replace the D. flos-aquae / P. megaterium expression mix with a Serratia-based cassette in E. coli, which is what finally gave them expression and purification and set the host for everything downstream. Protein design expert Paul Kittner diagnosed a real failure mode: hallucination tools like BindCraft are biased toward hydrophobic interfaces because their structure predictors are trained on natural ones, and GvpA's outward-facing surface is hydrophilic — he advised adding a custom penalty term to the generation loss, which worked and let them target the hydrophilic face, and also pointed them to diffusion models as less biased, which is why RFdiffusion3 became the backbone generator. Dr Michael Jendrusch told them bluntly that no existing pipeline suited their biosensing goal and sketched the binder-plus-latch architecture they built, including the multi-state scoring step and the advice to screen with pooled assays and Twist Oligo Pools rather than testing candidates individually. Prof Dr Karl Rohr's advice is why GVSAM fine-tunes CellSAM rather than training from scratch. Mentor Molina Ramirez moved from interview subject to collaborator, supervising imaging, adapting the xAM script to their specific transducer, and advising them to use beamformed rather than pixel data and median rather than mean normalisation to suppress outliers. Prof Dr Georg Schmitz gave them a falsifiable clinical test — look first with the transducer you already have at frequencies close to clinical scanners, and only go higher if nothing appears — which they followed and passed at 5-7 MHz. Dr Lakshmanan's practical warnings (closing a low-quality tube cap can generate enough pressure to collapse vesicles) shaped handling, and her advice to modify GvpC termini shaped the latch design.","realWorld":"The target application is monitoring engineered cell therapies, chosen because the alternatives are genuinely inadequate: PET tracking needs suitable radioactive tracers and costly specialised equipment, with each scan requiring tracer administration so measurements are days apart rather than continuous; bioluminescence imaging needs no such infrastructure but cannot penetrate patient tissue; and biopsy is invasive, reports only the sampled site, and cannot show how therapeutic cells redistribute. Ultrasound is comparatively inexpensive, widely available and needs no ionising radiation or tracer, which is the whole argument for a genetically encoded acoustic reporter. Crucially they tested the frequency question rather than assuming it: all measurements were made at 5-7 MHz, a range clinical scanners use, so purified vesicles, GV-expressing bacteria and mammalian cells were all detected as clear scattering signals against a degassed negative control without resorting to high frequencies that would limit penetration depth — while stating openly that whether a therapeutic cell can accumulate enough vesicles for a detectable in vivo signal remains to be established. The team has patent work on a modular, reversible acoustic label: because interchangeable binding proteins attach vesicles to a chosen target, the target's acoustic properties change without genetically modifying it, which matters for cell-therapy manufacturing where the product must not be altered. Named next steps are specific — test one ATLAS binder against a marker with existing clinical use such as HER2 or PD-L1, and test two gas vesicle variants with distinct collapse pressures in a tissue-mimicking phantom to establish whether both signals can be read in one measurement, with a published liver phantom protocol cited as a starting point — plus quantifying sorting efficiency and validating cell viability after label removal. A collaboration with PHOENICS is sketched on shared mechanism: the PHOENICS cell computes through phosphorylation and the GvpC sensor turns phosphorylation into ultrasound, so together they would make an engineered cell's internal decision readable from outside the body.","partners":["Prof Dr Frauke Gräter (molecular dynamics and modelling strategy)","Prof Felicitas Pfeifer (gas vesicle biology)","Dr Huber (gas vesicle structure)","Dr Anupama Lakshmanan (gas vesicle engineering)","Paul Kittner (protein design, hallucination bias)","Dr Michael Jendrusch (protein design pipelines)","Prof Dr Karl Rohr (biomedical image analysis)","Prof Dr Georg Schmitz, Chair of Medical Engineering, Ruhr University Bochum","Molina Ramirez (ultrasound imaging mentor and collaborator)","Brady Hughes, recreation therapist (Error Bar workshop)","Dr med Kathrin Haßdenteufel (documentary screening)","iGEM PHOENICS (proposed integration)","Unikino Heidelberg and ZEP Heidelberg","iGEM Heidelberg alumni network (2008-2026)","The OpenFlexure Project (open-source microscopy)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6249/wiki/img/model/gvpc-evolution-pipeline.svg","caption":"The complete in silico GvpC redesign pipeline: the template is split into interface and structural residues, interface redesign is deep-learning-guided with LigandMPNN while structural residues are chosen rationally, candidates are predicted and filtered by structure predictors and physical constraints, and validated variants feed back to retune the gating strategy.","credit":"Team Heidelberg, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6249/wiki/img/gvpa-binders/metrics-1-01788a672d.svg","caption":"Interface PAE distribution across all 1,429 de novo GvpA binder designs (orange) against the 13 accepted ones (blue). Nearly half of all designs failed on this metric alone, which is why the pipeline reports each gate separately rather than a single composite score.","credit":"Team Heidelberg, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6249/wiki/img/model/gvsam-base-vs-finetuned-a.webp","caption":"Recall, precision and F1 with bootstrapped 95% confidence intervals for GVSAM against the base CellSAM model on 10 held-out microscopy images containing 114 annotated gas vesicle clusters — F1 rose from 0.2% to 42% after fine-tuning on just 108 annotated images, with false positives down 97%.","credit":"Team Heidelberg, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6249/wiki/img/education/error-bar-1.webp","caption":"The Error Bar, 'a night of scientific f*ck ups': around 80 students and researchers in the university student bar hearing established scientists talk about their own failures, with interactive activities and a workshop on distinguishing a fiasco from a crisis.","credit":"Team Heidelberg, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"The transferable dry-lab habit is calibrating your filters against negative controls before you trust them: Heidelberg ran nine deliberately bad sequences plus the real binder through the identical pipeline, found that only AlphaFold2 interface PAE separated them, said so, and refused to select on the metrics that failed the test — a discipline that costs nothing but compute and turns a pile of prediction scores into an actual decision rule. On the human side, copy Fail Forward: an evening where senior scientists tell students about their own failures, plus a written guide that includes instructions for running the event yourself. For a high-school-plus research centre whose students will mostly meet failure before success, an institutional format that makes failure discussable is worth more than another outreach workshop — and the guide means you can run it next term without inventing it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/application","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/part-collection","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/heidelberg/public-outreach"],"rank":129},{"team":"UAlberta","id":"6254","section":"Undergrad","country":"Canada","village":"Therapeutics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/","projectTitle":"gleyeco","tagline":"A modular fusion protein that uses the eye's own sugar coating as an anchor, delivered in a hydrogel, so dry eye medicine stays put.","summary":"Dry eye disease affects an estimated 34.6% of people globally and 21.3% of Canadian adults, and the central problem is not the drug but the delivery: blinking and tear drainage clear a topical therapeutic within minutes. gleyeco treats the ocular glycocalyx as a docking site rather than a barrier, fusing a Galectin-3 carbohydrate-recognition domain (binding Core 1 O-glycans on mucins), a tandem pair of the 12-residue hyaluronic-acid-binding peptide HABpep, and IL-1RA (anakinra) as the therapeutic payload, delivered from a crosslinked PEG/hyaluronic acid hydrogel as a drop or a contact lens. The team built a five-stage dry lab pipeline with its own acronyms - GLARE, InSiGHT, VOIR, I-SPY, HOPE - that chooses the target, engineers the binder, sets the valency, predicts the yield and tunes the gel, and released GLARE as an installable Python package. It won Best Entrepreneurship on an unusually rigorous commercial case (38-patient survey, two clinician letters of support, a letter of intent from a clinic, competitor matrix against Restasis/Cequa/Xiidra, PESTEL, 10-year plan) and was nominated for the Inclusivity Award for an open-access Inclusivity in Research course, an Adaptive Technology Kit and a rural teachers' programme.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Inclusivity Award — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Entrepreneurship — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Therapeutics Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Five named operations, each handing its result to the next. GLARE (Glycan-Lectin Analysis for Recognition and Evaluation) mines global glycan databases of 50,000+ catalogued sequences, filters to the 134 unique structures documented in ocular tissue (cornea, tear film, lacrimal gland), confirms O-glycans dominate, then screens Galectin-3's off-target risk across other organs and three cancers under identical statistics with multiple-hypothesis-testing correction, and finally re-runs the analysis under a dry-eye disease signature, finding the disease phenotype exposes over 2.5x more accessible binding sites. InSiGHT (In Silico Glycan Honed Targeting) engineers Galectin-3 to favour Core 1 over LacNAc: mutation sites were chosen from the literature (H46, N48, R50, N62, W69, E72 hydrogen-bonding residues plus nearby polar R32, E53, R74), a Clustal Omega multiple sequence alignment of all available mammalian galectins from PDB and NCBI (which excluded the perfectly conserved W69 and flagged the poorly conserved E53), and a PyMOL structural alignment of 12 galectin CRDs against Galectin-3 (PDB 3ZSJ, 0.86 Angstrom) that identified the E53-V58 loop and the I33/N31 positions as differentiators; a Python script generates mutant combinations, FoldX builds the structures, HADDOCK and Schrodinger dock Core 1 and LacNAc in parallel after AutoDock Vina proved unable to handle large flexible glycans, and GROMACS scores binding strength - batched across RunPod for parallel FoldX jobs. Ten mutants (Alpha through Kappa) went to the bench. VOIR (Valency Optimization and Integrated Retention) uses the valentBind model to compute bivalent avidity as a function of linker length and glycan target density, concluding that a second binding domain gives the largest gain, a third a moderate one, and a fourth nothing - and recommending (GGGGS)6-16 linkers. I-SPY (I Shall Produce Yield) curates the BL21(DE3)-based iBB1018 genome-scale reconstruction in COBRApy for the SHuffle T7 Express chassis, adds a demand reaction for the heterologous fusion protein, and runs flux balance analysis to locate metabolic bottlenecks. HOPE (Hydrogel Optimization for Pharmacokinetic Evaluation) models mesh size with the Peppas-Merrill and then the Canal-Peppas form of Flory-Rehner, using interpolated PEGDA swelling data and MeHA degree of methacrylation, and derives protein diffusivity from it.","dataviz":"The modelling page is organised as a relay: five labelled operations (Choose, Improve, Multiply, Produce, Deliver) in a flow a reader clicks into, each with its own write-up and its own Impressiveness/Design/Validation/Exemplarity framing. The software page embeds a live in-browser CLI simulator for GLARE with clickable example commands (--cell eye, --binding-galectins eye, --target-compare eye core1 stn, --disease-compare dry_eye) so a judge can run the pipeline's logic without installing Python. For audiences without a biology background the team built the gleyecobuddies: Lecto (Galectin-3, with the binding site built into the character design), Ana (IL-1RA, a red plus sign) and Happy (HABpep, a blue rectangle standing for a wall that holds the drug in place), deliberately layered on top of the standard Symbol Nomenclature for Glycans rather than replacing it, and justified as serving visual learners, neurodivergent people and children. All InSiGHT results are reported with the predictors' disagreements visible rather than averaged.","datascience":"The team repeatedly uses modelling to avoid spending bench time, and reports the limits of each prediction. GLARE's stated limitations are unusually frank: the database records which structures have been documented in a tissue, not how much is displayed, so enrichment results describe structural patterns and must not be read as surface density; the diseased-versus-healthy split is built from literature signatures rather than a labelled patient dataset, and the pipeline is written so a real patient-derived list can be dropped in unchanged; binding strengths come from published microarray data, not from their own fusion protein; the tissue panel is small, and the cervix moved along the same mucin O-glycan axis as the eye so other mucosal surfaces would need direct testing. In InSiGHT they caught their own pipeline's instability - mutating key polar residues to alanine did not abolish predicted binding as it should, and one HADDOCK run gave mutant Gamma -6.495 kcal/mol against Core 1 while a separate run of the same mutant gave -3.84, which they report as evidence the pipeline needs repeated runs. For VOIR they state the point is the optimal linker length range, not the headline avidity ratio, because the values are literature-derived estimates not validated in vitro. For HOPE the model behaved as theory predicts (mesh falling from 16 nm at 5% w/v PEGDA to about 10 nm at 25%, predicted protein diffusivity from about 60 to 37 micrometres squared per second against 97 in free water) and they say plainly they could not build the equivalent alginate model, and that an OpenFOAM CFD attempt failed because the corneal absorption boundary condition had to be written in C++ and did not run reliably, so they fell back to a 1D Python model for sensitivity analysis.","software":"GLARE, released as an importable Python package (v0.2.0, Python 3.10+) at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/ualberta/software-glare, runnable from the command line with a clear module structure separating tissue validation, binding analysis, cross-tissue comparison, disease signatures and docking-structure generation behind a single pipeline interface, with generalised inputs so another team can point it at a different tissue, lectin or glycan. A browser-based CLI simulator on the wiki lets anyone try it without installing. The Adaptive Technology Kit is an open-source hardware release: 3D-printable PCR strip-tube and microfuge-tube openers (in multiple design variants, because user testing showed different researchers preferred different models), an agarose gel loader, an SDS gel loader, a braille stamper, a keyboard tool, a pencil holder, a non-slip tray, a microscope phone holder, and a tactile map of the iGEM lab with published blueprints. A Synthetic Biology Lab Manual and the full Rural Teachers Synbiosium materials are published as free PDFs, as is the DNA-as-Code Kit.","stack":["Python 3.10+","COBRApy","iBB1018 genome-scale reconstruction","BiGG Models","Glycowork","GlyTouCan / GlyGen","Glycam-Web","FoldX","HADDOCK","Schrodinger","GROMACS","AutoDock Vina (rejected)","AlphaFold 2","PyMOL","Clustal Omega (EMBL-EBI)","RunPod (parallel compute)","valentBind","OpenFOAM (attempted)","Next.js / React wiki"],"techniques":["Database mining with multiple-hypothesis-testing correction","Disease-signature enrichment analysis","Cross-tissue off-target screening","Multiple sequence alignment for conservation-guided site selection","Structural alignment of homologue binding pockets","Combinatorial in silico saturation mutagenesis","Protein-glycan docking","MD-based binding strength estimation","Batched GPU/cloud job parallelisation","Flux balance analysis","Genome-scale model curation for a non-reference strain","Bivalent avidity / valency modelling","Flory-Rehner (Peppas-Merrill and Canal-Peppas) mesh-size calculation","Diffusivity estimation from mesh size","1D transport modelling for sensitivity analysis","Isothermal titration calorimetry (experimental Kd)"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Three distinct, durable programmes rather than one-off booths. (1) The Inclusivity in Research Course, an open-access training run through Google Classroom, built from a nationwide survey and expert consultations, with modules on a Native Land activity, the team's own 'Just Ask' framework and neurodivergent inclusion, creating open spaces, blind and visually impaired inclusion, Deaf and hard-of-hearing inclusion, racialized people, women and 2SLGBTQIA+ and gender minorities, navigating reporting processes, and an accessibility resource bank - each with downloadable slides, infographics and interactive summaries. It was piloted with their own team, neighbouring labs and Canadian iGEM teams, and the three pieces of pilot feedback (offer multiple learning formats, add scenario-based practice, cover more groups) were each implemented as narrated audio and slideshows, self-reflection prompts and real lab case studies, and two new modules. (2) The Rural Teachers Synbiosium, a half-day online professional development event for rural Alberta teachers, mapped explicitly onto the Alberta middle and high school curriculum, covering case studies and how to secure funding, with three guest speakers including Amino Labs CEO Julie Legault, 9 free hands-on kits distributed (teachers chose between a one-day yeast-fermentation race car and a multi-day pigment-engineering agar art project) and all materials shipped in advance to lower the burden. (3) Public outreach at K-Days, an Edmonton summer festival, where visitors attempted lab tasks while simulating different visual impairments, plus a DNA-as-code bracelet activity, and tabling at Alberta's Women in STEM week after being invited by the Alberta Women in Science Network. The team also presented at the Canadian Glycomics Symposium, CNAS Research Day, Darwin Day, Cell Biology Research Day, the RE Peter Conference and the Canadian iGEM GCI Conference, and ran a hands-on workshop at Canmore Collegiate High School - which is what revealed the rural access problem and started the Synbiosium.","ihp":"Every stakeholder entry is logged as a Key Insight paired with an Action Taken, and the actions are real design reversals. Dr Warren Wakarchuk (Scientific Director of GlycoNet) warned that small-molecule binding pockets hold cargo too tightly to release on the eye and that yeast adds problematic sugar tags, and noted eukaryotic tissue autofluoresces green under UV - so the team abandoned the binding-pocket release concept, chose E. coli over yeast, switched the reporter from GFP to mScarlet, and retargeted abundant normal ocular glycans instead of rare disease-specific ones. Dr Lisa Willis (glyco-immunology) raised immune safety and challenged them to test whether the Galectin-3 and HA domains cooperate or compete for binding - so instead of assuming more binding is better, they redesigned the experimental plan to measure each domain separately by isothermal titration calorimetry. Dr Carr advised narrowing from retinal delivery to corneal inflammation in dry eye with IL-1RA cargo and offered Xenopus eye tissue for ex vivo testing. An advisor recommended the SHuffle T7 strain and told them their fusion protein was not complex enough to justify a chaperone plasmid, so they dropped it. Dr Amandeep helped unify the five separate models into one pipeline and suggested using eye images as GLARE input to make it diagnostic - the team pursued it, found it infeasible, said so, and redirected to modelling; he also pointed them to RunPod, which is why FoldX now runs in parallel. Jezlyn warned that chemical and UV crosslinking would denature mScarlet, so they moved to physical ionic crosslinking with calcium chloride, wrote autoclaving protocols for sterility, and were pointed at the Peppas-Merrill and Flory-Rehner equations that became HOPE. Dr Cortez told them not to add a second nickel column but to optimise their existing HisTrap/FPLC wash stringency, which they did and documented on Coomassie gels. Optometrist Dr Abdo Kattan explained that his multi-product dry eye package (omega 3s, drops, lid wipes, goggles) has very low compliance because it is time-consuming, expensive and impossible to attribute benefit to - which refocused the whole project on one affordable single-product treatment. Separately, CD44 was dropped entirely as the HA-retention domain in favour of tandem HABpep after it kept appearing in the insoluble fraction and the literature revealed isoforms linked to cancer therapy resistance. On the inclusivity side, Nicholas Piroddi (a medical researcher with autism) was consulted, then consulted a second time to review how the course represented neurodiversity, which added the institutional reporting-pathways module; ASVI's input became the blind and visually impaired module; and user testing of the 3D-printed tube openers at the WinSTEM expo is why the kit ships multiple variants of the same tool.","realWorld":"The strongest undergraduate commercial case of the five. Evidence of demand is tabulated by customer role with the strength of evidence named: letters of support from Dr Abdo Kattan and Dr Matthew Benson (and a signed letter of intent from Kattan's clinic to work with them), 86% of 38 surveyed dry eye patients interested in a synthetic biology treatment, and an application in interview stage with the Nucleate Canada accelerator. The patient survey plus three clinician interviews produced five explicit unmet needs - fewer doses, longer retention, no burning or stinging, targeted anti-inflammatory action, contact lens compatibility - which the product is then measured against. A competitor matrix compares gleyeco line by line with Restasis (AbbVie), Cequa (Sun Pharma) and Xiidra (Bausch + Lomb) on mechanism, dosing, time to effect, clinical evidence, tolerability, lens compatibility, 30-day cost and Canadian availability, citing prescribing information and Drugs.com prices as of 15 October 2026, and crucially labels gleyeco's own entries as design goals that preclinical and clinical data must confirm. The gap identified is precise: every anti-inflammatory option is dosed at least twice daily and none occupies targeted once-daily treatment. TAM/SAM/SOM, regional opportunity, bottom-up dynamics, current distribution network, a Size-Potential-Access analysis, a beachhead market defined by role (users, decision makers, payers, pharmaceutical partners), a 10-year implementation plan and an exit strategy all follow. The PESTEL analysis is specific to Canadian biotech: Health Canada's Reliance Order syncing review with the FDA if filed within 120 days, Alberta Innovates and FAST cutting trial start-up from 90+ to 45 days, PMPRB price controls pushing them toward private payers first, over 75% of Canadian life-science investment coming from abroad so Phase 2 needs a partner, the USD/CAD rate at 1.4, and the fact that the RE Peter Conference and Jamboree disclosures start the novelty clock (12 months in Canada, absolute novelty required in Europe). Clinical milestones were set directly from a clinician's roadmap, and lab eye models have been offered by two separate researchers for the move from proof of concept to preclinical.","partners":["GlycoNet (Canadian Glycomics Network)","Nucleate Canada","Amino Labs","Alberta Women in Science Network (AWSN)","Alberta Sports and Recreation Association for the Blind / ASVI","MolScreen","Genome Alberta","University of Alberta Student Innovation Centre","Canmore Collegiate High School","Dr Abdo Kattan's optometry clinic (letter of intent)","Alberta Innovates / FAST","Canadian iGEM GCI Conference","Canadian Glycomics Symposium","rural Alberta school teachers (Synbiosium cohort)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6254/wiki/model-page/glare-fig1.avif","caption":"GLARE, the glycan-lectin screening pipeline: 50,000+ catalogued glycan sequences filtered to the 134 documented in ocular tissue, then screened for Galectin-3 off-target risk across other organs and under a dry-eye disease signature.","credit":"Team UAlberta, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6254/wiki/model-page/insight-folder/insight-figure2.avif","caption":"Predicted binding strength of Galectin-3 mutants against the Core 1 target, scored by HADDOCK, Schrodinger and GROMACS, with the predictors' disagreements left visible rather than averaged.","credit":"Team UAlberta, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6254/wiki/model-page/hope-folder/hydrogel-modelling-figure-3.avif","caption":"HOPE hydrogel modelling: mesh size falls from 16 nm at 5% w/v PEGDA to about 10 nm at 25%, and predicted protein diffusivity falls from roughly 60 to 37 micrometres squared per second against 97 in free water.","credit":"Team UAlberta, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6254/wiki/inclusivity-page/map-in-use.avif","caption":"The tactile map of the iGEM lab in use - part of the open-source Adaptive Technology Kit, published with blueprints alongside 3D-printable tube openers, gel loaders and a braille stamper.","credit":"Team UAlberta, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6254/wiki/model-page/voir-folder/fig1-voir.avif","caption":"VOIR valency modelling: bivalent avidity as a function of linker length from the valentBind model, which showed a second binding domain gives the largest gain, a third a moderate one, and a fourth none.","credit":"Team UAlberta, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy UAlberta's two-column human practices habit - every conversation written as one Key Insight and one Action Taken, where the action is a concrete reversal (GFP to mScarlet, yeast to E. coli, CD44 dropped for HABpep, chemical crosslinking dropped for calcium) - because it forces a team to admit when advice changed nothing. The other directly adaptable asset is their rural pivot: they went in planning to help schools found competition teams, learned from the teachers that capacity was the real constraint, and instead built a curriculum-aligned half-day online session with pre-shipped kits and a free lab manual, which is exactly the model a Taiwanese research centre could run for under-resourced schools.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/overview","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/proof-of-concept","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/hydrogels","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/science-communication","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/ualberta/safety-and-security","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/ualberta/software-glare"],"rank":110},{"team":"UZurich","id":"6269","section":"Overgrad","country":"Switzerland","village":"Conservation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/","projectTitle":"SeisMIC","tagline":"Stabilising Alpine slopes from within: engineered bacteria precipitate calcite between soil grains, with four linked models deciding where it is worth doing.","summary":"SeisMIC adapts microbially induced calcite precipitation (MICP) to Alpine slope stabilisation, where heavy rainfall is now 26% more frequent and 12% more intense and concrete anchoring is often out of proportion to the risk. Sporosarcina pasteurii hydrolyses urea so calcium carbonate crystallises around soil particles, filling pores and binding loose grains, and the team worked on the four limits that stop MICP transferring to the Alps: uneven cementation, scaling across large slopes, cold sensitivity and ammonium production. The distinctive contribution is the dry lab, which is a geospatial and economic decision system rather than a protein pipeline: an infinite-slope Factor of Safety model run across all of Switzerland on daily historical rainfall, a PHREEQC reactive transport model of calcite formation down a soil column, a QGIS MICP feasibility map, and an economic model using Flow-Py runout simulation to put a Swiss franc figure on damage avoided. The models are linked to each other and were reshaped by named expert feedback, which is what earned the Best Model nomination.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Model — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Conservation Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Four models, explicitly chained so each one's output is the next one's input. (1) Slope Stability: an infinite-slope model computing the Factor of Safety from effective cohesion, effective friction angle, slope angle, soil unit weight and the pore-pressure ratio, split into a temporal model that drives soil saturation and pore-water pressure from daily historical rainfall through a bucket hydrology scheme and is validated against recorded landslide events, and a spatial model that runs the same calculation Switzerland-wide to find unstable pixels and, crucially, to output the additional effective cohesion MICP would have to supply - a direct, quantitative target the wet lab can be measured against. Root cohesion is modelled as a parallel natural source, with scenarios for bare soil, roots, MICP and roots plus MICP; the modelled unstable share falls from 82.8% to 28.9% under combined roots and MICP, stabilising 65.04% of initially unstable pixels. (2) Reactive Transport: an adaptation of the Razbani et al. model in PHREEQC, simulating a 52 cm sand column as ten stacked cells with bacteria pre-attached to grains and a urea/CaCl2 cementation solution pumped from below over ~52 hours, using Michaelis-Menten ureolysis kinetics (Rmax, Km, initial reactive surface area, biomass decay time td) and calcite precipitation rate constants k1 = 1.55e-6 and k2 = 0.501 mol m-2 s-1, reproducing both continuous-flow and stop-flow experiments; run with the wet lab's own recipe (1.015 M urea, 2 M CaCl2) it showed more than 2 M CaCl2 brings no benefit, and it predicts 77-79% of lab calcite still forms at 4 C, identifying acidic pH rather than temperature as the bigger obstacle. (3) MICP Feasibility: a binary suitability classification in QGIS combining public Swiss soil and terrain layers on stone content, water storage capacity, permeability and slope, giving 67.06% of Switzerland as technically feasible, then overlaid on landslide hazard and on the stability output to produce a MICP Injection Map - 67.32% of modelled unstable pixels fall inside feasible areas. (4) Economic: unstable cells (FoS <= 1) become release areas fed into the open-source Flow-Py gravitational mass-flow model, producing a runout envelope of 425.2 km2 from 568,627 release cells in the mpp = 1.0 scenario (62.0% of canton Glarus, 348.7-425.2 km2 across tested hydrological conditions); historical landslide frequencies and damage costs then give expected annual damage per asset class (roads, buildings, railways, forests, agricultural land), and tracing the highest-damage locations back to their contributing release areas says where treatment would pay. Combined roots and MICP lower estimated annual damage from CHF 583,000 to CHF 351,000, a 39.8% reduction. A directed evolution strand runs alongside, aimed at a non-GMO second strain.","dataviz":"The modelling work is presented as a prototype decision tool, not a figure dump. An Integrated Dashboard connects slope stability, MICP feasibility, rainfall and potential damage into a single interactive surface for deployment planning, and the team is unusually honest about its state: it currently covers slope stability and selected treatment scenarios and does not yet integrate all four models, so it is labelled a prototype of the intended end-user tool. The model landing page leads with four headline numbers (-53.9% unstable area, 67.06% of Switzerland feasible, 67.32% overlap, -39.8% annual damage, 77-79% calcite at 4 C) each linked to the model that produced it, with arrow navigation between models. Supporting visuals are decision-oriented rather than decorative: unstable share and p95 pore-pressure by canton (which ties instability to the cantonal offices that actually do prevention planning), seasonal cohesion requirements, a required-cohesion histogram, sensitivity analysis, an agreement map against the SilvaProtect reference dataset, and per-asset risk maps for roads, railways, buildings, forest and agricultural land. Derivations are offered as downloadable PDFs (infinite-slope FoS, critical saturation, bucket model) so the maths can be checked independently. An Awards page states plainly which awards the team is claiming and summarises the evidence for each.","datascience":"Their strongest data-science moment is a debugging story they chose to publish. The adapted PHREEQC model initially would not run at all, failing with 'Model failed to converge for initial solution 0'; raising iteration counts, changing tolerances and step size and improving initial guesses did nothing, and removing the calcite-equilibrium constraint made it converge to a physically absurd pH of 1.5. They computed the charge balance at fixed pH from 1 to 12, found it never reached zero, inspected the species and discovered ammonium was almost entirely in its neutral form (5.67e-2 M Amm against 8e-11 M AmmH+) because the reaction AmmH+ = Amm + H+ in the phreeqc.dat database carried no equilibrium constant, so PHREEQC set it to 0 instead of about -9.25; they fixed it with the parameters of the database's own NH4+ = NH3 + H+ reaction. The stated lesson - always check the values a model produces, not just the absence of errors - is the reusable part. The rest of the pipeline is integration work: joining national rainfall records, landslide inventories, digital elevation models, land cover and soil property rasters into one framework, validating predicted instability against recorded events and against SilvaProtect, running sensitivity analyses, and carrying each model's limitations on its own page.","software":"Code is published: the rainfall-triggered slope instability and MICP modelling at https://github.com/Alizzie/RainfallTriggeredSlopeInstabilityMICP, with the team repository at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/uzurich. They did not build a standalone released application; the deliverables are the four validated models, the nationwide MICP Feasibility Map and MICP Injection Map (which they note did not previously exist for Switzerland in any public form), and the Integrated Dashboard prototype on the wiki. They also published a measurement framework for MICP experiments - biological screening, calcium measurement, powder recovery and soil analysis as several independent readouts rather than one, with assay limitations and validation checks documented so future teams can interpret MICP experiments more reliably.","stack":["PHREEQC (+ patched phreeqc.dat)","QGIS","Flow-Py","Python","R","Swiss digital elevation and soil/terrain rasters","MeteoSwiss rainfall records and climate scenarios","Swiss landslide inventories","SilvaProtect (validation reference)","GitHub / iGEM GitLab","interactive web dashboard"],"techniques":["infinite-slope Mohr-Coulomb Factor of Safety","bucket hydrology model for antecedent soil moisture","pore-pressure ratio scenario sweeps","back-solving for required additional cohesion as a wet-lab target","root cohesion versus engineered cohesion scenario comparison","Michaelis-Menten ureolysis kinetics in a 1D reactive transport column","charge-balance diagnosis of a thermodynamic database error","sensitivity analysis to identify controlling parameters","binary multi-criteria raster suitability classification","map overlay to find instability-feasibility intersections","gravitational mass-flow runout simulation","expected annual damage (EAD) estimation per asset class","tracing damage back to contributing release areas","validation against an independent national hazard dataset"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was run as a two-way exchange explicitly tied to the values the team set in its Human Practices work - transparency, honesty, communication and collaboration - and they state that those became practical criteria for how they communicated rather than abstract principles. Social media carried three recurring formats: 'What even is...?' deep dives into a single concept, 'Word of the Week' introducing one piece of terminology at a time without burying readers in project detail, and 'Myth vs. Fact' separating common assumptions about bacteria, biotechnology and climate change from what the evidence supports. In-person work included an Activity Fair stand and workshops, plus ZurichLernt, and across every activity they tried to distinguish three things for their audience: what is already known, what their own project demonstrated, and what they could not investigate within the time and resources available. They brought in a strategic communication professional to test their assumptions about how to build a campaign, since nobody on the team had communications training.","ihp":"The interview process itself is the method: prepare (write down why this person, what you hope to learn, which assumptions you want tested, and how their input could change SeisMIC), interview with enough flexibility to follow unexpected leads, then reflect back in an interview log on what actually changed, what challenged an assumption, what new concern it raised and who to speak to next. Stakeholders were mapped on a power-interest matrix used to decide how to approach each one rather than to rank importance, and ELSI was treated as a connected framework rather than three boxes. Concrete changes: a researcher told them to start from the environment rather than the organism rather than defaulting to E. coli because it is well characterised. A pore-scale fluid dynamics researcher listed the parameters no MICP model should omit (temperature, pH, oxygen, ion and calcite concentrations, urea hydrolysis, water saturation) and recommended sensitivity analysis to find which dominate - so they dropped a machine learning idea rather than spending the summer discovering it could not be trained, moved the reactive transport work onto established software instead of a custom implementation, and added the sensitivity analysis. Dr. Joaquin Jimenez-Martinez's feedback changed how they treat temperature, bacterial survival and pH. Stakeholders and their PIs repeatedly asking how much vegetation already contributes is why root cohesion was added to the stability model. A microbiologist corrected their premise directly - ammonia release makes soil more basic, not acidic - sending them back to locate the real problem, and suggested reading pH change around colonies with plate indicators; another suggested nitrification inhibitors to stop nitrate forming. An expert pointed out that innovative biological methods usually fail on political opposition rather than feasibility, technology or financing, and suggested they also consider protected natural areas. They dropped CLIMADA after Prof. David Bresch's guidance and reframed the economic benefit as fewer slope failures. Residents below the slopes said what they value most is the surrounding nature, which made the ecological cost of the treatment something to investigate rather than assume; their hesitation about engineered bacteria eased once biomineralisation was explained as a natural process, which told the team their job is explaining the mechanism rather than defending the idea.","realWorld":"The business case is built on a gap they verified rather than assumed: many at-risk Swiss slopes get no protection because concrete and anchors are disproportionate to the risk, and two cantons, Lucerne and Glarus, independently described exactly such slopes. Interviews with Marco Peyer (Risktec) and Prof. David Bresch (ETH Zurich) moved them off insurers and onto the real decision chain - the canton decides and pays, an engineering office proposes the method, the federal government co-finances - so the customer is cantonal natural-hazard offices and their consulting engineers. The offer is deliberately a bundle: a site-specific treatment delivered together with the modelling that shows how much damage it avoids, because as Ulrich Kaiser pointed out, prevention has a visibility problem - a stabilised slope produces no event, no headline and no credit, while the cost is immediate and concrete, so proving avoided damage has to be part of the product. Interest is reported honestly as interest, not sales: one canton offered to connect them with a suitable slope and another asked to follow the damage model, with the team stating plainly that neither is a customer yet. The roadmap is about five to eight years from lab to a first slope, with Swiss approval staged as lab, then field trial, then market. Risks are named with mitigations: urea run-off requires rinsing (one company converts the run-off to fertiliser) and they are investigating a lower-urea route while stating nothing is settled at field scale; containment is handled by a kill switch plus a second strain adapted by directed evolution without genetic engineering.","partners":["Prof. David Bresch, ETH Zurich (climate risk / economic framing)","Dr. Joaquin Jimenez-Martinez (pore-scale fluid dynamics in MICP)","Marco Peyer, Risktec (natural hazard consulting)","Ulrich Kaiser (economics of prevention)","Canton of Lucerne (natural hazard office)","Canton of Glarus (natural hazard office)","University of Zurich (UZH MNF, ULF, Science Alumni)","Galbiati Stiftung","Alpine residents and farming communities below at-risk slopes"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6269/wiki/stability-model/pipeline-overview.avif","caption":"The slope stability modelling pipeline, which joins rainfall records, landslide inventories, terrain and soil data into an infinite-slope Factor of Safety calculation and outputs the extra cohesion MICP would need to supply.","credit":"Team UZurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6269/wiki/stability-model/stability-interactive.avif","caption":"The interactive slope stability view behind the Integrated Dashboard prototype, which the team presents as a planning tool for deciding where MICP treatment is worth investigating rather than as a finished product.","credit":"Team UZurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6269/wiki/economic-model/risk-map-rounded.avif","caption":"Economic risk map for canton Glarus: Flow-Py runout from unstable release cells combined with historical landslide frequency and damage costs to give expected annual damage by location.","credit":"Team UZurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6269/wiki/human-practices/education/activity-fair/af1.avif","caption":"The team at their Activity Fair stand, part of an education programme run as two-way exchange and explicitly tied to the transparency and honesty values set in their Human Practices work.","credit":"Team UZurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Steal their interview discipline, which needs no equipment: before every expert conversation write down why this person, what you hope to learn, which assumption you want tested and how their answer could change the project, then log afterwards what actually changed and who to talk to next - that single loop is what let them kill an untrainable machine learning idea in one conversation instead of losing a summer to it. Their published PHREEQC debugging story is the second lesson worth printing on the wall: a model that runs without errors can still be wrong, so check the values, not just the exit code.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/stability-model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/reactive-transport-model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/injection-model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/economic-model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/methods/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/directed-evolution/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/measurement/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/human-practices-overview/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/sustainability/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/contribution/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uzurich/awards/"],"rank":98},{"team":"Stanford University","id":"6278","section":"Undergrad","country":"USA","village":"Therapeutics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/","projectTitle":"KelePatch","tagline":"A logic-gated, cell-state-specific RNA therapy for keloid scars, delivered by a microneedle patch shaped to the wound.","summary":"KelePatch builds an ADAR-based RADAR RNA sensor that only switches on inside keloid myofibroblasts, so a therapeutic payload is produced in diseased cells and not in healthy skin. The team designed a 26-part modular sensor collection across five fibrosis transcripts (ADAM12, POSTN, ASPN, COL11A1, SPP1), validated them in HEK293 cells, combined the two best into a dual-input AND gate for extra selectivity, and tested in healthy dermal fibroblasts and keloid-derived fibroblasts; they also paired a sensor with Cas12a2 to show the output is not limited to fluorescent reporters. Alongside the wet lab they built RADAR-Scout, a published web platform that takes a cell type and a gene and returns a ranked, structure-aware list of RADAR sensor candidates plus a mechanistic simulation of how it will behave. It won the Grand Prize because every layer connects: 960,518 single cells of transcriptomics pick the trigger, ViennaRNA folding energy ranks the sensor, ODE models explain the delay they saw at the bench, and a U-Net++ computer vision pipeline turns a photo of a scar into a 3D-printable patch.","awards":["Grand Prize Winner (Undergrad)","Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Therapeutics Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Four stacked layers. (1) Single-cell transcriptomics at scale: a processing pipeline integrating 26 scRNA-seq cohorts across cardiac, cutaneous, renal, hepatic and pulmonary fibrosis - 960,518 cells and 1,728 donor-population observations from CELLxGENE, CELLxGENE Census and GEO - with donor-level statistics averaged at equal donor weight so large-cell-count donors do not dominate. Rather than ordinary differential expression, they score each candidate across three populations (Pathological, Healthy, Bystander) to ask whether a transcript is abundant in the target cell and absent from neighbours, which is what an intracellular sensor actually requires. (2) RNA structure prediction: 19 predictors computed with ViennaRNA 2.7.2 (RNAfold for MFE and ensembles, RNApfold for per-nucleotide unpaired probability, RNAcofold for the sensor-trigger duplex) spanning sequence composition, secondary structure, accessibility and hybridization. (3) Mechanistic kinetics: five interconnected deterministic mass-action ODE models with a stochastic counterpart, covering plasmid nuclear entry, single-trigger plasmid, single-trigger RNA, AND-gate plasmid and AND-gate RNA delivery, built on Ligon et al.'s lipoplex delivery cascade with occupancy resolved into nine pit states and ten endosome states; 82 parameters are tabulated with provenance, and four are fitted by simulated annealing in Python against Cohen et al.'s qPCR nuclear copy-number data (p_entry = 0.107, K_sat = 232, k_TX = 0.273). A nine-state hybridization/editing scheme tracks the joint status of both AND-gate sites. (4) Device physics: finite element analysis using Fick's law, the Stokes-Einstein relation and Darcy's law for LNP diffusion through keloid extracellular matrix, plus a nociceptor-count model for pain.","dataviz":"RADAR-Scout is the visualization: a deployed web app (https://radar-scout.vercel.app/, with a companion dashboard at https://radar-scout-dashboard.vercel.app/) where the keloid view ranks 600 filtered genes across five cohorts on the left and plots every candidate sensor for the selected gene on the right, so a target and a sensor are chosen in the same screen, with filters for region type, codon variant, duplex length and isoform, and shortlist/CSV export. Changing the selected tissue reruns the isoform analysis live. The wiki itself is a hand-built site with per-page accent colours and mascot illustrations, and the modelling page is written so a reader can follow each decision: predictor correlations are shown three ways (full Spearman matrix at n = 24, per-replicate rho, then all predictors ranked) and the team states plainly that non-significant predictors are 'unresolved at n = 24 rather than confirmed null'. Parameter tables flag which values are fitted, which are placeholders, and which disagreements remain unresolved.","datascience":"The core result is a replacement for a field convention. The informal RADAR design guideline was to pick the candidate with the lowest GC content. Stanford built a 5x5 experimental library of 25 sensors, measured them by flow cytometry in HEK293 across two replicates (r = 0.94), gated the top 5% mCherry-positive cells and took geometric-mean GFP / geometric-mean mCherry as the output metric, then correlated 19 computational predictors against it. Sensor partition-function folding free energy reached Spearman rho = 0.62-0.63, against -0.29 for GC content and -0.31 for longest homopolymer - so folding energy, not composition, became the ranking filter inside RADAR-Scout. They were honest about what failed: linear, Ridge and Elastic Net regression on the same features reached only Q2 = 0.20, which is explicitly what pushed them from statistical fitting to mechanistic ODEs. Cross-organ analysis of the top 300 genes per organ found 857 of 1,136 distinct genes appeared in only one organ and pairwise Jaccard similarity of 0.050-0.145, so they rejected the idea of a universal fibrosis biomarker set. Isoform filtering uses GTEx Oxford Nanopore long-read data (~150,000 transcripts, 92 samples, 15 tissues) to check the trigger sequence is actually present in the isoforms that cell type expresses.","software":"RADAR-Scout, live at https://radar-scout.vercel.app/ with a dashboard at https://radar-scout-dashboard.vercel.app/. It solves a real engineering problem: the Gao lab's published RADAR candidate table has roughly 7 million rows and could not be held in memory, so build_database.py ingests human_RADAR_sensor_candidates.csv.gz into a SQLite database in 100,000-row chunks with an index on the gene column, and the app queries it read-only with 50 candidates per API page. The computer vision pipeline for personalized microneedle patches uses a U-Net++ with a ResNet34 encoder (from segmentation_models.pytorch) to segment keloid wound photographs, then drives the Autodesk Fusion 360 API to emit a CAD/STL file for 3D printing. Separately, SynBase (https://synbase.web.app/home.html) is a released, free, account-based online learning platform. Parts: 26 modular RADAR parts including 15 functional sensors across five transcripts, novel composite synthetic triggers that each test an entire sensor panel for one gene, a dual-input AND gate, and T7 in vitro transcribed capped polyadenylated mRNA versions.","stack":["ViennaRNA 2.7.2 (RNAfold, RNApfold, RNAcofold)","CELLxGENE / CELLxGENE Census","GEO (GSE163973, GSE181316)","Human Dermal Fibroblast Atlas","GTEx long-read (Oxford Nanopore) RNA-seq","Ensembl / GENCODE annotations","Python","SQLite","Vercel","U-Net++ with ResNet34 encoder (segmentation_models.pytorch)","Autodesk Fusion 360 API","Benchling","Firebase (SynBase)"],"techniques":["Deterministic mass-action ODE modelling","Stochastic (occupancy-resolved) simulation","Simulated annealing parameter fitting","Quasi-steady-state approximation for competitive ADAR binding","Nine-state hybridization/editing state machine","Differential expression with pathological/healthy/bystander contrast","Isoform-level expression filtering","RNA minimum free energy and partition-function analysis","Spearman and Pearson predictor screening","Linear/Ridge/Elastic Net regression (reported as insufficient)","Jaccard similarity for cross-organ overlap","Flow cytometry gating (95th percentile mCherry)","Finite element analysis (Fick, Stokes-Einstein, Darcy)","Semantic image segmentation","Parametric CAD generation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Two deliberately linked programmes. SiBRP (Stanford iGEM Bioengineering Research Program) is a free, fully online three-week bioengineering bootcamp for high school students and incoming freshmen, with nine synchronous two-hour sessions plus three supplemental ones, progressing from molecular biology fundamentals through biodesign, genetic circuit design, plasmid design and experimental workflow to entrepreneurship and ethics. Students work in small groups to produce a full research proposal with literature review and experimental design, submitted as a draft, returned with TA feedback, then revised against a published rubric. It drew nearly 5,000 applications from all 50 US states and over 100 countries (up from 321 in its first year), with two-thirds of applicants female and over 60% from low-income and first-generation backgrounds; 230 participated. Nine guest speakers included iGEM co-founder Drew Endy. Because mentored capacity could not meet demand, the team converted the whole curriculum into SynBase (https://synbase.web.app/home.html) - 'Khan Academy, but for synthetic biology' - nine self-paced modules with accounts that save progress, knowledge checks, interactive activities and discussion boards, no application and no deadline, free worldwide. As of 17 October 2026 it had 750+ registered learners and 650+ free-response discussion contributions, with dry lab modules in development, and the team explicitly invites other iGEM teams to use or adapt it for onboarding their own members.","ihp":"The team was trained in Stanford's Biodesign process by Dr Ross Venook, who told them that biodesign means shadowing clinicians rather than searching online, and they built the whole project around that. Ideation ran as a weekly discipline: every member interviewed one person a week - over twenty conversations with professors, researchers and people in their lives - and a team member's experience with ReSurge, an NGO training reconstructive surgeons abroad, surfaced the statistic that 30% of global disease burden is surgical and reframed abnormal scarring as an access problem. Professor Fan Yang told them burns and keloids are separate research areas and doing both would muddle the work, so they picked one. At the Stanford BIOME Shark Tank they pitched to eight judges (faculty, investors, biotech entrepreneurs) who returned written scorecards; the published response table shows each criticism and what they did, including a judge's suggestion to build an AI-CAD platform for microneedles, which became the U-Net++ segmentation-to-Fusion-360 pipeline. When they looked for keloid patient advocacy groups there were almost none, and the one foundation that replied told them to talk to clinicians instead - the team says plainly that physicians cannot stand in for patients' lived experience and documents this as a second-best route. They then contacted 28 Stanford dermatologists and plastic surgeons and interviewed seven, with a standard instrument of Likert-scale ranking statements plus specialty-specific open questions. Three concerns recurred - recurrence within two years, pain and inconvenience of repeated injections, and difficulty getting drug into dense scar tissue - and each changed the design: Dr Teng's point that late-stage keloids are far harder to treat moved the target from prevention to established keloids; Dr Li's recommendation of collagen-scaffold fibroblast and hydrogel mechanotransduction assays changed the validation plan; physicians' complaint about repeated injections, plus Dr Li's endorsement of a patch, drove the wound-shaped microneedle array, held at 900 um because needles over 1000 um are documented to cause bleeding and pain. Dr Miranda consulted her own network on whether viral-derived parts are inflammatory, and the team decided against combining saRADAR with RNA export machinery as a result. With no animal model for keloids (they occur only in humans), they met Genoskin about their ex vivo human skin models and identified that as the next safety step.","realWorld":"The team was explicit at the Shark Tank that a target product profile was secondary to characterising a modular sensor platform other teams and scientists can reuse, and the contribution page is written as a handoff: swap the sensor region for a new transcript, add a second input as an AND gate, deliver as DNA or RNA, attach your own payload. The clinical path is staged and named - validate the Gao lab sensors and their own in HEK293, validate the AND gate, move to keloid and normal human fibroblast lines, add payload, then terminal assay in Genoskin's ex vivo human skin model for immunotoxicity, phenotypic and transcriptomic readouts - and Genoskin representatives engaged and advised on how to run it. The Cas12a2 payload work and the in vitro transcribed mRNA format are both framed as moving toward the form a real therapy would take rather than a reporter demo. SynBase is maintained beyond the competition as a continuing programme rather than a competition artefact.","partners":["ReSurge International","Genoskin","Stanford Biodesign","Stanford Hospital Department of Dermatology","Stanford Department of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery","Stanford BIOME (Biological Interdisciplinary Open Maker Environment)","Bay Area Bioengineering Symposium","Stanford Arc Institute","Gao Lab (RADAR technology)","Covert Lab","CELLxGENE / Chan Zuckerberg Initiative","GTEx Consortium"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m2-fig4-trigger-selection.webp","caption":"Trigger selection in RADAR-Scout: 600 filtered genes ranked across five keloid cohorts on the left (POSTN first at DSS 1.288), with every candidate sensor for the selected gene plotted on the right, so target and sensor are chosen in one view.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m2-fig10-rho-ranked.webp","caption":"All 19 computational predictors ranked by Spearman rho against sensor output: the two folding-energy terms lead at 0.62-0.63 while GC content, the field's informal selection rule, reaches only -0.29.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m3-cv1-patch.webp","caption":"A microneedle array patch generated from a photograph of a keloid scar: U-Net++ with a ResNet34 encoder segments the wound, and the Autodesk Fusion 360 API turns the mask into a 3D-printable CAD file.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m2-fig20-nine-state.webp","caption":"The nine-state hybridization and editing scheme for the AND-gate RADAR sensor; only the doubly-edited state is translated into GFP, and the enumerated transitions are how the system was verified to be complete.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/sibrp-call.webp","caption":"The final session of SiBRP 2026, the team's free three-week online bioengineering bootcamp, with students joining from 24 US states and 40 countries.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Stanford's SiBRP-to-SynBase pivot is the directly copyable move: they ran a small mentored cohort, discovered demand they could not staff (5,000 applications for 230 places), and then converted the exact same curriculum into a free self-paced online course with progress tracking and discussion boards - so the teaching asset outlives the cohort. A high-school-plus centre in Taiwan can do the same with its own course material, and should also adopt their dry-lab habit of testing the field's received wisdom (lowest GC content) against its own measured data and replacing it with a better predictor.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/safety","https://radar-scout.vercel.app/","https://radar-scout-dashboard.vercel.app/","https://synbase.web.app/home.html"],"rank":230},{"team":"LYON","id":"6280","section":"Undergrad","country":"France","village":"Bioremediation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/","projectTitle":"BioThetis","tagline":"De novo protein binders anchored on cheap cellulose that capture trifluoroacetic acid from industrial water and release it with heat — no living organism in the filter.","summary":"BioThetis goes after trifluoroacetic acid, the smallest and most stubborn PFAS: an NGO found it in 34 of 36 tap water samples across eleven European countries in 2024, activated carbon barely retains short chains, ion-exchange resins are not approved for French drinking water, and reverse osmosis only concentrates TFA five to ten times at enormous energy cost — the elected official in charge of water at the Metropole de Lyon told the team a single installation would run to roughly one billion euros. Their alternative is a protein binder fused to the cellulose-binding module CBM3a, which sticks to ordinary microcrystalline cellulose with no chemical coupling, captures TFA, and gives it back when the column is heated to 65 C so the material is reusable. Because TFA weighs only 114 Da and no natural binder was on offer, they designed binders from scratch with BoltzGen — 10,000 candidates, 6,823 through the filters, top 5 kept, three taken to the bench in two anchor orientations each — and demonstrated dose-dependent TFA binding by nanoDSF that does not respond to difluoroacetic or acetic acid, so the recognition is TFA-specific. They also solved the fluoroacetate dehalogenase DAR3835 to 1.10 A, the highest resolution reported for that enzyme, and said plainly that no density on the processed datasets is attributable to TFA. It won Best Bioremediation and Best Presentation on an unusually honest project: 15 new Registry parts, 25 printable protocols, all code and all dead ends released CC BY 4.0, and a human practices record that names exactly which conversation changed which page.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Education — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Presentation — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Bioremediation Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two strands, both documented with their failures intact. Binder design used BoltzGen 0.3.2 (Stark et al. 2025) on the Neurosnap platform with protein length constrained to 80-120 residues: 10,000 candidates generated, 6,823 passing BoltzGen's geometric and energetic filters, then re-filtered with the documented parameters (alpha 0.2, RMSD threshold 3.0 A) to keep the top 5 — across that shortlist median RMSD 0.982 A, median pTM 0.859, median ipTM 0.859, which the team notes shows consistent confidence rather than one lucky outlier. Three binders (Galatea, Ianassa, Melite) went forward in two anchor orientations each. Separately, a rational-design pipeline for DAR3835 variants combined structure prediction (Boltz-2 and AlphaFold3 via Neurosnap), sequence redesign with LigandMPNN, and binding-energy scoring with AutoDock Vina, run first on the related dehalogenase RPA1163 and then on DAR3835. Its result was negative and reported as such: no automatically redesigned mutant beat its wild-type benchmark on either scaffold, backbone confidence stayed high so the losses were interface rather than folding effects, and with the ligand pose at pLDDT 46-48 on the fluorines a Vina difference of 0.4 kcal/mol sits below the method's noise and cannot rank designs. The nine variants were therefore picked on explicit structural reasoning and rotamer feasibility, with docking used only as a consistency check, and one design was dropped at review after a rotamer check. The second strand is a packed-bed breakthrough column model in MATLAB: advection, axial dispersion and binder capture along the bed, Danckwerts inlet condition at C0, zero outlet gradient, bed starting empty, solved by finite differences with the stiff solver ode15s, reporting the breakthrough curve, BV10 and BV50, empty-bed contact time, bed Peclet number, Damkohler number and total TFA captured. All AI-designed proteins were screened through ToxinPred2 and BLAST against known toxins before anything was ordered.","dataviz":"The dry lab page is built to be read and reused rather than admired. The column model is introduced by a single annotated schematic that shows water entering at concentration C0 and flow rate Q, the two state variables C(z,t) dissolved and q(z,t) captured, a front moving down the bed, the three things that happen to TFA (carried by flow, spread by mixing, captured by binder), and the breakthrough curve as the output — so the whole model is legible before any equation. BoltzGen's 10,000 designs are shown as three scatter plots against design length (predicted error, pTM, ipTM) with grey for all designs, red for top quality after filtering and blue for quality-and-diversity picks, which makes the funnel from 10,000 to 5 visible instead of asserted. Retained designs are presented as superpositions of four AlphaFold3 predictions each. Every figure's generating script is published alongside it. The wiki itself carries a light/dark theme the visitor chooses, and the human practices section is organised as a stakeholder ledger where each entry is tagged Opened, Changed or Confirmed, with the date, the topic, and the specific wiki pages that entry altered.","datascience":"The honest handling of uncertainty is the real data science contribution. The team established an analytical limit case, a mass balance check and an independent re-implementation of the column model before trusting it, and published the parameter file with units and sources — flagging openly that some support and column parameters remain placeholders because they did not finish the model as intended. The conclusion they draw from it is genuinely useful and counterintuitive: affinity and capacity, not column geometry, decide how long a filter lasts. On the protein side they converted thermal shift assays into quantitative affinities (Ianassa N-term apparent Kd 25.0 +/- 4.9 mM, C-term 24.9 +/- 4.0 mM against TFA, versus 105.01 for wild-type DAR3835) and quantified the cellulose anchor by depletion assay (Bmax 1.18 +/- 0.11 nmol/mg, Kd 0.66 +/- 0.33 uM on microcrystalline cellulose). Engineering was run through explicit decision gates with pass criteria written in advance — TFA binding required a Tm shift of at least 0.5 C at 50 mM, reproducible and dose-dependent (met); thermal reversibility required at least 80% of the unfolding transition recovered on a second ramp (not met, for either DAR3835 or the de novo binders, and recorded as a failure with a proposed fix). They also list four checks required before the next synthesis round: redo the ligand pose by docking (AutoDock Vina or GNINA) on PDB 8SDC followed by 100 ns of molecular dynamics with recomputed RESP charges for TFA to test whether the Protenix pose reproduces; a rotamer scan on every bulky substitution; nanoDSF Tm plus post-cycle refolding yield for every variant; and acetate and propionate negative controls so affinity is attributed to the CF3 group rather than a generic carboxylate interaction.","software":"Everything is released under CC BY 4.0 on the team GitLab. The column model (MATLAB column_model.m, the parameter sweep scripts, a parameter file with units and sources, and the script behind each figure) is at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/lyon/-/tree/main/model/biothetis-column-model with an explicit invitation to plug in your own binder. BL21 is a browser video game that fits in a single 1.3 MB HTML file, runs entirely offline with no third-party assets, stores levels as data so the science is swappable (change the chassis and the target), takes about 15 minutes, works in French and English, and ships with 33 automated tests. The education kit adds three printable board games including an escape game with facilitator guides in English and French, colour-blind-safe design pairing every colour with a symbol, an experiments booklet, a handbook for school staff, and a before-and-after mind map to measure what pupils learned. They also published 15 new Registry parts (six binder constructs — Galatea, Ianassa and Melite each in two orientations — and nine DAR3835 variants, annotated region by region with one feature per mutated codon, numbered as PDB 8SDC plus 14 so mutations map straight onto the published structure), 25 bench protocols and three reference tables in one printable layout, and the survey questionnaire with anonymised aggregated results.","stack":["BoltzGen 0.3.2","Boltz-2","AlphaFold3","Neurosnap platform","LigandMPNN","AutoDock Vina (GNINA planned)","ToxinPred2 and BLAST toxin screening","MATLAB with ode15s stiff solver","X-ray crystallography (DAR3835 at 1.10 A, PDB 8SDC reference)","nanoDSF thermal shift","GitLab (iGEM instance)","Molecular dynamics with RESP charge recomputation (planned)"],"techniques":["De novo binder generation and multi-stage filtering (10,000 to 6,823 to 5)","Cofolding-based ligand pose prediction treated explicitly as a hypothesis","Rotamer feasibility screening of substitutions","Docking-based binding energy scoring with a stated noise floor","Packed-bed breakthrough modelling (advection-dispersion-capture PDE)","Danckwerts boundary conditions and finite-difference discretisation","Dimensionless analysis: Peclet, Damkohler, EBCT, BV10/BV50","Analytical-limit, mass-balance and independent-reimplementation validation","nanoDSF dose-response Kd determination with specificity comparators","Cellulose depletion binding assay (Bmax, Kd)","Pre-registered engineering decision gates with pass/fail criteria","Techno-economic cost-per-cubic-metre modelling"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"LYON built formats deliberately matched to audience rather than one activity repeated: children 6-14, high school students, adults, families, school staff, students, researchers and professionals, and anyone online from age 12. The flagship is four three-hour workshops run between 8 October and 19 November 2026 with a full ULIS class of 18 pupils in a primary school in Givors, south of Lyon, an area particularly affected by PFAS — ULIS being the unit that includes pupils with disabilities inside a mainstream school. Each session follows one line of reasoning: map environmental pollution with hands-on experiments, question filtration with a filter the pupils build themselves, define synthetic biology simply with a short video, and close with a board game. Pupils run the experiments themselves; the school aides who support them take part; the team adapts to children who need breaks and follows their rhythm rather than imposing a programme, and collects teacher and aide feedback after every session. They measured learning with a before-and-after mind map and published the experiments booklet, protocols and a school-staff handbook as a reusable kit, with partnerships continuing past the Jamboree and the guide left behind where the workshops happened. The materials are designed for reuse: three printable board games in French and English with facilitator guides, colour paired with symbols for colour blindness, and BL21, a single-file offline browser game with swappable science and 33 automated tests. A dialogue-free short film made with oKlama about the psychological impact of PFAS contamination carries no language barrier. They took shorter stand versions to Meet & Fabrik (winning two second prizes), visited three lycees, worked with biology, agriculture, dietetics, and history-and-geography teachers so PFAS gets studied from several angles, and organised an inter-school conference on 19 November where students present their own posters. A public survey drew 376 responses by 7 October, published anonymised with a frank list of what they would change. Community work included organising the French Mini Jamboree, a joint Jamboree talk with Berlin and Wageningen on PFAS, sending their game to iGEM Patras's community game library, joining Bochum's trading card project with 29 other teams, and a peer-reviewed article in Vector Volume 5 guided by Maastricht.","ihp":"The human practices record is structured as a ledger where every stakeholder entry is tagged Opened, Changed or Confirmed, dated, assigned a topic, and linked to the exact wiki pages it altered — which makes the influence auditable rather than claimed. The largest change: public concern about releasing engineered organisms moved them to a system containing no living cells at all, only purified protein on cellulose. Residents at an Institut Ecocitoyen PERFAO workshop set the target — their first request was depolluting drinking water because their catchment is over-contaminated and the installed activated carbon does not work on TFA — and testimony from PFAS Contre Terre, the plaintiffs of the 'proces des 250' against Arkema and Daikin, and team members' own relatives confirmed that TFA specifically, and the polluter-pays principle, is what local people want addressed. Veolia's technical director for France and two engineers across July and September calls reshaped the commercial design: they confirmed activated carbon barely works on short chains, that ion-exchange resins lack French health-authority approval for drinking water, that reverse osmosis concentrates TFA only five to ten times while being energy and water intensive and leaving an unmanageable concentrate, that resins and carbon are usually single-use so a regenerable system is commercially interesting, and that factory outflows typically carry 1-10 ug/L; they validated heat elution and CBM3a anchoring and suggested using it on reverse osmosis concentrate. Hector Osuna at Applexion pushed scale-up and multi-column systems, which became the carousel design. A nature protection association's plant visit taught them which treatment plants could realistically adopt it — large and relatively recent ones — and that economics, regulation and political decisions, not just feasibility, govern deployment. Florence Besoin of a residents' association in Saint-Genis-Laval, who approached them after seeing a flyer, set their public tone: residents are genuinely anxious and need to see people taking the problem seriously without being promised an unrealistic fix, so the team adopted 'honest and encouraging, never triumphant' and states on every page what the approach cannot do yet. A utopia-or-dystopia workshop at the French Mini Jamboree surfaced the failure mode of selling to households — clean water for those who can pay, risk left with the most exposed — confirmed by the survey where 96.3% of respondents wanted PFAS producers and users to pay, so customers are companies and operators only, never households. They also voted at the outset not to contact PFAS emitters, fearing reprisal and being used for greenwashing, and say so openly. After an intellectual property warning from Claude Mabillat they took IP training and then chose to release everything under an open licence anyway.","realWorld":"The product is specified as a treatment skid in a container: two cellulose columns carrying the binder, one capturing while the other is heated to 65 C to release TFA into a small volume that goes to destruction, capacity added by extending the carousel, sold, rented or charged per cubic metre treated with binder cartridges as consumables. First roadmap targets are pharmaceutical peptide synthesis, fluorinated chemistry and agrochemistry — industrial emitters and treatment operators, explicitly never households. The economics are worked and bounded: destroying the concentrate adds about 0.80 EUR per cubic metre at a placeholder 300 EUR per cubic metre of concentrate, cost barely moves between 1 and 10 ug/L, and since the support holds at most about 0.13 mg TFA per gram of cellulose the filter stays cheaper than destroying the whole stream up to roughly 40 mg/L at pilot pricing and a few hundred mg/L at industrial pricing, so the dilute regime is where it works — consistent with 2022 state measurements around the Pierre-Benite platform finding long-chain PFAS in industrial outlets from ug/L to low mg/L. Incumbents are costed side by side (activated carbon 29-33 kWh per kg to manufacture plus re-calcination above 850 C; ion-exchange resin 18-24 kWh per kg to make and 4.4 kWh per kg to regenerate at USD 18-33 per kg of medium), and one billion euros is used as the order of magnitude for the reference reverse-osmosis solution at drinking-water scale. The team's stated ethical position is that remediation complements regulation and never replaces it, repeated on every page and in every talk, with restriction supported first. They are open to founding a start-up as the 2025 Lyon team did, but on an open-licence basis, and a film screening with debate is planned for January.","partners":["Veolia (technical director for France plus two engineers)","Hector Osuna, R&D manager, Applexion","Metropole de Lyon (Anne Grosperrin, elected official for water)","Lyon-Feyssine water treatment plant staff","Institut Ecocitoyen pour la Connaissance des Pollutions","PFAS Contre Terre citizen collective (Lucas Miguel)","Florence Besoin, residents' ecological association, Saint-Genis-Laval","Plaintiffs of the 'proces des 250' against Arkema and Daikin","Marie-Charlotte Garin (member of parliament) and Stephane Horel (journalist)","Claude Mabillat (intellectual property)","ULIS class, primary school in Givors, with teachers and AESH school aides","oKlama (short film)","iGEM Berlin and iGEM Wageningen (joint PFAS talk)","iGEM Patras (community game library), iGEM Bochum (trading cards), iGEM Maastricht (Vector article)","iGEM Lyon 2025 (inherited plasmids)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6280/wiki/drylab/column-schematic.avif","caption":"The packed-bed filter model in one picture: water carrying TFA at concentration C0 and flow rate Q enters a cellulose column carrying the binder, with curves for C(z,t) dissolved and q(z,t) captured showing the front moving down the bed, and the breakthrough curve as the output.","credit":"Team LYON, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6280/wiki/drylab/image-1.avif","caption":"Design pTM against design length for every one of the 10,000 BoltzGen candidates — grey for all designs, red for top quality after filtering, blue for the quality-and-diversity picks — making the funnel down to five retained binders visible rather than asserted.","credit":"Team LYON, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6280/wiki/drylab/ian-overlay.avif","caption":"Superposition of the retained Ianassa designs, four AlphaFold3 predictions per design — the lead binder that went to the bench and showed TFA-specific, dose-dependent binding by nanoDSF.","credit":"Team LYON, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6280/wiki/education/image-2026-10-19-203152498.avif","caption":"Pupils running the experiments themselves during one of four three-hour workshops with a ULIS inclusion class of 18 in Givors, an area south of Lyon particularly affected by PFAS contamination.","credit":"Team LYON, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Steal LYON's human practices ledger: every stakeholder entry tagged Opened, Changed or Confirmed, with a date, a topic and a link to the specific project pages it altered — it turns 'we talked to people' into an auditable record and forces the team to admit when a conversation changed nothing. The second transferable habit is publishing failure as a deliverable: LYON state that their docking scores could not rank designs because the ligand pose confidence made a 0.4 kcal/mol difference meaningless, that thermal reversibility was never demonstrated, and that some column parameters are still placeholders — and release the code anyway so the next team starts further along. A high-school centre can adopt both tomorrow at zero cost, and both are exactly what judges and reviewers reward.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/design","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/safety-and-security","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/collaborations","https://2026.igem.wiki/lyon/communication","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/lyon/-/tree/main/model/biothetis-column-model"],"rank":129},{"team":"ETH-Zurich","id":"6289","section":"Overgrad","country":"Switzerland","village":"Foundational Advance","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/","projectTitle":"preCISe","tagline":"A modular tool for precise intracellular delivery using contractile injection systems","summary":"About 79% of the human proteome is intracellular and largely undruggable, and no approved protein therapeutic delivers an exchangeable protein cargo into the cytosol. preCISe engineers Photorhabdus virulence cassettes (PVCs), bacterial extracellular contractile injection systems that act as molecular syringes, into a retargetable delivery platform: a SpyTag/SpyCatcher-attached binder arm swaps the target without re-engineering the cage, demonstrated against CD20 on engineered mammalian cells with a piggyBac Cre-loxP reporter line (preCISion) that turns on GFP only when Cre reaches the cytosol. To raise specificity they added dual targeting, splitting the cargo across two PVCs against two receptors. It won Best Software and Best Foundational Advance because the platform ships as reusable infrastructure: 29 tested protocols, a part collection for evaluating intracellular delivery, and two open-source tools, deCISion and OpenSplitProtein.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Part Collection — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Software — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Foundational Advance Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Both tools are deliberately model-free, which is the team's central dry-lab argument. OpenSplitProtein ranks every peptide bond in a .pdb or .cif structure on two separate questions rather than one blended score: split tolerance (packing density weighted by inverse squared distance, distance from the buried core, solvent exposure summed over the flanking pair) and fragment reassembly (cross-cut contacts within 8 A, size-normalised interface, interface-to-cohesion ratio). No network was trained and no coefficient was fitted into the ranking; the only constants are the direction of each measurement, two size exponents from the domain-parsing literature and the 8 A contact cutoff. Crucially they defined a structure-free baseline (the length of the shorter fragment, plus relative position i/n) and showed that cross-cut contacts flip sign once it is held fixed, from +0.174 to -0.108, meaning the naive positive correlation was an artefact of chain geometry. Validation used Tsuboyama et al. (2023) cDNA display proteolysis insertion-stability labels at 13,622 backbone positions across 293 domains in 129 sequence families for split tolerance, and 223 split-intein sites from the Int-In dataset (126 with graded efficiency) for reassembly, with feature selection frozen before the test sites were seen and no sequence family shared at 40% identity nor fold at TM-score 0.3. deCISion is a database-integration pipeline rather than a predictor, chaining the Cell Surface Protein Atlas, Human Protein Atlas, SAbDab, Cellosaurus, UniProt, RCSB PDB, NCBI, the iGEM Registry and the Kazusa codon table.","dataviz":"The benchmark is reported as a percentile table against three comparators of different kinds, and the team says so explicitly: SplitSeek-Pro (a true competitor), the 2019 iGEM Protein-split Model, and ThermoMPNN-I (not split software at all, but the only released model predicting the insertion label directly, included as the hardest baseline). On 21 published split sites across superfolder GFP, Cre recombinase, horseradish peroxidase, firefly luciferase, Cas9 and NanoLuc, OpenSplitProtein puts the working site at a median 21st percentile with 13 of 21 in the top quarter, against a random expectation of 50; a 22nd site (Cas9 713/714) is excluded and the reason given. Both tools run in the browser (OpenSplitProtein needs only the HTML file opened; structures never leave the user's machine) with a 3D viewer showing the cut and both halves, and the wiki itself is a searchable static site with term callouts, a 'cut anatomy' diagram and an interactive outreach map rendered with d3-geo and topojson over OpenStreetMap and Natural Earth data. Published figures carry explicit tables for how to read a correlation column.","datascience":"The project's data-science contribution is the separation of proxy label from real task. Insertion stability measures the cost of perturbing an intact chain, never the cost of releasing two fragments, and the wiki states that difference rather than eliding it; the split-intein set is then used as the only label that directly tests reassembly. Scores are converted to within-structure ranks (0-1, displayed 0-100) so results are comparable across proteins of different size, and selection and testing datasets are separated with structural alignment checks. deCISion turns a public mass-spectrometry atlas into an actionable ranking: it reports each surface receptor's measurement strength on the chosen cell type and how many of the 47 CSPA cell types it was detected on as a specificity proxy, counter-checks normal tissue, cell-type, blood and tumour expression from the Human Protein Atlas, and lists only nanobodies and scFvs from solved structures with measured Kd where one exists (and states that only 1,019 of 14,860 SAbDab binders, 6.9%, have one). Limitations are enumerated rather than hidden: 47 cell types only, selectivity across cultured lines is not tissue specificity, and 'not detected' is not absence because Cell Surface Capture only sees N-glycosylated protein.","software":"Two released tools. OpenSplitProtein (gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/eth-zurich/opensplitprotein) ranks split sites from a structure file, installation-free in the browser. deCISion (gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/eth-zurich/decision) turns a cell type into a PVC construct in five steps: pick one of 47 CSPA human cell types by name, disease or tissue; choose a receptor; choose a nanobody or scFv binder (whole antibodies are rejected as too large for a PVC); accept the published XTEN linker or paste your own (pointing users to iGEM Calgary 2023's LINKED database); assemble binder-linker-SpyCatcher and export protein and DNA sequences as FASTA plus GenBank and SBOL3, with the three plasmids to order (cage, cargo, binder arm) listed. It ships MIT-licensed with a web interface, a JSON API and a CLI, 28 automated tests on every push through GitLab CI, a Docker build, a 2.5-minute demo video, and documentation of the architecture, the data build, what did not work and what the software cannot tell you. The team also published 29 tested eCIS protocols and a piggyBac/SpyTag part collection.","stack":["GitLab CI","Python 3.11","bun","Docker","MIT licence","JSON API + CLI + web UI","FASTA / GenBank / SBOL3 export","Cell Surface Protein Atlas (CSPA)","Human Protein Atlas","SAbDab","Cellosaurus","UniProt / RCSB PDB / NCBI","Kazusa codon usage database","iGEM Registry (BBa_K1159201 SpyTag)","d3-geo / topojson","BioRender"],"techniques":["geometric structure scoring (packing density, SASA, cross-cut contacts)","within-structure rank normalisation","confounder adjustment against a structure-free baseline","benchmark against published split sites and a stability-prediction baseline","family- and fold-level data leakage control (40% identity, TM-score 0.3)","surfaceomics-driven target selection and counter-selection","SpyTag/SpyCatcher modular retargeting","split-protein and split-intein design","piggyBac transposon reporter line construction","SWOT stakeholder analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was structured on three pillars (outreach, media, events) with a defined target audience per group and a before-and-after survey used as the measurement instrument, not just feedback. They visited four Swiss high schools (Kantonsschule Uster, Seetal, the cantonal high school for adults, Hohe Promenade) reaching over 130 students in formats from 45 to 90 minutes, and ran a hands-on week at the Wyss Zurich mint & pepper BioSTARS summer camp with 20 students from across Switzerland who performed their own PCR and gel electrophoresis, co-hosted with iGEM Zurich; over 150 students in total. They published two scientific-journal articles including one in BIOspektrum, ran a YouTube channel, co-hosted the Swiss iGEM Meetup for five teams, and presented at the Student Biolab welcome event, with about 16,900 social views. All templates and teaching material are published for reuse.","ihp":"24 interviews across six stakeholder groups, each analysed with a SWOT grid, and the changes are concrete. The project began as an eCIS biopesticide against Drosophila suzukii in Swiss crops; conversations with Drosophila experts and BFH European Meet-Up judges (Dr. Nicole Gensch, Amelia Wei) established that this was not viable and the team pivoted to human intracellular delivery while keeping eCIS. Scientific experts in the Pilhofer and Food Microbiology labs drove the choice of CD20 as the proof-of-concept target and the design of the reporter systems. The dual-targeting split-cargo concept was invented in direct response to expert questions about off-target risk, and OpenSplitProtein exists only because that concept needed a cut site: the wiki states the idea came from those conversations, recorded under Human Practices. Dr. Max Brackmann (NBC arms control laboratory, Spiez) assessed the work case-by-case against CDC and Robert Koch Institute checklists and concluded neither the PVC work nor deCISion required additional biosafety layers; the idea of building deCISion on the CSPA came from Prof. Bernd Wollscheid, after the team found the existing SISYPHUS interface unusable for their actual question. Investors and companies (Athebio, Procavea, Nanosyrinx) pushed specificity, off-target data, IP and scalability, and advised focusing a platform on one lead application.","realWorld":"The team is explicit that this is a foundational advance handed to others rather than a product: the stated purpose is to let future iGEM teams and researchers engineer eCIS more easily, backed by 29 protocols with recommended controls for teams with no prior wet-lab experience, a part collection, and two MIT-licensed tools. Their own validation honesty is part of the case: they state that the best validation of deCISion would be wet-lab targeting of new cell types from its outputs, that they had neither the time nor the scale, and that confidence therefore rests on the validated public databases underneath. The outlook names the sequence required before any clinical path: uniform and consistent PVC expression, verification of predicted binders, validation of split cargo, an industrially scalable PVC production workflow, then in-vivo mouse work on immune response, degradation, accumulation and efficacy. Clinicians (University Hospital Zurich, Hirslanden) were approached partly to establish contacts should the project continue past the competition.","partners":["Pilhofer Lab, ETH Zurich (Adam Pinsler, David Schaumann, Dr. Charles Ericson)","Prof. Bernd Wollscheid, ETH Zurich (CSPA)","Dr. Enea Maffei, Food Microbiology, ETH Zurich","Prof. Martin Loessner, ETH Zurich","Prof. Nadia Mazouz, ETH Zurich (ethics)","Dr. Max Brackmann, NBC arms control laboratory Spiez","Dr. James Lapworth, Nanosyrinx","Dr. Tom Edwardson, Procavea Biotech","Dr. Richard Woods and Dr. Johannes Urban, Athebio","Lukas Frischknecht, University Hospital Zurich","Jan Duscha, Hirslanden medical group","Wyss Zurich mint & pepper BioSTARS summer camp","iGEM Zurich (UZH)","BFH European Meet-Up","Student Biolab Zurich","ScopeM, ETH Zurich"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6289/wiki/images/software/opensplitprotein/web1-03-ranked-v3.webp","caption":"OpenSplitProtein's browser interface showing every cut site in an uploaded structure ranked separately for split tolerance and fragment reassembly, instead of a single blended score.","credit":"Team ETH-Zurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6289/wiki/images/software/decision/howto4-2-receptor.webp","caption":"deCISion's receptor step: surface receptors measured on the chosen CSPA cell type, with measurement strength, how many of the 47 cell types each was detected on as a specificity proxy, and the number of PVC-compatible binders available.","credit":"Team ETH-Zurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6289/wiki/images/results/retargeting-v3.webp","caption":"Retargeting result: PVCs carrying an anti-CD20 binder arm attached through SpyTag/SpyCatcher act specifically on CD20-expressing preCISion cells.","credit":"Team ETH-Zurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6289/wiki/images/education/summer-camp-gel.webp","caption":"Students running their own gel electrophoresis at the Wyss Zurich mint & pepper BioSTARS summer camp, where 20 high-school students from across Switzerland worked through a realistic research scenario.","credit":"Team ETH-Zurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"The transferable move is cheap-but-honest computation: ETH beat heavier published tools by scoring six geometric quantities, refusing to fit a model, and then spending their effort on validation design instead (a structure-free confounder baseline, frozen feature selection, no shared sequence family or fold between selection and test sets). A school research centre can reproduce that whole method on a laptop, and should copy the companion habit of publishing a 'what the software cannot tell you' section alongside the results.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/software/decision/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/contribution/"],"rank":136},{"team":"Princeton","id":"6295","section":"Undergrad","country":"United States","village":"Foundational Advance","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/","projectTitle":"SymPleco: Making Precision Sequestration Sym-ple","tagline":"One M13 phage chassis, any cargo — bulk-capacity adsorption with antibody-grade specificity and light-triggered release.","summary":"SymPleco attacks a trade-off that runs through all remediation chemistry: activated carbon captures a lot but cannot tell similar molecules apart, while monoclonal antibodies bind precisely but need harsh pH or chaotropic salts to release and cannot be recovered or scaled. Princeton's answer is a filamentous M13 bacteriophage used as a programmable adsorbent: sortase A-mediated ligation staples engineered fusion proteins onto the N-terminus of every pVIII copy (making 'furry' phage that bypass classical display size limits), with pIII carrying a Cg-Defensin anchor for PLA filter attachment, a VHH7 nanobody for macrophage clearance, and an AsLOV2 optomonobody that holds cargo in the dark and lets go under 450 nm light. Because swapping the target means swapping only the displayed binder, the team paired it with SymPlify, a de novo binder design pipeline, so a new target does not wait on a new antibody. It won on foundational-advance logic: a genuinely modular chassis demonstrated across three deliberately unrelated applications (melanoma exosome sequestration, Parkinson's alpha-synuclein interception, microcystin/BPA water remediation), backed by an unusually self-critical ODE model and an IRB-approved patient engagement programme.","awards":["2nd Runner-up (Undergrad)","Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Part Collection — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Measurement — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Foundational Advance Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two distinct computational efforts. SymPlify is a de novo protein binder design pipeline that routes a target by type: a protein chain goes to BindCraft (AlphaFold2-multimer hallucination with gradient descent on logits through softmax, SolubleMPNN sequence design, AF2-monomer re-prediction as a deliberately harsh filter, then PyRosetta physical metrics for binding energy and surface hydrophobicity), while a small-molecule or macrocycle chain goes to RFdiffusion3 all-atom diffusion plus LigandMPNN (eight sequences per backbone) and an independent complex-prediction model. PeSTo supplies hotspot residue scoring, RDKit renders structures and finds pharmacophores, and freesasa computes hydrophobic patch fraction. Separately, the measurement work is backed by a mechanistic ODE model of the recursive Phage Jumpstart Assay (jumpstart.py, jumpstart_mechanistic.py, passage.py, phase2.py, recursion.py) tracking naive, infected and free-particle pools with superinfection exclusion (sigma = 0.05), saturating infection, carrying capacity, and a hybrid-coat infectivity a_h derived from real pIII gene dosage (a_h ~0.729 at m13ko7_copies=20); the 1:11 passage step samples each particle pool by Poisson rather than plain division so a lineage can genuinely go extinct.","dataviz":"SymPlify's web interface is the visualisation centrepiece: an RDKit 3D viewer colours the target by PeSTo binding probability, lists top hotspot residues with confidence scores, lets the user delete low-scoring ones with an x to tighten the patch, and recomputes the difficulty breakdown (spread, hydrophobic surface fraction, globularity) live as the selection changes — plus a warning when residues sit more than 30 A apart, because one binder cannot reach them all. A run-status page shows pipeline stage, target type, elapsed time, auto-resubmits, design count and job ID, and a final ranking screen lets the user click through designs and download .pdb files with or without GS linkers. The model page is unusually transparent as data presentation: it prints the full command reference, labels every parameter as measured, derived or assumed, and ranks assumptions by their sensitivity on the round-2 plate ratio.","datascience":"The Jumpstart assay turned a single colony-ratio readout into a trajectory: because each recursion is both a manipulation and a measurement, repeated rounds on a population being driven toward one genotype reveal behaviour a single ratio cannot. Statistics are handled carefully — intervals bootstrap over wells, leave-one-well-out checks are reported, panels yielding 0-2 Amp colonies out of 165-272 are reported as upper bounds rather than measurements, and a chi-square test (197 on 5 df) establishes that the six Run B constructs are not interchangeable while the team explicitly declines to lean on the ordering because the bottom four are indistinguishable from zero. They also report that three magnitude-based titer estimators disagreed by up to 20,000-fold on the same panel, which is why they built a ratio readout instead. Most notably the model documents its own error history: an initial m13ko7_copies=200 was simply the wrong figure (wild-type M13 rolling-circle copy number rather than M13KO7's actual p15A-driven one), corrected to 10 and then 20 as evidence improved, with the admission that the corrected model is more parameter-sensitive, not less.","software":"SymPlify, an integrated de novo binder design pipeline with a web interface, hotspot prediction, cluster job submission with auto-resubmission, and post-design filtering and ranking; released for the wider synthetic biology community and wet-lab tested on the team's own targets. Team repository: https://github.com/dianalluu/puigem2026, with an AlphaFold deployment for the Princeton Della cluster at https://github.com/09aaronl/alphafold_princeton-della. The ODE model ships as a Python package with 52 automated checks in test_model.py (~80 s full run, ~8 s in fast mode) covering mass-balance and neutrality invariants, the hybrid-coat gene-dosage derivation, the Poisson passage statistics and pinned regression values. Hardware: a 3D-printed PLA gyroid biofilter cartridge whose two winning PITCCH designs are published as downloadable source STEP assemblies.","stack":["BindCraft","AlphaFold2-multimer","AlphaFold2 monomer","RFdiffusion3 (RosettaCommons foundry)","LigandMPNN","SolubleMPNN / ProteinMPNN","PeSTo","PyRosetta","RDKit","freesasa","ChimeraX","Python (NumPy/SciPy ODE integration)","Benchling","SLURM GPU cluster (Princeton Della)"],"techniques":["De novo binder design by AF2 hallucination with gradient descent on logits","All-atom diffusion backbone generation around a fixed ligand","Inverse folding / sequence design","Hotspot (epitope) prediction and patch-tightness scoring","Target difficulty scoring (0-100, Easy to Very Hard) from flexibility, globularity, hotspot patch, size, epitope chemistry","Small-molecule scoring by logP 30%, size 20%, rigidity 20%, TPSA 20%, aromaticity 10%","ODE compartment modelling of phage-host population dynamics","Poisson resampling of passage dilutions to allow true extinction","One-at-a-time parameter sensitivity ranking","Bootstrap confidence intervals over wells and leave-one-well-out robustness","Chi-square test of construct interchangeability","Regression-pinned unit testing of a scientific model"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"PITCCH, the Princeton iGEM Team CAD Competition for Hardware, is the standout: entrants were given a real open engineering problem from the project — design a 3D-printable gyroid biofilter, 20 mm diameter by 25 mm height, maximising contact surface area — along with the team's actual print parameters. It ran free, fully asynchronous and online in a junior division (grades 5-12) and an open division (18+), explicitly permitted responsible AI use, and drew 42 registered submissions from the United States, United Kingdom and Mexico, of which 26 passed dimensional and printability screening before being sliced and computationally ranked by total surface area. Announcement emails went to thousands more via high school superintendents, undergraduate engineering directors and makerspaces, each carrying a project description and iGEM links. The team frames it as citizen science and backed that framing with credit: two winning designs appear on the wiki with the designers named on the attribution form, and the registration terms stated plainly that the team reserves the right to draw inspiration from submissions with attribution. They also postered at symposiums and presented at bioethics exchanges, and at the US-China iGEM Human Practices Summit showed their halted immunology work as a case study in how to pivot.","ihp":"This is the strongest documented chain of design changes of any team here, and several of them cut capability rather than adding it. Dr Bowen Jing's technical advice seeded SymPlify and his steric clash filter was adopted, but the team then cut AlphaFold3 from the pipeline entirely because Dr Martin Pacesa argued it often mispredicts binding sites; Pacesa also supplied PeSTo as the hotspot engine and, after the team ran competing designs to compare, AF2 monomer was implemented on his side of the argument. Dr Reynold Panettieri talked them out of sequestering IL-6 — their original software benchmark target — by pointing out IL-6's dual-sided effects on blood cell production and T-cell regulation, redirecting them toward a niche where cheaper-than-mAb production is the real advantage and toward naming one indication and working from there, which is exactly how the final applications are framed. Dr Douglas Galasko's distinction between biomarkers and toxins moved them off amyloid-beta for Alzheimer's and into the Parkinson's and rabies work. Two multiple myeloma patients, interviewed under Princeton IRB Protocol #20003, independently said the word 'virus' for a bacteriophage was a problem — one noting roughly 80% of myeloma patients are treated in community settings and take their doctor's word — which changed how the team describes the project in all outreach. A former FDA biologics deputy director, Dr Celia Witten, pointed them to CBER's phage therapy work; EPA Office of Pesticide Programs and OPPT staff sent them to the statutes; and marine microbiologist Dr Stephen Giovannoni plus phage ecologist Prof Forest Rohwer turned the Cg-Defensin antimicrobial worry into a specific planned avidity-comparison assay. Dr John Wnek, who conserves Diamondback terrapins, chose their environmental targets: microcystins and BPA, because nearby New Jersey plants discharge effluent half a mile offshore with no process for xenobiotics.","realWorld":"The regulatory path is mapped rather than asserted: phage therapy under FDA CBER for the clinical arms, and statute-level grounding for the environmental arm after meetings with EPA Office of Pesticide Programs and Office of Pollution Prevention and Toxics, with the team careful to state that nothing should be read as EPA guidance. Containment is designed for deployment — the phage stays inside a closed water treatment facility bound to a biodegradable PLA filter so it is retrievable and localised, with macrophage clearance via VHH7 as the biosafety route for therapeutic use. The commercial argument is reconfiguration cost: because capture specificity lives in one genetic part on pVIII, changing target means changing that part and not the media, housing, attachment chemistry or capital cycle, which is the case made against granular activated carbon for PFAS where GAC retains poorly over realistic service lifetimes. Work is mapped to SDG 3.4, 6.3 and 9. Continuation is concrete: further IRB submissions to interview melanoma patients and Parkinson's patients and caregivers, the Rohwer avidity assay, qPCR of M13KO7 copy number to settle the model's most sensitive parameter, and phage hydrogels as an alternative anchor that would remove the Cg-Defensin concern altogether.","partners":["Dr Martin Pacesa (BindCraft author)","Dr Bowen Jing (computational biology)","Dr Reynold Panettieri, Rutgers Translational Medicine and Science","Dr Jared Radbel, Robert Wood Johnson Medical School","Dr Douglas Galasko (neurology)","Dr Celia Witten, former deputy director, FDA biologics center","US EPA Office of Pesticide Programs and Office of Pollution Prevention and Toxics","Dr John Wnek (environmental conservation, Diamondback terrapin)","Dr Stephen Giovannoni (marine microbiology)","Prof Forest Rohwer (marine phage ecology)","Prof Ned Wingreen and Lawrence Abad (phage biology)","Prof Jared Toettcher (faculty PI, IRB Protocol #20003)","Princeton Institutional Biosafety Committee","Multiple myeloma patient advocacy organisations","US-China iGEM Human Practices Summit","IDT, GenScript, ODI (sponsors)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/symplify-hotspots-protein.avif","caption":"SymPlify's hotspot selection screen: the protein target rendered in 3D and coloured by PeSTo binding probability, with top hotspot residues listed by confidence so low-scoring ones can be removed to leave the focused patch of three to five that BindCraft works best with.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/symplify-filtered-designs.avif","caption":"SymPlify's ranking and filtering view, where accepted binder designs are sorted by AlphaFold confidence metrics and PyRosetta physical scores and can be downloaded as .pdb files with or without GS linkers.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/hardware-pitcch-surface-area.avif","caption":"Ranked printable surface area for the 26 PITCCH entries that passed dimensional and printability screening, plotted against reference geometries — public CAD submissions evaluated computationally and used to pick the biofilter geometry.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/saf-macrophage-uptake-gfp.avif","caption":"Overlaid phase-contrast and green-fluorescence micrograph of cultured macrophages after incubation with GFP-expressing phage, showing uptake by the VHH7 clearance arm. Scale bar 150 micrometres.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy PITCCH: hand the public a genuine, unsolved sub-problem from your own project with real constraints and real print parameters, judge entries computationally on a single objective metric, then credit the winners by name and actually build their design in. It converts outreach into contributed engineering at almost no cost, and it works for high-school students because spatial creativity, not synthetic biology training, is what the task rewards. Pair it with Princeton's habit of writing down which model parameters are measured, derived or merely assumed — and publishing the history of their own corrected mistakes — which is the cheapest credibility a student dry lab can buy.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/design-rationale","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/application","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/safety-and-security","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/judging"],"rank":205},{"team":"KIT","id":"6296","section":"Overgrad","country":"Germany","village":"Foundational Advance","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/kit/","projectTitle":"ENCORE - Engineered Nanocompartments for Capture, Optimization and Reactor Engineering","tagline":"A standardized modular Golden Gate toolkit that expresses self-assembling encapsulin nanocompartments with custom cargo in yeast","summary":"KIT, the first iGEM team at Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, built ENCORE: a standardised modular cloning system (the toolKIT) that co-expresses a self-assembling encapsulin protein nanocompartment and its cargo in Saccharomyces cerevisiae, chosen because encapsulins assemble from a single repeating subunit and so need far fewer transcriptional units than bacterial microcompartments or virus-like particles. They won the 2026 Inclusivity Award for work that turned inward rather than outward: instead of role-model campaigns, they asked how tasks are actually distributed inside scientific teams and what role gender plays, surveyed 71 people, ran proper statistics, and published the negative result honestly. The resulting deliverables - a facilitated workshop with moderation guide and printable worksheet, and TeamKIT, a team-and-meeting-culture guide - are the reusable artefacts.","awards":["Inclusivity Award — Winner (Overgrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Survey research methodology applied with unusual rigour for an iGEM team: seven self-derived teamwork task categories (Coordination, Social, Operational, Scientific Conception, Creative Design, External Communication, Leadership/Mentoring) each with a written definition; separation of actual task distribution from perceived distribution; a 12-item five-point Likert instrument grouped into three constructs (Perception, Autonomy, Allocation) of four items each with one reverse-scored item; debriefing placed deliberately at the end of the instrument so as not to prime respondents, following social-science practice; expert instrument review; piloting; and an ethics-committee determination.","dataviz":"A heptagon dot-poster used as the physical pre-survey instrument at Campus Day, later rebuilt as an interactive clickable heptagon on the wiki; grouped mean-frequency bar charts of task distribution by gender for the full dataset and the iGEM subgroup; box plots of Likert sum scores for Perception, Autonomy and Allocation; diverging stacked bar charts of each Likert item by gender with sub-10% segments suppressed; an interactive TeamKIT map where a visitor picks their chapter and situation and is pointed to a model and an exercise; and a self-check tool so a reader can see which roles they take in their own team. Figures were generated with AI assistance (Claude) and labelled as such.","datascience":"Pre-survey n = 58 (30 female, 28 male) at KIT Campus Day; main survey n = 71 (45 female, 24 male, 2 non-binary, the latter excluded from gender comparison for sample size and stated as a limitation), of whom 59 were iGEM members, 8 from research groups and 4 from university groups, predominantly undergraduate (41) and graduate (18) students. Because the data were not normally distributed the team used two-sample Mann-Whitney U tests rather than Welch t-tests, at alpha = 0.05. Result: for every task area the null hypothesis held, except Scientific Conception in the full dataset (p = 0.039), where men reported taking it on more often; that difference vanished in the iGEM-only subgroup. All three Likert constructs showed no significant gender difference (p = 0.271, 0.750, 0.113) and scale reliability was good to excellent (Cronbach's alpha 0.822, 0.991, 0.930). The single most striking descriptive finding: for 'I receive adequate recognition for my contributions to the team', no male participant disagreed while 9 of 45 women did. Limitations are set out at length - voluntary-participation selection bias, no cultural-background data collected, comparability of iGEM teams with research groups uncertain, no participant reported being in a predominantly male team despite ~70% of KIT students being male, and the team states plainly it has no formal social-science training.","software":"Two integrated tools. The Open NC Database curates nanocompartment properties scattered across the primary literature - internal volume, pore diameter and charge, reported stability, associated targeting peptide - into a searchable, filterable form, with a data model that distinguishes the biological candidate from the experimentally reported assembly and its supporting evidence and explicitly marks unreported values as unreported; candidates are keyed by UniProt accession and structures by PDB identifier, a sequence enters the catalog only after a documented translation check, and the database accepts contributions by pull request. The ENCORE-Builder turns a database selection into a complete Golden Gate / MoClo design: it assigns fusion sites across three cloning levels, places start and stop codons, codon-optimises for S. cerevisiae, designs primers and oligonucleotide pairs, avoids sites forbidden by RFC 10 and RFC 1000, and emits annotated GenBank and SBOL 3 files plus order lists, with the toolKIT's combinatorial rules enforced so invalid designs cannot be produced. It was validated against a real cloning: the Builder's Level 2 design for the Quasibacillus thermotolerans shell with LGB4 matches the construct the team cloned in the lab.","stack":["Golden Gate / MoClo assembly","SBOL 3 and annotated GenBank output","UniProt and PDB identifiers as data keys","git / GitLab pull-request contribution model","Mann-Whitney U test","Cronbach's alpha","OpenDyslexic (SIL Open Font License 1.1)","Scientific Colour Maps v8.0.1 (Crameri)","Blender (encapsulin rendering)","Miro"],"techniques":["encapsulin shell self-assembly in S. cerevisiae","targeting-peptide cargo fusion","T=1/T=3/T=4 shell selection (24, 32, 42 nm)","VCPO reactor","oxygen-free space engineering","mRNA capture","building a lab from scratch"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"KIT won Inclusivity by doing what almost no previous iGEM gender-equity project had done: looking inward. The team read the iGEM Diversity & Inclusion Committee blog post 'Bursting demographic bubbles', surveyed prior iGEM work (Paris Bettencourt 2013, JIASHU-Shanghai 2024, Stony Brook 2022, Patras-Med 2024, Guelph 2024) and identified that almost all of it faces outward through role models, outreach to girls or awareness campaigns, with only Guelph coming close to an internal view by surveying its own composition. KIT's research question went further: not who is on the team, but how work is distributed - who ends up in the lab, who presents, who takes the organisational and invisible tasks, and, memorably, who empties the dishwasher in the institute kitchen. They derived seven task categories from reflecting on their own team, tested them as a physical heptagon dot-poster at KIT Campus Day (n = 58, colour-coded dots by gender), then designed a full instrument separating actual task distribution from perception, had it reviewed by Dr Melanie-Marita Leidecker-Sandmann of KIT's Department of Science Communication (who had them shorten items, add demographics, remove two questions that revealed the hypothesis, and add a proper debriefing), piloted it with Engineers Without Borders at KIT, obtained an ethics-committee determination, and distributed it through Instagram, the official iGEM Slack and a QR-code flyer at the Barcelona Meetup, plus KIT mailing lists. 71 responses came back. The honest headline is that their hypothesis was not supported - women did not take disproportionately more secretarial or communication work - but two findings held: most participants of either gender agreed organisational work receives less recognition than research work, and 9 of 45 women versus 0 of 24 men felt their contributions were not adequately recognised. They published the full statistics, the Cronbach's alphas, the raw data, both survey versions and the ethics statement, and wrote a long, specific limitations section. The transfer step is what makes this copyable. After discussing results with Ines Koehler, Head of KIT's Department for Equal Opportunities and Diversity Management, who invoked iGEM's 'give back to the community' principle, the team built two artefacts. The Gender Roles & Task Distribution Workshop is designed for newly formed teams at the start of collaboration, when patterns are easiest to shift: each person maps how their time splits across the seven task areas and rates satisfaction with each, then in paired conversations talks to someone who regularly carries a task area they themselves would feel least confident taking on, making invisible effort tangible; it ends with concrete next steps including a weekly challenge where members swap tasks and a check-in a few weeks later. The full moderation guide with a printable worksheet is downloadable, needs no prior gender-research knowledge, and adapts to 1.5-2.5 hour sessions. It was revised after written feedback from Ines Koehler and from Anietta Weckauff and Marina Jaehnert Giannitrapani, co-founders of the Women in STEM Initiative at the University of Stuttgart - changes included replacing 'blind spot' with 'perception gap' because unnoticed tasks are not wilfully ignored, changing 'a task I would never do' to 'a task area you feel least confident taking on' so the choice does not read as a judgment of the task's worth, adding a satisfaction rating, explicit ground rules, an opt-out in the warm-up, a note on intersectional factors and a recommendation for mixed-gender co-moderation. The team states plainly that neither version has been piloted yet and asks teams who run it to report back. TeamKIT is a downloadable guide to team and meeting culture built because, as the first KIT team with no predecessor, they had to work out task distribution, meetings and conflict from nothing - and found first-year teams at Aarhus and Berlin had the same problem; it moves from individual strengths and interests through interpersonal communication to the whole team, ending with meeting and conflict methods, with inclusion threaded through rather than placed in its own chapter, and it was reviewed by Freja from iGEM Aarhus. Public outreach for a Foundational Advance project was equally deliberate, since nanocompartments are invisible and hard to picture. Three members joined KIT's first science slam camp, eight workshop days on storytelling, stage presence, visualisation and PR, and performed to around 100 people at a Karlsruhe science communication event with a metaphor they arrived at only after scrapping draft after draft: the yeast cell is a ball pit and the nanocompartment a wonder machine that fetches a toy you are too big to reach, gives a child a protected space to make chewing gum without harming anything around it, and takes snapshots of children quarrelling among the balls (mRNA capture). CoLab was an art exhibition built on an open call that asked artists a question rather than commissioning illustrations: what becomes of a promoter, what could competent cells be, what does autoclaving mean, and what do you see in four unlabelled scientific figures from the team's own lab work; eleven works by nine artists (one dating from 1980) were shown over two weekends in October at the pavilions in the Fasanengarten in Karlsruhe with team members present throughout, and visitors asked questions that never come up in the lab - what exactly are you changing in those cells, what happens to them afterward, who decides what is allowed. 'The Yeast We Can Do' is a fully open board game for 3-6 players aged 12+, about 45 minutes, no prior knowledge needed, with 19 missions across six application areas from breaking down forever chemicals to capturing CO2 and growing nanocrystals, where all players compete for the same 20 amino acids on the same 37 fields of nutrient medium; rules, board image, mission cards, event cards, score pad and quick reference are downloadable PDFs and the yeast-cell and budding-yeast playing pieces are STL files for any FDM printer, all on GitLab. The team's stated design goal is that a complete newcomer leaves knowing a protein is a chain of twenty kinds of beads, some hard to come by, while an iGEMer might spot the catalytic triad in the plastic-degrading enzyme. They also ran two pub quizzes at the student-run venue Z10 (27 April and again on 15 October during first-year orientation week), staffed a Campus Day stand with a science quiz, a pipette-tip-box filling race and pipetting lessons, published ENCORE in BIOspektrum 04/26 (a leading German molecular life sciences journal, Springer Nature) and in the KIT student magazine clicKIT, built a social media identity from zero deliberately as team-building infrastructure for their successors, and hosted the first iGEM Baden-Wuerttemberg Meetup at KIT on 13 June with GU Frankfurt, Bonn, Saarland, Aachen, Berlin, Heidelberg and Stuttgart, opening with a poster-design session by Sylvia Erhardt of the Center for Synthetic Genomics and continuing with a peer-consultation workshop where teams brought their most pressing internal problems and bottlenecks for structured peer feedback, including team-dynamics questions that rarely surface in formal settings. The wiki itself is an accessibility deliverable, built following UniMuenster 2023's 'Inclusive Wiki Design' guide: large clear sans-serif type, a high-contrast palette of six colours in three shades each chosen for colour-vision deficiency, one-click OpenDyslexic font toggle, one-click dark mode, descriptive alt text on every figure for screen readers, a glossary of scientific terms and abbreviations, a site-wide search function, and shape-coded navigation (encapsulin-shaped octagons for research, circles for Human Practices) so section is distinguishable without relying on colour. Commitments extend past the competition: gender-specific task distribution may feature in next year's KIT Diversity Day, and an article about the project will appear in the KIT Diversity Magazine.","ihp":"Integrated Human Practices is tightly coupled to the inclusivity work - the interview with Ines Koehler of KIT's Equal Opportunities and Diversity Management department is documented on the iHP page and is what converted the survey from a finding into two deliverables. The survey's own development through expert feedback, piloting and revision is itself documented as an engineering cycle ('Survey on Task Distribution') on the Engineering page, applying DBTL to a social-science instrument. The team explicitly anchored the work to UN Sustainable Development Goal 5 (gender equality) and to the local fact that roughly 70% of KIT students in engineering and technical programmes are male, while in Europe only one in five ICT and engineering specialists is female.","realWorld":"Everything is downloadable and reusable: the workshop moderation guide and printable worksheet, the TeamKIT guide, the full board game (rules, board, cards, score pad, quick reference, STL print files) on GitLab, both survey versions, the raw data and the ethics statement, the Open NC Database (open to pull requests) and the ENCORE-Builder (new parts, adapters and backbones added as data files so other teams can extend it). The team's framing of why this matters for successors is worth copying: 'fair task distribution should not depend on whether a team happens to have someone who already cares about the topic', and the most useful thing a first team can leave behind is not a project but a starting point.","partners":["Ines Koehler, Head of Department for Equal Opportunities and Diversity Management, KIT","Dr Melanie-Marita Leidecker-Sandmann, Department of Science Communication, Institute for Technology Futures, KIT","Women in STEM Initiative, University of Stuttgart (Anietta Weckauff, Marina Jaehnert Giannitrapani)","Engineers Without Borders KIT (survey pilot)","KIT Ethics Committee","nine artists in the CoLab exhibition (Friederike Wallmen, Fabien Stark, Linus Hense, Silas Goettle, Marina Schreidl, Andreas Krueger, Lenara Bortscheller, Guenter Wallmen)","Z10 student-run venue, Karlsruhe","BIOspektrum (Springer Nature)","clicKIT magazine","Sylvia Erhardt, Center for Synthetic Genomics","iGEM Aarhus (TeamKIT review), iGEM Berlin","BaWue Meetup teams: GU Frankfurt, Bonn, Saarland, Aachen, Berlin, Heidelberg, Stuttgart","BFH European Meetup (Bielefeld, Frankfurt, Hamburg)","Mini Jamboree Eindhoven","KIT Diversity Magazine / Diversity Day"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6296/wiki/human-practices-resp/who-does-what-in-a-team/campus-day-presurvey-heptagon.avif","caption":"The heptagon pre-survey: visitors placed a gender-coded dot on the task category they most often take on in teamwork (n = 58)","credit":"Team KIT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6296/wiki/human-practices-resp/who-does-what-in-a-team/task-frequency-all-20data.avif","caption":"Mean frequency of task distribution by gender across seven task areas, Mann-Whitney U tested (n = 69)","credit":"Team KIT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6296/wiki/human-practices-resp/building-a-team-from-scrach/teamkit.avif","caption":"TeamKIT, the downloadable guide to team and meeting culture written for first-year iGEM teams","credit":"Team KIT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6296/wiki/human-practices-resp/public-outreach/the-yeast-we-can-do/game-board.avif","caption":"'The Yeast We Can Do': the open-licensed board game board, 19 missions over 37 fields of nutrient medium, with 3D-printable yeast pieces","credit":"Team KIT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6296/wiki/human-practices-resp/public-outreach/art-exhibition/pavillon-fasanengarten.avif","caption":"CoLab, the art exhibition at the Fasanengarten pavilions in Karlsruhe, built from an open call asking artists what lab words mean to them","credit":"Team KIT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6296/wiki/human-practices-resp/public-outreach/science-20slam-2.avif","caption":"The Science Slam performance to around 100 people, explaining the yeast cell as a ball pit and the nanocompartment as a wonder machine","credit":"Team KIT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6296/wiki/human-practices-resp/public-outreach/meetups/events/baw-meetup-everyone.avif","caption":"The first iGEM Baden-Wuerttemberg Meetup, hosted at KIT with eight German teams and a peer-consultation workshop","credit":"Team KIT, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Point inclusivity work at your own house before the public: survey how tasks are actually distributed inside your own teams with a properly reviewed instrument, publish the result even when it contradicts your hypothesis, and convert it into a ready-to-run workshop plus a team-culture guide that a brand-new cohort can use in week one, so fair task distribution does not depend on one person happening to care.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/kit/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kit/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/kit/public-outreach","https://2026.igem.wiki/kit/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/kit/integrated-human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/kit/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/kit/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/kit/building-a-lab"],"rank":26},{"team":"McMaster-Canada","id":"6297","section":"Undergrad","country":"Canada","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/mcmaster-canada/","projectTitle":"Chariot: A Platform Technology for OMV-based Therapeutics","tagline":"Inducible loading of bacterial outer membrane vesicles, plus the standardised generation, isolation and characterisation protocols the field does not have.","summary":"McMaster built Chariot, a platform that loads cargo into bacterial outer membrane vesicles (OMVs) using the VNp tagging system, so that accumulation of a VNp-fused protein forces membrane curvature and vesiculation under an inducible promoter. Having identified that OMV work is held back less by biology than by the absence of standard methods, the team made measurement the project: they characterised VNp-driven OMV generation, benchmarked four isolation methods against a shared filtrate reference, and wrote everything into an openly published Protocol Handbook. Alongside this sits Apollo and Artemis, an in-silico platform combining GFlowNet-based active learning for peptide design with molecular dynamics for VNp-cargo compatibility. The project was shaped by 40 stakeholder interviews run through a purpose-built OMVS framework.","awards":["Best Measurement — Nominee (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"A declared 'dual measurement' policy: every conclusion must be supported by at least two independent measurement techniques, and every comparison must run against a control. Integrated Human Practices uses a bespoke OMVS framework (Observe, Modify, Vision, Synthesis) built on the UKRI AREA principles, with each letter mapped to a specific iGEM Human Practices judging question. Machine learning is framed as an explicit design-test-learn loop in which measurements become training data.","dataviz":"Interactive wiki charts with toggles for 'individual measurements', 'mean +/- SD', 'logarithmic scale' and 'show data', so a reader can switch between the summary and the underlying points; an interactive stakeholder network of 40 stakeholders across 37 nodes, colour-coded by category, where clicking a node jumps to its position on an engagement timeline; a conceptual platform-workflow animation for the Apollo/Artemis pipeline.","datascience":"The measurement work is built around the idea that a single endpoint cannot be trusted. OMV preparations are characterised by dynamic light scattering (Z-average diameter and polydispersity index on a Zetasizer Nano ZS) and by nanoparticle tracking analysis (particle concentration), with the team noting explicitly that DLS readings and NTA runs are independent and not paired by row, and flagging a date discrepancy between the workbook label and the raw file timestamps rather than quietly fixing it. Total protein is quantified with a Pierce BCA assay against a BSA standard curve (R2 = 0.97), with blank-subtracted technical triplicates and the individual absorbance readings published; the resulting 709.2 ug/mL is reported alongside the caveat that BCA measures total protein and cannot distinguish lumenal cargo from membrane-associated or co-isolated protein. Comparisons between isolation methods are tested statistically rather than by eye: one-way ANOVA on log10-transformed values with Tukey post-hoc at alpha 0.05 for size and polydispersity across ultracentrifugation, centrifugal concentration, the Norgen kit, a novel 'Push-Pull' adaptation and 0.22 um filtration, all referenced to a single shared 0.45 um PES filtrate so no method gets a head start; lysis conditions are tested by one-way ANOVA with Dunnett's test against untreated OMVs; and the 4-hour versus 18-hour vesiculation comparison uses Welch's t-test on log10 values (p = 0.0005). SDS-PAGE is quantified by ImageJ densitometry, giving 41% recovery of total extracellular mNeonGreen through OMV isolation. Crucially the team kept and measured every fraction each method produced, including supernatants, flow-throughs and intermediate washes they were meant to discard, so losses can be attributed to a step. Limitations are stated as findings: the proteinase K protection assay that would distinguish genuine vesicle lysis from detergent micelle formation was not completed, so DLS size shifts remain the only lysis evidence; the 0.45 um filtrate was never run on NTA; and BCA reports preparation-level protein, not cargo. On the modelling side, Apollo is a generative flow network trained with trajectory balance and coupled to Bayesian optimisation: an ensemble of five independently initialised MLP regressors on padded one-hot peptide sequences acts as a proxy for a fixed D2-AlBERT oracle, with a UCB acquisition score converted into the GFN reward, and the released CLAMP/DBAASP dataset deduplicated and split by target identifier. The team is careful about what this does not establish, writing that negative labels do not represent experimentally demonstrated inactivity, that grouping by target does not separate closely related sequences, and that a falling training loss does not verify exact reward-proportional sampling.","software":"Apollo (GFlowNet + Bayesian optimisation peptide design, built on the published GFlowNet biological-sequence-design framework and the CLAMP/DBAASP dataset) and Artemis (molecular dynamics comparison of VNp-cargo pairings). A Protocol Handbook covering molecular cloning through OMV generation, isolation, lysis and analysis is published for reuse. Wiki source at gitlab.igem.org/2026/mcmaster-canada; site content licensed CC BY 4.0.","stack":["Malvern Zetasizer Nano ZS (DLS, zeta)","Nanoparticle tracking analysis (NTA)","Pierce BCA protein assay","SDS-PAGE with ImageJ densitometry","Norgen Bacterial EV Isolation Kit","Ultracentrifugation","50 kDa centrifugal concentrators","GFlowNets (trajectory balance)","Bayesian optimisation / GP-UCB","MLP ensembles","Molecular dynamics"],"techniques":["Dual orthogonal measurement per conclusion","Shared reference control across methods","One-way ANOVA on log10 values with Tukey post-hoc","Dunnett's test against untreated control","Welch's t-test on log10 values","BSA standard curve with R2","Technical triplicates with blank subtraction","Measuring discarded fractions to locate losses","Explicit statement of unmeasured confounds","Active learning / design-test-learn loop"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education outputs include a SynBio Handbook and a zine series, classroom and community sessions with named educators, and outreach materials documented on the education page.","ihp":"Forty stakeholders were consulted across five groups (patients, healthcare providers, regulatory experts, industry experts and biosafety experts), with sub-expertise in clinical infection, biofilms and pathogen biology, OMV and EV engineering, and drug delivery and nanomedicine. Each conversation is written up through the OMVS framework and tied to a project change; the stakeholder map records three kinds of link (referrals, sequences where a question raised in one meeting was answered in a later one, and convergences where two stakeholders who had never met reached the same conclusion), so a reader can trace a design decision back to the conversation that caused it. The first major pivot came from Dr Coombes, who raised uncontrolled inflammation and septic-like responses as unavoidable risks of systemic bacterial-derived therapeutics and cautioned that peanut-allergy mechanisms are not well enough understood for rational design; the team dropped its oncology and allergy concepts and moved to localised gut-associated systems, judging every subsequent concept on biological feasibility and patient safety before therapeutic potential.","realWorld":"The entrepreneurship case positions Chariot as a modular OMV loading platform for biotechnology and pharmaceutical partners developing protein-based therapeutics, with antimicrobial resistance as the first application because OMVs fuse with bacterial and mammalian membranes and can deliver non-membrane-disrupting antimicrobial peptides past the permeability barrier. The stated value proposition is improved cargo loading plus more consistent and scalable production. The team also frames its protocol standardisation as the deliverable most likely to outlive the competition, benchmarking itself against earlier OMV teams (Freiburg 2024, NYU Abu Dhabi 2025).","partners":["Farncombe Family Digestive Health Research Institute","Didar Lab, McMaster University","Dr Brian Coombes (McMaster)","Dr Michael Surette (McMaster)","Dr Elena Verdu (Farncombe Institute)","Dr Robert Hancock","Dr Greg Hussack","iGEM Freiburg 2024","iGEM NYU Abu Dhabi 2025"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6297/wiki/measurement/measure-sds-page.avif","caption":"12% SDS-PAGE of E. coli BL21(DE3) pET26b(+) 6xHis-mNeonGreen-VNp6 induction and OMV isolation products. ImageJ densitometry on this gel gave 41% recovery of total extracellular mNeonGreen through the isolation.","credit":"Team McMaster-Canada, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6297/wiki/measurement/omv-filtrate-plate.avif","caption":"The 0.45 um PES-filtered vesicle preparation plated on LB agar. No growth confirms the absence of whole-cell contamination in the shared filtrate that every isolation method was benchmarked against.","credit":"Team McMaster-Canada, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6297/wiki/measurement/fluorescence-post-isolation.avif","caption":"mNeonGreen-VNp OMVs after Norgen kit isolation on a bench-safe blue light box: a 4-hour vesiculation period on the left, 18 hours on the right. The visible difference was then quantified by NTA, where the 18-hour preparation yielded significantly more vesicles (Welch's t-test on log10 values, p = 0.0005).","credit":"Team McMaster-Canada, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Keep and measure the fractions you were told to throw away. Benchmarking every isolation or purification method against one shared starting material, then analysing the supernatants, flow-throughs and washes as well as the eluates, turns a yield number into a map of where material is lost and at which step.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/mcmaster-canada/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/mcmaster-canada/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/mcmaster-canada/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/mcmaster-canada/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/mcmaster-canada/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/mcmaster-canada/hardware"],"rank":7},{"team":"UCNZ","id":"6301","section":"Undergrad","country":"New Zealand","village":"Biomanufacturing","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/","projectTitle":"NanoBrew","tagline":"Enzymatic production of nanocellulose from brewers' spent grain - turning 39 million tonnes a year of brewing waste into a high-value biomaterial.","summary":"NanoBrew takes brewers' spent grain (BSG), which makes up about 85% of beer brewing waste by weight and is mostly landfilled or sold as cheap animal feed, and extracts nanocellulose from it enzymatically instead of with hot acid and energy-intensive mechanical grinding. The team expressed a Bacillus subtilis xylanase (XynA) to strip hemicellulose and open up the cellulose, plus an ancestrally reconstructed Cel5A endoglucanase fused to a carbohydrate binding module to cut cellulose down to nanofibrils, and tested six construct variants with and without the YebF secretion tag in E. coli. It was Top 10 and nominated for Presentation, Entrepreneurship and Biomanufacturing because the project is framed end to end as a New Zealand circular-economy story - built on a real brewery partnership, grounded in the Maori value of kaitiakitanga, with a full pilot-plant cost analysis and letters of support - and because the team is candid that it never managed to characterise its product. No previous iGEM team had extracted nanocellulose from lignocellulosic plant material rather than growing it bacterially.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Biomanufacturing Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Modest but purposeful, and honestly scoped. Before ordering genes, all six constructs were folded with AlphaFold 3 and the predicted structures aligned in PyMOL against the unfused enzymes to check the YebF tag, linkers and CBM had not disturbed the catalytic core - RMSD came out at 0.101 and 0.117 Angstrom for XynA and YebF-XynA, and 0.183, 0.155, 0.173 Angstrom for the endoglucanase variants, all well under 2 Angstrom, with pTM reported per model and the flexible linker correctly flagged as an unstructured region. Enzyme assays were run as time-series DNS and fitted to Michaelis-Menten-like behaviour on both the control substrate (birchwood xylan) and on BSG, with enzyme inactivation used to freeze each timepoint. For the system-level question they built a causal loop diagram with 12 nodes linking the environmental, economic and social effects of an upscaled process, signed each causal link positive or negative, and decomposed the graph into balancing and reinforcing loops - while explicitly refusing to quantify link strength. The microfluidic viscometer was designed entirely in simulation (Capillarix, an unreleased capillary-microfluidics simulator supplied by its creator) with a simulated error table, and the absorbance sensor uses a machine learning calibration running on the device itself.","dataviz":"The two devices are the visualization story: the project needed a way to read nanocellulose concentration from microlitre samples, and the engineering subteam built it. The differential microfluidic viscometer compares a sample against a reference fluid in a sealed channel to cancel out the variable hydrophilicity of PMMA, computing relative viscosity as the ratio of average length-times-velocity products over the first second of flow, then calibrating to the expected sample range so unknowns are read by linear interpolation. The optical sensor strobes red, green and blue LEDs sequentially and feeds the three absorbance values to a machine learning model on an onboard ESP32, which infers concentration - and is deliberately built so a biotechnologist can recalibrate it for whatever property they want to infer. The wiki itself keeps each page to a clear sectioned structure with a sidebar contents list, and the stakeholder work is presented as an influence-interest grid rather than a list.","datascience":"The dry lab's strongest quality is calibrated honesty rather than volume of computation. The team states plainly at the top of the entrepreneurship page that its market research targeted cellulose nanofibrils because that was the anticipated product from the literature, but that it was unable to characterise what it actually made; the FTIR comparison against a pure nanocellulose reference sample came back inconclusive, and they published that, named ATR-FTIR as the next attempt, and said they were waiting on expert advice as of the wiki freeze. For the absorbance sensor they ran out of nanocellulose and substituted guar gum, justifying it as shear-thinning with a similar concentration-opacity relationship, and tabulating the results as such. The viscometer error table notes that surface roughness is not modelled. They also report that only one student in their school talks had heard of iGEM, and that a 2019 survey of 9,000 New Zealanders found 30% opposed selling GM food at all - using measured baselines rather than assertions about public attitudes.","software":"No standalone software release. The hardware is the deliverable: two sensors for reading nanocellulose concentration in microlitre volumes, one a differential capillary-microfluidics viscometer validated in simulation, the other a built RGB-strobing absorbance/turbidity sensor with on-ESP32 machine learning calibration, both developed through two documented iterations of the engineering design cycle and integrated into University of Canterbury engineering students' coursework with some of those students joining as external collaborators. Parts are the other contribution: six constructs registered, with EG-CBM (BBa_26TOG7XZ) highlighted as expressing and purifying extremely well with good activity on complex substrates, plus Pichia/yeast-targeted versions using an OST1 signal sequence and T2A self-cleaving peptide bicistronic designs (BBa_266IKFQ3, BBa_26OB3D76), and a characterisation of the YebF secretion tag (BBa_K1610300, from TAS_Taipei 2015) intended to let future teams choose a secretion system on evidence.","stack":["AlphaFold 3","PyMOL","SnapGene 8.2.3","Capillarix (capillary microfluidics simulation)","ESP32","Google Patents / BLAST (freedom-to-operate searching)","FTIR spectroscopy","SEM","solid-state NMR (via Bioeconomy Science Institute)"],"techniques":["Structure prediction and RMSD alignment for fusion-construct validation","pTM / confidence-based interpretation of predicted models","Time-series DNS enzyme assay with Michaelis-Menten fitting","Causal loop diagramming with balancing and reinforcing loop decomposition","Differential capillary viscometry","Multi-wavelength absorbance with on-device ML calibration","Sedimentation assay for aspect ratio","FTIR purity assessment","Two-cycle engineering design iteration","PEST and SWOT analysis","Pilot-plant cost forecasting","Influence-interest stakeholder mapping"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Outreach was built around a deliberate hypothesis: in New Zealand, synthetic biology means genetic modification and genetic modification means risk, so the team tested whether a local story changes how people engage. They gave three secondary-school talks (one at Burnside High School, two at Christchurch Girls' High School, both schools some members had attended) covering synthetic biology, iGEM, what a science degree looks like and how NanoBrew works, and structured them for dialogue - long question time, small-group breakout discussion, a quiz where answers were argued through rather than marked, contact details left for follow-ups, and teacher feedback collected. Their findings are reported as findings: students already knew breweries and brewery waste so the local hook carried the science; small groups worked far better than whole-cohort presentation; most questions were about science careers and preparing for university, where undergraduates were better placed to answer than any teacher; and only one student had ever heard of iGEM. For the wider public they worked with the university communications team on a press release framed as a local problem with a local solution, which reached The Press, NZ Herald, Radio New Zealand, NewstalkZB, The Breeze, The Platform and deliberately rural and regional outlets including the Ashburton Courier and Selwyn Times, with a team member and the PI giving radio interviews that explained containment in plain language; the Instagram account passed 23,000 views. They also presented at the Biomolecular Interaction Centre Winter Symposium. The published lessons for future teams are practical: get your institution's comms team involved early, and contact schools months ahead because they plan a year out.","ihp":"Human practices is organised around kaitiakitanga, the Maori concept of guardianship, with Bree Bell (Kaiarahi for UC Science) consulted early on how the project interfaces with iwi and hapu, and further consultation explicitly deferred to the scale-up stage rather than claimed as done. Stakeholders are mapped on an influence-interest grid and the design changes are specific. The team was split between on-site plants at each brewery and one centralised facility; Professor Jack Heinemann and Dr Brigitta Kurenbach, both involved in international synthetic biology regulation, argued that a distributed model multiplies accidental-exposure risk and regulatory burden because each site needs separate approval, so the team went centralised. The same pair identified the circular-economy rebound effect - if BSG becomes valuable, beer gets cheaper to make, production rises and total waste could increase - and told them 'sustainable' depends on timeframe and is often marketing; the team consequently narrowed its claim to reducing environmental impact relative to alternative BSG disposal, up to a stated horizon, and no further. Materially Kiwi (a startup making leather from kiwifruit waste) independently warned that customers buy on performance, consistency and price, not sustainability, and that a free feedstock does not mean a cheap product once collection, processing, testing and equipment are counted - which moved yield confirmation and full process costing to the top of the priority list. Dr Stefan Hill and Dr Mathias Sorieul at the Bioeconomy Science Institute warned that BSG-derived nanocellulose may be indistinguishable from wood-derived, so the low-cost waste feedstock, not the material, is the selling point; they also reframed BSG's high hemicellulose as a possible advantage, citing an Australian spinifex grass result where hemicellulose remnants improved material properties, and prescribed the three characterisation methods (SEM for fibril width, sedimentation for aspect ratio, FTIR for purity) the team then attempted. Dr Andrew Prest at Foodstuffs Own Brands listed what a packaging buyer needs and confirmed food-contact use would need substantial safety assessment, which is why the team targets non-food packaging first. Lincoln Agritech's suggestion of rheology as a measurement route is what started the viscometer, and Professor Volker Nock was met at the BIC symposium after researchers there raised the energy cost of freeze-thaw cycles at scale.","realWorld":"The commercial case is unusually complete for an undergraduate team and backed by two letters of support (Cassels Brewing Co. and Lincoln University). Local grounding: in Christchurch alone BSG accounts for an estimated 680-1,000 tonnes to landfill a year, costing breweries up to NZ$220,000 in disposal fees, and nationally roughly 13,700 tonnes to landfill producing 14,000 tonnes CO2-equivalent, which the promotional video renders as 3,300 petrol cars running for a year. Market: nanocellulose at US$947 million in 2026 growing at 23.74% CAGR to a projected US$6.4 billion by 2035, with the team noting that estimates vary between reports and stating which figure it uses throughout. Price reality from stakeholders sets the strategy: at around $100/kg nanocellulose suits high-value medical uses while broad uptake needs roughly $10/kg, so packaging is the entry market. The page carries a development roadmap, a commercial-scale cost forecast, a downloadable preliminary CNF cost analysis for a pilot-scale plant, financial analysis and timeline, a business exit strategy, minimum viable product definition, PEST, SWOT, risk and competitor analyses (including Nano-Sea/AgriSea making kelp-derived nanocellulose locally, which does not target packaging), and a pitch deck. On IP, the UC Technology Transfer Office told them a first filing runs NZ$10-15K and is only worth it if results look promising, that only novel elements need protecting, that much of their approach is already published so finding the novel element may be hard, and that they should BLAST their enzyme sequences against patented sequences to check freedom to operate - and that they should document negative results because that is how an invention's origin is evidenced. The team notes everything submitted to iGEM enters the public domain, so any filing had to precede the wiki. Continuation is real: the media coverage plus the results won University of Canterbury funding for a summer research project carrying the work on under Dr Kelsi Hall, one of only 18 scholarships awarded across all faculties in 2026. The team also commits to licensing the process to other agricultural and food-processing waste streams once protected, with documentation, stakeholder map and methods open now.","partners":["Cassels Brewing Co.","Lincoln University","Lincoln Agritech","Bioeconomy Science Institute (BSI)","Foodstuffs Own Brands","Materially Kiwi","Kai Commitment","Biomolecular Interaction Centre (BIC), University of Canterbury","UC Technology Transfer Office","Momentum (UC entrepreneurship programme)","University of Canterbury Communications and Events","Burnside High School","Christchurch Girls' High School","University of Canterbury School of Biological Sciences","University of Canterbury engineering students (external collaborators)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6301/wiki/hp/fig-2.avif","caption":"The causal loop diagram: 12 nodes linking the environmental, economic and social effects of an upscaled NanoBrew process, with each link signed positive or negative and the graph decomposed into balancing and reinforcing loops - including the circular-economy rebound effect raised by regulatory stakeholders.","credit":"Team UCNZ, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6301/wiki/hp/fig-1.avif","caption":"The stakeholder influence-interest grid used to decide who to consult and in what order, spanning the brewery partner, regulators, characterisation experts, potential customers and Maori advisors.","credit":"Team UCNZ, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6301/wiki/hardware/figure-1-1.avif","caption":"The final design for the differential microfluidic viscometer, simulated in Capillarix, which infers nanocellulose concentration by comparing relative viscosity of a microlitre sample against a reference fluid.","credit":"Team UCNZ, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6301/wiki/results/results-fig02.avif","caption":"A time-series DNS assay showing XynA xylanase activity rising on both the control substrate (birchwood xylan) and on brewers' spent grain, in Michaelis-Menten-like fashion.","credit":"Team UCNZ, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6301/wiki/education/school.avif","caption":"A NanoBrew talk at a Christchurch secondary school, run with long question time and small-group breakouts after the team found whole-cohort presentation far less effective.","credit":"Team UCNZ, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"UCNZ shows how to publish an unfinished result well: they never characterised their nanocellulose, said so at the top of the page that depended on it, named the failed method (FTIR, inconclusive against a reference sample), the planned replacement (ATR-FTIR) and the substitute material they used when samples ran out - and still won Top 10. The second transferable move is the local hook: anchor the project in an industry students already recognise, then get the institution's communications office involved early enough that national and regional media carry the story, which is what converted their outreach into continuation funding.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/experiments","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/education-and-outreach","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/safety-and-security","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucnz/notebook"],"rank":91},{"team":"Baltimore","id":"6329","section":"High School","country":"United States","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/","projectTitle":"KeraTINT","tagline":"A chromoprotein-keratin-binding-peptide fusion that colours hair and repairs it in the same step","summary":"Baltimore Biocrew, based at the Baltimore Underground Science Space community lab, engineered fusion proteins joining chromoproteins (aeblue, UM) to keratin-binding peptides so that a semi-permanent dye deposits colour while reinforcing the hair shaft. The entrepreneurship work was run through iGEM's Venture Creation Labs curriculum, the same incubator route their 2023 project RhizeUp took, and it produced an OKR table with numeric key results, a scored risk register, a US-specific patent strategy and a competitor map. Market evidence rests on two IRB-approved consumer surveys (56 then 54 responses, translated into Chinese and Filipino) plus named interviews with a cosmetics founder, two L'Oreal North America hair-colour-lab staff, a cosmetology student and the Boeke Lab at NYU Langone. They were nominated for Best Entrepreneurship in the High School section.","awards":["Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"iGEM Venture Creation Labs curriculum as the scaffold, Golden Circle, Unique Value Proposition, Lean Business Model Canvas, SWOT, a seven-phase Objectives and Key Results table with business-day estimates and fixed-cost lines, a risk register with a published 1-5 impact x 1-5 likelihood scoring rubric and named owners, direct and indirect and future competitor tiers, and the iGEM judging ballot answered question by question.","dataviz":"Interactive click-through SWOT, a nine-slide pitch deck embedded as a carousel, a lean canvas, a long-term impact-assessment graphic, a 2027-2029 timeline, and a horizontally scrollable OKR and risk table. Survey results are reported as percentages and cross-tabulated by language group and age.","datascience":"Two-round survey analysis with a genuine iteration: a bimodal distribution on grey-coverage priority in Survey 1 (a quarter rating it 1, nearly half rating it 10) was hypothesised to be two age cohorts, and Survey 2 confirmed it by adding age, hair type and prior chemical-treatment questions. Reported figures include 64.3 percent rating less hair damage a 10/10 priority, 89 percent rating it 8 or above, 50 percent using at-home boxed kits and only 25.9 percent relying solely on salons.","software":"","stack":["Google Forms","chromoprotein-keratin-binding peptide fusion constructs"],"techniques":["fusion protein design","colour retention and wash-cycle testing (planned)","cytotoxicity assay development (started, not completed)","pH and thermal stability assessment (literature-based)"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Surveys were deliberately multilingual, translated into Chinese and Filipino to reach their own communities, and the results split cleanly by language and age in ways that changed the product thesis: younger English-speaking respondents wanted fashion blues, pinks and purples, which happens to be what their chromoproteins produce, while older Chinese and Filipino respondents wanted grey coverage and natural shades. They also found that the phrase 'genetically engineered' on a label worried more respondents than it attracted, with several linking it to harmful chemicals or illness, and responded by planning a consumer FAQ booklet distributed through partner salons instead of a call centre. They presented at the July 2026 Mid-Atlantic Meetup alongside eight other regional teams.","ihp":"A Closed-Loop Framework structured each stakeholder conversation into relevance, insight, integration and future question. IRB approval from the BUGSS Board of Directors was obtained before surveying, which is unusual rigour at high-school level. Specific conversations changed specific decisions: Savannah Shumaker (chemist, co-founder and CEO of Eight Count Cosmetics) pushed branding toward audience values and simplified their science communication, and suggested contacting cosmetology schools, which led to Rachel Lu; Lindsay Menzer and Yun Yong Jang (L'Oreal Hair North America, Head of Hair Color Lab) flagged patenting during early testing, which launched the US patent-strategy investigation; the Boeke Lab at NYU Langone recommended storage conditions for chromoproteins, prompting a preservation study; and a question from another team at the Mid-Atlantic Meetup about pH and temperature sensitivity, which they had deprioritised, is what sent them to the Boeke Lab in the first place.","realWorld":"This is a credible early-stage venture plan with the finance section still hollow. What is genuinely strong is the execution scaffolding. The OKR table is the best artefact: it sets falsifiable targets with costs and durations attached, including catalogue 15 salons statewide, a minimum of 10 qualitative interviews, at least 5 Letters of Intent, 10 investor pitches, 10,000 dollars from stakeholder investment plus research grants, cell viability above 70 percent in cytotoxicity assays, dye longevity of 40 wash cycles, protein stability at 50 degrees C, 1,000 sales per month and two new production labs in other mid-Atlantic states. Named funding targets are real and local (Wexford SciTech Venture Fund, TEDCO grants, Connect Labs at 4MLK as prospective lab space, youth competitions such as Blue Ocean, Paradigm and Slingshot). Crucially, every one of these is still an aspiration: the Letters of Intent are a key result, not a signed document; the grant is applied for, not won; there is no registered entity, though forming an LLC is floated inside the liability risk row. The regulatory path is the most concretely researched part of the whole analysis and is credible: they identify that the FDA Office of Cosmetics and Colors certifies coal-tar and synthetic colour additives but that a chromoprotein appears to fall into neither bucket, name 21 CFR parts 73 and 74 subpart C, name the Color Certifications Inquiry Branch as the body to ask, and note that certified and uncertified colours carry legally distinct names. The IP strategy is equally specific and actually actionable: a provisional patent on the aeblue-keratin fusion first, converted to a non-provisional within twelve months, with USPTO micro-entity status claimed for an 80 percent fee reduction, and they check their own eligibility against the 262,380 dollar gross-income threshold. Market sizing, by contrast, is the weakest link, resting on Grand View Research hair-colour market reports rather than any bottom-up estimate, with no price point, no cost of goods and no unit economics anywhere. Continuation is plausible rather than secured: they state plainly that as a high-school iGEM team they can never test on human hair, so KeraTINT must pursue third-party testing or operate independently of iGEM after the competition, and they sequence cytotoxicity, pH and thermal assays as the first post-Jamboree work. The precedent helps their case, since the same team's 2023 RhizeUp project went through the same incubator.","partners":["iGEM Venture Creation Labs / Venture Foundry (incubator curriculum)","Baltimore Underground Science Space (BUGSS) community lab and its Board of Directors (IRB approval)","Savannah Shumaker, chemist and co-founder/CEO, Eight Count Cosmetics","Lindsay Menzer and Yun Yong Jang, L'Oreal Hair North America, Head of Hair Color Lab","Rachel Lu, cosmetology student and freelance makeup artist","Dr. Jef Boeke and Aleksandra Wudzinska, Boeke Lab, NYU Langone Health","Mid-Atlantic Meetup regional iGEM teams"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/lbmc.avif","caption":"KeraTINT lean business model canvas identifying local salons and cosmetic companies as first customers","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/timeline.avif","caption":"Commercialisation timeline from 2027 stakeholder re-engagement to a 2029 salon product roll-out","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/pitch-deck/2.avif","caption":"Slide from the nine-slide KeraTINT pitch deck covering market sizing and distribution channels","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/impact-assessment.avif","caption":"Long-term positive and negative impact assessment for consumers and the wider hair-care market","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Steal the OKR table: every commercialisation objective gets a numeric key result, a business-day estimate and a fixed-cost line, including scientific gates such as cell viability above 70 percent and dye longevity of 40 wash cycles, so a business plan becomes a checklist a successor team can inherit. Also copy getting institutional IRB approval before running consumer surveys, which both protects participants and makes the resulting data citable.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/","https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/human-practices/"],"rank":7},{"team":"NJU-China","id":"6337","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/","projectTitle":"Starase Atlas — Atlas-BRIDGE, Atlas-COMPASS and Atlas-EDGE for enzyme discovery","tagline":"Given a target reaction and limited experimental resources, which enzyme candidates should a researcher test first?","summary":"NJU-China built a three-part platform around one decision: which enzyme to test next. Atlas-BRIDGE (Broad Retrieval with Inference-Driven Gated Experts) is a dual-tower contrastive retrieval model that will rank any protein it can encode, then lets specialist 'expert' models and two kinds of memory nudge that ranking only where their evidence genuinely applies; Atlas-COMPASS is a natural-language agent front end that resolves entities before it computes anything; Atlas-EDGE is a MySQL/graph knowledge base of compound nodes and enzyme edges built from UniProt, Rhea and ChEBI. The model's recommendation was then taken to the bench: M5AW86 was predicted and confirmed to convert FPP to beta-farnesene by GC-MS, TLC and 1H NMR against empty-vector controls. It won Best Software & AI Project and was nominated for Best Model.","awards":["Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Software & AI Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Atlas-BRIDGE is a machine-learning information-retrieval model, not a kinetic one, and its central design idea is worth copying regardless of domain: separate *eligibility to be ranked* from *availability of evidence*. The base model ('Broad') is a two-tower encoder — one tower for protein sequence, one for reaction transformation — trained with a multi-positive contrastive objective that respects the many-to-many nature of enzyme data, L_MP = lambda*L_R2E + (1-lambda)*L_E2R, where each directional loss sums over the full set of known positives rather than treating a single pair as the truth. Because an unrecorded enzyme-reaction pair is not a true negative, they apply positive-unlabeled (PU) learning plus sequence-cluster masking to decide which pairs are even admitted for contrast, and because experimental screening only tests the top of a list they add an explicit head-ranking hinge, L_head = softplus(n_K - p* + m), pushing the best positive above the K-th admissible negative, with hard negatives mined from the candidates the current model confuses most. The final score is additive and auditable: S = S_Broad + alpha_LT*Delta_LT + alpha_epi*Delta_epi + sum_k g_k*Delta_k. The Gated Experts are plug-in models carrying functional, structural/mechanistic or family/domain evidence; each must publish candidate-wise scores *and an availability mask*, has its applicability gate, normalisation and maximum correction magnitude locked on separate validation data before any external test, and reverts exactly to the prior ranking wherever its evidence is missing — so absent data is never silently read as a low score. Long-term memory replays the training-cutoff catalytic graph at inference, projecting 16 annotated protein neighbours and 8 annotated reaction neighbours back into the Broad space with query-conditioned permission weights fitted on 5,216 annotated development associations, and it only re-sorts the top 1,000 Broad candidates. Episodic memory handles positives that appear *after* the training cutoff (new curation, new assays, a collaborator's private hit) without retraining anything: each support is encoded in the frozen Broad space and combined by parameter-free attention, a_i = softmax(zscore(q^T s_i)), m_epi = sum a_i s_i, with authority scaled by support count, internal coherence and conflict with the baseline; positives already in the training graph are stripped out first to prevent double-counting with long-term memory. Atlas-COMPASS wraps this in a DeepSeek-API-driven agent that first resolves every reaction, protein and compound in a plain-language question to a Rhea / UniProt / ChEBI record and shows a confirmation card the user must approve before anything is computed, then plans the retrieval, and can additionally run thermodynamic Max-min Driving Force analysis and E. coli iML1515 genome-scale flux checks on candidate pathways when asked.","dataviz":"The visual argument is carried by the evaluation figures rather than by decoration. Benchmark comparisons are plotted as grouped MRR / Hit@10 / Hit@20 bars against EnzymeCAGE on two external test sets, with the harder Orphan-335 set shown alongside Enzyme-405 so the reader sees where the gain is largest. The four-configuration study is drawn as a controlled 2x2 (reference, coverage-only, ranker-only, complete system) in both retrieval directions, so the figure itself separates 'we found more candidates' from 'we ranked them better'. Ablation panels remove one evidence block at a time in both directions. Seed-count sensitivity is shown both as performance-versus-number-of-positive-seeds line plots under two evaluation protocols (random-positive and the much stricter protein-cluster-cold) and as grouped Hit@3/10/20 bars for one, two and three seeds. The model page also renders the architecture itself — dual-tower geometry, anchored reranking that visibly protects the base order, and query-level gating of each expert — as labelled schematics, and the five development cycles are laid out as a descending staircase so the reader walks the DBTL history. Atlas-EDGE's own interface is the other half of the dataviz: a hand-built SVG graph in which compounds are nodes and enzymes are directional edges, with composite edges that collapse multiple enzymes between two compounds and expand on click, click-through compound and enzyme info cards, zoom, drag, filtering, path highlighting and URL-synced view state.","datascience":"Evaluation is the strongest part of this project and is unusually self-sceptical. External benchmarking used the EnzymeCAGE authors' own test sets under their protocol: 21,505 training-unseen positive relationships ranked over the full encodable universe of 185,918 protein candidates (11,081 reaction candidates in the reverse direction). Reported gains: Enzyme-405 MRR +16.0% and Hit@10 +4.55pp; Orphan-335 MRR +44.0%, Hit@10 +17.32pp, Hit@20 +18.51pp. Four ablation studies follow, including a difficulty-balanced re-aggregation that freezes every prediction, candidate set and rank and changes *only* the averaging — giving equal weight to seen, protein-cold, reaction-cold and double-cold exposure classes and then to the graph-degree strata within each — explicitly to stop frequent, highly connected, easy relations dominating a micro-average. Domain experts were tested only on queries where they are applicable, with deterministic family held-out splits for P450 and phosphatase and a frozen semantic applicability gate selecting 18 of 1,042 strict double-cold queries for terpene synthases; gains were P450 MRR 0.0394 to 0.0503, phosphatase 0.3012 to 0.3077, TPS 0.1225 to 0.3114 with Hit@10 11.11% to 33.33%, candidate sets and core scores held fixed. Wet-lab closure: M5AW86 was recommended for FPP to beta-farnesene and confirmed by a converging evidence chain (differential GC-MS peak at ~5.5 min with base peak m/z 69, fragments at 41/81/93/107/121/135 and a low-abundance molecular ion at m/z 204; TLC spots absent from empty-vector controls; 1H NMR olefinic signals at delta 6.3-6.4 and 5.0-5.3 ppm matched to a literature reference). They also publish a disconfirmation: for D9XD61 the model predicted (+)-T-muurolol but the isolated product was identified as 7-epi-alpha-eudesmol, and they say so. Preliminary GC-MS hit rate was 7 of 14 recommended candidates (50%). The interface discipline matches: the 0-100 displayed model score is explicitly documented as 'not a catalytic success rate', not activity or yield, not comparable across retrieval routes, and a high rank means 'deserves priority verification, not that it has been biologically proven'; 'unknown' in a compatibility cell means missing records, not incompatibility.","software":"Both instruments are Apache 2.0 open source in the official iGEM GitLab repository, deployable with git clone plus bash scripts/bootstrap.sh, setup_release.sh --download and --check, then manage.sh configure-key / start / status. Linux with Python 3.12 is the validated environment, ~5 GiB for model assets, WSL2 for Windows, macOS explicitly flagged as not validated — and crucially no GPU is required, since validation and inference run on CPU. The DeepSeek API key is stored in a git-ignored runtime directory and the docs tell deployers to put the service behind HTTPS with access control, rate limiting, log monitoring and API quotas, and to explain how external model services handle data. Atlas-EDGE is a full production stack: React + TypeScript + Vite front end (hand-drawn SVG graph, History API URL sync, localStorage session persistence, AbortController to cancel stale searches), FastAPI + Uvicorn + Pydantic + async SQLAlchemy/aiomysql backend on a /api/v1 prefix, MySQL 8.0.46, a four-round UniProt REST crawl plus Rhea SPARQL and ChEBI flat files, pandas/RDKit ETL into entity and association tables with a unified sha1-keyed search index, local NCBI BLAST+ (blastp/makeblastdb, run in a thread with a mutex guarding database construction and an sha1 cache key), Ketcher for draw-a-molecule structure search, and DeepSolNet run locally with original weights to generate per-protein solubility scores used as a search filter.","stack":["Python 3.11 / 3.12","PyTorch (dual-tower encoders)","FastAPI","Uvicorn","Pydantic","SQLAlchemy asyncio + aiomysql","MySQL 8.0.46","React + TypeScript + Vite","pandas","RDKit","NCBI BLAST+","Ketcher","DeepSolNet","DeepSeek API","UniProt REST","Rhea SPARQL","ChEBI flat files","Pfam","cobrapy / iML1515 genome-scale model","scikit-learn / random forest / gradient boosting (experts)"],"techniques":["dual-tower contrastive retrieval","multi-positive InfoNCE-style loss","positive-unlabeled learning","sequence-cluster negative masking","hard-negative mining","listwise head-ranking hinge loss","mixture-of-experts gating with availability masks","retrieval-augmented long-term graph memory","parameter-free attention episodic memory (few-shot, training-free update)","protein language model embeddings","differential reaction fingerprints (DRFP)","learning-to-rank (LambdaMART lineage)","ablation studies","cold-start / double-cold stratified evaluation","difficulty-balanced metric re-aggregation","MRR and Hit@K ranking metrics","graph database modelling","pathway search over a directed multigraph","Max-min Driving Force thermodynamic analysis","genome-scale flux balance analysis (iML1515)","natural-language agent with entity grounding"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Nine primary-school lessons from March to July 2026 (five plant lessons, four summer science lessons), each treated as its own Design-Build-Test-Learn unit: Design fixed audience and objectives, Build made slides and materials, Test watched what pupils actually explained and where they needed help, Learn turned the observed problems into changes to the next lesson. From that they distilled SPARK as a reusable design and review lens — See, Probe, Ask, Record and Respond, Keep improving — and then applied it back onto their secondary-school intro and lab teaching and their public science conversations at Starlight Market. The handover is concrete: slides, activity materials, a lesson design checklist, guidelines for experimental and simulation activities, a feedback and evaluation form, and 'SPARK-Edu Skill', an AI-assisted guide that encodes their principles, workflow and examples so a future education team can adapt rather than restart.","ihp":"Human Practices is presented as evidence that changed the build, with a counted audit trail: 24 evidence records, 6 stakeholder lenses, 4 design shifts, 3 intended influence pathways, across academic (8), iGEM and user (4), industry (5), technology transfer (3), safety (2) and education contacts. The four shifts are stated as before/after pairs: from a black-box compatibility score to explicit experimental prioritisation; from searching known pairs to open exploration with uncertainty; from ranked names only to explainable recommendations; from a post-hoc warning to responsibility by design. The origin is traceable: in November 2025 Jing Shi pointed out that a promising enzyme record may behave differently in a new host, Fei Zhou described the preparatory work caused by scattered information, and Long Wang warned that sequence similarity is not function — which is why the platform separates recorded associations (the factual layer) from model candidates (the prediction layer) in every view. The team also took on the dual-use question directly: if an AI tool makes finding functional enzymes easier, which questions should it decline to answer lightly, how should high-risk queries be handled, and what may private data be used for.","realWorld":"The entrepreneurship case is for a screening *service* rather than another database: computational screening carried through to wet-lab validation so the customer receives a confirmed candidate, with the pipeline and its documentation left behind as a reusable toolkit and the methods and data kept public. Numbers are stated with their baselines: enzyme-to-reaction accuracy 15.32% (up from 8.47% in the team's own historical runs), a 1.92x top-20 hit rate against CLIPZyme (15.97% vs 8.33%) over 166,202 benchmark candidates, a 50% preliminary GC-MS hit rate (7 of 14), an estimated core enterprise TAM of RMB 14.42M annually in the baseline forecast, and a proposed tiered price range of RMB 4.3k to 319.5k per year from Academic to Enterprise Advanced. They are candid that the current demo only accepts target reactions and candidate proteins and returns rankings, with traceability, version management and full decision support still to come. The stated audiences are research labs (inspectable enzyme discovery), the iGEM community (reflective HP practice) and the bio-manufacturing ecosystem (governed, compatible pilots).","partners":["Nanjing University School of Life Sciences","Nanjing University School of Intelligence Science and Technology","Nanjing University School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering","Nanjing University School of Software","Vazyme","WuXi","BioSphere"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/model.avif","caption":"Atlas-BRIDGE architecture: a shared dual-tower retrieval geometry that will score any encodable protein or reaction, with query-level gated experts and dual (long-term graph plus episodic) memory applying additive corrections only inside their valid scope.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/7.avif","caption":"The Broad Retrieval dual-tower architecture for bidirectional enzyme-reaction ranking: one tower encodes protein sequence, the other the reaction transformation, trained with a multi-positive contrastive loss plus a head-ranking constraint so the top of the list — the part experiments actually test — is sharpened.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/seed-performance-lines-v2.avif","caption":"Performance as a function of the number of positive seeds, plotted under two evaluation protocols — random-positive and the much stricter protein-cluster-cold — so the reader can see how much of the apparent gain survives when close homologs of the known positives are withheld.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/9.avif","caption":"Query-level gating of the functional, structural/mechanistic, family/domain and memory experts. Each expert must emit an availability mask as well as scores, and reverts exactly to the prior ranking wherever its evidence is missing, so absent data is never read as a low score.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Make every model output carry an availability mask, so 'we have no evidence about this candidate' and 'this candidate scores badly' are never the same number — and separate the factual layer (what a database records) from the prediction layer (what your model guesses) in every table and figure you publish. Then re-aggregate your headline metric with equal weight across difficulty strata to check your result is not just the easy cases.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/compass/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/edge/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/proof/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/entrepreneurship/"],"rank":33},{"team":"ASIJ-Tokyo","id":"6338","section":"High School","country":"Japan","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/","projectTitle":"Project 茶(Sa)kure-it-all","tagline":"Yeast cocultures that produce four plant specialized metabolites from sakura and tea - sakuranetin, quercetin, EGCG and L-theanine - without the plant","summary":"ASIJ-Tokyo engineered Saccharomyces cerevisiae to make four plant specialized metabolites tied to Japanese culture: sakuranetin, quercetin, EGCG and L-theanine. Rather than one overloaded strain, they split pathways across cocultured strains at optimised ratios (7:3 for sakuranetin and quercetin, 5:3:2 for the three-strain EGCG system), with every enzyme cassette behind a GAL promoter and a constitutive pTEF1 fluorescent reporter so strain ratios can be verified before induction. Their education portfolio is unusually broad for a high-school team and explicitly gap-driven: each activity was created to fill a named gap they had identified. They contributed 34 new parts, a no-HPLC semi-quantitative screening workflow and a low-cost DIY bioreactor with full build instructions, all aimed at other resource-limited high-school teams.","awards":["Best Part Collection — Nominee (High School)","Best Education — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Nominee (Space / Foundational Advance / Software & AI / Biomanufacturing)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Stakeholder analysis structured into three domains (agricultural supply, health and consumer needs, production and commercial viability); Duke iGEM's Risk and Research Ethics Assessment tool used as an external bioethics compliance check.","dataviz":"Stakeholder map figure grouping identified issues and stakeholders into the three domains; coculture architecture diagrams.","datascience":"A semi-quantitative screening workflow designed specifically for teams without HPLC access, published as a contribution. In-silico modelling of a proposed active-transport system was explored with guidance from a Johns Hopkins biomedical engineering student, then explicitly abandoned because without HPLC the team could not measure intra- versus extracellular compound concentrations to validate it - a documented negative result.","software":"Village 茶(Sa)kura: a free Animal Crossing-style educational game built in Unity and coded in C# over two years with the Japanese technology entertainment company ABAL Inc. Players receive villager requests (for example 'bring me anti-inflammatory sakura cream'), scavenge ingredients in the Sakura Forest, read recipes at crafting stations, synthesise items in in-game labs and return them, with up to 10 encoded pathways; published free on itch.io with a game manual. Also a memory card game, a 'What is?' YouTube explainer series, and The Catalyst: Science & Curiosity, a student science news/publication website.","stack":["Unity Engine","C#","itch.io distribution","YouTube","web publishing platform (The Catalyst)","HPLC (via International Christian University)"],"techniques":["yeast coculture at optimised strain ratios","dual-plasmid selection (pRS316/URA3 + pRS315/LEU2 on SC -Ura -Leu dropout)","GAL-inducible enzyme cassettes","pTEF1 constitutive fluorescent reporter for ratio verification","modular pathway splitting across strains","DIY low-cost bioreactor build","CRISPR-Cas9 teaching lab","agarose gel electrophoresis","strawberry and banana DNA extraction"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"ASIJ-Tokyo opened its education work with a diagnostic question - who is not yet part of the synthetic biology conversation - and then built one artefact per identified gap, which makes the portfolio unusually legible. Gap: low public awareness of sakura's health properties, addressed by an Earth Day booth (22 April 2025 and 2026) where elementary, middle and high school students painted flower pots and took home sakura seeds while being briefed on anti-inflammatory and antioxidant properties, reaching 70+ participants each year, plus a Sakura & Matcha Goodies recipe book whose recipes each carry a 'the science behind' blurb. Gap: almost no synthetic biology video content pitched at high school level, addressed by a 'What is?' YouTube series running since 2023 covering bioethics, cellular transport, yeast in synthetic biology, metabolic engineering, genetic switches, cocultures, biosensors and cellular fluorescence, deliberately sequenced so concepts build on each other. Gap: little attention to memory degradation in Japan's ageing population, addressed by a memory card game run at a senior home on 13 October 2026, using six cards depicting cluttered domestic interiors with recall questions (colour of the couch, number of pencils) across repeated rounds individually and in groups of 2-3; the team was scrupulous about its limits, stating plainly that this was a small pilot and not a clinical study, and left the game with the facility and its nurses as a low-pressure cognitive exercise. Gap: no easy way for teachers outside iGEM or biology to run these activities, addressed by a free downloadable workbook of seven tested lesson plans covering DNA, natural pigment, cell modelling, chromatography, biomaterials and polymer creation, refined so that the labs connect into a holistic overview, plus The Catalyst: Science & Curiosity, a newspaper-style website that also acts as a publication platform where any secondary school student can publish science writing from 300-word pieces to 20-page experimental reports. The flagship format is Village 茶(Sa)kura, an Animal Crossing-style Unity/C# game co-developed over two years with ABAL Inc., a Japanese technology entertainment company, iterated against industry-expert feedback and published free on itch.io with a manual. Direct teaching reached a wide spread of ages: a four-year-running CRISPR-Cas9 and gel electrophoresis lab for 8th graders built into their sickle cell anaemia unit (13-14 May 2025 and 12-13 May 2026, including why researchers target the BCL11A enhancer rather than the mutation itself); DNA extraction workshops for over 30 students at Tokyo Bay International School and Montessori School Tokyo in Azabu (31 March and 22 July 2026); the same experiment with bananas for 80+ children aged 5-10 daily at the school's Summer Day Camp, deliberately reaching local and non-international-school children; a 60+ child booth with leaf-rubbing and plant-engineering discussion at Aoyama Gakuin's Global Day (21 October 2025), tied to the UN SDGs and to English-language learning; a Winterfest campus-wide CSI escape-room-style DNA mystery (4 December 2025) with three stations - DNA matching to eliminate suspects, leaf-morphology comparison to locate the crime scene, and gel electrophoresis to identify the culprit; a Community Health Fair obstacle-course race (11 April 2025) where participants acted as skin cells carrying sakuranetin past crawl-space 'constricted blood vessels' and hurdle 'blockages' on a two-minute timer; and Back to School Night in 2025 and 2026, the 2026 edition adding a hands-on flower pipetting activity and recipe-book matcha cookies for 100+ parents, students and teachers. Evidence of iteration is explicit and honest: PCR was cut from the 8th grade lab because crucial steps ran out of time, lesson plans were reworked so activities connect, pipetting was added to Back to School Night, and the team states openly that it did not systematically record feedback this year and therefore added a feedback page to the workbook so teachers who use it can report back. Collaboration was a deliberate education strategy in its own right: activities were created or joined with eight teams across eight countries (Duke, Maastricht, Manipal, Concordia, Ruhr University Bochum, St Stephen's College Hong Kong, Patras, KCIS Xiugang) plus the iGEM Community Blackboard - contributing a scent to Manipal's Scents of the World, an abstract to Maastricht's peer-reviewed MSP Vector journal, a Pokemon-style card to RUBochum's Trading Card Project, a Japanese translation of HKSSC's food-waste comic, and both of their games to Patras's downloadable game gallery.","ihp":"Four stakeholder engagements reshaped the project, each with a recorded design consequence. Tea producer Masahiro Okutomi of Sayama-cha Okutomien (10 April 2026) explained that overseas matcha orders have risen sharply while new tea plants take at least five years to mature and processing equipment three years to arrive, and that L-theanine is what gives matcha much of its quality - so extracting it from leaves for supplements would leave farmers with a degraded product. This directly strengthened the decision to produce L-theanine in yeast rather than extract it, and reframed the team's positioning from replacement to 'an extra source that works beside farms'. He also noted Japanese companies market catechins like EGCG while consumers do not know what L-theanine does, which changed the team's communication plan to explain each compound separately starting with L-theanine. Yukari Kawahito of Sakura Science Beauty (May 2026) introduced transdermal patches as a delivery format the team had not considered and opened a second business route of selling compounds to companies with existing formulation capability. Vikki Adams (Johns Hopkins) advised on an in-silico active-transport model that was ultimately dropped for lack of HPLC validation. A visit to the six-generation family-run Maruichi Mizuno Tea Farm in Shizuoka (16 June 2026) showed how the farm covers plants with sheets, harvests three times a year, processes its own hojicha and sencha and is now seeking matcha manufacturers to benefit from the boom - confirming producers already market L-theanine and EGCG benefits while demand for fermentation-derived ingredients remains untested. The visit was filmed as a vlog.","realWorld":"The project's framing is a supply constraint, not a novelty: sakura-flavoured products appear across Japanese supermarkets every spring and vanish by May because the raw material does, sakuranetin being bound to one of the shortest harvest windows in agriculture, while tea faces the opposite problem of a demand boom farms cannot meet. Contributions were deliberately designed for resource-limited high-school teams: a reusable coculture architecture where a future team can keep Strain N unchanged and only build its own downstream strain, 34 new Registry parts, a semi-quantitative screening workflow usable without HPLC, and a low-cost DIY bioreactor with full build instructions. The team hosted six students from St Mary's International School to build a biosensor, gained HPLC access through a bioanalytical chemistry professor at International Christian University, and will give an opening keynote alongside university professors at ASIJ's first semi-annual science fair on 14 November 2026 to roughly 100 students and teachers.","partners":["ABAL Inc. (Japanese technology entertainment company, two-year Unity game co-development)","Tokyo Bay International School","Montessori School Tokyo, Azabu","Aoyama Gakuin (Global Day)","International Christian University (bioanalytical chemistry professor, HPLC access)","St Mary's International School","Recolle Team Hayate (STEM Racing student team)","Sayama-cha Okutomien (tea producer Masahiro Okutomi)","Sakura Science Beauty (Yukari Kawahito)","Maruichi Mizuno Tea Farm, Shizuoka","Johns Hopkins University (Vikki Adams)","itch.io (game distribution)","Japan iGEM meet-up at Hiroo Gakuen (UTokyo, TUS, Waseda, Keio, Oakridge, HG)","iGEM teams: Duke, Maastricht, Manipal, Concordia, RUBochum, HKSSC, Patras, KCIS Xiugang"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/crafting-screenshot.avif","caption":"A crafting station in Village 茶(Sa)kura, the free Unity/C# synthetic biology game built with ABAL Inc. and published on itch.io","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/seniorhouse.avif","caption":"Running the six-card memory game with residents at a senior home, 13 October 2026","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/winterfest-igem.avif","caption":"The campus-wide CSI-themed DNA mystery stations at Winterfest, 4 December 2025","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/igem-backtoschool-2-1.avif","caption":"Back to School Night 2026: hands-on flower pipetting activity for 100+ parents, students and teachers","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/booth2.avif","caption":"The Earth Day booth, where 70+ students painted sakura flower pots and took home seeds","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/youtube-ss.avif","caption":"The 'What is?' YouTube explainer series, built to fill the gap in synthetic biology video pitched at high school level","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Start from a written list of named gaps (which audience, which missing resource) and build exactly one artefact per gap, then package the reusable ones - a seven-lesson tested workbook with a built-in feedback page, a free game, a sequenced video series - so teachers outside synthetic biology can run them without you.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/entrepreneurship"],"rank":21},{"team":"Muenster-University","id":"6339","section":"Overgrad","country":"Germany","village":"Agriculture","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/","projectTitle":"P.A.C. Immunity","tagline":"A modular platform that retrofits native plant immune sensors so a crop detects a pathogen's own protease directly and triggers an instant immune response.","summary":"P.A.C. Immunity rewires plant immunity instead of breeding for it. A plant NLR immune receptor is held inactive by a synthetic blocker peptide containing a protease cleavage site; when a pathogen secretes its effector protease into the host cell - a step many viruses, bacteria, fungi and nematodes cannot skip - the protease cleaves the blocker, the NLR fires, and the plant mounts a hypersensitive response. Because only the cleavage site changes between pathogens, swapping one short sequence retargets the whole system, which is the modularity argument: classical resistance breeding takes years and pathogens overcome new resistances quickly. The bottleneck they hit is that almost no pathogen protease has a known cleavage motif, so the dry lab built ProTEUS (Protease Target Elucidation Using Structural Modeling), a de-novo-binder-style pipeline chaining AlphaFold3, RFdiffusion3 and LigandMPNN with a mechanistically grounded geometric scoring function to generate candidate cleavage peptides for proteases with no characterised substrates at all.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best New Composite Part — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Agriculture Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"ProTEUS repurposes the de novo mini-binder design stack for an unsolved problem - predicting cleavage sites for proteases whose substrates are unknown - which existing tools (PROSPER, PROSPERous, Procleave, iProt-Sub) cannot do, since they only predict where a given substrate is cut by a protease with 30 or more already-described sites. The pipeline, named as machines on the wiki: AlphaFold3 predicts the target protease structure (AF3 machine I), since no crystal structure existed for any of RipT, RipE1, RipBN or HopN1; the structure is inspected in ChimeraX and aligned by Matchmaker against papain (PDB 9PAP) and other characterised proteases to derive catalytic triads and specificity residues, whose solvent-exposed atoms become hotspot constraints (scanner); RFdiffusion3 generates 5,000 binder backbones per target (1,000 batches of 5) with atom-level rather than residue-level hotspot conditioning and 'is_non_loopy' set to false so no helical bias blocks accessible peptides, with gamma_0 raised from 0.2 to 0.6 to reduce duplicate sequences (RFD3 machine); a Python filter rejects backbones closer than 1.7 A to the protease or farther than 8.0 A from the catalytic centre, with target-specific pass rates of 94.2% for HopN1, 51.1% for RipE1, 48.1% for RipT and 12.3% for RipBN, which they attribute to differences in pocket depth and hotspot geometry (cannon I); LigandMPNN (checkpoint ligandmpnn_v_32_010_25, seed 111, sampling temperature raised to 0.3 from 0.1 for short-sequence diversity) designs 6-10 sequences per backbone with sidechain conformations (MPNN machine); AlphaFold3 re-folds each unique designed sequence in complex with the target protease (AF3 machine II); and a Python scoring script extracts binding geometry and ranks with selected weights (cannon 2), handing the top 100 per protease to wet-lab screening (portal). Everything ran on the PALMA II HPC cluster at Munster - RFdiffusion3 on an RTX 4090 (24 GB) node at about 14 h per protease, LigandMPNN on 8 CPU cores at about 10 h, AlphaFold3 on an A100 (40 GB). Confidence handling is deliberate: they analysed pLDDT and minimum binder-target PAE rather than pTM/ipTM, which have been reported as weaker predictors for protein-peptide interactions, found mean binder pLDDT of 39.73-49.00 across the four targets, and chose explicitly not to filter hard on confidence (a pLDDT > 80 cut would keep only a handful; a pLDDT > 50 intermediate filter changed nothing in the final 100 after structural assessment), reasoning that badly predicted designs would fail the geometric assessment anyway.","dataviz":"The wiki is a single-page React application built as an explorable argument rather than a document. It opens with Gary, a farmer whose story carries the problem (30% of global yield losses to plant pathogens, pesticide treadmill, resistances overcome quickly) before any molecular biology appears, and that narrative thread was a direct result of human practices advice. The dry lab page presents the ProTEUS pipeline as a machine metaphor - scanner, RFD3 machine, cannon, MPNN machine, portal - with an animated workflow, and each of the four candidate proteases gets a card showing its AlphaFold3 prediction with per-residue pLDDT for each catalytic triad member and for the whole and core enzyme, plus the hotspot atoms chosen for diffusion. Quantitative figures include a pLDDT-versus-PAE hexbin density plot with the official AlphaFold confidence bands marked, before-and-after pLDDT bars showing what trimming disordered N-terminal secretion signals does to each protease's confidence (raising RipBN above 70), and side-by-side renders of a rejected design versus a kept one. An interactive 3Dmol viewer serves structures (including an AlphaFold3 resistosome model), Rive animations drive the cloning and engineering-cycle explanations, and inline tooltips expand every acronym. Education results are themselves visualised as radial comparison charts of pre- versus post-event survey answers.","datascience":"Their most transferable piece of work is converting enzymology into a scorable geometry, because no training data existed. Since their four targets have no known cleavage motifs, they could not fit a model; instead they derived scoring parameters from the catalytic mechanism of protein-nucleophile proteases - the nucleophile sulfur or oxygen within attack distance of the base nitrogen, the nucleophile-to-carbonyl-carbon distance, the hydrogen-bond distance from the oxyanion hole amide NH to the scissile carbonyl oxygen, the nucleophile's reactive rotamer (gauche- versus trans, which determines whether the oxyanion hole is formed by the nucleophilic residue's own amide NH or the one C-terminal to it), the near-universal angle between the nucleophile atoms and the base, and the nucleophilic attack angle, with the explicit correction that cysteine proteases take a different ideal attack angle than serine proteases because sulfur hybridises more weakly than oxygen. These were combined into a weighted score over AlphaFold3 complex geometry. They also engineered the cost down: profiling showed the jackhmmer MSA search accounted for over 86% of AlphaFold3 runtime, and because every binder in an iteration is co-folded with the same protease they cached the protease MSA and skipped MSA generation for the artificial peptides entirely (homologs of a designed peptide being meaningless), cutting time per prediction from about 13 minutes to 30 seconds and making a 5,000-30,000 prediction campaign feasible where the naive estimate was 45 to 270 days. Validation was retrospective: run on NIa-Pro, a protease with a known motif, the pipeline recovered the correct residues (E/P6, V/P4, Q/P1) among its top 100 candidates, and for the four uncharacterised proteases it pinpointed potentially important residues, with preliminary wet-lab signal for HopN1, RipBN and RipT that the team states needs a refined screening assay to confirm.","software":"ProTEUS is the team's software contribution, documented step by step on the model page with every tool version, checkpoint, seed, non-default parameter and HPC node specification stated - enough for another group to rebuild it - but no public repository link is given on the wiki. Their released artefact is instead the part collection on the iGEM Registry, which is where the Best New Composite Part came from: a modular toolkit for plant-immunity engineering comprising pathogen effector protease expression constructs (NIa-Pro from Potato Potyvirus Y with an N-terminal myc tag, plus AvrPphB, HopN1, RipT, RipE1 and RipBN from Pseudomonas syringae and Ralstonia with C-terminal myc tags), a blocker peptide that prevents NLR activity when fused to the N-terminus (experimentally demonstrated on autoactive AtNRG1.1 [D485V], BBa_26V5N8GY), graded series of protease cleavage sites for NIa-Pro, AvrRpt2 and AvrPphB with short, medium and asymmetric GS-linkers plus non-cleavable mutant negative controls, a FRET pair (mTurquoise / cpVenus173) for Nicotiana benthamiana, and synthetic BsaI, BsaI-with-internal-NdeI and PaqCI dropout segments designed so an insert can be swapped into a composite part by Golden Gate assembly - built specifically for pooled library cloning, which is what makes the collection modular 'for anyone to advance with standard cloning practices'.","stack":["AlphaFold3","RFdiffusion3","LigandMPNN / ProteinMPNN","ChimeraX (Matchmaker)","Python (filtering + geometric scoring)","jackhmmer (MSA, cached)","PALMA II HPC (NVIDIA RTX 4090, A100)","3Dmol.js","React + Vite (wiki)","Rive (animations)","iGEM Registry","Golden Gate / MoClo","Gibson assembly","Western blot and GFP degradation-tag reporter screening","Nicotiana benthamiana transient expression"],"techniques":["de novo binder design repurposed for protease substrate discovery","atom-level hotspot conditioning in structure diffusion","structural alignment to characterised homologs to locate catalytic triads without crystal structures","steric and distance filtering of backbone-only diffusion output","mechanism-derived geometric scoring in place of a trained model","reactive rotamer analysis to identify the active oxyanion hole","pLDDT and minimum binder-target PAE instead of pTM/ipTM for peptide complexes","disordered-terminus trimming before confidence assessment","MSA caching and MSA-free folding of synthetic peptides for throughput","retrospective validation on a protease with a known motif","screen-many-good-predictions rather than pick-the-best","GFP-cleavage-site-degradation-tag high-throughput plate screening","FRET-based cleavage readout in planta","Design-Build-Test-Learn cycles documented per module"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"The broadest and most systematically evaluated education programme of the five teams, and almost every activity was measured with pre- and post-activity questionnaires decided on in advance. Formats: a stand on the MS Wissenschaft science exhibition ship (a German federal research ministry project visiting 33 cities across Germany, Poland and Austria) during its Munster stop, drawing on roughly 5,000 weekend visitors, where people isolated DNA from bananas with household chemicals, built DNA double strands from 60+ gummy-bear model kits (deoxyribose, phosphate and four coloured bases) and translated their own codon to an amino acid on a take-home worksheet with a QR code back to the project; a workshop series at the LWL Museum of Natural History over the summer holidays bridging microscopic to macroscopic DNA, with agar-plate cultures cleared in writing with museum management and lab management, ending with participants designing their own Petri dishes, and follow-up feedback collected from parents rather than only participants; a protease workshop series for 8th and 9th grade school classes using digestive enzymes - ginger-juice proteases curdling milk at different temperatures, fruit juices degrading gelatine - iterated after anonymous participant feedback that the first round's instructions were 'a little hard to understand'; a bio pub quiz with the question rounds embedded in a narrative frame; 'Design Your Crop', a 3-6 player card game in which players become plant breeders guarding a hidden genome, built as a scalable substitute for events that only reach as many people as the team's time allows; a children's book featuring a farmer and a planned primary-school reading-plus-worksheet collaboration; a bioethics lecture analysing P.A.C. Immunity as a case study under the new EU NGT Directive; 'A Mosaic of Opinions', a dialogue event designed against the finding that consumer fear of gene editing in the food chain comes from campaigns plus shallow understanding; and a Fishbowl discussion in Nottuln co-organised with the Westphalia-Lippe Agricultural Association (WLV), with roughly two rounds pairing critical and supportive voices - fruit grower and pomology expert Hans-Joachim Bannier and Bioland farmers against Prof. Jochen Schmid (IMMB Munster) and the team - attended by regional farmers, Bioland farmers, association representatives, science students and the general public. They also hosted their own three-day conference, Junior Jam Munster, at the Munster castle, with expert talks, interactive workshops, poster sessions, a panel discussion and a session on iGEM judging criteria, and reported the meta-lesson from participant feedback that interactive workshops retain more than frontal ones and that Zoom talks land far weaker than in-person. The most durable piece is curricular: they developed a lesson concept on proteases for 8th and 9th grade biology, refined with teachers at a partner school and compliant with North Rhine-Westphalia's rules on genetic engineering experiments in schools; recognising that thermocyclers, electrophoresis chambers and photometers are not available in every school, they designed a lending experiment-kit concept, and they are working to shape teacher training through a university module in which master's students build and trial a lesson plan with invited school classes - aimed partly at the fifth Abitur examination subject with experimental assessment arriving in 2027-28.","ihp":"They state their principle as treating criticism as an ally and letting unanswered questions dictate which experts to approach next, and the chain is traceable. Prof. Dr. Dirk Prufer (Institute of Plant Biology and Biotechnology, Munster) validated the concept but said a project is only as strong as its ability to be explained, which is why the work up to that point - almost entirely experimental - was rebuilt around a narrative thread, and why they distilled their science communication learning into a reusable form. Public-trust interviews produced the framing that their biggest challenge is outside the lab, since without public trust there is no foundation for engineered crops in Europe. The bottleneck conversation is the most consequential: with no reliable way to identify protease cleavage motifs, they interviewed Prof. Dr. Kummel's working group and Lasse Middendorf, Michel Westermann and Michel Stevens, who told them that AlphaFold predicting a peptide in a pocket is not evidence it will be cleaved, advised a screen-many-good-predictions approach over seeking a single best prediction, recommended validating the pipeline on a protease with a known motif, and warned that AlphaFold would struggle with short peptides - all four of which became design decisions in ProTEUS, including the separate geometric scoring layer and the NIa-Pro retrospective validation. They advised against an alternative N-terminal-binding-domain NLR engineering route on feasibility grounds, which the team dropped. Verification strategy changed too: they settled on Western blotting with the cleavage site placed between two differently sized tagged proteins, and on co-infiltrating the NLR construct with the protease construct to guarantee both are expressed, judging the live-pathogen route better suited to stably transformed plants later. Industry contacts advised focusing on crops with higher production margins and identifying which chemical pesticides are likely to be restricted so the technology can be positioned as a replacement. An early farmer-focused interview changed the education strategy outright: they contacted the WLV agricultural association to reach farmers directly, and used the resulting discussion to extract farmers' specific requirements for genetic engineering, quoting farmers' own statements back as design references.","realWorld":"There is no entrepreneurship page; the real-world case is made through regulation, agronomic positioning and safety. The project is timed against the EU's new genomic techniques regulation: they tracked the European Parliament debate through the season, committed in advance to analysing the outcome's effect on their project, and published a bioethics case study assessing P.A.C. Immunity under the new NGT Directive alongside a risk analysis, noting that it remains to be seen how the rules play out in practice. Their market framing is agronomic rather than financial - pathogens cause roughly 30% of global yield losses, pesticide dependence carries economic and ecological costs, classical resistance breeding is slow and resistances are overcome quickly, so the selling point is a platform that updates by swapping one short cleavage sequence - and industry advice pushed them toward higher-margin crops and toward positioning against pesticides facing restriction. Safety is addressed as a staged transition: current work is under laboratory containment, and they state explicitly that moving toward pathogen-resistant crops in the field introduces a fundamentally different risk picture. The honest limitation is published as its own section: not all crop pathogens secrete proteases into host cells, so the platform is not universal, and ProTEUS is positioned as what would let P.A.C. Immunity expand from a few pathogens toward broad-spectrum use. The modular part collection on the Registry, plus the documented ProTEUS methodology, are the handover to whoever continues it.","partners":["Prof. Dr. Dirk Prufer, Institute of Plant Biology and Biotechnology, University of Munster","Prof. Dr. Kummel and working group, University of Munster (structural modelling)","Lasse Middendorf, Michel Westermann and Michel Stevens (modelling advisors)","Prof. Dr.-Ing. Jochen Schmid, Institute for Molecular Microbiology and Biotechnology (IMMB), University of Munster","Westphalia-Lippe Agricultural Association (WLV) - Stefan Wolfert, Michael Uckelmann, Tim Richter","Hans-Joachim Bannier (pomology expert, critical perspective)","Jorg Schulze Buschhoff (farmer)","Dr. Holger Ophoff (official oversight perspective)","LWL Museum of Natural History, Munster","MS Wissenschaft / German Federal Ministry of Research, Technology and Space","PALMA II HPC, University of Munster","Partner school and Biology Education Department, University of Munster","Junior Jam Munster participant teams; iGEM Dusseldorf Meetup; BFH"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6339/wiki/dry-lab/figures/fig3.avif","caption":"AlphaFold3 confidence for the four candidate effector proteases before and after excluding disordered N-terminal regions, averaged over 10 samples, with the share of each protein trimmed shown above each bar.","credit":"Team Muenster-University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6339/wiki/dry-lab/figures/fig4.avif","caption":"The HopN1 protease with the hotspot atoms used to steer RFdiffusion3, shown as ribbon and surface, next to a rejected design filtered out by the Python geometry check and one kept for sequence design.","credit":"Team Muenster-University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6339/wiki/education/cardgame/dsc9496-gro.avif","caption":"'Design Your Crop', the 3-6 player card game in which players become plant breeders guarding a hidden genome, built so the team's engagement could scale past events limited by their own time.","credit":"Team Muenster-University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6339/wiki/education/fish-bowl-discussion/dsc05178.avif","caption":"The Fishbowl discussion in Nottuln, co-organised with the Westphalia-Lippe Agricultural Association, bringing regional farmers, Bioland farmers, association representatives and researchers into the same conversation about green genetic engineering.","credit":"Team Muenster-University, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Two things are directly portable. First, when no training data exists, derive a scoring function from the mechanism instead of waiting for a dataset: Munster turned textbook protease enzymology - nucleophile-to-base distance, oxyanion hole hydrogen bonds, reactive rotamer, nucleophilic attack angle, with the serine-versus-cysteine correction - into a weighted score over predicted structures, and validated it by recovering a known motif. Second, copy their education evaluation habit: decide before each event how you will check whether the learning objective was met, run a short pre- and post-questionnaire, and publish the result including what failed - and copy the curricular endgame, where a workshop becomes a lesson plan for local schools plus a lending kit for schools without thermocyclers, which is how one season's outreach keeps running after the team disbands.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/","https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/experiments/","https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/contribution/","https://2026.igem.wiki/muenster-university/safety-and-security/"],"rank":117},{"team":"KAUST-KSA","id":"6341","section":"Overgrad","country":"Saudi Arabia","village":"Space","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/","projectTitle":"SHIHAB","tagline":"SHIHAB (shooting star): resilient microbial chassis and a suitcase bioreactor for on-demand therapeutic protein manufacturing in space","summary":"Stocking an inventory of medicines for long-duration missions costs payload mass, so SHIHAB makes the medicine instead: a small library of lyophilised engineered microbes ('pills'), each producing a different nanobody, plus a compact automated bioreactor and purification train that an untrained astronaut can run. The wet lab integrated Dsup, the tardigrade damage-suppressor protein, genomically into three chassis (Bacillus subtilis, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, Pichia pastoris) and quantified radiation resistance against UV-C and X-ray doses with four complementary readouts, then expressed and purified a payload protein by HisTrap. The dry lab answered which host, what feedstock and when to induce using genome-scale metabolic models plus an ODE layer, and COMSOL CFD decided the rotor geometry for mixing under Mars, Moon and transit gravity. It won Best Hardware and Best Space Project; the whole platform was taken out of the lab and run on desert dunes to prove the 'anywhere' claim.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Measurement — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Hardware — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Space Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Flux balance analysis on three published genome-scale reconstructions (Yeast9 for S. cerevisiae, iBsu1147 for B. subtilis, iMS837 for Synechococcus elongatus PCC 7942, itself built on iJB792), plus a dynamic ODE layer integrated with scipy.integrate.solve_ivp (RK45) because FBA assumes steady state and cannot answer a timing question. Shared constraints are stated: glucose exchange 5, glycerol 0, oxygen 10 (aerobic) or 0 (anaerobic) mmol/gDW/h, nitrate capped at 10 for anaerobic Bacillus, habitat-analogue light at 145 mmol photons/gDW/h for the cyanobacterium, maximum nanobody flux computed with no growth requirement. The team repaired iBsu1147 by adding two reactions (NadB L-aspartate oxidase for de novo NAD+ synthesis and PyrD/PyrK dihydroorotate dehydrogenase for orotate), each justified with in-vitro literature evidence, because the published model failed without oxygen. Results: under oxygen restriction B. subtilis out-produces yeast roughly 3.4-fold on nanobody flux provided nitrate is supplied, and loses 46% of growth capacity against yeast's 82%; an engineered cyanobacterial supplier raises anaerobic yeast growth 13-fold (0.014 to 0.18 /h) once its sucrose export (0.81 mmol/gDW/h, tripled by capping the supplier's own growth) and oxygen (up to 4.2 mmol/gDW/h) are passed in as uptake bounds; the supplier is nitrogen-limited rather than carbon- or light-limited, and candidate gene knockouts do nothing. The ODE layer returns a closed-form optimal induction window of 6.5 to 7 h. Hardware modelling is COMSOL Multiphysics CFD comparing three rotor geometries for bubble fate, air distribution and vortex depth at 50-400 rpm across Earth, Mars, Moon and transit gravity.","dataviz":"The dry-lab page is an interactive model map: a scheme of the three chassis plus the cyanobacterial supplier where every label is a clickable result, and a 'TRY ME' prompt tells the reader to select a label to open that finding and jump to the section that develops it. Each result is also stated as a one-line claim before any plot ('Induction window: 6.5 to 7 h', 'Knockouts do nothing', 'Capping growth triples sucrose export'), with headline deltas as large-type stat tiles (3.4x, -46% vs -82%, 13x, 2 reactions). Table 1 is the most unusual piece of visualisation discipline on any of these wikis: for every link between models it states what is actually passed between them and labels the link Modeled, Identified or Assumed, so the reader can see exactly where the chain is evidence and where it is an assumption. The hardware page carries an animated 3D walkthrough of the purification sequence in its carry case, labelled 'illustrative flow, not measured data', and a live simulation of the bioreactor's touchscreen dashboard where the reader can drag the motor-speed dial and change temperature and pH setpoints. CFD results are shown as four-gravity comparison panels. All stat tiles and dashboards are reader-operable rather than screenshots.","datascience":"Measurement was designed as a framework rather than a set of assays: wild-type and Dsup-expressing B. subtilis, S. cerevisiae and P. pastoris were exposed to UV-C (0-30 mJ/cm2, and 0-300 J/m2 series) or X-rays (mock to 2000 Gy in 200 Gy steps) and read out four complementary ways (serial-dilution spot assay, OD600 time course, resazurin dye-reduction and FUN-1 staining), so a resistance claim has to survive four independent readouts rather than one. Three Dsup integration strains per host were compared against wild type and a nanobody control. On the engineering side the team derived a calibration mapping the analog voltage of a DFRobot SEN0189 turbidity sensor to NTU and then a linear OD600 = a(NTU) + b fit against reference spectrophotometer readings, following Fan et al. (2016) on Bacillus coagulans, which is what makes automatic IPTG induction at OD600 0.5 possible with no human in the loop. The modelling output is explicitly handed to the bench: a 'For the Wet Lab' box tells the experimenters where to start the co-culture and what growth cap to impose on the cyanobacteria.","software":"No standalone released application, but the code is published: ODE models and GEM notebooks are linked from the model page, and the whole wiki plus hardware design lives at gitlab.igem.org/2026/kaust-ksa under CC BY 4.0. The hardware itself is the deliverable and is documented for reproduction: an Arduino Mega control stack with a touchscreen dashboard in a compact enclosure, closed-loop temperature (DS18B20 probe to heating elements), pH (DANOPLUS KIB-87 probe with signal-conditioning board to acid/base dispensing) and OD (turbidity sensor to IPTG induction), hollow structural supports routing all wiring, a filtration unit with user-selectable stages of decreasing pore size in gasketed modules (Kapton, paper, Acrodisc), syringe-pump buffer handling and affinity chromatography, all released with a Hardware User Manual, a Radiation Resistance Testing Handbook and a Protein Expression and AKTA Purification Handbook.","stack":["COBRA-style FBA on Yeast9, iBsu1147, iMS837 (iJB792 lineage)","scipy.integrate.solve_ivp (RK45)","Python / Jupyter notebooks","COMSOL Multiphysics (CFD)","Arduino Mega","DS18B20 temperature probe","DANOPLUS KIB-87 pH probe","DFRobot SEN0189 turbidity sensor","3D printing / CAD","Xstrahl X-ray irradiator","AKTA / HisTrap affinity chromatography","GitLab","React (wiki)"],"techniques":["genome-scale metabolic modelling and model curation (gap repair with literature-backed reactions)","anaerobic and oxygen-sweep constraint analysis","co-culture modelling by passing computed fluxes as uptake bounds","sequential nutrient co-limitation cascade","growth versus product-secretion trade-off (Pareto) analysis","in-silico knockout screening","ODE dynamic induction-timing optimisation with closed-form optimum","computational fluid dynamics across multiple gravity regimes","sensor calibration to OD600 for closed-loop autoinduction","four-readout radiation resistance characterisation","genomic integration of Dsup","Comet assay for DNA damage"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Engagement was built around a stated regional gap: synthetic biology is largely absent from secondary curricula in Saudi Arabia and the wider MENA region, and almost no learning resources exist in Arabic. The flagship is the Tawasol Initiative, a network uniting MENA iGEM teams through film, podcasts and social media specifically to reach Arabic speakers, run alongside a Tawasol Hackathon; it extended to a joint programme with NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM (28-30 August) and reached as far as Peru and Kazakhstan. Programmes are differentiated by audience: the Thuwal Science Pioneers Program brought middle and high school students onto the KAUST campus for a lecture with a before-and-after knowledge check, a 'design your own microbe to help astronauts' activity, tours of the Nanofabrication and Robotics core labs, and lunch with the team; a trip to Zahrat Al Sahra International School 80 km south reached 58 middle schoolers; a KAUST Summer Mentorship Program is paired with Oboor (Arabic for 'transition'), a university-adaptation programme for Saudi students built with a counselling-psychology researcher at Umm Al-Qura University; a farmer-facing course trains youth volunteers who then teach rural farmers; and family-facing 'Copy-Paste Plants' booths and science movie nights ran for the wider community, with just over two thirds of the film audience staying for the discussion. One standout artefact is a bilingual illustrated dialogue between two young Peruvian students working through whether a CRISPR edit of a plant's own gene is a Living Modified Organism under Peru's Law 31.111 moratorium or exempt under Ministerial Resolution D000068-2026-MINAM-DM of April 2026, a genuinely difficult regulatory distinction turned into a readable comic.","ihp":"Over 30 activities and 15 named conversations, and the wiki is explicit that seven parts of SHIHAB changed, with an interactive map letting the reader pick either a person or a component. The team built their own ORBIT framework (Observe, Reach, Build, Iterate, Translate) and four values (autonomy, adaptability, responsibility, accessibility). Documented changes: Dr. Christopher E. Mason's feedback made Dsup one of the protective genes in the chassis-engineering strategy and added the Comet assay to the experimental workflow; Konstantinos Vergos and Dr. Akshay Chandrashekhar converged on microgravity as a mandatory part of validation, which is why the COMSOL simulations exist (as a precursor to parabolic flight); Sarah Al Harbi's point about crew supervision load produced the dry-lab monitoring dashboard and shaped the hardware; KAUST Health, Safety and Environment directly wrote the structure of the Safety Handbook (hazard identification, risk assessment, containment failure, accidental exposure, environmental release, emergency response) and shaped the Hardware manual; a regulatory expert pushed them to prioritise therapeutics by the conditions astronauts actually face on specific missions rather than by ease of production, which is how Spaceflight-Associated Neuro-ocular Syndrome and carcinogenesis entered the payload list; and conversations with humanitarian responders Rachel Kessler and Jonathan Grant replaced a generic 'disaster zone' use case with a stated principle, design for a specific context, not an abstract disaster zone. The project also started on oxygen production and moved to therapeutics after team and PI discussion, with the earlier adviser's emphasis on automation, monitoring, product quality and space-relevant validation carried over. Astronaut Rayyanah Barnawi and Starr Corbin informed the astronaut-facing requirements; the Tawasol Hackathon surfaced three governance priorities (remote quality assurance, operational accountability, healthcare integration).","realWorld":"Two application contexts are carried in parallel and the second was added because of stakeholder input: space missions, and remote or disaster-affected settings with disrupted medicine supply chains, each treated as a specific deployment context rather than one generic 'remote healthcare' market. The continuation plan is staged as a roadmap, beginning with demonstrating small-scale biomanufacturing in Low Earth Orbit and evaluating production under microgravity and space radiation, with a long-term pathway developed out of conversations with Savi Glowe and industry contacts (Deep Space Biology, BIOASTRA). Practical adoptability is the strongest part of the case: the only manual step is loading the pill, the platform is designed for low power and low payload mass, and it is documented well enough for a non-specialist to run from the Hardware User Manual, which the team tested by setting the bioreactor and fluid-dispensing system up on a folding table in the desert dunes, far from any bench.","partners":["Rayyanah Barnawi (Saudi astronaut)","Starr Corbin","Dr. Christopher E. Mason, Weill Cornell Medicine","Prof. Alexandre Rosado, KAUST","Dr. Luis Zea","Konstantinos Vergos and Dr. Akshay Chandrashekhar","Kimberly Washington, Deep Space Biology","BIOASTRA","Savi Glowe","KAUST Health, Safety and Environment","Rachel Kessler and Jonathan Grant (humanitarian response)","NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM","Tawasol Initiative (MENA iGEM network)","Zahrat Al Sahra International School, Jeddah","Thuwal community schools","Renad Almalki, Umm Al-Qura University (Oboor programme)","StruBE Lab, KAUST"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6341/wiki/content/hardware/cfd-prototype-3.webp","caption":"COMSOL Multiphysics CFD of the third rotor concept (three rows of small tilted blades) at 200 rpm, comparing bubble fate under Earth, Mars, Moon and transit gravity: the simulation that selected the final rotor geometry.","credit":"Team KAUST-KSA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6341/wiki/content/hardware/final-bioreactor.avif","caption":"The built SHIHAB bioreactor: glass vessel with 3D-printed lid and rotor between two hollow supports carrying all wiring, heating band around the vessel, and the Arduino-driven touchscreen dashboard in the base.","credit":"Team KAUST-KSA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6341/wiki/content/hardware/desert-pic-1.avif","caption":"SHIHAB tested on desert dunes with a folding table and a laptop, putting the 'operable anywhere' claim to a field test rather than asserting it.","credit":"Team KAUST-KSA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6341/wiki/content/public-engagement/copy-paste-plants-activity.avif","caption":"The Copy-Paste Plants activity at a family-facing community booth, part of an engagement programme built around the near-absence of synthetic biology from MENA school curricula and of Arabic-language resources.","credit":"Team KAUST-KSA, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Steal Table 1: for every link between two models, write down what is actually passed and label it Modeled, Identified or Assumed. It costs one table and it immunises a student modelling chain against the usual criticism that the pieces were never really connected. Pair that with KAUST's four-readout rule (no resistance or phenotype claim unless it survives several independent assays) and the habit of ending each model section with a 'For the Wet Lab' instruction, so the dry lab visibly changes what gets done at the bench.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/science-communication","https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/kaust-ksa/safety-and-security"],"rank":143},{"team":"Marburg","id":"6350","section":"Overgrad","country":"Germany","village":"Alternative Platform","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/","projectTitle":"CLiPPR - Chloroplast Library for inducible PPRs","tagline":"Making the chloroplast switchable: inducible designer PPR proteins paired with synthetic 5'UTRs","summary":"Chloroplasts are arguably the cheapest protein factories available (around 90% of global primary production, up to 70% of a plant cell's protein as transgenic product, linear rather than capital-intensive scaling), but they lack the one tool microbial hosts have: inducibility, so growth and production compete permanently and a toxic product means no plant at all. CLiPPR outsources that inducibility to the nucleus: nuclear-encoded, inducibly expressed synthetic pentatricopeptide repeat proteins are imported into the plastid, where they bind matched synthetic 5'UTRs placed upstream of a chloroplast gene and switch translation up or down by three mechanisms (blocking the start codon, protecting an unstable 5'UTR from exonucleases, or resolving a hairpin over the ribosome binding site). The team designed 30 fully synthetic 5'UTRs de novo, produced, purified and tested 19 new-to-nature PPR proteins, reported the highest transgene expression yet recorded in a chloroplast in vitro, and validated across three systems of rising complexity: chloroplast cell-free extract, Chlamydomonas reinhardtii, and Nicotiana benthamiana. It won Best Alternative Platform and was nominated for Part Collection, Education, Presentation, Wiki and Foundational Advance.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Part Collection — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Alternative Platform — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Education — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Foundational Advance Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"The computational work is sequence and assembly engineering rather than structure prediction, and it is tightly coupled to a hard constraint: PPRs are long tandem repeats, and the high GC content of the C. reinhardtii nuclear genome makes conventional gene synthesis of those repetitive stretches expensive or impossible, which is what forced an organism-specific toolbox instead of ordering full coding sequences. Adapting the published GRASP Golden Gate toolbox (Dennis et al. 2025, consensus S-type repeats with a programmable PPR code, 42 level-1 modules assembling 9-, 14- and 19-nt binders) from E. coli to Chlamydomonas meant codon-optimising every module, which shifts the Golden Gate fusion sites and therefore changes assembly fidelity and module boundaries, a set of interdependent decisions the team first worked through by hand. They placed the new fusion sites inside the ARELF motif (the most variable codons in the repeat, giving the largest overhang pool), pulled C. reinhardtii codon usage from the Kazusa table, excluded every codon below 50% relative abundance among the most abundantly expressed proteins, searched the remaining ARELF translations for overhang sets, and scored candidates with the NEB ligase fidelity calculator against a target above 75%, first running the original GRASP level 0 and level 1 overhangs through the same calculator to get a baseline on the same scale rather than comparing a prediction against a published measurement. B- and D-modules were intronized per Baier et al., offered with and without the LHCBM1i2 intron. Binding was validated biophysically by fluorescence polarisation to extract dissociation constants, with one optimised PPR reaching a Kd among the best in the literature.","dataviz":"The wiki is unusually honest and unusually readable. Every page carries a per-page inventory in the subtitle ('7 sections / 17 pictures / 10 references'), a key-achievements list before any narrative, and a scroll progress indicator themed as a PPR walking along its binding sites ('PPR Bound Site 1 / 8'), with a Reinhardt mascot reacting to the content. Negative results are given the same prominence as positive ones: the XRN-1 titration and pair screening are reported as showing the nuclease is barely active under the chosen conditions, with the reason stated (room temperature and a buffer chosen to keep the chloroplast extract working rather than to keep the nuclease happy), followed by the redesigned assay that removed the extract and the translation step entirely; the semi-automated purification is reported as robust and reproducible but too low-yielding, with a recommendation about which other teams it would still suit. The Chlamydomonas screen reports that only a handful of mNeonGreen-positive transformants were usable because of their transplastomic background. The software is published as form-driven Colab notebooks with a 'click to explore our tools' interface, and the codon-usage comparison is given as a full 64-codon table with per-codon percentage-point differences between C. reinhardtii and E. coli rather than a summary statistic.","datascience":"The characterisation campaign is a throughput argument. Building and testing a library of this size by hand was not feasible, so three automation technologies were made integral to the experimental design rather than bolted on. An Echo 525 acoustic liquid handler, programmed with the PyEcho script, assembled all large assays (the largest replacing 868 final pipetting steps per replicate) and, by dispensing 25 nl droplets, allowed reaction volumes to be cut to a quarter of the 10 ul manual minimum, which both saved purified PPR protein and removed the pipetting inaccuracy inherent in sub-0.5 ul manual additions; planning the plate layout in software also made reagent demand calculable in advance (including when to buy expensive XRN-1), made pipetting-scheme errors catchable before they happened, and cut run-to-run variance because a 384-well plate is assembled in under five minutes rather than over an hour of reagents sitting out of the freezer. The BMG Clariostar autoinjector dispensed furimazine for the NanoLuc reporter at an identical incubation time every run, measurably lowering standard deviation against manual addition. A 96-well semi-automated protein purification workflow adapted from the Baker Lab protocol combined IMAC and automated size-exclusion chromatography so purification and characterisation happened in one run.","software":"PPR-opt, released as two form-driven Google Colab notebooks that run entirely remotely with no local install: PPR-opt Designer builds a PPR for a single target RNA sequence in a chosen host organism, and PPR-opt Inventory generates a whole GRASP-style toolbox able to clone every possible PPR for that organism. It automates the process the team first did manually: codon-optimising each PPR module for the target organism and redistributing the Golden Gate module boundaries accordingly. The team states plainly that all outputs are software predictions not yet experimentally validated, and that so far one PPR from the resulting Chlamy-GRASP toolbox has been successfully transformed into the C. reinhardtii nucleus. Alongside it they published the first PPR parts collection in the iGEM Registry: matched sets of synthetic 5'UTRs and their corresponding PPR proteins with sequence information, functional descriptions and characterisation data, plus the Chlamy-GRASP level 0 and level 1 modules; the wiki repository is gitlab.igem.org/2026/marburg.","stack":["Google Colab (form-driven notebooks)","GRASP Golden Gate PPR toolbox (Dennis et al. 2025)","Kazusa codon usage database","NEB ligase fidelity calculator","PyEcho (HN-lab)","Beckman Coulter Labcyte Echo 525 acoustic liquid handler","BMG Clariostar plate reader with autoinjector","automated size-exclusion chromatography / IMAC in 96-well format","NanoLuc / firefly luciferase / mNeonGreen reporters","fluorescence polarisation assay","iGEM Registry"],"techniques":["codon optimisation with fusion-site redistribution","Golden Gate overhang selection and fidelity prediction against a same-scale baseline","intronization for expression in C. reinhardtii","de novo synthetic 5'UTR design (30 UTRs)","three orthogonal PPR regulation mechanisms (blocking, exonuclease protection, hairpin resolution)","chloroplast cell-free extract screening","dissociation-constant determination by fluorescence polarisation","acoustic nanolitre liquid handling for 384-well assay assembly","automated substrate injection for reproducible luminescence","semi-automated parallel protein expression and purification","nuclear inducible/repressible promoter construction (estradiol, IPTG, rapamycin, MetE, THI4)"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Marburg's education work is explicitly meta: they did not just build activities, they studied how to build them, after their first Girls' Day event prompted the question of what happens once an event is over. They reviewed the literature, consulted 15 experts across education, science communication and evaluation research, and found that existing iGEM documentation covers how outreach was organised but almost never how to ground it in theory or evaluate it beyond whether participants enjoyed it. Four outputs followed. A curriculum-aligned Teaching Toolbox ('Die CLiPPR Toolbox'), co-designed with education experts and aligned to the four German KMK national competency domains, tested with seven teachers at three schools and revised on their feedback. Plasmid Panic, an escape game playable by anyone over 12 with no scientific background, distributed through schools and with iGEM Patras in English and German. A SciComm and Evaluation Guide written specifically for future iGEM teams, covering how to set goals, pick evaluation methods and feed findings back. And a documented Evidence and Feedback process, published so others can see not only what was made but why, what changed and what was learned. Three expert quotations frame the whole approach: 'Reach is not impact, and satisfaction is not learning' (Dorothee Menhart), and 'Why are we communicating, and why should people care?' (Dr. Sascha Vogel).","ihp":"45 stakeholders, organised by the six application domains the technology touches (scientific impact, agriculture, space, molecular farming, environment and sustainability, law and regulations) rather than by job title, and the design changes are specific and attributed. Dr. Silvia Ramundo rejected the team's original plan to base the 'resolving' 5'UTR on Prikyl et al., where the hairpin the PPR resolves also contains the ribosome binding site, and recommended a design in which the PPR site and the RBS are locally disconnected so the PPR cannot interfere with ribosome binding; the team adapted to the Rojas et al. 2019 binding mechanism as a result. Prof. Ralph Bock (Max Planck Institute of Molecular Plant Physiology, a pioneer of chloroplast transformation) strongly advised dropping variable RBS testing and sticking to his validated Shine-Dalgarno sequence for predictable chloroplast translation, which they did. Prof. Alison Smith's input led them to combine two well-established repressible parts, the MetE and THI4 promoters, in one construct specifically to minimise leakiness, after Dr. Reimo Zoschke identified leakiness as the real unsolved problem ('what remains problematic are tight systems'). Tania Ryzhkova and another adviser steered characterisation away from an EMSA toward a fluorescence polarisation assay and then advised on RNase-free practice, labelled-RNA design, protein concentration ranges and controls, and on the analysis. An interview with an explosives-degradation expert made them realise phytoremediation bottlenecks come from pollutant kinetics and bioavailability rather than chloroplast gene timing, so they dropped that application. Prof. Yuri Gleba supplied the economic case (recombinant protein from plants at around 100 USD per kilogram; plants scale linearly while fermentation requires buying the next-size bioreactor), and Elena Lesch and Farley Kwok advised on testing in the 2021 Marburg chloroplast cell-free extracts rather than only E. coli, warning that such extracts are far less common than standard E. coli systems. On the education side, Prof. Malte Schwinger (educational psychology, Marburg) told them to use validated Likert-scale instruments instead of writing their own, and one evaluation expert advised against factual recall as the primary measure for the Teaching Toolbox.","realWorld":"The team frames CLiPPR as enabling infrastructure for molecular farming rather than a product, and is candid that it is one of several missing pieces: chloroplast transformation is still not established in many plant species, selecting successful transformants is often as hard as making them, and the absence of post-translational processing limits which proteins can be made there. Fourteen molecular-farming stakeholders were interviewed on what integration would actually look like, covering compound classes, the advantages of stable transformants, inducer choice, production setup and regulation; Jess Bunchek and Dr. Don Stewart established that higher-plant molecular farming would realistically run in contained greenhouses rather than open fields. Containment is also a design argument, since in most higher plants the chloroplast is maternally inherited, so transgenes are not spread by pollen. Light-based induction via optogenetics was investigated with Lars Broker (PULSE system) because it costs nothing, leaves no residue, and is already applied by drone in modern agriculture, and is held as the most attractive long-term inducer from a safety standpoint. The five remaining application domains (agriculture, space, sustainability, law and regulation, scientific impact) each have their own analysis page, and the parts collection is the handover mechanism: documented, Registry-deposited, and intended for other teams doing organelle engineering and RNA-level regulation in plants.","partners":["Prof. Dr. Yuri Gleba (plant molecular farming)","Dr. Reimo Zoschke (chloroplast gene expression)","Prof. Dr. Ralph Bock, Max Planck Institute of Molecular Plant Physiology","Dr. Silvia Ramundo","Prof. Alison Smith","Prof. Dr. Ian Small / GRASP toolbox authors","Tania Ryzhkova, Philipps-Universitat Marburg","Elena Lesch and Farley Kwok","Lars Broker (PULSE optogenetics)","Jess Bunchek and Dr. Don Stewart (molecular farming in higher plants)","Prof. Dr. Malte Schwinger, Educational Psychology, Philipps-Universitat Marburg","Nina Miller (science communicator)","Monica Dechene, Munich Science Communication Lab","Dr. Sascha Vogel","Dorothee Menhart","Seven teachers across three schools (Teaching Toolbox co-design)","iGEM Patras (escape game distribution)","iGEM Marburg 2021 (chloroplast cell-free extract)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6350/wiki/src/contents/project/alternative-platform/ccfs-utr-screening-1280.avif","caption":"Screening the library of synthetic 5'UTR and designer PPR pairs in chloroplast cell-free extract, the lowest-complexity of the three test systems; one of these 30 de novo UTRs gave the highest transgene expression yet reported in a chloroplast in vitro.","credit":"Team Marburg, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6350/wiki/src/contents/project/alternative-platform/chlamy-ppr107-microscopy-1280.avif","caption":"Microscopy of PPR107 in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii, confirming that a nuclear-encoded designer PPR is imported into the chloroplast, the step the whole 'outsource inducibility to the nucleus' design depends on.","credit":"Team Marburg, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6350/wiki/src/contents/lab/ppropt/automation/sapp-plates-1024-1280.avif","caption":"Stacked plates for semi-automated protein purification in 96-well format: a fritted Ni-NTA plate, a filter plate and a flat-bottom plate assembled for centrifugation, adapted from the Baker Lab protocol for scaled characterisation of designed proteins.","credit":"Team Marburg, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6350/wiki/src/contents/education/escape-game/escape-game-cover-1280.avif","caption":"Plasmid Panic, the team's escape game, redesigned after school visits showed students were far more curious about what daily lab life looks like than about the science content; released in English and German with multiple difficulty levels.","credit":"Team Marburg, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Marburg's most transferable asset is the SciComm and Evaluation Guide mindset: decide the intended outcome first, use validated Likert instruments rather than home-made ones, and never accept attendance or enjoyment as evidence of learning ('reach is not impact, and satisfaction is not learning'). On the dry-lab side, copy their automation-as-design-decision argument: an acoustic liquid handler plus a planned plate layout does not just save pipetting, it shrinks reaction volumes fourfold, makes reagent budgets predictable in advance, and cuts variance, which is exactly what lets a small team characterise a real library instead of three constructs.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/project/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/alternative-platform","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/lab/automation","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/human-practices/molecular-farming","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/education/escape-game","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/project/parts-collection","https://2026.igem.wiki/marburg/contribution"],"rank":131},{"team":"Peking","id":"6366","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/","projectTitle":"TermiTox — engineered yeast baits for species-specific termite colony control","tagline":"Two engineered yeast strains, modelled from molecular dynamics to whole-colony collapse, to protect wooden heritage buildings without broad-spectrum pesticide.","summary":"Peking built a genuinely three-scale model of a termite bait. Strain A delivers a pH-gated listeriolysin O mutant that lyses the hindgut protozoa a termite depends on for cellulose digestion; Strain B releases hairpin RNA under hindgut hypoxia to silence RsEG (a termite cellulase) and Hexamerin-1 (a caste-development regulator). The Molecular Model used all-atom MD to verify the H311A-LLO safety premise, the Individual Model is a cohort-ODE system calibrated by MAP fitting against quantitative data extracted from 43 papers, and the Colony Model is a spatially explicit agent-based stochastic simulation of trophallaxis through a two-layer nest network with caste structure. The colony model produced a non-obvious and testable deployment schedule that the team then designed hardware for, plus a dose and collapse-time estimate anchored to published hexaflumuron field trials.","awards":["Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Safety and Security Award — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Conservation Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three models at three scales, each answering a different design question, and the output of each feeds the next. (1) MOLECULAR — all-atom molecular dynamics in GROMACS-style protocol on LLO (PDB 4CDB, residues 39-526) to test whether the H311A mutant is really pH-gated. PROPKA3 predicted pKa for all 141 titratable residues, identifying E247 and H311 as the residues whose protonation flips between pH 5.5 and 7.4; ten systems were built (four solution, two membrane, four crystal-contact dimers from ChimeraX packing) in CHARMM36-jul2022 with TIP3P water, 310 K V-rescale thermostat, 1 atm C-rescale barostat with semi-isotropic coupling for the membranes, 2 fs timestep, 1.2 nm non-bonded cutoff, LINCS on H-bonds, through minimisation, NVT, NPT and production. The result is quantitative: under acidic conditions the H311A acidic triad (D208/E247/D320) tightens to a D208-D320 distance of 3.94 Angstrom, while at neutral pH the distances open to 6.05-6.20 Angstrom and the salt bridges collapse, and in the membrane systems the protein sits 29 Angstrom from the phosphorus plane at pH 5.5 but drifts to 58 Angstrom at pH 7.4 by t = 20.5 ns. H311A is 17-18 Angstrom from the triad and never touches it, so the mechanism claimed is allosteric. (2) INDIVIDUAL — a cohort-structured ODE model. Because repeated bait feeding and in-gut yeast division create cells of different ages, yeast is divided into cohorts, each with its own hindgut-age clock; founder cells arrive with preloaded payload Q_0 while hindgut-born cells start empty and must build payload. All A and B cohorts share one logistic hindgut carrying capacity, b_s = r_s - [r_s]_+ Y_total/K_Y. A DAN1-calibrated hypoxia clock a_D(a) = l_D + (phi_H - l_D) G_2(a; tau_D) gates payload expression and Strain B's release module. Strain A uptake uses a delayed waiting function and saturates as protozoa are depleted, with a per-yeast kill probability p = 1 - exp(-Psi_A q), and because protozoan enzymes outlive the protozoa, digestive capacity follows through a first-order lag dE_P/dt = (ln2/T_EP)(F_P - E_P). Strain B's hairpin RNA feeds a hindgut pool with first-order decay, and the gut-pool-to-knockdown step is a convolution with an empirical time kernel fitted from oral RNAi studies, X_j(t) = A_j * integral u_j(s) K_j(t-s) ds with M_j = exp(-X_j); mRNA then drives protein pools through further lags, and total digestive function is a weighted sum D_E = w_R + w_P E_P + w_C C_H. (3) COLONY — a spatially explicit, stochastic agent-based model. The nest is a two-layer network: a macro graph linking the royal chamber, six wood modules and one or two bait modules by tunnels, with each module containing a micro branching tunnel network generated by a stochastic excavation process adapted from Lee and Lee's agent-based termite tunnelling model and converted to a continuous centreline graph. Colony members are coarse-grained weighted agents (2,000 worker agents at 250 workers each, 300 larval agents at 125 each, one explicit king and 50 explicit queens) with resolution convergence checked at 1,000/150, 2,000/300 and 4,000/600. Workers run a three-state FORAGE/RETURN/DISTRIBUTE behavioural loop; when two meet, trophallaxis fires with probability P_ij = p_max * normalised_local_density * |F_i - F_j| and half the food-load difference transfers, with a one-relay-only rule for food en route to the royal chamber. Each termite tracks food load, transferable A and B payload and accumulated damage, with worker damage Delta_D = alpha_A Q_A + alpha_B Q_B + gamma_AB * 2 Q_A Q_B/(Q_A + Q_B + eps) — a harmonic-mean synergy term that only fires when both payloads are active in the same insect, with coefficients ordered alpha_A > alpha_B > gamma_AB from the individual model. Strain A damage is assigned only to workers on the biological ground that queens and larvae lack the worker hindgut protist system. The model deliberately rejects a conventional SIR epidemic formulation because nest space and caste direction are what make trophallactic spread non-uniform.","dataviz":"The headline is interactivity that is genuinely explanatory rather than ornamental: an embedded individual-model explorer where the reader sets total dose and treatment duration, presses Update model, and then drags a timeline through 42 days watching a labelled flow diagram (INPUT, YEAST, PAYLOAD, ACTION, TARGETS, HOST) light up — clicking any element in the diagram highlights that quantity's trajectory. The colony model ships six embedded simulation videos selectable by schedule (nest construction, worker movement, A-only, B-only, A+B mixed, best schedule) with a draggable progress bar and playback speed, and one video follows a single tracked worker's food load, A payload, B payload and damage in an inset as it feeds, transfers and dies. Both explorers degrade gracefully, with the page explicitly stating that the static figures below show the same modelled outcomes when JavaScript is off. The spatial figures are the best static work: Figures 8 and 9 are matrices with treatment days 10-120 as columns and worker cohorts grouped by home module as rows, showing ever-exposed fraction, payload burden in a no-damage tracer run, and functional-worker fraction — making the distance-to-bait gradient and the spatial refuges immediately visible, then showing them disappear when a second bait station is added. Stochastic outputs are reported as three-seed medians with Q25-Q75 shading, and every caption lists the parameter values used (s = 1.40, home/adjacent/global target probabilities 0.65/0.20/0.15, global bait weights 4/2/1).","datascience":"This is a parameter-provenance project. The team collected quantitative experiments from 43 papers covering yeast persistence, trophallaxis, hindgut oxygen, LLO, protozoa, oral RNAi, cellulase activity and yeast release systems, recording species, delivery route, exposure schedule, observation times, units and readout for each — and then published an EVIDENCE MAP table that states, row by row, what each dataset can estimate, what remains unresolved, and an identifiability status of Composite, Partly identifiable, Structural constraints only, Scenario only, Structure identifiable but rates unresolved, or Not identifiable. For example, LLO entering protozoan food vacuoles is marked 'Not identifiable', and A+B synergy is marked 'Not identifiable' with the honest note that no LLO-plus-RNAi study exists, so independent, additive, limiting-factor and multiplicative scenarios are all carried instead of one being asserted. The model structure was then deliberately built as the SMALLEST structure the data can constrain: when several microscopic steps were only ever observed through one final readout, they were kept together as one effective process rather than split into unidentifiable rates. Parameters were calibrated by maximum a posteriori fitting, theta_hat = argmin[-sum_e log p(y_e | H_e(x_e(theta))) - log p(theta)], jointly across studies rather than one parameter set per paper, following the biological chain (yeast dynamics, then protozoa-to-digestion, then hex RNAi, then cellu RNAi, then terminal dose-response), with directly reported quantities fixed first, multiple starting points per block, and each study keeping its own dose schedule and control. Validation is reproduction before prediction: seven published experiments were reproduced by the model before it was used to predict TermiTox. Real time was anchored by simulating a reference compound through the same two models — hexaflumuron, chosen because both individual and colony data exist — giving a model-time ratio of 137/120 = 1.14 that converts Im and Han's 56-day arena collapse into ~65 days for TermiTox at ~10^4 termites and Tsunoda's 9-month field result into ~10.3 months, with the dose converted to a bait loading of 2.03e9 cells per g dry bait, or ~5.1% w/w at 4e10 cells per g lyophilised preparation.","software":"RNAiWeave, a local web application that turns one target gene sequence into ranked, traceable hairpin RNAi candidates. It does sliding-window fragment filtering (default arm 300-400 nt, GC 30-60%, 21-nt siRNA window, all adjustable) screening out homopolymers, low complexity and tandem repeats with siRNA features computed once and reused via prefix sums; an activity estimate from four features reported in the published dsRIP study (terminal thermodynamic asymmetry, antisense self-folding, antisense GC and others); off-target screening against a local FASTA or RefSeq RNA scope that retains matching positions and records; and full-precursor folding in ViennaRNA. The structure score H is an explicit weighted formula over seven quantities (inter-arm stem coverage R at weight 0.50, linker-pairing burden J, MFE-per-nucleotide suitability E benchmarked to a project reference of 0.80 kcal/mol/nt, equilibrium probability of the MFE structure P, 21-nt unpaired-window accessibility U, overall paired fraction F, and a penalty for unexpected pairs X), so a reader can see exactly why one candidate outranked another. Environmental persistence is modelled separately as first-order decay with DT50 and DT90, with a Q10 temperature correction and literature reference scenarios (15-28 h for DvSnf7 RNA in agricultural soils, 56-72 h for non-bioactive dsRNA in water-sediment microcosms). Exports are FASTA, CSV, versioned JSON, dot-bracket and SVG. The team is scrupulous about limits: without off-target screening the output is labelled a preview ranking with sequence safety 'not assessed', and they state plainly that they have not compared knockdown efficacy or runtime against dsRIP. Model code is public at gitlab.igem.org/2026/peking/-/tree/main/Model_code.","stack":["Python","GROMACS (CHARMM36-jul2022, TIP3P)","PROPKA3","ChimeraX","ViennaRNA / RNAplot","NumPy / SciPy ODE solvers","custom agent-based simulator","local web app (RNAiWeave)","NCBI RefSeq / local BLAST-style off-target search"],"techniques":["all-atom molecular dynamics","pKa prediction and constant-protonation-state MD","membrane-bilayer simulation with semi-isotropic pressure coupling","protein-protein dimer MD for oligomerisation tendency","salt-bridge and distance trajectory analysis","cohort-structured ODE modelling","logistic growth with shared carrying capacity","delay / convolution kernel modelling of oral RNAi","first-order lag chains (mRNA to protein to function)","maximum a posteriori parameter estimation with literature priors","structural identifiability assessment","minimal-model construction from evidence","literature benchmark reproduction (7 experiments)","spatially explicit agent-based simulation","stochastic tunnel-network generation","coarse-grained weighted agents with resolution convergence check","contact-process trophallaxis modelling","multi-seed stochastic replication with quantile bands","cross-scale parameter handoff (individual to colony)","reference-compound anchoring of model time to real time","RNA secondary-structure thermodynamics","multi-criteria weighted scoring","first-order environmental fate modelling with Q10 correction"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was pitched per audience: a 'Code of Life' session at Huangchenggen Primary School on 13 April 2026 explaining DNA, genes and proteins with locally resonant analogies (the double helix as a twisted Chinese fried dough twist) followed by hands-on strawberry DNA extraction with Grade 6 pupils using dish soap, salt and ethanol; lab visits connecting textbook content to a working research environment; interactive games on termites, evolution and biodiversity; a May Day fun fair co-hosted with PKUHSC; and a Biodiversity Fair booth, reaching primary and middle school students, university students, families and the general public.","ihp":"The origin story is unusually concrete and honest: a team member looked up at a century-old campus building and saw termite tunnels inside a wooden beam no maintenance record had noted, and the team then realised every member came from a termite-affected region (Beijing, Sichuan, Fujian, Shanghai, Chongqing, Suzhou). Before any protocol was drafted they ran a questionnaire across ages, backgrounds and regions in China and overseas, collecting 539 valid responses; respondents rated heritage-building termite risk at 7.99 out of 8 and named three priorities — early-warning monitoring, low environmental impact, and target specificity — which the team acknowledge essentially outline the final project. Fifteen students ran a closed-loop review every Saturday evening from April to October 2026, feeding ~17 deep consultations with entomologists, heritage conservators, frontline control officers, RNAi industry people, PIs and past iGEMers and judges. They record what they killed as well as what they built: broad-spectrum pesticides were ruled out as leaving residues unacceptable for sites like the Forbidden City, and crude biocontrol strains were ruled out as too blunt for ancient wooden halls, each discarded idea costing weeks. Community work included six gatherings (NCIM meetup, Synbiopunk-CCiC Dalian, the 1st Grand APiC where TermiTox won Best Project, Synbio Challenge Shenzhen, the Peking-Rice International HP Summit they co-organised with 50+ universities, and the SZU green-bio roundtable).","realWorld":"The application is cultural-heritage conservation — the Forbidden City, old temples and traditional courtyard houses — where residue-free, species-specific control matters more than speed. The modelling fed directly into deliverables: the colony model's counter-intuitive finding that Strain B must be *kept* in the bait when Strain A is added (because withdrawing B lets surviving larvae become replacement queens and the colony recovers) is implementable only with a refillable bait station, which the team designed as hardware; and the finding that multiple bait stations break the distance gradient and remove spatial refuges became a stated deployment principle for placing several recoverable stations around a protected structure. The quantitative deliverable is a usable specification: ~5% w/w dry yeast loading, ~65 days to collapse for a constrained colony of order 10^4 termites, ~10-10.5 months for a field colony of several hundred thousand.","partners":["Peking University","PKUHSC (Peking University Health Science Center)","Huangchenggen Primary School","Rice University (co-organised International HP Summit)","Shenzhen University (SZU green-bio roundtable)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6366/wiki/model/quick-view/termitox-model-whole-picture-v5.webp","caption":"The whole picture of the TermiTox multiscale model: molecular dynamics of the pH-gated LLO mutant, a cohort-ODE individual model of yeast persistence through payload release to functional decline, and a spatially explicit agent-based colony model of trophallactic spread, with each scale's output feeding the next.","credit":"Team Peking, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6366/wiki/model/colony-scale/figure-08-spatial-progression-one-bait.avif","caption":"Figure 8: spatial progression with one bait station. Columns are treatment days 10-120, rows are worker cohorts grouped by home module, and the three stacked panels show ever-exposed fraction, payload burden in a no-damage tracer run, and functional-worker fraction. Distant modules are exposed later and stay functional longer, acting as spatial refuges — a result that only appears because the nest geometry is modelled explicitly instead of as an SIR epidemic.","credit":"Team Peking, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6366/wiki/project/dry-design/model-validation-seven-literature-benchmarks-v2.avif","caption":"Seven published experiments reproduced by the individual-level model before it was used to predict anything about the team's own system — on yeast persistence, protozoa-dependent digestion and oral RNAi.","credit":"Team Peking, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6366/wiki/model/molecular-scale/figure-03-acidic-triad-closeup.avif","caption":"The acidic triad (D208/E247/D320) of H311A-LLO under acidic (left) and neutral (right) conditions. The salt-bridge network holds at pH 5.5 and separates at pH 7.4, supporting the safety premise that the pore-forming toxin is active only inside acidic protozoan food vacuoles — an allosteric effect, since the mutation site sits 17-18 Angstrom away and never contacts the triad.","credit":"Team Peking, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Before writing a single equation, build an evidence map: a table listing every process you want to model, the published experiment that constrains it, what that experiment can and cannot separate, and a verdict of identifiable / composite / not identifiable — then build only the smallest model those data support, and carry multiple scenarios where the verdict is 'not identifiable' instead of asserting one. Then anchor model time to reality by simulating a well-documented reference compound through the same pipeline.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/education/"],"rank":21},{"team":"UCL","id":"6381","section":"Undergrad","country":"United Kingdom","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/","projectTitle":"SHELLter — capturing cisplatin from patient urine with a chelator made from shellfish waste","tagline":"A point-of-care cartridge that chelates platinum chemotherapy drugs out of patient urine, with the chelator made enzymatically from crab shell.","summary":"About half of each cisplatin dose leaves the patient in urine within days, and UK hospital effluent is not treated for cytotoxics at all. SHELLter intercepts it at the point of care with a monoacetylchitosan chelating ligand produced from shellfish-processing waste by an engineered SmChiA-VcCDA fusion enzyme. The dry lab is presented as four Python files on an interactive laptop — kinetic_model.py, docking_simulation.py, life_cycle_assessment.py and techno_economic_analysis.py — and all four feed each other: the kinetic model sets reaction conditions and predicts how downstream purification affects product yield, that yield drives the techno-economic cost per gram, and the same mass balance underpins the openLCA life cycle assessment. The docking study is a rigorously controlled computational experiment with negative controls, a pre-specified acceptance rule, multiple-comparison correction and a stated power analysis.","awards":["Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Sustainable Development Impact — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Bioremediation Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Four models, deliberately coupled. (1) KINETIC MODEL — a mechanistic ODE system rather than an empirical fit, chosen precisely because the fusion enzyme is novel and PpChi is barely characterised, so no dataset exists to fit against. It is built from inhibited Michaelis-Menten forms: competitive inhibition for product rebinding, and noncompetitive inhibition for process impurities where, lacking any single inhibition constant for a mixture, they substitute chitin's measured protein-holding capacity (Cm = 0.032573 g/g) times substrate concentration as the rate-limiting term, giving dP/dt = kcat*ET*S / [Km(1 + I/Ki + D/(Cm*S)) + S(1 + D/(Cm*S))]. SmChiA's non-Michaelian processive mechanism on insoluble chitin is handled by converting polymer mass to N-acetylglucosamine residues (R0 = S0/203 g/mol), and PpChi's substrate is converted to GlcN-GlcN-GlcNAc motifs with the motif yield derived from degree of acetylation, Y = DA(1-DA)^2, and an average residue weight W = DA*203 + (1-DA)*161. Environmental dependence is interpolated with double-sigmoid rate modifiers for pH (F) and temperature (G) multiplying kcat, with all nine fitted sigmoid constants tabulated per enzyme, and thermal enzyme decay modelled with Bigelow's law. Twelve assumptions are tabulated with their impact AND their limitation, and the most influential are tested in a dedicated Choice Sensitivity section. (2) DOCKING SIMULATION — a ten-stage pipeline built as a controlled experiment, which is what makes it exceptional. Four fusion constructs (varying only the two things literature could not settle: domain order and His-tag terminus; all 1015 residues, GGGGSGGGGS inter-domain linker, GGGGS tag linker, native signal peptides retained) were each predicted five times by AlphaFold 3 with different seeds, giving twenty structures. Two design rules govern everything: every measurement is a difference taken WITHIN a single predicted structure, never between structures, because across seeds the CDA domain moved 25.6 +/- 11.0 Angstrom relative to ChiA so a between-structure comparison would measure AlphaFold rather than the protein; and every effect is measured against an apo' control in which the stochastic relaxation is re-run with nothing changed but the random seed, giving a genuine noise floor. Active-site residues were MEASURED rather than quoted: heavy atoms within 4.5 Angstrom of a bound chito-oligosaccharide across multiple crystal structures (1EHN, 1NH6 for SmChiA; 4NZ1, 4NZ3, 4OUI for VcCDA, 98.7-99.8% identity), with a residue required to appear in at least two independent structures, a 6.0 Angstrom criterion defining the mobile cleft, ligands under ten heavy atoms ignored to exclude buffer and cryoprotectant, and SIX NEGATIVE-CONTROL structures expected to return nothing, which returned zero contacts. Relaxation is Rosetta FastRelax under ref2015 via PyRosetta, five rounds, with a movemap freeing only the ~400 residues within 10 Angstrom of either cleft, computed once per replicate from the apo structure and reused unchanged across all four conditions so that apo and holo are compared over an identical mobile set. Substrates (tetraacetylchitotetraose, 57 heavy atoms, for the chitinase groove; diacetylchitobiose, 29 heavy atoms, for the deacetylase pocket) were built from PubChem with RDKit, parameterised with Meeko, and verified atom by atom. Docking is AutoDock Vina at exhaustiveness 32 with three independent seeds per receptor-substrate pair, 148 searches total, into a box centred on the contacting residues padded 6 Angstrom and clamped 18-32 Angstrom with a guard flagging any box centre more than 5 Angstrom from the cleft centroid. (3) LIFE CYCLE ASSESSMENT — a cradle-to-use LCA of four process steps (chitin extraction from shellfish waste, fusion production in E. coli, ligand production, device use), built in two independent halves that must agree: a component mass balance tracking every stream in every unit operation, and an openLCA model reproducing that arithmetic separately. Functional unit is one treatment course: one patient, five cycles, five cartridges per cycle, 60 kg of urine carrying 0.336 g of drug, with the baseline being the status quo of untreated discharge. (4) TECHNO-ECONOMIC MODEL — built on the same central mass balance, with a localised impurity-prediction part that feeds back into project design and a full cost-of-goods and discounted-cash-flow analysis.","dataviz":"The model landing page is an interactive laptop whose screen lists the four models as Python filenames, which the reader clicks to open — a small piece of framing that tells the reader immediately that the dry lab is four programs, not one vague 'model'. The kinetic results are presented as parameter sweeps with the optimum marked by a dot on each curve: time to 50% and 90% substrate conversion against temperature for each enzyme separately (PpChi 45 C, SmChiA 43 C, VcCDA 39 C) and a two-dimensional temperature-by-pH heatmap for the fusion (optimum 43 C, pH 6.1) drawn in the Okabe-Ito colour-blind-safe palette with darker colours for faster conversion — the team say so in the caption. Figure 20 is the decisive one: an enzyme molar-ratio sweep from 0.1 to 40 at constant total enzyme mass, showing a 15:1 SmChiA:VcCDA optimum roughly twice as fast as the 1:1 ratio a fusion is structurally locked into. The docking pipeline is drawn as a ten-stage flow diagram where each box names the stage, what it does and the software used, with stage 0 (measuring active sites from crystal structures) and stage 3 (building substrates from PubChem) shown as completing before the work they feed. Structures are shown in an embedded 3Dmol.js v2.5.5 viewer with a toggle between colour-by-part and colour-by-pLDDT confidence, and every AlphaFold figure caption carries the warning 'NOT experimentally determined or validated'. The LCA opens with a calibrated comparison instead of an abstract number: one SHELLter course emits 19.76 kg CO2eq, about 36 caffe lattes, 2.1 CT scans or one chemotherapy cycle — immediately followed by a note that these equivalence figures come from papers using different impact methods and databases and are 'given only for contextual perspective'.","datascience":"The statistical design of the docking study is the standout. The initial pose-convergence test (maximum pairwise RMSD across seeds) was found to conflate two different failure modes — seeds genuinely scattering versus a majority agreeing with one outlier — and was replaced mid-run with a rule written down BEFORE the run finished and then applied uniformly: a pose is accepted only if a strict majority of seeds agree within 2.0 Angstrom, the best-scoring pose falls inside that majority, at least 60% of its heavy atoms lie within 5 Angstrom of the cleft, and it clears every face of the search box by at least 1 Angstrom; every non-unanimous replicate got two further seeds. Results are reported with explicit counts and the honest verdict for each of five pre-set questions: both sites still bind (35 of 40 dockings accepted); each site responds structurally to its OWN substrate and not the other (25 of 32 paired comparisons, p = 0.0021, with Holm-Bonferroni correction cited); both sites can be occupied simultaneously (16 of 20 replicates); occupying one site produces no detectable effect at the other by three independent measurements, with the effect size BOUNDED below the apo' noise floor and the Rosetta additivity residual below ~4 REU; and domain order is reported as 'Not resolved at this sample size' with the explicit gloss that this means insufficient power, not absence of an effect. They then do the power analysis properly: at 9-12 replicates per group the null can only be rejected on unanimity, at 16 one may disagree, at 20 three may, so twenty per group is where the design starts to be able to detect what it currently cannot — with the per-step compute cost given (7.4 min per ligand docking, 16-24 min per relaxation on four cores) so the extension is actionable. Ten numbered assumptions are tabulated with impact and limitation, including that the VcCDA catalytic Zn2+ is simply absent from predicted structures (which they offer as the likely cause of DACB poses sitting partly off the pocket), that Vina affinities 'should be read as a ranking aid, not as delta-G', and that the docking score's 8 Angstrom interatomic cutoff could not register a direct 34-47 Angstrom cross-site interaction even if one existed — which is precisely why they also ran the cutoff-free Rosetta additivity test. The models genuinely changed decisions: the kinetic model's 15:1 ratio finding and the fusion-vs-dual-enzyme-vs-PpChi comparison table (time to 90% conversion: fusion 2.05 h, dual single-pot 1.17 h, dual multi-pot 1.84 h, PpChi 12.68 h) reframed the construct choice; the impurity model showed that going from 6 to 8 downstream unit operations nearly doubled product at 10 hours (11.44 to 22.40 mM), and feeding that into the TEA gave a cost per gram of chitobiose for each of five flowsheets (78.66, 84.57, 59.98, 172.62, 180.01 GBP/g), selecting PEG precipitation over chromatography on the reasoning that the fusion is not a biopharmaceutical so its purity target exists only to improve function. The DCF analysis, solved with GoalSeek to NPV = 0 at a 12% discount rate, gives a 17-year payback and a unit price of GBP 146.45, contextualised as ~1.9% of the GBP 7,670 NHS cost of treating one patient with systemic anti-cancer therapy.","software":"The kinetic model is released on the team's software GitLab at gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/ucl/model. The docking pipeline is parameterised and scripted end to end, which is what makes the stated extension (add seeds, re-run the same scripts) credible. Structures are presented in-browser with 3Dmol.js v2.5.5 (BSD-3-Clause) with a pLDDT colouring toggle. The LCA is built in openLCA alongside an independent spreadsheet mass balance as a cross-check.","stack":["Python","SciPy ODE integration","AlphaFold 3 (AlphaFold Server)","PyRosetta / Rosetta FastRelax (ref2015)","AutoDock Vina","Meeko","RDKit","3Dmol.js v2.5.5","openLCA","Excel / GoalSeek (discounted cash flow)","PubChem","Protein Data Bank","Benchling"],"techniques":["mechanistic ODE enzyme kinetics","competitive and noncompetitive inhibited Michaelis-Menten kinetics","processive-enzyme substrate conversion (polymer to residue/motif)","double-sigmoid environmental rate modifiers for pH and temperature","Bigelow thermal inactivation","multi-dimensional parameter sweeps for process optimisation","enzyme ratio optimisation / rate-limiting step identification","mass-balance error checking as a solver test","structure prediction with multi-seed replication","within-structure paired comparison design","stochastic-relaxation noise-floor control (apo')","active-site definition measured from crystal structures with negative controls","localised movemap relaxation","molecular docking (AutoDock Vina) with multi-seed convergence acceptance rules","induced-fit measurement","dual-occupancy / additivity testing","Holm-Bonferroni multiple-comparison correction","statistical power analysis and sample-size planning","effect-size bounding when a null is not rejected","life cycle assessment (cradle-to-use, openLCA)","component mass balance with independent reproduction","techno-economic analysis","cost-of-goods and flowsheet comparison","discounted cash flow, NPV and IRR analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"An education programme with its own substantial page, built around two recurring illustrated characters (Cisplatin and Shelly) who appear throughout the wiki, including in the loading animation — a consistent visual voice that makes a technical oncology-waste project approachable. The team's public-facing identity extends to Instagram and a LinkedIn company page for SHELLter.","ihp":"The strongest part of the project's non-technical work, and genuinely regulatory rather than decorative. Conversations with NHS England's healthcare and sustainability teams uncovered a specific, exploitable regulatory gap: Water UK forbids direct discharge of cytotoxic and cytostatic medications to foul sewers under any circumstances, including rinsing their containers, but patient bodily discharges containing 'metabolised medications' are permitted — while the actual degree of metabolism varies from 10-97%, giving patient discharge rates of roughly 3-90%, so metabolic-rate statistics can be selected to bypass waste-management rules. Dr Anne Woolridge explained that England's focus is on protecting personnel from cytotoxic exposure rather than downstream treatment, visible in the Pan London Cytotoxic Policy; the team then cite evidence that even tertiary membrane-bioreactor treatment removes only up to 51% of hydrophilic cisplatin, and that 22% of platinum compounds were detected in the outflows of a major UK hospital (Derriford, Plymouth) after conventional treatment. Anne O'Connor and Dr Kumud Kantilal established why point-of-care capture is the right intervention: outpatient chemotherapy is becoming the norm, but patient bodily fluids remain hazardous for up to seven days after discharge, so a large fraction of excreted cytotoxic residue can never reach any hospital treatment system. Joe Burton and Dr Sharon Pfleger surfaced a gap in NHS clinical-waste sustainability priorities. Stakeholders including Dr Jay Burt, Professor Louis Grandjean, Dr Anne Woolridge, Professor Laurence Lovat, Dr Sophie Fridman and Alex Senechal independently asked for the economic case, which is explicitly why the techno-economic model exists and why it is structured to produce a cost per unit of environmental benefit.","realWorld":"The commercial and environmental case is quantified rather than asserted. The LCA's functional unit is a real clinical course (one patient, five cycles, fifteen cartridges, 60 kg of urine carrying 0.336 g of drug) measured against the actual status quo of untreated discharge, and the headline is framed as a trade: 19.76 kg CO2eq to prevent an emission that currently happens unchecked, roughly a fifth added to the ~100 kg CO2eq of the five-cycle treatment it cleans up. The objective is stated as ecotoxicity avoided per unit of climate and energy burden, with unit operations ranked by their contribution to each impact category. The techno-economic model selects the full nine-step downstream train (centrifugation, resuspension, homogenisation, flocculation, centrifugation, depth filtration, PEG precipitation, tangential flow filtration, resolubilisation) on cost-per-gram grounds, and gives a sellable unit price of GBP 146.45 at NPV = 0 with a 17-year payback, deliberately benchmarked against NHS treatment cost. The feedstock is shellfish-processing waste, which makes the chelator itself a valorised waste stream.","partners":["University College London","UCL Department of Biochemical Engineering","UCL Faculty of Engineering Sciences","VaxHub","Resilience","Ribbon","NHS England healthcare and sustainability teams"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6381/wiki/dockingpipeline.avif","caption":"The ten-stage docking pipeline, with each box naming the stage, what it does and the software used. Stage 0 defines the active sites by measuring them from substrate-bound crystal structures independently of any model built later, and stage 3 builds and verifies the substrates from PubChem — both complete before the work they feed into begins.","credit":"Team UCL, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6381/wiki/figure-20-km.avif","caption":"Figure 20: the enzyme-ratio parameter sweep that settled a construct decision. Holding total enzyme mass constant at 0.111 g/L, the SmChiA:VcCDA ratio was swept from 0.1 to 40; the 90%-conversion optimum sits at 15:1, roughly twice as fast as the 1:1 ratio a fusion protein is structurally locked into, identifying SmChiA as the rate-limiting step.","credit":"Team UCL, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6381/wiki/figure-18-km.avif","caption":"Figure 18: time to 50% and 90% substrate conversion for the fusion enzyme across temperature and pH, drawn in the Okabe-Ito colour-blind-safe palette with darker colours for faster conversion and a dot marking the optimum (43 C, pH 6.1). The surface shows the fusion optimum is weighted far more toward SmChiA than VcCDA.","credit":"Team UCL, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6381/wiki/fusion.avif","caption":"AlphaFold 3 model of the ChiA-CDA-His fusion, coloured by prediction confidence: two compact well-predicted domains with an extended low-confidence loop arcing above them. The team use this honestly — because the CDA domain moved 25.6 +/- 11.0 Angstrom relative to ChiA across five seeds, every measurement in the study is a difference taken within a single structure rather than between them.","credit":"Team UCL, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Run a computational study as a real experiment: define your measurement target by measuring it (active-site residues from crystal structures, with negative controls that must return nothing) rather than quoting a number, include a do-nothing control that captures the method's own noise (re-run the stochastic relaxation with only the seed changed), write the acceptance rule down before you finish the run, and when a comparison fails to reach significance say 'not resolved at this sample size' and state how many replicates would be needed.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/kinetic-model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/kinetic-model-structure/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/kinetic-model-results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/docking-simulation/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/life-cycle-assessment/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/techno-economic-analysis/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/techno-economic-analysis-results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucl/education/"],"rank":28},{"team":"Oxford","id":"6391","section":"Undergrad","country":"United Kingdom","village":"Agriculture","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/","projectTitle":"NECTAR","tagline":"An end-to-end platform for designing, validating, producing and deploying RNAi interventions, stress-tested on Varroa destructor","summary":"NECTAR is a programmable RNAi pipeline: FORAGER (computational dsRNA design with off-target screening against the Apis mellifera transcriptome), microbial production in yeast, and delivery to hives in a pollen patty. Varroa destructor, the single biggest threat to honeybees, is the first real-world case study. The team built HIVE, an explicit four-step human-practices decision procedure (Hear, Investigate, Verdict, Evaluate) run over six open project questions with 28+ named stakeholders including US EPA/FDA/USDA staff, the Chair of the UK Advisory Committee on Releases to the Environment, and the Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity. Stakeholder evidence changed the chassis (living engineered Snodgrassella alvi to heat-inactivated yeast), the delivery format, the off-target screening stage, the survey instrument and the definition of success.","awards":["Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Sustainable Development Impact — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Agriculture Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"FORAGER, a three-stage standardised dsRNA design pipeline: target gene identification (most abundant transcripts of well-connected genes, weighted by known Varroa RNAi data), within-gene region selection (endogenous Varroa siRNA sequence features, guide competence, RNAplfold target accessibility, combined as three equally weighted complementary evidence layers rather than one fused score), and off-target analysis. Also a BEEHAVE-based colony population model and a bespoke partial-equilibrium economic model.","dataviz":"Hand-drawn mechanism illustrations throughout (FORAGER stages, the five-step path of a dose through the hive, the SDG goal map), a geocoded stakeholder map with latitude/longitude per interviewee, and per-cycle engineering tables; the wiki is a custom React/Astro-style build with light and dark themes.","datascience":"Construction of a clean Varroa destructor small-RNA dataset from public sequencing, basic population feature analysis of that sRNA pool, sequence feature analysis, whole-region homology and local exact-match screening against the honeybee transcriptome (16-nt strict mapping plus relaxed whole-region similarity), BLAST-based non-target screening, and a documented limitations table stating that the three evidence layers are equally weighted only because no Varroa RNAi efficacy data existed to fit weights.","software":"FORAGER is released with one file per stage and a documented frontmatter/body contract so other teams can re-run it; the HIVE method and its full interview question bank are released under CC BY 4.0. Hive insert and PCR-tube feeding adaptor are downloadable 3D-printable hardware.","stack":["ViennaRNA / RNAplfold","BLAST","Bowtie","BEEHAVE (NetLogo)","Boltz structure prediction","molecular docking","R","Python","React / Astro wiki build"],"techniques":["functional network connectivity analysis (STRING-type)","mRNA abundance weighting","small-RNA population profiling","RNA secondary structure and accessibility prediction","MS2 hairpin array folding optimisation (6% to 67% complete-array folding)","partial-equilibrium economic modelling with supply/demand elasticities","sensitivity analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Buzz In, a three-episode stakeholder podcast built deliberately to put stakeholders who disagreed with each other into contact rather than collecting views in isolation: episode 1 paired Varroa-resistance academic Prof. Stephen Martin with commercial beekeeper Paul Horton on whether to treat at all; episode 2 brought together the CEO of the Australian Honey Bee Industry Council, Amateur Beekeepers Australia and Hive & Wellness; episode 3 set the project against social, environmental and economic lenses with a social scientist, a bee researcher and an agricultural economist. GMO 101, a joint Zoom workshop with NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM on 8 October 2026, measured participation through Nearpod (24 participants, 83% took part). A beekeeper survey received a favourable opinion from a subcommittee of the Oxford Central University Research Ethics Committee (ref MS IDREC 3973787), with public task as the lawful basis and informed consent before questions; 100+ respondents, 63% reported struggling with Varroa at mean severity 5.74/10 and 70% said they would be open to a GMO-based treatment. School sessions at St Clare's summer course were run as a two-way choice exercise (present two delivery routes with honest trade-offs, ask which students would accept, measure the acceptance shift before and after) rather than a lecture. Plus street interviews, a bee campaign, Instagram and TikTok.","ihp":"Oxford's IHP is the strongest kind: a documented decision procedure with a mandatory 'How we implemented the advice to change NECTAR' field on every one of 28+ interview write-ups, and a rule that 'We considered their feedback is not an implementation'. The largest single change is the chassis. Danny Le Feuvre (CEO, Australian Honey Bee Industry Council) and Mike Allerton (VP and Biosecurity Officer, Amateur Beekeepers Australia) both preferred a persistent living engineered Snodgrassella alvi gut symbiont because it minimises beekeeper labour; the team did not simply follow them but took the question to regulators, and Laura Bowden (GM manager, Science and Advice for Scottish Agriculture), Elizabeth Frost (NSW Department of Primary Industries), Tom Hartley (Senior Certification Officer, Soil Association Certification) and the Scottish Government Honey Bee Health Team pushed the opposite way, so the team switched to heat-inactivated, dried engineered yeast and stated plainly what that cost: the persistence that made S. alvi attractive, meaning repeat dosing and more beekeeper labour, against the preference of beekeepers they had interviewed. Austein McLoughlin of the Convention on Biological Diversity Secretariat then confirmed the inactivated product is not a living modified organism under the Cartagena Protocol, but also refused to let the team stop at SDG alignment, pushing them to ask whether NECTAR produces 'measurable improvement' - which is why the sustainability analysis widened to the whole NECTAR lifecycle and built baseline comparisons into the modelling. A joint meeting with Mike Mendelsohn and Alan Reynolds (US EPA), Adam Moyer and Laura Epstein (FDA) and Alan Pearson (USDA) then 'prevented us from treating our shift to heat-inactivated yeast as a universal regulatory shortcut', forcing jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction regulatory mapping instead of one global classification. The deepest economic chain runs through Prof. Wally Thurman (Agricultural and Resource Economics, NC State, interviewed 29 July 2026) and Prof. Brittney Goodrich (Agricultural and Consumer Economics, Illinois, 7 August 2026): Thurman set five methodological commitments (start from an average commercial operation not an industry aggregate; use existing treatments as the counterfactual; assess costs and revenues not survival rates; use simple partial equilibrium; restrict to paid pollination markets), and Goodrich's operation-level cost structure redefined the cost of Varroa to include labour, colony replacement and lost honey and pollination revenue rather than just the price of acaricide. The team then ran the chain backwards - what a beekeeper can afford, to allowable cost per dose, to allowable manufacturing cost, to required mass of intact dsRNA per gram of dry yeast - converting 'high expression' into a pass/fail wet-lab specification of 0.005 mg dsRNA per g yeast for US viability and 0.05 mg/g for both markets, against a fermentation cost of about 2.33 USD per gram. They also deliberately recruited dissent: the Oxfordshire Natural Beekeeping Group (about 20 chemical-free hobbyists) and treatment-free beekeeper Mark Sandham argued that treating removes the selection pressure that would produce tolerant bees, an argument against the entire premise; rather than abandoning the project the team narrowed its target user to commercial operations and wrote that OxNatBees changed 'not the molecular mechanism of NECTAR, but the problem we believed NECTAR should - and should not - try to solve'. Smaller but equally traceable: Lord John Krebs (first FSA Chairman) and Prof. Jim Dunwell (Chair, ACRE) made environmental fate and off-target effects explicit engineering requirements, moving non-target organisms into the FORAGER off-target screening stage and adding in vivo dsRNA-persistence experiments; Prof. Paul Lam (environmental chemistry, Hong Kong Metropolitan University) drove the adoption of BLAST off-target screening and reframed public engagement as risk management rather than promotion; Dr Michael Morrison (HeLEX, Oxford Law) rewrote the survey instrument so it asked separately about pollen patties, constitutively active engineered bacteria and inducible engineered bacteria rather than about 'GMOs' in general; Dr Théotime Colin's point about secondary pests prompted dsRNA constructs for wax moth and small hive beetle; Prof. Giles Budge's reassurance kept the adult-bee oral delivery route alive and produced the 300/500/700 bp length comparison; and Melanie Teece (Head of Technical, Hilltop Honey) and Wade Ford (Hive & Wellness) turned honey purity into a reason for additional in vivo work. The published HIVE guide names its own failure modes (interviewing agreeable people, treating 'the public' as one stakeholder, letting the Verdict drift into a summary, consulting regulators too late to change anything) and requires every Verdict to state what was decided, what was rejected and what that cost.","realWorld":"The need is framed with numbers rather than adjectives: Varroa is an invasive alien species across most of its range and the most serious pest of the western honeybee; Australia's failed eradication attempt cost A$101 million and destroyed about 30,000 hives, and surveyed US commercial operations lost around 62% of colonies in 2024-25, estimated at over $600 million. SDGs are chosen, not collected: 15 (Life on Land) and 2 (Zero Hunger) are the primary goals with 17 and 4 as enabling goals, each mapped to named sub-targets (15.8 on invasive alien species is the headline, plus 15.1, 15.5, 15.9), and the analysis extends into the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets 7, 10, 11 and 17 at the CBD Secretariat's suggestion. The team then writes a dedicated negative-impacts section on goal conflicts - accessibility across economic status, pollution, ecological threat, and whether healthier managed colonies put pressure on wild pollinators (goal 15 against goal 2) - and admits an unresolved contradiction: stakeholders insisted cost was decisive while 48% of their own survey respondents ranked cost least important, with the honest line 'We have not resolved this'. Deployment rides on existing practice (dsRNA-bearing heat-killed yeast in a conventional pollen patty laid over the frames, plus a 3D-printable hive insert), the regulatory path is mapped per jurisdiction after the EPA/FDA/USDA conversation, and the cost model gives both a per-colony net private benefit and the titre threshold manufacturing must hit. They are unusually candid about what is not demonstrated: no dsRNA titre was measured against the model thresholds, the yeast could not be cloned and the E. coli route gave no detectable dsRNA, so 'this is a case for the approach, not a demonstration of the product'. Handover material - FORAGER, the HIVE cycle and question bank, the bee research guide, protocols and hardware files - is released CC BY 4.0 for future teams.","partners":["Amateur Beekeepers Australia","Australian Honey Bee Industry Council","Hive & Wellness Australia","NSW Department of Primary Industries","Science and Advice for Scottish Agriculture (SASA)","Scottish Government Honey Bee Health Team","Advisory Committee on Releases to the Environment (ACRE)","Soil Association Certification","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity","US EPA, FDA and USDA","Hilltop Honey","Sustainable Conservation / Almond Board of California","Oxfordshire Natural Beekeeping Group","NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM","University of Oxford Department of Biology (Prof. Geraldine Wright)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/drawings/sdg-description.avif","caption":"NECTAR at the centre of four chosen SDGs - 15 Life on Land and 2 Zero Hunger as primary goals, 17 Partnerships and 4 Quality Education as enabling goals - each with its own justification rather than a logo wall","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/education/gmo-webinar-zoom.avif","caption":"GMO 101, the joint Zoom workshop with NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM on 8 October 2026, with participation measured through Nearpod","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/bee-research-guide/walking-to-the-hives.avif","caption":"The team walking to the hives - fifteen members with zero beekeeping experience at the start of the project","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/education/street-interview.avif","caption":"Street interviews on public attitudes to GMOs in bee treatment, run as risk management rather than promotion after advice from Prof. Paul Lam","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Make the write-up template itself enforce integration: every stakeholder record must carry a 'what this changed' field, and every verdict must name what was decided, what was rejected and what that cost - then run the affordability chain backwards from what the user can pay to a numerical pass/fail specification for the bench.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/economic-modelling","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/case-studies","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/beekeeper-survey","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/project-description"],"rank":47},{"team":"NUS-Singapore","id":"6395","section":"Undergrad","country":"Singapore","village":"Biomanufacturing","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/","projectTitle":"ORCHID","tagline":"Multiplexed fluorescent stress biosensors in yeast plus a machine-learning layer that turns intracellular stress into the next experiment to run.","summary":"Industrial bioreactors hold hundreds of thousands of litres and a single bad batch is expensive, yet conventional monitoring tracks only physical and chemical parameters such as pH and dissolved oxygen — nothing about what is happening inside the cell. Omics and LC-MS can see inside, but they are offline, destructive and slow enough that results arrive after the batch has finished, so they cannot act on anything. ORCHID puts biosensors for endoplasmic reticulum, oxidative and ATP stress into Saccharomyces cerevisiae as fluorescent reporters readable straight from a plate reader, solves the resulting spectral overlap with a linear unmixing model, and feeds the separated stress signals into SABLe, a neural-network framework that predicts growth, product and stress from process conditions and then proposes which culture to run next. It won nominations across parts, presentation, wiki and integrated human practices because the loop actually closed: SABLe's exploration rule proposed 27 C, the team grew that plate, and the measured product signal reached 491 mApple RFU/OD600 against 204 and 179 at the 24 and 30 C conditions it was trained on.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Part Collection — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Presentation — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Biomanufacturing Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three layers, built in sequence. First, a linear unmixing model adapted from multispectral fluorescence imaging to plate-reader wells: each sample's readings are modelled as a mixture matrix with one column per reporter and one row per channel, with diagonal entries carrying a reporter's own signal and off-diagonal entries the crosstalk, inverted to recover each reporter's share. It was validated on mixtures of single-reporter strains including untransformed BY4741 for autofluorescence and water-only wells for background, six replicates per condition. Second, a mechanistic baseline: Hill dose-response curves fitted at each temperature for DTT against the stress reporter and galactose against the product reporter, plus simple ODE time-course equations. The ODEs tracked growth well (R-squared about 0.91-0.93) but fitted stress (0.33-0.47) and product (0.41-0.42) poorly, and the team uses that failure as the explicit justification for moving to machine learning rather than assuming ML was needed. Third, SABLe: a multilayer perceptron trained on 700 culture-time records from 40 unique condition combinations (two temperatures at 24 and 30 C, five DTT concentrations 0-1 mM, four galactose concentrations 0-0.2%, three independent plate cultures each, measured at 0, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 24-26 hours), predicting OD600, mApple/OD600 and mCitrine/OD600 without ever receiving their measured values as inputs. On top of the trained network they run gradient ascent (network frozen, 64 starting conditions, 500 steps each, 32,000 evaluations without growing a culture), a ten-member bootstrap ensemble for disagreement-based uncertainty, and UMAP projection of the 64-dimensional latent response to 20,000 queried conditions to inspect what the network learned.","dataviz":"The wiki is a Three.js scrollytelling experience that walks a reader from the $2-4 trillion bioeconomy projection down to a single yeast cell, with hoverable bioreactors explaining Titer, Rate and Yield before the project is even introduced — the argument is built visually before any data appears. The dry lab page is laid out as a numbered slide sequence (raw extremes, normalisation, normalised trajectories, stress dose fit, product dose fit, time model fit, fivefold split, held-out scores, held-out trajectories, gradient search, ensemble uncertainty, exploration score, optimisation versus exploration, latent UMAP, measured DTT response, UMAP robustness), so the reasoning chain is the navigation. Two choices stand out. The exploit-versus-explore panel shows side by side that maximising predicted signal just returns to a corner already measured, while the exploration rule spreads eight proposed tests across under-sampled combinations — making the value of the method visible rather than argued. And the UMAP is drawn sixteen different ways with the caption telling the reader to compare colour gradients rather than outlines, with an explicit note that the map axes have no biological units, which is an honest treatment of a technique usually presented as if its shape meant something.","datascience":"The statistical discipline here is well above the usual standard. Cross-validation groups all biological replicates and time points from a single cultivation condition into one split, because splitting correlated measurements across train and test would inflate performance — so the reported scores (R-squared 0.955 for OD600, 0.891 for mApple/OD600, 0.842 for mCitrine/OD600) measure generalisation to genuinely unseen condition combinations. Channel selection asked how few reads are needed and found one well-chosen channel per reporter matches a full spectral scan (three channels as good as six for three reporters, four as good as eight for four), turning an hour-long scan into minutes so a bioreactor can be sampled routinely. A simulation study set the design rule for adding reporters: for each separation angle from 2 to 90 degrees they generated 3,000 random two-reporter mixtures with 5% noise and found median error stays within 5 percentage points once reporters are at least 28 degrees apart — which is why mCitrine and eGFP at 20 degrees are ruled out. Errors are diagnosed rather than reported: the consistent 3-point overestimate of mTurquoise2 and underestimate of mApple in three-reporter mixtures is attributed to sample preparation rather than the algorithm because noise alone would scatter in both directions, and the larger four-reporter errors are traced to the whole plate being 6-17 times dimmer, leaving mApple and mTurquoise2 only about 160 and 690 RFU above water. Then the model generated a biological hypothesis: UMAP showed conditions with more DTT clustering where both stress and product are predicted high (79% of high-predicted-product conditions were also high DTT, 81% high predicted stress, against 25% expected by chance), the team went back to the measured cultures and confirmed it (Spearman rho, p < 0.001), checked that mCitrine bleed into the mApple channel could explain at most 8% of the rise, and named the next mechanistic question. They also caught their own education-survey analogue of this problem in the model: the 27 C validation plate was read at 24 hours while earlier plates were read at about 25, and they say so.","software":"SABLe and the linear unmixing algorithm are released on the iGEM GitLab at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nus-singapore/model, framed explicitly as a reusable framework rather than a one-project script — future teams can use the unmixing to quantify several fluorescent proteins at once and track multiple cellular processes from a single strain design. The part collection is the other deliverable: 17 modular basic parts (stress-sensing elements including altered and native Yap1 response elements, UPRE1 and UPRE2 Hac1 binding sites, biosensor tuning elements, promoters of differing strengths, terminators, and fluorescent reporters including a genetically encoded green ATP sensor) plus 12 composite parts for the ER, oxidative and ATP stress biosensors, designed for plug-and-play assembly. They also characterised leakiness-reduction design principles as a reusable pattern: Sko1 repression upstream of the TATA box, a cP_CYC1 core promoter to lower basal expression, an ODC degradation tag for continuous turnover of background protein, and an RPP2Bt terminator. Two education artefacts are software: a browser game in which players build genetic circuits in virtual yeast populations, with every number and equation documented by source category (published, game-balance tuned by scripted playtesting, or stated rule) and an honest statement that no value was fitted to a real culture so the game shows the shape of the biology rather than predictions; and SynVision, an app whose camera scanner links everyday objects to synthetic biology concepts and then runs DBTL-guided questioning.","stack":["Python with a multilayer perceptron (ReLU)","Deep ensembles for predictive uncertainty","UMAP dimensionality reduction","Gradient ascent over a frozen network","Linear unmixing / matrix inversion on plate-reader channels","Hill dose-response curve fitting","ODE time-course models","Spearman correlation with Holm correction","Saliency-map style latent inspection","Three.js scrollytelling wiki","Microsoft Forms with anonymous participant linking codes","GitLab (iGEM instance)"],"techniques":["Multiplexed fluorescent biosensing with spectral deconvolution","Reference-spectra calibration including autofluorescence and background wells","Condition-grouped cross-validation to prevent leakage from correlated replicates","Channel-subset selection to minimise readout time","Simulation-based design thresholds (28-degree separation rule)","Bootstrap ensemble disagreement as an uncertainty proxy","Exploit-versus-explore acquisition scoring (normalised disagreement plus distance to nearest tested condition)","Latent-space projection to generate testable biological hypotheses","Robustness checks across 16 hyperparameter settings","Model-proposed condition validated at the bench","Systematic error diagnosis distinguishing preparation artefacts from algorithm error"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"NUS treated their educational game as a research object, which is rare at any level. It was designed against Self-Determination Theory with autonomy, competence and relatedness mapped to specific mechanics (free choice of parts, a shared leaderboard, facilitated peer discussion), and every simulation value published with its provenance. Evaluation used paired pre- and post-surveys via Microsoft Forms where participants generated the same anonymous linking code on both forms from details only they would know, so before-and-after answers could be matched without collecting names or emails. They deployed across six settings — Project STEMpact Workshop 3 with secondary school students, NCEB Engineering Biology Week FUTURES Day with polytechnic students, two undergraduate modules (SP2718C and BN4501), NUS Telegram channels and a Raffles Girls' School email blast — getting 58 pre-surveys and 27 post-surveys between 5 September and 18 October 2026, of which 24 paired. The analysis is where it becomes exemplary: four Intrinsic Motivation Inventory subscales were shortened and reworded, then checked for internal consistency with Cronbach's alpha in their own data, which exposed that Perceived Choice at alpha 0.23 was not a single construct (its two items measured having choices and feeling obliged to play) so they reported them separately instead of averaging. Three items were negatively worded and reverse-scored to catch acquiescent responders. Attrition bias was tested — only 41% of pre-survey takers paired — and no item differed (all p > .18). Self-ratings on all three understanding statements rose 1.3-1.8 points (d = 0.76-0.95) and intent to pursue synthetic biology from 4.0 to 4.9 (d = 0.64), all surviving Holm correction. But they also scored free-text definitions as an independent check: promoters rose from 64% to 91% correct, sensors from 23% to 45%, genes stayed at 55%, and the overall written score moved only 1.4 to 1.9 out of 3 (p = .06). Crucially, self-ratings matched written answers before playing (rho = .65) and did not afterwards (rho = -.04) — participants felt they understood more than they could explain — so the team explicitly treats the self-reported gains as an upper bound. Their own headline finding is that participants valued the game more than they enjoyed it: the highest-rated statement in the survey was that the game is useful for understanding synthetic biology (5.5) and the lowest was about enjoying it while playing (4.3).","ihp":"The project's scope was set and then substantially narrowed by stakeholders. An application scientist at TJX Bioengineering established that a single failed batch causes substantial financial loss and shifted the team's attention from the product to the production process and the changing conditions cells experience; Anomaly Bio, a precision fermentation startup, established that conventional monitoring tracks physical and chemical parameters while the cell's inner workings are overlooked because they are hard to measure and hard to interpret. The largest single change came from Dr Bruce Li, Alan Yang and Prof Yong-Su Jin raising scale-up challenges, deployment barriers and startup resource constraints: in response the team moved ORCHID's primary intended application from continuous industrial monitoring to early-stage R&D, naming strain-engineering and process-development teams at startups as the primary users and dropping production-scale deployment as an immediate objective. Several design elements are individually attributable — Dr Jee Loon Foo's guidance set the initial oxidative and ER stress targets, Sheena Chan's feedback added the ATP depletion sensor, and Dr Bruce Li's concerns about whether the biosensors themselves perturb the cell led them to build parental-versus-biosensor-strain comparisons into the experimental design. Sook Wei explained how genome-scale metabolic models represent competition between growth and product formation but warned that conventional metabolic models may not match real cellular behaviour because of unaccounted byproducts, competing pathways and biological constraints, stressing that experimental data is what closes that gap and pointing toward combining genome-scale modelling with machine learning — advice visible in the final architecture, where a mechanistic ODE baseline is fitted first, shown to fail on stress and product, and only then replaced by a learned model. Later feedback also asked them to extend the model to weigh productivity, yield and cost together rather than optimising a single predicted output, which is recorded as future work rather than claimed as done.","realWorld":"The deployment story is deliberately modest after the industry reality check. ORCHID is positioned as an early-stage R&D platform for strain engineering and process development teams at startups, not as production-scale instrumentation, because the team accepted that industrial implementation carries technical and financial constraints they cannot yet meet. The workflow they specify is concrete: build or adopt modular biosensors for the analytes of interest in whatever chassis is being engineered, culture under varied process conditions, train the model on how conditions map to stress responses, use it to interpolate and recommend conditions, then run the organism under those conditions — with the intracellular data also making unintended effects on the host easier to spot. The framing they use for the value proposition is a grey box rather than a black box: in-line pH and dissolved oxygen sensors show how organism and environment affect each other, but when production is inefficient researchers still cannot tell which pathway is the constraint, and high-dimensional intracellular stress data gives a window into that. Because the biosensors are modular, they argue the collection builds toward a shared library: metabolic engineering teams can track the burden a heterologous pathway places on the cell, and environmental synthetic biology teams can assess strain robustness in harsh conditions. Troubleshooting is published as a first-class deliverable, with issues and fixes in segmented boxes down the results page specifically so later teams hit them less hard.","partners":["TJX Bioengineering (application scientist, cell culture bioprocess)","Anomaly Bio (precision fermentation startup)","Dr Bruce Li","Alan Yang","Prof Yong-Su Jin","Dr Jee Loon Foo","Sheena Chan","Prof Matthew Chang","Prof Dong-Yup Lee","Prof Tom Ellis","Sook Wei (genome-scale metabolic modelling)","Lucas Gelain","Goh Lay Koon","Chionh Yok Hian","Project STEMpact","NCEB Engineering Biology Week (FUTURES Day)","Raffles Girls' School"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6395/wiki/pages/model/ml-stuff/sable-slide-12-exploration-score.avif","caption":"SABLe's acquisition rule made visible: model disagreement across the ten bootstrap networks and distance from already-tested conditions highlight different gaps, and their combined exploration score first selected 27 C, 1 mM DTT and 0.15% galactose as the next culture to grow.","credit":"Team NUS-Singapore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6395/wiki/pages/model/ml-stuff/figure-18-27c-product-comparison.avif","caption":"The closed loop: measured mApple per OD600 at 0.2% galactose across DTT doses for 24, 27 and 30 C. The model-proposed 27 C plate reached 491 RFU/OD600 at 0.5 mM DTT, against 204 and 179 for the matched 24 and 30 C cultures the model was trained on.","credit":"Team NUS-Singapore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6395/wiki/pages/model/ml-stuff/sable-slide-14-latent-umap.avif","caption":"UMAP projection of the network's 64-dimensional internal response to 20,000 untested process conditions, the same points coloured by DTT dose, predicted stress and predicted product — the view that generated the testable hypothesis that higher DTT accompanies both more stress and more product.","credit":"Team NUS-Singapore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6395/wiki/pages/outreach-and-education/game/game-fig2-written-definitions.avif","caption":"Share of 22 paired participants giving a correct written definition before and after playing the circuit-building game — the independent check that showed smaller gains than self-ratings and led the team to treat the self-reported improvement as an upper bound.","credit":"Team NUS-Singapore, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Two things are directly copyable and neither needs a bioreactor. First, NUS's active-learning loop: fit a simple mechanistic model, show where it fails, train a small neural net on your own plate data, then use ensemble disagreement plus distance from tested conditions to pick the next experiment — and actually run it, so the write-up ends with a measured confirmation rather than a prediction. Second, and more unusual, evaluate your outreach the way you evaluate an assay: paired pre/post surveys with anonymous linking codes, reverse-scored items to catch acquiescence, Cronbach's alpha on any scale you shorten, attrition checks, and a free-text knowledge score as an independent check on self-reports. NUS found their participants felt they had learned more than they could explain, and said so — that kind of honesty about one's own teaching is both rare and cheap to adopt.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/experiments","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/implementation","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/nus-singapore/safety-and-security","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/nus-singapore/model"],"rank":105},{"team":"Kyoto","id":"6399","section":"Undergrad","country":"Japan","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/","projectTitle":"OiLipase — a protein timer that stops rice-bran lipase without destroying rice-bran protein","tagline":"Degrade the enzyme that spoils rice bran oil, then build a molecular timer that degrades the protease once the job is done.","summary":"Rice bran goes rancid because endogenous lipase attacks the oil after milling. Kyoto tethered trypsin to an interface-seeking carrier so it preferentially cleaves lipase at the oil-water interface, then faced the real problem: once the lipase is gone, unselective trypsin starts eating the valuable albumin and globulin. Their answer is a protein timer — a split HRV 3C protease whose N-terminal half is sequestered by an excess decoy that is also a 3C substrate, producing an autocatalytic, tunable lag before 3C fires and destroys the trypsin. Three models carry the project: an ODE timer model with full sensitivity analysis, an induction-diffusion model that derives an interior optimum for leucine-zipper dissociation rate, and a protease-resistance design model that failed its own experiment and was then rebuilt into CleaveRank, released under GPL-3.0.","awards":["Best Part Collection — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Food & Nutrition Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three distinct models, and the first thing to notice is that each one is a piece of mechanistic reasoning rather than a simulation run for its own sake. (1) TIMER MODEL — a competing-substrate ODE system for a split-protease delay circuit. Species: A (N-terminal split HRV 3C + zipper AN3.5), B (C-terminal + BN3, Kd 279 nM), B' (decoy: zipper BN3.5 with Kd 5.15 nM plus a 3C cleavage site), AB (active 3C), AB' (inactive), and Tr (engineered trypsin). Because 3C sees Tr, B' and AB' as competing substrates, all three rates share a Michaelis denominator den = KM + [Tr] + rel*([B'] + [AB']), with rel being the designable relative cleavability of decoy versus trypsin. Zipper association is treated as at equilibrium, justified because heterodimeric leucine zippers associate in seconds while the lag spans minutes to hours — a reduction that drops k_on from the equations and takes the integration from four variables to two, with A recovered by solving a conservation law; the team notes no measured k_on exists for their zippers and that the equilibrium approximation was checked only against a kinetic version of their own model. Integration is SciPy solve_ivp with LSODA. The conceptual heart of the model is the derivation of WHY the lag scales with decoy amount: a first-order decoy-consumption reaction has a time constant independent of initial concentration, so adding more decoy buys no time; two independent routes to zeroth-order kinetics are identified — Route 1 enzyme saturation (rel*[B']_0 >> KM) and Route 2 sequestration ([B']_0/Kd_Bp >> [B]_0/Kd_B AND [B']_0 >> [A]_0, making [AB] proportional to 1/[B'] so [B'] cancels out of k[AB][B']) — and the team computes their own degree of saturation as 0.38, concluding Route 1 contributes only modestly and the lag is bought almost entirely by sequestration, with the two routes together pushing the order toward -1 and the delay toward quadratic in decoy. They then derive the general result that an n-th order consumption gives depletion time proportional to amount^(1-n), and show the measured local exponent runs 1.75 to 2.82, with the operative 'amount' being u = [B']_0 - [A]_0, the decoy not spent sequestering A. (2) INDUCTION-DIFFUSION MODEL — a transport-and-kinetics model of a two-component induced system (a trypsinogen-zipper-carrier 'Z form' at 0.99 uM and an enterokinase-zipper-carrier 'E form' at 0.01 uM, plus a 1.0 uM free zipper-trypsinogen pool), where the carrier is an inactivated trypsin-resistant lipase that self-targets the oil-water interface. The mean reaction time is assembled from an induction rate k_ind = p_B/tau_search (bulk diffusion to the interface times adsorption probability), a reloading rate k_re = k_on * C_free_TG, the zipper off-rate and the interfacial reaction rate, giving tau_total = 1/k_ind + k_off/(k_ind k_re) + 1/k_off + 1/k_r. The insight that generalises: when the tethered enzyme cannot reach its target (K_act near 0), dissociation becomes an OBLIGATORY step on the reaction path, but dissociation that is too fast wastes transport — so there is an interior optimum in k_off, which means a catalogued leucine-zipper collection spanning a range of dissociation rates has real design value, and a covalent SpyTag/SpyCatcher linkage cannot work at all. They then tested the generalisation against six literature and past-iGEM systems (liposomal release, inhaled formulations, antibody-drug conjugates, Linkoping 2019, Imperial 2010, an intra-articular PLGA depot) and found only one case met both conditions — publishing the degenerate cases as figures. Reported gains: ~18.5x selectivity and 1.6x time-to-completion versus free trypsin, with 91% of water-soluble protein preserved against 0%. (3) PROTEASE RESISTANCE / CleaveRank — see datascience and software.","dataviz":"The timer model's figures are a model of how to present a sensitivity analysis. Figure 3 shows the lag directly (no decoy: Tr below 90% in under a minute; with 3.0 uM decoy: a 27-minute lag) alongside the active-3C trace held near zero through the lag then rising sharply, so the reader sees the autocatalysis rather than being told about it. Figure 4 plots the decoy sweep on log-log axes and reports the LOCAL EXPONENT of the tangent (1.75 to 2.82) rather than claiming a single power law. Figure 5 sweeps the four waveform-shaping parameters in a top row of lag and rise time and a bottom row of sharpness (defined as lag divided by rise time), and the caption explains why kcat gets no panel — it only rescales the time axis and leaves sharpness exactly unchanged. A parameter table gives every sweep range with its resulting lag, rise and sharpness span, so a reader can see that Kd_Bp moves the lag from 8.3 min to 4.5 days and sharpness from 0.59 to 310. The induction-diffusion generalisation is visualised as six SVG figures of the objective function for six different reference systems, with captions that state the failure mode ('there is nothing to optimise', 'without the stage of induction the objective function degenerates', 'Pi_rel is not a design variable') — negative results drawn as carefully as positive ones. CleaveRank's output is organised into four quadrants on three axes (cleavability, impact, risk to activity) so design targets are chosen by rank within a quadrant.","datascience":"The protease-resistance work is the single most honest piece of dry-lab self-criticism in this cohort. The team wrote a 317-line script to design trypsin-resistant 3C protease variants: compute secondary structure and SASA, enumerate loops flanked by stable elements, evaluate with a 'slack' geometry term (slack = 3.3*(loop residues - 7) + 11.3 - anchor distance, from the 7-residue P4-P3' extension spanning 11.3 Angstrom that a protease requires), exclude everything within 14 Angstrom of the catalytic triad, and tier exposed Lys/Arg. Five variants were built. THE EXPERIMENT DENIED BOTH PREMISES: wild-type 3C was already trypsin-resistant across the whole tested range (5 uM substrate, 5-500 nM trypsin, 5-120 min, no cleavage detected), and four of five variants failed to fold. The team then re-ran the original script and diagnosed six specific defects in public. (i) Secondary structure was assigned in-house via a local Kabsch-Sander implementation instead of DSSP; against mkdssp 4.2.2 agreement was 0.761 with entirely one-sided error — 43 beta-strand residues called loop, 44 of 94 'loop' residues actually beta strand, 15 of 20 enumerated loops containing strand, and the largest-slack 'loop' 61-72 actually two beta strands joined by a turn. (ii) The replacement residue was never really chosen: after the exclusion rules the pool was six residues, within which Lys-to-Gln and Arg-to-Gln are uniquely maximal under BLOSUM62, so 'Gln everywhere' was a deterministic artefact — and the exclusions were hard-coded for chymotrypsin and elastase, proteases absent from the experiment. (iii) A crystal structure's B-factor column was read as pLDDT; given the X-ray structure 6KYZ of the same protein the script silently returns zero tier-A positions. (iv) Ranking used descending exposure alone, and later validation showed a structure-only predictor reaches AUC 0.4466 — indistinguishable from chance — so the original code ranked on the one axis that does not work while computing and discarding the axis (specificity) that does. (v) Thirteen thresholds were hand-set, and K175 entered the design by clearing a 14.0 Angstrom cut-off with 0.12 Angstrom to spare. (vi) Substitution sites were scored independently although they are not. Every defect was fixed: DSSP output only; a continuous specificity score built from 22,992 MEROPS records; risk = cleavability x impact; input structure provenance and MD5 recorded with a warning on replacement; an interaction layer for combined substitutions; and 1180 regression-test checks freezing every numeric value, with measurements that would move a threshold PRE-REGISTERED before they are run. Re-ranking the same ten positions under the fixed implementation spreads them 22-fold and puts mut1's K82 and R84 first and third — mut1 being the only variant that folded. Validation went from 4 systems to 48 systems and 9 proteases, giving a mean AUC of 0.7186 over the 47 systems with positive sites, 95% CI [0.642, 0.791] — with the explicit caveat that per-system performance spans [0.38, 1.00] so the interval must not be applied to a single target. Benchmarks: against ExPASy PeptideCutter rules (which by construction cannot rank within their own set, AUC 0.5) CleaveRank reaches 0.7225; against the trained sequence model ProsperousPlus over 45 systems, 0.9305 vs 0.8957 over all residues and 0.7787 vs 0.6873 within the candidate set, with sensitivity 45/45 vs 36/45, paired difference +0.0914 [+0.0043, +0.1855], winning in 25 of 45 — and they then decompose that advantage and report that it CANNOT be attributed: sequence-only features tie the comparator (+0.0036), and neither the structural factors nor the impact weight reaches significance alone, so 'part of the advantage rests on an uncalibrated weight'. They also rule out contamination by stratifying into systems that may and may not have been in ProsperousPlus's training data, showing the gap widens in the unseen stratum. Finally, they tested whether a structure predictor could do the job instead: AlphaFold3 given human trypsin plus LIP2 plus Ca2+, five samples from one seed, with the trypsin-BPTI inhibitor complex as positive control. BPTI gave ipTM 0.95, minimum inter-chain PAE 0.78 Angstrom, P1 Lys15 carbonyl at 2.6 Angstrom from the catalytic Ser, 14/14 shared interface residues; LIP2 gave ipTM 0.14-0.15, PAE 15.3-15.8 Angstrom, nearest carbonyl 5.4-7.0 Angstrom and not at any cleavage site, 0 of 85 shared interface residues, and 23.8-29.3 Angstrom chain displacement — while both monomers were reproduced at 0.16-0.52 Angstrom RMSD. Their conclusion is a genuinely sharp piece of reasoning about method limits: structure prediction returns stable complexes, the PDB is full of protease-inhibitor complexes and almost empty of Michaelis complexes with folded globular substrates because those dissociate on cleavage, and susceptibility to cleavage is not a property of a stable complex — 'this asymmetry is the mechanism of the result, and it will not be removed by scale'.","software":"CleaveRank, a command-line tool released under GPL-3.0-or-later. It takes a target's sequence and structure, the residues whose cleavage abolishes function, and the cleavage rules and complex structure of the protease you want resistance against; it ranks the sites that will actually be cleaved, proposes concrete mutations (substitution, loop shortening, proline introduction, disulfide or salt-bridge insertion), evaluates each one's effect on activity and the interactions between them, then builds and relaxes the chosen structure in PyRosetta and verifies it on three axes. The development discipline is the lesson: 1180 frozen regression checks where the current output is the expected value so any code change that moves a number fails at the point it moved, expected values updated only for an intended change with the change recorded first, documented 'conditions under which the tool may and may not be used' fixed as explicit statements, and threshold-moving measurements pre-registered. The timer model's rate-equation derivation and lag derivation are published as separate companion PDF notes.","stack":["Python","SciPy solve_ivp (LSODA)","PyRosetta","mkdssp 4.2.2 (DSSP)","MEROPS database (22,992 records)","AlphaFold3 (tested as a comparator and rejected)","ExPASy PeptideCutter (benchmark)","ProsperousPlus (benchmark)","BLOSUM62","regression-test harness (1180 checks)"],"techniques":["competing-substrate Michaelis-Menten ODE system","quasi-equilibrium reduction of binding steps","conservation-law solving for free species","reaction-order analysis (zeroth-order routes to a tunable delay)","autocatalytic positive-feedback circuit design","analytical approximation and derivation of a lag expression","one-at-a-time parameter sweeps with lag / rise / sharpness metrics","local-exponent (log-log tangent) analysis instead of assumed power laws","diffusion-limited transport modelling (Damkohler number)","mean-first-passage-time reasoning","interior-optimum optimisation of a kinetic design variable","cross-system generalisation testing against literature and past iGEM projects","structural bioinformatics (DSSP, SASA, loop geometry)","continuous specificity scoring from a curated proteomics database","ranking-metric validation (AUC) with paired bootstrap confidence intervals","training-contamination stratification","ablation decomposition of a benchmark advantage","negative control design (BPTI) for a structure-prediction test","regression-test value freezing and pre-registration"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A dialogue-first mission — 'to communicate the fun of synthetic biology to a wide range of age groups through dialogue and hands-on experiences' — delivered at Kyoto University's November Festival, TEDxKyotoUniversity 2026 and CHILDREN'S PARK UNIVERSITY. Activities were hands-on with real research equipment: a broccoli DNA extraction booth with the buffer recipe published (38 g salt and 50 mL neutral detergent in 1 L tap water) so anyone can reproduce it, plus colour-change demonstrations. The stated method was to adjust the content and depth of explanation to each visitor's interest and level rather than deliver a fixed script.","ihp":"Kyoto draw a line most teams blur: 'not all HP constitute Integrated Human Practices'. They ran 19 interactions (interviews, site visits, email exchanges), state that 16 of them led to a design decision, and keep two separate pages — an HP page documenting everything and an IHP page tracing only the interactions that changed the design, decision by decision. They are also candid that they abandoned their chosen food-related project theme more than once after researcher conversations exposed real-world implementation problems, before arriving at rice bran. Stakeholder interviews with industrial processors and smaller milling businesses established that rice bran is not simply waste — it already flows to oil extraction, feed, fertiliser and household uses — which reframed the project question as whether better preservation and quality assessment can make higher-value uses feasible inside existing collection routines, and surfaced the key commercial constraint that bran-specific capital investment is hard to justify when the material has low sale value.","realWorld":"Rice bran is roughly 10% of brown rice by weight in Japan and contains about 20% oil plus dietary fibre, functional lipids and proteins, vitamins and minerals, but endogenous lipase starts hydrolysing triacylglycerides to free fatty acids as soon as milling disrupts the grain. Existing stabilisation is thermal (extrusion and heating), which costs energy and capital and affects bran components. The venture, OiLipase, has two parts: KANNAME, the enzymatic stabilisation system that localises protease activity at the oil-water interface to knock down lipase at ambient temperature, and NIINAME, a quality-assessment component. The team state plainly what remains undemonstrated in their own system: selective degradation of Lipase-II, preservation of other valuable proteins, and controlled termination of proteolysis. The computed dose is 115 mg/L, and the timer gives 91% preservation of water-soluble protein against 0% for free trypsin.","partners":["Kyoto University","COSMO BIO","MIYAKO CAPITAL","Twist Bioscience","IDT","IDEA BANK"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6399/wiki/dry/fig3-timer-core.avif","caption":"Figure 3: the timer working. Without decoy, trypsin falls below 90% in under a minute; with 3.0 uM decoy a 27-minute lag appears. The lower panel shows active 3C held near zero through the whole lag then rising sharply as the decoy runs out — the autocatalysis made visible rather than asserted.","credit":"Team Kyoto, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6399/wiki/dry/fig4-sens-decoy-1.avif","caption":"Figure 4: the decoy sweep on log-log axes. Rather than claiming a single power law, the team report the local exponent of the tangent moving from 1.75 to 2.82, consistent with their derivation that an n-th order decoy consumption gives depletion time proportional to amount^(1-n) and that two independent routes to zeroth-order kinetics push the system toward quadratic.","credit":"Team Kyoto, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6399/wiki/dry/fig5-sens-shape-2.avif","caption":"Figure 5: sweeps of the parameters that shape the waveform, with lag and rise time on the top row and sharpness (lag divided by rise time) below. kcat gets no panel because it only rescales the time axis and leaves sharpness exactly unchanged — the caption says so, which is how a sensitivity analysis should read.","credit":"Team Kyoto, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6399/wiki/dry/fig05-linkoping.svg","caption":"One of six figures testing whether the induction-diffusion generalisation applies to other systems. This one, for Linkoping 2019's reservoir-type design, is captioned 'there is nothing to optimise' — a negative result drawn with the same care as a positive one, which is how the team established that only one of six reference systems met both conditions for an interior optimum in dissociation rate.","credit":"Team Kyoto, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"When an experiment contradicts your model, treat the model's source code as the next object of study: Kyoto re-ran their own script, enumerated six specific defects (DSSP done in-house, B-factor read as pLDDT, ranking on the one axis that measured at chance AUC, thresholds cleared by 0.12 Angstrom), published every one, and then froze 1180 regression checks so no number can move unnoticed. Pre-registering any measurement that would move a threshold is a practice a school lab can adopt immediately and it is worth more than a bigger model.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/kyoto/entrepreneurship/"],"rank":21},{"team":"CalPolySLO","id":"6405","section":"Undergrad","country":"United States","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/","projectTitle":"Accessible Science: Expanding the Synthetic Cell Toolkit for All","tagline":"A cheap polyphosphate-powered energy module, a reproducible synthetic-cell fabrication device, and the calibrated ATP assay that makes both measurable.","summary":"Cal Poly SLO built a cell-free and synthetic-cell platform aimed at putting synthetic cells into undergraduate hands. The science problem is that PURE-type cell-free reactions run out of energy and that commercial creatine-phosphate regeneration is one of the most expensive parts of the kit, so the team screened a library of polyphosphate kinase 2 (PPK2) enzymes, filtered from roughly 18,000 sequences down to 16 phylogenetically diverse candidates, that regenerate ATP from cheap inorganic polyphosphate. To compare those enzymes they had to build the measurement first: a scalable, interference-corrected luciferase ATP assay with matched standard curves, published protocols, OpenTrons scripts and Excel templates. They also designed a 3D-printed emulsification 'washboard' and a tip-wipe recovery step to make hand-made vesicles reproducible, and wrote the whole thing into CHEM 471, a new undergraduate synthetic-cell lab course.","awards":["Best Alternative Platform — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Measurement — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Education — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Foundational Advance Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Design-Build-Test-Learn applied separately to the assay, to the 3D-printed emulsification device (v0 to v8) and to the course materials; an explicit 'problem -> approach -> solution -> outcome' framing on every major page; measurement treated as a prerequisite for enzyme screening rather than a reporting step; Human Practices feedback logged as 'feedback -> action' pairs that changed protocols.","dataviz":"Interactive charts embedded in the wiki (per-condition time courses, Michaelis-Menten fits, reproducibility datasets) with selectable conditions; side-by-side uncorrected vs corrected Michaelis-Menten fits; log-scaled ATP standard curves; per-cell log10 GFP scatter plots across 24 timepoints with per-timepoint median lines and a dashed per-plate subpopulation threshold; a two-clock explainer diagram teaching readers how to read the quench-validation figures; SolidWorks drawings of every finalized 3D-printed part.","datascience":"The core contribution is an ATP assay that was validated before it was trusted. Three quench methods were compared head to head (cold dilution, 95 C heat stop, EDTA chelation) by sampling a DrPPK2 reaction at fixed reaction times and then varying the hold time before adding CellTiter-Glo; a flat line means the reaction stopped, a rising line means it did not. EDTA was chosen because it scales to 16 enzymes in pre-poured plates, and the concentration was then tuned from 10 mM to 5 mM when enzyme load dropped from 50 to 7.5 ng/uL. The team then defined the assay's reliable ATP range by testing whether they could extend Promega's 10 nM to 1 uM window in either direction, found they could not (0.061 nM ATP still read above 10,000 RLU; the 100 uM standard deviated from linearity), and committed to reading only inside that range and never extrapolating, discarding data that fell outside. Because EDTA, AMP and ADP all suppress luciferase, every sample is compared against ATP standards prepared at the same pre-reagent EDTA concentration and the same substrate concentration, and the ratio of slopes gives per-condition correction factors (tabulated for all eight AMP and ADP levels at both 1:25 and 1:50 dilutions, e.g. AMP CF 0.0822 at 2 mM and 0.6113 at 0.0156 mM) that are hard-coded into the published Excel templates. Correcting recovers the kinetic trend that inhibition had destroyed: the same DrPPK2 AMP dataset moves from Km 0.020 mM / Vmax 5.66 uM/min uncorrected to Km 0.139 mM / Vmax 23.2 uM/min corrected, and the 2 mM AMP point is openly excluded from the fit because the correction does not fully recover it. Benchmark kinetics are reported with R2 (DrPPK2 ADP Km 0.985 mM, Vmax 11.7 uM/min, R2 0.9894; ManPPK2 ADP Km 2.67 mM, Vmax 42.6 uM/min, R2 0.9993; FtPPK2 ADP Km 3.16 mM, Vmax 80.05 uM/min, R2 0.9963). Reproducibility was tested as an experiment in its own right: three team members ran the same full-range assay on the same enzyme aliquot on the same day, giving Km 0.0775 +/- 0.0145 mM and Vmax 8.62 +/- 1.39 uM/min, and the team states plainly that these do not match the earlier full-range values and lists the candidate causes (different purification batch, new polyP lot, new AMP lot) as unresolved. Microscopy is handled by a named pipeline, CellScope, which runs Cellpose segmentation at 1.332 um/pixel after 4x downsampling, exports one row per object per timepoint to parquet (over a million objects on one plate), applies a QC gate on diameter 5 to 60 um, circularity 0.80 to 1.25 and no saturated pixels, reports each well as fold over its own background, and runs a two-mode (delta-BIC) test for subpopulations; a dedicated figure documents a case where the segmentation silently failed on a visibly good well, which the team published rather than hid. Every bulk plate carries a 0.125 uM fluorescein well as a dye reference, and the wiki maintains an explicit glossary distinguishing RFU (plate reader) from AU (microscope) as non-comparable units.","software":"OpenTrons OT-2 Python protocols for dilution-plate creation and dilution execution (full-range and confirmatory, four enzymes), published as .py downloads; Excel analysis templates for the full-range AMP, full-range ADP and confirmatory assays with correction factors built in; a master-mix calculator, titration design workbooks, plate-map template and osmolarity worksheet; the CellScope image-analysis pipeline (Cellpose segmentation, OzaLabCP/Imageanalysis) with a published vesicle_pipeline.txt; STL files for the washboard insert and rubber-band sled; raw time-course data and raw image data/masks released. All downloads are collected on https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/downloads and the lab manuals are released under CC BY 4.0.","stack":["Promega CellTiter-Glo 2.0 luciferase ATP assay","Promega GloMax luminometer","BioTek Cytation5 plate reader","Cephla Squid automated fluorescence microscope","OpenTrons OT-2 liquid handler","Cellpose / CellScope segmentation pipeline","Python","Parquet","Excel analysis templates","SolidWorks","FDM 3D printing"],"techniques":["Matched-condition standard curves","Interference correction factors","Quench-method validation","Defined linear measurement range (no extrapolation)","Michaelis-Menten fitting with R2","Inter-operator reproducibility testing","Fluorescein dye reference wells","Image segmentation with explicit QC gates","Bimodality testing (delta BIC)","Osmolarity control","DBTL hardware iteration"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"The team designed CHEM 471: Engineering Synthetic Cells, described as the first known undergraduate synthetic-cell course in the US, wrote and tested ten open-licence (CC BY 4.0) lab manuals plus an instructor packet with prep lists, reagent costs, timing and common failure points, taught it to ten students in spring 2026, and surveyed them before and after. Confidence rose in 9 of 12 skills; the three that did not became design inputs. Students rated fluorescence microscopy lower at the end than at the start, so the microscope protocols were rewritten and re-tested by the team's least experienced members; manual cytosol-recipe maths was replaced by auto-scaling Excel calculators; NanoOrange/A280 protein quantification was replaced with a Bradford assay plus BSA standard curve; and the Promega AMP/ADP protocol was replaced with the team's own real-time assay. A student's suggestion became the tip-wipe oil-removal method that was then tested against the standard method.","ihp":"Consultations with ethicists, legal and biosecurity scholars and industry scientists were run as feedback-to-action pairs. Dr Hank Greely advised publishing safety and containment thinking before starting work, so the team published aims, protocols, lab manuals and safety forms. Dr Ryan Jenkins recommended survivability testing as risk-assessment evidence, so the team tested whether synthetic cells survive outside the tube across pH, salinity and real environmental water samples (Los Osos Creek wetland, Avila Beach ocean water, campus pond, tap and deionised water) and found the cells lyse outside pH 7 +/- 1, at seawater salinity and in every environmental sample; he also prompted a lab-notebook safety checklist. Dr Keith Robison raised dual-use scenarios, which produced a benefits-vs-misuse framework. Dr Tae Seok Moon raised public perception, which produced IRB-approved surveys (Cal Poly IRB #2026-194 and #2026-195) of 10 CSHL Summer Synthetic Biology participants and 30 Cal Poly FSURP students on comfort, trust and regulation. Dr Zachary Sun's view that education may be the realistic near-term application reshaped the project's scope toward enzyme characterisation and teaching.","realWorld":"The framing throughout is cost of access rather than novelty: PPK2 plus polyphosphate is pursued because it lowers the price floor of cell-free protein synthesis, and the 3D-printed washboard replaces a hand-raking step that varies between operators. The course is explicitly packaged as a template other undergraduate institutions can adopt, with reagent costs and set-up in an instructor packet, and the full protocol set released so other programmes can either reproduce the six enzyme-characterisation experiments or swap in their own enzymes. Six characterised PPK2 enzymes and sixteen candidate-panel enzymes are deposited as registry parts with their datasets linked.","partners":["Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory Summer Synthetic Biology Program","Cal Poly Frost Summer Undergraduate Research Program (FSURP)","b.next (Nucleus cytosol kit)","Promega","Dr Hank Greely (Stanford)","Dr Ryan Jenkins","Dr Keith Robison","Dr Tae Seok Moon","Dr Zachary Sun"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6405/wiki/measurement/meas-opentrons-deck.avif","caption":"The Opentrons OT-2 deck loaded with tip racks and plates; the robot was revived after five years to prepare the EDTA stop plates and the fixed 1:25 and 1:50 dilution plates for the ATP assay.","credit":"Team CalPolySLO, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6405/wiki/measurement/meas-glomax-reader.avif","caption":"The Promega GloMax plate reader used to convert quenched, diluted PPK2 reactions into luminescence, which is then converted to ATP concentration against EDTA- and substrate-matched standard curves.","credit":"Team CalPolySLO, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6405/wiki/measurement/meas-celltiter-glo.avif","caption":"CellTiter-Glo 2.0, the luciferase chemistry the assay is built on. Because the reagent carries an undisclosed amount of magnesium, the safe upper EDTA limit could not be calculated and had to be measured.","credit":"Team CalPolySLO, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6405/wiki/emulsion-device/emu-final.avif","caption":"The finalized 3D-printed emulsification device: a semicircle chassis with a ridged washboard insert and a 30-degree rubber-band sled, which shears cytosol into lipid-in-oil for four tubes at once in place of hand-raking a single tube along an empty rack.","credit":"Team CalPolySLO, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Before comparing anything, prove the measurement: validate the quench, establish the instrument's honest linear range and refuse to extrapolate outside it, then build a separate standard curve for every interfering condition and hard-code the resulting correction factors into the analysis template you ship. Then test reproducibility by handing the written protocol to people who have never run it, and publish the disagreement when their numbers differ from yours.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/","https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/alternative-platform","https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/emulsion-device","https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/calpolyslo/downloads"],"rank":47},{"team":"RDFZ-China","id":"6409","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/","projectTitle":"GlacierGuard","tagline":"Protect the ice. Leave less behind.","summary":"RDFZ-China engineered E. coli to produce bacterial cellulose (BC) and built GlacierGuard, a biodegradable BC composite membrane intended to slow localized glacier surface ablation. Three genetic systems support it: heterologous bcsABCD plus a pgm/galU UDP-glucose precursor boost for BC yield, a urease (ureABC) biomineralization module that precipitates white CaCO3 inside the BC fibre network to raise albedo, and a CspA cold-shock-protein module to lower the fermentation temperature needed on the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau. BC production reached 230.6 +/- 15.3 mg/L in C41(DE3), roughly 65.9x the empty-vector control, and covered ice retained 3.2 percentage points more mass than geotextile-covered ice in a refrigerator day-night simulation. The team deliberately deployed only commercially sourced (non-engineered) BC membranes during its Tibet field trial and removed all material before leaving.","awards":["Best Sustainable Development Impact — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"ESM-C protein language model scoring combined with a MuZero reinforcement-learning agent and Monte Carlo Tree Search over AlphaFold-predicted structure; Rosetta used for structural-energy scoring. Candidates ranked by AI Score = 0.5 x ESM-C percentile + 0.5 x Rosetta percentile.","dataviz":"Figure-based reporting of screening hit rates and benchmark score distributions, BC yield comparisons, ice-mass-retention curves, infrared transmission by membrane layer count, and a power/interest stakeholder map. The wiki also carries an interactive 3D 'Glacier Atlas' globe with five Chinese glacier records (Dagu, Azha, Urumqi No. 1, Baishui River No. 1, Mingyong).","datascience":"Benchmarked MuZero/MCTS against genetic algorithms, Beam Search and Random Search, and against SaProt, ESM-C and ESM-1v foundation models, using 50 candidate sequences per method and a hit rate defined as the fraction falling in the top 10% of the benchmark score distribution. MuZero/MCTS reached 24% hit rate vs GA 10%, Beam Search 8%, Random Search 0%; 40% vs 0% for all three foundation models. 1,855 unique BcsD variants were generated after 10,000 model updates; top candidate W19R + G113E (AI score 0.8445).","software":"No standalone released software tool; the dry lab is an in-house screening pipeline. The wiki itself is a hand-built server-rendered site with an interactive glacier atlas. Note: the /model path on this wiki serves leftover template content from an unrelated project and is not the team's dry lab.","stack":["ESM-C","ESM-1v","SaProt","AlphaFold","Rosetta","MuZero","Monte Carlo Tree Search"],"techniques":["protein language model variant scoring","reinforcement-learning sequence search","structural energy calculation","method benchmarking against GA/Beam/Random search","life-cycle and cost reasoning drawn from published BC LCA and techno-economic studies"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A 440-response public survey (online forms, social media, QR codes on posters) found 93.6% thought glacier protection 'very necessary' but 92.0% knew little or nothing about glacier blankets, and 70.5% named 'biodegradable and free from harmful substances or microplastic release' as a top material requirement. The team turned respondents' nicknames and messages into a physical team flag and carried it to the glacier. Other engagement: a visit to the China Quaternary Glacial Vestige Exhibition Hall in Beijing (interview with a staff member surnamed Ma); Xiaohongshu/RedNote science communication that deliberately shifted the message from 'how do we protect a glacier' to 'why protect glaciers and what can individuals do'; a campus exhibition of BC samples and expedition photographs; glacier-themed penguin badges; project slogans printed on racing apparel and a racing helmet sticker; and an online fundraiser for Tibet disaster relief after the August 2026 Gyirong Port debris-flow disaster. In Tibet the team ran outreach for about 150 students across three classes at Bomi County Middle School, built around local glaciers and ecosystems, with a Tibetan-language title and a strawberry DNA extraction experiment; feedback cards came back in both Chinese and Tibetan.","ihp":"This is one of the strongest high-school IHP chains of the year: 27 interviews, site visits and engagement activities organised under an explicit Why-How-What-Adaptation (WHWA) loop in which each entry states the change made. Dr. Donghui Shangguan (cryosphere scientist, glacier change) said in substance 'rather than trying to protect every glacier, we should first determine which glaciers should be prioritised,' and pointed out that glacier surfaces host microorganisms and pioneer plants; the team therefore abandoned 'cover as much glacier area as possible' and repositioned GlacierGuard as targeted protection of high-value, human-accessible sites (parks, research stations, culturally or scientifically significant glaciers), and rewrote its customer definition from bulk consumers to institutions with monitoring and impact-assessment capacity. Jiaojiao Hu of Polar Future warned that glacier winds lift and tear thin membranes and that the team's planned metal anchoring nails would add thermal conductivity and foreign material; she suggested using stones already present on site, and the team both adopted stone/biodegradable anchoring and converted her concern into measurable tests - 11.7 N peak force to failure under freezing conditions and a twisted BC rope carrying 9.5 kg. Professor Haibo Zhang told them a working genetic module does not guarantee an efficient host, so instead of keeping their assumed chassis they built bcsABCD in both BL21(DE3) and C41(DE3), measured yields, and switched to C41(DE3). Polar scientist Limin Xia said unmodified BC alone would probably not reach the needed optical performance and recommended biomineralisation whitening plus attention to infrared wavelengths - this is the direct origin of the ureABC/CaCO3 System 2, and of the added water-absorption (842.0% mass gain) and layered infrared transmission tests (1200 W/m2 bare, falling to 605/370/340 W/m2 for one/two/three layers). Associate Professor Xuan Yu of Xihua University was consulted about using highland barley (qingke) liquor residues as a cheap local carbon source (an idea seeded by iGEM team BUCT-China); she judged the residue route technically difficult and instead recommended the cold-shock protein CspA to cut fermentation heating, which became System 3, while the barley residue was honestly demoted to 'future investigation, yield unmeasured.' Arctic researcher Dr. Ma Liheng pushed them on a hypothetical polar bear ingesting the membrane, which triggered a comparative soil-burial test (BC fully degraded in 7 days, geotextile unchanged) and an explicit admission that wildlife-ingestion safety is unresolved. Professor Jian Zhang (Tsinghua) attacked the evidence base - summer-only observations cannot represent annual glacier trends, biodegradation in soil does not predict biodegradation at glacier temperatures, retention and refreezing on sloping ice is unproven, and someone must pay for production, transport, installation and maintenance - and the team responded by date-stamping its late-August observation window, flagging those uncertainties as open, and adding campus exhibition and Glacier Casualty List-style storytelling to its plan. Negative results are reported with equal weight: essential-oil hydrophobic coating failed the contact-angle test and was dropped, mechanical reinforcement was dropped because the unmodified membrane already passed the tests and extra layers would hurt biodegradability and cost, and a judge's suggestion (Ho Chun Loong, Shenzhen SynBio Challenge) to add ice-nucleating proteins was researched and then explicitly rejected because INPs address nucleation rather than meltwater runoff or radiation absorption. The stakeholder map is two-dimensional (power x interest) and, unusually, names non-human stakeholders - glacier ecosystems, glacier microbial communities, alpine ecosystems and downstream aquatic ecosystems - as parties whose needs must enter design decisions.","realWorld":"Implementation is specified down to a four-step field procedure (site preparation to remove sharp gravel, membrane placement with overlapping sheets and minimal trapped air, biodegradable edge anchoring, final inspection), with membranes dried, cut, folded or rolled for compact transport and hand-carried in batches to glaciers vehicles cannot reach, installed in a cold window before the melt season. Named customers are glacier conservation organisations, national park and nature reserve authorities, high-altitude local governments and restoration/adaptation programmes, with glacier tourism sites and high-altitude ski resorts as early pilot hosts. The regulatory path is real rather than gestural: the team read the Ecological and Environmental Code of the People's Republic of China and the Regulations of the Tibet Autonomous Region on Glacier Protection, emailed iGEM Headquarters and submitted an Environmental Risk Assessment before fieldwork, used only commercial non-engineered BC on the ice, and removed every membrane before leaving. SDG claims are tied to named sub-targets with the team's own evidence: 13.1/13.2/13.3 (melt-reduction test, policy reference materials, climate education), 12.2/12.4/12.5/12.8 (BC life-cycle, cited cradle-to-gate LCA by Forte et al. 2021, residue reuse), 9.5 (industrial fermentation feasibility, citing Dourado et al. 2016), 6.1/6.4/6.6 and 15.1/15.4, plus a candid spillover analysis flagging SDG 7 energy demand from scale-up, SDG 8 high-altitude worker safety, and an SDG 10 equity risk that high deployment cost would concentrate protection in well-funded locations. On cost the team refuses to overclaim: it states that agricultural by-products do not automatically make BC cheaper, that the alternative-carbon-source process is unvalidated and its yield unmeasured, and that a full-life-cycle cost model covering production, maintenance, replacement, waste treatment and recovery - expressed as cost per unit area and per unit of ice melt avoided - must precede any scale-up. Next steps are paired control/treatment plots on real glaciers with continuous monitoring through research stations such as the Aza Glacier Citizen Science Monitoring Site (now part of Polar Future's long-term observation network), plus a standing offer from Professor Xian-En Zhang (Shenzhen University of Advanced Technology) to host further experiments.","partners":["Polar Research Institute of China (virtual tour led by Dr. Wang Luo)","China's Zhongshan Station, Antarctica (station leader Cui Xiangbin)","Dr. Ma Liheng, Arctic field researcher","Dr. Donghui Shangguan, glacier/cryosphere scientist","Jiaojiao Hu, Polar Future","Limin Xia, polar expedition scientist","Professor Haibo Zhang","Professor Weilin Xu","Professor Jian Zhang, Tsinghua University","Associate Professor Xuan Yu, Xihua University","Professor Xian-En Zhang, Shenzhen University of Advanced Technology","Lanzhou University Chayu Monsoon Corridor Multisphere Research Station, Tibet","Southeast Tibet Observation and Research Station for the Alpine Environment, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Aza Glacier Citizen Science Monitoring Site","Daosheng Bio fermentation facility","China Quaternary Glacial Vestige Exhibition Hall, Beijing","Bomi County Middle School, Tibet","iGEM team BUCT-China","Ho Chun Loong, Shenzhen SynBio Challenge judge"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6409/wiki/human-practices/img16.avif","caption":"GlacierGuard stakeholder map plotted on power and interest axes, including non-human stakeholders such as glacier microbial communities and downstream aquatic ecosystems.","credit":"Team RDFZ-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6409/wiki/human-practices/img18.avif","caption":"The team interviewing glacier scientist Dr. Donghui Shangguan, whose advice to prioritise specific glaciers rather than cover area redefined the project's target users.","credit":"Team RDFZ-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6409/wiki/human-practices/img55.avif","caption":"Temporary on-site placement of a commercially sourced (non-engineered) bacterial cellulose membrane on the Aza Glacier surface during the Tibet field investigation; all material was removed before leaving.","credit":"Team RDFZ-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6409/wiki/human-practices/img26.avif","caption":"Nature education session at Bomi County Middle School in Tibet, linking glaciers to forests, wetlands and grasslands for roughly 150 local students.","credit":"Team RDFZ-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Adopt RDFZ-China's Why-How-What-Adaptation template, where every stakeholder entry must end in a named design change or an explicitly rejected option, and copy their habit of converting a stakeholder's worry into a measurable bench test (wind-tearing concern becomes an 11.7 N tensile figure; reflectivity advice becomes an infrared transmission curve).","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/safety-and-security","https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/team","https://2026.igem.wiki/rdfz-china/education"],"rank":7},{"team":"NFLSHS-CHINA","id":"6439","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/","projectTitle":"CroTeen","tagline":"Microbial crocetin: a controllable cell-factory supplement to saffron farming, taken all the way to B2B ingredient specs","summary":"NFLSHS-China built a fully heterologous crocetin pathway in E. coli (idi, CrtE, CrtB, CrtI, CrtY, CrtZ upstream to zeaxanthin; CsCCD2 plus Neurospora crassa NcALD downstream to free crocetin), then re-engineered it after wet-lab failure from a two-plasmid system into a single-plasmid, two-T7-cassette construct (pET-Ds-Cro) for strain stability under scale-up. They used AlphaFold3, PrankWeb and CB-Dock2 to guide semi-rational mutagenesis of the rate-limiting CsCCD2 enzyme, with the N366G mutant giving a 1.13-fold yield increase confirmed by HPLC. The commercial side is unusually disciplined for a high-school team: a named B2B beachhead, a filed and accepted patent application on the CsCCD2 mutant, two physical MVPs with full formula tables, and an explicit separation of what is designed from what is still unvalidated. They won Best Entrepreneurship and Best Presentation in the High School section.","awards":["Best Presentation — Winner (High School)","Best Entrepreneurship — Winner (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Lean Canvas (problem, solution, UVP, key metrics, customer segments, channels, unfair advantage, cost structure, revenue streams), TAM-SAM-SOM with an explicit refusal to take a percentage of the macro market, SWOT, a risk-assessment matrix, a stage-gated Gantt chart from 2026 to 2032, three named exit options, and Techno-Economic Analysis plus Life Cycle Assessment named as future obligations.","dataviz":"Nineteen numbered entrepreneurship figures: pathway maps for the two-plasmid and single-plasmid constructs, the biomanufacturing-to-application three-stage chain, a capability-gap table, a partnership-network map with a disclaimer about which relationships are confirmed, a risk matrix, a SWOT quadrant and an SDG panel. HPLC chromatograms carry the quantitative claims.","datascience":"HPLC quantification of crocetin as the core analytical readout; mutant screening comparing predicted variants against measured titre (N366G at 1.13x). No fermentation kinetics model or cost model with numbers.","software":"","stack":["AlphaFold3","PrankWeb","CB-Dock2","HPLC","pET-Duet-1 backbone","E. coli BL21(DE3)","E. coli DH5-alpha"],"techniques":["heterologous carotenoid pathway assembly","single-plasmid multi-cassette expression with RBS re-tuning","semi-rational enzyme engineering from binding-pocket prediction","site-directed mutagenesis","vacuum freeze-drying and bead-beating extraction","soft-capsule and essence-oil formulation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"An online survey plus street interviews run 12-17 March 2026 across mixed demographics found over 60 percent of respondents had never heard of crocetin and routinely confused it with crocin, and that over 40 percent would pay only 5-10 RMB per day for a mood-soothing product. That cognitive gap triggered a dedicated education page and a Xiaohongshu (Rednote) account translating biosynthesis into public infographics. They also presented at the 10th iSCRM and the 1st Grand APiC conference, and built peer resources after hearing that team members elsewhere felt unsure their assigned role mattered.","ihp":"Five explicitly staged iHP phases, each written as motivation, action and integration, with dates and named interlocutors. Phase I traced the supply chain to source: an online interview with Iranian saffron grower Ali Norouzi (17 March) produced concrete constraints (15-20 day harvest window, 1-2 g dried saffron per person per day, water shortage, currency volatility), and visits to Daofengtang Rende TCM Clinic and chain pharmacies produced a real price point of 60-90 RMB per gram and the observation that pharmacies barely stock it. Phase II is the strongest: after near-zero crocetin yield on 12 May, Dr Huang Danqiong diagnosed metabolic burden and plasmid instability and told them to collapse to a single plasmid, which they did; Prof Huang Guanqin then reviewed the single-plasmid circuit against scale-up cost on 20 June; Fapon's technical director on 22 July pushed them off blind screening onto AI-guided rational design. Phase III used Associate Professor Li Chenyang twice to kill an unstable aqueous formulation on physicochemical grounds and pick the oil and capsule routes, and a perfumer, Ms Weng, taught the actual blending process. Phase IV took the business plan to Attorney Zhang on 17 September for NMPA/FDA classification, licensing and labelling risk, which changed their claims language across the wiki and social media. This is iHP that visibly rewrote both the construct and the marketing copy.","realWorld":"This is a real venture attempt, not a canvas exercise. The business model is specifically B2B-first: the beachhead is small-to-medium functional skincare brands and boutique cosmetic R&D labs, chosen because they have shorter decision cycles than multinationals, and the first deliverable is explicitly not a consumer product but standardised samples plus HPLC chromatograms to support a customer's own formulation prototyping. Market evidence is real but qualitative: interviews across the full chain (Iranian grower, TCM clinic and pharmacies, boutique formulators, Fapon, an iGEM-mentor founder, a lawyer), a public survey with reported percentages and a willingness-to-pay band, and a visit to Shenzhen University's fermentation facility. They refuse to multiply the roughly USD 2.0 billion TAM by a made-up share and instead say SOM must be computed from reachable R&D nodes times sample volume per test, which is the correct instinct even though they then do not finish the arithmetic. Hard assets exist: a patent application, Carotenoid Cleavage Dioxygenase 2 Mutant, Preparation Method and Application, filed and accepted, and two physical MVPs with published formula tables down to percentages (essence oil at 0.03 percent crocetin, 0.5 percent vitamin E in cold-pressed olive oil; capsule at 70 percent crocetin-olive oil, 15 percent L-theanine, 13 percent soy lecithin, 2 percent vitamin E). The regulatory path is credible and specific, naming NMPA and FDA classification, production and distribution licensing, and the prohibition on medical claims, with a consequence they actually implemented, standardising functional claims site-wide. The continuation plan is a stage-gated Gantt from 2026 lab validation through 2027 customer testing, 2028 CDMO pilot and 2029 B2B commercialisation to 2030-2032 international registration, where each gate has named prerequisite resources and a numeric-in-principle pass condition, plus three exit routes (trade sale to BASF, Givaudan or DSM-Firmenich; technology licensing with royalties; VC/PE buyout). What is genuinely missing is the money: there are no unit economics, no cost of goods, no price per gram, no funding raised, no registered entity and no incubator place, and the partnership map carries its own honest disclaimer that everything except university support and paid service relationships is a prospective contact rather than a formal agreement. They also list, in their own words, the data still absent before industrialisation, including yield, purity, recovery, batch consistency and pilot-scale mutant validation. Even the downside is faced squarely, including the possibility that fermentation's carbon footprint exceeds agriculture's and that cheap biomanufacturing could destroy saffron growers' livelihoods, for which they propose market segmentation that leaves the high-end plant premium alone.","partners":["Fapon (biotech company; technical director and business development; AI platform exposure and commercialisation advice)","Shenzhen University (Prof. Wang Chaogang, Prof. Huang Guanqin, Prof. Chen Weizhao; fermentation facility access)","Dr. Huang Danqiong (fermentation expert; diagnosed the two-plasmid failure)","Associate Professor Li Chenyang (formulation and safety screening)","Ali Norouzi (Iranian saffron grower and supplier)","Daofengtang Rende TCM Clinic and local chain pharmacies","Li Shiyuan (founder, Shanghai Transformant Information Technology Co.; iGEM mentor; technical evaluation)","Attorney Zhang (regulatory and IP legal review)","Ms. Weng (perfumer; essential-oil production process)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship8.avif","caption":"Patent application for the CsCCD2 mutant, filed and accepted, shown on the entrepreneurship page as the project's first hard IP asset","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship10.avif","caption":"Stage-gated development plan from 2026 laboratory validation through 2029 B2B commercialisation to 2030-2032 international expansion","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship9.avif","caption":"Business exit strategy: trade sale to an ingredient major, technology licensing with royalties, or VC/PE buyout","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship17v2.avif","caption":"SWOT analysis supporting the B2B-first ingredient strategy","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy their honesty architecture: a 'Current Validation Status' block that splits completed designs from data still required, and a partnership map annotated to say which relationships are confirmed and which are merely prospective contacts. Pair it with their stage-gate habit, where each phase of the roadmap names its prerequisite resources and the single pass condition that unlocks the next phase, so a project cannot drift forward on enthusiasm.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/product/"],"rank":52},{"team":"Jilin-China","id":"6456","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/jilin-china/","projectTitle":"A DNA-based recorder of transient fermentation stress in Pichia pastoris","tagline":"Turn fleeting glucose and oxygen limitation during a fermentation run into permanent, readable marks in DNA.","summary":"Jilin-China built a two-channel genetic event recorder: pGTH1 drives Cre (recording glucose limitation) acting on a lox71/lox66 pair, and PsADH2 drives FLP (recording oxygen limitation) acting on an FRT-LE/FRT-RE pair, each inversion flipping a fluorescent reporter read after the run. Five separate models answer five design questions that experiments could not settle: which synonymous FLP sequence to synthesise, how bulk glucose maps to a promoter input, whether an alginate capsule isolates its cells from the broth, what DNA states the construct can occupy, and which published promoter evidence actually applies. The most striking feature is epistemic discipline: nearly every claim is accompanied by a statement of what the calculation does NOT establish, and when the glucose model's predicted low-glucose crossing time (7.56 h) disagreed with the measured 0.11 g/L at 8 h, the team reported the disagreement and downgraded the 0.05 g/L figure to a scenario threshold rather than a promoter activation threshold.","awards":["Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Five models, each tied to a design action. (1) RecombiOpt — synonymous CDS design. It works in the real construct context (the 6,628 nt pET28a-FLP plasmid with a BL21(DE3) host profile), correctly identifies that translation starts at position 107 while FLP starts at 221 because of a 114 nt / 38 aa fusion tag, and computes accessibility as an opening free energy Delta_G_open = -RT ln P_unpaired for the contiguous 48 nt window [-24, +24) around the ACTUAL translation start using local RNA folding in ViennaRNA at 30 C with W = L = 210 nt. A greedy coordinate-descent local search over the first 10/15/30 FLP codons (max three rounds, methionine preserved) produced 'target5prime-30', which changed only 15 nt across 14 codons and cut opening energy from 10.6473 to 7.9007 kcal/mol while leaving CAI essentially flat (0.5184 to 0.5246), versus a constrained-maxCAI design that changed 352 nt to reach CAI 0.9948 but only reached 8.3520 kcal/mol. A complementary RNAstructure 6.6 analysis at 30 C compared MFE and mean single-base unpaired probability across the first 30, 90, 150 nt and full 1,272 nt CDS windows. (2) Glucose model — a coupled ODE of enzymatic glucose release, cellular uptake and OD growth. Sterile release was calibrated FIRST and separately in water and in YP medium (no parameter transfer between them) as a sum of first-order releasable pools dD_i/dt = -k_i D_i with r_h(t) = sum k_i D_i, giving the closed form G(t) = G_0 + sum A_i(1 - exp(-k_i t)); a one-pool model was retained unless adding a second pool cut fitting RMSE by more than 40%. Uptake and growth were then fitted jointly with shared parameters across conditions, with time and condition holdouts. (3) Capsule2Memory — a spherically symmetric reaction-diffusion PDE for an immobilisation bead, dG/dt = (D_eff/r^2) d/dr(r^2 dG/dr) - X q_max G/(K_m + G) with Monod uptake, solved under three distinct boundary regimes (prescribed surface concentration; a Robin condition D_eff dG/dr|_R = k_L[G_b - G(R,t)] for finite interfacial transfer; and a finite-bath case with instantaneous partitioning c_g(R,t) = K C_b(t) that solves bath and interior together including the sampling withdrawals). Four model variants are compared — M0 (follows input directly), M1 (diffusion only), M2 (diffusion plus uptake), M2-lumped (one uniform internal state with k_eff = 15 D_eff/R^2, where the factor 15 is derived from the steady-state mean deficit of a sphere with constant volumetric uptake) — and the page explicitly derives the exact volume-averaged balance and states why the lumped closure is an approximation, since Monod uptake is nonlinear so <G/(K_m+G)>_V is not G_bar/(K_m+G_bar). Numerics are a spherical-shell finite-volume discretisation with implicit BDF integration, solved separately across input discontinuities. (4) Recombistate — a discrete state machine over DNA sequences. The 16,098 bp final plasmid is annotated down to coordinates (lox71 at 4,767-4,800, lox66 at 6,050-6,083, FRT-LE at 6,272-6,305, FRT-RE at 7,784-7,817, with the reverse-strand FRT-RE reported in both genomic-forward and feature-oriented conventions), and the state graph is enumerated on an earlier 3,367 bp reporter fragment under specified Cre/lox and FLP/FRT rules, max two recombination steps, yielding four distinct DNA sequences, four transitions and five event histories. The key result is a negative one about observability: the two orderings of events A and B converge on the SAME final sequence, so the final DNA does not retain event order. (5) FermentPromoterBank — a promoter evidence registry (9 core constructs, 8 watchlist candidates, 252 discovery entries, 12 design entries) that keeps sequence boundaries, host and culture conditions in one record, explicitly described as not an activity predictor.","dataviz":"Twenty-two numbered figures, and the captions are the most instructive part of them. Each caption states the axis meaning, the exact computational settings used, and what the figure may not be read as: 'connecting lines guide the eye and do not represent a time course'; 'the panels use different metrics and horizontal scales'; 'Error bars and asterisks are reproduced from the original figure. Replicate counts, the error measure and the statistical comparisons are unspecified, so we do not infer P values or statistical significance from these marks'; 'the graph is a structural calculation under the registered FRT rule; it is not a measurement of four fluorescence channels'. Figure 3 plots mean paired gains with 95% intervals from 5,000 group-bootstrap resamples, and the caption notes the intervals come from saved out-of-fold predictions and do not include full-retraining uncertainty. Figure 1 is a model-dependency diagram that distinguishes solid arrows (within-module workflows and documented design selections) from dashed arrows (conceptual interfaces that are NOT a validated coupled prediction) — a distinction almost no iGEM team draws. Figure 4 shows the three-way trade-off (CAI, opening energy, nucleotide edits) as separate aligned panels rather than a composite score. Figure 20 is a conditional DNA-state graph that visually separates sequence states from event histories, making the two converging paths to S_CF legible.","datascience":"The machine-learning work is a model of restraint. They tested whether RNA structure features add information to expression proxy models by holding the ExtraTrees algorithm, base features and data splits fixed and only adding opening energy and local MFE, across three tasks (Boel expression levels, TIsigner detectable-expression labels, Nieuwkoop mRFP synonymous variants) with grouped five-fold validation and five random seeds — grouping by protein family for the first two and by experimental plate for the third, specifically to stop related samples appearing in both train and test. Gains were small but consistent: Boel QWK 0.1391 to 0.1677 (+0.0286 [0.0229, 0.0344]), TIsigner AP 0.8851 to 0.8997 (+0.0146), Nieuwkoop Spearman 0.7622 to 0.7906 (+0.0283), all five seeds positive — and they then note that even with RNA features the Boel QWK of ~0.168 means expression-level agreement 'remained limited', and that Boel and TIsigner share 2,363 protein-family components so positive results in both are not two independent replications. Crucially, they then DECLINED to use those models to rank their FLP candidates: an out-of-distribution protocol (MMseqs2 10-6d92c family search with predefined thresholds fident >= 0.30 and bidirectional coverage qcov, tcov >= 0.80, plus explicit feature distance, prediction disagreement and construct context) classified all 12 candidate-task combinations as OOD, so candidate selection was made on hard constraints (protein and vector invariance) plus a qualitative trade-off, with the team stating 'we did not add numerical weights after the fact' and that target5prime-30 'is not the unique optimum across every metric and window' since a five-window sensitivity analysis changed the ranking. Model selection followed predefined performance-and-complexity rules that retained simple linear models for two tasks and rejected frozen CodonBERT representations for lack of consistent gain. Wet-lab closure: FLP-Var (target5prime-30) gave a stronger anti-His Western signal than FLP-WT after 0.5 mM IPTG for 6 h and a higher OD600-normalised RFP recombination-reporter curve — reported as qualitative support only, with the team noting the two assays use different regulatory constructs, that OD600 normalisation does not equalise FLP amounts, and that no RNA-decay assay was run so the proposed hydrolysis-resistance mechanism remains a hypothesis. The glucose dataset is 138 concentration and OD600 records; six '<0.02 g/L' readings were retained as upper bounds rather than coerced to 0.02.","software":"RecombiOpt is a released tool (v0.3.0 noted) with host profiles for E. coli BL21(DE3) and P. pastoris GS115, a conditional synonymous generator, a separate 'engineering repair' step that searches for synonymous fixes to declared rule violations and then re-runs all checks, protein and vector invariant verification, and retained tool versions and complete parameters alongside every analysis. The team is explicit that a current optimize run cannot reproduce the entire historical study, and that repair 'addresses whether a sequence meets engineering requirements; it does not predict expression'. The validation table lists, per module, exactly which checks were completed and what evidence supports them — analytical and numerical checks plus replay of a public transport subcase for Capsule2Memory, comparison against an independent solver for the bacterial parameter example, mass-balance and numerical-refinement checks for the tracer experiment, grouped internal validation and OOD checks for RecombiOpt, sterile-source calibration plus joint fitting plus time and condition holdouts for the glucose model, and DNA/junction/convergence checks for Recombistate.","stack":["Python","SciPy (implicit BDF integration)","ViennaRNA (local folding)","RNAstructure 6.6","MMseqs2 10-6d92c","scikit-learn (ExtraTrees, linear models)","CodonBERT (evaluated and rejected)","custom finite-volume PDE solver","custom DNA state-machine enumerator (RecombiOpt / Recombistate)"],"techniques":["synonymous codon design with greedy coordinate descent","RNA local folding accessibility / opening free energy","RNA secondary-structure MFE and base-pair probability analysis","codon adaptation index with project-specific host reference","supervised regression and classification with grouped cross-validation","group bootstrap confidence intervals (5,000 resamples)","out-of-distribution applicability protocol with prespecified thresholds","predefined model-selection rules favouring simplicity","coupled ODE fitting with shared parameters across conditions","parsimony criterion for adding model components (40% RMSE rule)","time and condition holdout validation","spherically symmetric reaction-diffusion PDE with Monod kinetics","Dirichlet, Robin and finite-bath boundary conditions compared","volume averaging and lumped-parameter model reduction with derived closure","finite-volume spatial discretisation","mass-balance and independent-solver numerical verification","discrete DNA state-machine enumeration","event-history vs state-observability analysis","structured evidence registry"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A sustained programme rather than one-off events, organised under the 'Continue' step of their 5C Guidelines: the 5th Annual Community Day, the Apricot Blossom Festival, high-school and middle-school outreach, a Popular Science Series Course, a Summer Science Exploration Camp, senior-community outreach and a Sci-Tech Festival, reaching audiences across generations and educational backgrounds through lectures, hands-on activity and conversation. A promotion brochure is published as a downloadable PDF.","ihp":"The team built a named framework from the sociology-of-technology literature: a Social Shaping Loop of Encounter, Reflect, Respond, Rewrite, grounded in Argyris's distinction between single-loop learning (improving the existing method) and double-loop learning (questioning the assumptions behind it), layered on their own 5C Guidelines (Collect, Comprehend, Compromise, Continue) carried over from 2025, and framed by MacKenzie and Wajcman's The Social Shaping of Technology. They then applied the distinction honestly: single-loop work was adjusting recombinant expression conditions, codon optimisation, leakage-test conditions and hydrogel diffusion; the double loop was realising that a strong fluorescence signal does not establish industrial usefulness, and that the real questions are whether a recorded state accurately represents a relevant environmental event, whether the information stays interpretable after the event has passed, and whether the system works under representative fermentation conditions. Industrial engagement at BYHEALTH and vaccine and pharmaceutical production facilities surfaced stability, process compatibility, data interpretation and operational safety as criteria. Scientific exchange at the iGEM Northeast China Exchange and university visits fed directly into a modelling result: the Recombistate finding that different event sequences converge on the same final DNA state changed the design goal, pushing them toward a modular sensing-memory-readout toolkit while stating plainly that the toolkit is a direction rather than a validated set.","realWorld":"The application target is industrial fermentation monitoring: a recorder that preserves information about transient stress events (glucose limitation, oxygen limitation) that a bulk probe reading cannot capture, read out after the run. Alginate-style encapsulation is part of the deployment concept, which is exactly why Capsule2Memory exists — the model shows that splitting one large bead into several of equal total biomass shortens the diffusion distance, but that insufficient interfacial exchange can still produce substantial error during a long low-glucose event, so size, cell loading and exchange capacity must be screened together rather than separately. The team repeatedly marks the boundary of the claim, noting that glucose trajectory, promoter response and DNA writing have not yet been measured in the same experiment.","partners":["Jilin University","BYHEALTH","iGEM Northeast China Exchange (NECE)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6456/wiki/day-lab/model/model-1.avif","caption":"Figure 1: connections among the five models, with solid arrows for within-module workflows and documented design selections and dashed arrows for conceptual interfaces that are explicitly NOT a validated coupled prediction — a distinction that lets a reader see exactly where the chain of evidence is real and where it is aspiration.","credit":"Team Jilin-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6456/wiki/day-lab/model/model-4.avif","caption":"Figure 4: the three-way trade-off between codon adaptation index, predicted RNA opening energy of the 48 nt translation-initiation region, and number of nucleotide edits, shown as three aligned panels. The locally optimised candidate changed 15 nt and reached the lowest opening energy (7.90 kcal/mol); the full-length maxCAI design changed 352 nt to reach CAI 0.9948 but only 8.35 kcal/mol.","credit":"Team Jilin-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6456/wiki/day-lab/model/model-12.avif","caption":"Figure 12: dynamic responses to low-glucose events under equal-resource encapsulation designs, from the spherically symmetric reaction-diffusion model with Monod uptake. Splitting one bead into several of the same total biomass shortens the diffusion path, but finite interfacial exchange can still mistime a long low-glucose event.","credit":"Team Jilin-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6456/wiki/day-lab/model/model-20.avif","caption":"Figure 20: the conditional DNA-state graph for the reporter fragment, which separates sequence states (S0, SC, SF, SCF) from event histories. The two paths into SCF are different orders of the same two events reaching an identical final sequence, so the recorder cannot report event order — a negative result that changed the design goal.","credit":"Team Jilin-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Write every figure caption and every model claim with an explicit scope limit attached — what this calculation does not establish, which constructs and conditions it covers, which arrows in your diagram are validated and which are aspirational — and when a prediction disagrees with a measurement, publish the disagreement and demote the prediction rather than quietly dropping it. Jilin also shows the right way to use an off-the-shelf ML model: run a prespecified out-of-distribution check first, and if your target fails it, do not use the score.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/jilin-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/jilin-china/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/jilin-china/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/jilin-china/human-practices/"],"rank":7},{"team":"Eton-College-UK","id":"6462","section":"High School","country":"United Kingdom","village":"Infectious Diseases, Therapeutics, Diagnostics & Oncology","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/","projectTitle":"ProteaSign","tagline":"A point-of-care electrochemical sensor that quantifies individual protease activities in chronic wound exudate in about ten minutes.","summary":"ProteaSign is a disposable-cartridge electrochemical diagnostic that separately measures MMP-9 (which degrades the extracellular matrix) and human neutrophil elastase (which degrades the growth factors needed for repair) in chronic wound fluid, turning wound assessment from a visual judgement into a measurement. The sensing interface is a gold electrode carrying methylene-blue-labelled, protease-cleavable peptides whose cleavage is read by square-wave voltammetry, with an independently anchored ferrocene reporter providing internal ratiometric normalisation and a matched non-cleavable peptide control on a second working electrode. The peptide sequences were not guessed: the team built a seven-protease machine-learning substrate predictor trained on 10,472 curated cleavage windows, and validated the predictions with two reprogrammable recombinant sfGFP protease biosensors. It won on the rare combination of genuinely publication-grade dry lab (cluster-aware cross-validation, Laplace-transform kinetics derivations, a physics-based SWV forward model), working hardware, and a human practices programme with over twenty named clinical and industrial stakeholders that visibly moved the design.","awards":["Top 10 (High School)","Best Education — Nominee (High School)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Nominee (Infectious Diseases / Therapeutics / Diagnostics / Oncology)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three distinct modelling efforts, all unusually deep for high school. (1) A protease substrate predictor: for each of seven proteases (MMP-2, MMP-8, MMP-9, MMP-14, neutrophil elastase, LasB, and a pooled digestive serine protease group) they trained two additive scorers on 8-residue cleavage windows — an L2-regularised logistic regression on VHSE physicochemical descriptors (8 descriptors per residue, so 64 standardised features) and a BLOSUM62-smoothed position-specific scoring matrix — using 10,472 confirmed windows from MEROPS, Eckhard et al. 2016 proteomic MMP profiling, and a re-extracted LasB elastin-digestion table from Yang et al. 2015. Negatives were constructed 3:1 (half other proteases' real windows, half random Swiss-Prot 8-mers at natural amino-acid frequencies). (2) A surface-bound enzyme kinetics model: they derived both the steady-state solution and the exact biexponential solution for Michaelis-Menten kinetics on a surface-attached substrate using Laplace transforms and partial-fraction decomposition, then proved analytically and numerically that the steady-state approximation is only valid as their single parameter lambda approaches 1. (3) A square-wave voltammetry forward model (swv_forward_model.py) that computes net SWV current from probe-level physics — about 3.1e10 probes at 7.4 nm spacing, with a 1.4 nm methylene-blue lateral excursion used to justify treating probes as non-interacting.","dataviz":"The substrate predictor page ends in a live in-browser scorer ('Try It — Score a Peptide'): you type a peptide, mark the scissile bond, and every protease in the panel scores it as you type, with warm/cool per-position attribution bars that sum exactly to the logit and the PSSM score, and grey bars for unknown (gapped) positions. The sensor probe itself is shown scored at every bond by the MMP-9 and elastase models, with the P4-to-P4' window shaded and the actual cut marked. The SWV page carries an interactive forward-model plot where moving across reads every predicted current curve at one potential. Results are presented as out-of-fold ROC curves, precision-recall curves against a 0.25 prior, and held-out percentile distributions against a uniform null, plus paired difference plots with 95% cluster-bootstrap intervals where a filled marker means the interval excludes zero. Equations are rendered in KaTeX and the human practices page uses globe.gl.","datascience":"The evaluation methodology is the real achievement and is more careful than most undergraduate iGEM dry lab. They recognised that near-duplicate cleavage windows cause data leakage under ordinary K-fold CV, so they defined Hamming-distance clusters (any pair with d_H <= 2, taken as connected components, found in near-linear time via a 3+3+2-block pigeonhole trick with union-find) and used stratified group 5-fold splitting whole clusters, with a nested 3-fold inner CV to pick the regularisation strength C per protease, and the null percentile distribution and PSSM rebuilt per outer fold to avoid leaking into the background. They separated two questions — Detection (held-out positives vs random background) and Discrimination (held-out positives vs another protease's confirmed sites), noting the latter is what actually matters for a multiplexed sensor. They derived and Monte-Carlo-verified an analytic ceiling on measured AUROC under false negatives (measured AUROC <= 1 - pi/2 for a fraction pi of mislabelled negatives), ran three controls (label shuffle collapsing AUROC to 0.49-0.53; composition-only ablation showing e.g. 0.664 vs 0.874 for MMP-2 but 0.886 vs 0.877 for LasB because 76% of its positives come from elastin; and random vs cluster-aware folds, where LasB drops 0.886 to 0.879 once leakage is removed). Scores from the two models are converted to percentiles against 100,000 random Swiss-Prot background windows so they are comparable across proteases, combined by harmonic mean (high only when both are high), and reported as a 'specificity margin' = target consensus percentile minus the strongest competitor's. They also published an explicit list of eight assumptions and limitations, including that additivity costs at most 0.007 AUROC against gradient-boosted trees, that VHSE PCA captures only 64-79% of the full positional signal, and that the harmonic-mean consensus was reasoned rather than validated.","software":"The substrate predictor is released as a protease-agnostic Python package (modules spred.data, spred.model, spred.pssm, spred.train, spred.validation, spred.evaluation, spred.panel) that another team retrains on its own protease panel's known substrates on a basic laptop in a few hours; the team explicitly frames it as a reusable contribution. Hardware: a handheld reader built around the Analog Devices AD5940 electrochemical analogue front end with an nRF52840 microcontroller and a TI TMUX1104 4:1 precision multiplexer, sequentially interrogating four working electrodes against a shared reference and counter electrode, with full KiCad schematics (5 sheets) and an exported bill of materials released as PDF. The hardware builds on the open-source Biocoin wearable multiplexed biosensing platform (github.com/ProfDrewHall/Biocoin; Hack & Hall, npj Biosensing 2026). They also contribute two reprogrammable recombinant protease biosensors with swappable substrate 'cassettes': pES-01 (FLAG-sfGFP / peptide / C-terminal His6, read by Ni-NTA depletion, ELISA, Western blot or SDS-PAGE) and pDUAL (split-sfGFP with GFP11 embedded in an eglin c surface loop, giving a fluorescence readout). Code and wiki at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/eton-college-uk.","stack":["Python","scikit-learn","NumPy","SciPy","logistic regression (L2)","position-specific scoring matrix (PSSM)","BLOSUM62","VHSE amino-acid descriptors","gradient-boosted trees (ablation)","MEROPS database","NCBI/UniProt","Swiss-Prot background frequencies","React + Vite wiki SPA","KaTeX","globe.gl","KiCad","AD5940 AFE","nRF52840","TMUX1104 multiplexer","GitLab (iGEM)"],"techniques":["cluster-aware stratified group K-fold cross-validation","Hamming-distance clustering with union-find + pigeonhole blocking","nested inner cross-validation for hyperparameter selection","cluster bootstrap confidence intervals (500 resamples)","AUROC and precision-recall evaluation","label-shuffle, composition-only and fold-construction controls","analytic false-negative bound on AUROC with Monte Carlo verification","percentile normalisation against a 100,000-window null","harmonic-mean consensus scoring and specificity margin","gapped-window masking (neutral logistic features, background-weighted PSSM expectation)","Laplace transform solution of surface enzyme kinetics","partial fraction decomposition","square-wave voltammetry forward modelling","ratiometric (MB/Fc) internal normalisation","self-assembled monolayer electrode functionalisation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A genuinely reusable education portfolio rather than a set of one-off visits. 'What If?' is a team-written and illustrated comic modelled on Marvel's What If...? series, built directly from the four ideas children drew at the Eton Porny workshop (colour-changing plants, bacteria making medicines, programmable cells, healing wounds); over 350 printed copies were distributed in two months and it was translated into two further languages. A 58-page Activities Handbook of printable exercises at graded difficulty, with instructions and answer keys written in conversation with a secondary school teacher, was inspired by Leiden iGEM 2025's approach to reusable materials. 'Bio-Detectives' is a museum-sabotage mystery workshop for Year 8 in which teams work five stations — reconstructing DNA sequences, exploring protein production, critiquing flawed experiments, comparing lab labels, and modelling genetic control through AND/OR/NOR gates — and must explain their reasoning to unlock each piece of evidence, ending in a fixed-budget redesign task; student and facilitator packs plus the presentation are released so it can be rerun. Year 11 volunteers facilitated and reported their own gains ('I'm not a science student but still really enjoyed it because of the mystery format'). They report honestly that the content was too hard for pupils who had not yet met DNA and recommend targeting Year 10/11 instead. Other sessions: a hands-on goat-DNA genetic modification build for 34 Year 5 pupils at St George's Windsor (physically assembling glowing / curly-hair / spider-silk gene templates from spare nucleotides), an edible animal-cell modelling workshop with a published allergen risk assessment, protease kits for a carers' workshop, street interviews (8 of N respondents had no personal experience of chronic wounds), and biosafety videos.","ihp":"Over twenty named stakeholders across wound care, electrochemistry, health economics, regulation and patient advocacy, with a 'pinpoint / event / action' structure that states what changed. Concrete examples: Dr Anatoliy Markiv (King's College London) moved them off pET-24a(+) to pSB1C3 as a reusable starting plasmid, reviewed their primers and proposed a better substrate-swapping method, and pointed them to the split-GFP protease-sensing paper that became their second design cycle; he also required construct sequences for biosafety assessment before permitting experiments. Dr Ben Bullen (podiatry) cemented the reusable-reader / disposable-cartridge split and the ~20 microlitre sample volume, and set a 15-minute workflow target against the team's estimated 30 minutes — which the team records as an unmet goal requiring shorter incubation. Ms Kathleen Leak (tissue viability) and Trish Idensohn drove the requirement for fast readouts and simple operating procedures, which produced the cartridge's standardised metering, dilution and incubation steps before SWV acquisition. Dr Honda Hsu (plastic surgeon, Taiwan) confirmed the device fits a real clinical workflow and his account of delegated wound care created an explicit usability requirement that routine dressing-change staff, not just specialists, must be able to run it. Dr Kevin Lye (Accorto Regulatory Solutions) and Dr Douglas Queen (health economics of wound care) covered the regulatory and reimbursement path; Dr Martin Peacock (Zimmer and Peacock) and Professor Kevin Lam (synthetic electrochemistry, Greenwich) and Dr Ana Maria Totea advised on electrodes and liquid handling; Professor Keith Harding (Cardiff), Professor Steven Jeffery (Pioneer Wound Clinics), Professor Dato Harikrishna Nair, Professor Paul Davis (co-founder of Mologic) and Professor Eleanor Stride (Oxford biomedical engineering) reviewed the clinical and device case. Patient and charity voices included 'Bob', a person living with a chronic wound, Breakthrough T1D and Diabetes Scotland. Feedback also reinforced measuring cleavage rate with calibration across known enzyme concentrations. The anonymised 'David' narrative on the description page is explicitly constructed from real accounts heard through these conversations.","realWorld":"The implementation page is a structured device risk assessment rather than a business pitch: it enumerates infection transfer through reusable components, pain or damage to fragile healing tissue, allergic reaction to wound-contacting materials, unnecessary anxiety or treatment escalation from false positives, and exposure of identifiable patient data, each with a named mitigation (prefer single-use patient-contacting components, biocompatibility assessment of all wound-contacting materials, clinically justified interpretation criteria with evaluated specificity and false-positive rates, calibration and quality-control procedures with error alerts and clear invalid-test instructions, minimised personal data collection with access restriction and retention policies). The intended route to market is a reusable reader plus disposable cartridge for community wound care — GPs, wound-care nurses, podiatrists and carers — positioned to guide selection of the existing protease-modulating dressings (e.g. UrgoStart) and to track proteolytic activity over time alongside wound size. Costing came from the KiCad bill of materials plus estimated enclosure and reagent costs. The team is candid that clinical validation, bacterial confounding of the chosen peptides, and testing on real clinical samples all remain outstanding. A Smith + Nephew work experience business insight day and an Imperial iGEM mini-jamboree (24 August, UK and European teams) fed the commercial framing.","partners":["Dr Anatoliy Markiv (King's College London)","Dr Ciaran Devoy (author of the synthetic protein protease sensor platform)","Dr Brian Callahan (split-GFP protease sensing)","Dr Ben Bullen (podiatry and wound care)","Ms Kathleen Leak (tissue viability specialist)","Trish Idensohn","Dr Honda Hsu (plastic surgeon, Taiwan)","Professor Keith Harding (Wound Healing Research Unit, Cardiff University)","Professor Steven Jeffery (Pioneer Wound Clinics)","Professor Dato Harikrishna Nair","Professor Eleanor Stride (University of Oxford)","Professor Robert Marks (biosensors and biotechnology)","Professor Kevin Lam (University of Greenwich)","Professor Paul Davis (co-founder of Mologic)","Dr Martin Peacock (Zimmer and Peacock)","Dr Ana Maria Totea","Dr Kevin Lye (Accorto Regulatory Solutions)","Dr Douglas Queen (health economics of wound care)","Breakthrough T1D","Diabetes Scotland","Smith + Nephew (business insight day)","Imperial College London iGEM (mini-jamboree)","Professor Drew Hall / Biocoin open-source platform"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6462/wiki/f09.avif","caption":"Out-of-fold performance of the consensus score for all seven proteases: ROC curves with per-protease detection AUROC, precision-recall curves against a 0.25 prior, and the distribution of held-out positives' consensus percentiles against the uniform null.","credit":"Team Eton-College-UK, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6462/wiki/probe-cleavage-mmp9.webp","caption":"The sensing probe before and after MMP-9 cleavage: methylene blue on peptide SGKIPRTLTA joined by a PEG6 spacer to the gold electrode among ferrocene-hexanethiol, cut between T7 and L8, releasing the MB-ATL fragment.","credit":"Team Eton-College-UK, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6462/wiki/wiring-diagram.webp","caption":"Wiring diagram of the ProteaSign reader, built around the AD5940 electrochemical analogue front end and an nRF52840 microcontroller multiplexing four working electrodes.","credit":"Team Eton-College-UK, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6462/wiki/bio-detectives.webp","caption":"Year 8 pupils at Holyport working through the Bio-Detectives museum-sabotage clues with a team member; student and facilitator packs were released so the workshop can be rerun.","credit":"Team Eton-College-UK, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy their evaluation discipline, not just their model: before reporting any ML metric, cluster near-duplicate sequences by Hamming distance and split whole clusters across folds, then run a label-shuffle control and an ablation that strips the information you claim to be using — three cheap steps that turn a high-school classifier from a number into evidence. Their second transferable move is shipping the model as a live in-browser scorer with per-position attribution bars, so a judge or clinician can interrogate the prediction instead of taking the AUROC on trust.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/assets/DescriptionPage-CRFAzJI5.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/assets/SubstratePredictorPage-CyEQQMJS.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/assets/SurfaceEnzymeKineticsPage-B3hFHqb0.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/assets/SWVPage-Dx0cJgd7.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/assets/HardwarePage-DvJAf84t.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/assets/HumanPracticesPage-Dqe8y5eH.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/assets/EducationPage-DjCpVrhR.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/assets/ImplementationPage-kdhRbM8d.js","https://2026.igem.wiki/eton-college-uk/assets/ContributionPage-brszWapD.js","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/eton-college-uk"],"rank":91},{"team":"Athens","id":"6478","section":"Overgrad","country":"Greece","village":"Conservation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/","projectTitle":"ApiconIASIS","tagline":"Healing honeybees, sustaining pollinators: a precision phage-derived enzyme therapy for European Foulbrood","summary":"ApiconIASIS targets European Foulbrood (EFB), a statutory honeybee brood disease caused by Melissococcus plutonius for which the only legal response in most of Europe is burning the colony. The team mined 165 M. plutonius genomes for dormant prophages, ranked hundreds of candidate lysins and selected two enzymes (an endolysin and a GH25 muramidase), each built with and without a fused antimicrobial peptide, expressed and purified in E. coli. They showed roughly 40% loss of OD600 against M. plutonius (LMG 20360) with 50 ug/mL purified endolysin while producing no effect distinguishable from the untreated control against the bee-gut commensals Lactobacillus apis and Bombilactobacillus mellis, both Lactobacillales like the pathogen. It won because the whole chain is closed: a protein (not a replicating phage) delivered in brood food, three linked dry-lab models (protein optimisation, larval-gut pharmacokinetics, bioprocess scale-up), and an entrepreneurship case built on the cost of hive burning.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Model — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Education — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Entrepreneurship — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Conservation Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three distinct model layers. (1) A semi-rational protein engineering cascade for the poorly soluble GH25 muramidase (Protein-Sol 0.411, below the 0.45 aggregation threshold, with inclusion bodies confirmed in the wet lab): a 40-residue 'freeze list' built from three independent evidence sources (structural role, ESM-2 650M evolutionary constraint, literature), validated by all-atom molecular dynamics (RMSF < 2 A), then ThermoMPNN point-mutation ddG scanning, an ESM-2 log-likelihood filter at -2.0 to drop family-implausible substitutions, NSGA-II multi-objective search via pymoo over the 17 surviving mutations, and ColabFold refolding as a final structural gate catching epistasis; AlphaFold pLDDT > 70 was a stated input requirement. (2) A deterministic single-compartment ODE pharmacokinetic model of the honeybee larval midgut treating the lumen as a sealed, expanding compartment and the pathogen as absolute cell number N, with an Emax saturating kill law fitted to in-vitro lysis data (phi_max ~ 0.006125 /min, KE ~ 0.633 ug/mL, R2 = 0.935). (3) A bioprocess flowsheet model of a medium-scale E. coli plant with a three-stage seed train (baffled shake flask, seed bioreactor, production fermenter under high-cell-density culture), solving for biomass and product mass per batch.","dataviz":"The wiki stages results as a narrative rather than a figure dump: the landing page counts down the disease (90% brood mortality, 36% of surveyed colonies carrying the bacterium with 72% of those looking healthy) before showing the funnel 165 genomes to hundreds of candidates to two enzymes to one lead, then flask to bioreactor to hive to landscape. The model section front-loads a one-sentence summary of each pipeline and marks P1-P4 as falsifiable predictions, with P4 flagged as the main untested exposure because binding selectivity was never an optimisation objective. The optimisation page carries a Venn diagram of the three freeze-list evidence sources, RMSF and DCCM plots from the MD run, an ESM-2 log-likelihood filter plot and the NSGA-II Pareto front.","datascience":"Biological measurements were converted into decision rules rather than descriptive statistics. A pH-plate experiment was decomposed into two separable effects: kill rate is essentially flat across the lumen band (pH 5.5-7.5) while stability is strongly pH-dependent (t_half ~140-280 h at pH 5.5-6.7, ~7 h at pH 4, ~12 h at pH 7.5), which showed the enzyme is about 30x more stable in the lumen than assumed and dropped the required food concentration about 31-fold, making dilution rather than degradation the dominant loss term. Because nurse bees feed roughly 4,300 times a day, delivery was modelled as a continuous infusion at quasi-steady state. Full sterilisation within 48 h holds only up to about 455 cells (a saturation, not a dose, limit; a 300-cell inoculum already needs 26.2 ug/mL against a larva-safe cap of 23 ug/mL), so the team switched to a 'recovery' criterion that raised the handleable inoculum by four to five orders of magnitude to about 10^7 cells. The model's stated output is a starting dose plus an exposure profile (peak concentration, AUC, dosing duration, clearance time) handed to the wet lab and to a companion toxicity model.","software":"ApiconIASIS: Bee Wanderer, a browser-based educational game built for the EU-CONEXUS Hackathon in plain JavaScript, HTML5 Canvas, CSS3 and Three.js with no game engine: a custom 2D platformer engine (requestAnimationFrame loop, AABB collision, sinusoidal moving platforms, parallax) in level-engine.js with per-level config files, plus an interactive WebGL globe using Three.js OrbitControls and raycasting for level selection across Athens, the Himalayas, Tanzania, Uganda and New Zealand. Collectibles map to biotech stages (DNA Pollen, Enzyme Drops, Purification Beads); progression persists in localStorage with no backend, and a Weather Station pulls live data from Open-Meteo and wttr.in. All dry-lab scripts are released at https://github.com/igemathens2026/iGEM-Athens-2026---Software-; the wiki repository is gitlab.igem.org/2026/athens.","stack":["ESM-2 650M","ThermoMPNN","pymoo (NSGA-II)","ColabFold","AlphaFold","Protein-Sol","all-atom molecular dynamics (RMSF, DCCM)","Python","JavaScript / HTML5 Canvas","Three.js / WebGL","Open-Meteo API","wttr.in API","GitHub","GitLab"],"techniques":["multi-objective Pareto optimisation (NSGA-II)","protein language model constraint profiling","hard-constraint freeze lists from multi-source evidence","ddG stability scanning","structural validation gate (refolding)","single-compartment ODE pharmacokinetics","Emax saturating kill-law fitting","prophage mining across 165 genomes","bioprocess flowsheet simulation","seed-train scale-up modelling"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was run as a programme rather than a set of visits, across age groups and education levels, and deliberately bilingual (every pamphlet and deck in English and Greek). At NTUA Open Days (17 March 2026) they reached over 100 STEM-track candidates at an engineering school where students have largely dropped biology, pairing an interactive presentation on industrial biotechnology and its ethical framework with a tour of the Laboratory of Biotechnology and Industrial Biocatalysis; the team recorded that the students were most engaged by ethics, and built that framing into every later event. The portfolio also includes a multi-day summer programme at Villa Aimilia (design-a-microbe, strawberry DNA extraction, bees-leaves-cells), Second Chance Schools in Peristeri and Piraeus for adult learners, the 4th Panhellenic Synthetic Biology Riddle Competition, ChemExpo, the Pasteur institute and iGEM Fest. The Bee Wanderer game extends the same material into a free browser format.","ihp":"The human practices page is written as a ledger of what the project gave up. Two requirements came from beekeeping, not from the team, and now define success: the protein must reach the brood, and it must survive the larval gut. Delivery was moved from spray to feed because adjuvant surfactants are documented to harm bees and because the person holding the sprayer inhales it; protective equipment therefore appears in the protocols, not in the product. They chose a purified non-replicating enzyme over a live phage knowing it is weaker and more expensive, because a protein cannot spread, establish or transfer genes. They counter-screened against Lactobacillus apis and Bombilactobacillus mellis, the closest relatives of the pathogen in the hive, instead of easier distant organisms, and they used a reduced-virulence M. plutonius lineage although a virulent strain was available, accepting a weaker efficacy claim. Advisor Anastasis Galanis and beekeeper Peter Kasztelewicz (whose own hive footage is on the wiki) shaped these choices while they were being made; Peter's gentler methods, requeening and partial clean-out, are what motivated the feed route.","realWorld":"ApiconIASIS is positioned as the first target-specific sustainable EFB treatment, with the value proposition pinned to a quantified loss: quarantine and hive burning halt honey production for about eight weeks on top of the destroyed colony. Unlike antibiotics (not authorised in the EU, and resistance-breeding where used) or fungal extracts, the enzyme is argued to leave the bee microbiome intact and to leave no residue in honey, preserving market value. The business plan carries a pitch deck and an investor one-pager; the entrepreneurial analysis includes a customer map built from interviews with commercial beekeeping companies and individual beekeepers, and the scale-up model gives the costing basis (105 L final working volume, 51.62 g/L maximum biomass, 2.55 g product per batch) with the explicit target of pricing below the financial loss of hive burning so that no beekeeper is excluded.","partners":["Peter Kasztelewicz ('Beeman'), beekeeper, UK","Anastasis Galanis (team advisor)","National Technical University of Athens, School of Chemical Engineering (Prof. E. Topakas)","Laboratory of Biotechnology and Industrial Biocatalysis, NTUA","EU-CONEXUS Hackathon","TechnosetBee","Greek beekeeping associations","Villa Aimilia summer programme","Second Chance Schools, Peristeri and Piraeus","Hellenic Pasteur Institute","Twist Bioscience","SnapGene"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6478/wiki/optimization/fig7-pareto.avif","caption":"Pareto front from the NSGA-II search over the 17 mutable positions, trading predicted solubility against thermostability (ddG) for the GH25 muramidase; variants A, B and C were selected along this front.","credit":"Team Athens, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6478/wiki/optimization/venn-freeze.avif","caption":"Venn diagram of the three independent evidence sources (structural role, ESM-2 evolutionary constraint, literature) used to build the 40-residue freeze list; a position was frozen only if at least two sources agreed.","credit":"Team Athens, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6478/wiki/optimization/fig6a-rmsf.avif","caption":"Root-mean-square fluctuation from the all-atom MD run, confirming that the frozen residues are genuinely rigid (RMSF below 2 A) and turning the freeze list from a heuristic into a tested claim.","credit":"Team Athens, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6478/wiki/education/day2-strawberry-dna-villa-aimilia.avif","caption":"Strawberry DNA extraction with children at the Villa Aimilia summer programme, day two of a hands-on sequence that began with design-a-microbe and ended with bees, leaves and cells.","credit":"Team Athens, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy the freeze-list discipline: before optimising anything, lock the residues (or variables) that at least two independent lines of evidence call functional, then prove the lock with a simulation, and only then search aggressively over what is left; it converts a student ML pipeline into a defensible claim. Equally transferable is Athens' habit of writing down what each stakeholder conversation cost them (spray to feed, phage to enzyme, virulent to weak strain), which is far more persuasive to judges than a list of meetings.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/model/in-silico-optimization","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/model/pharmacokinetics-model","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/model/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/entrepreneurship/business-plan"],"rank":136},{"team":"WUXI-DSAS","id":"6485","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/","projectTitle":"ReNovo","tagline":"A hidden signal in a drop.","summary":"ReNovo is a whole-cell E. coli biosensor for urinary gentisic acid (2,5-dihydroxybenzoic acid), built as a non-invasive early-screening route for renal cell carcinoma (RCC), which is 80-85% of primary kidney malignancies and asymptomatic exactly when it is curable. The team adapted the maleylpyruvate-responsive GtdR/PgtdA system of Cupriavidus necator, deliberately omitting the gtdT transporter from the published circuit to obtain a monotonic, quantifiable dose-response, and used mCherry to dodge urine's green autofluorescence. Because urinary analyte concentration tracks hydration, a parallel colorimetric creatinine assay is run on the same chip and the result is reported as a gentisic acid-to-creatinine ratio in a three-tier risk band. The second-generation sensor PG2 gave a linear response over 0-12 uM (y = 1.094x + 16.8, R2 = 0.9764) with no significant artificial-urine matrix effect and no cross-reactivity to 2-, 3-, 4-HBA or salicyluric acid.","awards":["Best Sustainable Development Impact — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Business and policy analysis frameworks applied explicitly: PEST analysis of China and global markets, Porter's five forces, SWOT, a marketing-mix positioning exercise, a five-phase evidence-gated development roadmap to 2035 with documented decision gates, and a dated risk register. A concentric three-layer SDG impact model (Inner Core / Proximal Field / Outer Ripple) organises the sustainability analysis by causal distance rather than importance.","dataviz":"Unusually strong, data-first wiki: an interactive GLOBOCAN 2024 choropleth of age-standardised kidney-cancer incidence across 186 countries and territories with the underlying CSV published for download, keyboard-navigable; per-question survey charts that print exact n, option counts and percentages as accessible text; a concentric SDG framework figure and a cross-goal synergy/trade-off figure; an experiment-report calendar.","datascience":"Survey instrument run in two waves (n = 131 general awareness; n = 70 product-preference, including a ranked-preference question scored out of 5) with results reported per option and used to justify named design choices. Wet-lab statistics are reported properly: linear regressions with R2 (PG2 0.9764; creatinine Jaffe calibration R2 = 0.98; enzymatic cascade calibration R2 = 0.9614; undiluted induction R2 = 0.9294), two-way ANOVA for matrix effect (concentration p < 0.0001, matrix p = 0.6), one-way ANOVA for reciprocal interference (p = 0.9837), exponential and log-linear fits of positive-cell fraction (R2 = 0.9954 and 0.9723).","software":"No standalone software deliverable. The /software page states plainly that it covers only wiki tools (interactive kidney-cancer map, scientific illustrations, experiment-report calendar) with the implementation in the wiki repository. The /model page is equally candid: it is marked RECORDS PENDING and states that a team model with documented assumptions, parameters, code and validation has not been published, pointing readers to Results instead. A phone mini-program that computes the gentisic-acid-to-creatinine ratio, asks a pre-test aspirin question and prints a tiered report is described as a proposal, not a built artefact.","stack":["interactive web map (GLOBOCAN 2024 / IARC data)","CAD hardware drawings","proposed WeChat-style mini-program","wiki repository on GitLab"],"techniques":["microfluidic chip and optical-reader design with spatial and temporal baseline subtraction against a sealed fluorescent standard","four-enzyme creatinine cascade design with documented interferents (endogenous creatine, hemoglobin)","survey design and reporting","regression and ANOVA analysis","PEST / Porter / SWOT market analysis","risk register with gated review points"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Two public surveys underpin the engagement work rather than decorating it: n = 131 on awareness (71.76% had only heard the name of kidney cancer, 35.11% had never considered that early RCC is asymptomatic, 57.25% each named hospital queues and fear of invasive procedures as screening barriers) and n = 70 on product expectations (safe handling ranked top at 4.39/5; 61.43% wanted digital confirmation of a colour readout rather than reading it themselves; 61.43% felt only 'somewhat reassured' about engineered bacteria and wanted more safety information; 44.29% wanted a built-in inactivation step before disposal; 58.57% would share anonymised data only with identity confidentiality; 68.57% preferred short science videos and 67.14% detailed articles with charts). Outreach was then built to that evidence: dual-track materials (comics, posters, brochures in English and Chinese, videos for younger audiences; rigorous seminars for senior students and faculty), age-tiered workshops from kindergarten to Grade 11 (LEGO cell models for Grades 1-3, strawberry DNA extraction for Grades 6-8, gel electrophoresis loading and interpretation for IB Grade 11), a presentation for parents and faculty, and WeChat / RedNote / Instagram channels. On Ms. Fiona You's challenge to 'go beyond iGEM', the team committed to a permanent campus Synthetic Biology Club and structured knowledge transfer so the next cohort builds on rather than restarts the work.","ihp":"Nineteen named stakeholders across clinical medicine, diagnostics, regulation, bioethics and education, each mapped to the specific project section their input changed - and the changes are real, not cosmetic. The single strongest chain is the circuit redesign: the first-generation gtdR-gtdA-gtdT-mCherry sensor gave a non-monotonic response that peaked around 2 uM and then fell, making quantification impossible. Professor Hangxiang Wang suggested testing lower concentration gradients to check for promoter saturation; the team did, and the non-linearity persisted. Dr. Jing Shi then diagnosed the mechanism from the sequence architecture - the large expressed proteins and heavy transporter expression imposed metabolic strain - and Dr. Tengfei Hu proposed replacing active symport with passive diffusion by excising gtdT to stop intracellular 3-maleylpyruvate over-accumulation. Dr. Christina Feng reinforced it from a different angle: heavy metabolic load creates selection pressure for spontaneous loss-of-function mutations and plasmid clearance, which would make a deployed biosensor unreliable. The team deleted gtdT, and the second-generation PG2 construct restored linearity to R2 = 0.9764. Second chain, normalisation: Dr. Zhou (diagnostics researcher) warned that a single urinary metabolite moves with hydration, diet, medication and age so an elevated reading cannot be distinguished from a concentrated sample, and Dr. Gu (urology director) specifically recommended creatinine correction and first-morning-urine collection, while Dr. Xuan supplied the physiology - before a tumour invades the collecting system, biomarker secretion is very low. The team added a parallel creatinine assay as an internal reference and changed its reported output from raw fluorescence to a gentisic acid-to-creatinine ratio; it then changed chemistry between contexts for a stated reason, keeping the Jaffe picric-acid method for 96-well lab characterisation but switching the device to a four-enzyme cascade because Jaffe responds to non-creatinine urinary compounds and needs strongly alkaline conditions unsuited to dried reagents. Third chain, specificity: Dr. Wu (clinical laboratory physician, Wuxi No. 2 People's Hospital) said routine urine tests cannot separate nephritis or UTI from RCC, and Dr. Zhong warned that herbal medicines and diet confound urinary organic acids; the team ran an artificial-urine matrix panel (Na2HPO4, NaCl, KCl, creatinine, albumin, urea) and a look-alike panel (2-, 3-, 4-hydroxybenzoic acid, salicyluric acid, salicylic acid as the main aspirin metabolite), finding no matrix effect and no cross-reactivity, and added a pre-test aspirin question to the proposed app. Fourth chain, hardware and logistics: Dr. Wu demanded elimination of manual pipetting in favour of a single-use enclosed cassette plus automated reader; Dr. Vargas pointed them to Cologuard's non-contact sample handling as a benchmark; Dr. Christina Feng said in Chinese that she would 'recommend that you consider freeze-drying', naming -40 to -60 C cold chain as a commercial killer, and Dr. Zhong noted community clinics lack refrigeration - so lyophilised cells dried into the chip became the design target. Fifth chain, ethics and framing: Mr. Matthew Peatman argued a false positive is ethically acceptable because it only triggers non-invasive imaging whereas a false negative is disastrous, which set sensitivity as the priority over specificity; he also warned of 'psychosomatic health dysmorphia' from unregulated self-testing, and Dr. Martin Goffriller argued results must stay in the user's own hands, which produced local-only processing, opt-in anonymised data sharing and a three-tier Low/Borderline/Elevated report with an explicit disclaimer instead of a raw number. Dr. Wang Keming's insistence that the device be positioned strictly as a preliminary screening aid rather than a diagnosis is carried through the whole wiki. The notable discipline here is how consistently the team labels unvalidated claims: the 80 C / 20-minute thermal inactivation hold, hydrophobic vent containment, post-lyophilisation viability, and the three-tier cutoffs are each written as 'requires validation' or 'has not yet been demonstrated'.","realWorld":"The real-world framing is quantified and sourced: 442,570 new kidney-cancer cases and 144,871 deaths worldwide in 2024 (GLOBOCAN), China carrying roughly 18% of cases (80,211) and the highest national mortality (25,029) while sitting below the world age-standardised rate, so the burden is population scale rather than per-capita risk; 93.6% five-year relative survival for localised disease against 20.3% once metastatic; around 60% of cases found incidentally. The SDG case is built on named sub-targets with the team's own evidence rather than a logo wall: SDG 3 (3.4, 3.8, 3.d), 4 (4.4, 4.7, 4.a), 9 (9.1, 9.5, 9.b), 12 (12.4, 12.5, 12.8), 8 (8.2, 8.3, 8.5), 10 (10.2, 10.3, 10.4) and 17 (17.6, 17.16, 17.17), arranged in a concentric model and accompanied by a cross-goal synergy and trade-off analysis. The team also does something most teams skip: it checks its claim against an external commitment, noting that the UICC World Cancer Declaration 2025-2035 target of diagnosing 60% of cancers early is framed around cancers with existing evidence-based screening (breast, cervical, colorectal) and that RCC's absence from that list is precisely the gap. Regulation is specific: the National Medical Products Administration catalogues instruments and in-vitro diagnostic reagents separately and assesses them independently, so the reader and the biologically active chip follow two tracks of different length and the chip governs the launch date; the EU IVDR is cited as a separate conformity regime; China's Cancer Prevention and Control Action Plan 2023-2030 is noted as favouring early detection but not listing kidney cancer. On cost the team refuses a headline number, stating instead that cost per result is dominated by how often the reusable reader is amortised and that it avoids setting a selling price at this stage, while positioning unit cost low enough to be absorbed into a subsidised programme or public insurance, and identifying that payer, decider and beneficiary are three different parties. After the Jamboree: a five-phase evidence-gated roadmap to 2035 with a documented decision gate between phases and a dated risk register (clinical validity 2028, specificity confounded by salicylate or diet 2028, unresolved creatinine-versus-specific-gravity normalisation 2028, production QC variability 2031, regulatory classification or evidence gap 2033), plus the campus Synthetic Biology Club and the Jiangnan University School of Life Sciences link as the institutional carriers.","partners":["Dr. Haiwen Zheng, community hospital physician","Dr. Wang Keming, Head of Oncology, Second Affiliated Hospital of Nanjing Medical University","Dr. Xuan, Urology Department Director, Wuxi People's Hospital","Dr. Gu, Urology Department Director, Clinical Urological Center","Dr. Zhou, diagnostics researcher, Jiangnan University","Dr. Wu, clinical laboratory physician, Wuxi No. 2 People's Hospital","Dr. Zhong, integrative oncology and early diagnosis expert","Dr. Hangxiang Wang, First Affiliated Hospital of Zhejiang University","Dr. Jing Shi, synthetic biology specialist","Dr. Tengfei Hu, synthetic biology specialist","Dr. Christina Feng, academic principal and biochemist","Dr. Vargas, diagnostic testing and regulatory expert","Mr. Matthew Peatman, Head of Upper Secondary Humanities","Dr. Martin Goffriller, humanities specialist","Ms. Fiona You, Executive Principal","Ms. Lulu Zhu, Director of School Pastoral","Ms. Shuyi Xu, biology educator","Fan Jia, public representative","Ms. Gu, public representative","Jiangnan University School of Life Sciences"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6485/wiki/sustainability-figure-01-concentric-impact-framework.avif","caption":"ReNovo's concentric SDG framework: SDG 3 at the clinical core, SDGs 4/9/12 at the user interface, and SDGs 8/10/17 in the outer ripple, with layer position set by causal distance rather than importance.","credit":"Team WUXI-DSAS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6485/wiki/sustainability-figure-02-cross-goal-synergies-and-trade-offs.avif","caption":"Cross-goal synergy and trade-off analysis across the seven claimed Sustainable Development Goals.","credit":"Team WUXI-DSAS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6485/wiki/human-practices-specificity-panel.avif","caption":"The specificity panel built in response to clinicians Dr. Wu and Dr. Zhong: urine matrix components (Na2HPO4, NaCl, KCl, creatinine, albumin, urea) and structurally related phenolics (2-, 3-, 4-HBA, salicyluric acid) tested against gentisic acid.","credit":"Team WUXI-DSAS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6485/wiki/human-practices-reader-concept.avif","caption":"Proposed reusable optical reader, shown closed and open, 90 x 110 x 100 mm, designed after survey respondents asked for digital confirmation rather than reading a colour change themselves.","credit":"Team WUXI-DSAS, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy WUXI-DSAS's stakeholder map that indexes each of nineteen interviewees to the numbered project section their advice changed, so a reader can audit the causal claim; and copy their discipline of writing 'requires validation' next to every unproven design feature (thermal inactivation hold, lyophilisation viability, risk cutoffs) instead of letting a proposal read as a result.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/safety-and-security","https://2026.igem.wiki/wuxi-dsas/team"],"rank":7},{"team":"Worldshaper-Shanghai","id":"6497","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/worldshaper-shanghai/","projectTitle":"SweetCycle","tagline":"Don't sell the molecule, sell the step that makes it cheap to separate: engineered glycosyltransferases licensed into existing Reb A refineries","summary":"Worldshaper-Shanghai engineered the two glycosyltransferases that perform the final steps to Rebaudioside M (SrUGT76G1 and SrUGT91D2), screened eight designed variants in a contained Stevia hairy-root chassis with qPCR and HPLC read-out, and found that T284I raised Reb M to 21.96 micrograms per gram fresh weight against 15.44 for wild type while the SrUGT91D2 variants did nothing. The commercial insight is genuinely original: Reb M is expensive not because the pathway is hard but because it sits at about 2 percent of a pool of near-identical glycosides, so the only lever that matters is raising that fraction (eta), and the party who benefits is whoever owns the separation column. They therefore sell a licensed process asset to Reb A refiners rather than a product, backed by a published, recomputable techno-economic model that ranks three production routes and finds their own current enzyme performance gives a negative NPV in every scenario. They were nominated for Best Entrepreneurship in the High School section.","awards":["Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"A purpose-built Reb M Commercialisation Decision Model (25-year NPV at a 12 percent discount rate across mild, neutral and fast price-convergence scenarios) plus PESTEL, Porter's Five Forces scored force by force, SWOT with deliberately unbalanced counts because the null results are listed as weaknesses, 4P, Business Model Canvas rewritten for licensing, bottom-up TAM/SAM/SOM, a four-phase milestone table with quantitative gates and pre-committed fallback decisions, and a risk register where every row has a triggering measurement and the decision that follows.","dataviz":"Eighteen figures carrying analysis rather than decoration: an evidence chain where node area is influence on the plan and colour is source type, a log-scale cost-per-sweetness positioning map, an S3 cost build-up split into bought-in versus engineerable lines, a Sherwood-type separation-cost curve, a compliance-status matrix with ok / work-in-progress / not-assessed, a 25-year NPV chart, an economic-window heatmap, a product-architecture diagram and a stakeholder map that deliberately includes the parties their success would harm.","datascience":"The standout dry-lab contribution. They normalise every price to RMB per kilogram of sucrose sweetness equivalent (RMB/SSE) because a 300x and a 250x sweetener cannot be compared per kilogram, which immediately shows that their real competitor is sucralose at 0.33 RMB/SSE, not sucrose at 5.40. The separation-cost model is calibrated as C_sep = 40/eta on top of a shared RMB 198.9/kg, giving a twenty-fold fall from about RMB 2,000/kg at eta = 2 percent to about RMB 100/kg at 40 percent. Decision thresholds are stated as checkable numbers: alpha greater than or equal to 0.9 or TON greater than or equal to 108 for the enzyme route to beat the fermentation proxy, eta greater than or equal to 9, 16.8 and 26.5 percent for three different comparisons. The model is published as runnable code with a parameter spreadsheet.","software":"model_core.py producing model_results.json and then the figures, published so a counterparty can re-run the analysis, with a supplementary Excel of parameters and scenarios.","stack":["Python (model_core.py)","JSON results pipeline","Excel parameter and scenario workbook","HPLC","qPCR","Stevia hairy-root culture via Rhizobium rhizogenes","Questionnaire Star"],"techniques":["glycosyltransferase variant design (T284I, T284S)","RFC-compliant part registration","contained hairy-root functional screening in 8-10 weeks","steviol glycoside quantification by HPLC","techno-economic route modelling with NPV and sensitivity","Sherwood separation-cost calibration"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A 503-respondent consumer survey (Questionnaire Star, 2026) reported with the uncomfortable parts intact: natural/healthy/safe drives 61.95 percent of motivation but taste only 12.06 percent and price 4.16 percent, while 57.38 percent would preferentially buy if taste approached sucrose at a reasonable price, and 35.34 percent had never heard of stevia or monk-fruit sweeteners at all. They pair this with a brand-marketing observation that 80-90 percent of consumers cannot distinguish sweetener types, and draw the warning rather than the flattering conclusion: a natural label does not sell itself. Consumer content on Xiaohongshu and Douyin is scoped explicitly to label literacy and evidence-based sugar reduction, with a stated compliance boundary of no health claims and no in-house taste panels.","ihp":"Thirteen stakeholder interviews plus the survey, tabulated with who said what and, in a separate column, exactly what it changed. Three decisions are traceable to specific conversations rather than to any market report. AGV (Ai-Chi-Wei) brand R&D gave the priority order cost before supply stability before natural origin, which moved the success metric to cost per unit sweetness and narrowed the first market to premium beverage; AGV marketing added where not to compete, naming mass-market fruit tea as unwinnable and partial replacement as the realistic ask. Tiancao Technology, a functional food ingredient supplier, killed the plant-is-cheaper claim outright and pushed them from building capacity toward licensing. A fermentation engineer reordered the roadmap by stating that fermentation or in vitro catalysis is the pragmatic route for 5-10 years, that a new plant variety takes 7-12 years to commercialise, and that LCAs typically favour fermentation over leaf extraction by roughly 92 percent less cropland and 90 percent less water, which caused them to delete every claim that sunlight makes plants greener. Mr Lu, a grower and extension author, supplied the three clauses growers actually respond to (yield not reduced, purchase premium, guaranteed contract) and the line that without a demonstration field there is no trust, which became a hard Phase 3 gate. The China Food and Beverage Association established that different production processes are legally different raw materials even for an identical molecule, turning process disclosure into a named workstream. Most tellingly, three separate interviews told them whole-plant residue valorisation is a brand story rather than a purchase criterion, and they removed it from the base business case rather than argue with the data.","realWorld":"The most intellectually honest commercialisation analysis in this cohort, high school or otherwise, and the honesty is structural rather than decorative. The business model is a licence, not a product: a characterised SrUGT76G1 variant plus immobilisation and UDP-glucose-recycling parameters delivered as a technology transfer file, priced at a running royalty of 15 percent of the licensee's incremental gross margin (range 10-25 percent) with signing and milestone fees sized only to recover transfer cost, plus a per-hectare trait royalty of 10-15 percent later and a hairy-root pre-screening service throughout. Every price is anchored to a number the counterparty can verify in their own plant, which they state as the point: nobody is asked to believe a claim, only to run a calculation. Unit economics are fully built: target process cost RMB 438/kg at alpha 0.90 and TON 30, licensee incremental margin RMB 362/kg at a Reb M price of RMB 800/kg, royalty about RMB 54/kg, a five-year indicative P&L with every input as a substitutable row, cumulative break-even in commercial year 2 to 3, and a next-twelve-months funding ask of RMB 150,000-200,000 (USD 21,000-29,000) itemised down to calibration standards and two counterparty visits, with the explicit comment that they would rather present a small itemised ask than a round number. Market evidence is primary: 13 interviews and a 503-person survey, with secondary market data (Mordor, Grand View) used only where it is the right layer and with the two series explicitly declared non-comparable. The TAM is left unquantified and flagged as data gap G1 rather than fabricated. The regulatory path is specific and already open for the lead route: China's National Health Commission announcements 2024 No. 2 and 2025 No. 1 approved steviol glycosides by enzymatic conversion and by fermentation as food additives, which is precisely why the enzyme route leads and the engineered trait follows, with the non-GM conventional-crossing route (published at 56.8-61.6 percent Reb M) kept as a pre-planned fallback that avoids the GM layer. Now the blunt assessment. There is no company, no signed licence, no letter of intent, and one engaged counterparty rather than a customer; they say so in their own weaknesses. Their SrUGT91D2 variants produced a null result across five samples and they publish it, arguing correctly that doing so is what makes the SrUGT76G1 result credible. Their own Reb M quantification is labelled directional only: single batch, no biological replicate, all peak areas below the lowest calibration standard, so absolute values are extrapolated, and the fix (0.5, 1 and 2 microgram standards) is listed as outstanding work. Every performance number in the business case is still a literature value, which they say outright. And the model's most important finding is one that undermines their own pitch: at today's enzyme performance the NPV is negative in every scenario, and the economic window for the in vitro route is 0.7-4.6 years, shorter than the payback period, so the advantage is a window rather than a moat. The continuation plan is correspondingly disciplined: a four-artefact customer-validation gate for the next twelve months (a named refinery completing non-food performance testing, a brand defining partial-replacement acceptance criteria, a toll manufacturer quoting the kilogram-scale process, a regulatory and freedom-to-operate screen), with the statement that until those exist the NPV and P&L remain scenario analyses rather than sales forecasts. They even list the parties their success would disadvantage, including Reb A-only refiners holding the wrong asset and smallholders likely to be reached last, and the public-health trade-off that cheaper sucrose-like sweetness is only a gain if it displaces sugar rather than water.","partners":["Tiancao Technology (functional food ingredient supplier; first named economic buyer, engaged)","AGV / Ai-Chi-Wei, China Taiwan (brand R&D and marketing; specification pull)","China Food & Beverage Association (regulatory and standard-setting convening)","Mr Lu, stevia grower and extension author (trait adoption and demonstration plots)","Shanghai Chenshan Botanical Garden (plant and scientific infrastructure)","A fermentation engineer and a plant synthetic biologist (route and containment advice)","Toll manufacturer or enzyme house (to be contracted in Phase 2)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6497/wiki/worldshaper-shanghai/entrepreneurship/image3.webp","caption":"Positioning map of cost per sucrose-sweetness equivalent on a log scale, showing sucralose at 0.33 RMB/SSE as the ceiling over the whole natural-sweetener category","credit":"Team Worldshaper-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6497/wiki/worldshaper-shanghai/entrepreneurship/image15.webp","caption":"Twenty-five-year NPV by route and scenario at a 12 percent discount rate; only the combined enzyme-then-trait strategy stays positive across all three price-convergence paths","credit":"Team Worldshaper-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6497/wiki/worldshaper-shanghai/entrepreneurship/image16.webp","caption":"Economic-window heatmap: the year the enzyme licence stops making money under each price path, giving the 0.7 to 4.6 year window on today's enzyme performance","credit":"Team Worldshaper-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6497/wiki/worldshaper-shanghai/entrepreneurship/image1.webp","caption":"Evidence chain in which node area is the influence a source had on the plan and colour is the source type, built from 13 interviews and a 503-person survey","credit":"Team Worldshaper-Shanghai, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Build one recomputable decision model per project, published as code plus parameters, whose job is to tell you which route your asset belongs in and what performance threshold makes it worth pursuing, then write every milestone as a quantitative gate with a pre-committed continue, modify or stop decision. The practice that makes it work is publishing the result that hurts: their model says today's enzyme gives a negative NPV, and saying so is what makes every other number believable.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/worldshaper-shanghai/","https://2026.igem.wiki/worldshaper-shanghai/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/worldshaper-shanghai/assets/entrepreneurship-DyASDvRD.js"],"rank":7},{"team":"NIS Almaty-Medeu","id":"6523","section":"High School","country":"Kazakhstan","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/nis-almaty-medeu/","projectTitle":"MiRVOX (Pulmora and OncoNose)","tagline":"Two approaches to accessible lung cancer screening: programmable RNA detection of blood microRNAs, and exploratory breath volatile organic compound patterns","summary":"NIS Almaty-Medeu built MiRVOX, two parallel platforms for accessible lung cancer research: Pulmora, a synthetic biology platform for programmable detection of circulating microRNAs in blood, and OncoNose, an exploratory engineering project on volatile organic compound patterns in exhaled breath. The team states plainly on its landing page that neither platform is a clinically validated diagnostic test. The project is framed by hard numbers - 2.5 million lung cancer diagnoses and 1.8 million deaths worldwide in 2022, 12.4% of new cancers and 18.7% of cancer deaths, and 4,429 estimated new cases in Kazakhstan where it is the second most diagnosed cancer and the most common cancer in men. Their education and inclusivity work is extraordinarily large for a high-school team and is written with unusual methodological honesty, including a published disagreement between two of their own documents' participant counts.","awards":["Inclusivity Award — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"An explicit four-step human practices loop (Ask, Understand, Change, Check back) with the questions for each stakeholder group published before the conversations happened, deliberately 'to keep us honest about what we learned afterwards'. Inclusivity was structured as three questions: what prevents participation, whose input should guide our response, and what can we change so people have a meaningful opportunity to contribute - with a barrier-to-response table published for each strand. A five-stage workshop framework (Introduction, Demonstration, short explanation, Practical work, Presenting the result) with three prepared difficulty levels per activity.","dataviz":"Barrier-and-response tables mapping each identified barrier to the specific adaptation it produced; a day-by-day accelerator schedule table with focus, activities and output per day; a feedback table mapping each piece of feedback to the implication it had for programme delivery; partnership tables distinguishing institutions from contributions. The wiki also uses explicitly labelled 'Limitation' callout boxes throughout.","datascience":"Reach figures are reported with deliberate methodological care: the team distinguishes the 70-school partnership network from the 23 schools visited in person, flags that summer attendance figures may include returning children, and separately records mentor-network size versus completed mentoring outcomes. Most strikingly, they publish a 'Limitation: two different participant counts' box stating that their Inclusivity document reports 120 participants in the biocomputing course while the Education document reports 64, that they have not established which is correct, and that neither figure should be quoted until confirmed. They also state that their evidence shows participation and application but that longer-term changes in understanding would require more consistent follow-up.","software":"Code Against Cancer, a three-module no-equipment computational biology course: 'Find the Bug in DNA' (compare sequences in Google Sheets, identify differences, then encounter sequence search via BLAST), '3D Molecular Tetris' (explore protein structures in Mol* Viewer and relate molecular shape to interaction) and 'Pipeline of Life' (order the stages of a simplified biological analysis workflow on a collaborative Miro board). Lab Ladder, a 54-page illustrated code-free handbook, walks beginners through six tool 'rungs' with screenshots and worked accessions: NCBI (NM_000207), UniProt (P42212), Benchling (BBa_J23100), BLAST, Geneious Prime and AlphaFold (Q9NZQ7). A Kazakh-language facilitator platform, Tagylym, hosts the methodology.","stack":["BLAST","NCBI","UniProt","Benchling","Geneious Prime","AlphaFold","Mol* Viewer","Google Sheets","Miro","LEGO robotics programming","Google Meet","Kahoot"],"techniques":["microRNA biomarker detection","cell-free reactions intended for use outside a laboratory","primer design","volatile organic compound pattern analysis from exhaled breath","simple optical reader concept for low-cost local manufacture"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Three large programmes, each documented barrier-first. First Qadam, run jointly with Panheya Robotics (Panheya supplied LEGO sets and robotics mentors, the iGEM team supplied biological content and volunteers), brings practical STEAM, engineering and biotechnology education to children aged 6-17 facing barriers of location, money, institutional setting, health or unadapted teaching formats. Six school visits in January 2026 reached 226 children; the team then contacted over 100 school directors and built a 70-school collaborating network (the Keleshek network), visiting 23 schools in person near Almaty, Taldykorgan and Taraz between February and May with online sessions and lesson recordings for the rest. Their key finding is quotable: access to equipment did not automatically mean access to meaningful participation, because teachers and children often needed help to start using resources they already had. So they trained teachers alongside students and supplied reusable instructions - and by September teachers and older students in nine partner schools were delivering activities themselves, with five school teams entering a dedicated competition league in June after the team helped create an entry route for Keleshek schools. Daniyar, a research project mentor at Al-Farabi School No. 31, reported running his own activity using the materials from their joint workshop. With Integrity Unite, a volunteer organisation supporting students from unstable financial backgrounds, a first online robotics session for 40 children showed the format was too theory-heavy and depended on kit the children did not own; the team responded by building the no-equipment, mentor-supported Code Against Cancer biocomputing course, run over one week in March after two weeks of recruitment, which produced roughly 34 completed mini-projects with 21 in revision and 26 at the idea stage. With 'House of Mercy: Autism Can Be Overcome', a first April session for 15 children revealed that shared LEGO sets meant nothing stayed with the children; the team then partnered with Sheber Bala to add supervised woodworking so each child made an individual wooden honey spoon plus a drawing on ecological and biological themes to take home, used calm communication and named badges, and allowed flexibility in pace, repetition, breaks and individual assistance across roughly eight summer visits of 10-20 children each. Crucially they write that they adapted to the particular group 'rather than treating a diagnostic label as a complete description of a learner' and explicitly refuse to generalise from one group of autistic children to all. An April workshop for 20 children in residential care combined supported LEGO work with age-banded books on innovation and biotechnology. A paediatric oncology visit for 15 children in Bishkek exposed a continuity barrier - the volunteers and the LEGO left with them - so after consulting KAUST iGEM representatives and a Central Asian iGEM ambassador the team built 12 individual hospital kits containing warm socks, stickers, a book, science colouring activities and crosswords, a change prompted by medical oncologist Baurzhan Ospanovich and summarised as a shift in planning question from 'what can we deliver during a visit?' to 'what opportunities will remain afterwards?'. Delivery was in Russian and Kazakh with visual demonstration alongside verbal instruction, three difficulty levels, two-to-four-hour sessions with flexible pacing, groups capped at five where intensive support was needed and 15 otherwise, with three to five volunteers per group, and multiple contribution routes within a session (assembling, programming, drawing, choosing materials, explaining a result, presenting). Second programme: Girls Go First, which targets the gap between interest in STEM and access to mentorship, networks, resources and leadership roles. A January online workshop with Technovation Girls put six female speakers in front of more than 130 girls across CIS countries; a spring global forum with Yurt Space (a girls-led aerospace engineering community) and Technovation Girls drew participants from more than three countries; a National Forum and StartUp Contest in January-February required every project to be girl-led with girls at least a third of each team, drew about 700 applications from around 386 teams, brought roughly 300 participants from 70 teams to finals in Almaty and Astana, and distributed a KZT 1,000,000 prize pool (backed by Women in Tech, InvestlyHub and StemTrades) as seed funding to seven girl-led teams; an April-June Business Case Championship with Astana Hub and its 11 regional subhubs involved 137 teams and 441 girls across 11 regions of Kazakhstan, each girl-led, with the challenge being to examine existing STEM systems and propose ways to make them more inclusive - and the ideas from that championship fed back into First Qadam's own inclusion policy. An ongoing global mentorship programme with the National Student Research Institute links 65 mentors to more than 45 mentees across over 15 countries, 31 of the mentors being FIRST Robotics and iGEM alumni. The team documents one concrete continuation chain honestly and modestly: Aidana, a Grade 11 participant, met judge Alexander Tyutin (Google Developer Groups) at one of their events, he became mentor for her EcoHub project, and her team went on to win an award at Technovation Girls - with the team noting that the award was Aidana's team's achievement and their own contribution was the opportunity through which the mentoring began. They also publish a limitation box stating that mentor-network size and completed mentoring outcomes are counted separately and that further follow-up is needed. Third: two flagship events. 'Can We Outsmart Cancer?', a free online forum on 8-9 August 2026 on Google Meet with five invited speakers (from UC Berkeley, IIT Patna, Oxford and KAUST) plus two student organisers, three tracks (fundamental research; applications, startups and commercialisation; synthetic biology and student participation through iGEM), a 'Truth or Myth?' Kahoot quiz on cancer misconceptions, and Q&A after each talk; 54 participants on day one and 41 on day two (95 participations) from ten countries - Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, the United States, Romania, Tanzania, Indonesia, India, Pakistan, Saudi Arabia and the United Kingdom - with an EdTech partner, 1609+, giving three free subscriptions and a 10% discount to everyone else. The team published both their own organising lessons (cold LinkedIn outreach works when personal and specific; one Instagram post is not enough so registration plus a WhatsApp group plus reminders are needed; attendance dropped on day two so send inter-day reminders; split content into beginner and advanced next time) and the speakers' critical feedback (schedule delays, technical difficulties, a suggestion to prepare backup questions). Then the Bio-Innovator Accelerator, a ten-day hybrid programme from 10-21 August 2026 for 55 participants in teams of three or four - 23 in person at American Corner Almaty and 32 online from Almaty, Astana and regional centres - built because promising laboratory developments in Central Asia rarely reach commercialisation. Week one covered problem validation, customer discovery, TAM/SAM/SOM market sizing, no-code MVP prototyping and unit economics; week two covered biosafety by design, regulatory pathways, intellectual property and freedom-to-operate searching in Google Patents, scientific storytelling, pitch decks and a live Demo Day before an independent jury. Eleven named guest lecturers ran the modules, including founders of ArtSkin, Grader.ai, LUMO/AsarQ and SoundVision, NU iGEM members on Human Practices and dry-lab prototyping, and the KAUST iGEM lead on project design and storytelling with optional 30-minute per-team mentoring. Demo Day projects included FermentAI, a computational platform predicting fermentation outcomes and simulating strains and reactor conditions, and MycoBlock, insulation and building panels grown from Ganoderma lucidum mycelium on local oak sawdust waste as an alternative to imported petroleum insulation. Finally, four open-access magazines published digitally in Kazakh, Russian and English, each deliberately serving one reader intent: Synapse (two issues, 15 and 20 pages - what synthetic biology is, iGEM's history from a 2003 MIT course, Kazakhstan's iGEM history from the first Nazarbayev University team in 2013, a five-step roadmap for joining a lab, questions to ask a lab before joining, six career paths, and national grants such as Zhas Galym and Bolashak); Beyond the Diagnosis (24 pages - cancer causes and types, modern treatments, eight myths versus facts, a patient story and clinician insight, microRNA biomarkers); Lab Ladder (54 pages - the illustrated code-free tool handbook); and iGEM Atlas (20 pages - the Oncology Village, award-winning 2024 projects, commercial transitions such as PvP Biologics to Takeda, startup guides). All four are archived in a public folder and offered explicitly as an adaptable blueprint future Central Asian teams may freely adopt, translate and build on, with Lab Ladder positioned as a new-member onboarding manual.","ihp":"The human practices page is built around a published commitment: list the groups whose answers would change the project and publish the questions for each before the conversations, so the team cannot retrofit what it learned. Five stakeholder groups are specified with their questions - clinicians (where in the pathway a triage test could help, what accuracy would earn their trust, what a false alarm costs), patients and the public (whether breathing into a device is acceptable and where, how an elevated-risk result should be explained, what should happen next and how fast), public health and policy (how lung cancer screening is organised in Kazakhstan today, what evidence a pilot would require, which regions have least CT access), bioethics and biosafety (what is required before collecting human breath samples, how to handle results the project is not qualified to interpret, what biosafety rules apply to cell-free reactions used outside a lab) and engineers and manufacturers (what a simple optical reader would cost to build locally, how cell-free reagents could ship without a cold chain). The team's framing sentence is that a screening test is a health-system decision, not a device decision, and that affordability, portability and follow-up capacity shape the design as much as the biology.","realWorld":"Consent and privacy practice is unusually explicit for a high-school team: activities were pre-coordinated with receiving organisations through correspondence, calls and preliminary visits; parents or legal representatives gave permission, with photography consented separately so children whose parents declined were not photographed; participants could decline an activity, a photograph or a question; access to original photographs and video was restricted to designated representatives of the visiting team and the receiving organisation and publication followed agreement with the organisation; and the team states that their photographs therefore do not represent every participant or activity. Prize-pool money in Girls Go First went through official consent-related documentation. Open resources: four trilingual magazines in a public folder, lesson recordings, age-banded books, science colouring activities and crosswords, guides and workshop methodology, with the full methodology available through partnership at a published contact address. External recognition includes a letter dated 22 April 2026 from the Ministry of Health of the Kyrgyz Republic thanking the team for supporting people with oncological conditions. Partnership documentation covers hospital work across multiple countries and is presented with an explicit distinction between resources already delivered and offers of future collaboration.","partners":["Panheya Robotics (LEGO sets, robotics mentors)","Integrity Unite (students from unstable financial backgrounds)","House of Mercy: Autism Can Be Overcome","Sheber Bala (woodworking expertise)","Keleshek school network (70 collaborating schools; 23 visited)","Al-Farabi School No. 31","National Centre for Motherhood and Childhood, Bishkek","Asan ophthalmology department, Dushanbe","MIOT International, Chennai","BLK-Max","Marengo Asia Hospitals","Artemis Hospital (Gurugram)","Fortis Healthcare / National Research Center for Maternal and Child Health, Astana (UMC branch)","Ministry of Health of the Kyrgyz Republic (letter of thanks)","KAUST iGEM and a Central Asian iGEM ambassador (hospital kit design)","Technovation Girls","Yurt Space","Astana Hub and its 11 regional subhubs","Women in Tech Kazakhstan, InvestlyHub, StemTrades (prize pool)","National Student Research Institute (mentor network)","American Corner Almaty (accelerator venue)","1609+ EdTech platform","Nazarbayev University iGEM","Startup Lab 119, iHub, Accelerate Prosperity and High Technology Park (Kyrgyzstan)"]},"images":[],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy two habits: publish your stakeholder questions before the interviews so you cannot retrofit your conclusions, and shift the planning question from 'what can we deliver during a visit?' to 'what will remain afterwards?' - which is what turned a hospital workshop into 12 take-home kits and turned 23 school visits into nine schools now running the activities themselves.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/nis-almaty-medeu/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nis-almaty-medeu/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nis-almaty-medeu/inclusivity/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nis-almaty-medeu/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nis-almaty-medeu/model/"],"rank":7}];
